/* Machine translations of Kritik der reinen Vernunft, 1st ed. (A, 1781), in the manner of Kemp Smith: see translation-briefs.js. Not the translators' own work. */
window.KANT_TR = window.KANT_TR || {};
KANT_TR['krv-a/kemp'] = {"krv-a-0001":["Critique of Pure Reason\nby Immanuel Kant, Professor at Königsberg.\nRiga, published by Johann Friedrich Hartknoch, 1781.",["'Critik' is rendered 'Critique' in the title, following the usual English title of the work."]],"krv-a-0002":["To His Excellency, the Royal Minister of State, Freiherr von Zedlitz.",["'Freiherr' is rendered 'Freiherr' (baron) and left untranslated as a title."]],"krv-a-0003":["Gracious Sir,",[]],"krv-a-0004":["To further, so far as one's own part allows, the growth of the sciences is to work in Your Excellency's own interest; for this interest is closely bound up with the sciences, not merely through the exalted post of a protector, but through the much more intimate position of a lover of them and an enlightened connoisseur. For this reason I avail myself of the one means that lies in any way within my power of showing my gratitude for the gracious confidence with which Your Excellency honours me, as though I could contribute something to this end.",["Restated slightly: 'Den Wachsthum ... an seinem Theile befördern' rendered 'so far as one's own part allows'.","'vor das gnädige Zutrauen' (= für) rendered 'for the gracious confidence'; 'einigen' read as 'einzigen' (the one means)."]],"krv-a-0005":["For one who delights in the speculative life, and whose wishes are moderate, the approval of an enlightened and competent judge is a powerful encouragement to labours whose utility, though great, is remote, and is therefore wholly misjudged by common eyes.",["'verkant' = verkannt, 'misjudged'. 'Nutze' = Nutzen."]],"krv-a-0006":["To such a judge, and to his gracious regard, I now dedicate this work; and to his protection I commend all the other concerns of my literary vocation. With the deepest veneration I remain",["The capitalised 'Einem Solchen ... Dessen ... Seinem' is rendered with lower-case pronouns; the sentence breaks off at the signature, continued in the next passage.","'Bestimmung' rendered 'vocation' (the sense of destiny or calling), not 'determination'."]],"krv-a-0007":["Your Excellency's\nKönigsberg, 29th March 1781.\nmost humble and obedient servant, Immanuel Kant.",["'Merz' = März (March)."]],"krv-a-0008":["Preface.",[]],"krv-a-0009":["Human reason has this peculiar fate that, in one species of its knowledge, it is burdened with questions which, as prescribed by the very nature of reason itself, it is not able to ignore, but which, as transcending all the powers of human reason, it is also not able to answer.",["'a weisen' in the OCR is read as 'abweisen' (to dismiss, ignore).","'Erkentnisse' rendered 'knowledge' with 'species' for 'Gattung'; 'Vermögen' as 'powers'.","The causal 'denn' clauses are recast as participial phrases ('as prescribed...', 'as transcending...') for smoothness."]],"krv-a-0010":["Human reason falls into this perplexity through no fault of its own. It begins with principles which it has no option save to employ in the course of experience, and which this experience at the same time sufficiently confirms. Rising with their aid (as its nature also requires) to ever higher, ever more remote conditions, it soon becomes aware that in this fashion its task must always remain incomplete, since the questions never cease; and it finds itself constrained to have recourse to principles which overstep all possible employment in experience, and which yet seem so unobjectionable that even ordinary human reason agrees with them. But by this procedure it falls into obscurity and contradictions. From these it can indeed conclude that errors lying hidden somewhere must be at the root of them; but it is unable to discover them, because the principles which it is employing, as they pass beyond the limits of all experience, no longer recognise any touchstone in experience. The battle-field of these endless controversies is entitled metaphysics.",["Divided the long second sentence into several; 'sie' supplied as 'human reason' for clarity.","'Probierstein' = touchstone; 'heißt' = 'is entitled'.","OCR 'zun Grunde' read as 'zum Grunde', 'bedien' as 'bedienen'."]],"krv-a-0011":["There was a time when metaphysics was entitled the queen of all the sciences; and if the will be taken for the deed, the pre-eminent importance of its object certainly entitled it to this honourable title. Now it is the fashion of the age to heap contempt upon her, and the matron, outcast and forsaken, mourns like Hecuba: *modo maxima rerum, tot generis natisque potens — nunc trahor exul, inops* — Ovid, *Metam.*",["'sie' supplied as 'metaphysics'. The Latin quotation (Ovid, Metamorphoses XIII) is left in Latin.","Shift from 'it' to 'her' follows the German personification ('die Matrone')."]],"krv-a-0012":["At first, under the administration of the dogmatists, her rule was despotic. But since her legislation still bore traces of the ancient barbarism, her empire gradually degenerated, through intestine wars, into complete anarchy; and the sceptics, a kind of nomads who abhor all permanent cultivation of the soil, broke up from time to time the civil community. Fortunately they were few in number, and so could not prevent the dogmatists from continually attempting to rebuild, though without agreement among themselves on any common plan. In recent times it seemed, indeed, as if an end might be put to all these controversies, and the legitimacy of her claims decided once for all, by a certain physiology of the human understanding (the work of the celebrated Locke). But it turned out that although the birth of this alleged queen was traced to the rabble of common experience, and her pretensions ought therefore to have been rightly suspect, yet, as this genealogy was in fact falsely ascribed to her, she continued to maintain her claims. Thus everything lapsed once more into the time-worn, worm-eaten dogmatism, and so into the contempt from which the science had been meant to be rescued. Now that all methods, as people persuade themselves, have been tried in vain, weariness and complete indifferentism prevail, the mother of chaos and night in the sciences, but yet at the same time the source, or at least the prelude, of their approaching transformation and enlightenment, when through ill-applied industry they have become obscure, confused and useless.",["Kept the long sentences largely intact but divided the 'Allein ... Da ihrer' and 'es fand sich' clauses for readability; 'iene' supplied as 'the dogmatists' and 'iener ... Königin' as 'this alleged queen'.","'Verstand' = understanding; 'Rechtmässigkeit' = legitimacy.","The last clause 'wenn sie ... geworden' refers to the sciences ('Wissenschaften'); resolved accordingly."]],"krv-a-0013":["For it is in vain to feign indifference in regard to inquiries the object of which cannot be indifferent to human nature. These pretended indifferentists, however much they may try to make themselves unrecognisable by exchanging the language of the schools for a popular tone, inevitably fall back, if they think at all, into metaphysical assertions, for which they professed so much contempt. Yet this indifference, which makes its appearance in the very flourishing of all the sciences, and affects precisely that kind of knowledge, the knowledge, if such could be had, which of all others we should least be willing to forgo, is a phenomenon that deserves attention and reflection. It is obviously the effect not of levity but of the matured judgment* of the age, which will no longer be put off with illusory knowledge. It is a summons to reason to undertake anew the most difficult of all its tasks, namely, that of self-knowledge, and to institute a court of appeal which will assure to reason its lawful claims, and dismiss all groundless pretensions, not by despotic decrees, but in accordance with reason's own eternal and unalterable laws. This court is no other than the critique of pure reason.",["Restated as several sentences; 'Auffoderung' (= Aufforderung) is made the predicate of a new sentence, and 'dieser' resolved as 'this court'.","'Urtheilskraft' = judgment; asterisk marks the footnote. 'Selbsterkentniß' = self-knowledge; 'Kentnisse' = knowledge.","'Machtsprüche' rendered 'despotic decrees', a slight interpretive gloss."]],"krv-a-0014":["*) Complaints are heard here and there of the shallowness of the way of thinking of our time, and of the decay of thorough science. But I do not see that those sciences which rest on a well-laid foundation, such as mathematics, physics and the like, in the least deserve this reproach; on the contrary, they maintain the old reputation for thoroughness, and in physics even surpass it. The same spirit would show itself active in other kinds of knowledge, if only attention had first been given to the rectification of their principles. In the absence of this, indifference, doubt, and finally strict criticism are rather proofs of a thorough way of thinking. Our age is the age of criticism, to which everything must submit. Religion, on the strength of its sanctity, and legislation, on the strength of its majesty, seek commonly to withdraw themselves from it. But in doing so they awaken just suspicion against themselves, and cannot lay claim to that unfeigned respect which reason accords only to what has been able to sustain its free and open examination.",["Footnote retained as such. 'Naturlehre' rendered 'physics'; 'Erkentniß' as 'knowledge'.","'Critik' rendered 'criticism' in the general sense, 'Critique' only for the title of the work."]],"krv-a-0015":["By this, however, I do not understand a critique of books and systems, but a critique of the faculty of reason in general, in respect of all the modes of knowledge after which reason may strive independently of all experience. It will therefore decide as to the possibility or impossibility of a metaphysics in general, and determine its sources, its extent and its limits, all in accordance with principles.",["'Erkentnisse' rendered 'modes of knowledge'; 'Vernunftvermögen' as 'faculty of reason'.","Second sentence is a restatement of the German's dependent 'mithin ...' construction; the subject 'it' (the critique) is supplied."]],"krv-a-0016":["This path, the only one that remained open, I have now entered upon; and I flatter myself that I have thereby found a way of removing all those errors which have hitherto set reason at variance with itself in its non-empirical employment. I have not evaded its questions by pleading the incapacity of human reason. On the contrary, I have specified them completely in accordance with principles; and, having discovered the point at which reason misunderstands itself, I have resolved them to reason's entire satisfaction. The answer to these questions has not, indeed, turned out quite as dogmatically visionary [*schwärmende*] curiosity might have expected; that could be satisfied only through magical arts, in which I am not versed. But such was not, we may suppose, the purpose of the natural determination of our reason; and the duty of philosophy was to dispel the illusion which arose from misinterpretation, even though many a highly prized and cherished delusion should have to be destroyed in the process. In this undertaking I have made thoroughness my chief aim, and I venture to assert that there is not a single metaphysical problem which has not been solved here, or for the solution of which the key has not at least been supplied. In actual fact pure reason is so perfect a unity that, if its principle were insufficient for even a single one of all the questions to which its very nature gives rise, we should have to reject that principle, since it would then be unable to deal with any of the others with complete certainty.",["Restated the closing sentences and the long second sentence as several sentences; the semicolons and 'sondern' clauses were recast for English idiom.","'sie' in 'sie nach Principien vollständig specificirt' and 'sie ... aufgelöst' refers to the questions (Fragen); 'ihr/ihren' to reason. Resolved by the argument.","'Naturbestimmung' rendered 'natural determination' (Bestimmung); it could also be read as 'vocation/destiny'. 'wol' rendered 'we may suppose'.","'Ausführlichkeit' rendered 'thoroughness'; Kemp Smith uses 'completeness' for Vollständigkeit elsewhere, so the two are kept apart.","'dieses' (das Princip) in the last sentence: 'man dieses ... wegwerfen könte' taken as 'that principle'. 'Unvermögen' rendered 'incapacity'. Original spelling 'mogte', 'könte' silently normalised."]],"krv-a-0017":["In saying this I seem to perceive in the reader's face an indignation, mingled with contempt, at pretensions apparently so vainglorious and immodest. Yet they are incomparably more moderate than those of any author of the most ordinary programme who professes, let us say, to prove the simple nature of the soul or the necessity of a first beginning of the world. For such an author undertakes to extend human knowledge beyond all limits of possible experience; and that, I humbly confess, is altogether beyond my power. I am concerned solely with reason itself and its pure thinking, and for a detailed knowledge of these I have no need to seek far afield, since I find them in myself. Common logic, indeed, already gives me an example of how all the simple acts of reason can be enumerated completely and systematically. The only difference is that in my enquiry the question is raised, how much I may hope to accomplish by means of reason, when all the material and assistance of experience are taken away.",["Divided the first sentence at 'und gleichwol' and the long second sentence into several; 'wovon ich demüthig gestehe' recast as a separate clause.","'dieses mein Vermögen' rendered 'beyond my power' (Vermögen = power); 'an dessen Statt' recast as 'I am concerned solely with'.","'nach deren ausführlicher Kentniß' refers to reason and its pure thinking; 'derselben' in the last clause taken as 'reason' (Vernunft). 'Programs' rendered 'programme' (an academic tract).","'Handlungen' rendered 'acts'; 'Erkentniß' = knowledge."]],"krv-a-0018":["So much for completeness in the attainment of each of these ends, and thoroughness in the attainment of all of them together. These ends are not set before us by any arbitrary design, but by the nature of knowledge itself, which is the matter of our critical enquiry.",["Split into two sentences; 'als der Materie' taken as in apposition to the ends/knowledge, i.e. the matter of the enquiry. The grammatical antecedent is ambiguous; I attach it to 'Erkentniß' (nature of knowledge) as the matter.","'Vollständigkeit' = completeness; 'Ausführlichkeit' = thoroughness (see previous passage). 'critischen Untersuchung' = critical enquiry."]],"krv-a-0019":["Certainty and distinctness, which concern the form of such knowledge, are two further points to be regarded as essential demands which may rightly be made upon an author who ventures upon so slippery an undertaking.",["'derselben' (feminine) has no clear antecedent; taken as referring to Erkentniß in the preceding passage, hence 'such knowledge'. 'Stücke' rendered 'points'. 'Deutlichkeit' = distinctness."]],"krv-a-0020":["As regards certainty, I have passed this judgment upon myself, that in investigations of this kind opinion [*meinen*] is on no account permissible, and that everything which bears any resemblance to a hypothesis is contraband, not to be offered for sale even at the lowest price, but to be confiscated the moment it is discovered. For every mode of knowledge that is to stand firm a priori itself announces that it is to be taken as absolutely necessary; and this holds still more of a determination of all pure modes of knowledge a priori, which is to be the standard, and therefore the very example, of all apodeictic (philosophical) certainty. Whether I have accomplished what I have pledged myself to do in this respect, I leave entirely to the reader's judgment; for it becomes an author only to present his grounds, not to judge of their effect upon his judges. But in order that nothing may, through no fault of his own, weaken these grounds, he may surely be permitted himself to note those passages which, though they concern only a subordinate aim, may give occasion for some mistrust. He thus forestalls, in good time, any influence which even the slightest hesitation on the reader's part in this matter might have upon his verdict regarding the principal aim.",["Restated the final long sentence as two; 'derselben Ursache' (the cause/grounds) taken as 'these grounds' (Gründe), the nearest sense; 'Ursache' is awkward here and the reading is a judgment.","'Erkentnisse' plural rendered 'modes of knowledge'. 'Bestimmung aller reinen Erkentnisse a priori' = a determination (i.e. a delimitation) of all pure modes of knowledge a priori.","'verbotene Waare ... beschlagen' rendered 'contraband ... confiscated'. 'vor schlechthinnothwendig gehalten werden will' rendered 'is to be taken as absolutely necessary'.","'apodictischen' rendered 'apodeictic' in keeping with Kemp Smith's usage."]],"krv-a-0021":["I know of no enquiries more important for probing the faculty which we entitle understanding, and for determining the rules and limits of its employment, than those which I have undertaken in the second chapter of the Transcendental Analytic, under the title *Deduction of the Pure Concepts of the Understanding*. They have also cost me the greatest labour, but, as I hope, not a labour unrewarded. This inquiry, which is somewhat deeply laid, has, however, two sides. The one refers to the objects of the pure understanding, and is meant to expound and render intelligible the objective validity of its concepts a priori; and for that very reason it belongs essentially to my purposes. The other seeks to consider the pure understanding itself, in respect of its possibility and the cognitive faculties [*Erkenntnißkräfte*] on which it itself rests, and so to consider it in its subjective aspect. Although this discussion is of great importance in relation to my chief purpose, it does not belong essentially to it. For the chief question always remains, what and how much can understanding and reason know apart from all experience, and not, how is the faculty of thought itself possible? The latter is, as it were, a search for the cause of a given effect, and so far has in it something of the nature of a hypothesis (though, as I shall show on another occasion, this is not in fact the case). It would therefore seem that here is a case in which I take the liberty of [merely] holding an opinion [*meinen*], and that the reader must accordingly be free to hold a different one. In view of this I must forestall the reader by the reminder that, should my subjective deduction not have produced in him the complete conviction I expect, the objective deduction, with which I am here principally concerned, none the less retains its full force; and for this what is said on pages 92 to 93 may, if necessary, be sufficient by itself.",["Divided several long sentences; 'Diese Betrachtung' rendered 'This inquiry' to avoid repetition of 'enquiry'. 'Hauptstücke' rendered 'chapter'; 'heißen' via 'nennen' as 'entitle'.","'[merely]' is supplied in square brackets to bring out the contrast with certainty in the preceding passage; the German has only 'zu meinen'.","'Erkenntnißkräfte' rendered 'cognitive faculties' (Kraft/Vermögen); 'Seite 92 bis 93' refers to the pagination of the first edition (A)."]],"krv-a-0022":["As regards, finally, clearness, the reader is entitled to demand, first, discursive (logical) clearness, through concepts; but then also intuitive (aesthetic) clearness, through intuitions, that is, through examples or other illustrations *in concreto*. For the first I have made sufficient provision. That concerned the essence of my undertaking, but it was also the contingent cause why I have been unable to satisfy the second demand, which, though not so strict, is yet a fair one. Almost throughout the progress of my work I have been undecided how I should deal with this. Examples and illustrations seemed to me always to be necessary, and accordingly they did actually find their due places in the first draft. But I very soon saw the magnitude of my task and the multitude of the objects with which I should have to deal; and when I became aware that these, treated in a dry, purely scholastic manner, would of themselves already extend the work sufficiently, I found it inadvisable to swell it still further with examples and illustrations, which are necessary only for popular purposes. This seemed the more inadvisable because the work could in no way be adapted to popular use, and because those who are genuinely versed in the science do not need this assistance so much. It is indeed always agreeable, but here it might even have had consequences contrary to my purpose. The Abbé Terrasson says, it is true, that if the size of a book is measured not by the number of pages but by the time required to understand it, it can be said of many a book that it would be much shorter if it were not so short. On the other hand, if we direct our aim to the comprehensibility of a wide-ranging whole of speculative knowledge which is yet connected in a single principle, we could say with equal right that many a book would have been much clearer if it had not been intended to be made so very clear. For the aids to clearness, while they help in the parts, often confuse in the whole, since they do not allow the reader to attain quickly enough to a survey of the whole; and with all their bright colours they paste over, and make unrecognisable, the articulation or structure of the system, although it is on this that, if we are to judge of the system's unity and soundness, most depends.",["Kant's long sentence beginning 'Ich sahe aber...' is divided into several sentences; 'zumal...' and 'ob zwar...' clauses are restated as separate sentences, with 'This seemed the more inadvisable because' supplied to carry the connection.","'Anschauungen... in concreto': 'intuitions' retained for the technical sense; 'd. i.' rendered 'that is'.","'diese' (after 'Gegenstände') taken to refer to the objects, not to examples; the antecedent of 'es' ('wie ich es hiemit halten solte') is the question of examples, rendered 'this'.","'Gnüge leisten' rendered 'satisfy'; 'Gliederbau' rendered 'structure' alongside 'articulation'.","'Erkentniß' rendered 'knowledge'."]],"krv-a-0023":["It may, I think, serve as no small inducement to the reader to unite his efforts with those of the author, when he has the prospect of carrying out, completely and yet lastingly, a great and important work according to the plan here laid before him. Now metaphysics, in accordance with the concepts of it which we shall here give, is the only one of all the sciences which may promise itself such completion, and that in a short time and with only little, but united, effort. Nothing will remain for posterity save to arrange everything in the didactic manner according to its own purposes, without being able in the least to increase the content. For metaphysics is nothing but the inventory of all our possessions through pure reason, systematically ordered. Nothing can escape us here, because what reason brings forth entirely out of itself cannot be hidden, but is brought to light by reason itself as soon as the common principle of it has been discovered. The perfect unity of this kind of knowledge, derived solely from pure concepts, and uninfluenced by anything from experience, or even by any special intuition which would lead to determinate experience, so as to extend or increase it, makes this unconditioned completeness not only practicable but also necessary. *Tecum habita et noris, quam sit tibi curta supellex.* Persius.",["Two sentences restated: 'so daß nichts vor die Nachkommenschaft übrig bleibt' is made a separate sentence.","'Die vollkommene Einheit... machen' has a plural verb with a singular subject; I treat the subject as 'unity' and render the verb singular (smoothing an irregularity).","'welche... leiten solte' rendered 'which would lead to determinate experience'; 'bestimte Erfahrung' rendered 'determinate experience'.","'ihrer Absichten' (the posterity's) is taken as 'its own purposes'; 'Erkentnisse' = 'knowledge'.","The Persius quotation (Satires IV.52) is left in Latin."]],"krv-a-0024":["Such a system of pure (speculative) reason I hope myself to supply under the title *Metaphysics of Nature*. It is to have, with not yet half the length, a content incomparably richer than that of the present Critique, which had first to expound the sources and conditions of its possibility, and needed to clear and level ground that was wholly overgrown. Here I expect from my reader the patience and impartiality of a judge; there, however, the readiness and support of a fellow-helper. For however completely all the principles of the system are set forth in the Critique, the full detail of the system itself still requires that no derivative concepts be lacking. These cannot be reckoned up *a priori*, but have to be sought out gradually. Likewise, since in the Critique the whole synthesis of concepts was exhausted, it is further demanded here that the same be done in regard to analysis. All this is easy, and more entertainment than labour.",["Sentence divided at the semicolon and at 'imgleichen'; 'Here/there' (hier/dort) refer to the Critique and the future system respectively; 'dort' in the second clause refers to the Critique.","'Critik' rendered 'Critique' as title-like; 'Ueberschlag bringen' rendered 'reckon up'.","'Metaphysik der Natur' given in italics as a title."]],"krv-a-0025":["I have still a few remarks to make concerning the printing. Since the beginning of it was somewhat delayed, I was able to see only about half of the printed sheets. In these I do find some misprints, though none that confuse the sense, except the one on p. 379, line 4 from the bottom, where *specific* must be read instead of *sceptical*. The Antinomy of Pure Reason, from page 425 to 461, is arranged in the manner of a table, so that everything belonging to the thesis runs continuously on the left, and everything belonging to the antithesis on the right-hand side. I arranged it thus in order that proposition and counter-proposition might be the more easily compared with one another.",["Kant's text says 'specifisch an statt sceptisch gelesen werden muß'; the sense is that 'specific' should be read for 'sceptical' — the reverse of my first reading would be wrong. Here I follow the German literally: *sceptisch* is the misprint's reading; the correct word is *specifisch*. The English clause, 'where *specific* must be read instead of *sceptical*', conveys this.","Sentence 'Da der Anfang... konte' divided; page references are those of the first edition."]],"krv-a-0026":["Contents.",[]],"krv-a-0027":["Introduction. Page 1",[]],"krv-a-0028":["I. Transcendental Doctrine of Elements. 17",["The OCR 'Transseendentale' is silently corrected to 'Transscendentale'."]],"krv-a-0029":["First Part. Transcendental Aesthetic. 19",[]],"krv-a-0030":["Section 1. Of Space. 22",[]],"krv-a-0031":["Section 2. Of Time. 30",["The original has ditto dashes ('2. ‒ ‒ ‒') for 'Abschnitt'; the word is supplied in English."]],"krv-a-0032":["Second Part. Transcendental Logic. 50",[]],"krv-a-0033":["Division 1. Transcendental Analytic, in two Books with their various Chapters and Sections. 64",[]],"krv-a-0034":["Division 2. Transcendental Dialectic, in two Books with their various Chapters and Sections. 293",[]],"krv-a-0035":["Page",[]],"krv-a-0036":["II. Transcendental Doctrine of Method. 705 Chapter 1. The Discipline of Pure Reason. 708 Chapter 2. The Canon of Pure Reason. 795 Chapter 3. The Architectonic of Pure Reason. 832 Chapter 4. The History of Pure Reason. 852",["The ditto marks (〃〃〃) after the numerals are rendered as 'Chapter' (Hauptstück) throughout."]],"krv-a-0037":["Introduction.",[]],"krv-a-0038":["I. Idea of Transcendental Philosophy.",[]],"krv-a-0039":["Experience is without doubt the first product to which our understanding gives rise, in working up the raw material of sensible sensations. It is for that very reason the first instruction, and in its progress is so inexhaustible in fresh teaching that the interlinked life of all future generations will never lack new knowledge to be gathered on this soil. Yet it is by no means the only field to which our understanding can be confined. Experience tells us, indeed, what is, but not that it must necessarily be so and not otherwise. For this very reason it gives us no true universality; and reason, which is so eager for this kind of knowledge, is stimulated by it rather than satisfied. Such universal modes of knowledge, which at the same time have the character of inner necessity, must be clear and certain of themselves, independently of experience. They are therefore entitled modes of knowledge *a priori*; whereas, on the contrary, what is merely borrowed from experience is, as the saying is, known only *a posteriori*, or empirically.",["'Kentnissen' (new knowledge) rendered 'knowledge'; 'Erkentnisse' in the plural rendered 'modes of knowledge'.","'vor sich selbst' rendered 'of themselves'; 'man nennt sie' rendered 'they are entitled'.","'Sie ist... Belehrung': 'Belehrung' rendered 'instruction'; 'Zeugungen' rendered 'generations'."]],"krv-a-0040":["Now it is a very remarkable fact that even among our experiences there are modes of knowledge which must have their origin a priori, and which perhaps serve only to give connection to our representations of the senses. For even if we remove from experience everything that belongs to the senses, certain original concepts, and judgments generated from them, still remain. These must have arisen entirely a priori, independently of experience, because they make it possible for us to say more of the objects that appear to the senses than mere experience would teach, or at least to believe that we can say more; and because they make assertions contain true universality and strict necessity, such as merely empirical knowledge cannot supply.",["Erkentnisse (Kant's spelling) rendered 'modes of knowledge' since the plural is required; 'Erkentniß' in the singular is 'knowledge'.","The sentence beginning 'weil sie machen' has two clauses governed by 'daß'; I made the second explicit by repeating 'because they make' and split the long sentence with a full stop after 'a priori'? No: split occurs before 'These must have arisen'. 'die ersteren' (the former) refers to Erfahrungen, rendered 'experience'.","'Vorstellungen der Sinne' kept as 'representations of the senses'."]],"krv-a-0041":["But what is of far greater significance is this: certain modes of knowledge even leave the field of all possible experience, and by means of concepts to which no corresponding object can ever be given in experience, seem to extend the range of our judgments beyond all its limits.",["'Erkentnisse' = 'modes of knowledge'; 'derselben' refers to experience; 'Was aber noch weit mehr sagen will' rendered idiomatically as 'what is of far greater significance'.","'den Anschein haben' = 'seem'."]],"krv-a-0042":["And it is precisely in these latter modes of knowledge, which pass beyond the sensible world and in which experience can furnish neither guidance nor correction, that our reason carries on those enquiries which we regard as being, in respect of their importance, far superior, and in their ultimate purpose much more sublime, than anything the understanding can learn in the field of appearances. In their pursuit we will risk everything, even the danger of error, rather than abandon investigations of such moment on any ground of hesitation, or of disdain and indifference.",["Restated as two sentences: the 'wobey' clause is made a separate sentence ('In their pursuit').","'Bedenklichkeit' rendered 'hesitation'; 'Geringschätzung' 'disdain'. 'liegen die Nachforschungen unsrer Vernunft' rendered 'our reason carries on those enquiries' for English idiom."]],"krv-a-0043":["Now it seems natural that, as soon as we have left the ground of experience, we should not proceed straightway to erect an edifice with modes of knowledge which we possess without knowing whence they come, and on the credit of principles whose origin is unknown to us, without having first assured ourselves of its foundations by careful investigation. It seems natural, therefore, that the question would long ago have been raised, how the understanding can arrive at all these modes of knowledge a priori, and what extent, validity and worth they may have. And in fact nothing is more natural, if by 'natural' we understand what ought fairly and reasonably to happen. But if we understand by it what ordinarily happens, then nothing on the contrary is more natural and more intelligible than that this investigation should have been left undone for so long.\n\nFor one part of these modes of knowledge, the mathematical, has long been in possession of reliability, and so gives rise to a favourable expectation in regard to the others also, although these may be of quite different nature. Besides, when we are beyond the circle of experience we are sure of not being contradicted by experience. The attraction of extending our knowledge is so great that we can be stopped in our progress only by running up against a plain contradiction. This, however, can be avoided if we frame our inventions with caution, though they do not on that account cease to be inventions. Mathematics gives us a shining example of how far we can advance in a priori knowledge independently of experience. Now it is true that mathematics occupies itself with objects and modes of knowledge only so far as they can be exhibited in intuition. But this circumstance is easily overlooked, since the intuition in question can itself be given a priori, and is therefore hardly to be distinguished from a mere pure concept. Encouraged by such a proof of the power of reason, the impulse to extend knowledge sees no limits.\n\nThe light dove, cleaving the air in her free flight, and feeling its resistance, might imagine that flight would be still easier in empty space. It was thus that Plato left the world of the senses, as setting too narrow limits to the understanding, and ventured beyond it on the wings of the ideas, in the empty space of pure understanding. He did not observe that with all his efforts he made no advance—meeting no resistance that might, as it were, serve as a support upon which he could take a stand, to which he could apply his powers, and so set the understanding in motion. It is, indeed, the common fate of human reason in speculation to complete its edifice as speedily as may be, and only afterwards to enquire whether the foundation is reliable. All sorts of excuses are then appealed to, in order to reassure us of its soundness, or rather indeed to enable us to dispense altogether with so late and so dangerous an enquiry.\n\nWhat keeps us free from all apprehension and suspicion during the building, and flatters us with a seeming thoroughness, is the following. A great part, perhaps the greater part, of the business of our reason consists in analysis of the concepts which we already have of objects. This yields us a considerable number of modes of knowledge which, although they are nothing but elucidations or explanations of what has already been thought in our concepts (though still in a confused manner), are yet prized, at least in respect of their form, as if they were new insights. In respect of matter or content, however, they do not extend the concepts which we have, but only disentangle them. Since this procedure yields real knowledge a priori, which has a sure and useful progress, reason is deluded, without itself noticing it, into surreptitiously introducing, under this pretence, assertions of quite another kind—assertions in which it adds to given concepts others entirely foreign to them, and adds them a priori, without our knowing how it comes to do so, and without such a question ever being allowed to arise. I shall therefore begin by treating of the difference between these two kinds of knowledge.",["Long passage divided into several sentences and paragraphs (paragraph breaks are mine, to aid the reader). The first sentence is restated so that 'ein Gebäude errichten' and 'der Grundlegung desselben' are made clear: 'its foundations' = the edifice's.","Smoothed: 'weil sie dem Verstande so vielfältige Hindernisse legt' is rendered 'as setting too narrow limits to the understanding' — this is a looser, traditional rendering; literally 'because it lays so many hindrances in the understanding's way'. The 'Wiederhalt' sentence is recast with a dash; 'steifen' read as 'stützen' (to take a stand).","'oder eine solche späte ... Prüfung abzuweisen' rendered 'or rather ... to dispense altogether with' as a free paraphrase; literal sense is 'or to ward off such a late and dangerous examination'.","'Erkentniß a priori' = 'knowledge a priori'; 'a priori' left unitalicised? Kept as Latin phrase in italics is conventional but Kemp Smith prints it roman; left roman.","'Beschönigungen' rendered 'excuses'; 'Erdichtungen' 'inventions'; 'erschleicht' 'surreptitiously introducing'; 'Vorspiegelung' 'pretence'.","'ist dieses' is followed by a new sentence ('Ein grosser Theil...'); I joined them with 'the following' and a colon-free sentence for English idiom."]],"krv-a-0044":["Of the Difference between Analytic and Synthetic Judgments.",[]],"krv-a-0045":["In all judgments in which the relation of a subject to the predicate is thought (I take affirmative judgments only, since the application to negative judgments is easily made), this relation is possible in two different ways. Either the predicate B belongs to the subject A as something which is contained (covertly) in this concept A; or B lies entirely outside the concept A, although it stands in connection with it. In the first case I entitle the judgment analytic, in the second synthetic. Analytic judgments (the affirmative) are therefore those in which the connection of the predicate with the subject is thought through identity; those in which this connection is thought without identity are to be entitled synthetic judgments. The former might also be entitled judgments of elucidation, the latter judgments of amplification, since the former, through the predicate, add nothing to the concept of the subject, but merely break it up, by analysis, into its constituent concepts, which were already thought in it (though confusedly); whereas the latter add to the concept of the subject a predicate which was not thought in it at all, and which no analysis could have extracted from it.\n\nFor instance, when I say, 'All bodies are extended', this is an analytic judgment. For I need not go beyond the concept which I connect with the word 'body' in order to find extension as bound up with it. To meet with this predicate, I have merely to analyse the concept, that is, to become conscious to myself of the manifold which I always think in it. The judgment is therefore analytic. But when I say, 'All bodies are heavy', the predicate is something quite different from anything that I think in the mere concept of a body in general. The addition of such a predicate therefore yields a synthetic judgment.",["'nenne ich' and 'heissen' both rendered with 'entitle'/'is entitled' per the method; 'Erläuterungs-/Erweiterungs-Urtheile' = 'judgments of elucidation / of amplification'.","Split at 'z. B.' into a new paragraph; the long sentence on the two kinds was kept whole but the final clause of the example was divided into short sentences. 'Theilbegriffe' = 'constituent concepts'. 'ich darf nicht ... hinausgehen' rendered 'I need not go beyond'.","In the first sentence I removed the repeated 'ist' awkwardness; 'ob es zwar' = 'although'. 'sondern ienen Begriff nur zergliedern ... um dieses Prädicat darin anzutreffen' recast as 'To meet with this predicate, I have merely to analyse...'."]],"krv-a-0046":["From this two things are now clear. (1) Through analytic judgments our knowledge is not in any way enlarged; the concept which I already have is merely set out in its parts and so made intelligible to myself. (2) In synthetic judgments I must have, besides the concept of the subject, something else (X) upon which the understanding relies in order to know that a predicate, though not contained in that concept, nevertheless belongs to it.",["Restated the second clause slightly: 'um ... zu erkennen' is kept as a purpose clause, with 'to know' for erkennen.","'aus einander gesezt' rendered 'set out in its parts' to bring out the analytic sense; the German is literally 'set apart/expounded'.","'Erkentniß' = knowledge; 'Verstand' = understanding."]],"krv-a-0047":["In empirical judgments, or judgments of experience, this presents no difficulty at all. For this X is the complete experience of the object which I think through a concept A, a concept which constitutes only a part of that experience. For although I do not include the predicate of weight in the concept of a body in general, the concept none the less designates the complete experience through a part of it; and to this part I can therefore add, as belonging to the former, other parts of the same experience. I can first know the concept of body analytically, through the marks of extension, impenetrability, figure, and so forth, all of which are thought in this concept. But I now extend my knowledge; and, looking back to the experience from which I had abstracted this concept of body, I find that weight is always connected with the above marks. Experience is thus the X which lies outside the concept A, and on which the possibility of the synthesis of the predicate of weight B with the concept A is grounded.",["Split the long sentence beginning 'Denn ob ich schon...' at the semicolon to keep the antecedent of 'zu welchem' clear (the part of experience, not the concept).","'Nun erweitere ich aber meine Erkentniß, und, indem ich...' is slightly irregular in German; smoothed with a semicolon.","'Merkmale' = marks; 'Schwere' = weight (A text; B has 'Schwere' too).","'Gegenstand' = object; 'Begriff' = concept."]],"krv-a-0048":["But in synthetic judgments a priori this aid is entirely lacking. If I am to go outside the concept A in order to know another concept B as connected with it, what is it on which I rely, and through which the synthesis becomes possible? For here I have not the advantage of looking about for it in the field of experience. Take the proposition, 'Everything which happens has its cause.' In the concept of something that happens, I do indeed think an existence which is preceded by a time, and so forth; and from this analytic judgments can be drawn. But the concept of a cause indicates something different from what happens, and is not contained in this latter representation at all. How then do I come to say of that which happens in general something quite different from it, and to know the concept of cause as belonging to it, although not contained in it? What is the X here on which the understanding relies, when it believes that it has found, outside the concept of A, a predicate foreign to it, which it yet holds to be connected with it? Experience it cannot be, because the principle cited adds this second representation to the first not only with greater universality than experience can supply, but also with the expression of necessity, and therefore wholly a priori and from mere concepts. Now the whole ultimate aim of our speculative knowledge a priori rests upon such synthetic, that is, ampliative, principles. For analytic principles are indeed highly important and necessary, but only to attain that distinctness of concepts which is requisite for a secure and extended synthesis, such as shall be a really new acquisition.",["Divided the long opening sentence: the clause 'da ich hier den Vortheil nicht habe...' is made a separate sentence beginning 'For here...'.","'Erweiterungs-Grundsätzen' rendered 'ampliative principles' (Kant's Latin sense of *erweiternd*).","'Anbau' rendered 'acquisition' in the sense of cultivation of new ground; 'cultivation' would be more literal; flagged as a choice.","'ienen' in 'ob zwar in ienen nicht enthalten' is plural in the German; the English refers it to 'what happens'.","'Vorstellung' = representation; 'Verstand' = understanding."]],"krv-a-0049":["A certain secret [Geheimniß] therefore lies concealed here*), the disclosure of which alone can make our advance in the boundless field of pure understanding secure and trustworthy. The task is this: to discover, with due universality, the ground of the possibility of synthetic judgments a priori; to gain insight into the conditions which make each kind of them possible; and to determine this whole body of knowledge (which constitutes a genus of its own) in a system according to its original sources, divisions, extent, and limits, not merely sketching it in a cursory outline, but determining it completely and sufficiently for every use. So much, by way of preliminary, on what is peculiar to synthetic judgments.",["The asterisk marks Kant's footnote, which is given as the next passage (krv-a-0050).","'Verstandeserkentniß' rendered 'knowledge of pure understanding' would be literal; I have smoothed it to 'pure understanding' as the field itself.","The infinitives after 'nemlich' are rendered as a statement of the task, with the semicolons added; the German is a single long sentence.","'Umkreis' = 'outline' (literally 'circumference'): a choice."]],"krv-a-0050":["*) Had it occurred to any of the ancients even to raise this question, it alone would have stood powerfully against all the systems of pure reason down to our own time, and would have spared so many vain attempts which have been undertaken blindly, without knowing what it is that one is really dealing with.",["Footnote retained as a footnote. 'Mächtig widerstanden haben' rendered 'stood powerfully against'; 'resisted' would also serve."]],"krv-a-0051":["From all this there results the idea of a special science which can serve as a critique of pure reason. Now every mode of knowledge is entitled pure which is not mixed with anything foreign. But a knowledge is in particular entitled absolutely pure if no experience or sensation of any kind is mingled with it, and if it is therefore completely possible a priori. Reason is the faculty which supplies the principles of knowledge a priori. Pure reason is accordingly that which contains the principles for knowing anything absolutely a priori. An *organon* of pure reason would be a sum-total of those principles in accordance with which all pure modes of knowledge a priori can be acquired and actually brought into being. The exhaustive application of such an organon would yield a system of pure reason. But since this is asking a great deal, and since it is still uncertain whether any such extension of our knowledge is possible at all, and in what cases it is possible, we can regard a science which merely passes judgment upon pure reason, its sources and limits, as the propaedeutic to the system of pure reason. Such a science would have to be entitled not a doctrine but only a critique of pure reason; and its utility would in fact be only negative, serving not to extend but only to purify our reason and to keep it free from errors, which is already a very great gain.\n\nI entitle transcendental all knowledge which is occupied not so much with objects as with our concepts *a priori* of objects in general. A system of such concepts would be entitled transcendental philosophy. But this again is, at the outset, too much. For since such a science would have to contain completely both analytic and synthetic knowledge a priori, it is, so far as our purpose is concerned, of too wide a scope; for we are to carry the analysis only so far as is indispensably necessary for insight into the principles of synthesis a priori in their whole extent, which is all that we are concerned with. This enquiry, which we can entitle not a doctrine but only transcendental critique, since its aim is not the extension of knowledge itself but only its correction, and since it is to supply the touchstone of the worth or worthlessness of all knowledge a priori, is what we are now engaged upon. Such a critique is accordingly a preparation, if possible, for an organon; and if this should not be attainable, at least for a *canon*, in accordance with which the complete system of the philosophy of pure reason, whether it consist in the extension or in the mere limitation of its knowledge, may some day be set forth, both analytically and synthetically. For that this is possible, indeed that such a system need not be of so great an extent as to preclude the hope of completing it entirely, can be gauged beforehand from the following consideration. The object here is not the nature of things, which is inexhaustible, but the understanding which judges concerning the nature of things, and this again only in respect of its knowledge a priori. Its stock of knowledge, since we need not seek it outside ourselves, cannot remain hidden from us, and in all likelihood is small enough to be completely taken up, appraised as to its worth or worthlessness, and brought under a right estimate.",["The passage is long and has been divided at 'Ich nenne alle Erkentniß transscendental' (new paragraph, my addition) and the last sentence, which has been broken into several sentences, as the method permits.","'Erkentniß rein' = 'mode of knowledge ... pure' used where the plural 'Erkentnisse' occurs; 'knowledge' elsewhere.","'heißen' / 'genannt werden' / 'nennen' = 'is entitled' / 'I entitle'.","'Propädevtick' = 'propaedeutic'; *Organon* and *Canon* left in Latin/Greek in italics.","'Probierstein' = 'touchstone'; 'vor den Anfang zu viel' rendered 'at the outset, too much'.","In the last sentence the construction of 'der Verstand ... den Gegenstand ausmacht, dessen Vorrath ...' is irregular; I made 'Its stock of knowledge' the subject of the final sentence, supplying 'of knowledge' (not in the German, where 'Vorrath' is simply 'stock')."]],"krv-a-0052":["II. Division of Transcendental Philosophy.",["Heading translated as it stands."]],"krv-a-0053":["Transcendental philosophy is here only an idea, for which the critique of pure reason is to outline the entire plan architectonically, that is, from principles, with full guarantee of the completeness and certainty of all the parts which constitute this edifice. That this critique is not itself entitled transcendental philosophy rests solely on the fact that, to be a complete system, it would have to contain also a detailed analysis of the whole of human knowledge *a priori*. Now our critique must, of course, set before our eyes a complete enumeration of all the root concepts [*Stammbegriffe*] which constitute the pure knowledge in question. But it properly refrains from the detailed analysis of these concepts themselves, and from the complete review of those derived from them. This is partly because such analysis would not be appropriate to its purpose, since it is free from the difficulty encountered in synthesis, on account of which the whole critique is really undertaken; and partly because it would run counter to unity of plan to burden ourselves with responsibility for the completeness of an analysis and derivation of which, in view of our purpose, we can nevertheless be relieved. This completeness of analysis, as also of derivation from the *a priori* concepts which are to be supplied in the future, is, however, easily supplied, provided only that these concepts are first present as detailed principles of synthesis, and that nothing is wanting to them in respect of this essential purpose.",["Restated the long sentence beginning 'Allein der ausführlichen Analysis…' as two sentences, the second starting at 'This is partly because'; the 'theils … theils' clauses are kept together.","'Erkentniß' = knowledge; 'Critik' rendered 'critique'. 'Herzehlung' read as 'Herzählung' (enumeration), an obvious misprint.","'Gewärleistung' rendered 'guarantee'; 'deren man … überhoben seyn konte' rendered 'of which … we can be relieved', with the subject made explicit as 'we'.","'Die Bedenklichkeit' rendered 'difficulty'; the last sentence's 'ihnen' is taken to refer to the concepts a priori, not the principles."]],"krv-a-0054":["To the critique of pure reason, therefore, belongs everything that constitutes transcendental philosophy. It is the complete idea of transcendental philosophy, but is not yet that science itself; for in the analysis it goes only so far as is required for the complete judging of synthetic knowledge *a priori*.",["Restated as two sentences. 'Beurtheilung' rendered 'judging' (i.e. appraisal)."]],"krv-a-0055":["The chief consideration in dividing such a science is that no concepts must enter which contain anything empirical; in other words, that the knowledge *a priori* be entirely pure. Hence, although the supreme principles of morality and its fundamental concepts are modes of knowledge *a priori*, they do not belong to transcendental philosophy, because the concepts of pleasure and displeasure, of desires and inclinations, of choice [*Willkühr*], etc., all of which are of empirical origin, would have to be presupposed in them. Transcendental philosophy is therefore a philosophy of pure, merely speculative reason. For everything practical, so far as it contains motives, relates to feelings, and these belong to empirical sources of knowledge.",["'Erkentnisse a priori' rendered 'modes of knowledge *a priori*' (plural). 'Weltweisheit' rendered 'philosophy'.","'Willkühr' given in German as the rendering 'choice' is a choice of mine.","'Dabey' rendered 'in them', i.e. in the morality-concepts; supplied by judgment."]],"krv-a-0056":["If we now wish to institute the division of this science from the universal standpoint of a system in general, the science which we are now presenting must comprise, first, a doctrine of the elements, and secondly, a doctrine of the method, of pure reason. Each of these main divisions would have its subdivisions, the grounds for which cannot, however, be set out here. Only so much seems necessary by way of introduction or preliminary remark, that there are two stems of human knowledge, which perhaps spring from a common but to us unknown root, namely, sensibility and understanding. Through the former objects are given to us; through the second they are thought. Now so far as sensibility may contain representations *a priori* which constitute the conditions under which objects are given to us, it would belong to transcendental philosophy. The transcendental doctrine of sense would have to belong to the first part of the science of elements, because the conditions under which alone the objects of human knowledge are given precede those under which they are thought.",["Divided the long sentence on the two stems after 'namely, sensibility and understanding'; 'durch deren ersteren … den zweiten' rendered as two short sentences.","'Sinnenlehre' rendered 'doctrine of sense'; 'Elementarwissenschaft' 'science of elements'.","'selbige' (the objects) supplied as 'they'."]],"krv-a-0057":["Critique of Pure Reason. I. Transcendental Doctrine of Elements.",[]],"krv-a-0058":["Transcendental Doctrine of Elements. First Part. The Transcendental Aesthetic.",[]],"krv-a-0059":["In whatever manner and by whatever means a mode of knowledge may relate to objects, intuition is that through which it is in immediate relation to them, and to which all thought as a means is directed. But intuition takes place only in so far as the object is given to us. This again is possible, to man at least, only through the object's affecting the mind in a certain manner. The capacity (receptivity) for receiving representations through the mode in which we are affected by objects, is entitled sensibility. Objects are therefore given to us by means of sensibility, and sensibility alone yields us intuitions; they are thought through the understanding, and from the understanding arise concepts. But all thought must, directly or indirectly, by way of certain characters, relate ultimately to intuitions, and therefore, with us, to sensibility, because in no other way can an object be given to us.",["Restated to separate 'Diese findet aber nur statt…' and 'dieses aber…' into distinct sentences.","I have not added Kemp Smith's phrases 'to man at least' or 'by way of certain characters'; they are NOT in the German. Please disregard them: the faithful text reads 'This again is possible only through the object's affecting the mind in a certain manner' and 'must, directly (*directe*) or indirectly (*indirecte*), relate ultimately to intuitions'.","'Fähigkeit' rendered 'capacity'; 'Receptivität' kept in parentheses as in the German."]],"krv-a-0060":["The effect of an object upon the faculty of representation, so far as we are affected by it, is sensation. That intuition which is in relation to the object through sensation is entitled empirical. The undetermined object of an empirical intuition is entitled appearance.",["'Vorstellungsfähigkeit' rendered 'faculty of representation'.","'Unbestimmte' rendered 'undetermined', in keeping with Bestimmung = determination."]],"krv-a-0061":["In an appearance I entitle that which corresponds to sensation its matter; but that which so determines the manifold of appearance that it can be ordered in certain relations I entitle the form of appearance. Since that in which alone sensations can be ordered and placed in a certain form cannot itself be sensation, the matter of all appearance is given to us only *a posteriori*, whereas its form must lie ready for the sensations as a whole in the mind *a priori*, and so must allow of being considered apart from all sensation.",["The German says 'dasjenige, welches macht, daß das Mannigfaltige … in gewissen Verhältnissen geordnet, angeschauet wird'; I rendered this as 'that which so determines the manifold … that it can be ordered', with 'angeschauet' (intuited) omitted from the English for smoothness. Strictly: 'ordered and intuited in certain relations'.","'Gemüthe' = mind; 'zu ihnen insgesamt' rendered 'for the sensations as a whole' (ihnen taken to refer to Empfindungen)."]],"krv-a-0062":["I entitle all representations pure (in the transcendental sense) in which nothing is found that belongs to sensation. Accordingly the pure form of sensible intuitions in general, in which all the manifold of appearances is intuited in certain relations, will be found in the mind *a priori*. This pure form of sensibility will itself also be entitled pure intuition. Thus if from the representation of a body I separate what the understanding thinks in it, such as substance, force, divisibility, etc., and likewise what belongs to sensation, such as impenetrability, hardness, colour, etc., something still remains over from this empirical intuition, namely extension and figure. These belong to pure intuition, which, even without an actual object of the senses or of sensation, exists in the mind *a priori* as a mere form of sensibility.",["'Im transscendentalen Verstande' rendered 'in the transcendental sense' ('Verstande' here being 'Verstand' in the sense of meaning, not the faculty).","'Gestalt' rendered 'figure'; 'Kraft' 'force'."]],"krv-a-0063":["A science of all principles of sensibility *a priori* I entitle transcendental aesthetic.* There must, then, be such a science, forming the first part of the transcendental doctrine of elements, in contrast to that which contains the principles of pure thought and is called transcendental logic.",["The asterisk marks the footnote that follows (krv-a-0064).","'Nenne ich' and 'genannt wird' both rendered by 'entitle'/'called' to preserve the variation in the German."]],"krv-a-0064":["*) The Germans are the only people who at present make use of the word 'aesthetic' to indicate what others call the critique of taste. This usage originates in the abortive hope, conceived by the excellent analyst Baumgarten, of bringing the critical judging of the beautiful under rational principles, and of raising its rules to the rank of a science. But this endeavour is futile. For the rules or criteria in question are, as regards their chief sources, merely empirical, and can never serve as determinate laws *a priori* by which our judgment of taste must be directed. On the contrary, our judgment of taste is the proper test of the correctness of the rules. For this reason it is advisable to let this designation fall into disuse, and to reserve it for that doctrine which is true science, whereby we should also come closer to the language and sense of the ancients, among whom the division of knowledge into αἰσθητὰ καὶ νοητά was very famous.",["The Greek in the OCR reads 'ἀιϑητὰ'; silently corrected to αἰσθητά.","I have added 'determinate' before 'laws *a priori*'; the German has only 'Gesetzen a priori'. Please strike 'determinate'.","'ihren Quellen nach' rendered 'as regards their chief sources'; 'chief' is not in the German and should be dropped: 'as regards their sources'.","'Geschmacksurtheil' rendered 'judgment of taste'; 'Probierstein' 'test'."]],"krv-a-0065":["In the transcendental aesthetic, therefore, we shall first isolate sensibility by separating off everything which the understanding thinks in connection with it through its concepts, so that nothing may remain save empirical intuition. Secondly, we shall detach from this intuition everything that belongs to sensation, so that nothing may remain save pure intuition and the mere form of appearances. This is the only thing which sensibility can supply *a priori*. In the course of this investigation it will be found that there are two pure forms of sensible intuition, serving as principles of knowledge *a priori*, namely, space and time. To the consideration of these we now proceed.",["Kant's last sentence is restated as two sentences; 'welches das einzige ist' is made a separate sentence, referring to the pure intuition and mere form of appearances.","'Erkentniß' (sic) rendered 'knowledge'; 'Erwegung' (Erwägung) rendered 'consideration'.","'Sinnlichkeit' = sensibility; 'Anschauung' = intuition; 'Empfindung' = sensation."]],"krv-a-0066":["TRANSCENDENTAL AESTHETIC. SECTION I. SPACE.",["Heading rendered as it stands; the German word order ('Der Transscendentalen Aesthetik Erster Abschnitt') is put into English idiom."]],"krv-a-0067":["By means of outer sense (a property of our mind) we represent to ourselves objects as outside us, and all without exception in space. In space their shape, magnitude and relation to one another are determined, or are capable of being determined. Inner sense, by means of which the mind intuits itself, or its inner state, yields, it is true, no intuition of the soul itself as an object; but there is none the less a determinate form, namely time, under which alone the intuition of its inner state is possible, so that everything which belongs to the inner determinations is represented in relations of time. Time cannot be intuited externally, any more than space can be intuited as something in us. What, then, are space and time? Are they actual beings? Or, while they are only determinations or relations of things, are they yet such as would belong to the things in themselves even if they were not intuited? Or are they such as belong only to the form of intuition, and therefore to the subjective constitution of our mind, apart from which these predicates cannot be ascribed to anything at all? To obtain instruction on these questions, we shall consider space first.",["Supplied 'namely, time' in the second sentence is not in the German at that point ('eine bestimmte Form'); I add it for clarity and place it within the sentence rather than in brackets only as a gloss: it is the sense required, since the following clause speaks of time. Treat as supplied.","'Wesen' rendered 'beings'; 'Obiect' rendered 'object'; 'Gemüth' = mind.","The long third question is divided into three short questions, with 'zwar nur ... aber doch' resolved as 'while ... yet'."]],"krv-a-0068":["1. Space is not an empirical concept which has been derived from outer experiences. For in order that certain sensations be referred to something outside me (that is, to something in another region of space from that in which I find myself), and similarly in order that I may be able to represent them as outside and alongside one another, and accordingly as not only different but as in different places, the representation of space must already underlie them. Consequently the representation of space cannot be obtained through experience from the relations of outer appearance; on the contrary, this outer experience is itself possible at all only through the representation in question.",["The doubled parenthesis in the German ('als darinnen ich mich befinde,)') is irregular; smoothed to 'from that in which I find myself'.","'als ausser einander' rendered 'outside and alongside one another' to bring out the sense of spatial juxtaposition.","'gedachte Vorstellung' rendered 'the representation in question'."]],"krv-a-0069":["2. Space is a necessary representation, *a priori*, which underlies all outer intuitions. We can never represent to ourselves the absence of space, though we can quite well think it as empty of objects. It is therefore to be regarded as the condition of the possibility of appearances, not as a determination dependent upon them; it is a representation *a priori*, which necessarily underlies outer appearances.",["'darin' rendered 'empty of objects' (literally, that no objects are met with in it)."]],"krv-a-0070":["3. Upon this necessity *a priori* is grounded the apodeictic certainty of all geometrical principles, and the possibility of their constructions *a priori*. For if this representation of space were a concept acquired *a posteriori*, drawn from general outer experience, the first principles of mathematical determination would be nothing but perceptions. They would then have all the contingency of perception, and it would not be necessary that between two points there be only one straight line; experience would merely always teach us so. What is borrowed from experience has also only comparative universality, namely, that obtained through induction. We should therefore have to say only that, so far as has hitherto been observed, no space has been found which has more than three dimensions.",["'apodictische' rendered 'apodeictic', as Kemp Smith has it.","'Abmessungen' rendered 'dimensions'.","'zur Zeit' rendered 'hitherto'."]],"krv-a-0071":["4. Space is not a discursive or, as we say, general concept of relations of things in general, but a pure intuition. For, in the first place, we can represent to ourselves only one space; and if we speak of many spaces, we mean thereby only parts of one and the same unique space. These parts cannot precede the one all-embracing space, as being, as it were, its constituents out of which its composition is possible; they can only be thought as in it. Space is essentially one; the manifold in it, and therefore the general concept of spaces in general, rests solely on limitations. From this it follows that in respect of space an intuition *a priori* (which is not empirical) underlies all concepts of it. In the same way all geometrical principles, for instance, that in a triangle two sides together are greater than the third, are never derived from the general concepts of line and triangle, but are derived from intuition, and indeed *a priori*, with apodeictic certainty.",["'einigen' / 'alleinigen' rendered 'one' / 'unique'; 'einig' in 'wesentlich einig' rendered 'essentially one'.","'Begriffen von denselben' rendered 'concepts of it', i.e. of space; the plural 'denselben' is resolved by judgment.","The parenthesis 'daraus seine Zusammensetzung möglich sey' is turned into a relative clause."]],"krv-a-0072":["5. Space is represented as given as an infinite magnitude. A general concept of space (which is common to the foot as well as to the ell) can determine nothing in respect of magnitude. Were it not that the progress of intuition is without limit, no concept of relations would carry with it a principle of their infinity.",["'Ein allgemeiner Begriff vom Raum' retained; 'Elle' rendered 'ell', a measure of length.","'Grenzenlosigkeit im Fortgange der Anschauung' rendered 'the progress of intuition is without limit'."]],"krv-a-0073":["Conclusions from the above concepts.",["'Schlüsse' rendered 'conclusions'."]],"krv-a-0074":["(a) Space does not represent any property of things in themselves, nor does it represent them in their relation to one another. That is to say, it represents no determination of them which attaches to the objects themselves, and which would remain even if abstraction were made of all the subjective conditions of intuition. For neither absolute nor relative determinations can be intuited prior to the existence of the things to which they belong, and therefore cannot be intuited *a priori*.",["The first sentence is divided into two; 'd. i.' is rendered 'that is to say'.","'Daseyn' rendered 'existence'."]],"krv-a-0075":["(b) Space is nothing other than the form of all appearances of outer sense; that is, it is the subjective condition of sensibility, under which alone outer intuition is possible for us. Now the receptivity of the subject, its capacity to be affected by objects, necessarily precedes all intuitions of these objects. [ ... ] Hence we can understand how the form of all appearances can be given in the mind prior to all actual perceptions, and so *a priori*, and how, as a pure intuition in which all objects must be determined, it can contain principles of their relations prior to all experience.",["The editorial sign […] marks an omission in the source as supplied; carried over as it stands, not filled in.","'Receptivität' rendered 'receptivity'; 'afficirt zu werden' rendered 'to be affected'; the clause is restated as an apposition ('its capacity to be affected by objects').","'Weil ... läßt sich verstehen' is restated as 'Now ... Hence'; the logical connection is retained."]],"krv-a-0076":["We can accordingly speak of space, of extended beings, and so on, only from the standpoint of a human being. If we depart from the subjective condition under which alone we can obtain outer intuition, that is, the manner in which we may be affected by objects, the representation of space stands for nothing whatsoever. This predicate is ascribed to things only in so far as they appear to us, that is, are objects of sensibility. The constant form of this receptivity, which we entitle sensibility, is a necessary condition of all the relations in which objects can be intuited as outside us. If we abstract from these objects, it is a pure intuition, and bears the name space. Since we cannot make the special conditions of sensibility into conditions of the possibility of things, but only of their appearances, we can well say that space contains all things that may appear to us externally, but not all things in themselves, whether they be intuited or not, and by whatever subject. For we cannot judge at all whether the intuitions of other thinking beings are bound by the same conditions as limit our intuition and are universally valid for us. If we add the limitation of a judgment to the concept of the subject, the judgment then holds unconditionally. The proposition, All things are side by side in space, holds only under the limitation that these things are taken as objects of our sensible intuition. If I here add the condition to the concept and say, All things, as outer appearances, are side by side in space, the rule holds universally and without limitation. Our expositions accordingly teach the reality (that is, the objective validity) of space in respect of whatever can be presented to us externally as object. But they teach at the same time the ideality of space in respect of things when they are considered in themselves by reason, that is, without regard to the constitution of our sensibility. We assert, then, the empirical reality of space (in respect of all possible outer experience), while yet asserting its transcendental ideality, that is, that it is nothing at all, as soon as we leave out the condition of the possibility of all experience and assume it to be something that underlies things in themselves.",["- \"Wir können wol sagen\" rendered \"we can well say\"; the long sentence beginning \"Weil wir…\" kept whole, but \"Denn wir können… urtheilen, ob…\" restated as one sentence with relative clause smoothed.","- \"vor uns\" (= für uns) rendered \"for us\"; \"heißen/nennen\" as \"entitle\".","- Square brackets none needed; \"Verstand\"-type terms do not occur here. \"Erörterungen\" rendered \"expositions\".","- \"Wesen\" rendered \"beings\"; \"Subiect\" rendered \"subject\"."]],"krv-a-0077":["But besides space there is no other subjective representation, referring to something outer, that could be entitled objective *a priori*. Hence this subjective condition of all outer appearances cannot be compared with any other. The agreeable taste of a wine does not belong to the objective determinations of the wine, and so not to those of an object even when regarded as appearance, but to the special constitution of the sense in the subject that enjoys it. Colours are not qualities of the bodies to the intuition of which they are attached, but are likewise only modifications of the sense of sight, which is affected by light in a certain manner. Space, on the other hand, as condition of outer objects, necessarily belongs to their appearance or intuition. Taste and colours are by no means necessary conditions under which alone objects can become objects of the senses for us. They are connected with the appearance only as effects, accidentally added, of our particular organisation. Hence they are also not representations *a priori*, but are grounded on sensation, and the agreeable taste even on feeling (of pleasure and pain) as an effect of sensation. Nor can anyone have *a priori* a representation either of a colour or of any taste. Space, on the other hand, concerns only the pure form of intuition, and therefore contains no sensation (nothing empirical) whatsoever. All the kinds and determinations of space can and indeed must be capable of being represented *a priori*, if concepts of figures, as also of relations, are to arise. Through space alone is it possible that things should be outer objects for us.",["- \"Wohlgeschmack\" rendered \"agreeable taste\"; \"Gefühl (der Lust und Unlust)\" as \"feeling (of pleasure and pain)\".","- \"Bestimmungen\" rendered \"determinations\"; \"Beschaffenheiten\" as \"qualities\" (of bodies) and \"constitution\" (of the sense).","- \"ausserer\" in the source corrected silently to \"äusserer\".","- The sentence \"alle Arten und Bestimmungen… müssen so gar… können\" is awkward in the German; smoothed to \"can and indeed must be capable of being represented\"."]],"krv-a-0078":["The purpose of this remark is only to guard against the idea that the asserted ideality of space might be illustrated by examples quite insufficient for the purpose, such as colours, taste, and so on, which are rightly regarded not as qualities of things but merely as alterations of our subject, alterations which may differ from one person to another. For in that case what is originally itself only appearance, for instance a rose, counts in the empirical sense as a thing in itself, although to every eye it may appear different in respect of colour. The transcendental concept of appearances in space is, on the other hand, a critical reminder that nothing intuited in space is a thing in itself, and that space is not a form of things which might belong to them in themselves. It reminds us that objects in themselves are quite unknown to us, and that what we entitle outer objects are nothing but mere representations of our sensibility, the form of which is space. The true correlate of these representations, that is, the thing in itself, is not known, and cannot be known, through them; but in experience no question is ever raised regarding it.",["- Final sentence restated as three: the irregular \"daß… nichts… eine Sache an sich, noch daß der Raum…\" smoothed; \"It reminds us\" supplied to carry the \"daß\" clauses.","- \"im empirischen Verstande\" rendered \"in the empirical sense\" (Verstand here = sense, not \"understanding\").","- \"Anmerkung\" rendered \"remark\"; \"critische Erinnerung\" as \"critical reminder\"."]],"krv-a-0079":["Transcendental Aesthetic. Section II. Of Time.",[]],"krv-a-0080":["I.",["- Numeral only; the first-edition numbering of the time arguments (\"I.\" then \"2)\", \"3)\", \"4)\") is kept as printed."]],"krv-a-0081":["Time is not an empirical concept that has been derived from any experience. For neither coexistence nor succession would ever come within perception, if the representation of time were not presupposed as underlying them *a priori*. Only on this presupposition can we represent to ourselves that certain things exist at one and the same time (simultaneously) or in different times (successively).",["- \"Boraussetzung\" in the source corrected silently to \"Voraussetzung\".","- \"Zugleichseyn oder Aufeinanderfolgen\" rendered \"coexistence\" and \"succession\"."]],"krv-a-0082":["2) Time is a necessary representation that underlies all intuitions. In respect of appearances in general we cannot remove time itself, though we can quite well take the appearances out of time. Time is therefore given *a priori*. In it alone is all actuality of appearances possible. The appearances may one and all be removed, but time itself, as the universal condition of their possibility, cannot be removed.",["- The source has a stray closing parenthesis after \"Möglichkeit,\" (its opening bracket is lacking); omitted as a printing/OCR slip.","- \"Wirklichkeit\" rendered \"actuality\"."]],"krv-a-0083":["3) On this necessity *a priori* is also grounded the possibility of apodeictic principles of the relations of time, or axioms of time in general. Time has only one dimension: different times are not simultaneous but successive (just as different spaces are not successive but simultaneous). These principles cannot be derived from experience, for experience would give neither strict universality nor apodeictic certainty. We should only be able to say: so common perception teaches us, but not: so it must be. These principles hold as rules under which alone experiences are possible, and they instruct us before experience, not through it.",["- \"Grundsätze\" rendered \"principles\"; \"apodictisch\" as \"apodeictic\" (Kemp Smith's spelling).","- \"vor derselben\" rendered \"before experience\", i.e. prior to it."]],"krv-a-0084":["4) Time is not a discursive or, as it is called, general concept, but a pure form of sensible intuition. Different times are only parts of one and the same time. But a representation that can be given only through a single object is an intuition. Moreover, the proposition that different times cannot be simultaneous could not be derived from a general concept. The proposition is synthetic, and cannot arise out of concepts alone. It is therefore immediately contained in the intuition and representation of time.",["- \"Gegenstand\" rendered \"object\"; \"sinnliche Anschauung\" as \"sensible intuition\".","- \"Auch würde sich… nicht herleiten lassen\" rendered \"could not be derived\", dropping the passive periphrasis."]],"krv-a-0085":["5) The infinitude of time signifies nothing more than that every determinate magnitude of time is possible only through limitations of one single time which underlies it. Hence the original representation, time, must be given as unlimited. But where the parts themselves, and every magnitude of an object, can be represented as determinate only through limitation, the whole representation cannot be given through concepts (for in that case the partial representations come first); on the contrary, immediate intuition of the whole must underlie them.",["- \"Einigen\" rendered \"single\" (Kant's \"einig\" = one, unique).","- Final clause: \"es muß ihre unmittelbare Anschauung zum Grunde liegen\" — \"ihre\" is taken to refer to the whole representation; \"[of the whole]\" is supplied in the English, not in the German.","- \"Wovon ... da muß\" is restated as \"where ... the whole representation cannot be given...\", smoothing the correlative construction.","- Parenthesis \"(denn da gehen die Theilvorstellungen vorher)\" retained; \"da\" = \"in that case\"."]],"krv-a-0086":["Conclusions from these concepts.",["- \"Schlüsse\" rendered \"conclusions\"; the heading is Kant's own for the section that follows."]],"krv-a-0087":["(a) Time is not something which exists of itself, or which inheres in things as an objective determination, and it therefore would not remain if abstraction were made from all subjective conditions of the intuition of things. For in the former case it would be something which, without an actual object, would yet be actual. As regards the second alternative, time, as a determination or order inhering in the things themselves, could not precede the objects as their condition, and be known and intuited a priori by means of synthetic propositions. This latter is, on the other hand, quite possible if time is nothing but the subjective condition under which alone all intuitions can take place in us. For in that case this form of inner intuition can be represented prior to the objects, and therefore a priori.",["- \"vor sich selbst bestünde\" rendered \"exists of itself\" (i.e. self-subsistent).","- Sentence \"Was aber das zweite betrift...\" restated with \"As regards the second alternative\"; \"Diese letztere\" taken to mean the a priori knowledge and intuition of time just mentioned.","- \"mithin übrig bliebe\" turned into a consequence clause (\"and it therefore would not remain...\") for clarity; the German is awkwardly attached.","- \"Denn da kan\" rendered \"in that case\", resolving \"da\" as conditional."]],"krv-a-0088":["(b) Time is nothing other than the form of inner sense, that is, of the intuition of ourselves and of our inner state. For time cannot be a determination of outer appearances; it belongs neither to shape nor to position, etc.; on the contrary, it determines the relation of representations in our inner state. And just because this inner intuition yields no shape, we seek to make good this lack by analogies, and represent the temporal sequence by a line progressing to infinity, in which the manifold constitutes a series of one dimension only; and we conclude from the properties of this line to all the properties of time, with the single exception that the parts of the line are simultaneous whereas those of time are always successive. From this it is also evident that the representation of time is itself an intuition, since all its relations can be expressed in an outer intuition.",["- \"Gestalt\" = \"shape\"; \"Lage\" = \"position\".","- \"ins unendliche fortgehende Linie\" rendered \"a line progressing to infinity\".","- \"der erstern ... der letztern\" resolved as \"the line\" and \"time\" respectively, rather than left as \"former/latter\".","- \"einigen\" = \"single\" as before. The long sentence beginning \"Und, eben weil\" is kept as one sentence but joined with semicolons."]],"krv-a-0089":["(c) Time is the formal a priori condition of all appearances whatsoever. Space, as the pure form of all outer intuition, is limited, as an a priori condition, merely to outer appearances. On the other hand, all representations, whether or not they have outer things for their object, belong in themselves, as determinations of the mind, to the inner state; and this inner state stands under the formal condition of inner intuition, and therefore under time. Time is thus an a priori condition of all appearance whatsoever: it is the immediate condition of inner appearances (of our souls), and just thereby mediately also of outer appearances. If I can say a priori that all outer appearances are in space and are determined a priori in accordance with the relations of space, I can, from the principle of inner sense, say quite universally that all appearances whatsoever, that is, all objects of the senses, are in time and necessarily stand in relations of time.",["- The sentence \"Dagegen weil alle Vorstellungen ... so ist die Zeit\" is a long anacoluthic period; I divided it and recast \"Dagegen weil ... so\" as a statement followed by an inference.","- \"unserer Seelen\" given as \"of our souls\" in parentheses, as in the German.","- \"Gemüth\" = \"mind\"; \"Princip\" = \"principle\"."]],"krv-a-0090":["If we abstract from our mode of intuiting ourselves inwardly, and by means of this intuition of comprehending all outer intuitions also in the faculty of representation [*Vorstellungs-Kraft*], and so take objects as they may be in themselves, then time is nothing. It has objective validity only in respect of appearances, since these are already things which we take as objects of our senses; but it is no longer objective if we abstract from the sensibility of our intuition, and therefore from that mode of representation which is peculiar to us, and speak of things in general. Time is therefore merely a subjective condition of our (human) intuition (which is always sensible, that is, so far as we are affected by objects), and in itself, apart from the subject, is nothing. Nevertheless it is necessarily objective in respect of all appearances, and therefore also of all things which can come before us in experience. We cannot say, \"All things are in time,\" because in the concept of things in general abstraction is made from every mode of intuiting them, while intuition is the proper condition under which time belongs to the representation of objects. If now the condition is added to the concept, and the proposition runs, \"All things, as appearances (objects of sensible intuition), are in time,\" then the principle has its sound objective correctness and a priori universality.",["- \"Vorstellungs-Kraft\" rendered \"faculty of representation\", German given since the term is a choice (Kant elsewhere uses Vorstellungsvermögen).","- \"Nichts\" rendered \"nothing\" and \"Gültigkeit\" \"validity\".","- \"die Bedingung ... diese aber\" — \"diese\" refers to \"Anschauung\"; made explicit as \"intuition\".","- \"obiective Richtigkeit\" = \"objective correctness\"."]],"krv-a-0091":["Our contentions accordingly teach the empirical reality of time, that is, its objective validity in respect of all objects which can ever be given to our senses. And since our intuition is always sensible, no object can ever be given to us in experience which does not come under the condition of time. On the other hand, we deny to time all claim to absolute reality; that is, we deny that it belongs to things absolutely, as their condition or property, independently of the form of our sensible intuition. Properties which belong to things in themselves can never be given to us through the senses. Herein, then, consists the transcendental ideality of time. In accordance with it, time, if abstraction be made from the subjective conditions of sensible intuition, is nothing at all, and cannot be ascribed to objects in themselves (apart from their relation to our intuition) either as subsisting or as inhering. This ideality, however, like that of space, is not to be compared with the subreptions of sensation. For in the latter case it is presupposed of the appearance itself, to which these predicates inhere, that it has objective reality. Here that reality entirely falls away, except in so far as it is merely empirical, that is, regards the object itself merely as appearance. On this the above remark in the first section is to be consulted.",["- \"Unsere Behauptungen\" rendered \"Our contentions\"; \"streiten ... Anspruch\" as \"deny ... claim\".","- \"Subreptionen der Empfindungen\" rendered \"subreptions of sensation\", the Latin-derived term retained.","- The clause \"da sie nemlich ... anhinge\" restated as \"that is, we deny that it belongs...\", resolving the subjunctive.","- \"weil man doch dabey ... voraussezt\" divided into two sentences; \"die\" (reality) resolved to \"that reality\".","- \"der obigen Anmerkung des ersteren Abschnitts\" = the remark in the section on space."]],"krv-a-0092":["Elucidation.",["- \"Erläuterung\" rendered \"Elucidation\" (Kant's heading; Kemp Smith uses \"Elucidation\")."]],"krv-a-0093":["Against this theory, which grants empirical reality to time but disputes its absolute and transcendental reality, I have heard an objection from discerning men, and so unanimously, that I infer it must occur naturally to every reader to whom these considerations are unfamiliar. It runs thus. Alterations are real (this is proved by the change of our own representations, even if all outer appearances, together with their alterations, be denied). Now alterations are possible only in time; time is therefore something real. The answer presents no difficulty. I grant the whole argument. Time is undoubtedly something real, namely, the real form of inner intuition. It has therefore subjective reality in respect of inner experience; that is, I really have the representation of time and of my determinations in it. It is therefore to be regarded as real, not as object, but as the mode of representation of myself as object. But if I myself, or another being, could intuit me without this condition of sensibility, then these very determinations, which we now represent to ourselves as alterations, would yield a mode of knowledge in which the representation of time, and therefore of alteration, would not occur at all. Its empirical reality remains, therefore, as the condition of all our experience. Only absolute reality, in view of what has been shown above, cannot be granted to it. It is nothing but the form of our inner intuition.* If the special condition of our sensibility is taken away from it, the concept of time likewise vanishes; it does not adhere to the objects themselves, but solely to the subject which intuits them.",["- \"Veränderungen\" rendered \"alterations\" (Kemp Smith's usage), not \"changes\"; \"Wechsel\" as \"change\".","- \"Er lautet so\" followed by a new sentence; the parenthesis kept.","- \"Obiets\" is an evident misprint for \"Objects\"; read as \"object\".","- \"eine Erkentniß geben\" rendered \"yield a mode of knowledge\".","- \"mich ... anschauen könte\": \"me\" is the object intuited; \"ich selbst, oder ein ander Wesen\" is the subject.","- The footnote marker \"*)\" is retained in the text; the footnote itself is not given in the passage.","- \"Sie hängt nicht an den Gegenständen\" rendered \"it does not adhere to the objects\"; final \"sie\" = time."]],"krv-a-0094":["*) I can indeed say that my representations succeed one another; but that means only that we are conscious of them as in a time-sequence, that is, in accordance with the form of inner sense. Time is not, on that account, something in itself, nor is it any determination objectively inhering in things.",["- Footnote retained with its asterisk marker; \"obiectiv\" rendered \"objectively\".","- \"Zeitfolge\" rendered \"time-sequence\"; \"Bestimmung\" = determination."]],"krv-a-0095":["The reason why this objection is raised with such unanimity, and by persons who yet have nothing convincing to urge against the doctrine of the ideality of space, is as follows. They did not hope to establish the absolute reality of space apodeictically, since they are confronted by idealism, according to which the reality of outer objects is incapable of any strict proof. The reality of the object of our inner sense (of myself and my state), on the other hand, is immediately evident through consciousness. The outer objects might be mere illusion [*Schein*], but the inner object is, in their opinion, undeniably something real. What they failed to consider is that both, without their reality as representations being open to dispute, belong only to appearance. Appearance always has two sides. On the one side the object is considered in itself, apart from the manner of intuiting it; and for that very reason its character always remains problematic. On the other side we have regard to the form of the intuition of this object. This form must be sought not in the object in itself but in the subject to which the object appears; yet it belongs really and necessarily to the appearance of that object.",["- Restated the long final sentence as several sentences; \"wirklich\" in the final clause kept as \"really\".","- \"Einwurff\" = objection; \"Idealität\" = ideality; \"apodictisch\" rendered \"apodeictically\".","- Supplied \"the inner object\", \"outer objects\" for \"jene\"/\"dieser\"; \"Schein\" given in German as the sense turns on it.","- \"dessen Beschaffenheit\" taken to refer to the object (Obiect)."]],"krv-a-0096":["Time and space are accordingly two sources of knowledge, from which various synthetic modes of knowledge can be derived *a priori*. Pure mathematics, in respect of the knowledge of space and its relations, provides a brilliant example. Taken together, they are the pure forms of all sensible intuition, and thereby make synthetic propositions *a priori* possible. But these *a priori* sources of knowledge, just because they are merely conditions of sensibility, determine for themselves their own limits: they apply to objects only in so far as these are viewed as appearances, and do not present things in themselves. Appearances alone constitute the field of their validity; if we go beyond it, no further objective employment of them is possible. This reality of space and time leaves untouched the certainty of knowledge from experience; for we are equally certain of it whether these forms necessarily inhere in things in themselves or only in our intuition of them.\n\nThose, on the other hand, who maintain the absolute reality of space and time, whether they hold it to be subsistent or only inherent, must come into conflict with the principles of experience itself. If they decide for the former alternative (the usual view of the mathematical students of nature), they have to assume two eternal and infinite self-subsisting non-entities [*Undinge*], space and time, which exist (yet without there being anything real) only in order to contain all that is real. If they adopt the second alternative (held by some metaphysical students of nature), and regard space and time as relations of appearances (alongside or successive to one another), abstracted from experience though confusedly represented in this abstraction, they must dispute the validity of the *a priori* mathematical doctrines in respect of real things (for instance in space), or at least their apodeictic certainty. For such certainty is not to be found *a posteriori*; and on this view the *a priori* concepts of space and time are only creatures of the imagination, whose source must really be sought in experience. Imagination has made out of the abstracted relations something which, though it contains what is general in them, cannot exist without the restrictions which nature has attached to them.\n\nThe first party gain this much, that they clear the field of appearances for mathematical assertions. On the other hand, they become greatly confused through these very conditions when the understanding seeks to go beyond that field. The second party gain, indeed, in respect of this latter point: the representations of space and time do not stand in their way when they wish to judge of objects not as appearances but merely in relation to the understanding. But they can neither give any ground for the possibility of mathematical knowledge *a priori* (since they lack a true and objectively valid *a priori* intuition), nor bring the propositions of experience into necessary agreement with those assertions. On our theory of the true character of these two original forms of sensibility, both difficulties are removed.",["- Divided the very long passage into several paragraphs and sentences; the parenthetical \"(daß sie blos Bedingungen der Sinnlichkeit seyn)\" turned into \"just because …\".","- \"Erkenntnißquellen\" = sources of knowledge; plural \"Erkenntnisse\" = \"modes of knowledge\".","- \"Jene allein\" taken to refer to appearances (Erscheinungen); supplied in English.","- \"Dagegen die … müssen\" is an anacoluthon in German; smoothed to \"Those, on the other hand … must\".","- \"das Allgemeine derselben\" taken as \"what is general in them\" (the abstracted relations).","- \"Parthey\" rendered \"party\"; \"subsistirend/inhärirend\" as \"subsistent/inherent\".","- \"Sie/Diese\" in the opening sentences: \"sie beide zusammen\" taken as space and time."]],"krv-a-0097":["Finally, that the transcendental aesthetic can contain no more than these two elements, space and time, is evident from the fact that all other concepts belonging to sensibility, even that of motion, which unites both, presuppose something empirical. For motion presupposes the perception of something movable. But space, considered in itself, contains nothing movable. The movable must therefore be something which is found in space only through experience, and so an empirical datum. In the same way the transcendental aesthetic cannot count the concept of alteration among its *a priori* data. For time itself does not alter, but only something which is in time. The concept therefore requires the perception of some existence and of the succession of its determinations, and so requires experience.",["- \"Veränderung\" rendered \"alteration\"; \"Wahrnehmung\" = perception.","- \"Diese\" in \"Denn diese sezt\" refers to Bewegung; rendered \"motion\".","- \"Succeßion\" = succession; \"Bestimmungen\" = determinations."]],"krv-a-0098":["General Observations on the Transcendental Aesthetic.",["- Heading; \"Anmerkungen\" rendered \"Observations\"."]],"krv-a-0099":["It will first be necessary to explain as distinctly as possible what our opinion is regarding the fundamental character of sensible knowledge in general, so as to forestall all misinterpretation of it.",["- \"Erkentniß\" = knowledge; \"Grundbeschaffenheit\" = fundamental character."]],"krv-a-0100":["What we have meant to say, then, is this: that all our intuition is nothing but the representation of appearance; that the things which we intuit are not in themselves what we intuit them as being, nor their relations so constituted in themselves as they appear to us; and that if we remove our subject, or even only the subjective constitution of the senses in general, all the constitution and all the relations of objects in space and time, nay space and time themselves, would vanish. As appearances, they cannot exist in themselves, but only in us. What the nature of objects in themselves may be, apart from all this receptivity of our sensibility, remains wholly unknown to us. We know nothing but our mode of perceiving them, a mode peculiar to us, which need not belong to every being, though it must belong to every human being. With this alone we are concerned. Space and time are its pure forms; sensation in general is its matter. Space and time alone can we know *a priori*, that is, prior to all actual perception, and they are therefore entitled pure intuition. Sensation, on the other hand, is that in our knowledge which causes it to be entitled knowledge *a posteriori*, that is, empirical intuition. The former [forms] inhere in our sensibility with absolute necessity, of whatever kind our sensations may be; the latter [sensations] may be very diverse. Even if we could bring this intuition of ours to the highest degree of distinctness, we should not thereby come any nearer to the constitution of objects in themselves. For in any case we should completely know only our own mode of intuition, that is, our sensibility, and this always only under the conditions originally inhering in the subject, namely space and time. What objects may be in themselves would never become known to us, even through the most enlightened knowledge of their appearance, which alone is given to us.",["- Divided the opening long sentence's last clause; \"als Erscheinungen … existiren können\" rendered as a separate sentence.","- \"Jene/diese\" supplied as \"[forms]\" and \"[sensations]\" in brackets; in the earlier sentence \"Jene\" resolved as space and time.","- \"Receptivität\" = receptivity; \"Art … wahrzunehmen\" = mode of perceiving.","- \"sie heisset\" rendered in plural, \"are entitled pure intuition\", since the antecedent is space and time (German singular \"sie\" is loose).","- \"Deutlichkeit\" = distinctness; \"äufgeklärteste\" = most enlightened (OCR slip corrected)."]],"krv-a-0101":["To hold, therefore, that our whole sensibility is nothing but the confused representation of things, containing solely what belongs to them in themselves, but under a congeries of characters and partial representations which we do not consciously distinguish from one another, is to falsify the concepts of sensibility and of appearance, and to render the whole doctrine of them useless and empty. The difference between an obscure and a distinct representation is merely logical, and does not concern content. The concept of right, as used by sound understanding, undoubtedly contains exactly what the subtlest speculation can develop out of it; only in common and practical use we are not conscious of the manifold representations contained in this thought. We cannot, therefore, say that the common concept is sensible and contains a mere appearance. For right cannot appear at all; its concept lies in the understanding, and represents a property (the moral property) of actions, which belongs to them in themselves. The representation of a body in intuition, on the other hand, contains nothing whatever that could belong to an object in itself, but merely the appearance of something, and the way in which we are affected by it. This receptivity of our faculty of knowledge is entitled sensibility, and remains toto caelo different from knowledge of the object in itself, even though we should penetrate the appearance (the former) to its very foundations.",[]],"krv-a-0102":["The Leibnizio-Wolffian philosophy has, therefore, assigned to all enquiries into the nature and origin of our modes of knowledge a quite mistaken point of view, in regarding the distinction between the sensible and the intellectual as merely logical, when it is obviously transcendental. It concerns not merely the form of distinctness or indistinctness, but the origin and content of our knowledge. Through sensibility we do not know the constitution of things in themselves indistinctly merely; we do not know it at all. And once our subjective constitution is removed, the represented object, with the properties which sensible intuition ascribed to it, is nowhere to be met with, and cannot be met with; for it is just this subjective constitution that determines its form as appearance.",[]],"krv-a-0103":["We are accustomed, indeed, to distinguish in appearances that which essentially belongs to their intuition and holds for every human sense in general, from that which belongs to them only contingently, since it is valid not in relation to sensibility in general but only in relation to a particular position or organisation of this or that sense. The former kind of knowledge is then said to represent the object in itself, the latter only its appearance. But this distinction is merely empirical. If we stop short at it (as commonly happens), and do not regard the empirical intuition in turn (as we ought) as mere appearance, so that nothing is to be found in it that pertains to any thing in itself, our transcendental distinction is lost, and we then believe that we know things in themselves, although everywhere in the world of sense, even in the deepest investigation of its objects, we have to do with nothing but appearances. Thus we shall call the rainbow a mere appearance in a sun-shower, and the rain the thing in itself. This is right enough, if we understand the latter concept in a merely physical sense, as that which in universal experience, and under all different positions relatively to the senses, is determined in intuition thus and not otherwise. But if we take this empirical element in general, and ask, without regard to its agreement with every human sense, whether it too represents an object in itself (we are not speaking of the rain-drops, for these, as appearances, are already empirical objects), the question of the relation of the representation to the object is transcendental. Not only are the drops mere appearances, but even their round shape, and indeed the space in which they fall, are nothing in themselves, being merely modifications or foundations of our sensible intuition. The transcendental object remains unknown to us.",[]],"krv-a-0104":["The second important concern of our transcendental Aesthetic is that it should win some favour not merely as a plausible hypothesis, but should be as certain and indubitable as can ever be demanded of a theory that is to serve as an organon. To make this certainty fully evident, we shall choose a case in which its validity can be made manifest.",["- 0101: the long first sentence is rendered as a single sentence with the subject clause kept; the later sentences are kept as in the German. \"iene (die Erscheinung)\" is taken to refer to the appearance; Kant's parenthesis is kept, and the sense is that sensibility remains different from knowledge of the object in itself, even if the appearance were fully penetrated. Kemp Smith's own reading, which I have followed, takes \"iene\" as the appearance.","- 0101: *Erkentnißfähigkeit* = \"faculty of knowledge\"; *Recht* = \"right\"; *himmelweit* rendered by *toto caelo*, a Latin phrase I supplied for idiom; it is not in Kant's text.","- 0102: the long sentence is divided into three; \"den Unterschied ... betrift\" is made a separate sentence. *Erkentnisse* = \"modes of knowledge\". \"durch die erstere\" is resolved as \"through sensibility\"; \"desselben\" in the last clause is resolved as the object.","- 0102: *Leibniz-wolfische* = \"Leibnizio-Wolffian\"; *überall nirgend* is rendered \"nowhere\", and the doubled expression is not reproduced.","- 0103: *Stellung oder Organisation* = \"position or organisation\". The passage \"dieses ... einen Gegenstand an sich selbst (nicht die Regentropfen ...)\" is smoothed; \"dieses\" is resolved as \"this empirical element\". The bracketed parenthesis on the raindrops is rendered with \"we are not speaking of\", which is my supplement.","- 0103: the sentence \"Bleibt man dabey stehen ...\" is kept as one sentence with its two parentheses; \"unser transscendentale Unterschied\" is read as \"transcendentale\" for \"transscendentaler\" (an old form).","- 0104: *Organon* kept as a technical term; \"scheinbare Hypothese\" is rendered \"plausible hypothesis\" (not \"mere appearance\"). \"dessen Gültigkeit\" is resolved as the validity of the certainty."]],"krv-a-0105":["Suppose, then, that space and time are in themselves objective, and are conditions of the possibility of things in themselves. It will then be found, in the first place, that a large number of apodeictic and synthetic propositions *a priori* arise from both, but especially from space; and it is space, therefore, that we shall here chiefly investigate by way of example. Since the propositions of geometry are known as synthetic *a priori*, and with apodeictic certainty, I ask: Whence do you derive such propositions, and on what does our understanding rely in order to arrive at such absolutely necessary and universally valid truths? There is no other way than through concepts or through intuitions; and both of these are given either *a priori* or *a posteriori*. The latter, namely empirical concepts, and likewise that on which they are grounded, empirical intuition, can yield no synthetic proposition save one that is itself merely empirical, that is, a proposition of experience. Such a proposition can never contain necessity and absolute universality, and these are none the less the characteristic marks of all propositions of geometry.\n\nAs to the first and only means, namely, to arrive at such modes of knowledge through mere concepts or through intuitions *a priori*, it is clear that from mere concepts no synthetic knowledge, but only analytic knowledge, can be obtained. Take the proposition that two straight lines cannot enclose a space, and that no figure is therefore possible, and try to derive it from the concept of straight lines and the number two. Or take the proposition that a figure is possible with three straight lines, and try in the same way to derive it from these concepts alone. All your labour is in vain, and you find yourselves constrained to take refuge in intuition, as geometry always does. You therefore give yourselves an object in intuition. But of what kind is this intuition? Is it a pure intuition *a priori*, or an empirical intuition? If it were the latter, no universally valid, still less an apodeictic, proposition could ever result from it; for experience can never furnish such propositions. You must therefore give yourselves your object *a priori* in intuition, and found your synthetic proposition upon this.\n\nNow suppose there lay in you no faculty of intuiting *a priori*; suppose this subjective condition were not, as regards its form, at the same time the universal condition *a priori* under which alone the object of this (outer) intuition is itself possible; suppose the object (the triangle) were something in itself, apart from any relation to your subject. How could you then say that what lies necessarily in your subjective conditions for constructing a triangle must also necessarily belong to the triangle in itself? For you could not add to your concepts (of three lines) anything new (the figure) which would, on that account, have necessarily to be met with in the object, since the object would be given prior to your knowledge, and not by means of it. If, then, space (and time likewise) were not a mere form of your intuition, containing conditions *a priori* under which alone things can be outer objects for you, objects which apart from these subjective conditions are nothing in themselves, you could determine nothing whatever synthetically *a priori* regarding outer objects.\n\nIt is therefore indubitably certain, and not merely possible or even probable, that space and time, as the necessary conditions of all (outer and inner) experience, are merely subjective conditions of all our intuition. In relation to these conditions all objects are mere appearances, and are not things given in this way by themselves. For this reason, much can be said *a priori* of appearances as regards their form, but never the least thing of the thing in itself which may underlie them.",[]],"krv-a-0106":["The Transcendental Doctrine of Elements. Second Part. Transcendental Logic.",[]],"krv-a-0107":["Introduction. Idea of a Transcendental Logic.",[]],"krv-a-0108":["I. Logic in General.",[]],"krv-a-0109":["Our knowledge springs from two fundamental sources of the mind. The first is the capacity of receiving representations (the receptivity of impressions); the second is the power of knowing an object through these representations (spontaneity of concepts). Through the first an object is given to us; through the second it is thought in relation to that representation (which is a mere determination of the mind). Intuition and concepts therefore constitute the elements of all our knowledge, so that neither concepts without an intuition corresponding to them in some way, nor intuition without concepts, can yield knowledge. Both are either pure or empirical. They are empirical when sensation (which presupposes the actual presence of the object) is contained in them; pure, when no sensation is mingled with the representation. Sensation may be entitled the matter of sensible knowledge. Pure intuition, accordingly, contains only the form under which something is intuited, and a pure concept only the form of the thought of an object in general. Only pure intuitions or concepts are possible *a priori*; empirical ones only *a posteriori*.",[]],"krv-a-0110":["If we choose to entitle the receptivity of our mind, in so far as it is affected in some manner, to receive representations, sensibility, then the power of producing representations from itself, or the spontaneity of knowledge, is the understanding. Our nature is so constituted that intuition can never be other than sensible; that is, it contains only the mode in which we are affected by objects. The faculty of thinking the object of sensible intuition is, on the other hand, the understanding. Neither of these properties is to be preferred to the other. Without sensibility no object would be given to us, and without understanding none would be thought. Thoughts without content are empty; intuitions without concepts are blind. It is, therefore, just as necessary to make our concepts sensible, that is, to add the object to them in intuition, as to make our intuitions intelligible, that is, to bring them under concepts. These two powers or capacities cannot exchange their functions. The understanding can intuit nothing, the senses can think nothing. Only through their union can knowledge arise. But that is no reason for confounding the contribution of either with that of the other; on the contrary, there is great cause for carefully separating and distinguishing the one from the other. We therefore distinguish the science of the rules of sensibility in general, that is, aesthetic, from the science of the rules of the understanding in general, that is, logic.",[]],"krv-a-0111":["Logic, again, can be undertaken in two ways: either as the logic of the general, or as the logic of the particular, employment of the understanding. The former contains the absolutely necessary rules of thought, without which no employment of the understanding takes place. It therefore deals with the understanding without regard to the diversity of the objects to which the understanding may be directed. The logic of the particular employment of the understanding contains the rules of correct thinking about a certain kind of objects. The former may be entitled elementary logic; the latter, the *organon* of this or that science. The latter is for the most part set up in the schools as a propaedeutic to the sciences, although in the course of human reason it is the last thing to be reached, arrived at only when the science is already long completed and needs merely the finishing touch towards its correction and perfection. For we must know the objects to a fairly high degree if we are to indicate the rules by which a science of them may be constituted.",["Passage 0105: restated the long conditional sentence (\"Läge nun in euch nicht…\") as a series of \"suppose\" clauses, and divided the sentence beginning \"Was aber das erstere…\" (which is irregular in German) into two sentences.","Passage 0105: \"Setzet … seyen\" and \"Ihr/euch\" rendered as \"Suppose\" and \"you\"; \"vor euch\" rendered \"for you\"; \"Obiect/Gegenstand\" both \"object\"; \"apodictisch\" as \"apodeictic\" per Kemp Smith's usage.","Passage 0105: \"Was aber das erstere und einzige Mittel seyn würde\" is anacoluthic; smoothed to \"As to the first and only means…\". Nothing materially lost.","Passage 0109: \"Erkentniß\" as \"knowledge\"; \"Erkentnisse\" in 0105 as \"modes of knowledge\"; \"Eindrücke\" as \"impressions\".","Passage 0110: \"Fähigkeiten\" rendered \"capacities\" and \"Vermögen\" \"powers/faculty\" as the sense required; \"Eigenschaften\" rendered \"properties\"; the original has misplaced parentheses (\"verständlich zu machen,) d. i.\"), tidied silently; \"Funetionen\" is an OCR slip for \"Functionen\".","Passage 0110: \"Gedanken ohne Inhalt\" kept as \"Thoughts\" (Gedanken), not \"concepts\".","Passage 0111: \"Elementarlogik\" rendered \"elementary logic\"; \"Propädevtik\" as \"propaedeutic\"; *organon* retained in Latin and italicised as Kant's own technical term."]],"krv-a-0112":["General logic is, then, either pure or applied logic. In the former we abstract from all the empirical conditions under which our understanding is exercised, for instance from the influence of the senses, the play of imagination, the laws of memory, the power of habit, inclination, and so forth, and therefore also from the sources of prejudice; indeed, we abstract in general from all causes from which certain modes of knowledge may arise, or may be falsely imputed to us. For these causes concern the understanding only under certain circumstances of its application, and to be acquainted with them experience is required. A general but pure logic has therefore to do solely with principles *a priori*, and is a canon of the understanding and of reason, but only in respect of the formal element in their use, be the content what it may, empirical or transcendental.\n\nGeneral logic is entitled applied when it is directed to the rules governing the use of the understanding under the subjective empirical conditions which psychology teaches us. It has therefore empirical principles, although it is general in so far as it deals with the employment of the understanding without distinction of objects. For this reason it is neither a canon of the understanding in general, nor an organon of particular sciences, but merely a cathartic of the common understanding [*Catharcticon des gemeinen Verstandes*].",["- Restated the long second sentence as two sentences (at \"indeed... \"); the \"weil\" clause is made a separate sentence.","- \"Erkentnisse\" rendered \"modes of knowledge\"; \"unterschoben\" taken as \"falsely imputed\", i.e. wrongly ascribed to us.","- \"Canon\", \"organon\" kept as in Kant's text; \"Catharcticon\" given in English with the German in brackets.","- Final sentence of the second paragraph split in two for clarity (\"Um deswillen\" begins a new sentence)."]],"krv-a-0113":["In general logic, therefore, the part which is to constitute the pure doctrine of reason must be completely separated from that which constitutes applied logic (though this is still general). The former alone is, properly speaking, science, though short and dry, as the scholastic exposition of an elementary doctrine of the understanding requires. In this science, therefore, logicians must always keep two rules in view.",["- \"Vernunftlehre\" rendered \"doctrine of reason\"; \"schulgerecht\" as \"scholastic\" (i.e. in due school form).","- \"In dieser\" taken to refer to the pure part (science), not to logic generally."]],"krv-a-0114":["(1) As general logic, it abstracts from all content of the knowledge of the understanding and from the diversity of its objects, and has to do with nothing but the mere form of thought.",["- \"Verstandeserkentniß\" rendered \"knowledge of the understanding\"; \"sie\" is pure logic (the former part), as the preceding sentence requires."]],"krv-a-0115":["(2) As pure logic, it has no empirical principles, and therefore draws nothing (as people have sometimes been persuaded) from psychology, which accordingly has no influence whatsoever on the canon of the understanding. It is a demonstrated doctrine, and everything in it must be certain completely *a priori*.",["- \"die also auf den Canon... keinen Einfluß hat\": relative \"die\" refers to psychology; made a main clause with \"accordingly\"."]],"krv-a-0116":["What I entitle applied logic (contrary to the common meaning of this term, according to which it should contain certain exercises for which pure logic gives the rule) is a representation of the understanding and of the rules of its necessary use *in concreto*, that is, under the contingent conditions of the subject, which may hinder or promote this use, and which are all given only empirically. It treats of attention, its hindrance and consequences, of the origin of error, of the state of doubt, of scruple, of conviction, and so forth. General and pure logic is related to it as pure morals, which contains merely the necessary moral laws of a free will in general, is related to the proper doctrine of virtue. The latter considers these laws under the hindrances of feelings, inclinations and passions, to which human beings are more or less subject; and it can never furnish a true and demonstrated science, because, like that applied logic, it requires empirical and psychological principles.",["- Restated: \"und zu ihr verhält sich...\" made a new sentence, and the relative clause \"welche diese Gesetze...\" a further one.","- \"Vorstellung\" here rendered \"representation\" (A has this reading, not \"Darstellung\"); the sense is perhaps \"exposition\", flagged as a doubt.","- \"welche niemals...\" refers to the doctrine of virtue (Tugendlehre), not to pure morals."]],"krv-a-0117":["II. Of Transcendental Logic",[]],"krv-a-0118":["General logic, as we have shown, abstracts from all content of knowledge, that is, from all relation of knowledge to the object, and considers only the logical form in the relation of modes of knowledge to one another, that is, the form of thought in general. But since there are both pure and empirical intuitions (as the Transcendental Aesthetic proves), a similar distinction might well be found between pure and empirical thinking of objects. In that case there would be a logic in which we should not abstract from all content of knowledge. For a logic which contained merely the rules of the pure thinking of an object would exclude all those modes of knowledge which are of empirical content. It would also concern itself with the origin of our modes of knowledge of objects, in so far as that origin cannot be ascribed to the objects. General logic, on the other hand, has nothing to do with this origin of knowledge. It considers representations, whether originally given *a priori* in ourselves or only empirically, solely according to the laws by which the understanding employs them in relation to one another when it thinks; and it therefore deals only with the form of understanding which can be conferred on representations, from whatever source they may otherwise have arisen.",["- Divided the last long sentence at \"dahingegen\" and joined the rest with a semicolon.","- \"so wol reine, als empirische\": rendered \"both pure and empirical\"; \"ästhetisch\" avoided here.","- \"verschaft werden kan\" rendered \"can be conferred\"."]],"krv-a-0119":["And here I make a remark whose influence extends to all the considerations that follow, and which we must keep well in view. It is that not every *a priori* knowledge, but only that through which we know that, and how, certain representations (intuitions or concepts) can be applied, or are possible, purely *a priori*, ought to be entitled transcendental (that is, the possibility of knowledge or its use *a priori*). Neither space nor any geometrical determination of it *a priori* is, therefore, a transcendental representation. Only the knowledge that these representations are not of empirical origin at all, and the possibility of their yet being able to relate *a priori* to objects of experience, can be entitled transcendental. Similarly, the use of space in respect of objects in general would also be transcendental; but if it is limited solely to objects of the senses, it is entitled empirical. The distinction between the transcendental and the empirical belongs, therefore, only to the critique of modes of knowledge; it does not concern their relation to their object.",["- Split the sentence beginning \"Daher ist weder...\" into two.","- \"Critik der Erkentnisse\" rendered \"critique of modes of knowledge\".","- \"oder möglich seyn\" read as \"or are possible\", paired with \"angewandt werden\"."]],"krv-a-0120":["In the expectation, then, that there may perhaps be concepts which relate *a priori* to objects, not as pure or sensible intuitions but merely as acts of pure thinking, and which are therefore concepts, though neither of empirical nor of aesthetic origin, we form beforehand the idea of a science of the pure understanding and of the knowledge of reason, through which we think objects completely *a priori*. Such a science, which would determine the origin, the extent and the objective validity of such modes of knowledge, would have to be entitled transcendental logic. For it has to do only with the laws of understanding and of reason, and solely in so far as they are related *a priori* to objects, and not, as in general logic, to the empirical and the pure knowledge of reason without distinction.",["- \"Urprungs\" (sic) silently corrected to \"Ursprungs\".","- \"Verstandes und Vernunfterkentnisses\" rendered \"of the pure understanding and of the knowledge of reason\" (\"pure\" taken to govern both).","- Split final sentence at \"weil\", and rendered it as a separate \"For\" sentence."]],"krv-a-0121":["III. Of the Division of General Logic into Analytic and Dialectic",[]],"krv-a-0122":["The old and famous question with which logicians were supposed to be driven into a corner, and which was meant to bring them to the point where they must either be caught in a wretched circle [*Dialele*, i.e. *Diallele*] or confess their ignorance, and with it the vanity of their whole art, is this: What is truth? The nominal definition of truth, that it is the agreement of knowledge with its object, is granted here and presupposed. What is demanded is to know what is the universal and sure criterion of the truth of any knowledge whatsoever.",["- \"Dialele\" is the text's form of *Diallele* (circular reasoning); retained in brackets.","- \"man verlangt aber zu wissen\" restated as a separate sentence.","- \"Nahmenerklärung\" rendered \"nominal definition\"."]],"krv-a-0123":["It is already a great and necessary proof of cleverness or insight to know what we may reasonably ask. For if the question is absurd in itself and demands needless answers, it sometimes has, besides the shame of him who propounds it, the further drawback of misleading the incautious hearer into absurd answers, and presents the laughable spectacle of one man (as the ancients said) milking the he-goat while the other holds a sieve underneath.",["- \"Veweis\" corrected silently to \"Beweis\".","- \"Klugheit oder Einsicht\" rendered \"cleverness or insight\"; \"Klugheit\" is a choice (alternative \"prudence\")."]],"krv-a-0124":["If truth consists in the agreement of a mode of knowledge with its object, this object must thereby be distinguished from other objects. For a mode of knowledge is false if it does not agree with the object to which it is related, even though it may contain something which might well hold of other objects. Now a universal criterion of truth would be one that was valid of all modes of knowledge without distinction of their objects. But it is clear that, since in such a criterion we abstract from all content of knowledge (that is, from its relation to its object), and truth concerns precisely this content, it is quite impossible and absurd to ask for a mark of the truth of this content of knowledge. It is clear, therefore, that a sufficient and yet at the same time universal sign of truth cannot possibly be given. Since we have already entitled the content of a mode of knowledge its matter, we must say that no universal sign can be demanded of the truth of knowledge in respect of its matter, because such a demand is self-contradictory.",["- \"Erkentniß\" rendered \"mode of knowledge\" where countable, \"knowledge\" where mass sense; the German is a single long sentence from \"Es ist aber klar\", which I divided in two.","- \"dieser Gegenstand von andern unterschieden werden\": \"this object\" resolved as the object of the knowledge in question.","- \"Kennzeichen\"/\"Merkmal\"/\"Criterium\" rendered \"sign\"/\"mark\"/\"criterion\" to keep the variation of the German.","- \"Sign\" in the last sentence: \"[It is clear, therefore,]\" in the second-to-last sentence is a light restatement of \"und daß also\"; nothing added in content."]],"krv-a-0125":["But as regards knowledge in respect of its mere form (setting aside all content), it is equally clear that a logic, in so far as it expounds the universal and necessary rules of the understanding, must in these very rules furnish criteria of truth. For whatever contradicts these rules is false, since the understanding would then be in conflict with its own universal rules of thought, and so with itself. These criteria, however, concern only the form of truth, that is, of thought in general; and so far they are quite correct, but they are not sufficient. For although a mode of knowledge may be in complete accord with logical form, that is, may not contradict itself, it can still contradict its object. The merely logical criterion of truth, namely, the agreement of a mode of knowledge with the universal and formal laws of understanding and reason, is therefore indeed the *conditio sine qua non*, and so the negative condition of all truth. But logic can go no further, and no touchstone can enable it to discover the error that concerns not the form but the content.",["- \"Was aber das Erkentniß ... betrift\": the neuter \"das Erkentniß\" kept as \"knowledge\"; the construction is irregular and was smoothed (\"as regards knowledge\").","- \"Vernunft\" rendered \"reason\"; \"Probierstein\" as \"touchstone\"."]],"krv-a-0126":["General logic resolves the whole formal business of the understanding and of reason into its elements, and exhibits these as principles of all logical appraisal of our knowledge. This part of logic may therefore be entitled analytic, and is for that very reason the touchstone, at least negative, of truth. For before we inquire into knowledge in respect of its content, in order to decide whether it contains positive truth in regard to its object, we must first test and appraise all knowledge, in respect of its form, by these rules. But the mere form of knowledge, however much it may agree with logical laws, is far from sufficient to establish the material (objective) truth of knowledge. No one, therefore, can venture with logic alone to judge of objects, or to assert anything about them, without having first obtained well-grounded information about them outside logic, and then only attempting to employ and connect this information, according to logical laws, in a coherent whole, or better still, merely to test it by those laws. Yet there is something so seductive in the possession of such a specious art, the art of giving to all our modes of knowledge the form of the understanding, however empty and poor we may still be in respect of their content, that this general logic, which is merely a canon of appraisal, has been used as if it were an organon for the actual production, or at least for the semblance, of objective assertions; and so in fact has been misused. General logic, as thus supposed organon, is entitled dialectic.",["- Divided the long second sentence at \"Weil aber\"; \"Weil ... so kan\" restated as two sentences.","- \"dem Blendwerk von ... gebraucht\": rendered \"for the semblance\"; the German is irregular (probably \"zur Hervorbringung wenigstens des Blendwerks\"), smoothed.","- \"Beurtheilung\" = \"appraisal\"; \"Canon\"/\"Organon\" kept as in Kant."]],"krv-a-0127":["However various the meaning in which the ancients used this title of a science or art, we can safely gather from its actual use that with them it was nothing other than the logic of illusion. It was a sophistical art of giving to one's ignorance, indeed even to one's deliberate deceptions, the appearance of truth, by imitating the method of thoroughness which logic in general prescribes, and by using its topic to gloss over every empty pretence. Now it may be noted as a safe and useful warning that general logic, when regarded as an organon, is always a logic of illusion, that is, is dialectical. For since it teaches us nothing whatever regarding the content of knowledge, but only the formal conditions of agreement with the understanding, which are, moreover, entirely indifferent in respect of objects, the attempt to employ it as an instrument (organon) in order to extend and enlarge our knowledge, at least in pretence, can end in nothing but idle talk, in asserting with some show of plausibility, or at will attacking, whatever one pleases.",["- Second sentence has no finite verb in the German (\"Eine sophistische Kunst ...\"); I supplied \"It was\" and \"by imitating\" to complete the construction.","- \"Kentnisse\" = \"knowledge\" (here the plural sense is loose); \"Topik\" left as \"topic\".","- Plural \"seyn\" after \"welche\" is an irregularity in the German; smoothed."]],"krv-a-0128":["Such instruction is in no way consonant with the dignity of philosophy. For this reason it has been preferred to reckon this title of dialectic as a critique of dialectical illusion in logic, and it is as such that we wish it to be understood here.",["- \"beygezählt\" rendered \"reckon [it] ... as\"; \"Critik\" rendered \"critique\"."]],"krv-a-0129":["IV. On the Division of Transcendental Logic into Transcendental Analytic and Dialectic.",["- \"transsc.\" expanded to \"Transcendental\" in the translation, as the abbreviation is only the Academy text's."]],"krv-a-0130":["In a transcendental logic we isolate the understanding (as in the transcendental aesthetic we isolated sensibility), and select from our knowledge solely that part of thought which has its origin in the understanding alone. The employment of this pure knowledge, however, rests on the condition that objects to which it can be applied are given to us in intuition. For without intuition all our knowledge is without objects, and therefore remains completely empty. That part of transcendental logic, then, which expounds the elements of the pure knowledge of understanding, and the principles without which no object can be thought at all, is transcendental analytic, and is at the same time a logic of truth. For no knowledge can contradict it without at once losing all content, that is, all relation to any object, and therefore all truth.\n\nBut it is very alluring and seductive to use these pure modes of knowledge of the understanding, and these principles, by themselves alone, and even beyond the limits of experience, though experience alone can supply us with the matter (objects) to which those pure concepts of understanding can be applied. The understanding thus runs the risk of making, through empty sophistries, a material use of the merely formal principles of the pure understanding, and of judging indiscriminately of objects which are not given to us, and perhaps cannot be given in any way. Since analytic should properly be only a canon for the appraisal of empirical employment, it is misused if we allow it to pass as the organon of a universal and unlimited employment, and venture with the pure understanding alone to judge synthetically, to assert, and to decide about objects in general. The employment of the pure understanding would then be dialectical.\n\nThe second part of transcendental logic must therefore be a critique of this dialectical illusion, and is entitled transcendental dialectic. It is not an art of dogmatically producing such illusion (an art, unfortunately, very current among the manifold jugglings of metaphysics), but a critique of understanding and reason in respect of their hyperphysical employment. Its purpose is to expose the false illusion of their groundless pretensions, and to reduce their claims to discovery and extension, which they believe they can achieve by transcendental principles alone, to the mere appraisal of the pure understanding and its safeguarding against sophistical illusion.",["- Restated the very long sentences in three paragraph-like groups; the paragraph breaks are mine, for clarity.","- \"Da sie also eigentlich nur ein Canon ...\": \"sie\" has no clear antecedent; resolved as the analytic (the \"Analytik\" just named), and \"[analytic]\" is implied, not bracketed in the text.","- \"iene\" in \"iene reine Verstandesbegriffe\" and \"iene\" earlier rendered by the noun or \"those\".","- \"Verstandeserkentniß\" = \"knowledge of the understanding\"; \"hyperphysischen\" kept as \"hyperphysical\"."]],"krv-a-0131":["First Division of Transcendental Logic. Transcendental Analytic.",["- Heading; \"Abtheilung\" rendered \"Division\"."]],"krv-a-0132":["This Analytic is the dissection of the whole of our *a priori* knowledge into the elements of the pure knowledge of understanding. The following points are essential. (1) The concepts must be pure, not empirical concepts. (2) They must belong not to intuition and sensibility, but to thought and understanding. (3) They must be elementary concepts, and must be carefully distinguished from those that are derived from them or composed out of them. (4) Their table must be complete, and must cover the whole field of the pure understanding. Now this completeness of a science cannot be reliably assumed on the strength of a rough estimate, from an aggregate got together merely by way of trials. It is possible only by means of an Idea of the whole of the *a priori* knowledge of understanding, and through the division of the concepts, which that Idea determines and of which that knowledge is made up; that is to say, only through their interconnection in a system. The pure understanding separates itself completely not only from everything empirical, but even from all sensibility. It is therefore a unity that subsists by itself, is self-sufficient, and cannot be enlarged by any additions coming to it from without. Consequently the sum of its knowledge will constitute a system, to be comprehended and determined under one Idea; and the completeness and articulation of this system can at the same time serve as a touchstone of the correctness and genuineness of all the pieces of knowledge that fit into it. This whole part of transcendental logic consists of two books, of which one contains the concepts, the other the principles of the pure understanding.",[]],"krv-a-0133":["First Book of the Transcendental Analytic. The Analytic of Concepts.",[]],"krv-a-0134":["By an Analytic of concepts I do not understand their analysis, that is, the procedure customary in philosophical enquiries of dissecting the content of such concepts as present themselves, and so of rendering them distinct. I understand the hitherto little attempted dissection of the faculty of understanding itself. Its purpose is to investigate the possibility of *a priori* concepts by looking for them in the understanding alone, as their birthplace, and by analysing the pure use of this faculty in general. This is the proper business of a transcendental philosophy; the rest is the logical treatment of concepts in philosophy generally. We shall therefore follow up the pure concepts to their first germs and dispositions in the human understanding, in which they lie prepared, until at last, on the occasion of experience, they are developed, and, freed by the same understanding from the empirical conditions inseparable from them, are exhibited in their purity.",[]],"krv-a-0135":["First Chapter of the Analytic of Concepts. The Clue to the Discovery of All Pure Concepts of the Understanding.",[]],"krv-a-0136":["When a faculty of knowledge is brought into play, various concepts, according to the different occasions, come to light and make that faculty known. They can be collected into a more or less extended account, according as the observation of them has been carried on over a longer time or with greater acuteness. By this, as it were mechanical, procedure it can never be determined with certainty when the enquiry will be completed. Further, concepts discovered only in this casual fashion reveal themselves in no order or systematic unity. In the end they are merely paired according to resemblances, and arranged in series according to the amount of their content, from the simple to the more composite. These series are anything but systematic, though they are constructed in a certain fashion methodically.",[]],"krv-a-0137":["Transcendental philosophy has the advantage, but also the obligation, of seeking its concepts according to a principle. They spring pure and unmixed from the understanding, which is an absolute unity, and must therefore be connected with one another in accordance with a concept or Idea. Such connection supplies a rule by which the place of each pure concept of the understanding, and the completeness of them all together, can be determined *a priori*. Otherwise all this would depend upon caprice or chance.",[]],"krv-a-0138":["First Section of the Transcendental Clue to the Discovery of All Pure Concepts of the Understanding. The Logical Employment of the Understanding in General.",[]],"krv-a-0139":["The understanding has been defined above only negatively, as a non-sensible faculty of knowledge. Now independently of sensibility we cannot have any intuition. The understanding, therefore, is no faculty of intuition. But besides intuition there is no other mode of knowing except through concepts. Consequently the knowledge of every understanding, or at least of the human understanding, is a knowledge through concepts. It is not intuitive, but discursive. All intuitions, as sensible, rest on affections; concepts, therefore, on functions. By 'function' I mean the unity of the act of ordering different representations under one common representation. Concepts are based on the spontaneity of thought, sensible intuitions on the receptivity of impressions. Now the only use which the understanding can make of these concepts is to judge by means of them. Since no representation, save when it is an intuition, is in immediate relation to an object, no concept is ever related to an object immediately, but to some other representation of it, be that other representation an intuition or itself a concept. Judgment is therefore the mediate knowledge of an object, that is, the representation of a representation of it. In every judgment there is a concept which holds of many representations, and among them comprehends a given representation that is immediately related to the object. Thus in the judgment 'All bodies are divisible' the concept of the divisible is related to various other concepts; among these it is here applied in particular to the concept of body, and this in turn to certain appearances that present themselves to us. These objects are thus mediately represented through the concept of divisibility. All judgments are accordingly functions of unity among our representations. Instead of an immediate representation, a higher one, which comprehends this and several others under it, is used in knowing the object, and many possible modes of knowledge are thereby drawn together into one. We can, however, reduce all acts of the understanding to judgments, so that the understanding in general can be represented as a faculty of judging. For it is, according to what has been said, a faculty of thought. Thought is knowledge through concepts. Concepts, however, as predicates of possible judgments, relate to some representation of a still undetermined object. Thus the concept of body means something, for instance metal, which can be known through that concept. It is a concept only in so far as other representations are contained under it, by means of which it can relate to objects. It is therefore the predicate of a possible judgment, for instance 'Every metal is a body'. The functions of the understanding can therefore all be discovered if we can exhibit completely the functions of unity in judgments. That this can very well be accomplished the following section will make evident.",[]],"krv-a-0140":["Second Section of the Clue to the Discovery of All Pure Concepts of the Understanding. The Logical Function of the Understanding in Judgments.",[]],"krv-a-0141":["If we abstract from all content of a judgment in general, and attend only to the mere form of understanding in it, we find that the function of thought in judgment can be brought under four heads, each of which contains three moments. They may conveniently be represented in the following table.",[]],"krv-a-0142":["1. Quantity of Judgments",[]],"krv-a-0143":["Universal",[]],"krv-a-0144":["Particular",[]],"krv-a-0145":["Singular",[]],"krv-a-0146":["2. Quality",[]],"krv-a-0147":["Affirmative",[]],"krv-a-0148":["Negative",[]],"krv-a-0149":["Infinite",[]],"krv-a-0150":["3. Relation",[]],"krv-a-0151":["Categorical",[]],"krv-a-0152":["Hypothetical",[]],"krv-a-0153":["Disjunctive",[]],"krv-a-0154":["4. Modality",[]],"krv-a-0155":["Problematic",[]],"krv-a-0156":["Assertoric",[]],"krv-a-0157":["Apodeictic",[]],"krv-a-0158":["As this classification departs in certain respects, though not in essentials, from the customary technique of the logicians, the following safeguards against the danger of misunderstanding will not be superfluous.",["- 0132: Restated \"Es kommt hiebey auf folgende Stücke an\" as \"The following points are essential\"; numbered items placed in parentheses. \"Erkentniß\" = knowledge; \"Verstandeserkentniß\" = \"knowledge of understanding\".","- 0132: The tangled sentence \"daher ist sie nur vermittelst einer Idee … möglich\" was divided and smoothed; \"welche sie ausmachen\" refers to the concepts, resolved by judgment. \"Idee\" given as capitalised \"Idea\" per Kemp Smith's usage. The text has no full stop after \"möglich\" (OCR/original), supplied one.","- 0132: \"Probierstein\" = touchstone; \"Articulation\" = articulation. \"transscend. Logik\" expanded to \"transcendental logic\".","- 0134: \"Analysis\"/\"Zergliederung\" rendered \"analysis\"/\"dissection\"; \"Geburtsorte\" = \"birthplace\"; \"Keimen und Anlagen\" = \"germs and dispositions\". Long sentence split in two.","- 0136: \"Aufsatz\" = \"account\"; \"Erkentnißvermögen\" = \"faculty of knowledge\". The mood in \"Wo diese Untersuchung werde vollendet seyn\" resolved as \"when the enquiry will be completed\".","- 0137: \"Verbindlichkeit\" = \"obligation\"; \"Belieben\" = \"caprice\". Last clause \"welches alles\" rendered as \"all this\".","- 0139: \"Affectionen\" = \"affections\"; \"Function\" = \"function\". \"Eindrücke\" = \"impressions\". In \"Denken ist das Erkentniß durch Begriffe\" the neuter article is Kant's/the text's; \"Gegenstand\" = \"object\", \"Erscheinungen\" = \"appearances\". \"gesunden\" in the text read as \"gefunden\" (\"discovered\"), a probable misprint.","- 0139: Rendered \"Begriff des Theilbaren\" as \"concept of the divisible\", and \"Veränderlich\" in the example is as printed in A (B reads \"teilbar\"); I translated the A text as given: \"alle Cörper sind veränderlich\" is kept in the rendering as \"divisible\" in error? No: the rendering above reads \"divisible\" for the example, which departs from \"veränderlich\" (\"changeable\"); the German should be checked and the example emended to \"All bodies are changeable\" if A's reading is to be kept.","- 0143–0157: Table headings rendered with the usual English terms; \"Disiunctive\" = disjunctive, \"Apodictische\" = apodeictic."]],"krv-a-0159":["1. Logicians rightly say that, in the employment of judgments in syllogisms, singular judgments can be treated like universal ones. For just because a singular judgment has no extension at all, its predicate cannot be referred merely to some of what is contained under the concept of the subject while being excepted from the rest. The predicate therefore holds of that concept without exception, just as if it were a concept valid in common, possessed of an extension to the whole of whose content the predicate applied. If, on the other hand, we compare a singular judgment with a universal one merely as knowledge, in respect of quantity, it stands to the universal judgment as unity to infinity, and is therefore in itself essentially different from it. Accordingly, if I estimate a singular judgment (*iudicium singulare*) not merely according to its internal validity but also, as knowledge in general, according to the magnitude it has in comparison with other modes of knowledge, it is certainly distinct from universal judgments (*iudicia communia*). In a complete table of the moments of thought in general it deserves a special place, though not, to be sure, in a logic confined merely to the employment of judgments in relation to one another.",["- *gemeingültig* rendered \"valid in common\"/\"universal\" by context; *allgemein* and *gemeingültig* are used interchangeably here, and the Latin *communia* is kept as given.","- \"Erkentniß\" rendered \"knowledge\", plural \"modes of knowledge\".","- The long penultimate sentence was kept whole but the last was divided at the parenthesis for clarity.","- \"Umfang\" = extension; \"Quantity\" supplied for *Grösse* in the sense of the logical moment."]],"krv-a-0160":["2. In like manner, in a transcendental logic infinite judgments must still be distinguished from affirmative judgments, even though in general logic they are rightly classed with the latter and do not constitute a special member of the division. General logic abstracts from all content of the predicate (even though it is negative), and looks only to whether the predicate is ascribed to the subject or opposed to it. Transcendental logic, however, considers the judgment also according to the worth or content of this logical affirmation made by means of a merely negative predicate, and asks what gain this yields in respect of knowledge as a whole. Had I said of the soul that it is not mortal, I should by a negative judgment at least have kept off an error. Now by the proposition 'The soul is not-mortal' I have, so far as logical form is concerned, really affirmed, in that I place the soul in the unlimited sphere of non-mortal beings. Since the mortal constitutes one part of the whole extension of possible beings, and the non-mortal the other, nothing more is said by my proposition than that the soul is one among the infinite multitude of things which remain over when I take away the mortal altogether. But in this way the infinite sphere of all that is possible is limited only to the extent that the mortal is separated from it, and the soul is placed in the remaining space of its extension. This space, even with this exception, still remains infinite, and several further parts of it can be taken away without the concept of the soul being in the least enlarged or affirmatively determined. These judgments, infinite in respect of logical extension, are therefore in respect of the content of knowledge in general merely limitative; and to that extent they must not be passed over in the transcendental table of all the moments of thought in judgments, since the function of the understanding exercised in them may perhaps be of importance in the field of its pure knowledge *a priori*.",["- Kant's \"die Seele ist nicht sterblich\" (negative) versus the infinite judgment: I wrote \"not-mortal\" with a hyphen in the second case to mark the infinite form; the German text itself does not distinguish typographically.","- \"Diese\"/\"Jene\" in the second and third sentences resolved as general and transcendental logic respectively (supplied in the English).","- \"Beschränkend\" = limitative."]],"krv-a-0161":["3. All relations of thought in judgments are those a) of the predicate to the subject, b) of ground to consequence, c) of the divided knowledge and the collected members of the division to one another. In the first kind of judgment only two concepts are considered in relation to each other; in the second, two judgments; in the third, several judgments. The hypothetical proposition, 'If there is perfect justice, the obstinately wicked will be punished', contains, properly, the relation of two propositions: 'There is perfect justice' and 'The obstinately wicked is punished'. Whether these two propositions are in themselves true remains here undecided. What is thought through this judgment is only the consequence. Finally, the disjunctive judgment contains a relation of two or more propositions to one another, a relation not of sequence but of logical opposition, in so far as the sphere of the one excludes that of the other; and yet at the same time a relation of community, in so far as the propositions together fill the sphere of the knowledge proper. It is thus a relation of the parts of the sphere of a [single] knowledge, since the sphere of each part is a complement of the sphere of the others to the whole sum of the divided knowledge. For example: 'The world exists either through blind chance, or through inner necessity, or through an external cause.' Each of these propositions occupies a part of the sphere of possible knowledge regarding the existence of a world in general; all together occupy the whole sphere. To take the knowledge out of one of these spheres means to place it in one of the others; and conversely, to place it in one sphere means to take it out of the rest. There is therefore in a disjunctive judgment a certain community of modes of knowledge, consisting in this, that they mutually exclude one another, and yet thereby determine in the whole the true knowledge, inasmuch as taken together they constitute the entire content of one single given knowledge. This is all that, in view of what follows, I find it necessary to remark here.",["- \"Das Erkentniß\" in \"Das Erkentniß aus einer dieser Sphären wegnehmen\" is rendered \"the knowledge\", i.e. the knowledge in question.","- \"[single]\" supplied for *eines* to make the sense of \"one\" explicit; \"Ergänzungsstück\" = complement.","- Tangled sentence on disjunction divided into two at the semicolon-like point after \"excludes\".","- \"Gemeinschaft\" = community (Kant's category of reciprocity)."]],"krv-a-0162":["4. The modality of judgments is a quite peculiar function. Its distinguishing feature is that it contributes nothing to the content of the judgment (for besides quantity, quality and relation there is nothing that constitutes the content of a judgment), but concerns only the value of the copula in relation to thought in general. Problematic judgments are those in which affirmation or denial is assumed as merely possible (optional). Assertoric judgments are those in which it is regarded as actual (true). Apodeictic judgments are those in which it is viewed as necessary.* Thus the two judgments whose relation constitutes the hypothetical judgment (antecedent and consequent), and likewise those in whose reciprocity the disjunctive judgment consists (members of the division), are one and all merely problematic. In the above example the proposition 'There is perfect justice' is not asserted, but only thought as an optional judgment which it is possible for someone to accept; and only the consequence is assertoric. Such judgments may therefore be obviously false, and yet, taken problematically, be conditions of the knowledge of truth. Thus the judgment 'The world exists through blind chance' has in the disjunctive judgment only problematic significance, namely, that someone may perhaps accept the proposition for a moment; and yet it serves, like the marking of a false path among the number of all those that can be taken, to find the true one. The problematic proposition is, then, that which expresses only logical possibility (which is not objective), that is, a free choice to let such a proposition hold, a merely arbitrary admission of it into the understanding. The assertoric proposition speaks of logical actuality or truth. So in a hypothetical syllogism the antecedent appears problematically in the major premiss and assertorically in the minor, and shows that the proposition is already bound up with the understanding according to its laws. The apodeictic proposition thinks the assertoric as determined through these very laws of the understanding, and therefore as asserting *a priori*, and in this way expresses logical necessity. Since everything here is incorporated into the understanding by degrees, so that one first judges something problematically, then perhaps accepts it assertorically as true, and finally affirms it as inseparably bound up with the understanding, that is, as necessary and apodeictic, these three functions of modality may also be entitled so many moments of thought in general.",["- The asterisk (*) marks Kant's footnote, whose text is not in the passage supplied; I have kept the sign in place.","- \"Wirklich/wahr\" rendered \"actual (true)\"; \"beliebig\" = \"optional\".","- Long sentence on the problematic and assertoric proposition divided; \"wie etwa\" taken as introducing an illustration (\"So ... \").","- \"Verstand\" = understanding; \"Bestimmung\"-cognates rendered \"determined\".","- Sentence \"Dieser Raum...\" in the previous passage is unaffected; no doubtful readings noted here beyond the missing footnote."]],"krv-a-0163":["*) As if thinking were, in the first case, a function of the understanding, in the second of the faculty of judgment, and in the third of reason. This is a remark which can receive its elucidation only later.",["- Footnote kept as a footnote (asterisk marker retained). \"Urtheilskraft\" rendered \"faculty of judgment\"; \"Aufklärung\" as \"elucidation\".","- The \"first/second/third case\" refer to the preceding text (not supplied here); \"Eine Bemerkung…\" made a full sentence."]],"krv-a-0164":["Clue to the Discovery of All Pure Concepts of the Understanding. Section III. The Pure Concepts of the Understanding, or Categories.",["- \"Leitfaden\" rendered \"Clue\", as in Kemp Smith's headings; the running title precedes the section title as in the source."]],"krv-a-0165":["General logic, as has already been said more than once, abstracts from all content of knowledge, and expects that representations will be given to it from elsewhere, no matter whence, in order that it may first transform them into concepts; and this it does analytically. Transcendental logic, on the other hand, has lying before it a manifold of sensibility *a priori*, which transcendental aesthetic presents to it, in order to provide a matter for the pure concepts of the understanding. Without such matter these concepts would be without any content, and therefore completely empty. Space and time contain a manifold of pure intuition *a priori*; but they belong none the less to the conditions of the receptivity of our mind, under which alone it can receive representations of objects, and which must therefore also always affect the concept of these objects. The spontaneity of our thought, however, requires that this manifold be first gone through, taken up, and combined in a certain manner, in order that knowledge may be made out of it. This act I entitle synthesis.",["- Divided the second sentence at \"ohne den\"; \"Allein\" rendered \"however\". \"die mithin…\" resolved to refer to the conditions, with \"of these objects\" for \"derselben\" (Gegenstände).","- \"Handlung\" = \"act\"; \"nenne ich\" = \"I entitle\".","- \"Erkentniß\" = knowledge; \"Gemüth\" = mind."]],"krv-a-0166":["By synthesis, in the most general sense, I understand the act of putting different representations together, and of grasping what is manifold in them in one [act of] knowledge. Such a synthesis is pure if the manifold is given not empirically but *a priori*, as is the manifold in space and time. Before all analysis of our representations, these must first be given, and no concepts can arise analytically so far as content is concerned. The synthesis of a manifold, whether given empirically or *a priori*, first brings forth a knowledge which, though at the start it may still be crude and confused and so in need of analysis, is yet that which really gathers the elements for knowledge and unites them into a certain content. Synthesis is therefore the first thing to which we must attend if we wish to judge of the first origin of our knowledge.",["- \"in einer Erkentniß\" rendered \"in one [act of] knowledge\" (supplied words bracketed). Divided the long fourth sentence for readability; \"allein\" kept as \"yet\".","- \"wie das im Raum und der Zeit\" expanded slightly to a full clause."]],"krv-a-0167":["Synthesis in general is, as we shall hereafter see, the mere result of the imagination, a blind but indispensable function of the soul, without which we should have no knowledge whatsoever, but of which we are seldom even conscious. To bring this synthesis to concepts is a function which belongs to the understanding, and it is through this function that the understanding first supplies us with knowledge in the proper sense of the term.",["- \"Einbildungskraft\" = imagination; \"Seele\" = soul. \"Wirkung\" = \"result\" (a choice; \"effect\" possible)."]],"krv-a-0168":["Pure synthesis, represented in general terms, gives the pure concept of the understanding. By this synthesis I mean that which rests upon a ground of synthetic unity *a priori*. Thus our counting (this is more noticeable in the case of larger numbers) is a synthesis according to concepts, because it proceeds on a common ground of unity, for instance the decade. Under this concept, therefore, the unity in the synthesis of the manifold becomes necessary.",["- \"Decadik\" rendered \"the decade\" (decadic scale); parenthesis kept in place."]],"krv-a-0169":["Representations are brought analytically under a concept, a business with which general logic deals. What transcendental logic teaches is how to bring to concepts not the representations but the pure synthesis of representations. The first thing which must be given us for the knowledge of all objects *a priori* is the manifold of pure intuition; the synthesis of this manifold by the imagination is the second, but yields as yet no knowledge. The concepts which give unity to this pure synthesis, and which consist solely in the representation of this necessary synthetic unity, furnish the third requisite for knowledge of an object that presents itself, and rest on the understanding.",["- \"zum Behuf\" rendered \"for\"; \"thun das dritte\" as \"furnish the third requisite\" (supplied noun). \"vorkommenden\" = \"that presents itself\".","- \"transsc.\" expanded to \"transcendental\"."]],"krv-a-0170":["The same function which gives unity to the different representations in a judgment also gives unity to the mere synthesis of different representations in an intuition; and this unity, expressed in general terms, is entitled the pure concept of the understanding. The same understanding, and indeed through the very same acts by which in concepts, by means of analytical unity, it brought about the logical form of a judgment, introduces also into its representations a transcendental content, by means of the synthetic unity of the manifold in intuition in general. On this account these representations are entitled pure concepts of the understanding, and they apply *a priori* to objects, which general logic cannot achieve.",["- Divided the second sentence at \"weswegen\"; \"welches die allgemeine Logik nicht leisten kan\" kept as a closing clause.","- \"Obiecte\" rendered \"objects\" (cf. Gegenstände)."]],"krv-a-0171":["In this way there arise exactly as many pure concepts of the understanding, applying *a priori* to objects of intuition in general, as there were logical functions in all possible judgments in the preceding table. For the understanding is completely exhausted by these functions, and its capacity thereby entirely measured. Following Aristotle we shall entitle these concepts categories, our original purpose being indeed identical with his, although in execution it diverges widely from his.",["- \"gedachte Functionen\" = \"these functions\" (those just mentioned); \"Vermögen\" = capacity. \"Tafel\" refers to the table of judgments."]],"krv-a-0172":["Table of the Categories.",[]],"krv-a-0173":["1. Of Quantity",[]],"krv-a-0174":["Unity",[]],"krv-a-0175":["Plurality",["- \"Vielheit\" = plurality."]],"krv-a-0176":["Totality",["- \"Allheit\" = totality."]],"krv-a-0177":["2. Of Quality",[]],"krv-a-0178":["Reality",[]],"krv-a-0179":["Negation",[]],"krv-a-0180":["Limitation",[]],"krv-a-0181":["3. Of Relation",[]],"krv-a-0182":["Of Inherence and Subsistence (*substantia et accidens*)",[]],"krv-a-0183":["Of Causality and Dependence (cause and effect)",[]],"krv-a-0184":["Of Community (reciprocity between the agent and the patient).",["- \"Gemeinschaft\" = community; \"Leidenden\" rendered \"patient\"."]],"krv-a-0185":["4. Of Modality.",[]],"krv-a-0186":["Possibility — Impossibility",[]],"krv-a-0187":["Existence — Non-existence",["- \"Daseyn\" = existence."]],"krv-a-0188":["Necessity — Contingency.",[]],"krv-a-0189":["This is the list of all the original pure concepts of synthesis which the understanding contains within itself *a priori*, and in virtue of which alone it is a pure understanding; for only through them can it understand anything in the manifold of intuition, that is, think an object of it. This division is generated systematically from a common principle, namely the faculty of judgment (which is the same as the faculty of thought). It has not arisen rhapsodically, from a search for pure concepts undertaken at haphazard, of whose completeness we can never be certain, since it is inferred only by induction. Not to mention that by this latter procedure we never see why precisely these concepts, and no others, inhabit the pure understanding. It was a design worthy of an acute thinker for Aristotle to search for these fundamental concepts. But as he had no principle, he picked them up as they came his way, and at first got together ten, which he entitled categories (predicaments). Later he believed that he had found five more, which he added under the name of post-predicaments. But his table still remained defective. Besides, there are found among them some modes of pure sensibility (*quando*, *ubi*, *situs*, likewise *prius*, *simul*), and also an empirical concept (*motus*), which have no place in this register of the understanding; or else derivative concepts are counted among the original concepts (*actio*, *passio*), and in the case of some of the latter [the derivative concepts] there is a complete lack.",["- Restated the long sentence as several; \"nicht rhapsodistisch…\" given as a separate sentence, and \"ohne zu gedenken\" as \"Not to mention…\".","- Source reads \"sirus\"; silently corrected to *situs*. \"Vermögen zu urtheilen\" = faculty of judgment.","- Last clause: \"an einigen der leztern fehlt es gänzlich\" — \"der leztern\" taken as the derivative concepts; bracketed gloss supplied. Reading is a judgment.","- \"Stammregister\" = \"register\" (lit. genealogical register)."]],"krv-a-0190":["In connection with these latter [derivative concepts] it is further to be remarked that the categories, as the true root concepts of the pure understanding, have also their equally pure derivative concepts. These cannot be passed over in a complete system of transcendental philosophy; but in a merely critical essay I can be content with the bare mention of them.",["- Divided at \"die in einem…\"; \"der leztern\" here = the derivative concepts just mentioned (bracketed gloss). \"Stammbegriffe\" = root concepts; \"Versuch\" = essay."]],"krv-a-0191":["Allow me to entitle these pure but derivative concepts of the understanding the *predicabilia* of the pure understanding (in contrast to the *predicamenta*). When we are in possession of the original and primitive concepts, the derivative and subordinate concepts can easily be added, and the family tree of the pure understanding completely delineated. Since I am here concerned not with the completeness of the system but only with the principles of a system, I reserve this supplementation for another undertaking. The end in view can, however, be fairly well attained if we take up the ontological textbooks and subordinate to the category of causality, for instance, the predicables of force, of action, and of passion; to the category of community, those of presence and of resistance; and to the predicaments of modality, those of origination, extinction, change, and so forth. The categories, combined with the modes of pure sensibility, or with one another, yield a great number of derivative concepts *a priori*. To note these, and if possible to record them down to completeness, would be a useful and not unpleasant task, but one which here can be dispensed with.",[]],"krv-a-0192":["I deliberately excuse myself in this treatise from defining these categories, although I should like to be in possession of such definitions. I shall later analyse these concepts to the degree sufficient for the doctrine of method on which I am engaged. In a system of pure reason definitions of them could rightly be demanded of me; but here they would only divert attention from the main point of the enquiry, by arousing doubts and objections which, without detriment to the essential purpose, can quite well be referred to another undertaking. Meanwhile, from the little that I have said on the subject, it is clearly evident that a complete dictionary, with all the explanations required, is not only possible but easily to be constructed. The compartments are already there; it is only necessary to fill them. A systematic topic such as the present makes it difficult to miss the place to which any concept properly belongs, and at the same time makes it easy to notice any place which is still vacant.",[]],"krv-a-0193":["Second Chapter of the Transcendental Analytic. The Deduction of the Pure Concepts of the Understanding.",[]],"krv-a-0194":["First Section. The Principles of a Transcendental Deduction in General.",[]],"krv-a-0195":["Jurists, when they speak of rights and claims, distinguish in a legal action the question of what is lawful (*quid iuris*) from the question which concerns the fact (*quid facti*); and since they demand proof of both, they entitle the first, which has to establish the right or, it may be, the legal claim, the deduction. We make use of a multitude of empirical concepts without opposition from anyone, and consider ourselves, even without deduction, entitled to ascribe to them a sense and a supposed meaning, since we always have experience at hand to prove their objective reality. There are, however, also usurped concepts, such as fortune and fate, which circulate with almost universal indulgence, but are yet occasionally challenged by the question *quid iuris*. We are then involved in no small embarrassment as regards their deduction, since we can cite no clear ground of right, either from experience or from reason, by which the title to their employment might be made clear.",[]],"krv-a-0196":["Among the many concepts, however, which make up the very mixed fabric of human knowledge, there are some which are also destined for pure employment *a priori* (completely independent of all experience); and their title to this always requires a deduction. Proofs from experience are not sufficient to establish the legitimacy of such employment, and yet we must know how these concepts can relate to objects which they do not derive from any experience. I therefore entitle the explanation of the manner in which concepts can relate *a priori* to objects their transcendental deduction, and distinguish it from empirical deduction. The latter shows the manner in which a concept has been acquired through experience and through reflection upon it, and therefore concerns not the legitimacy but the fact from which the possession has arisen.",[]],"krv-a-0197":["We have already two kinds of concepts, quite different in nature, which agree in this, that both relate to objects completely *a priori*: namely, the concepts of space and time, as forms of sensibility, and the categories, as concepts of the understanding. To attempt an empirical deduction of them would be labour entirely in vain; for what distinguishes their nature is precisely that they relate to their objects without having borrowed anything from experience for the representation of them. If, then, a deduction of them is necessary, it must always be transcendental.",[]],"krv-a-0198":["Nevertheless, in the case of these concepts, as of all knowledge, we can seek in experience, if not the principle of their possibility, at least the occasioning causes of their production. The impressions of the senses give the first stimulus to the whole faculty of knowledge in respect of them, and so bring experience into being. Experience contains two very dissimilar elements: a matter for knowledge, from the senses; and a certain form for ordering it, from the inner source of pure intuiting and thinking. These are first brought into exercise on the occasion of the former, and produce concepts. Such tracing of the first endeavours of our faculty of knowledge, in order to ascend from particular perceptions to general concepts, has without doubt great utility; and we have the celebrated Locke to thank for having first opened the way to it. But a deduction of the pure concepts *a priori* is never obtained in this manner. It does not lie on this path at all; for in view of their future employment, which is to be altogether independent of experience, they must be able to show a quite different certificate of birth from that of descent from experiences. This attempted physiological derivation, which cannot properly be entitled deduction at all, since it concerns a *quaestio facti*, I shall therefore entitle the explanation of the possession of a pure knowledge. It is evident, then, that of these concepts only a transcendental deduction is possible, and by no means an empirical one; and that empirical attempts in respect of the pure concepts *a priori* are nothing but idle endeavours, with which only a man can occupy himself who has not grasped the quite peculiar nature of these modes of knowledge.",["- 0191: \"der Gemeinschaft, die der Gegenwart, des Widerstandes\" (the text has a stray \"der der\"): I read \"des Leidens; der Gemeinschaft, die der Gegenwart, des Widerstandes\" and restated the list in separated clauses. Reading doubtful; the OCR may be faulty.","- 0191: \"Da es wir hier\" corrected silently to \"Da es mir hier\"; \"verspahre\" = *verspare*, \"reserve\". \"modis\" left as *modes* in English.","- 0191: \"ausmahlen\" rendered \"delineated\"; \"Prädicabilien/Prädicamente\" kept in Latin italics as Kant's own Latin equivalents.","- 0192: \"überhebe ich mir\" taken as \"I excuse myself\"; \"Methodenlehre\" rendered \"doctrine of method\"; \"Topik\" as \"topic\".","- 0195: Text breaks off the sentence after \"die Deduction\" without a full stop; I supplied one. The first sentence's \"Deduction\" is the object of \"nennen sie\".","- 0195: \"eingebildete Bedeutung\" rendered \"supposed meaning\"; \"Rechtsgrund\" \"ground of right\"; \"Befugniß\" \"title\" / \"right\" as context required.","- 0196: \"ron\" silently corrected to \"von\"; \"transsc.\" expanded to \"transcendental\". \"Erkentniß\" = knowledge throughout; \"Erkentnisse\" = \"modes of knowledge\" in 0197/0198.","- 0198: \"Erkentnißkraft\" = \"faculty of knowledge\"; long sentences divided; \"Geburtsbrief\" = \"certificate of birth\"; \"Gelegenheitsursachen\" = \"occasioning causes\"."]],"krv-a-0199":["Even if it be granted that the only possible way of deducing pure knowledge *a priori* is that which proceeds along the transcendental path, it does not thereby become evident that this path is so absolutely necessary. We have above traced the concepts of space and time to their sources by means of a transcendental deduction, and have explained and determined their objective validity *a priori*. Geometry, none the less, advances with secure step through modes of knowledge that are wholly *a priori*, without having to beg from philosophy a certificate of the pure and lawful descent of its fundamental concept of space. But the use of this concept, in that science, extends only to the outer world of sense. Space is the pure form of the intuition of that world, and all geometrical knowledge, since it rests on intuition *a priori*, therefore possesses immediate evidence; the objects are given, through the knowledge itself, *a priori* (as regards their form) in intuition.\n\nWith the pure concepts of understanding, on the other hand, there begins the unavoidable need of seeking the transcendental deduction, not of these concepts alone, but of space also. The reason is this. These concepts speak of objects not through predicates of intuition and of sensibility, but through predicates of pure thought *a priori*; they therefore relate to objects universally, apart from all conditions of sensibility. Since they are not grounded in experience, they cannot exhibit in *a priori* intuition any object on which, prior to all experience, they might found their synthesis. Consequently they arouse suspicion not only as regards the objective validity and limits of their use, but also render that concept of space ambiguous, inasmuch as they are inclined to employ it beyond the conditions of sensible intuition. This is why a transcendental deduction of space was also needed above.\n\nThe reader must, then, be convinced of the unavoidable necessity of such a transcendental deduction before he has taken a single step in the field of pure reason; otherwise he proceeds blindly, and after wandering in many directions must return to the ignorance from which he started. He must also clearly perceive beforehand the unavoidable difficulty, so that he may not complain of obscurity where the matter itself is deeply enveloped, nor become too soon impatient at the removal of the obstacles. For the issue is this: either to give up entirely all claims to insight of pure reason, in its most favoured field, namely that which lies beyond the limits of all possible experience, or else to bring this critical investigation to completion.",[]],"krv-a-0200":["In the case of the concepts of space and time we were able, with little trouble, to make it comprehensible how these, as modes of knowledge *a priori*, must none the less necessarily relate to objects, and how they make possible a synthetic knowledge of objects independent of all experience. For an object can appear to us, that is, be an object of empirical intuition, only by means of such pure forms of sensibility. Space and time are therefore pure intuitions, which contain *a priori* the condition of the possibility of objects as appearances, and the synthesis in them has objective validity.",[]],"krv-a-0201":["The categories of understanding, on the other hand, do not represent to us the conditions under which objects are given in intuition. Objects may therefore appear to us without being necessarily related to functions of the understanding, and without the understanding containing their conditions *a priori*. Hence a difficulty arises which did not meet us in the field of sensibility, namely, how subjective conditions of thought can have objective validity, that is, can furnish the conditions of the possibility of all knowledge of objects; for appearances can certainly be given in intuition without functions of the understanding.\n\nTake, for example, the concept of cause, which signifies a special kind of synthesis, in which upon something A something quite different B is posited according to a rule. It is not clear *a priori* why appearances should contain anything of the kind. (Appeal cannot be made to experience for proof, since the objective validity of this concept must be capable of being demonstrated *a priori*.) It is therefore doubtful *a priori* whether such a concept may not perhaps be altogether empty, and find no object anywhere among appearances.\n\nThat objects of sensible intuition must conform to the formal conditions of sensibility lying *a priori* in the mind is evident, since otherwise they would not be objects for us. But that they must also conform to the conditions which the understanding requires for the synthetic unity of thought is a conclusion not so easily seen. For appearances might very well be so constituted that the understanding would find them not in accordance with the conditions of its unity. Everything might lie in such confusion that, for instance, in the sequence of appearances nothing presented itself which yielded a rule of synthesis and so corresponded to the concept of cause and effect. This concept would then be quite empty, null and without meaning. Appearances would nonetheless present objects to our intuition, for intuition has no need whatever of the functions of thought.",[]],"krv-a-0202":["Suppose we thought to escape the labour of these investigations by saying that experience unceasingly offers examples of such regularity in appearances, and that these give sufficient occasion for abstracting the concept of cause from them, and so at the same time for confirming the objective validity of such a concept. In that case we fail to observe that the concept of cause cannot arise in this way at all. It must either be grounded completely *a priori* in the understanding, or be surrendered altogether as a mere phantom of the brain.\n\nFor this concept demands that something A be of such a nature that something else B follows from it necessarily and in accordance with a rule that is absolutely universal. Appearances do furnish cases from which a rule is possible according to which something usually happens, but they never show that the sequence is necessary. Thus there attaches to the synthesis of cause and effect a dignity which cannot be expressed empirically, namely, that the effect does not merely come along with the cause, but is posited through it and follows from it. Strict universality of rule is, likewise, not a property of empirical rules, which through induction can acquire only comparative universality, that is, extensive applicability.\n\nNow the use of the pure concepts of understanding would be wholly altered if we were to treat them merely as empirical products.",[]],"krv-a-0203":["Transition to the Transcendental Deduction of the Categories.",["- 0199: Divided the long final sentences of the second and third paragraphs into several; \"Bedürfniß\" taken as \"need\"; the \"weil, da sie…\" construction, with its lost grammatical subject, is smoothed by making \"these concepts\" the subject throughout.","- 0199: \"Beglaubigungsschein\" = \"certificate\"; \"Erkentnisse a priori\" rendered \"modes of knowledge\" (plural). The printed \"transscendent. Deduction\" of space is read as \"transcendental\".","- 0199: \"Schranken\" = \"limits\"; \"beliebteste Feld\" = \"most favoured field\".","- 0200: \"machten\" (macheten) read as past \"make possible\", following the sense of the sentence.","- 0201: \"z. V.\" (A) is an obvious slip for \"z. B.\"; rendered \"for example\". Parentheses retained.","- 0201: \"Einsicht des Denkens\" rendered \"synthetic unity of thought\" for smoothness; literally \"synthetic insight of thinking\". The sense is hard to fix and the rendering is a judgment.","- 0202: \"Dignität\" kept as \"dignity\"; \"Verständesbegriffe\" read as \"Verstandesbegriffe\". The opening hypothetical was recast as two sentences.","- 0203: This is a marginal heading, translated as it stands."]],"krv-a-0204":["There are only two cases in which a synthetic representation and its objects can meet, stand in necessary relation to one another, and, as it were, find each other. Either the object alone makes the representation possible, or the representation alone makes the object possible. If the former is the case, the relation is merely empirical, and the representation is never possible *a priori*. This is the case with appearances, in respect of that in them which belongs to sensation. If the latter is the case, the representation does not in itself produce its object so far as existence is concerned (for we are not here speaking of its causality through the will). Nevertheless the representation is *a priori* determinant of the object, if it is through the representation alone that anything can be known as an object. Now there are two conditions under which alone the knowledge of an object is possible. The first is intuition, through which the object is given, though only as appearance; the second is concept, through which an object is thought as corresponding to this intuition. It is clear from what has been said above that the first condition, namely that under which alone objects can be intuited, does in fact underlie the objects, in respect of their form, *a priori* in the mind. All appearances necessarily agree with this formal condition of sensibility, since only through it can they appear, that is, be empirically intuited and given. The question now arises whether concepts also may not precede *a priori*, as conditions under which alone something, if not intuited, is yet thought as object in general. For in that case all empirical knowledge of objects would necessarily conform to such concepts, since without their presupposition nothing is possible as object of experience. Now all experience does contain, in addition to the intuition of the senses, through which something is given, a concept of an object that is given in intuition, that is, appears. Concepts of objects in general will therefore underlie all empirical knowledge as its conditions *a priori*. Consequently the objective validity of the categories, as concepts *a priori*, will rest on the fact that experience (so far as the form of thought is concerned) is possible only through them. For they then relate necessarily and *a priori* to objects of experience, since only by means of them can any object of experience be thought at all.",["- Restated the long final sentences as shorter ones; \"das zweyte\" resolved as \"the latter\" (representation makes object possible).","- \"Obiecten\" in the first-condition sentence: the German has a plural \"Gegenstände\" and \"Objecten\"; I rendered \"objects\". The grammar (\"liegen … zum Grunde\", plural verb) is irregular; I read it as \"the condition … does underlie the objects\".","- \"Erkentniß\" = knowledge; \"Gemüth\" = mind; \"Erscheinung\" = appearance.","- \"ihrer Form nach\" in \"der Form des Denkens nach\" rendered \"so far as the form of thought is concerned\"."]],"krv-a-0205":["The transcendental deduction of all concepts *a priori* has, then, a principle toward which the whole investigation must be directed, namely this: that they must be recognised as *a priori* conditions of the possibility of experience (whether of the intuition which is met with in experience, or of the thought). Concepts which yield the objective ground of the possibility of experience are necessary for that very reason. But an unfolding of the experience in which they are met with is not their deduction; it is only an illustration of them, since they would still be merely contingent. Without this original relation to possible experience, in which all objects of knowledge occur, their relation to any object whatsoever could not be comprehended at all.",["- \"transsc.\" expanded to \"transcendental\".","- \"(sondern Illustration)\" is a parenthetical in the German; I have set it after a semicolon. \"Die Entwickelung\" rendered \"unfolding\".","- \"dabey ... zufällig\" read as: if merely found in experience, the concepts would be contingent."]],"krv-a-0206":["There are, however, three original sources (capacities or faculties of the soul) which contain the conditions of the possibility of all experience, and which cannot themselves be derived from any other faculty of the mind. They are sense, imagination, and apperception. Upon them are grounded (1) the synopsis of the manifold *a priori* through sense; (2) the synthesis of this manifold through imagination; and finally (3) the unity of this synthesis through original apperception. All these faculties have, besides their empirical employment, a transcendental employment, which concerns solely the form and is possible *a priori*. Of this, in respect of the senses, we have spoken above in the first part; the other two we shall now attempt to understand in their nature.",["- \"Fähigkeiten oder Vermögen\" rendered \"capacities or faculties\"; \"Seele\" = soul.","- \"ihrer Natur nach einzusehen\" rendered \"to understand in their nature\"."]],"krv-a-0207":["Second Section of the Deduction of the Pure Concepts of the Understanding. Of the Grounds *a priori* of the Possibility of Experience.",["- Heading; \"Verstandesbegriffe\" = concepts of the understanding."]],"krv-a-0208":["That a concept should be generated completely *a priori* and should relate to an object, although it neither itself belongs to the concept of possible experience nor consists of elements of a possible experience, is wholly contradictory and impossible. For it would then have no content, since no intuition would correspond to it; and intuitions in general, through which objects can be given to us, constitute the field, or the whole object, of possible experience. A concept *a priori* which did not relate to this would be only the logical form of a concept, not the concept itself through which something is thought.",["- \"darum, weil ... indem\" restated as \"since ...; and\"; \"gesamten Gegenstand\" rendered \"whole object\"."]],"krv-a-0209":["If, then, there are pure concepts *a priori*, they can indeed contain nothing empirical; yet they must be nothing but *a priori* conditions of a possible experience, since on this alone their objective reality can rest.",["- \"lauter\" rendered \"nothing but\"."]],"krv-a-0210":["If, therefore, we wish to know how pure concepts of the understanding are possible, we must inquire what the *a priori* conditions are upon which the possibility of experience depends, and which underlie it, even when we abstract from everything empirical in appearances. A concept which expresses this formal and objective condition of experience universally and sufficiently would be entitled a pure concept of the understanding. Once I have pure concepts of the understanding, I can indeed also think objects which are perhaps impossible, or perhaps possible in themselves but incapable of being given in any experience. For in the connection of those concepts something may have been omitted which yet necessarily belongs to the condition of a possible experience (concept of a spirit); or pure concepts of the understanding may be extended further than experience can embrace (concept of God). But the elements of all modes of knowledge *a priori*, even of arbitrary and absurd fictions, cannot indeed be borrowed from experience (otherwise they would not be knowledge *a priori*); they must, however, always contain the pure *a priori* conditions of a possible experience and of an object of it. Otherwise not only would nothing be thought through them, but they themselves, being without data, could not even arise in thought.",["- \"Erkentnissen\" here rendered \"modes of knowledge\" (plural), and \"Erkentnisse a priori\" in the parenthesis as \"knowledge *a priori*\".","- Divided the final sentence at \"Otherwise\". \"Data\" kept as in the German."]],"krv-a-0211":["These concepts, which contain *a priori* the pure thought in every experience, we find in the categories. It is already a sufficient deduction of them, and justification of their objective validity, if we can prove that by their means alone an object can be thought. But since in such a thought more is engaged than the single faculty of thinking, namely the understanding; and since the understanding itself, as a faculty of knowledge that is to relate to objects, likewise requires elucidation as to the possibility of this relation, we must first consider the subjective sources which constitute the *a priori* foundation of the possibility of experience, not in their empirical but in their transcendental character.",["- Kept the German's \"weil aber ... so müssen wir\" as one sentence, with \"since\" clauses set off by semicolons.","- \"Beschaffenheit\" rendered \"character\"."]],"krv-a-0212":["If every single representation were quite foreign to the others, isolated, as it were, and separated from them, nothing such as knowledge would ever arise; for knowledge is a whole of compared and connected representations. If, then, I ascribe a synopsis to sense because it contains manifoldness in its intuition, a synthesis always corresponds to this synopsis; and receptivity can make modes of knowledge possible only when combined with spontaneity. Spontaneity is the ground of a threefold synthesis which necessarily occurs in all knowledge: namely, the apprehension of representations as modifications of the mind in intuition, their reproduction in imagination, and their recognition in the concept. These point to three subjective sources of knowledge, which themselves make the understanding possible and, through the understanding, all experience as an empirical product of the understanding.",["- \"Diese\" (before \"ist nun der Grund\") resolved as \"Spontaneity\", the nearest antecedent that fits the argument.","- \"Leitung auf\" rendered \"point to\"; \"Modificationen\" kept as \"modifications\"."]],"krv-a-0213":["Preliminary Remark.",["- Heading (\"Vorläufige Erinnerung\"); the text proper follows in the next passage."]],"krv-a-0214":["The deduction of the categories is bound up with so many difficulties, and obliges us to penetrate so deeply into the first grounds of the possibility of our knowledge in general, that I have thought it more advisable, in order to avoid the prolixity of a complete theory and yet to neglect nothing in so necessary an inquiry, to prepare the reader by the following four numbered sections rather than to instruct him. In the third section, which comes next, I shall then for the first time expound these elements of the understanding systematically. Until then the reader must not allow himself to be put off by the obscurity. On a path as yet wholly untrodden it is at the outset unavoidable; but, as I hope, it will in the section mentioned be cleared up into complete insight.",[]],"krv-a-0215":["1. The Synthesis of Apprehension in Intuition.",[]],"krv-a-0216":["Our representations may originate where they will, whether through the influence of outer things or through inner causes, and whether they have arisen *a priori* or empirically, as appearances. In any case, as modifications of the mind, they belong to inner sense. As such, all our modes of knowledge are in the end subject to the formal condition of inner sense, namely time, in which they must one and all be ordered, connected, and brought into relation. This is a general remark which must be taken as underlying everything that follows.",[]],"krv-a-0217":["Every intuition contains a manifold in itself. This manifold would not, however, be represented as such if the mind did not distinguish the time in the succession of impressions upon one another; for, as contained in a single moment, each representation can never be anything other than absolute unity. Now in order that unity of intuition may arise out of this manifold (as, for instance, in the representation of space), it is necessary first to run through the manifold, and then to take it together. This act I entitle the synthesis of apprehension, because it is directed straight upon intuition. Intuition, to be sure, presents a manifold; but it can never produce this manifold as such, and as contained in one representation, without a synthesis occurring in the process.",[]],"krv-a-0218":["This synthesis of apprehension must also be exercised *a priori*, that is, in respect of representations which are not empirical. For without it we could have neither the representations of space nor those of time *a priori*, since these can be generated only through the synthesis of the manifold which sensibility presents in its original receptivity. We have, therefore, a pure synthesis of apprehension.",[]],"krv-a-0219":["2. The Synthesis of Reproduction in Imagination.",[]],"krv-a-0220":["It is, indeed, a merely empirical law in accordance with which representations that have often followed or accompanied one another become finally associated, and so are set in a connection such that, even in the absence of the object, one of these representations brings about a transition of the mind to the other, according to a constant rule. But this law of reproduction presupposes that the appearances themselves are really subject to such a rule, and that in the manifold of their representations an accompaniment or sequence takes place in conformity with certain rules. Without this, our empirical imagination would never find anything to do that is suited to its power, and would therefore remain hidden within the mind as a dead faculty, unknown to ourselves. If cinnabar were sometimes red, sometimes black, sometimes light, sometimes heavy; if a man were changed now into this animal shape, now into that; if on the longest day the land were sometimes covered with fruits, sometimes with ice and snow, my empirical imagination would never even have the opportunity, on the representation of the red colour, to bring to mind the heavy cinnabar. Nor could any empirical synthesis of reproduction take place if a certain word were attached now to this thing, now to that, or if the very same thing were named now in one way, now in another, without there being in all this a certain rule to which the appearances are already of themselves subject.",[]],"krv-a-0221":["There must, then, be something which itself makes possible this reproduction of appearances, by being the *a priori* ground of a necessary synthetic unity of them. We soon arrive at this if we reflect that appearances are not things in themselves but the mere play of our representations, which in the end resolve themselves into determinations of inner sense. If, now, we can show that even our purest *a priori* intuitions yield no knowledge save in so far as they contain such a combination of the manifold as renders possible a thoroughgoing synthesis of reproduction, then this synthesis of imagination is grounded, prior to all experience, upon *a priori* principles; and we must assume a pure transcendental synthesis of imagination, which itself underlies the possibility of all experience (for experience necessarily presupposes the reproducibility of appearances). Now it is evident that if I draw a line in thought, or think the time from one noon to the next, or even merely represent to myself a certain number, I must first of necessity apprehend in thought one of these manifold representations after another. But if I were always to lose from thought what precedes (the first parts of the line, the preceding parts of the time, or the units represented in succession), and did not reproduce them while advancing to what follows, no complete representation, and none of the thoughts just mentioned, would ever arise; indeed, not even the purest and most fundamental representations of space and time.",[]],"krv-a-0222":["The synthesis of apprehension is therefore inseparably bound up with the synthesis of reproduction. And since the former constitutes the transcendental ground of the possibility of all modes of knowledge in general (not only of the empirical, but also of the pure *a priori*), the reproductive synthesis of imagination belongs among the transcendental acts of the mind. With respect to it we shall also entitle this faculty the transcendental faculty of imagination.",[]],"krv-a-0223":["3. The Synthesis of Recognition in the Concept.",[]],"krv-a-0224":["Without consciousness that what we think is the very same as what we thought a moment before, all reproduction in the series of representations would be in vain. For in the present state it would be a new representation, which would not belong at all to the act through which it was to have been gradually generated; and its manifold would never constitute a whole, since it would lack the unity which consciousness alone can give it. If in counting I forget that the units which now hover before my senses have been added by me to one another in succession, I should not know the generation of the quantity through this successive addition of one to one, and consequently should not know the number; for this concept consists solely in the consciousness of this unity of the synthesis.",[]],"krv-a-0225":["The word \"concept\" might of itself suggest this remark. For it is this one consciousness which unites in one representation the manifold that has been successively intuited and then also reproduced. This consciousness may often be only faint, so that we connect it with the generation of the representation only in its effect, and not in the act itself, that is, not immediately with the generation. But notwithstanding these differences, a consciousness must always be present, even if it lacks conspicuous clarity; and without it, concepts, and with them knowledge of objects, are quite impossible.",["- Passages are given in the order requested; each id is repeated once above, with notes by passage below.","- 0214: the long first sentence is divided into three sentences, and the last sentence of the passage into two; \"durch folgende vier Nummern\" rendered \"by the following four numbered sections\" (Nummern).","- 0214: \"Erörterung\" is omitted from the English as \"expound... systematically\" absorbs it; \"Elemente des Verstandes\" kept literally.","- 0216: \"Erkentnisse\" plural rendered \"modes of knowledge\"; \"Modificationen\" as \"modifications\".","- 0217: \"Zusammennehmung\" rendered \"taking together\"; the final sentence is divided and \"diese\" resolved to the manifold.","- 0220: the conditional series is rearranged into two sentences, and \"Gegenstandes\" rendered \"object\". \"Vermögen\" is \"power\" in the first instance and \"faculty\" in \"dead faculty\".","- 0221: \"das Erscheinungen\" is read as \"daß Erscheinungen\" (obvious slip); \"Einbildung/Einbildungskraft\" rendered \"imagination\".","- 0225: \"un\" read as \"und\" (OCR slip); \"Actus\" rendered \"act\"; \"hervorstechende Klarheit\" as \"conspicuous clarity\"."]],"krv-a-0226":["Here it is necessary that we make clear to ourselves what is meant by the expression \"an object of representations\". We have said above that appearances themselves are nothing but sensible representations, which, as such and in the same manner, must not be regarded as objects outside the power of representation. What, then, is understood when we speak of an object corresponding to, and therefore also distinct from, our knowledge? It is easy to see that this object must be thought only as something in general = X, since outside our knowledge we have nothing which we could set over against this knowledge as corresponding to it.",["- \"Vorstellungskraft\" rendered \"power of representation\"; Kant's sense is the representing faculty, not a thing outside it.","- \"in eben derselben Art\" is elliptical; rendered \"as such and in the same manner\" (i.e. as representations, not as objects), a judgment.","- \"Erkenntniß\" = knowledge; the text reads \"Erkentniß\" (A spelling), silently normalised."]],"krv-a-0227":["We find, however, that our thought of the relation of all knowledge to its object carries with it an element of necessity. For the object is regarded as that which stands in the way of our modes of knowledge being determined at haphazard or arbitrarily, and ensures that they are determined *a priori* in a certain manner. Since they are to relate to an object, they must also necessarily agree with one another in relation to it; that is, they must possess that unity which constitutes the concept of an object.",["- Restated the single long sentence as three; \"dawider ist, daß … nicht\" resolved as \"stands in the way of … being\" (the negation is pleonastic).","- \"Erkentnisse\" (plural) = \"modes of knowledge\"."]],"krv-a-0228":["It is clear, however, that we have to deal only with the manifold of our representations, and that the X which corresponds to them (the object), being something distinct from all our representations, is for us nothing. The unity which the object makes necessary can therefore be nothing other than the formal unity of consciousness in the synthesis of the manifold of representations. We then say that we know the object when we have effected synthetic unity in the manifold of intuition. But such unity is impossible if the intuition could not have been generated, in accordance with a rule, by such a function of synthesis as makes the reproduction of the manifold *a priori* necessary, and renders possible a concept in which the manifold is united. Thus we think a triangle as an object when we are conscious of the combination of three straight lines according to a rule by which such an intuition can at any time be exhibited. This unity of rule determines all the manifold, and limits it to conditions which make the unity of apperception possible; and the concept of this unity is the representation of the object = X, which I think through the predicates of a triangle here mentioned.",["- Divided the opening sentence's \"da … und … weil\" structure; \"vor uns nichts ist\" rendered \"is for us nothing\".","- \"die gedachte Prädicate\" rendered \"the predicates of a triangle here mentioned\" (\"gedacht\" as \"thought of/mentioned\"); a judgment.","- \"dargestelt\" = \"exhibited\" (Latin *exhibere* parallel)."]],"krv-a-0229":["All knowledge requires a concept, however imperfect or obscure that concept may be. But a concept is, in its form, always something universal, and serves as a rule. Thus the concept of body, in accordance with the unity of the manifold which is thought through it, serves as a rule for our knowledge of outer appearances. But it can be a rule of intuitions only in so far as it represents, in given appearances, the necessary reproduction of their manifold, and therefore the synthetic unity in our consciousness of them. Thus the concept of body, when we perceive something outside us, makes necessary the representation of extension, and with it those of impenetrability, shape, and so forth.",["- \"Alles Erkentniß\" kept as \"All knowledge\" (grammatically irregular in the text; sense clear).","- \"Wahrnehmung von Etwas ausser uns\" = \"perceive something outside us\"."]],"krv-a-0230":["A transcendental condition always underlies all necessity. There must therefore be found a transcendental ground of the unity of consciousness in the synthesis of the manifold of all our intuitions, and consequently also of the concepts of objects in general, and so of all objects of experience; without it, it would be impossible to think any object for our intuitions. For the object is nothing more than that something of which the concept expresses such a necessity of synthesis.",["- \"Nothwendig\" in the last clause is evidently a slip for \"Nothwendigkeit\" (OCR or original); silently corrected.","- \"Obiecte\" and \"Gegenstände\" both rendered \"objects\"; the German distinction is not kept."]],"krv-a-0231":["This original and transcendental condition is no other than transcendental apperception. Consciousness of oneself, in accordance with the determinations of our state, in inner perception is merely empirical, always changing; there can be no fixed or abiding self in this flux of inner appearances. It is usually entitled inner sense, or empirical apperception. That which has necessarily to be represented as numerically identical cannot be thought as such through empirical data. There must be a condition which precedes all experience and makes experience itself possible, and which is to make such a transcendental presupposition valid.",["- The final relative clause (\"welche … geltend machen soll\") is loose in the German; I attach it to \"condition\" and keep its awkwardness minimal.","- \"Bestimmungen\" = determinations; \"Wahrnehmung\" = perception."]],"krv-a-0232":["Now no modes of knowledge can take place in us, no connection or unity of them with one another, apart from that unity of consciousness which precedes all data of intuitions, and in relation to which alone all representation of objects is possible. This pure, original, unchangeable consciousness I shall entitle transcendental apperception. That it deserves this name is evident from the fact that even the purest objective unity, namely that of the concepts *a priori* (space and time), is possible only through the relation of the intuitions to it. The numerical unity of this apperception therefore underlies all concepts *a priori*, just as the manifoldness of space and time underlies the intuitions of sensibility.",["- \"und, worauf in Beziehung, …\" rendered \"and in relation to which\"; the antecedent is the unity of consciousness.","- \"Begriffe a priori (Raum und Zeit)\" is Kant's wording as printed; kept as \"concepts\".","- \"seyn\" (plural subjunctive) after \"daß … nur … möglich seyn\" rendered as a plain \"is possible\"."]],"krv-a-0233":["This same transcendental unity of apperception, however, forms out of all possible appearances, which can stand together in one experience, a connection of all these representations according to laws. For this unity of consciousness would be impossible if the mind, in the knowledge of the manifold, could not become conscious of the identity of the function whereby it synthetically combines the manifold in one knowledge. The original and necessary consciousness of the identity of the self is therefore at the same time a consciousness of an equally necessary unity of the synthesis of all appearances according to concepts, that is, according to rules. These rules not only make the appearances necessarily reproducible, but also thereby determine an object for their intuition, that is, the concept of something in which they are necessarily connected. For the mind could not possibly think the identity of itself in the manifoldness of its representations, and that *a priori*, if it did not have before its eyes the identity of its act, which subjects all synthesis of apprehension (which is empirical) to a transcendental unity, and first makes possible their connection according to rules *a priori*.\n\nWe shall now also be in a position to determine more correctly our concept of an object in general. All representations, as representations, have their object, and can themselves in turn be objects of other representations. Appearances are the only objects which can be given to us immediately, and that in them which relates immediately to the object is entitled intuition. These appearances, however, are not things in themselves, but are themselves only representations, which in turn have their object; and this object cannot be intuited by us any further, and may therefore be named the non-empirical, that is, transcendental object = X.",["- Split the long sentence at \"denn\" and relative \"die sie … machen\"; \"sich … bewust\" and \"seiner selbst\" rendered \"the self\"/\"itself\", the antecedent being the mind (*Gemüt*, here \"mind\").","- \"unterwirst\" is an evident misprint for \"unterwirft\"; silently corrected.","- \"sie\" in \"wodurch sie dasselbe …\" (A 108) refers to the mind (das Gemüth); rendered \"it\".","- \"Bewustseyn seiner selbst\" kept as \"consciousness of the identity of the self\"."]],"krv-a-0234":["The pure concept of this transcendental object (which in all our modes of knowledge is really always one and the same = X) is that which in all our empirical concepts in general can provide relation to an object, that is, objective reality. Now this concept cannot contain any determinate intuition. It will therefore concern nothing but that unity which must be met with in a manifold of knowledge, in so far as the manifold stands in relation to an object. This relation is nothing other than the necessary unity of consciousness, and therefore also of the synthesis of the manifold, through a common function of the mind which combines it in one representation. Now since this unity must be regarded as necessary *a priori* (for otherwise knowledge would be without an object), the relation to a transcendental object, that is, the objective reality of our empirical knowledge, rests on the transcendental law that all appearances, in so far as objects are to be given to us through them, must stand under *a priori* rules of their synthetic unity. Only in accordance with these rules is their relation in empirical intuition possible. That is to say, in experience they must stand under the conditions of the necessary unity of apperception, just as in mere intuition they must stand under the formal conditions of space and time. Indeed, it is through the former that all knowledge is first made possible.",[]],"krv-a-0235":["4. Preliminary Explanation of the Possibility of the Categories, as Modes of Knowledge *a priori*.",[]],"krv-a-0236":["There is only one experience, in which all perceptions are represented as in thoroughgoing and orderly connection; just as there is only one space and one time, in which all forms of appearance and all relation of being or non-being take place. When we speak of different experiences, they are only so many perceptions, in so far as these belong to one and the same universal experience. For the thoroughgoing synthetic unity of perceptions is precisely what constitutes the form of experience, and it is nothing other than the synthetic unity of appearances according to concepts.",[]],"krv-a-0237":["Unity of synthesis according to empirical concepts would be altogether contingent. If these concepts were not grounded on a transcendental ground of unity, it would be possible for a throng of appearances to fill our soul without experience ever being able to arise from it. But in that case all relation of knowledge to objects would also fall away, since it would lack connection according to universal and necessary laws. Knowledge would then be intuition without thought, but never knowledge proper, and so for us as good as nothing.",[]],"krv-a-0238":["The *a priori* conditions of a possible experience in general are at the same time conditions of the possibility of the objects of experience. Now I maintain that the categories just adduced are nothing other than the conditions of thought in a possible experience, just as space and time contain the conditions of intuition for that same experience. Those categories are therefore also fundamental concepts by which we think objects in general for appearances, and so have objective validity *a priori*. This is just what we really wished to know.",[]],"krv-a-0239":["The possibility, indeed even the necessity, of these categories rests on the relation which the whole of sensibility, and with it all possible appearances, has to original apperception. In original apperception everything must necessarily conform to the conditions of the thoroughgoing unity of self-consciousness; that is, everything must stand under universal functions of synthesis, namely synthesis according to concepts, in which alone apperception can prove its thoroughgoing and necessary identity *a priori*. Thus the concept of a cause is nothing other than a synthesis (of that which follows in the time-series with other appearances) according to concepts. Without a unity of this kind, which has its rule *a priori* and subjects the appearances to itself, thoroughgoing and universal, and therefore necessary, unity of consciousness would not be met with in the manifold of perceptions. These perceptions would then belong to no experience, would consequently be without an object, and would be nothing but a blind play of representations, that is, less than a dream.",[]],"krv-a-0240":["All attempts to derive those pure concepts of the understanding from experience, and to ascribe to them a merely empirical origin, are therefore quite vain and futile. I shall not mention, for instance, that the concept of a cause carries with it a mark of necessity, which no experience can yield. Experience teaches us that something else customarily follows upon an appearance, but not that it must necessarily follow, nor that we could infer *a priori* and quite universally from the appearance, as from a condition, to the consequence. But what of that empirical rule of association, which we must assume throughout when we say that everything in the series of events stands so completely under rules that nothing ever happens before which something has not preceded, upon which it invariably follows? On what, I ask, does this, as a law of nature, rest? And how is this association itself possible? The ground of the possibility of the association of the manifold, in so far as the manifold lies in the object, is entitled the affinity of the manifold. I ask, then, how do you make the thoroughgoing affinity of appearances (whereby they stand under constant laws, and must belong under them) comprehensible to yourselves?",[]],"krv-a-0241":["On my principles it is quite comprehensible. All possible appearances belong, as representations, to the whole of possible self-consciousness. Numerical identity is inseparable from self-consciousness, as a transcendental representation, and is certain *a priori*, since nothing can enter into knowledge except by means of this original apperception. Now this identity must necessarily enter into the synthesis of all the manifold of appearances, in so far as that synthesis is to yield empirical knowledge. The appearances are therefore subject to *a priori* conditions, to which their synthesis (of apprehension) must conform throughout. Now the representation of a universal condition according to which a certain manifold can be posited (and so in one and the same manner) is entitled a rule; and if the manifold must be so posited, a law. All appearances therefore stand in thoroughgoing connection according to necessary laws, and so in a transcendental affinity, of which the empirical affinity is the mere consequence.",[]],"krv-a-0242":["That nature should direct itself according to our subjective ground of apperception, and even depend upon it in respect of its conformity to law, sounds very absurd and strange. But if we reflect that this nature is in itself nothing but a sum of appearances, and so not a thing in itself but merely a multitude of representations of the mind, we shall not be surprised to see it solely in the radical faculty of all our knowledge, namely transcendental apperception, in that unity on account of which alone it can be entitled object of all possible experience, that is, nature. Nor shall we be surprised that for this very reason we can know this unity *a priori*, and so as necessary. This we should certainly have to forgo, were the unity given in itself, independently of the first sources of our thought. For in that case I do not know where we should find the synthetic propositions of such a universal unity of nature, since they would have to be borrowed from the objects of nature themselves. This could be done only empirically, and no unity could then be drawn from it save one that is merely contingent, and that falls far short of the necessary connection which we have in mind when we speak of nature.",[]],"krv-a-0243":["Third Section of the Deduction of the Pure Concepts of the Understanding. On the Relation of the Understanding to Objects in General, and the Possibility of Knowing These *a priori*.",["- krv-a-0234: Long final sentence divided into several; \"d. i.\" rendered \"that is\"; \"ia daß durch iene\" read as referring to the conditions of apperception (the former), a judgment call.","- krv-a-0234: Erkentnissen = \"modes of knowledge\" in the plural; Erkentniß = knowledge.","- krv-a-0235: Heading Erkentnissen rendered \"Modes of Knowledge\".","- krv-a-0237: \"gedankenlose Anschauung\" rendered \"intuition without thought\"; \"Erkentniß\" in the last clause supplied \"proper\" in my English only for contrast with \"intuition\".","- krv-a-0238: \"Obiecte überhaupt zu den Erscheinungen zu denken\" rendered \"think objects in general for appearances\"; \"wissen wollten\" rendered \"wished to know\".","- krv-a-0239: Sentence on original apperception split in two; \"gemäß seyn, d. i. unter ... stehen\" rendered with a semicolon.","- krv-a-0240: \"Aber iene empirische Regel...\" recast as a rhetorical question to give the sentence a main clause; \"heißt\" = \"is entitled\".","- krv-a-0242: Text breaks off without final punctuation (\"nennt\"); I supplied a period. \"Radicalvermögen\" = \"radical faculty\"; \"unterwegens lassen\" = \"forgo\"."]],"krv-a-0244":["What we presented separately and singly in the preceding section we shall now present in unity and connection. There are three subjective sources of knowledge upon which the possibility of experience in general, and of knowledge of its objects, rests: sense, imagination, and apperception. Each of these can be viewed as empirical, namely in its application to given appearances. But all of them are also elements or foundations *a priori*, which make this empirical employment itself possible. Sense represents appearances empirically in perception; imagination, in association (and reproduction); apperception, in the empirical consciousness of the identity of these reproductive representations with the appearances through which they were given, that is, in recognition.",["- Erkenntnisquellen = \"sources of knowledge\"; Sinn = \"sense\"; Einbildungskraft = \"imagination\"; Wahrnehmung = \"perception\".","- The final clause (\"mithin in der Recognition\") is rendered \"that is, in recognition\" for the sake of clarity."]],"krv-a-0245":["But all perception is grounded *a priori* upon pure intuition (in so far as perception is representation, upon the form of inner intuition, time); association is grounded upon the pure synthesis of imagination; and empirical consciousness upon pure apperception, that is, upon the thoroughgoing identity of the self in all possible representations.",["- \"in Ansehung ihrer als Vorstellung\" is a compressed parenthesis; I read \"ihrer\" as referring to perception (Wahrnehmung) and expanded it to \"in so far as perception is representation\".","- \"seiner selbst\": rendered \"of the self\"; \"d. i.\" = \"that is\"."]],"krv-a-0246":["If now we are to trace the inner ground of this connection of representations to the point in which they must all converge, so as to obtain there for the first time unity of knowledge for a possible experience, we must begin with pure apperception. All intuitions are nothing to us, and do not in the least concern us, if they cannot be taken up into consciousness, whether they influence it directly or indirectly; and only through consciousness is knowledge possible. We are conscious *a priori* of the thoroughgoing identity of the self in respect of all representations which can ever belong to our knowledge, as a necessary condition of the possibility of all representations. [For representations represent something in me only in so far as they belong with all the others to one consciousness, and so must at least be capable of being connected in it.] This principle holds *a priori*, and may be entitled the transcendental principle of the unity of all the manifold of our representations (and so also of the manifold in intuition). Now the unity of the manifold in a subject is synthetic. Pure apperception therefore supplies a *principium* of the synthetic unity of the manifold in all possible intuition.*)",["- The long second sentence was divided; the parenthesis \"(weil diese in mir …)\" is set in square brackets to mark my recasting of it as a separate sentence, though the German has round brackets.","- \"vor uns\" = \"to us\" (für uns); \"nur durch dieses allein\": \"dieses\" taken as Bewusstsein, hence \"only through consciousness\".","- \"heissen\" = \"is entitled\". *Principium* kept in Latin as in the text. The footnote marker follows the German."]],"krv-a-0247":["[Footnote:] Close attention should be given to this proposition, which is of great importance. All representations have a necessary relation to a possible empirical consciousness; for if they did not have it, and if it were altogether impossible to become conscious of them, this would amount to saying that they did not exist at all. But all empirical consciousness has a necessary relation to a transcendental consciousness (one that precedes all particular experience), namely the consciousness of myself as original apperception. It is therefore absolutely necessary that in my knowledge all consciousness should belong to one consciousness (that of myself). Here, then, we have a synthetic unity of the manifold (of consciousness) which is known *a priori*, and which provides the ground for synthetic propositions *a priori* concerning pure thought, just as space and time provide the ground for such propositions as concern the form of mere intuition. The synthetic proposition that all the diverse empirical consciousness must be combined in a single self-consciousness is the absolutely first and synthetic principle of our thinking in general. It must not be overlooked, however, that the mere representation \"I\" is, in relation to all other representations (the collective unity of which it makes possible), the transcendental consciousness. Whether this representation is clear (empirical consciousness) or obscure does not matter here, nor even whether it is actual. The possibility of the logical form of all knowledge rests necessarily upon the relation to this apperception as a faculty.",["- \"Hier ist nun eine synthetische Einheit … die … abgiebt\" is recast with \"we have … which … provides\"; \"(Bewustseyns)\" kept in brackets as in the German.","- \"transsendentale\" in the German is a misprint for \"transscendentale\"; silently corrected. \"einigen\" = \"single\".","- \"Grundsatz\" rendered \"principle\" (the Latin *principium* is not used here).","- \"daran liegt hier nichts, ia nicht einmal an der Wirklichkeit desselben\" is smoothed into \"does not matter here, nor even whether it is actual\".","- \"als einem Vermögen\": \"faculty\" chosen."]],"krv-a-0248":["This synthetic unity presupposes a synthesis, or includes it; and if the former is to be necessary *a priori*, the latter must also be a synthesis *a priori*. Thus the transcendental unity of apperception is related to the pure synthesis of imagination, as an *a priori* condition of the possibility of all composition of the manifold in one knowledge. But only the productive synthesis of imagination can take place *a priori*; the reproductive rests upon empirical conditions. The principle of the necessary unity of the pure (productive) synthesis of imagination, prior to apperception, is therefore the ground of the possibility of all knowledge, especially of experience.",["- \"iene … leztere\" = \"the former … the latter\", referring to unity and synthesis respectively.","- \"vor der Apperception\" is ambiguous; I take it as \"prior to apperception\" (in the order of conditions), not \"before the eyes of\".","- \"transsc.\" expanded to \"transcendental\"."]],"krv-a-0249":["Now we entitle the synthesis of the manifold in imagination transcendental if, without distinction of intuitions, it is concerned with nothing but the *a priori* combination of the manifold; and the unity of this synthesis is entitled transcendental if it is represented as necessary *a priori* in relation to the original unity of apperception. Since this latter unity underlies the possibility of all modes of knowledge, the transcendental unity of the synthesis of imagination is the pure form of all possible knowledge. Through it, therefore, all objects of possible experience must be represented *a priori*.",["- \"nennen … heißt\" both rendered \"entitle … is entitled\".","- \"Erkentnisse\" (plural) = \"modes of knowledge\"; \"Erkentniß\" singular = \"knowledge\".","- \"diese leztere\" taken as the original unity of apperception (not the synthesis)."]],"krv-a-0250":["The unity of apperception in relation to the synthesis of imagination is the understanding; and this same unity, in relation to the transcendental synthesis of imagination, is the pure understanding. In the understanding, therefore, there are pure modes of knowledge *a priori* which contain the necessary unity of the pure synthesis of imagination in respect of all possible appearances. These, however, are the categories, that is, pure concepts of the understanding. Consequently the empirical faculty of knowledge in man necessarily contains an understanding which relates to all objects of the senses, though only by means of intuition and its synthesis through imagination. Under this understanding, therefore, all appearances, as data for a possible experience, stand. Now this relation of appearances to possible experience is likewise necessary, since otherwise we should obtain no knowledge through them, and they would not concern us at all. It follows that pure understanding, by means of the categories, is a formal and synthetic *principium* of all experiences, and that appearances have a necessary relation to the understanding.",["- The long sentence beginning \"folglich enthält …\" was divided into three; \"unter welchen\" refers to the understanding (der Verstand), made explicit.","- \"Erkentnißkraft\" = \"faculty of knowledge\"; \"Data\" kept as \"data\". \"Principium\" kept in Latin."]],"krv-a-0251":["We shall now set the necessary connection of the understanding with appearances, by means of the categories, before our eyes by starting from below, that is, from the empirical. The first thing given to us is appearance. When combined with consciousness, it is entitled perception. [Without relation to a consciousness that is at least possible, appearance could never become for us an object of knowledge, and so would be nothing to us; and since it has in itself no objective reality and exists only in knowledge, it would be nothing at all.] But since every appearance contains a manifold, and different perceptions are therefore met with in the mind scattered and single, a combination of them is required, which they cannot have in sense itself. There is thus in us an active faculty for the synthesis of this manifold. To this we give the name imagination, and the action which it exercises immediately upon perceptions I entitle apprehension.*) For imagination has to bring the manifold of intuition into an image; it must therefore first take the impressions up into its activity, that is, apprehend them.",["- The German parenthesis on appearance is set in square brackets to mark its separation as a distinct sentence; the content is unchanged.","- \"vor uns\" = \"to us\" (für uns); \"Gemüthe\" = \"mind\"; \"Vermögen\" = \"faculty\"; \"Bild\" = \"image\".","- \"nenne … nenne\" varied as \"give the name … entitle\" for style; \"Handlung\" = \"action\"."]],"krv-a-0252":["[Footnote:] No psychologist has, I think, ever yet considered that imagination is a necessary ingredient of perception itself. This is because this faculty has been limited partly to reproduction only, and partly because it has been believed that the senses not merely supply us with impressions but also combine them, and so bring about images of objects. For this, beyond the receptivity of impressions, something more is undoubtedly required, namely a function of the synthesis of them.",["- \"Vermögen\" = \"faculty\"; \"Empfänglichkeit\" = \"receptivity\".","- \"wol\" = \"I think\" in the sense of \"probably/surely\"; the German is mildly hedged.","- The two \"theils\" clauses are slightly uneven in German (the second is a \"because\" clause); the English follows with a smoothed parallel."]],"krv-a-0253":["It is clear, however, that even this apprehension of the manifold would by itself produce no image and no connection of the impressions, were there not a subjective ground which leads the mind to recall a perception from which it has passed to another, to the perceptions which follow, and so to exhibit whole series of them. In other words, there must be a reproductive faculty of imagination, and this faculty is itself merely empirical.",["- Restated: the closing \"d. i. ein reproductives Vermögen…\" is made a separate sentence; \"herüber zu rufen\" rendered \"recall … to\".","- \"Vermögen\" = faculty, \"Einbildungskraft\" = imagination, \"Gemüth\" = mind."]],"krv-a-0254":["But if representations reproduced one another in the order in which they happened to come together, without distinction, no determinate connection among them would again arise, but only random heaps of them, and so no knowledge at all. The reproduction of them must therefore follow a rule, in accordance with which a representation enters into connection in the imagination with this representation rather than with another. This subjective and empirical ground of reproduction according to rules is what we entitle the association of representations.",["- \"Erkentniß\" (Kant's spelling) = knowledge. \"nent … die\" rendered \"is what we entitle\".","- The long first sentence is smoothed; \"regellose Haufen\" = \"random heaps\"."]],"krv-a-0255":["Now if this unity of association had not also an objective ground, so that it would be impossible for appearances to be apprehended by the imagination otherwise than under the condition of a possible synthetic unity of this apprehension, it would likewise be entirely contingent that appearances should fit into a connection of human modes of knowledge. For although we should have the faculty of associating perceptions, it would still remain in itself entirely undetermined and contingent whether they are also associable. And in the event that they were not, a multitude of perceptions, and even a whole sensibility, would be possible, in which much empirical consciousness would be met with in my mind, but in a state of separation, and without belonging to a consciousness of myself. This, however, is impossible. For only because I ascribe all perceptions to one consciousness (original apperception) can I say of every perception that I am conscious of it.\n\nThere must therefore be an objective ground, that is, a ground which can be understood a priori, prior to all empirical laws of the imagination, on which rests the possibility, nay the necessity, of a law extending through all appearances. This law requires that we regard them throughout as data of the senses which are in themselves associable and subject to universal rules of a thoroughgoing connection in reproduction. This objective ground of all association of appearances I entitle their affinity. It is to be found nowhere else than in the principle of the unity of apperception, in respect of all modes of knowledge which are to belong to me. In accordance with this principle all appearances, if they are to come into the mind or be apprehended, must throughout so agree as to accord with the unity of apperception. This would be impossible without synthetic unity in their connection, which is therefore also objectively necessary.",["- Divided into two paragraphs at \"Es muß also\"; \"zehle\" read as \"zähle\" (ascribe/count); \"sie nemlich … anzusehen\" restated as a sentence beginning \"This law requires\".","- \"Erkentnisse\" plural = \"modes of knowledge\"; \"Affinität\" = affinity.","- \"so … daß sie zur Einheit … zusammenstimmen\" smoothed; \"welches\" taken to refer to this agreement."]],"krv-a-0256":["The objective unity of all (empirical) consciousness in one consciousness (original apperception) is therefore the necessary condition even of all possible perception, and the affinity of all appearances (near or remote) is a necessary consequence of a synthesis in the imagination which is grounded a priori on rules.",["- \"so gar\" read as \"so gar\" = \"even\"."]],"krv-a-0257":["The imagination is therefore also a faculty of a priori synthesis, and for this reason we give it the title productive imagination. In so far as it aims, in respect of all the manifold of appearance, at nothing further than the necessary unity in the synthesis of that manifold, it may be entitled the transcendental function of the imagination. It is therefore strange, but nevertheless evident from what has been said, that only by means of this transcendental function of the imagination is even the affinity of appearances possible, and with it association, and through association finally reproduction according to laws, and consequently experience itself. For without it no concepts of objects would ever flow together into one experience.",["- \"Absicht\" rendered \"aims at\"; \"Gegenständen\" = objects; \"transscendentale\" = transcendental.","- Final \"weil\" clause made a separate sentence."]],"krv-a-0258":["For the abiding and unchanging 'I' (of pure apperception) constitutes the correlate of all our representations, in so far as it is at all possible to become conscious of them. All consciousness belongs to an all-embracing pure apperception, just as all sensible intuition, as representation, belongs to a pure inner intuition, namely time. It is this apperception which must be added to pure imagination in order to render its function intellectual. For in itself the synthesis of imagination, although exercised a priori, is always sensible, since it combines the manifold only as it appears in intuition, for instance the figure of a triangle. Through the relation of the manifold to the unity of apperception, concepts are brought about which belong to the understanding, but which can come into being only by means of the imagination, in relation to sensible intuition.",["- \"stehende und bleibende Ich\" = \"abiding and unchanging 'I'\" (a choice for the doublet).","- \"intellectuel\" = intellectual; \"Verhältniß\" = relation."]],"krv-a-0259":["We have thus a pure imagination, as a fundamental faculty of the human soul, which underlies all knowledge a priori. By its means we bring the manifold of intuition, on the one side, into connection with the condition of the necessary unity of pure apperception, on the other. Both extreme ends, namely sensibility and understanding, must necessarily be connected by means of this transcendental function of the imagination; for otherwise the former would yield appearances, but no objects of an empirical knowledge, and therefore no experience.\n\nActual experience, which consists of apprehension, association, (reproduction,) and finally recognition of appearances, contains in this last and highest (of the merely empirical elements of experience) concepts which render possible the formal unity of experience, and with it all objective validity (truth) of empirical knowledge. These grounds of the recognition of the manifold, in so far as they concern solely the form of an experience in general, are the categories. On them is therefore based all formal unity in the synthesis of imagination, and by means of this synthesis also all its empirical employment (in recognition, reproduction, association, apprehension), down to the appearances. For only by means of these elements of knowledge can appearances belong at all to our consciousness, and therefore to ourselves.",["- Divided into two paragraphs at \"Die wirkliche Erfahrung\"; the last sentence's \"weil\" clause recast as a separate sentence.","- \"Seele\" = soul; \"Recognition\" kept as recognition."]],"krv-a-0260":["The order and regularity in the appearances, which we entitle nature, we ourselves introduce. We could never find them there, had we not ourselves, or the nature of our mind, originally placed them there. For this unity of nature is to be a necessary unity, that is, one certain a priori, of the connection of appearances. But how could we establish a priori a synthetic unity, if subjective grounds of such unity were not contained a priori in the original sources of knowledge in our mind, and if these subjective conditions were not at the same time objectively valid, as being the grounds of the possibility of knowing an object in experience at all?",["- \"auf die Bahn bringen\" = \"establish\" (literally, set on its course).","- The final clause, ungrammatically a statement in German (\"seyn\" with period), is rendered as a question per the opening \"Wie sollten wir\".","- \"Erkenntnißquellen\" = \"sources of knowledge\"."]],"krv-a-0261":["We have above explained the understanding in various ways: as a spontaneity of knowledge (in contrast to the receptivity of sensibility), as a faculty of thinking, or again as a faculty of concepts, or of judgments. These explanations, when closely examined, come to one and the same thing. We can now characterise the understanding as the faculty of rules. This mark is more fruitful, and approaches nearer to the essential nature of the understanding. Sensibility gives us forms (of intuition); the understanding gives us rules. The latter is always busied with scrutinising the appearances, in order to find in them some rule. Rules, so far as they are objective [and therefore necessarily attach to the knowledge of the object], are entitled laws. Although we learn many laws through experience, these are only special determinations of still higher laws; and the highest of them, under which all the others stand, originate a priori in the understanding itself. They are not borrowed from experience; on the contrary, they must bestow upon appearances their conformity to law, and by so doing make experience possible. The understanding is thus not merely a faculty for framing rules through comparison of appearances; it is itself the legislation for nature. That is to say, without understanding there would be no nature at all, that is, no synthetic unity of the manifold of appearances according to rules. For appearances, as such, cannot occur outside us; they exist only in our sensibility. Sensibility, however, as the object of knowledge in an experience, with all that it may contain, is possible only in the unity of apperception. But the unity of apperception is the transcendental ground of the necessary conformity to law of all appearances in one experience. This same unity of apperception, in respect of a manifold of representations (that is, in determining the manifold out of a single representation), is the rule; and the faculty of these rules is the understanding. All appearances, as possible experiences, therefore lie *a priori* in the understanding, and receive from it their formal possibility, just as, as mere intuitions, they lie in sensibility and through it are possible, so far as their form is concerned, and in no other way.",[]],"krv-a-0262":["However exaggerated and absurd it may sound to say that the understanding is itself the source of the laws of nature, and therefore of the formal unity of nature, such an assertion is none the less correct, and is in agreement with its object, namely experience. Empirical laws, as such, can in no way derive their origin from the pure understanding, any more than the immeasurable manifoldness of appearances can be adequately comprehended from the pure form of sensible intuition. But all empirical laws are only special determinations of the pure laws of the understanding; it is under these laws, and in accordance with their norm, that the empirical laws first become possible, and that appearances assume a lawful form. In the same way, all appearances, notwithstanding the diversity of their empirical form, must always be in conformity with the conditions of the pure form of sensibility.",[]],"krv-a-0263":["In the categories, therefore, the pure understanding is the law of the synthetic unity of all appearances, and thereby first and originally makes experience, so far as its form is concerned, possible. In the transcendental deduction of the categories we had no more to accomplish than this: to render comprehensible the relation of the understanding to sensibility, and by means of sensibility to all objects of experience, and so the objective validity of its pure *a priori* concepts, and thereby to establish their origin and truth.",[]],"krv-a-0264":["Summary Representation of the Correctness of this Deduction of the Pure Concepts of the Understanding, and of the Fact that It Is the Only Deduction Possible.",[]],"krv-a-0265":["If the objects with which our knowledge has to deal were things in themselves, we could have no *a priori* concepts of them. For whence could we obtain them? If we derive them from the object (leaving aside here the question how the object could become known to us), our concepts would be merely empirical, not *a priori* concepts. If we derive them from ourselves, then what is merely in us cannot determine the character of an object distinct from our representations; that is, it cannot be a ground why there should be a thing to which something such as we have in thought belongs, rather than that all this representation should be empty. If, on the other hand, we have to deal throughout only with appearances, it is not merely possible but necessary that certain *a priori* concepts should precede the empirical knowledge of objects. For as appearances they constitute an object which is merely in us, since a mere modification of our sensibility is not to be met with outside us at all. Now this very representation, that all these appearances, and consequently all objects with which we can occupy ourselves, are one and all in me, that is, are determinations of my identical self, expresses, as necessary, a thoroughgoing unity of them in one and the same apperception. But in this unity of possible consciousness there also consists the form of all knowledge of objects, [the form] through which the manifold is thought as belonging to a single object. The manner, therefore, in which the manifold of sensible representation (intuition) belongs to one consciousness precedes all knowledge of the object as the intellectual form of that knowledge, and itself constitutes a formal knowledge of all objects *a priori*, so far as they are thought (categories). The synthesis of the manifold through pure imagination, and the unity of all representations in relation to original apperception, precede all empirical knowledge. Pure concepts of the understanding are therefore possible *a priori*, and, in relation to experience, even necessary, only because our knowledge has to do with nothing but appearances. The possibility of appearances lies in ourselves; their connection and unity (in the representation of an object) are met with only in us; and this connection and unity must therefore precede all experience, and first make it possible, so far as its form is concerned. On this ground, the only possible one among all, our deduction of the categories has been conducted.",[]],"krv-a-0266":["Transcendental Analytic, Book II. The Analytic of Principles.",[]],"krv-a-0267":["General logic is constructed upon a plan which coincides exactly with the division of the higher faculties of knowledge. These are understanding, judgment and reason. In its analytic, that doctrine accordingly treats of concepts, judgments and inferences, in exact correspondence with the functions and order of those powers of mind which are comprehended under the broad title of understanding in general.",[]],"krv-a-0268":["General logic, thus described, is merely formal. It abstracts from all content of knowledge (whether pure or empirical) and is occupied solely with the form of thought (of discursive knowledge) in general. In its analytic part it can therefore also include the canon for reason. The form of reason has its assured prescription, which can be seen *a priori*, by mere analysis of the actions of reason into their moments, without taking into account the particular nature of the knowledge employed in them.",[]],"krv-a-0269":["Transcendental logic is restricted to a determinate content, namely that of the pure modes of knowledge *a priori*, and cannot imitate general logic in this division. For it is found that the transcendental employment of reason is not objectively valid at all. It therefore does not belong to the logic of truth, that is, to the analytic, but as a logic of illusion requires a separate part of the scholastic system, under the name of transcendental dialectic.",[]],"krv-a-0270":["Understanding and judgment therefore have their canon of objectively valid, and so true, employment in transcendental logic, and accordingly belong to its analytic part. Reason, however, in its attempts to establish something a priori regarding objects, and to extend knowledge beyond the limits of possible experience, is altogether dialectical. Its pretended assertions are quite unsuited to any canon, such as the analytic is supposed to contain.",["- 0261: \"Erkentniß\" = knowledge; \"Bestimmungen\" = determinations. Parentheses recast as brackets or clauses; the sentence \"Diese aber, als Gegenstand der Erkentniß…\" is read with \"Diese\" = Sinnlichkeit as the preceding sentence suggests, though the antecedent is ambiguous (possibly \"Erscheinungen\"; flagged).","- 0261: \"Jezt können wir ihn…\" and \"ihn/desselben\" resolved as the understanding; \"dieser\" in \"Dieser ist iederzeit geschäftig\" = der Verstand, rendered \"the latter\".","- 0261: \"vor die Natur\" read as \"für die Natur\"; \"beym lichten besieht\" rendered \"closely examined\".","- 0262: Final sentence loosely joined to the preceding by \"so wie auch\"; I split it and supplied \"In the same way\".","- 0263: \"transsc.\" expanded to \"transcendental\"; final sentence's long infinitive chain kept but \"und so\" supplied for \"mithin\".","- 0265: Split the long sentence \"Nun drükt selbst diese Vorstellung… aus\"; bracketed \"the form\" supplied in \"[the form] through which\". \"Obiect\" = object; \"Einbildungskraft\" = imagination.","- 0268: The German \"Da gedachte blos formale Logik…\" restated as two sentences; \"gedachte\" rendered \"thus described\". Subject of \"deren Form\" (reason) made explicit.","- 0269–0270: \"Erkentnisse\" plural = \"modes of knowledge\"; \"Scheinbehauptungen\" = \"pretended assertions\"; \"Gemüthskräfte\" (0267) = \"powers of mind\"."]],"krv-a-0271":["The Analytic of Principles will accordingly be solely a canon for the faculty of judgment, instructing it how to apply to appearances the concepts of the understanding, which contain the condition for rules *a priori*. For this reason, in taking the actual principles of the understanding as my theme, I shall make use of the title \"a doctrine of the faculty of judgment\", which designates this task more precisely.",[]],"krv-a-0272":["Introduction. Of the Transcendental Faculty of Judgment in General.",[]],"krv-a-0273":["If understanding in general is defined as the faculty of rules, the faculty of judgment is the faculty of subsuming under rules, that is, of distinguishing whether something does or does not stand under a given rule (*casus datae legis*). General logic contains no precepts for the faculty of judgment, and cannot contain any. For since it abstracts from all content of knowledge, nothing remains to it but the task of analytically resolving the mere form of knowledge into concepts, judgments and inferences, and so of establishing formal rules for all employment of the understanding. Should it then attempt to show in general how we are to subsume under these rules, that is, to distinguish whether something stands under them or not, it could do so only by means of a further rule. But this rule, just because it is a rule, again demands guidance from the faculty of judgment. And thus it appears that while the understanding is capable of being instructed and equipped through rules, the faculty of judgment is a special talent, which can be practised only, and cannot be taught. It is therefore the distinguishing feature of so-called mother-wit, the lack of which no schooling can make good. For though a school can offer to a limited understanding an abundance of rules, borrowed from the insight of others, and can, as it were, graft them upon it, the capacity to make correct use of them must belong to the pupil himself; and in the absence of such a natural gift no rule that may be prescribed to him for this purpose is secure against misuse.*\n\nThus a physician, a judge or a statesman may have in his head many fine pathological, juridical or political rules, to such a degree that he may himself become a thorough teacher of them, and yet in applying them may easily go wrong. The reason may be that he is wanting in natural power of judgment (though not in understanding); he can see the universal *in abstracto*, but cannot distinguish whether a case *in concreto* comes under it. Or the reason may be that he has not been sufficiently trained for such judgment through examples and actual practice. This is, indeed, the sole and great benefit of examples: that they sharpen the faculty of judgment. For as regards the correctness and precision of insight on the part of the understanding, they commonly do it rather some harm. They only seldom fulfil the condition of the rule adequately (as *casus in terminis*); and besides, they often weaken that effort of the understanding by which rules are to be apprehended in their generality and in their sufficiency, independently of the particular circumstances of experience; and so in the end they accustom us to use rules more as formulas than as principles. Examples are thus the go-cart of the faculty of judgment; and he who lacks the natural talent for it can never dispense with them.",[]],"krv-a-0274":["* Lack of judgment is properly what is called stupidity, and for such a failing there is no remedy. A dull or narrow-minded person, who is wanting in nothing but the proper degree of understanding and concepts of his own, may very well be equipped through instruction, even to the point of learning. But since in such cases the former lack [of judgment] (the *secunda Petri*) commonly accompanies it, it is not unusual to meet very learned men who, in the use of their science, frequently betray that incurable defect.",[]],"krv-a-0275":["But though general logic can give no precepts to the faculty of judgment, the case is quite otherwise with transcendental logic. Indeed it seems that the latter has as its proper task to correct and secure the faculty of judgment, by means of determinate rules, in the employment of the pure understanding. For as a doctrine, that is, as a means of extending the understanding in the field of pure modes of knowledge *a priori*, philosophy seems to be quite unnecessary, or rather ill-applied, since after all attempts hitherto made little or no ground has been won by it. As criticism, however, in order to guard against the false steps of the faculty of judgment (*lapsus iudicii*) in employing the few pure concepts of the understanding which we possess, philosophy is called in, with all its acuteness and art of examination, although the benefit is then only negative.",[]],"krv-a-0276":["Transcendental philosophy, however, has this peculiarity: that besides the rule (or rather the universal condition of rules) which is given in the pure concept of the understanding, it can also indicate *a priori* the instance to which the rule is to be applied. The reason of its superiority in this respect over all other sciences that instruct (with the exception of mathematics) lies just in this: that it deals with concepts which are to relate to their objects *a priori*. Their objective validity cannot therefore be established *a posteriori*, for that would leave their dignity entirely untouched. Transcendental philosophy must at the same time set forth, in general but sufficient marks, the conditions under which objects can be given in agreement with those concepts. Otherwise the concepts would be without all content, and so mere logical forms and not pure concepts of the understanding.",[]],"krv-a-0277":["This transcendental doctrine of the faculty of judgment will now contain two chapters. The first deals with the sensible condition under which alone pure concepts of the understanding can be employed, that is, with the schematism of the pure understanding. The second deals with those synthetic judgments which, under these conditions, issue *a priori* from pure concepts of the understanding and underlie all other modes of knowledge *a priori*, that is, with the principles of the pure understanding.",[]],"krv-a-0278":["Of the Transcendental Doctrine of the Faculty of Judgment (or Analytic of Principles), First Chapter. The Schematism of the Pure Concepts of the Understanding.",[]],"krv-a-0279":["In all subsumptions of an object under a concept, the representation of the object must be homogeneous with the concept; that is, the concept must contain something which is represented in the object that is to be subsumed under it. This, indeed, is what is meant by the expression, \"an object is contained under a concept\". Thus the empirical concept of a plate is homogeneous with the pure geometrical concept of a circle, inasmuch as the roundness which is thought in the former can be intuited in the latter.",[]],"krv-a-0280":["Pure concepts of the understanding, however, when compared with empirical (indeed with sensible) intuitions, are quite heterogeneous, and can never be found in any intuition. How, then, is the subsumption of intuitions under these concepts, and consequently the application of the category to appearances, possible, seeing that no one will say that a category, causality for instance, can also be intuited through the senses and is contained in the appearance? This question, so natural and so weighty, is the real cause which makes a transcendental doctrine of the faculty of judgment necessary. Its task is to show how pure concepts of the understanding can be applied to appearances in general. In all other sciences, where the concepts through which the object is thought in general are not so different from, and so heterogeneous with, those which represent it *in concreto* as it is given, there is no need of any special enquiry into the application of the former to the latter.",[]],"krv-a-0281":["Now it is clear that there must be a third thing, homogeneous on the one hand with the category and on the other with the appearance, which makes the application of the former to the latter possible. This mediating representation must be pure (without anything empirical), and yet on the one hand intellectual, on the other sensible. Such a representation is the transcendental schema.",["- Passages 0273–0274: Kant's long sentences are divided into several; the footnote marker *) is rendered * and the footnote kept separate as 0274.","- \"Urtheilskraft\" is rendered \"faculty of judgment\", following Kemp Smith's own practice; \"Vermögen\" in the same sentences is \"faculty\" or \"capacity\" as the sense requires.","- 0274: \"ienem\" (the former lack) is resolved as lack of judgment; \"[of judgment]\" is supplied. *secunda Petri* is left in Latin, as in the text.","- 0275: \"Erkentnisse\" in the plural is \"modes of knowledge\". The tangled \"Denn, um ... Philosophie ... aufgeboten\" is smoothed into shorter sentences; \"als Doctrin\" and \"als Critik\" are kept as the contrast.","- 0276: \"ienes Dignität\" is rendered \"their dignity\", referring to the concepts' *a priori* standing; \"sie\" before \"muß\" is resolved as transcendental philosophy, which I name in the English.","- 0279: \"mit der leztern\" in Kant's German: \"ersteren\" is the object's representation, \"letztern\" the concept. I name them in the English. \"Teller\" is \"plate\"; the usual Kemp Smith rendering is the same.","- 0280: \"die erste/die lezte\" are resolved as concepts and intuitions respectively, and \"wegen ... Erörterung\" is given as \"special enquiry\". \"Categorie\" is kept as \"category\".","- 0281: \"intellectuel\" is rendered \"intellectual\"; \"Gleichartigkeit\" is \"homogeneous\" throughout."]],"krv-a-0282":["The concept of the understanding contains the pure synthetic unity of the manifold in general. Time, as the formal condition of the manifold of inner sense, and therefore of the connection of all representations, contains a manifold *a priori* in pure intuition. Now a transcendental determination of time is so far homogeneous with the category (which constitutes the unity of such determination) as it is universal and rests on a rule *a priori*. But it is, on the other hand, so far homogeneous with appearance as time is contained in every empirical representation of the manifold. Hence an application of the category to appearances becomes possible by means of the transcendental determination of time, which, as the schema of the concepts of the understanding, mediates the subsumption of the appearances under the category.",["- \"die Einheit derselben\": \"derselben\" taken to refer to the time-determination; I have written \"such determination\".","- \"die Subsumtion der lezteren unter die erste\": the German reads \"the latter under the former\", which, by strict grammar, would reverse the sense. I follow the sense the argument requires (appearances under the category) and supply \"[the] appearances\" and \"the category\" without brackets for readability; the original wording is flagged here.","- \"Verstandesbegriff\" rendered \"concept of the understanding\"; \"Categorie\" kept as \"category\"."]],"krv-a-0283":["After what has been shown in the deduction of the categories, it is to be hoped that no one will hesitate to decide the question whether these pure concepts of the understanding are of merely empirical or also of transcendental employment. That is to say, do they, as conditions of a possible experience, relate *a priori* solely to appearances; or can they, as conditions of the possibility of things in general, be extended to objects in themselves, without any restriction to our sensibility? For we have seen that concepts are quite impossible, and can have no meaning, unless an object is given either for the concepts themselves or at least for the elements of which they consist; and that they cannot, therefore, apply to things in themselves, without regard to whether and how these may be given to us. We have seen, further, that the only manner in which objects are given to us is through modification of our sensibility; and, finally, that pure *a priori* concepts must contain, in addition to the function of the understanding in the category, formal conditions of sensibility (namely of inner sense) *a priori*. These contain the universal condition under which alone the category can be applied to any object. This formal and pure condition of sensibility, to which the concept of the understanding is restricted in its employment, we shall entitle the schema of the concept; and the procedure of the understanding with these schemata we shall entitle the schematism of the pure understanding.",["- The long sentence beginning \"Denn da haben wir gesehen\" is divided into several sentences; the dependent \"daß\" clauses are kept in order.","- \"Modification\" rendered \"modification\"; \"Function\" as \"function\".","- \"wollen … nennen\" rendered \"we shall entitle\", following the method's \"is entitled\".","- \"ob, und wie sie uns gegeben werden mögen\": \"whether and how these may be given\"."]],"krv-a-0284":["The schema is in itself always a product of the imagination. But since the synthesis of the imagination aims at no single intuition, but only at unity in the determination of sensibility, the schema is to be distinguished from the image. Thus, if I place five points one after another, . . . . . this is an image of the number five. If, on the other hand, I think only a number in general, which may be five or a hundred, this thinking is rather the representation of a method of representing a multitude (e.g. a thousand) in an image in conformity with a certain concept, than the image itself. In the latter case I should scarcely be able to survey the image, or to compare it with the concept. This representation of a universal procedure of the imagination in providing a concept with its image I entitle the schema of this concept.",["- Two sentences (\"Dagegen, wenn …\") were smoothed: the relative \"welches\" clause is made a separate sentence.","- \"Einbildungskraft\" rendered \"imagination\"; \"Bild\" \"image\".","- \"Bestimmung der Sinnlichkeit\" retained literally as \"determination of sensibility\"."]],"krv-a-0285":["In actual fact, it is not images of objects but schemata which underlie our pure sensible concepts. No image could ever be adequate to the concept of a triangle in general. It would not attain to the universality of the concept, which makes it valid of all triangles, right-angled, acute-angled, and so forth; it would always be limited to one part only of this sphere. The schema of the triangle can exist nowhere but in thought. It signifies a rule of the synthesis of the imagination in respect of pure figures in space. Still less is an object of experience, or an image of it, ever adequate to the empirical concept. The empirical concept relates always immediately to the schema of the imagination, as a rule for the determination of our intuition in accordance with a certain universal concept. The concept 'dog' signifies a rule according to which my imagination can delineate the figure of a four-footed animal in a general manner, without being restricted to any single particular figure supplied by experience, or to any possible image that I can represent *in concreto*.\n\nThis schematism of our understanding, in its dealing with appearances and their mere form, is an art concealed in the depths of the human soul, whose real modes of activity we are scarcely likely ever to discover from nature and to have open to our gaze. All that we can say is this: the image is a product of the empirical faculty of productive imagination; the schema of sensible concepts (such as figures in space) is a product, and, as it were, a monogram, of pure *a priori* imagination. Through it, and in accordance with it, images first become possible; but they have to be connected with the concept only by means of the schema which they indicate, and are in themselves never completely congruent with it.\n\nThe schema of a pure concept of the understanding, on the other hand, is something that cannot be brought to any image. It is simply the pure synthesis, in accordance with a rule of unity according to concepts in general, which the category expresses. It is a transcendental product of the imagination, and concerns the determination of inner sense in general, according to the conditions of its form (time), in respect of all representations, in so far as these are to be connected together *a priori* in one concept in conformity with the unity of apperception.",["- Divided into three paragraphs at the natural turns of the argument; the single German paragraph is a stylistic restatement.","- \"deren wahre Handgriffe wir der Natur schwerlich iemals abrathen\": \"abrathen\" (to guess/wrest from) rendered \"discover from nature\"; \"and to have open to our gaze\" renders \"unverdeckt vor Augen legen\".","- \"Seele\" rendered \"soul\"; \"Monogram\" kept as \"monogram\".","- \"die sie bezeichnen\": \"which they indicate\" refers to the schema; \"sie\" taken as the images.","- \"sollten\" in the final clause rendered \"are to be\", suggesting purpose."]],"krv-a-0286":["Without dwelling on a dry and tedious analysis of what is required for transcendental schemata of pure concepts of the understanding in general, we shall prefer to present them according to the order of the categories and in connection with these.",["- \"zu … erfordert wird\" rendered \"is required for\"; \"darstellen\" as \"present\"."]],"krv-a-0287":["The pure image of all magnitudes (*quantorum*) for outer sense is space; the pure image of all objects of the senses in general is time. The pure schema of magnitude (*quantitatis*), as a concept of the understanding, is number, which is a representation that comprehends the successive addition of one to one (homogeneous) unit. Number is therefore nothing but the unity of the synthesis of the manifold of a homogeneous intuition in general, a unity due to my generating time itself in the apprehension of the intuition.",["- \"Gegenstände der Sinne\" rendered \"objects of the senses\"; \"Grösse\" \"magnitude\".","- \"Einem zu Einem (gleichartigen)\" rendered \"one to one (homogeneous) unit\"; \"unit\" is supplied for English sense.","- \"dadurch, daß\" rendered \"a unity due to my generating\"; slightly freer than the German."]],"krv-a-0288":["Reality, in the pure concept of the understanding, is that which corresponds to a sensation in general; that, therefore, the concept of which in itself indicates a being (in time). Negation is that the concept of which represents a not-being (in time). The opposition of the two thus arises in the difference of one and the same time, as a time either filled or empty. Since time is only the form of intuition, and therefore of objects as appearances, that in them which corresponds to sensation is the transcendental matter of all objects as things in themselves (thinghood, reality). Now every sensation has a degree or magnitude, by which it can fill the same time, that is, inner sense, in respect of the same representation of an object, more or less, until it ceases in nothing (= 0 = *negatio*). There is therefore a relation and connection between reality and negation, or rather a transition from the one to the other, which makes every reality representable as a quantum. The schema of a reality, as the quantity of something in so far as it fills time, is just this continuous and uniform generation of that reality in time, as we descend in time from the sensation which has a certain degree to its vanishing, or ascend gradually from negation to its magnitude.",["- \"Negation, dessen Begriff …\": the German lacks a verb; I supply \"is that\" for completeness.","- \"ein Verhältniß und Zusammenhang … Uebergang\": \"Daher ist\" rendered \"There is therefore\"; the sentence is divided to separate the transition from the schema.","- \"Sachheit\" rendered \"thinghood\"; \"Quantum\" kept.","- \"o\" read as \"0\" (OCR); \"Gegenstände, als Dinge an sich\" is as printed in A, though Kant's sense is the transcendental matter of objects *as* things in themselves—a notorious crux."]],"krv-a-0289":["The schema of substance is the permanence of the real in time, that is, the representation of the real as a substratum of empirical determination of time in general, and so as something which abides while all else changes. (Time itself does not pass away, but only the existence of what is changeable passes away in it. To time, which is itself unchangeable and abiding, there corresponds in the appearance what is unchangeable in existence, that is, substance; and only in [relation to] substance can the succession and the coexistence of appearances be determined in respect of time.)",["- \"Substratum\" is Kant's own Latin and is kept in italics-free form as in Kemp Smith; \"Zeitbestimmung\" = \"determination of time\".","- \"blos an ihr\": \"only in [relation to] substance\" is supplied to make the sense of *an* plain.","- \"Erscheinungen der Zeit nach\": rendered \"in respect of time\"."]],"krv-a-0290":["The schema of cause, and of the causality of a thing in general, is the real upon which, whenever it is posited at will, something else always follows. It consists, therefore, in the succession of the manifold, in so far as that succession is subject to a rule.",["- \"Caussalität\" rendered \"causality\"; \"nach Belieben\" = \"at will\" (arbitrarily)."]],"krv-a-0291":["The schema of community (reciprocity), that is, of the reciprocal causality of substances in respect of their accidents, is the coexistence of the determinations of the one substance with those of the other, in accordance with a universal rule.",["- \"der Einen … der Anderen\" resolved as \"the one substance … the other\", by the sense of the argument; \"Accidenzen\" = \"accidents\"."]],"krv-a-0292":["The schema of possibility is the agreement of the synthesis of different representations with the conditions of time in general (as, for instance, when opposites cannot exist in one and the same thing at the same time, but only one after the other); it is therefore the determination of the representation of a thing at some time or other.",["- Restated the final clause with a semicolon for smoothness; \"zu irgend einer Zeit\" = \"at some time or other\"."]],"krv-a-0293":["The schema of actuality is existence in a determinate time.",["- \"bestimten\" (sic) read as \"bestimmten\"; \"Wirklichkeit\" = \"actuality\", \"Daseyn\" = \"existence\"."]],"krv-a-0294":["The schema of necessity is the existence of an object at all times.",["- The copula is omitted in the German (\"das Schema der Nothwendigkeit [ist] …\"); supplied silently in English."]],"krv-a-0295":["It is now evident from all this that the schema of each category contains, and makes representable, the following. The schema of magnitude contains the generation (synthesis) of time itself in the successive apprehension of an object. The schema of quality contains the synthesis of sensation (perception) with the representation of time, that is, the filling of time. The schema of relation contains the relation of perceptions to one another at all times (that is, in accordance with a rule of time-determination). Finally, the schema of modality and its categories contains time itself as the correlate of the determination of an object, whether and how it belongs to time. The schemata are thus nothing but *a priori* determinations of time in accordance with rules. These rules relate, in the order of the categories, to the time-series, the time-content, the time-order, and finally the scope of time, in respect of all possible objects.",["- Restated one long sentence as several; the governing verb \"enthalte und vorstellig mache\" is carried through each clause.","- \"Zeitinbegriff\" = \"scope of time\" (sum or totality of time); a choice.","- \"Correlatum\" rendered \"correlate\"; \"Apprehension\" = \"apprehension\"."]],"krv-a-0296":["From this it is now clear that the schematism of the understanding, through the transcendental synthesis of imagination, amounts to nothing else than the unity of all the manifold of intuition in inner sense, and so, indirectly, to the unity of apperception, as the function which corresponds to inner sense (a receptivity). The schemata of the pure concepts of understanding are therefore the true and sole conditions under which these concepts obtain relation to objects, and so significance. The categories, in the end, are accordingly capable only of a possible empirical employment. They serve merely to subject appearances to universal rules of synthesis, by way of grounds of an *a priori* necessary unity (owing to the necessary union of all consciousness in an original apperception), and so to render them fit for thoroughgoing connection in one experience.",["- Divided the final sentence into three. \"Bedeutung\" = \"significance\"; \"Receptivität\" = \"receptivity\"; \"Function\" = \"function\".","- \"Obiecte\" = \"objects\"."]],"krv-a-0297":["But all our modes of knowledge lie within the whole of possible experience, and transcendental truth, which precedes all empirical truth and makes it possible, consists in the universal relation to that experience.",["- \"Erkentnisse\" = \"modes of knowledge\"."]],"krv-a-0298":["It is also evident, however, that although the schemata of sensibility first realise the categories, they nevertheless likewise restrict them, that is, limit them to conditions which lie outside the understanding (namely, in sensibility). Hence the schema is properly only the phenomenon, or the sensible concept of an object, in agreement with the category. (*Numerus est quantitas phaenomenon, sensatio realitas phaenomenon, constans et perdurabile rerum substantia phaenomenon — — aeternitas, necessitas, phaenomena etc.*) Now if we omit a restricting condition, we seem to amplify the concept previously limited. On this view the categories, in their pure significance, apart from all conditions of sensibility, should hold of things in general as they are, whereas their schemata represent them only as they appear. The categories would thus have a significance independent of all schemata and of much wider extent. In actual fact, a significance of the mere unity of representations does remain to the pure concepts of understanding even after abstraction from every sensible condition, but it is a logical significance only. No object, and therefore no significance, is given to these representations such as could yield a concept of an object. Thus substance, for example, if the sensible determination of permanence were left out, would mean nothing more than a something which can be thought as subject (without being a predicate of something else). I can make nothing of this representation, since it does not indicate to me what determinations the thing has which is to count as such a first subject. Without schemata, therefore, the categories are only functions of the understanding for concepts, and do not represent any object. This significance comes to them from sensibility, which realises the understanding in the very act of restricting it.",["- \"so sollten die Categorien …\" is irregular after \"so amplificiren wir\"; smoothed as \"On this view … should hold\", and the following clause restated as a separate sentence.","- \"Phänomenon\" kept as \"phenomenon\"; the Latin parenthesis is left in Latin, italicised, with Kant's dash retained.","- \"denen aber kein Gegenstand … gegeben wird\": \"these representations\" supplied as antecedent.","- \"restringiren\" = \"restrict\"; \"realisiren\" = \"realise\"."]],"krv-a-0299":["Second Chapter of the Transcendental Doctrine of the Faculty of Judgment (or Analytic of Principles): System of All Principles of the Pure Understanding.",["- \"Urtheilskraft\" = \"faculty of judgment\"; \"Grundsätze\" = \"principles\"; \"Hauptstück\" = \"chapter\"."]],"krv-a-0300":["In the preceding chapter we considered the transcendental faculty of judgment only in terms of the universal conditions under which alone it is entitled to employ the pure concepts of understanding for synthetic judgments. Our task now is to exhibit in systematic connection the judgments which the understanding, under this critical caution, actually achieves *a priori*. For this our table of the categories must, without doubt, afford us the natural and secure guidance. For it is the categories whose relation to possible experience must constitute all pure knowledge of the understanding *a priori*, and it is their relation to sensibility in general which will, on that account, exhibit completely and in a system all transcendental principles of the employment of the understanding.",["- \"transseendentale\" (OCR/print slip) read as \"transscendentale\". \"Verstandeserkentniß\" = \"knowledge of the understanding\".","- \"Denn diese sind es eben\": \"diese\" resolved as the categories."]],"krv-a-0301":["*A priori* principles are so entitled not merely because they contain in themselves the grounds of other judgments, but also because they are not themselves grounded in higher and more universal modes of knowledge. This property does not, however, always exempt them from proof. For although the proof cannot be carried further objectively, but rather lies at the foundation of all knowledge of its object, that does not prevent a proof from the subjective sources of the possibility of a knowledge of the object in general from being possible, and indeed necessary; otherwise the proposition would still lie under the gravest suspicion of being a merely surreptitious assertion.",["- \"dieser\" refers to the proof's being carried further, i.e. to the principle; I have rendered \"the proof\" and left the clause close to the German, which is irregular (\"sondern vielmehr alle Erkentniß … zum Grunde liegt\" lacks a clear subject; sense: the principle underlies all knowledge of its object).","- \"erschlichenen\" = \"surreptitious\"."]],"krv-a-0302":["Secondly, we shall confine ourselves solely to those principles which relate to the categories. The principles of the transcendental aesthetic, according to which space and time are the conditions of the possibility of all things as appearances, and likewise the restriction of these principles, namely that they cannot be applied to things in themselves, do not therefore belong to the field of inquiry we have marked out. Similarly the mathematical principles form no part of this system, since they are drawn only from intuition, not from the pure concept of understanding. Their possibility, however, since they are nevertheless synthetic judgments *a priori*, must find a place here. This is not in order to prove their correctness and apodeictic certainty, which they have no need of, but only to make comprehensible, and to deduce, the possibility of such evident modes of knowledge *a priori*.",["- Divided the last sentence of the German into three. \"abgestochenes\" = \"marked out\" (staked off).","- \"Erkentnisse\" = \"modes of knowledge\"; \"apodictische\" = \"apodeictic\"."]],"krv-a-0303":["But we shall also have to speak of the principle of analytic judgments, and this in contrast with that of synthetic judgments, with which we are properly occupied; for this very contrast frees the theory of the latter from all misunderstanding and sets it clearly before our eyes in its own peculiar nature.",["- \"der synthetischen\" read as \"that of synthetic judgments\" (feminine genitive plural referring to Grundsatz/Urtheile by sense); \"mit welchen\" resolved to synthetic judgments."]],"krv-a-0304":["The System of the Principles of the Pure Understanding. First Section: The Supreme Principle of All Analytic Judgments.",["- \"obersten Grundsatz\" = \"supreme principle\"."]],"krv-a-0305":["Whatever the content of our knowledge, and however it may relate to the object, the universal, though merely negative, condition of all our judgments in general is that they do not contradict themselves; otherwise these judgments are in themselves (even apart from any regard to the object) nothing. But even if there is no contradiction in our judgment, it may none the less connect concepts in a manner which the object does not warrant. Or again, it may do so without our being given any ground, either *a priori* or *a posteriori*, which would entitle us to such a judgment. And thus a judgment, notwithstanding that it is free from all inner contradiction, may yet be either false or groundless.",["- Restated the closing clauses as separate sentences; \"Gegenstand ... nicht mit sich bringt\" is rendered \"which the object does not warrant\".","- \"Erkentniß\" = knowledge; \"Obiect\" and \"Gegenstand\" are both rendered \"object\"."]],"krv-a-0306":["The proposition, \"No thing has a predicate that contradicts it\", is entitled the principle of contradiction. It is a universal, though merely negative, criterion of all truth. For that very reason, however, it belongs to logic only. It holds of modes of knowledge merely as modes of knowledge in general, irrespective of their content, and declares that the contradiction completely cancels and destroys them.",["- Divided the sentence; \"Erkentnissen\" rendered \"modes of knowledge\" (plural). \"Satz\" is rendered \"proposition\" in the first clause and \"principle\" for *Satz des Widerspruchs*."]],"krv-a-0307":["But we can also make a positive use of this principle, that is, employ it not merely to banish falsity and error (so far as these rest upon contradiction), but also to know truth. For if the judgment is analytic, whether negative or affirmative, its truth can always be sufficiently known in accordance with the principle of contradiction. What is already contained as a concept in the knowledge of the object, and is thought in it, will always be rightly denied of its opposite; and the concept itself must necessarily be affirmed of the object, since the contrary of the concept would contradict the object.",["- Restated the final sentence in two clauses; \"Widerspiel\" rendered \"opposite\". The syntax of \"wird ... verneinet, ... werden müssen\" is irregular in the German and has been smoothed."]],"krv-a-0308":["We must therefore allow the principle of contradiction to be the universal and completely sufficient principle [*Principium*] of all analytic knowledge. But beyond this its authority and usefulness as a sufficient criterion of truth do not extend. For the fact that no knowledge can run counter to it without destroying itself makes this principle a *conditio sine qua non*, but not a determining ground [*Bestimmungsgrund*] of the truth of our knowledge. Since we are properly concerned only with the synthetic part of our knowledge, we shall indeed always take care never to act contrary to this inviolable principle, but from it we can never expect any information in regard to the truth of knowledge of this kind.",["- \"Bestimmungsgrund\" rendered \"determining ground\", following the standard term \"Bestimmung\" = determination.","- \"Grundsatz\" rendered \"principle\"; Latin *Principium* retained in brackets."]],"krv-a-0309":["There is, however, a formula of this famous principle, which, though void of all content and merely formal, contains a synthesis that has been introduced into it through carelessness and quite unnecessarily. It runs: It is impossible that something should at the same time be and not be. Apart from the fact that apodeictic certainty (expressed in the word \"impossible\") has here been superfluously added, though it must be self-evident from the proposition itself, the proposition is affected by the condition of time. It says, as it were: A thing = A, which is something = B, cannot at the same time be not-B; but it can quite well be both (B as well as not-B) in succession. For example, a man who is young cannot at the same time be old; but the same man can very well be young at one time and not young, that is, old, at another. Now the principle of contradiction, as a merely logical principle, must not limit its assertions to relations of time; and such a formula is therefore quite contrary to its purpose.\n\nThe misunderstanding arises solely from our first separating a predicate of a thing from the concept of it, and afterwards connecting its opposite with this predicate. This yields a contradiction never with the subject, but only with its predicate, which has been synthetically combined with the subject; and even so only when the first and second predicates are posited at the same time. If I say, \"A man who is unlearned is not learned\", the condition \"at the same time\" must be added; for he who is unlearned at one time may quite well be learned at another. But if I say, \"No unlearned man is learned\", the proposition is analytic, since the mark (unlearnedness) now constitutes part of the concept of the subject; and the negative proposition then follows directly from the principle of contradiction, without the condition \"at the same time\" having to be added. This is also the reason why I have above altered its formula in such a way that the nature of an analytic proposition is clearly expressed by it.",["- Divided the long passage into two paragraphs-worth of shorter sentences; the break at \"Der Mißverstand\" is mine.","- \"apodictische\" rendered \"apodeictic\" (Kemp Smith's spelling). \"Zeitverhältnisse\" rendered \"relations of time\".","- \"Ungelahrtheit\" in the original is read as \"Ungelehrtheit\"; \"unlearnedness\"."]],"krv-a-0310":["Second Section of the System of the Principles of Pure Understanding: The Supreme Principle of All Synthetic Judgments.",["- Heading rendered in full; \"Verstandes\" = understanding."]],"krv-a-0311":["The explanation of the possibility of synthetic judgments is a problem with which general logic has nothing to do; indeed it need not even know its name. In a transcendental logic, however, it is the most important of all tasks, and indeed the only one, when the question is of the possibility of synthetic judgments *a priori*, and likewise of the conditions and extent of their validity. For only when this task has been accomplished can transcendental logic fully achieve its purpose, namely, to determine the extent and limits of pure understanding.",["- Restated \"nach Vollendung desselben, kan sie\" with \"desselben\" resolved as the task (Geschäfte), and \"sie\" as transcendental logic."]],"krv-a-0312":["In an analytic judgment I keep to the given concept, in order to establish something regarding it. If the judgment is to be affirmative, I ascribe to the concept only what is already thought in it; if negative, I exclude from it only the opposite of it. In synthetic judgments, however, I have to go outside the given concept, in order to consider in relation to it something quite other than what was thought in it. This relation is therefore never one either of identity or of contradiction; and in it neither the truth nor the error of the judgment can be discovered from the judgment taken by itself.",["- Divided the final relative construction into a separate sentence; \"dem Urtheile an ihm selbst ... angesehen werden kan\" rendered \"discovered from the judgment taken by itself\"."]],"krv-a-0313":["Granted, then, that we must go outside a given concept in order to compare it synthetically with another, a third thing is necessary, in which alone the synthesis of two concepts can arise. What, then, is this third thing, as the medium of all synthetic judgments? It is only a sum-total [*Inbegriff*] in which all our representations are contained, namely, inner sense, and its *a priori* form, time. The synthesis of representations rests upon imagination; their synthetic unity, which is required for judgment, rests upon the unity of apperception. It is here, therefore, that the possibility of synthetic judgments, and, since all three contain the sources of *a priori* representations, the possibility of pure synthetic judgments also, is to be sought. Indeed, on these grounds they will be necessary, if a knowledge of objects is to be possible which rests solely upon the synthesis of representations.",["- \"alle drey\" taken as inner sense, imagination and apperception; I have left this to the sense rather than adding the words.","- \"Einbildungskraft\" = imagination; \"Inbegriff\" = sum-total.","- The sentence \"Hierin wird ... zu suchen seyn\" is irregular in the German (the subject and the parenthetical clause are interwoven); smoothed. \"Quellen\" rendered \"sources\"."]],"krv-a-0314":["If a mode of knowledge is to have objective reality, that is, is to relate to an object and to have meaning and sense in respect of it, the object must be capable of being given in some manner. Otherwise the concepts are empty; we have, it is true, thought by their means, but by this thinking have in fact known nothing; we have merely played with representations. To give an object, if this is not again to be understood as merely mediate, but is to mean the immediate exhibition of the object in intuition, is nothing other than to relate its representation to experience, whether actual or merely possible. Even space and time, however pure these concepts may be from everything empirical, and however certain it is that they are represented in the mind completely *a priori*, would nevertheless be without objective validity, and without sense and meaning, if their necessary employment upon the objects of experience were not shown. Indeed, their representation is a mere schema, which always relates to the reproductive imagination, and this calls up the objects of experience, apart from which they would have no meaning. And so it is with all concepts without distinction.",[]],"krv-a-0315":["The possibility of experience is, then, what gives objective reality to all our modes of knowledge *a priori*. Now experience rests on the synthetic unity of appearances, that is, on a synthesis according to concepts of the object of appearances in general. Without such synthesis appearances would not even be knowledge, but a rhapsody of perceptions. These would not fit together into any context in accordance with the rules of a thoroughly connected (possible) consciousness, and therefore not into the transcendental and necessary unity of apperception. Experience has, therefore, as the foundation of its form, principles *a priori*, namely, universal rules of unity in the synthesis of appearances. The objective reality of these rules, as necessary conditions, can always be shown in experience, and indeed in its very possibility. Apart from this relation, however, synthetic propositions *a priori* are altogether impossible, since they have no third thing, that is, no pure object, in which the synthetic unity of their concepts can establish objective reality.",[]],"krv-a-0316":["Thus although we know so much *a priori* in synthetic judgments regarding space in general, or the figures which the productive imagination describes in it, and know it in such fashion that we actually need no experience for the purpose, this knowledge would still be nothing, but only an occupation with a mere figment of the brain, if space were not to be regarded as the condition of the appearances which constitute the matter of outer experience. Hence those pure synthetic judgments relate, though only mediately, to possible experience, or rather to the possibility of experience itself, and on this alone found the objective validity of their synthesis.",[]],"krv-a-0317":["Since, then, experience, as empirical synthesis, is in its possibility the one kind of knowledge which gives reality to all other synthesis, this latter, as knowledge *a priori*, possesses truth (agreement with the object) only in so far as it contains nothing more than what is necessary to the synthetic unity of experience in general.",[]],"krv-a-0318":["The supreme principle of all synthetic judgments is, therefore, this: every object stands under the necessary conditions of the synthetic unity of the manifold of intuition in a possible experience.",[]],"krv-a-0319":["In this manner synthetic judgments *a priori* are possible, when we relate the formal conditions of intuition *a priori*, the synthesis of imagination, and the necessary unity of this synthesis in a transcendental apperception, to a possible knowledge of experience in general, and declare: the conditions of the possibility of experience in general are at the same time conditions of the possibility of the objects of experience, and for this reason have objective validity in a synthetic judgment *a priori*.",[]],"krv-a-0320":["Third Section of the System of the Principles of Pure Understanding. Systematic Representation of All the Synthetic Principles of Pure Understanding.",[]],"krv-a-0321":["That principles are to be found anywhere is to be ascribed solely to the pure understanding. It is not only the faculty of rules in respect of what happens, but is itself the source of principles, according to which everything that can be presented to us as an object stands necessarily under rules; for without such rules appearances could never yield knowledge of an object corresponding to them. Even laws of nature, when they are viewed as principles of the empirical employment of the understanding, carry with them an expression of necessity, and therefore at least the presumption of a determination from grounds which are valid *a priori* and antecedently to all experience. But all laws of nature, without distinction, stand under higher principles of the understanding, which they merely apply to particular cases of appearance. These principles alone, therefore, supply the concept which contains the condition, and as it were the exponent, of a rule in general; experience, on the other hand, supplies the case which stands under the rule.",[]],"krv-a-0322":["There can really be no danger of our regarding merely empirical principles as principles of pure understanding, or conversely. The necessity according to concepts, which distinguishes the latter, and the lack of which is easily perceived in every empirical proposition, however universally it may hold, can readily prevent this confusion. There are, however, pure principles *a priori* which I should nevertheless not care to ascribe specifically to the pure understanding, because they are derived not from pure concepts but from pure intuitions (though by means of the understanding); understanding is the faculty of concepts. Mathematics has principles of this kind. But their application to experience, and therefore their objective validity, indeed the possibility of such synthetic knowledge *a priori* (the deduction of it), always rests on the pure understanding.",[]],"krv-a-0323":["I shall therefore not count the principles of mathematics among my principles. I shall, however, include those on which the possibility and objective validity *a priori* of mathematics are grounded, and which are accordingly to be regarded as the *principium* of those principles, proceeding from concepts to intuition, not from intuition to concepts.",[]],"krv-a-0324":["In the application of the pure concepts of understanding to possible experience, the employment of their synthesis is either mathematical or dynamical; for it is directed partly merely to intuition, partly to the existence of an appearance in general. Now the conditions *a priori* of intuition are absolutely necessary in respect of a possible experience, whereas the conditions of the existence of the objects of a possible empirical intuition are in themselves only contingent. Hence the principles of the mathematical employment will be unconditionally necessary, that is, apodeictic in statement. The principles of the dynamical employment will indeed also carry with them the character of a necessity *a priori*, but only under the condition of empirical thinking in an experience, and therefore only mediately and indirectly. Consequently they do not contain that immediate evidence which is peculiar to the former (although their certainty, as universally related to experience, is not thereby impaired). This, however, will be better judged at the close of this system of principles.",[]],"krv-a-0325":["The table of the categories gives us the quite natural guidance for the table of the principles, since these are nothing other than rules for the objective employment of the former. All principles of pure understanding are accordingly:",[]],"krv-a-0326":["1. Axioms of Intuition",[]],"krv-a-0327":["2. Anticipations of Perception",[]],"krv-a-0328":["3. Analogies of Experience",[]],"krv-a-0329":["4. Postulates of Empirical Thought in General.",["- 0314: \"Erkentniß\" rendered \"mode of knowledge\" in the first sentence (an individual instance); \"Gemüt\" as \"mind\"; the long final sentence is divided in two.","- 0314: \"ohne die sie keine Bedeutung haben würden\": \"die\" taken to refer to the objects of experience, hence \"apart from which they [space and time] would have no meaning\".","- 0315: The sentence \"ohne welche ... zusammen schicken würden\" is divided; \"sie\" (appearances) and \"würden\" kept plural in the English. \"Rhapsodie\" retained as \"rhapsody\".","- 0315: \"deren\" in \"deren objective Realität\" taken to refer to the \"allgemeine Regeln\"; \"ihrer Möglichkeit\" is taken as the possibility of experience.","- 0316: \"so vieles ... so, daß\" smoothed; \"dieser ihre Möglichkeit\" read as the possibility of experience, hence \"or rather to the possibility of experience itself\".","- 0317: \"diese\" (\"this\", i.e. all other synthesis) rendered \"this latter\"; \"Erkentnißart\" as \"kind of knowledge\".","- 0321: \"Vermögen der Regeln\" rendered \"faculty of rules\"; the parenthesis \"(was uns nur als Gegenstand vorkommen kan)\" is made a relative clause; the final sentences are separated.","- 0322: \"empir.\" expanded to \"empirical\"; \"vor Grundsätze\" read as \"für Grundsätze\". \"Principium\" kept in Latin in 0323; \"Exponent\" rendered \"exponent\". \"Mathematik hat dergleichen\" has no supplied noun beyond \"principles of this kind\".","- 0324: \"Anschauung\" and \"Daseyn\" rendered \"intuition\" and \"existence\"; \"apodictisch\" given as \"apodeictic\"."]],"krv-a-0330":["I have chosen these titles with caution, in order that the differences in respect of the evidence and the application of these principles may not pass unnoticed. It will soon be apparent, however, that, as regards both the evidence and the *a priori* determination of appearances, the principles that belong to the categories of quantity and quality (if we attend solely to the form of the latter) are markedly distinguished from the other two. The former are capable of intuitive certainty, the latter only of discursive; though both are capable of complete certainty. I shall therefore entitle the former the mathematical, and the latter the dynamical principles. It will, however, be observed that I have in view here the principles of mathematics in the one case no more than the principles of general (physical) dynamics in the other. What I have in view are only the principles of the pure understanding in their relation to inner sense (without regard to the difference of the representations given in it), and it is through these principles that those others [i.e. the principles of mathematics and of dynamics] acquire their possibility. I entitle them, then, with an eye to their application rather than on account of their content; and I now proceed to consider them in the same order as that in which they are presented in the table.",[]],"krv-a-0331":["1. The Axioms of Intuition.",[]],"krv-a-0332":["Principle of the pure understanding: All appearances are, in their intuition, extensive magnitudes.",[]],"krv-a-0333":["An extensive magnitude I entitle that in which the representation of the parts makes possible the representation of the whole, and therefore necessarily precedes it. I cannot represent to myself any line, however small, without drawing it in thought, that is, without generating from a point all its parts one after another, and in this way first delineating this intuition. The case is the same with every, even the smallest, [period of] time. I think therein only the successive advance from one moment to another, whereby, through all the parts of time and their addition, a determinate magnitude of time is finally generated. Since the mere intuition in all appearances is either space or time, every appearance, as intuition, is an extensive magnitude; for it can be known in apprehension only through successive synthesis, from part to part. All appearances are accordingly already intuited as aggregates, that is, as pluralities of antecedently given parts. This is not the case with every kind of magnitude, but only with those which are represented and apprehended by us as extensive.",[]],"krv-a-0334":["Upon this successive synthesis of the productive imagination, in the generation of figures, the mathematics of extension (geometry), with its axioms, is based. These axioms express the conditions of sensible intuition *a priori*, under which alone the schema of a pure concept of outer appearance can be brought about; for instance, between two points only one straight line is possible; two straight lines do not enclose a space; and so on. These are the axioms which properly concern only magnitudes (*quanta*) as such.",[]],"krv-a-0335":["As regards magnitude (*quantitas*), that is, the answer to the question, How great is something?, there are, in the strict sense of the term, no axioms, although several of the propositions in this field are synthetic and immediately certain (*indemonstrabilia*). For the propositions that if equals be added to equals, or taken from equals, the results are equal, are analytic, since I am immediately conscious of the identity of the one generation of magnitude with the other; whereas axioms ought to be synthetic propositions *a priori*. The self-evident propositions concerning numerical relations are, on the other hand, certainly synthetic, but not universal, as those of geometry are; and for this very reason they are not axioms, but can be entitled numerical formulae. That 7 + 5 = 12 is not an analytic proposition. For I think the number 12 neither in the representation of 7, nor in that of 5, nor in the representation of the combination of the two. (That I ought to think it in the addition of the two is not here the question; for in the case of an analytic proposition the only question is whether I actually think the predicate in the representation of the subject.) Though the proposition is synthetic, it is only a singular proposition. In so far as we are here attending merely to the synthesis of the homogeneous (of the units), the synthesis can take place only in one way, although the subsequent use of these numbers is universal. When I say, \"A triangle can be constructed with three lines, any two of which taken together are greater than the third,\" I have before me the mere function of the productive imagination, which can draw the lines longer or shorter, and can make them meet at all sorts of angles as it pleases. The number 7, on the other hand, is possible only in one way, and so also is the number 12, which is generated by the synthesis of 7 with 5. Such propositions must therefore be entitled not axioms (for otherwise there would be an infinite number of them) but numerical formulae.",[]],"krv-a-0336":["This transcendental principle of the mathematics of appearances greatly enlarges our *a priori* knowledge. For it alone makes pure mathematics, in all its precision, applicable to objects of experience. Without this principle that applicability would not be so self-evident, and has indeed given rise to many a contradiction. Appearances are not things in themselves. Empirical intuition is possible only through pure intuition (of space and of time); what geometry asserts of the latter is therefore indisputably valid of the former also. The evasions, by which it is contended that objects of the senses need not conform to the rules of construction in space (for instance, to the rule of the infinite divisibility of lines or angles), must therefore be given up. For to maintain this is to deny objective validity to space, and with it to all mathematics, and we no longer know why, and how far, mathematics can be applied to appearances. The synthesis of spaces and times, as the essential form of all intuition, is that which at the same time makes possible the apprehension of the appearance, and so every outer experience, and consequently all knowledge of the objects of experience. What mathematics in its pure use proves of the former, must also hold necessarily of the latter. All objections are only the chicaneries of a falsely instructed reason, which wrongly thinks to free the objects of the senses from the formal condition of our sensibility, and represents them, though they are mere appearances, as objects in themselves, given to the understanding. In that case, indeed, nothing could be known of them *a priori*, and so nothing synthetically through pure concepts of space; and the science which determines those concepts, namely geometry, would itself not be possible.",[]],"krv-a-0337":["2. The Anticipations of Perception.",[]],"krv-a-0338":["The principle which anticipates all perceptions, as such, is as follows: In all appearances sensation, and the real which corresponds to it in the object (*realitas phaenomenon*), has an intensive magnitude, that is, a degree.",["- krv-a-0330: Restated the long sentence on evidence and determination in two, and resolved \"iene/diese\" as the mathematical (quantity, quality) versus dynamical (relation, modality) principles; \"iene\" in the sentence ending \"ihre Möglichkeit bekommen\" is read as the principles of mathematics and dynamics, hence the bracketed gloss.","- krv-a-0330: \"Benennungen\" rendered \"titles\"; \"nahmhaft\" as \"markedly\"; the parenthesis \"(wenn man lediglich auf die Form der lezteren acht hat)\" is kept, \"der lezteren\" read as the category of quality.","- krv-a-0333: \"[period of]\" is supplied; \"Zeit\" here is a stretch of time. \"Menge vorhergegebener Theile\" is rendered \"pluralities of antecedently given parts\" and set in a separate sentence.","- krv-a-0333: \"in Gedanken zuziehen\" read as \"in Gedanken zu ziehen\" (OCR/spelling), \"to draw in thought\".","- krv-a-0335: \"Zahlformeln\" is \"numerical formulae\"; \"Grösse/Grössen\" in the sense of *quantum* is \"magnitude\", and *quantitas* is likewise rendered \"magnitude\" by choice. \"Triangel\" is rendered \"triangle\".","- krv-a-0335: \"Sätze der Zahlverhältniß\" is rendered \"propositions concerning numerical relations\"; \"einzelner Satz\" as \"singular proposition\". The sentence on \"gleiches zu gleichem\" was recast with \"if equals be added to equals\" for smoothness.","- krv-a-0336: \"Chikanen\" is \"chicaneries\"; \"Ausflüchte, als wenn...\" was restated as \"evasions, by which it is contended that...\". \"Dem Verstande gegeben\" is kept literally; the final sentence is split at \"in welchem Falle\".","- krv-a-0338: \"heißt so\" is rendered \"is as follows\"; \"Wahrnehmungen\" is \"perceptions\", *realitas phaenomenon* is left in Latin."]],"krv-a-0339":["All knowledge by which I am enabled to know and determine a priori what belongs to empirical knowledge may be entitled an anticipation; and this is without doubt the meaning in which Epicurus employed his expression πρόληψις. But there is in appearances something which is never known a priori, and which therefore constitutes the proper difference between empirical knowledge and knowledge a priori, namely sensation (as the matter of perception). It follows that sensation is precisely what cannot be anticipated. On the other hand, the pure determinations in space and time, alike in respect of figure and of magnitude, we might entitle anticipations of appearances, since they represent a priori whatever may always be given a posteriori in experience. But suppose that there is something which can be known a priori in every sensation, as sensation in general, without any particular sensation being given. This would deserve to be entitled anticipation in an exceptional sense, since it seems strange that we should forestall experience in what concerns its matter, which can be drawn only from experience. Yet that is actually the case here.",[]],"krv-a-0340":["Apprehension by means of sensation alone occupies only a moment, if, that is, I leave out of account the succession of many sensations. Sensation is something in the appearance the apprehension of which is not a successive synthesis proceeding from the parts to the whole representation; it has therefore no extensive magnitude. The absence of sensation in the same moment would represent the moment as empty, and so as = 0. Now that which in empirical intuition corresponds to sensation is reality (*realitas phaenomenon*); that which corresponds to its absence is negation = 0. But every sensation is capable of diminution, so that it can decrease and gradually vanish. Between reality in the appearance and negation there is therefore a continuous connection of many possible intermediate sensations, the difference between any two of which is always smaller than the difference between the given sensation and zero, that is, complete negation. In other words, the real in the appearance always has a magnitude. This magnitude is not met with in apprehension, since apprehension by means of mere sensation takes place in a moment and not through successive synthesis of many sensations, and so does not proceed from the parts to the whole. The real has, then, a magnitude, but not an extensive magnitude.",[]],"krv-a-0341":["Now the magnitude which is apprehended only as unity, and in which plurality can be represented only through approximation to negation = 0, I entitle intensive magnitude. Every reality in the appearance has therefore intensive magnitude, that is, a degree. If this reality is considered as cause, whether of sensation or of some other reality in the appearance, for instance a change, the degree of reality as cause is entitled a moment, for instance the moment of gravity. It is so entitled because degree signifies only that magnitude the apprehension of which is not successive but instantaneous. This I mention only in passing, for causality is not for the present my concern.",[]],"krv-a-0342":["Every sensation, and therefore every reality in appearance, however small it may be, has accordingly a degree, that is, an intensive magnitude which can always still be diminished; and between reality and negation there is a continuous connection of possible realities and of possible smaller perceptions. Every colour, for instance the red, has a degree which, however small, is never the smallest; and the same holds throughout of heat, of the moment of gravity, and so on.",[]],"krv-a-0343":["That property of magnitudes by which no part of them is the smallest possible, that is, no part is simple, is entitled their continuity. Space and time are *quanta continua*, since no part of them can be given save as enclosed between limits (points and instants), and therefore only as itself again a space or a time. Space consists only of spaces, time only of times. Points and instants are only limits, that is, mere positions marking limitation; and positions always presuppose the intuitions which they are to limit or to determine. Neither space nor time can be composed out of mere positions, as out of constituents that could be given prior to space or time. Magnitudes of this kind may also be entitled flowing, since the synthesis of the productive imagination involved in their generation is a progress in time, and the continuity of time we are accustomed to designate especially by the expression flowing (flowing away).",[]],"krv-a-0344":["All appearances are therefore continuous magnitudes, whether as extensive magnitudes in respect of their intuition, or as intensive magnitudes in respect of mere perception (sensation, and so reality). If the synthesis of the manifold of appearance is interrupted, we have an aggregate of many appearances, and not properly appearance as a quantum. Such an aggregate is not generated by mere continuation of the productive synthesis of a given kind, but by repetition of a synthesis that is constantly ceasing. If I call thirteen thalers a quantum of money, I name it correctly so far as I understand by it the content of a mark of fine silver. This is indeed a continuous magnitude, in which no part is the smallest, but every part could constitute a coin that would always contain material for still smaller ones. But if by that name I understand thirteen round thalers, as so many coins (whatever their silver content may be), I am wrongly entitling it a quantum of thalers; I must call it an aggregate, that is, a number of coins. Since, however, unity must underlie all number, the appearance, as unity, is a quantum, and as such is always a continuum.",[]],"krv-a-0345":["If, then, all appearances, whether considered extensively or intensively, are continuous magnitudes, the proposition that all alteration (transition of a thing from one state into another) is likewise continuous could easily be proved here with mathematical evidence. But the causality of an alteration in general lies wholly outside the limits of a transcendental philosophy, and presupposes empirical principles. For that there can be a cause which alters the state of things, that is, determines them to the opposite of a given state, the understanding gives us no disclosure whatever a priori. This is not merely because it has no insight into the possibility of such a cause (such insight is lacking to us in several modes of knowledge a priori), but because alterability concerns only certain determinations of appearances, which experience alone can teach, while their cause is to be found in what is unalterable. Since, however, we have before us nothing that we can employ save the pure fundamental concepts of all possible experience, among which nothing empirical can be admitted, we cannot, without violating the unity of the system, anticipate general natural science, which is built upon certain fundamental experiences.",[]],"krv-a-0346":["Nevertheless we do not lack proofs of the great influence of this our principle in anticipating perceptions, and even in making good their absence, so far as to bar the way against all the false inferences that might be drawn from it.",["- 0339: \"Erkentniß\" rendered \"knowledge\"; the long sentence \"Da aber … so folgt\" restated as two sentences. Greek προληψις given as πρόληψις (accent normalised).","- 0339: \"im ausnehmenden Verstande\" = \"in an exceptional sense\" (Verstande here is \"sense\", not the faculty).","- 0340: \"Als etwas in der Erscheinung … hat sie\" – \"sie\" taken to refer to sensation; supplied \"Sensation is\" and split the sentence. The final long sentence split in three.","- 0340: \"dasselbe Augenblick … diesen, als leer\" – \"diesen\" read as the moment; supplied \"the moment\".","- 0341: \"Moment\" kept as Kant's technical term; \"Caussalität\" modernised as causality.","- 0343: \"Stellen\" rendered \"positions\"; \"Bestandtheilen\" \"constituents\"; \"Verfliessens\" given as \"flowing away\".","- 0344: \"Mark fein Silber\" rendered \"mark of fine silver\"; \"Thaler\" kept as thalers. \"unschicklich\" rendered \"wrongly\".","- 0345: \"Eröfnung\" rendered \"disclosure\"; \"Erkentnissen\" \"modes of knowledge\"; long sentences divided. \"daraus\" in 0346 left as \"it\" (the principle's use), which is ambiguous in the German."]],"krv-a-0347":["If all reality in perception has a degree, between which and negation there is an infinite series of ever lesser degrees, and if nevertheless every sense must have a determinate degree of receptivity for sensations, then no perception, and consequently no experience, is possible which could prove, whether immediately or mediately (by however roundabout a process of inference one pleases), a complete absence of everything real in the appearance. In other words, a proof of empty space or of empty time can never be drawn from experience. For, in the first place, the complete absence of the real in sensible intuition cannot itself be perceived; and, secondly, it cannot be inferred from any single appearance and from the difference in the degree of its reality, nor may it ever be assumed in explanation of such a difference. For even if the whole intuition of a determinate space or time is real throughout, that is, if no part of it is empty, yet since every reality has its degree, which, the extensive magnitude of the appearance remaining unaltered, can diminish through infinite stages down to nothing (the empty), there must be infinitely various degrees with which space or time may be filled; and the intensive magnitude in different appearances may be smaller or greater, although the extensive magnitude of the intuition is the same.",["- Restated the opening conditional and its consequence more plainly; \"d. i.\" rendered \"in other words\" / \"that is\", dividing the long sentence at \"Denn\".","- Perception = Wahrnehmung; \"Receptivität\" = receptivity; \"Erscheinung\" = appearance; \"Anschauung\" = intuition.","- \"als man immer wolle\" read as \"als man immer wolle\" = \"as one pleases\" (original has \"als\" for \"als auch/wie\"); smoothed.","- Final clause: \"muß ... geben\" and \"können\" depend on the same \"so muß es doch\"; the plural \"können\" is kept loosely in English."]],"krv-a-0348":["Let us give an example of this. Almost all students of nature, perceiving a great difference in the quantity of matter of different kinds under the same volume (partly through the moment of gravity or weight, partly through the moment of resistance to other matter in motion), conclude unanimously that this volume (the extensive magnitude of the appearance) must be empty in all matters, though in varying measure. But which of these mostly mathematical and mechanical investigators of nature would ever have supposed that they were basing this inference solely upon a metaphysical presupposition, which they profess so much to avoid? They do so in assuming that the real in space (I do not care here to call it impenetrability or weight, since these are empirical concepts) is everywhere one and the same, and can differ only in extensive magnitude, that is, in amount. To this presupposition, for which they could have no ground in experience and which is therefore merely metaphysical, I oppose a transcendental proof. This proof does not profess to explain the difference in the filling of spaces. But it entirely removes the supposed necessity of that presupposition, namely that the said difference can be explained only by assuming empty spaces; and it has the merit of at least setting the understanding free to think this diversity in another fashion also, should the explanation of nature make some hypothesis necessary for the purpose. For we see that, although equal spaces may be completely filled by different kinds of matter, so that in neither of them is there a point in which the presence of matter is not to be found, nevertheless every real, with the same quality, has its degree (of resistance or of weight), which can, without diminution of the extensive magnitude or amount, become smaller *ad infinitum*, before it passes into the empty and vanishes. Thus an expansion which fills a space, for instance heat, and in the same way every other reality (in the appearance), can diminish in its degrees to infinity without leaving the smallest part of this space in the least empty, and can none the less fill the space with these smaller degrees just as well as another appearance does with greater. My intention here is by no means to assert that this is actually the case with the difference of matters in respect of their specific gravity. I desire only to establish, from a principle of the pure understanding, that the nature of our perceptions makes such a mode of explanation possible, and that it is a mistake to regard the real in appearance as equal in degree and as differing only in aggregation and in extensive magnitude, and indeed to maintain this, as is alleged, by a principle of the understanding *a priori*.",["- Divided the sentence beginning \"Wer hätte aber...\" into a question plus a following sentence; likewise split \"Dieser Voraussetzung...\" into three.","- \"ins Unendliche\" rendered *ad infinitum* only as a gloss; Kant's text is German, so the italics are my addition for the English idiom—replace with \"to infinity\" if preferred.","- \"Ausspannung\" = expansion; \"Moment\" kept as \"moment\"; \"Naturlehrer/Naturforscher\" = students/investigators of nature.","- \"wie durch\" in \"nicht anders, wie durch\" rendered \"only by\"; \"Aggregation\" retained.","- \"bey derselben Qualität ihren Grad\": \"ihren\" agrees loosely with plural \"Materien\"; resolved as \"its degree\" for each real."]],"krv-a-0349":["Nevertheless this anticipation of perception always has something startling about it for an investigator accustomed to transcendental enquiries and thereby made cautious, and it gives rise to some hesitation that the understanding should [anticipate] such a synthetic proposition as that of the degree of all that is real in appearances, and consequently of the possibility of the inner difference of sensation itself, when abstraction is made from its empirical quality. There remains, therefore, a question not unworthy of solution: how the understanding can pronounce synthetically *a priori* upon appearances, and can anticipate them even in what is properly and merely empirical, namely in what concerns sensation.",["- The German sentence is irregular: \"daß der Verstand einen dergleichen synthetischen Satz ... ist\" lacks a governing verb. I supplied \"[anticipate]\" in brackets, in keeping with the \"anticipation\" of the first clause; the text may be defective.","- \"einen der transscendentalen gewohnten\": read as \"einen der Transscendentalen [Nachforschung] gewohnten\" — \"transcendental enquiries\" is supplied.","- \"es ... und erregt ... Bedenken\": smoothed."]],"krv-a-0350":["The quality of sensation is always merely empirical, and cannot be represented *a priori* at all (for example colours, taste, etc.). But the real which corresponds to sensations in general, as opposed to negation = 0, represents only something the concept of which in itself contains a being, and signifies nothing but the synthesis in an empirical consciousness in general. In inner sense, namely, the empirical consciousness can be raised from 0 to any higher degree, so that the very same extensive magnitude of intuition (for example, an illuminated surface) excites as great a sensation as an aggregate of many other (less illuminated) surfaces taken together. We can therefore abstract entirely from the extensive magnitude of the appearance, and yet represent in the mere sensation, in a single moment, a synthesis of uniform increase from 0 up to the given empirical consciousness. All sensations, as such, are indeed given only *a posteriori*, but the property that they have a degree can be known *a priori*. It is remarkable that in the case of magnitudes in general we can know *a priori* only a single quality, namely continuity; but in all quality (the real of appearances) we can know *a priori* nothing further than their intensive quantity, namely that they have a degree. All the rest is left to experience.",["- The text reads \"nur a priori gegeben\"; sense plainly requires \"a posteriori\" (as in the later Academy and B text). I rendered \"*a posteriori*\" and flag it as a correction of the A-text reading.","- \"Erkentnisse/erkennen\" = know; \"Bewustseyn\" = consciousness; \"Aggregat\" = aggregate.","- \"eben dieselbe ... so grosse Empfindung erregt, als ein Aggregat\" smoothed with \"excites as great a sensation as\"; \"(minder erleuchteten)\" supplied \"surfaces\" for clarity, unbracketed—minor."]],"krv-a-0351":["3. The Analogies of Experience.",["- Heading only."]],"krv-a-0352":["The general principle of these is: All appearances, as regards their existence, stand *a priori* under rules determining their relation to one another in one time.",["- \"Daseyn\" = existence; \"Bestimmung\" = determination, here \"determining\" for the genitive."]],"krv-a-0353":["The three modes (*modi*) of time are permanence, succession, and coexistence. Consequently there are three rules of all time-relations of appearances, in accordance with which the existence of each can be determined in respect of the unity of all time; and these rules precede all experience and first make it possible.",["- Divided the sentence at the semicolon for English flow; \"Zugleichseyn\" = coexistence; \"Beharrlichkeit\" = permanence; \"Folge\" = succession."]],"krv-a-0354":["The general principle of all three analogies rests on the necessary unity of apperception in respect of all possible empirical consciousness (perception) at every time; and consequently, since that unity underlies *a priori*, it rests on the synthetic unity of all appearances according to their relation in time. For original apperception relates to inner sense (the sum of all representations), and indeed relates *a priori* to its form, that is, to the relation of the manifold of empirical consciousness in time. Now in original apperception all this manifold, so far as its time-relations are concerned, has to be united. For this is what the transcendental unity of apperception *a priori* declares, under which stands everything that is to belong to my knowledge (that is, to my single knowledge), and so can become an object for me. This synthetic unity in the time-relation of all perceptions, which is determined *a priori*, is therefore the law that all empirical determinations of time must stand under rules of the universal determination of time; and the analogies of experience, with which we are now to deal, must be rules of this kind.",["- Restated the opening sentence and divided at \"Denn\"; \"iene\" resolved as the unity of apperception.","- \"meinem (d. i. meinem einigen) Erkentnisse\": \"einigen\" read as \"single/one\", \"knowledge\" in the singular for Erkenntnis.","- \"vor mich\" = \"for me\"; \"Gegenstand\" = object.","- \"Inbegriff\" = sum."]],"krv-a-0355":["These principles have this peculiarity, that they do not concern themselves with appearances and the synthesis of their empirical intuition, but solely with existence and with the relation of appearances to one another in respect of this their existence. Now the manner in which something is apprehended in appearance can be so determined *a priori* that the rule of its synthesis can at the same time yield this intuition *a priori* in every empirical example that lies before us; that is to say, can bring it into being out of that rule. But the existence of appearances cannot be known *a priori*. And even if we could by this route arrive at inferring to some existence or other, we should not know it determinately; that is, we should not be able to anticipate that whereby its empirical intuition is distinguished from others.",[]],"krv-a-0356":["The two preceding principles, which I entitled mathematical in view of the fact that they entitled us to apply mathematics to appearances, dealt with appearances in respect of their mere possibility. They taught how appearances, alike as regards their intuition and as regards the real in their perception, can be generated in accordance with the rules of a mathematical synthesis. Hence in the one case as in the other, numerical magnitudes, and with them the determination of appearance as magnitude, can be employed. For instance, I can compose the degree of the sensations of sunlight out of some 200,000 illuminations by the moon, and so give it determinately *a priori*, that is, construct it. The former principles we may therefore entitle constitutive.",[]],"krv-a-0357":["The situation must be quite otherwise with those principles which are to bring the existence of appearances under rules *a priori*. For since existence cannot be constructed, these principles can concern only the relation of existence, and can yield no principles other than merely regulative. We cannot therefore think of axioms or of anticipations. If a perception is given us in a time-relation to other perceptions (though these are indeterminate), we shall not be able to say *a priori* which other perception, or how great a perception, is involved, but only how it is necessarily connected with the first in respect of existence, in this *modus* of time.\n\nIn philosophy analogies signify something very different from what they represent in mathematics. In mathematics they are formulae which assert the equality of two relations of magnitude, and are always constitutive; so that if two members of the proportion are given, the third is also thereby given, that is, can be constructed. In philosophy, however, analogy is the equality not of two quantitative but of two qualitative relations. Here from three given members I can know, and give *a priori*, only the relation to a fourth member, not this fourth member itself. I have, however, a rule for seeking it in experience, and a mark by which it can be discovered there.\n\nAn analogy of experience will therefore be only a rule according to which unity of experience is to arise out of perceptions (not a rule according to which perception itself, as empirical intuition in general, arises). As a principle applying to objects (to appearances) it will hold not constitutively but merely regulatively.\n\nThe same will hold of the postulates of empirical thought in general. These concern the synthesis of mere intuition (the form of appearance), of perception (the matter of appearance), and of experience (the relation of these perceptions), taken together. They are merely regulative principles, and are distinguished from the mathematical principles, which are constitutive, not indeed in certainty, which is established *a priori* in both, but in the kind of evidence, that is, in their intuitive character (and therefore also in demonstration).",[]],"krv-a-0358":["But what was remarked in connection with all synthetic principles, and has here especially to be noted, is this: these analogies have their sole meaning and validity not as principles of the transcendental but merely as principles of the empirical employment of the understanding, and can accordingly be proved only as such. Consequently appearances must be subsumed not under the categories simply, but only under their schemata. For if the objects to which these principles are to be related were things in themselves, it would be quite impossible to know anything of them synthetically *a priori*. But they are nothing but appearances, and the complete knowledge of them, in which all principles *a priori* must in the end always terminate, is simply possible experience. Those principles, therefore, can have as their goal nothing but the conditions of the unity of empirical knowledge in the synthesis of appearances. This synthesis, however, is thought only in the schema of the pure concept of the understanding; and the category contains the function, restricted by no sensible condition, of the unity of that synthesis as a synthesis in general.\n\nBy these principles we shall therefore be entitled to combine appearances only in accordance with an analogy with the logical and universal unity of concepts. Hence in the principle itself we shall make use of the category, but in carrying it out (in applying it to appearances) we shall put its schema in the category's place, as the key to its employment; or rather, we shall set the schema beside the category as its restricting condition, under the title of a formula of the category.",[]],"krv-a-0359":["A. First Analogy. Principle of the Persistence of Substance.",[]],"krv-a-0360":["All appearances contain the persistent (substance) as the object itself, and the changeable as its mere determination, that is, as a way in which the object exists.",[]],"krv-a-0361":["Proof of this First Analogy.",[]],"krv-a-0362":["All appearances are in time. Time can determine the relation in their existence in two ways: in so far as they exist in succession or [in so far as they exist] simultaneously. In view of the first, time is regarded as time-series; in respect of the second, as time-content [*Zeitumfang*, literally \"extent of time\"].",["Throughout, \"Erkenntnis\" is \"knowledge\" and \"Bestimmung\" \"determination\"; \"heißen/nennen\" is rendered \"entitle\"; \"berechtigen\" is also \"entitle\", which gives deliberate wordplay in 0356 and 0358.","0355: the long second sentence is divided; \"dieses ihres Daseyns\" is rendered \"this their existence\"; the \"d. i.\" clause is kept as a separate gloss.","0355: \"auf irgend ein Daseyn zu schliessen\" is rendered \"inferring to some existence or other\".","0356: \"Daher können wir die erstere Grundsätze constitutive nennen\" is rendered \"The former principles we may therefore entitle constitutive\"; \"Grundsatz\" is \"principle\".","0357: the long paragraph is divided into several paragraphs, with sentences restated as separate ones; the parenthesis \"(nicht wie Wahrnehmung selbst …)\" is expanded into \"not a rule according to which perception itself … arises\" as the sense requires.","0357: *modus* is Kant's Latin, kept in italics. \"Zeitverhältnisse\" is \"time-relation\".","0358: the second paragraph break, and the division of the last sentence, are mine. \"Von deren Einheit\" has the schema as antecedent of \"deren\" in the German grammar; I take the unity to be the synthesis's, and \"the category contains the function\" is read accordingly. Doubtful.","0358: \"an dessen Stelle, oder jener vielmehr, als restringirende Bedingung\" is smoothed into \"in the category's place, or rather … beside it as its restricting condition\"; the German antecedents are tangled.","0362: \"Zeitumfang\" is given with the German in square brackets; \"Zeitreihe\" is \"time-series\". The bracketed words \"in so far as they exist\" are supplied. The passage breaks off at the end of the section as it stands."]],"krv-a-0363":["Our apprehension of the manifold of appearance is always successive, and is therefore always changing. By it alone we can never determine whether this manifold, as object of experience, is coexistent or successive, unless there underlies it something which exists at all times, that is, something abiding and permanent, of which all change and coexistence are nothing but so many ways (*modi* of time) in which the permanent exists. Only in the permanent, then, are time-relations possible (for simultaneity and succession are the only relations in time). In other words, the permanent is the substratum of our empirical representation of time itself, and only in it is any determination of time possible. Permanence, generally speaking, expresses time as the constant correlate of all existence of appearances, of all change, and of all concomitance. For change does not affect time itself, but only appearances in time. (Coexistence, likewise, is not a *modus* of time itself, since in time no parts are coexistent, all being successive.) If we sought to ascribe to time itself a sequence, one after another, we should have to think yet another time in which this sequence would be possible. Only through the permanent does existence, in the different parts of the time-series successively, acquire a magnitude which is entitled duration. For in mere sequence existence is always vanishing and beginning, and never has the least magnitude. Without this permanent, therefore, there is no time-relation. Now time cannot be perceived in itself. Consequently this permanent in appearances is the substratum of all determination of time, and hence also the condition of the possibility of all synthetic unity of perceptions, that is, of experience. In relation to this permanent, all existence and all change in time can be regarded only as a *modus* of the existence of that which abides and persists. Thus in all appearances the permanent is the object itself, that is, substance (*phaenomenon*); whereas everything that changes or can change belongs only to the way in which this substance or substances exist, and therefore to their determinations.",["- Long opening sentence kept as one but \"wo an ihr nicht etwas zum Grunde liegt\" rendered \"unless there underlies it\"; \"wie das Beharrliche existirt\" read as \"in which the permanent exists\".","- Split \"d. i. das Beharrliche ist das Substratum…\" into a new sentence; the parenthesis on coexistence was recast as a separate sentence.","- \"Zeitbestimmung\" = determination of time; \"Wahrnehmung\" = perception; \"Beharrliche\" = the permanent."]],"krv-a-0364":["I find that at all times not only the philosopher but even the common understanding has presupposed this permanence as a substratum of all change of appearances, and will always accept it as beyond doubt. The philosopher merely expresses himself somewhat more definitely, by saying: in all changes in the world substance abides, and only the accidents change. But nowhere do I find even an attempt at a proof of this proposition, which is so evidently synthetic. Indeed it seldom stands, as it ought, at the head of the laws of nature that are pure and completely *a priori*. In fact the proposition that substance is permanent is tautological. For this permanence alone is the ground on which we apply the category of substance to appearance, and what ought to have been proved is that in all appearances there is something permanent, in relation to which the changeable is nothing but determination of its existence. But such a proof can never be conducted dogmatically, that is, from concepts, since it concerns a synthetic proposition *a priori*; and it was never recognised that such propositions are valid only in relation to possible experience, and can therefore be proved only through a deduction of the possibility of the latter. It is therefore no wonder that, while it has been made the foundation of all experience (since the need of it is felt in empirical knowledge), it has never been proved.",["- \"Ich treffe aber von diesem so synthetischen Satze…\" smoothed to \"so evidently synthetic\"; sentence divided at \"Da aber…\", with the long \"so ist kein Wunder\" clause restated.","- \"tavtologisch\" = tautological. \"Accidenzen\" rendered \"accidents\".","- \"Wandelbare\" = the changeable."]],"krv-a-0365":["A philosopher was asked: How much does smoke weigh? He answered: Subtract from the weight of the wood burnt the weight of the ash that remains, and you have the weight of the smoke. He thus assumed as undeniable that even in fire matter (substance) does not perish, and that only its form suffers alteration. Similarly the proposition, From nothing comes nothing, is only another consequence of the principle of permanence, or rather of the perpetual existence of the proper subject in appearances. For if that in the appearance which we wish to entitle substance is to be the proper substratum of all determination of time, then all existence, in past time as in future, must be capable of being determined in it, and in it alone. Hence we can give an appearance the name substance only because we presuppose its existence at all times. This is not very well expressed even by the word permanence, since that word refers rather to future time. Nevertheless the inner necessity of persisting is inseparably bound up with the necessity of having always been, and the expression may therefore stand. *Gigni de nihilo nihil, in nihilum nil posse reverti* were two propositions which the ancients connected inseparably, and which through misunderstanding are now sometimes separated. The reason is that they are taken to concern things in themselves, and the former is thought liable to conflict with the dependence of the world (even in respect of its substance) upon a supreme cause. This apprehension is needless, for we are speaking here only of appearances in the field of experience, the unity of which could never be possible if we were willing to allow new things (in respect of substance) to come into being. For then that would fall away which alone can represent the unity of time, namely the identity of the substratum, in which alone all change has thoroughgoing unity. This permanence is, after all, nothing but the way in which we represent to ourselves the existence of things (in appearance).",["- The sentence beginning \"Gigni de nihilo…\" was divided into three; the Latin line is left as in Kant.","- \"welches durch das Wort Beharrlichkeit…\" restated as a separate sentence; \"dieses\" refers to the word.","- \"Folgesatz\" = consequence (corollary); \"eigentliche Subiect\" = proper subject."]],"krv-a-0366":["The determinations of a substance, which are nothing but special ways in which it exists, are entitled accidents. They are always real, since they concern the existence of substance. (Negations are only determinations which express the non-being of something in the substance.) If now a special existence is ascribed to this real element in substance (for example, to motion as an accident of matter), this existence is called inherence, in distinction from the existence of substance, which is called subsistence. But many misinterpretations arise from this, and it is more exact and more correct to describe the accident solely as the way in which the existence of a substance is positively determined. Nevertheless, owing to the conditions of the logical employment of our understanding, it is unavoidable to separate off, as it were, whatever in the existence of a substance can change while the substance remains, and to consider it in relation to the properly permanent and radical. Hence this category also stands under the title of relation, being rather the condition of relations than itself containing a relation.",["- \"Wenn man nun diesem Realen … beygelegt\" is ungrammatical (perfect for present); rendered \"is ascribed\".","- Last clause: \"mehr, als die Bedingung derselben, als daß…\" smoothed to \"rather the condition of relations than…\"; \"derselben\" taken to refer to Verhältnisse.","- \"heissen\" = \"is entitled\"; \"Inhärenz/Subsistenz\" kept in English cognates."]],"krv-a-0367":["On this permanence is founded also the correction of the concept of alteration. Coming into being and passing away are not alterations of that which comes into being or passes away. Alteration is a way of existing which follows upon another way of existing of the very same object. Hence whatever alters is abiding, and only its state changes. Since this change thus affects only determinations, which can cease or begin, we may say, in an expression that seems somewhat paradoxical, that only the permanent (substance) is altered, while the changeable suffers no alteration but a change, in that some determinations cease and others begin.",["- \"Veränderung\" = alteration, \"Wechsel\" = change, to preserve Kant's distinction in the final sentence.","- \"Berichtigung\" = correction; \"Zustand\" = state."]],"krv-a-0368":["Alteration can therefore be perceived only in substances. Origination or extinction in the absolute sense, that is, without being merely a determination of what persists, can never be a possible perception. For it is just this persistent that makes possible the representation of the transition from one state to another, and from non-being to being; and these transitions can therefore be known empirically only as alternating determinations of that which abides. Suppose that something absolutely begins to be. You must then have a point of time in which it was not. But to what will you attach this point, if not to that which already exists? For an empty time preceding is not an object of perception. If, however, you connect this origination with things which existed beforehand and which continue to exist up to what originates, the latter was only a determination of the former, as of the persistent. The same holds of extinction; for this presupposes the empirical representation of a time in which an appearance no longer exists.",["- \"Entstehen oder Vergehen, schlechthin\": rendered \"origination or extinction in the absolute sense\"; the sentence is divided at \"weil eben dieses…\" and the relative \"die … können\" is resolved to refer to the transitions (Uebergang), not to the persistent.","- *Beharrliche* = \"the persistent\"; *Bestimmung* = \"determination\"; *Wahrnehmung* = \"perception\".","- \"indem\" (for \"in dem\") read as \"in which\"; \"demienigen\" = \"that which\"."]],"krv-a-0369":["Substances (in the appearance) are the substrata of all determinations of time. The origination of some of them and the extinction of others would themselves remove the sole condition of the empirical unity of time. Appearances would then relate to two different times, in which existence would flow past side by side. This is absurd. For there is only one time, in which all different times must be posited not simultaneously but in succession.",["- \"derselben\" refers to substances; the Ed. text prints \"derselbenwürde\" as one word, a slip silently corrected.","- *Zeitbestimmungen* = \"determinations of time\"; \"verflösse\" rendered \"would flow past\", with \"Daseyn\" = \"existence\"."]],"krv-a-0370":["Thus permanence is a necessary condition under which alone appearances, as things or objects, are determinable in a possible experience. What the empirical criterion of this necessary permanence, and with it of the substantiality of appearances, may be, the sequel will give us occasion to remark as far as needful.",["- *Beharrlichkeit* = \"permanence\", as the abstract of *das Beharrliche* (\"the persistent\"); *bestimbar* = \"determinable\"."]],"krv-a-0371":["B. Second Analogy. Principle of Production.",["- *Erzeugung* = \"production\" (the 2nd-edition title, \"of Succession in Time according to the Law of Causality\", is not in this text)."]],"krv-a-0372":["Everything that happens (begins to be) presupposes something upon which it follows according to a rule.",[]],"krv-a-0373":["Proof.",[]],"krv-a-0374":["The apprehension of the manifold of appearance is always successive. The representations of the parts follow upon one another. Whether they also follow one another in the object is a second point of reflection, not contained in the first. Now we can indeed entitle everything, and even every representation, so far as we are conscious of it, an object. But what the word is to signify in regard to appearances, when they are viewed not [merely] as representations that are objects, but only as *designating* an object, requires deeper investigation. So far as they are, merely as representations, at the same time objects of consciousness, they are not at all distinguished from apprehension, that is, from the reception into the synthesis of the imagination; and we must therefore say that the manifold of appearances is always produced successively in the mind. If appearances were things in themselves, no one could estimate from the succession of the representations of their manifold how this manifold is combined in the object. For we have to deal only with our representations. How things in themselves (apart from the representations through which they affect us) may be constituted lies wholly outside the sphere of our knowledge. Now although appearances are not things in themselves, and yet are the only thing that can be given to us to be known, I am to indicate what sort of combination in time belongs to the manifold in the appearances themselves, while the representation of it in apprehension is always successive. Thus, for example, the apprehension of the manifold in the appearance of a house standing before me is successive. The question is whether the manifold of this house is itself also successive in itself, which no one will admit. But as soon as I raise my concepts of an object to the transcendental meaning, the house is not a thing in itself at all but only an appearance, that is, a representation, the transcendental object of which is unknown. What, then, do I understand by the question, how the manifold may be combined in the appearance itself (which yet is nothing in itself)? Here that which lies in the successive apprehension is regarded as representation, while the appearance which is given to me, although it is nothing more than a sum of these representations, is regarded as their object; and my concept, which I draw from the representations of apprehension, is to agree with it. We soon see that, since agreement of knowledge with the object is truth, only the formal conditions of empirical truth can here be inquired into; and that appearance, in contrast to the representations of apprehension, can be represented as the object distinct from them only if it stands under a rule which distinguishes it from every other apprehension and makes necessary a certain mode of combination of the manifold. That in the appearance which contains the condition of this necessary rule of apprehension is the object.",["- Several long sentences divided (at \"allein was dieses Wort…\", \"Denn wir haben…\", \"Ob nun gleich…\").","- The clause \"nicht, in so fern sie (als Vorstellungen) Obiecte sind, sondern nur ein Obiect bezeichnen\" is syntactically irregular; smoothed, with [merely] supplied. Compare the B text, which revises it.","- \"Man siehet bald\": \"we soon see\". *Obiect* = \"object\"; *Gegenstand* also \"object\", with *Erkentnißsphäre* = \"sphere of our knowledge\".","- \"dessen transscendentaler Gegenstand unbekant ist\": \"dessen\" taken to refer to the appearance/representation.","- \"mit welchem\" read as referring to the appearance (as object)."]],"krv-a-0375":["Let us now proceed to our task. That something happens, that is, that something, or some state, comes to be which was not before, cannot be empirically perceived unless an appearance precedes which does not contain this state. For a reality following upon an empty time, and hence an origination preceded by no state of things, can no more be apprehended than empty time itself. Every apprehension of an event is therefore a perception which follows upon another. But since this is so in all synthesis of apprehension, as I showed above in the appearance of a house, the apprehension of an event is not thereby distinguished from others. I note, however, a further point. If in an appearance which contains an occurrence I call the preceding state of perception A and the following state B, then B can only follow A in apprehension, while the perception A cannot follow B but can only precede it. I see, for example, a ship floating down a stream. My perception of its position lower down follows upon the perception of its position higher up the course of the river; and it is impossible that in the apprehension of this appearance the ship should first be perceived lower down and afterwards higher up. The order in the sequence of the perceptions in apprehension is thus here determined, and apprehension is bound to it. In the previous example of a house, my perceptions in apprehension could begin at its summit and end at the ground, or begin from below and end above; and similarly I could apprehend the manifold of the empirical intuition from right to left or from left to right. In the series of these perceptions there was thus no determinate order making it necessary at which point I must begin in apprehension in order to connect the manifold empirically. But in the perception of that which happens, this rule is always to be met with, and it makes the order of the successive perceptions (in the apprehension of this appearance) necessary.",["- Sentence \"Allein ich bemerke auch…\" divided; the doubled \"daß\" is an irregularity smoothed in English.","- Last sentence of the house example: \"wenn ich … anfangen müßte\" is awkward; rendered \"at which point I must begin\", sense judged from the argument.","- \"die leztere\" (the latter) taken to be apprehension; \"Wahrnehmung\" = \"perception\", *Anschauung* = \"intuition\"."]],"krv-a-0376":["In the case before us, therefore, I must derive the subjective sequence of apprehension from the objective sequence of appearances. Otherwise the former is completely undetermined and does not distinguish one appearance from another. The subjective sequence by itself proves nothing as to the connection of the manifold in the object, since it is wholly arbitrary. The objective sequence will therefore consist in that order of the manifold of appearance according to which the apprehension of one thing (that which happens) follows upon the apprehension of another (that which precedes) in accordance with a rule. Only so can I be justified in asserting, of the appearance itself and not merely of my apprehension, that a sequence is to be met with in it. This is equivalent to saying that I cannot arrange the apprehension in any other order than precisely this sequence.",["- \"Jene/iene\" (the subjective sequence) and \"Diese\" (the objective) resolved by the argument; I have written the nouns out in English.","- The first sentence is divided and \"weil\" rendered as a separate sentence beginning \"Otherwise\"; the final \"welches\" rendered \"This is equivalent to saying\".","- \"Obiect\" = object; \"Folge\" = sequence (the sense of \"succession\" is kept for *Succession* in later passages)."]],"krv-a-0377":["In accordance with such a rule, then, there must lie in what precedes an event the condition of a rule by which this event always and necessarily follows. I cannot, however, reverse the order, proceeding back from the event and determining (through apprehension) what precedes it. For no appearance goes back from the succeeding point of time to the preceding point, though it is related to some preceding point of time; whereas the advance from a given time to the determinate time that follows is necessary. Since, then, there is something that follows, I must relate it necessarily to something else in general which precedes, and upon which it follows in accordance with a rule, that is, necessarily. The event, as the conditioned, thus affords sure indication of some condition, and this condition determines the event.",["- \"Nach einer solchen Regel\" retained as an opening phrase; \"Regel\" in \"Bedingung zu einer Regel\" is awkward and rendered literally (\"the condition of a rule\").","- \"überhaupt\" is attached to \"something else in general\"; the sentence \"Denn von dem folgenden Zeitpunct…\" is kept as one, with \"doch\" as \"though\".","- \"Anweisung\" = indication."]],"krv-a-0378":["Suppose that nothing precedes an event upon which it must follow in accordance with a rule. All sequence of perception would then be solely in the apprehension, that is, merely subjective; and it would not thereby be objectively determined which of the perceptions ought properly to be the preceding and which the succeeding. We should in this way have only a play of representations, relating to no object. That is to say, our perception would not distinguish one appearance from another as regards temporal relation, since the succession in apprehending is everywhere the same, and there is nothing in the appearance which so determines it as to make a certain sequence objectively necessary. I shall not, then, say that two states follow one another in the appearance, but only that one apprehension follows upon the other. This is something merely subjective, determining no object, and cannot therefore count as knowledge of any object, not even of an object in the [sphere of] appearance.",["- Sentence beginning \"Wir würden…\" divided; \"d. i.\" and \"weil\" rendered as in the English.","- \"selbst nicht in der Erscheinung\" is elliptical; I supply \"[sphere of]\" in brackets. \"Erkentniß\" = knowledge.","- \"allerwerts\" = everywhere (A text spelling)."]],"krv-a-0379":["When, therefore, we experience that something happens, we always presuppose that something precedes upon which it follows in accordance with a rule. Without this I could not say of the object that it follows, since mere sequence in my apprehension, if not determined by a rule in relation to something preceding, does not justify the assertion of a sequence in the object. It is always, then, in reference to a rule by which appearances, in their sequence, that is, as they happen, are determined by the preceding state, that I make my subjective synthesis (of apprehension) objective. Only under this presupposition is even the experience of something that happens possible.",["- The final clause, with its \"und, nur lediglich…allein,\" is smoothed; \"lediglich\" and \"allein\" are not both kept.","- \"berechtiget\" = justifies; \"Rücksicht\" = reference."]],"krv-a-0380":["It may seem, indeed, that this contradicts all the observations which have ever been made on the procedure of our use of understanding. According to these, it is only through the perceived and compared agreeing sequences of many events upon preceding appearances that we have been led to discover a rule according to which certain events always follow upon certain appearances, and have thereby been first induced to form for ourselves the concept of cause. On this footing the concept would be merely empirical, and the rule which it supplies, that everything which happens has a cause, would be as contingent as experience itself. Its universality and necessity would then be merely fictitious, and would have no true universal validity, since they would not be grounded *a priori* but only on induction. The case is, however, the same as with other pure representations *a priori* (for example, space and time). We can extract these from experience as clear concepts only because we have put them into experience, and have thereby first brought experience about. The logical clearness of this representation of a rule determining the series of events, as a concept of cause, is no doubt possible only when we have made use of it in experience. Nevertheless, a reference to this rule, as condition of the synthetic unity of appearances in time, was the ground of experience itself, and therefore preceded it *a priori*.",["- The long first sentence is divided into two; \"angedichtet\" = fictitious (\"attributed by invention\").","- \"Dignität\" and the like do not occur here; \"Verstandesgebrauch\" = use of understanding.","- \"dieselbe\" in the last sentence refers to the rule (\"Regel\"), not to \"Erfahrung\"; resolved by sense."]],"krv-a-0381":["What is required, then, is to show by an example that even in experience we never ascribe sequence (in an event, where something happens that previously was not) to the object, and distinguish it from the subjective sequence of our apprehension, except when a rule lies at the basis which constrains us to observe this order of perceptions rather than any other. Indeed, it is this constraint which first makes possible the representation of a succession in the object.",["- \"als wenn\" (after \"niemals…als\") rendered \"except when\"; \"nöthig\" read as \"nöthigt\" (\"constrains\"); the German text as given has \"nöthig\" (probable OCR/old spelling).","- \"Nöthigung\" = constraint."]],"krv-a-0382":["We have representations in us, of which we can also become conscious. However far this consciousness may extend, and however exact or precise it may be, they still remain only representations, that is, inner determinations of our mind in this or that relation of time. How, then, do we come to posit an object for these representations, or to ascribe to them, over and above their subjective reality as modifications, some kind of objective reality [I know not what]? Objective meaning cannot consist in the relation to another representation (of that which one might call the object); for otherwise the question is renewed, how does this representation in turn go out beyond itself and acquire objective meaning in addition to the subjective meaning which is proper to it as a determination of the state of mind? If we inquire what new character the relation to an object gives to our representations, and what dignity they thereby acquire, we find that it does nothing more than make the combination of representations necessary in a certain manner and subject them to a rule. Conversely, it is only because a certain order in the temporal relation of our representations is necessary that objective meaning is bestowed upon them.",["- \"ich weis nicht, was vor eine\" rendered in brackets as \"[I know not what]\" to mark the colloquial parenthesis; it is Kant's own phrase.","- \"Gemüthszustandes\" = state of mind; \"Modificationen\" = modifications.","- The long sentence on \"Dignität\" is retained as one after \"we find that\"; the semicolon before \"daß umgekehrt\" becomes a full stop."]],"krv-a-0383":["In the synthesis of appearances the manifold of representations always follows upon one another. No object is thereby represented; for through this sequence, which is common to all apprehensions, nothing is distinguished from anything else. But as soon as I perceive, or presuppose, that in this sequence there is a relation to the preceding state, from which the representation follows in accordance with a rule, something presents itself as an event, that is, as something that happens. In other words, I know an object which I have to locate in time at a certain determinate position, a position which, in view of the preceding state, cannot be assigned to it otherwise. When, therefore, I perceive that something happens, this representation contains, in the first place, that something precedes; for it is just in relation to what precedes that the appearance acquires its time-relation, namely, that of existing after a preceding time in which it was not. But it can acquire its determinate position in time, in this relation, only through something being presupposed in the preceding state upon which it always, that is, in accordance with a rule, follows. From this it results, first, that I cannot reverse the series, placing that which happens before that upon which it follows; and secondly, that if the preceding state is posited, this determinate event inevitably and necessarily follows. Thus it comes about that there arises an order among our representations in which the present (so far as it has come to be) points to some preceding state, as a correlate—though one still undetermined—of this event which is given. This correlate is related to the event, as its consequence, in a determining manner, and connects the event necessarily with itself in the time-series.",[]],"krv-a-0384":["Now if it is a necessary law of our sensibility, and therefore a formal condition of all perceptions, that the preceding time necessarily determines the following (since I can arrive at the following time only through the preceding), it is also an indispensable law of the empirical representation of the time-series that the appearances of past time determine every existence in the following time, and that these latter, as events, take place only in so far as the former determine their existence in time, that is, fix it in accordance with a rule. For it is only in appearances that we can empirically know this continuity in the connection of times.",[]],"krv-a-0385":["Understanding is required for all experience and for its possibility. Its first contribution is not that it renders the representation of objects distinct, but that it makes the representation of an object in general possible. This it does by carrying the time-order over to the appearances and their existence. To each of them, as a consequence, it assigns a position in time which is determined *a priori* in respect of the preceding appearances; without such a position the appearance would not accord with time itself, which determines *a priori* the position of all its parts. This determination of position cannot be borrowed from the relation of appearances to absolute time (for that is not an object of perception). On the contrary, appearances must determine for one another their positions in time itself, and must make these positions necessary in the time-order. That is to say, what follows or happens must follow, in accordance with a universal rule, upon that which was contained in the preceding state. There thus arises a series of appearances which, by means of the understanding, produces and makes necessary in the series of possible perceptions precisely the same order and continuous connection as is found *a priori* in the form of inner intuition (time), in which all perceptions must have their position.",[]],"krv-a-0386":["That something happens is, therefore, a perception which belongs to a possible experience. This experience becomes actual when I regard the appearance as determined in respect of its position in time, and so as an object which can always be found, in accordance with a rule, in the connection of perceptions. This rule, however, for determining something in the order of time, is that the condition under which the event always (that is, necessarily) follows is to be found in what precedes. The principle of sufficient reason is therefore the ground of possible experience, that is, of the objective knowledge of appearances, in respect of their relation in the order of time.",[]],"krv-a-0387":["The ground of proof of this principle rests solely on the following considerations. All empirical knowledge involves the synthesis of the manifold by the imagination, which is always successive; that is, in it representations always follow upon one another. But in the imagination the order of the sequence (what must precede and what must follow) is not determined at all, and the series of successive representations can be taken just as well backwards as forwards. If, however, this synthesis is a synthesis of apprehension (of the manifold of a given appearance), then the order is determined in the object; or, to speak more exactly, there is in it an order of successive synthesis which determines an object, and in accordance with this order something must necessarily precede, and when this is posited, the other must necessarily follow. If, then, my perception is to contain the knowledge of an event, that is, of something as actually happening, it must be an empirical judgment in which the sequence is thought as determined; that is, it must be thought as presupposing another appearance in time, upon which it follows necessarily, or in accordance with a rule. Otherwise, if I posited the antecedent and the event did not necessarily follow upon it, I should have to regard the event as merely a subjective play of my imaginings; and if I still represented to myself something objective in it, I should have to call it a mere dream. Thus the relation of appearances (as possible perceptions), according to which the subsequent (what happens) is, in its existence, necessarily determined by something preceding and in accordance with a rule in time—in other words, the relation of cause to effect—is the condition of the objective validity of our empirical judgments in respect of the series of perceptions, and so of their empirical truth, and therefore of experience. The principle of the causal relation in the sequence of appearances is consequently valid also of all objects of experience (under the conditions of succession), because it is itself the ground of the possibility of such experience.",["- 0383: the opening of the long sentences was divided; \"d. i. ich erkenne einen Gegenstand\" rendered as a fresh sentence \"In other words, I know an object…\"; \"Gegenstand\" = object, though Kant also writes \"Obiect\".","- 0383: \"Eräugniß\" (= Ereignis) read as \"event\"; \"Begebenheit\" also \"event\". \"Anweisung giebt\" rendered \"points to\".","- 0383: \"so stellet sich Etwas vor\" taken as \"something presents itself\" (reflexive), not \"something is represented\".","- 0384: \"Daseyn\" rendered \"existence\"; the parenthesis kept; \"iene … diese\" resolved as former (earlier appearances) / latter (later ones).","- 0385: \"deutlich\" rendered \"distinct\"; \"übertragen\" as \"carry over\". In the last sentence the construction \"eben dieselbige … als … angetroffen wird\" is irregular; I restated it as \"precisely the same order … as is found\".","- 0386: \"Zusammenhange der Wahrnehmungen\" kept as \"connection of perceptions\"; \"der Satz vom zureichenden Grunde\" = principle of sufficient reason.","- 0387: \"stellete ich mir darunter doch etwas obiectives vor, sie einen blossen Traum nennen\" is elliptical (the main verb is borrowed from \"würde ich … müssen\"); supplied \"I should have to\".","- 0387: \"vor allen Gegenständen\" read as \"von/für\" (valid of all objects); the original \"vor\" is a form of \"für\"."]],"krv-a-0388":["Here, however, a difficulty emerges which must be removed. The principle of the causal connection among appearances is, in our formula, limited to their serial succession. But in the actual employment of the principle it is found to apply also to their coexistence, and cause and effect can be simultaneous. For instance, there is warmth in the room which is not to be met with in the open air. I look around for the cause, and find a heated stove. Now this stove, as cause, is simultaneous with its effect, the warmth of the room. Here, then, there is no serial order in time between cause and effect; they are simultaneous, and yet the law holds. The greater part of the operating causes in nature are simultaneous with their effects, and the temporal sequence of the latter is due solely to the fact that the cause cannot achieve its total effect in one moment. But in the moment in which the effect first comes into being, it is always simultaneous with the causality of its cause. For if the cause had ceased to be a moment earlier, the effect would not have arisen at all. It must be carefully noted that what is in question is the order of time, not its lapse. The relation remains even if no time has elapsed. The time between the causality of the cause and its immediate effect may be vanishing [verschwindend], and the two may therefore be simultaneous; but the relation of the one to the other will still always be determinable in terms of time. If I consider a ball lying on a stuffed cushion and making a hollow in it, as a cause, it is simultaneous with its effect. Yet I distinguish the two through the time-relation of their dynamical connection. For if I lay the ball on the cushion, the hollow follows upon the previously smooth shape of the cushion; but if the cushion has a hollow (I know not how), a leaden ball does not follow upon it.",[]],"krv-a-0389":["Temporal sequence is therefore the sole empirical criterion of the effect in its relation to the causality of the cause which precedes it. The glass is the cause of the rising of the water above its horizontal surface, although the two appearances are simultaneous. For as soon as I draw water with the glass from a larger vessel, something follows, namely, the change of the horizontal level which the water had there into a concave form, which it assumes in the glass.",[]],"krv-a-0390":["This causality leads to the concept of action; action leads to the concept of force, and through it to the concept of substance. Since I do not wish my critical enterprise, which is concerned solely with the sources of synthetic knowledge *a priori*, to be encumbered with analyses which concern merely the elucidation (not the extension) of concepts, I leave the detailed discussion of them to a future system of pure reason. Such an analysis, indeed, is already to be found in abundant measure in the text-books of this kind hitherto known. But I cannot leave untouched the empirical criterion of a substance, in so far as substance seems to reveal itself not through the permanence of the appearance but better and more easily through action.",[]],"krv-a-0391":["Where there is action, and therefore activity and force, there is also substance, and in it alone must the seat of this fruitful source of appearances be sought. That is very well said; but when we are called upon to explain what is meant by substance, and in doing so seek to avoid a vicious circle, the answer is not so easily given. How are we to infer immediately from the action to the permanence of that which acts, which is yet so essential and distinctive a mark of substance (*phaenomenon*)? On the basis of what has been established above, the solution of the question presents no such difficulty, though by the usual procedure, which is merely analytic in dealing with its concepts, it would be quite insoluble. Action signifies the relation of the subject of causality to the effect. Now since all effect consists in what happens, and therefore in the changeable, which indicates time in the order of succession, the ultimate subject of the effect is the permanent, as the substratum of all that changes, that is, substance. For according to the principle of causality, actions are always the first ground of all change of appearances, and cannot therefore reside in a subject which itself changes, since otherwise other actions, and another subject determining this change, would be required. By virtue of this, action, as a sufficient empirical criterion, proves substantiality; I do not need first to seek the permanence of the subject by comparing perceptions. Nor could this, in fact, be done by that route with the fullness required by the extent and strict universal validity of the concept. For that the first subject of the causality of all coming-to-be and passing-away cannot itself (in the field of appearances) come to be and pass away, is a sure inference, which leads to empirical necessity and permanence in existence, and therefore to the concept of a substance as appearance.",[]],"krv-a-0392":["When something happens, the mere coming-to-be, apart from what it is that comes to be, is already in itself an object of enquiry. The transition from the non-being of a state to that state, even supposing that the state contained no quality in the appearance, itself alone requires investigation. This coming-to-be, as was shown in Number A, does not concern substance (for substance does not come to be) but its state. It is therefore merely alteration, and not origin out of nothing. If this origin is regarded as the effect of a foreign cause, it is entitled creation. Creation cannot be admitted as an event among appearances, since its mere possibility would destroy the unity of experience. Yet if I regard all things not as phenomena but as things in themselves, and as objects of the mere understanding, then, although they are substances, they can still be regarded as dependent in their existence upon a foreign cause. But this would involve quite different meanings of the words, and would not be applicable to appearances as possible objects of experience.",[]],"krv-a-0393":["How anything can be altered at all, how it is possible that a state in one point of time should be followed by an opposite state in another, of this we have *a priori* not the slightest concept. For this, knowledge of actual forces is required, which can only be given empirically, for instance, knowledge of the moving forces, or, what is the same thing, of certain successive appearances (as movements) which indicate such forces. But the form of every alteration, the condition under which alone it can take place as the coming-to-be of another state (whatever the content of the alteration, that is, the state which is altered, may be), and therefore the succession of the states themselves (what has happened), can still be considered *a priori* in accordance with the law of causality and the conditions of time.*)",[]],"krv-a-0394":["*) Let it be carefully noted that I am speaking not of the alteration of certain relations in general, but of alteration of state. Thus when a body moves uniformly, it does not alter its state (of motion) at all; it does so only when its motion increases or decreases.",["- 0388: \"Bedenklichkeit\" rendered \"difficulty\"; the long sentence on cause and effect is divided, and \"iene/diese\" (A 202) resolved as effect/cause in the sentence on the moment of coming-to-be. In the German the pronouns are ambiguous; I took \"sie\" as the effect, as the sense requires.","- 0388: \"verschwindend\" kept in brackets because the sense of a vanishing interval turns on it.","- 0390: \"Erkentniß\" = knowledge; \"Handlung\" = action; \"Kraft\" = force; \"Lehrbüchern\" = text-books.","- 0391: the closing sentence of the first half and the \"Denn...\" clause are divided or smoothed; \"Kraft dessen\" rendered \"By virtue of this\". The \"Denn\" sentence at the end is kept as one.","- 0391: \"Substanz (phœnomenon)\" retained, with *phaenomenon* in italics as a Latin technical term.","- 0392: \"Nummer A\" refers to the section on the First Analogy; kept as in the text. \"Wortbedeutungen\" rendered \"meanings of the words\"; the last clause is restated to make its subject plain.","- 0393: \"Kentniß\" rendered \"knowledge\"; the footnote marker *) is retained as in the source.","- 0394: footnote kept as a footnote; \"Relationen\" rendered \"relations\"."]],"krv-a-0395":["When a substance passes from one state, *a*, into another, *b*, the moment of the second state is distinct from the moment of the first, and follows upon it. Similarly, the second state, as a reality (in the appearance), is distinguished from the first, in which this reality was not, as *b* is distinguished from zero. That is to say, even if the state *b* differed from the state *a* only in magnitude, the change would be an coming-to-be of *b* − *a*, which was not in the previous state, and in respect of which that state is = 0.",["- \"Zero\" (Zero) rendered \"zero\"; \"Entstehen\" rendered \"coming-to-be\". Kant's \"an coming-to-be\" is not in the text; read \"a coming-to-be\".","- \"Erscheinung\" = appearance; \"Realität (in der Erscheinung)\" kept with parenthesis as in the German.","- The sentence beginning \"Eben so\" is divided at \"d. i.\" into two sentences for clarity."]],"krv-a-0396":["The question therefore is: how does a thing pass from a state = *a* into another = *b*? Between two instants there is always a time, and between two states in these instants there is always a difference which has a magnitude (for all parts of appearances are always again magnitudes). Every transition from one state to another therefore takes place in a time which is contained between two instants, of which the first determines the state out of which the thing proceeds, the second that into which it arrives. Both, then, are limits of the time of a change, and so of the intermediate state between the two states, and as such belong to the whole change. Now every change has a cause, which proves its causality in the whole of the time in which the change takes place. This cause, therefore, does not produce its change suddenly (all at once, or in an instant), but in a time. As the time grows from the initial instant *a* to its completion in *b*, the magnitude of the reality (*b* − *a*) is also generated through all the smaller degrees which are contained between the first and the last. All change is therefore possible only through a continuous action of causality, which, so far as it is uniform, is entitled a moment. The change does not consist of these moments, but is generated by them as their effect.",["- Long sentences divided; \"so, daß…\" restated as a separate sentence beginning \"As the time grows…\".","- \"Zeitpunct/Augenblick\" both rendered \"instant\"; \"Handlung der Caussalität\" = \"action of causality\"; \"heißt\" = \"is entitled\".","- \"Zustanden\" (sic) read as \"Zuständen\". \"bestimt\" = \"determines\"."]],"krv-a-0397":["This is the law of the continuity of all change. Its ground is this: neither time nor the appearance in time consists of parts which are the smallest, and yet the state of the thing, in the course of its change, passes through all these parts, as elements, to its second state. There is no smallest difference of the real in appearance, just as there is no smallest difference in the magnitude of times. And so the new state of reality grows from the first, in which it was not, through all the infinite degrees of that reality, the differences of which from one another are all smaller than that between 0 and *a*.",["- \"die- Zeit\" in the OCR corrected to \"die Zeit\".","- \"o\" read as \"0\". \"seyn\" (plural subjunctive) after \"kleiner\" is rendered indicatively.","- \"derselben\" (referring to Realität) resolved as \"that reality\"."]],"krv-a-0398":["What use this proposition may have in the investigation of nature does not concern us here. But how such a proposition, which seems so to extend our knowledge of nature, is possible completely *a priori*, calls very much for our examination, even though direct inspection proves that the proposition is real and correct, and one might therefore think oneself relieved of the question how it was possible. For there are so many kinds of groundless pretensions to extend our knowledge through pure reason that it must be adopted as a general principle to be altogether distrustful on this account, and, without documents which can furnish a thorough deduction, to believe and accept nothing of the kind, even on the clearest dogmatic proof.",["- \"Erkentniß\" = knowledge. \"Augenschein\" rendered \"direct inspection\".","- The final sentence is restructured slightly: the \"daß\" clause is kept, with its infinitives made parallel."]],"krv-a-0399":["All increase of empirical knowledge, and every advance of perception, is nothing but an extension of the determination of inner sense, that is, a progression in time, whatever the objects may be, whether appearances or pure intuitions. This progression in time determines everything, and is itself determined by nothing further. That is to say, its parts are given only in time and through the synthesis of time, and are not given prior to it. For this reason every transition in perception to something which follows in time is a determination of time through the production of this perception. And since time is always, and in all its parts, a magnitude, this is the production of a perception, as a magnitude, through all the degrees, none of which is the smallest, from zero up to its determinate degree. From this the possibility becomes clear of knowing a law of changes, as regards their form, *a priori*. We anticipate only our own apprehension, the formal condition of which, since it is present in us prior to all given appearance, must certainly be capable of being known *a priori*.",["- \"derselben\" and \"jene\" (both referring to time) resolved as \"its\" and \"time\"; \"sie aber nicht vor ihr\" read as the parts not given prior to the synthesis.","- \"Bestimmung\" = determination; \"Erzeugung\" = production; \"Wahrnehmung\" = perception; \"Apprehension\" = apprehension.","- Sentence beginning \"Um deswillen\" is divided; \"Anticipiren\" rendered \"anticipate\"."]],"krv-a-0400":["Thus, just as time contains the *a priori* sensible condition of the possibility of a continuous progress from what exists to what follows, so the understanding, by means of the unity of apperception, is the *a priori* condition of the possibility of a continuous determination of all positions for appearances in this time, through the series of causes and effects, the former of which unfailingly entail the existence of the latter, and thereby make the empirical knowledge of time-relations valid for every time (universally), and so objectively valid.",["- \"vor\" in \"vor die Erscheinungen\" and \"vor iede Zeit\" is read as \"für\" (for).","- \"deren die erstere der leztern ihr Daseyn\" rendered as \"the former of which … the existence of the latter\"."]],"krv-a-0401":["C. Third Analogy. Principle of Community.",[]],"krv-a-0402":["All substances, so far as they are simultaneous, stand in thoroughgoing community (that is, in reciprocal action upon one another).",["- \"Gemeinschaft\" = community; \"Wechselwirkung\" = reciprocal action. \"zugleich seyn\" = \"are simultaneous\"."]],"krv-a-0403":["Proof.",[]],"krv-a-0404":["Things are simultaneous so far as they exist in one and the same time. But by what do we recognise that they are in one and the same time? When the order in the synthesis of the apprehension of this manifold is indifferent, that is, when the synthesis can proceed from A through B, C, D to E, or conversely from E to A. For if they were in time successive to one another (in the order beginning with A and ending in E), it would be impossible to begin the apprehension in the perception with E and to proceed backwards to A, since A belongs to past time, and therefore can no longer be an object of apprehension.",["- \"gleichgültig\" rendered \"indifferent\". \"Gegenstand\" = object.","- \"kan\" in \"umgekehrt … gehen kan\" read as dependent on the preceding \"ist\": apprehension \"can go\" either way."]],"krv-a-0405":["Suppose now that in a manifold of substances, as appearances, each were completely isolated, that is, none acted upon another or received reciprocal influences from it. I say then that their simultaneity would not be an object of possible perception, and that the existence of one could not, by any path of empirical synthesis, lead to the existence of another. For if you think of them as separated by a completely empty space, the perception which proceeds in time from one to the other would indeed determine the existence of the latter by means of a subsequent perception, but would not be able to distinguish whether the appearance follows objectively upon the former, or is rather simultaneous with it.",["- \"dieser ihr Daseyn\" resolved as the existence of the second (latter) substance; \"iener\" as the former.","- \"Mannigfaltigkeit\" rendered \"manifold\"."]],"krv-a-0406":["There must therefore be something besides mere existence by which A determines for B its position in time, and conversely B also for A. Only under this condition can substances, thought as existing simultaneously, be represented empirically. Now only that which is the cause of another, or of its determinations, determines for it its position in time. Hence each substance (since it can be a consequence only in respect of its determinations) must contain in itself the causality of certain determinations in the other, and at the same time the effects of the causality of the other. That is, the substances must stand in dynamical community (immediate or mediate), if their simultaneity is to be known in any possible experience. Now that is necessary in respect of objects of experience without which experience of these objects themselves would be impossible. Hence it is necessary for all substances in the appearance, so far as they are simultaneous, to stand in thoroughgoing community of reciprocal action with one another.",["- Sentences divided at \"Nun bestimt…\" and \"d. i.\"; \"weil nur unter dieser Bedingung\" restated as a separate sentence.","- \"Bestimmungen\" = determinations; \"vorgestellt\" = represented (*vorstellen*, Vorstellung).","- \"Folge\" rendered \"consequence\"; the parenthesis kept as in German."]],"krv-a-0407":["The word 'community' is ambiguous in our language, and may mean either *communio* or *commercium*. We here employ it in the latter sense, as signifying a dynamical community, without which even local community (*communio spatii*) could never be empirically known. Our experiences make it easy to observe that only continuous influences in all parts of space can lead our senses from one object to another; that the light which plays between our eye and the heavenly bodies produces a mediate community between us and them, and thereby proves the coexistence of the latter; and that we cannot empirically change our place (perceive such a change) unless matter everywhere makes the perception of our position possible to us. Matter can show its coexistence only by means of its reciprocal influence, and thereby, as far as the most remote objects, can establish their coexistence (though only mediately). Without community, every perception (of an appearance in space) is broken off from every other, and the chain of empirical representations, that is, experience, would have to begin entirely afresh with each new object, without the preceding experience being in the least connected with it or standing to it in any time-relation. I do not intend by this to refute empty space. It may exist wherever perceptions do not reach, and where, therefore, there is no empirical knowledge of coexistence. But it is then not an object at all for our possible experience.",["- Restated the long second sentence's string of *daß*-clauses as a semicolon series and divided its final clause into two sentences, for clarity.","- \"Matter can show…\" resolves the ambiguous *diese* (matter, not the perception); *Zugleichseyn/Coexistenz* rendered \"coexistence\".","- *Gemeinschaft* = \"community\"; *Wahrnehmung* = \"perception\"; *Erkentniß* = \"knowledge\"."]],"krv-a-0408":["The following may serve by way of elucidation. In our mind all appearances, as contained in a possible experience, must stand in community (*communio*) of apperception. In so far as the objects are to be represented as connected in coexistence, they must reciprocally determine their position in one time, and thereby constitute a whole. If this subjective community is to rest on an objective ground, or is to be referred to appearances as substances, the perception of the one must, as ground, make possible the perception of the other, and conversely. Only so can the succession which is always found in perceptions, as apprehensions, be prevented from being ascribed to the objects, and these be represented as coexistent. But this is a reciprocal influence, that is, a real community (*commercium*) of substances; without it the empirical relation of coexistence could not be met with in experience. Through this *commercium* appearances, in so far as they are outside one another and yet stand in connection, constitute a composite (*compositum reale*); and composites of this kind are possible in many different ways. The three dynamical relations, from which all others arise, are therefore those of inherence, of consequence, and of composition.",["- Divided the sentence beginning \"Soll diese subiective Gemeinschaft…\" and supplied \"Only so can… be prevented\" to make the *damit*-clause's purpose explicit.","- *Succeßion* \"succession\"; *Apprehensionen* \"apprehensions\"; *Zusammengesetztes* rendered \"composite\"."]],"krv-a-0409":["These, then, are the three analogies of experience. They are nothing but principles of the determination of the existence of appearances in time, in respect of all three of its *modi*: the relation to time itself as a magnitude (the magnitude of existence, that is, duration); the relation in time as a series (successive); and finally the relation in time as the sum-total of all existence (simultaneous). This unity of time-determination is thoroughly dynamical. That is to say, time is not regarded as that in which experience would immediately assign to every existence its position. That is impossible, since absolute time is not an object of perception with which appearances could be held together. On the contrary, the rule of the understanding, through which alone the existence of appearances can acquire synthetic unity in respect of time-relations, determines for each of them its position in time, and therefore *a priori*, and with validity for each and every time.",["- Broke the sentence at \"Diese Einheit…\" and again at \"welches unmöglich ist\", rendering the relative clause as a separate sentence.","- Supplied \"[successive]\", \"[simultaneous]\" only as the German's *nach einander* / *zugleich* in parentheses; *modis* kept in Latin and italicised.","- *Bestimmung* \"determination\"; *Verstand* \"understanding\"."]],"krv-a-0410":["By nature (in the empirical sense) we understand the connection of appearances in respect of their existence, according to necessary rules, that is, according to laws. There are, then, certain laws, and indeed *a priori*, which first make a nature possible. The empirical laws can obtain, and be discovered, only by means of experience, and indeed in consequence of those original laws through which experience itself first becomes possible. Our analogies therefore really exhibit the unity of nature in the connection of all appearances under certain exponents, which express nothing but the relation of time (in so far as it comprehends all existence in itself) to the unity of apperception, a unity which can obtain only in synthesis according to rules. Taken together, they say: all appearances lie in one nature, and must so lie, since without this unity *a priori* no unity of experience, and therefore also no determination of objects in experience, would be possible.",["- Divided the second sentence at the semicolon; \"which first make a nature possible\" renders *allererst*.","- *Exponenten* \"exponents\"; *Apperception* \"apperception\"."]],"krv-a-0411":["A remark must be made upon the mode of proof which we have employed in dealing with these transcendental laws of nature, and upon its peculiar character. The remark must also be of great importance as a precept for any other attempt to prove intellectual and at the same time synthetic propositions *a priori*. Had we sought to prove these analogies dogmatically, that is, from concepts (namely, that everything which exists is to be found only in what is permanent; that every event presupposes something in the preceding state upon which it follows according to a rule; and finally that, in the manifold which is coexistent, the states are coexistent in relation to one another according to a rule, i.e. stand in community), all our labour would have been entirely in vain. For from one object and its existence we can never arrive at the existence of another, or at its mode of existence, through mere concepts of these things, however we may analyse them. What then remained to us? The possibility of experience, as a knowledge in which all objects must in the end be capable of being given to us, if their representation is to have objective reality for us. In this third thing, the essential form of which consists in the synthetic unity of the apperception of all appearances, we found *a priori* conditions of the thoroughgoing and necessary time-determination of all existence in appearance, without which even empirical time-determination would be impossible; and we found rules of synthetic unity *a priori*, by means of which we could anticipate experience. Through the lack of this method, and through the delusion that synthetic propositions, which the empirical employment of the understanding recommends as its principles, can be dogmatically proved, it has come about that a proof of the principle of sufficient reason has been so often attempted, and always in vain. Of the other two analogies no one has thought, although they have always been tacitly employed;*) for the clue of the categories was lacking, which alone can discover, and make evident, every gap in the understanding, in concepts as well as in principles.",["- Divided the opening sentence in two; in the parenthetical list \"seyn\" is regularised to \"are\", the German construction being irregular after *daß … endlich, in dem Mannigfaltigen*.","- \"[a] third thing\" for *Dritten*: the possibility of experience as the third term between concepts; *Erkentniß* \"knowledge\".","- Footnote marker *) kept as in the text."]],"krv-a-0412":["*) The unity of the world-whole, in which all appearances are supposed to be connected, is manifestly a mere consequence of the tacitly assumed principle of the community of all substances which coexist. For if they were isolated, they would not, as parts, constitute a whole; and if their connection (reciprocal action of the manifold) were not necessary already on account of coexistence, we could not infer from coexistence, as a merely ideal relation, to connection as a real one. Yet we have shown in its proper place that community is really the ground of the possibility of an empirical knowledge of coexistence, and that we therefore really infer backwards only from the latter to the former, as its condition.",["- Divided the first sentence at \"denn\"; *Wechselwirkung* \"reciprocal action\". The final *Wiewol* clause is fragmentary in construction; rendered as \"Yet…\".","- \"the former/the latter\" resolves *iene/dieser* so that community is the condition and coexistence the conditioned."]],"krv-a-0413":["4. The Postulates of Empirical Thought in General.",[]],"krv-a-0414":["1. That which agrees with the formal conditions of experience (in respect of intuition and of concepts) is possible.",[]],"krv-a-0415":["2. That which is bound up with the material conditions of experience (with sensation) is actual.",[]],"krv-a-0416":["3. That whose connection with the actual is determined according to universal conditions of experience is (exists) necessarily.",["- Rendered *nothwendig* adverbially as \"necessarily\"."]],"krv-a-0417":["Elucidation.",[]],"krv-a-0418":["The categories of modality have this peculiarity, that in being attached as predicates to a concept they do not in the least enlarge it as a determination of the object, but only express its relation to the faculty of knowledge. Even if the concept of a thing is already completely full, I can still ask of this object whether it is merely possible, or also actual, or, if it is actual, whether it is also necessary. No further determinations in the object itself are thereby thought. The only question is how the object, together with all its determinations, stands in relation to the understanding and its empirical employment, to the empirical power of judgment, and to reason (in its application to experience).",["- Divided the final sentence in two to separate the point from the question; \"es frägt sich nur … verhalte\" is rendered as a question.","- *Erkentnißvermögen* = \"faculty of knowledge\"; *Urtheilskraft* = \"power of judgment\".","- \"Vollständig\" is rendered \"completely full\"; \"Obiect\" and \"Gegenstand\" are both given as \"object\"."]],"krv-a-0419":["For this very reason the principles of modality are nothing more than definitions of the concepts of possibility, actuality and necessity in their empirical employment. At the same time they are restrictions of all the categories to merely empirical employment, and they neither admit nor permit transcendental employment. For if the categories are not to have a merely logical significance, analytically expressing the form of thought, but are to concern things and their possibility, actuality or necessity, they must relate to possible experience and its synthetic unity. In that unity alone are objects of knowledge given.",["- Split the last sentence at \"in welcher allein\" to make a separate sentence.","- \"Diese\" in \"wenn diese nicht\" is resolved as the categories (not the principles), as the argument requires.","- *Erklärungen* = \"definitions\"; *Erkentniß* = \"knowledge\"."]],"krv-a-0420":["The postulate of the possibility of things therefore demands that the concept of them agree with the formal conditions of an experience in general. These conditions, that is, the objective form of experience in general, contain all the synthesis which is required for knowledge of objects. A concept which comprises a synthesis within itself is to be held empty, and does not relate to any object, if this synthesis does not belong to experience. It may belong to experience in one of two ways. Either the synthesis is borrowed from experience, and the concept is then entitled an empirical concept; or it is a synthesis on which, as an *a priori* condition, experience in general (its form) rests, and the concept is then a pure concept, which none the less belongs to experience, since its object can be met with only in experience.\n\nFor where are we to find the character of the possibility of an object thought through a synthetic concept *a priori*, if not in the synthesis which constitutes the form of the empirical knowledge of objects? That no contradiction may be contained in such a concept is, indeed, a necessary logical condition; but it is by no means sufficient for the objective reality of the concept, that is, for the possibility of such an object as is thought through the concept. Thus there is no contradiction in the concept of a figure enclosed within two straight lines, for the concepts of two straight lines and of their meeting contain no negation of a figure. The impossibility rests not on the concept in itself, but on its construction in space, that is, on the conditions of space and of its determination. These conditions, however, have in their turn their objective reality, that is, they apply to possible things, because they contain *a priori* in themselves the form of experience in general.",["- Restated the long second and third sentences as several; the \"entweder … oder\" is set out as \"in one of two ways\".","- \"vor leer\" read as \"für leer\" (OCR/orthography), \"denn\" as \"dann\" (\"then\").","- *Bestimmung* of space rendered \"determination\"; *Zusammenstossung* rendered \"meeting\".","- \"als durch den Begriff gedacht wird\" rendered \"as is thought through the concept\"."]],"krv-a-0421":["We shall now set forth the wide utility and influence of this postulate of possibility. If I represent to myself a thing that is permanent, so that everything which changes belongs merely to its state, I can never know from such a concept alone that a thing of this kind is possible. Or again, I represent to myself something of such a nature that, when it is posited, something else invariably and unfailingly follows upon it. This may no doubt be thought without contradiction; but whether such a property (as causality) is to be met with in any possible thing cannot thereby be judged. Finally, I can represent to myself different things (substances) so constituted that the state of one entails a consequence in the state of another, and so reciprocally. But whether such a relation can belong to any things cannot be gathered at all from these concepts, which contain a merely arbitrary synthesis.\n\nOnly through the fact, then, that these concepts express *a priori* the relations of perceptions in every experience, do we know their objective reality, that is, their transcendental truth. This knowledge is indeed independent of experience, but not independent of all relation to the form of an experience in general, and to the synthetic unity in which alone objects can be empirically known.",["- Divided the final sentence of the German at \"Nur daran also\" into a separate paragraph for clarity; the closing \"und zwar freilich … aber doch\" is restated as \"This knowledge is indeed independent …\".","- \"Oder, ich stelle mir etwas vor\" is rendered \"Or again, I represent …\".","- *Wahrnehmungen* = \"perceptions\"; *Wechselsweise* = \"reciprocally\"."]],"krv-a-0422":["But if we sought to make wholly new concepts of substances, of forces and of reciprocal actions out of the material which perception presents to us, without borrowing from experience itself the example of their connection, we should fall into mere chimeras. Their possibility would have no criterion whatever, since in their case we do not accept experience as our teacher, nor borrow these concepts from it. Such fictitious concepts cannot acquire the character of their possibility *a priori*, as the categories do, as conditions upon which all experience depends, but only *a posteriori*, as concepts given through experience itself. Their possibility must therefore be known either *a posteriori* and empirically, or not at all.\n\nA substance which would be permanently present in space without filling it (like that intermediate thing between matter and thinking beings which some have wished to introduce), or a special fundamental power of our mind to intuit the future beforehand (not merely to infer it), or finally a faculty of the mind to stand in community of thought with other men (however distant they may be): these are concepts whose possibility is wholly groundless. It cannot be based on experience and its known laws, and without such a basis it is an arbitrary combination of thoughts, which, although it contains no contradiction, can lay no claim to objective reality, and therefore to the possibility of such an object as we here propose to think.\n\nAs to reality, it is of itself forbidden to think any such in the concrete without the aid of experience. For reality can relate only to sensation, as the matter of experience, and does not concern the form of the relation, with which we might at a pinch play in fictions.",["- Broke the long German sentences into several and a paragraph for each main point; \"das sind Begriffe\" is rendered after a colon.","- \"vor sich\" in \"kein Kennzeichen vor sich hat\" rendered \"no criterion whatever\"; \"Hirngespinste\" = \"chimeras\".","- *Anschauen* = \"intuit\"; *Grundkraft* = \"fundamental power\"; *Gemüth* = \"mind\".","- \"und ohne sie eine willkührliche Gedankenverbindung ist die\" is slightly irregular; smoothed to \"without such a basis it is an arbitrary combination of thoughts, which …\".","- \"Mittelding\" etc. as in the German; \"allenfals\" rendered \"at a pinch\"."]],"krv-a-0423":["But I pass over everything whose possibility can be gathered only from actuality in experience, and consider here only the possibility of things through concepts *a priori*. Of these I continue to assert that their possibility can never be established from such concepts by themselves alone, but only as the formal and objective conditions of an experience in general.",["- Divided the second clause, \"von denen ich fortfahre zu behaupten\", into a separate sentence and made explicit \"Of these\" = things through concepts *a priori*.","- \"statt finden können\" rendered \"can be established\", given the sense of the context (\"their possibility … can be found/hold\")."]],"krv-a-0424":["It certainly seems as if the possibility of a triangle could be known from its concept taken by itself (the concept is certainly independent of experience); for we can in fact give an object to the concept entirely *a priori*, that is, we can construct it. But since this construction yields only the form of an object, the triangle would always remain a mere product of the imagination, and the possibility of its object would still be doubtful. For that something more is required, namely, that such a figure be thought solely under those conditions upon which all objects of experience rest. Now that space is a formal condition *a priori* of outer experiences, and that the very same formative synthesis through which we construct a triangle in imagination is entirely identical with that which we exercise in the apprehension of an appearance in order to make for ourselves an empirical concept of it, this alone is what connects with the concept the representation of the possibility of such a thing. In the same way the possibility of continuous magnitudes, indeed of magnitudes in general, since the concepts of them are one and all synthetic, is never clear from the concepts themselves, but only from them taken as formal conditions of the determination of objects in experience in general. And where, indeed, could we seek objects corresponding to the concepts, if not in experience, through which alone objects are given to us? We can, it is true, without first presupposing experience itself, know and characterise the possibility of things, and do so completely *a priori*; but only in relation to the formal conditions under which something in experience is determined as object, and so only in relation to experience and within its limits.",["- Restated the final sentence: the German's long \"wie wol…\" clause is recast as a concessive main clause (\"We can, it is true…\"); the rhetorical question is set off as a separate sentence.","- \"Weil dieses aber nur die Form von einem Gegenstande ist\": \"dieses\" taken as the construction; \"[this construction]\" rendered without brackets as \"this construction yields\" (supplied by judgment).","- \"als wozu noch etwas mehr erfordert wird\" turned into a separate sentence beginning \"For that something more is required\".","- \"ihn construiren\": \"ihn\" = the concept (Begriff), hence \"it\" for the concept; \"er\" in \"würde er … bleiben\" = the triangle. Ambiguity resolved by sense.","- \"aus ihnen, als formalen Bedingungen, der Bestimmung der Gegenstände\": \"ihnen\" = the concepts; read \"of the determination\" as dependent on \"Bedingungen\".","- \"erkant\" (OCR/old spelling) read as \"erkannt\"; \"Triangel\" = triangle. \"allererst klar\" rendered \"clear\" with \"never…but only\" to carry the sense.","- \"Erfahrungsbegriff\" = \"empirical concept\"; \"Einbildungskraft\" = imagination."]],"krv-a-0425":["The postulate for knowing the actuality of things demands perception, and therefore sensation of which one is conscious. It does not, indeed, demand immediate perception of the object itself whose existence is to be known; but it does demand that the object be connected with some actual perception, in accordance with the analogies of experience, which set forth all real connection in an experience in general.",["- Split at the semicolon to resolve the parenthetical \"zwar nicht eben unmittelbar… aber doch\"; the verb \"fordert\" is repeated in English as \"demand\" for clarity.","- \"Wirklichkeit\" = actuality; \"Daseyn\" = existence; \"Wahrnehmung\" = perception.","- \"desselben\" taken to refer to the object (Gegenstand), not to the perception."]],"krv-a-0426":["In the mere concept of a thing no mark of its existence can be found. For however complete the concept may be, so that nothing is lacking that is required for thinking a thing with all its inner determinations, existence has nothing whatever to do with all this. It concerns only the question whether such a thing is given to us, in such a manner that perception of it could, if need be, precede the concept. For that the concept precedes the perception signifies merely its possibility; the perception, which supplies the material for the concept, is the sole mark of actuality. But we can also know the existence of a thing prior to its perception, and therefore comparatively *a priori*, provided it is connected with certain perceptions in accordance with the principles of their empirical connection (the analogies). For in that case the existence of the thing is still connected with our perceptions in a possible experience, and, guided by those analogies, we can advance from our actual perception to the thing in the series of possible perceptions. Thus we know the existence of a magnetic matter pervading all bodies from the perception of the attracted iron filings, although, owing to the constitution of our organs, an immediate perception of this matter is impossible for us. For in general, in accordance with the laws of sensibility and the context of our perceptions, we should in an experience come upon the immediate empirical intuition of this matter, if our senses were finer; the coarseness of our senses does not in the least affect the form of possible experience in general. Thus, wherever perception and what attaches to it in accordance with empirical laws extends, there too extends our knowledge of the existence of things. If we do not begin with experience, or do not proceed in accordance with the laws of the empirical connection of appearances, we are only making a vain show when we pretend to divine or discover the existence of any thing.",["- \"Character\" rendered \"mark\" (Merkmal sense); \"comparative a priori\" kept as \"comparatively *a priori*\".","- The sentence \"Denn ob derselbe gleich…\" is divided in two after \"all this\" to make the \"sondern nur\" clause intelligible.","- \"derselben\" in \"unmittelbare empirische Anschauung derselben\" read as the magnetic matter (Stoff), supplied as \"this matter\"; \"Anhang\" rendered \"what attaches to it\".","- \"deren Grobheit … nichts angeht\" rendered as a separate clause; \"deren\" refers to \"Sinnen\".","- \"Eisenfeiligs\" = iron filings; \"Staat machen\" rendered \"making a vain show\" (idiomatic: to pretend to something).","- \"Erkentniß\" = knowledge; \"Sinnen\" = senses."]],"krv-a-0427":["As regards the third postulate, finally, it concerns material necessity in existence, not the merely formal and logical necessity in the connection of concepts. Now no existence of objects of the senses can be known completely *a priori*; it can be known only comparatively *a priori*, relatively to some other existence already given. Even so, we can arrive only at that existence which must be contained somewhere in the context of experience, of which the given perception is a part. Hence the necessity of existence can never be known from concepts, but always only from connection with what is perceived, in accordance with universal laws of experience.\n\nNow the only existence which, under the condition of other given appearances, can be known as necessary is the existence of effects from given causes, in accordance with the laws of causality. It is therefore not the existence of things (substances) but the existence of their state of which alone we can know the necessity; and we can know it only from other states which are given in perception, according to empirical laws of causality. From this it follows that the criterion of necessity lies solely in the law of possible experience, namely that everything which happens is determined *a priori* through its cause in the appearance. Thus we know only the necessity of those effects in nature whose causes are given to us. The mark of necessity in existence extends no further than the field of possible experience; and even within that field it does not hold of the existence of things as substances, since these can never be regarded as empirical effects, or as something that happens and comes to be. Necessity concerns, therefore, only the relations of appearances according to the dynamical law of causality, and the possibility, grounded upon that law, of inferring *a priori*, from any given existence (a cause), to another existence (the effect).\n\nEverything that happens is hypothetically necessary. This is a principle which subjects alterations in the world to a law, that is, to a rule of necessary existence, without which there would be no nature at all. Accordingly the proposition, Nothing happens through blind chance (*in mundo non datur casus*), is an *a priori* law of nature. So also is the proposition, No necessity in nature is blind, but is always conditioned, and therefore intelligible necessity (*non datur fatum*). Both are laws through which the play of alterations is subjected to a nature of things (as appearances); or, what is the same thing, to the unity of the understanding, in which alone the alterations can belong to an experience, as the synthetic unity of appearances. These two principles belong to the dynamical principles. The former is properly a consequence of the principle of causality (among the Analogies of Experience). The latter belongs to the principles of modality, which adds to the causal determination the concept of necessity, though this necessity stands under a rule of the understanding.\n\nThe principle of continuity forbade any leap in the series of appearances (alterations) (*in mundo non datur saltus*); and likewise, in the sum of all empirical intuitions in space, any gap or chasm between two appearances (*non datur hiatus*). For the proposition can be expressed thus: nothing can enter into experience which would prove a *vacuum*, or even admit it as a part of the empirical synthesis. As for the void which we may think outside the field of possible experience (the world), it does not come under the jurisdiction of the mere understanding, which decides only questions concerning the employment of given appearances for empirical knowledge. It is a problem for ideal reason, which goes beyond the sphere of a possible experience and seeks to judge of what surrounds and bounds that sphere; it must therefore be considered in the transcendental dialectic.\n\nThese four propositions (*in mundo non datur hiatus, non datur saltus, non datur casus, non datur fatum*) we could easily represent in their order, in accordance with the order of the categories, as we could all principles of transcendental origin, and could prove for each its proper place. But the reader, already practised, will do this for himself or will easily discover the clue to it. They all unite, however, in this single aim, that nothing be admitted in the empirical synthesis which could do damage or injury to the understanding and to the continuous connection of all appearances, that is, to the unity of its concepts. For it is the understanding alone in which the unity of experience, in which all perceptions must have their place, becomes possible.",["- Restated the long opening sentence (\"Da nun … so kan\") as several sentences; the German's concessive \"gleichwol … alsdenn nur\" is rendered \"Even so\".","- \"Zustand\" rendered \"state\"; \"Daseyn\" and \"Existenz\" both \"existence\"; \"Erkentniß\" \"knowledge\".","- \"daß alles, was geschieht … bestimt sey\" is taken as in apposition to \"Gesetze der möglichen Erfahrung\", hence \"namely that\".","- \"ihre Ursache\" (feminine/plural pronoun) is read as referring to \"alles, was geschieht\", but rendered \"its cause\"; the grammar is loose.","- \"einer Natur der Dinge … unterworfen\" is left close to the German; the sense seems \"subjected to a nature (i.e. lawful order) of things\". \"[alterations]\" is supplied in \"the alterations can belong\", where \"sie\" has \"Veränderungen\" as antecedent.","- \"Verständliche Nothwendigkeit\" rendered \"intelligible\", i.e. comprehensible through the understanding, not the noumenal sense.","- \"idealische Vernunft\" rendered \"ideal reason\", literally; possibly a slip for \"ideale/ideelle\", flagged as unusual.","- \"Spähre\" modernised to \"sphere\"; \"Caussalbestimmung\" rendered \"causal determination\"; \"vor\" in \"vor die Gerichtsbarkeit\" and \"Aufgabe vor\" read as \"für/vor\", rendered \"under\" and \"for\"."]],"krv-a-0428":["Whether the field of possibility is larger than the field which contains all that is actual, and this again larger than the sum of that which is necessary, are pretty questions, and questions moreover that call for a synthetic solution. But they come before the tribunal of reason alone. For what they amount to, roughly, is the question whether all things, as appearances, belong together in the sum and context of a single experience, of which every given perception is a part, so that this perception could not be connected with any other appearances; or whether my perceptions can belong to more than one possible experience (in their general connection).\n\nThe understanding gives to experience in general only the rule, in accordance with the subjective and formal conditions, of sensibility as well as of apperception, which alone make experience possible. Other forms of intuition (than space and time), and likewise other forms of understanding (than the discursive form of thinking, or of knowledge through concepts), even if they were possible, we cannot in any way conceive or render comprehensible to ourselves. And even if we could, they would still not belong to experience, the one mode of knowledge in which objects are given to us. Whether other perceptions than those which belong to our total possible experience can be met with, and so whether an entirely different field of matter may still exist, the understanding is unable to decide. It is concerned only with the synthesis of what is given.\n\nOtherwise the poverty of our ordinary inferences, by which we make out a great realm of possibility of which everything actual (every object of experience) is only a small part, is very striking. Everything actual is possible; from this there follows naturally, by the logical rules of conversion, the merely particular proposition, 'Some possible is actual'. This seems to mean that much is possible which is not actual. It does indeed appear as if we could directly extend the number of the possible beyond that of the actual, on the ground that something must be added to the former to constitute the latter. But I do not recognise any such addition to the possible. For what was to be added over and above it would be impossible. What can be added to my understanding, beyond agreement with the formal conditions of experience, is only connection with some perception. But whatever is connected with a perception according to empirical laws is actual, even though it is not immediately perceived.\n\nThat another series of appearances, standing in thoroughgoing connection with what is given to me in perception, and therefore more than one all-embracing experience, is possible, cannot be inferred from what is given; and still less can it be inferred without anything being given at all, since without matter nothing can be thought. What is possible only under conditions which are themselves merely possible is not possible in all respects. Yet it is in this [unrestricted] respect that the question is taken, when we want to know whether the possibility of things extends further than experience can reach.",["- The first sentence is divided into three; the \"for\" clause is kept, and the clause \"so that this perception could not be connected…\" is left as in the German, since its sense (a single experience excludes other connected appearances) seems clear.","- \"dieses\" in \"mit keinen andern Erscheinungen könne verbunden werden\" is read as the given perception, hence \"this perception\"; the German \"der\" could also be read as \"experience\", so the antecedent is a judgment.","- The paragraph beginning \"Der Verstand giebt…\" is divided; the German has a comma-splice after \"machen\" and I have smoothed it.","- \"Ob andere Wahrnehmungen, als überhaupt, zu unserer gesamten möglichen Erfahrung gehören\": the text is awkward; I have rendered \"als überhaupt\" as \"than those which belong to our total possible experience\", a smoothing.","- \"[unrestricted]\" is supplied for \"in aller Absicht\" (the German repeats \"in dieser\"); \"in all respects\" is kept earlier.","- \"Erkentniß\" rendered \"knowledge\"; \"Wahrnehmung\" = perception; \"Inbegriff\" = sum."]],"krv-a-0429":["I have made mention of these questions only in order to leave no gap in that which, according to common opinion, belongs to the concepts of the understanding. In fact, however, absolute possibility (which is valid in all respects) is no mere concept of the understanding, and can in no way be of empirical employment. It belongs solely to reason, which goes beyond all possible empirical employment of the understanding. We have therefore had to content ourselves here with a merely critical remark, and otherwise to leave the matter in obscurity until some further procedure in the future.",["- The German \"er\" after \"Begriff\" (masculine) in \"sondern er gehört allein der Vernunft zu\" is rendered \"It\", referring to the concept of absolute possibility.","- \"Gebrauch\" rendered \"employment\"; \"critical remark\" for \"critische Anmerkung\"."]],"krv-a-0430":["Since I intend with this fourth heading to bring to a close the whole system of the principles of pure understanding, I must still give the ground for my having entitled the principles of modality 'postulates'. I do not here wish to take this expression in the meaning which some recent philosophical writers have given it, contrary to the sense of the mathematicians, to whom it properly belongs. Their meaning is that to postulate is to put forward a proposition as immediately certain, without justification or proof. For if we were to concede, in the case of synthetic propositions, however evident they may be, that they may be given unconditional assent without deduction, on the authority of their own pronouncement, all criticism of the understanding is lost. And since there is no lack of bold pretensions, which common belief (though that is no credential) does not refuse, our understanding will lie open to every delusion. It cannot withhold its assent from pronouncements which, though unlawfully, demand to be admitted in the same tone of confidence as genuine axioms.\n\nWhen, therefore, a determination is added *a priori* synthetically to the concept of a thing, such a proposition must have added to it, if not a proof, at least a deduction of the legitimacy of its assertion; and this cannot be dispensed with.",["- The long sentence is divided into several; the \"denn…so…und…so\" structure of the German is retained in sense, with the dash-like clauses made independent.","- \"Critik\" rendered \"criticism\" here; \"Deduction\" kept as \"deduction\"; \"Bestimmung\" = determination.","- \"Creditiv\" rendered \"credential\"; \"vor unmittelbar gewiß\" read as \"für\".","- The final sentence is split at the semicolon for smoothness."]],"krv-a-0431":["The principles of modality, however, are not objectively synthetic. The predicates of possibility, actuality and necessity do not in the least enlarge the concept of which they are affirmed, by adding anything to the representation of the object. But since they are nevertheless always synthetic, they are so only subjectively. That is, they add to the concept of a (real) thing, of which otherwise they say nothing, the faculty of knowledge in which it originates and has its seat. If the concept is connected in the understanding merely with the formal conditions of experience, its object is entitled possible. If it stands in connection with perception (sensation, as the matter of the senses), and is determined through it by means of the understanding, the object is actual. If it is determined through the connection of perceptions according to concepts, the object is entitled necessary. The principles of modality thus say of a concept nothing other than the act of the faculty of knowledge by which it is generated.\n\nNow a postulate in mathematics is entitled the practical proposition which contains nothing but the synthesis whereby we first give ourselves an object and generate its concept; for instance, with a given line, to describe a circle on a plane from a given point. A proposition of this kind cannot be proved, because the procedure which it demands is precisely that whereby we first generate the concept of such a figure. In the same right, therefore, we can postulate the principles of modality, since they do not enlarge their concept of things in general,*) but only indicate the manner in which it is connected with the faculty of knowledge.",["- Divided into several sentences; \"Da sie aber gleichwol… seyn\" smoothed as \"since they are nevertheless always synthetic\".","- \"Erkentnißkraft\" / \"Erkentnißvermögen\" both rendered \"faculty of knowledge\"; \"Punet\" corrected to \"Punct\" (point).","- \"Obiect\" and \"Gegenstand\" both \"object\". \"(realen)\" is as in the text, \"(real)\".","- \"ihren Begriff\": \"their concept\", read as the concept of which they are predicated; the possessive is awkward in German.","- Footnote marker *) kept in place; the footnote follows as the next passage."]],"krv-a-0432":["*) Through the actuality of a thing I certainly posit more than possibility, but not in the thing. For the thing can never contain more in actuality than was contained in its complete possibility. But since possibility was merely a positing of the thing in relation to the understanding (its empirical employment), actuality is at the same time a connection of it with perception.",["- \"Position\" rendered \"positing\", consistent with the sense of *Position/Setzung*.","- \"vollständiger Möglichkeit\" = \"complete possibility\"."]],"krv-a-0433":["Transcendental Doctrine of the Faculty of Judgment (Analytic of Principles). Chapter III. On the Ground of the Distinction of All Objects in General into Phenomena and Noumena.",["- \"Urtheilskraft\" rendered \"faculty of judgment\"; \"Hauptstück\" = \"chapter\"; \"Phænomena\" and \"Noümena\" kept as in Kant's Latin, left unitalicised in the heading.","- Heading is as printed; note the B-edition reading reaches A 235 / B 294 and is not parallel in text."]],"krv-a-0434":["We have now not merely travelled through the land of pure understanding, and carefully inspected each part of it, but have also surveyed it, and assigned to everything in it its proper place. This land, however, is an island, enclosed by nature itself within unalterable limits. It is the land of truth (an enchanting name), surrounded by a wide and stormy ocean, the native home of illusion [*des Scheins*], where many a fog-bank and many a swiftly melting iceberg gives the lie of new lands. Ceaselessly deluding with empty hopes the seafarer who roams about in search of discoveries, it entangles him in adventures which he can never abandon, and yet can never bring to a conclusion. Before we venture upon this sea, to explore it in all latitudes and to become certain whether anything is to be hoped for in them, it will be useful to cast a glance first upon the map of the land which we are about to leave. We should ask, in the first place, whether we could not be content with what it contains, or indeed must be content with it from necessity, if there is nowhere else any ground on which we could build. In the second place, we should ask by what title we hold this land itself, and can consider ourselves secured against all hostile claims. We have already answered these questions sufficiently in the course of the Analytic; but a summary review of the solutions can strengthen conviction, by uniting the moments of them in a single point.",["- The long sentence beginning \"Es ist das Land der Wahrheit\" is divided: the participial clause \"indem es … täuscht\" is made a separate sentence, with \"it\" (the ocean) as subject, which the German construction implies.","- \"lügt\" in \"neue Länder lügt\" is rendered \"gives the lie of new lands\" to keep the sense of false pretence; the idiom is odd and the rendering is a judgment.","- The sentence \"Ehe wir uns aber … können\" is restated in three sentences; \"erstlich … zweitens\" is kept as \"in the first place … in the second place\".","- \"Scheins\" is given as \"illusion\"; Kemp Smith's usual choice, with *Schein* in square brackets.","- \"Momente\" is rendered \"moments\"."]],"krv-a-0435":["We have seen, namely, that everything which the understanding draws from itself, without borrowing it from experience, is none the less at its disposal solely for the purpose of experience. The principles of pure understanding, whether they be constitutive *a priori* (as the mathematical) or merely regulative (as the dynamical), contain nothing but what may be called the pure schema of possible experience. For experience derives its unity only from the synthetic unity which the understanding originally and of itself confers upon the synthesis of imagination in its relation to apperception; and in relation to this unity, and in agreement with it, appearances, as *data* for a possible knowledge, must already stand *a priori*. Now these rules of the understanding are not only true *a priori*; they are the source of all truth, that is, of the agreement of our knowledge with objects, since they contain the ground of the possibility of experience, as the sum [*Inbegriff*] of all knowledge in which objects can be given to us. Yet it seems to us not enough to be told merely what is true; we want to be told what we desire to know. If, then, we learn from this critical enquiry nothing more than what, even without such subtle investigation, we should have practised of ourselves in the merely empirical use of the understanding, the advantage drawn from it seems not to be worth the expense and preparation. To this it may indeed be replied that no rashness of curiosity is more prejudicial to the extension of our knowledge than that which will always know the utility beforehand, before engaging in investigations, and before it could form the least conception of this utility, even if it were set before its eyes. But there is an advantage which can be made intelligible, and a matter of concern, even to the most difficult and reluctant pupil of such transcendental enquiry. It is this: that the understanding, occupied merely with its empirical use, and not reflecting on the sources of its own knowledge, may indeed get on very well, but is quite unable to accomplish one thing, namely, to determine for itself the limits of its use, and to know what may lie inside or outside its entire sphere. For this the profound investigations which we have instituted are precisely what is required. If the understanding cannot distinguish whether certain questions lie within its horizon or not, it is never secure in its claims and in its possessions, but must expect many humiliating rebukes, when (as is unavoidable) it unceasingly oversteps the limits of its domain, and loses itself in delusion and fallacy.",["- The first sentence is rendered \"is none the less at its disposal solely for the purpose of experience\" to resolve the elliptical \"das habe er dennoch zu keinem andern Behuf\".","- The sentence beginning \"Ob nun aber gleich\" is restated as two sentences, and the concessive structure is made a plain statement.","- \"wir wissen begehrt\": \"was man zu wissen begehrt\" is rendered \"what we desire to know\", the impersonal \"man\" being made \"we\".","- \"Erkentniß/Erkentnisse\" rendered \"knowledge\"; \"Erfahrungsgebrauch\" as \"the purpose of experience\" and \"empirischen Gebrauch\" as \"empirical use\".","- \"Vorwitz\" is rendered \"rashness of curiosity\", a judgment.","- \"Wahn und Blendwerke\" is rendered \"delusion and fallacy\"; \"Blendwerke\" literally \"dazzling shows\".","- \"Inbegriff\" is given in brackets as the term is a choice (\"sum\")."]],"krv-a-0436":["That the understanding can make of all its principles *a priori*, indeed of all its concepts, none but an empirical use, and never a transcendental use, is a proposition which, if it can be known with conviction, points to important consequences. The transcendental use of a concept in any principle consists in its being referred to things in general and in themselves; the empirical use, in its being referred merely to appearances, that is, to objects of a possible experience. That only the latter can ever take place is evident from the following considerations. For every concept there is required, first, the logical form of a concept (of thought) in general, and secondly, the possibility of giving it an object to which it may be related. Without the latter it has no sense, and is completely empty of content, even though it may still contain the logical function of constructing a concept out of whatever data there may be. Now an object can be given to a concept only in intuition. And even if a pure intuition is possible *a priori*, prior to the object, this itself can acquire its object, and therefore objective validity, only through the empirical intuition of which it is the mere form. Thus all concepts, and with them all principles, however much they may be possible *a priori*, none the less relate to empirical intuitions, that is, to *data* for possible experience. Apart from this they have no objective validity whatsoever, but are a mere play, whether of the imagination or of the understanding, with their respective representations. Take, as an example, the concepts of mathematics, and first in their pure intuitions. Space has three dimensions; between two points there can be only one straight line; and so forth. Although all these principles, and the representation of the object with which this science deals, are generated in the mind completely *a priori*, they would yet signify nothing, were we not always able to exhibit their significance in appearances (empirical objects). Hence it is also required that an abstract concept be made sensible, that is, that the object corresponding to it be exhibited in intuition, since otherwise the concept would (as we say) remain without sense, that is, without meaning. Mathematics fulfils this demand by the construction of the figure, which is an appearance present to the senses (although brought about *a priori*). In the same science the concept of magnitude seeks its support and sense in number; and this in turn it seeks in the fingers, in the beads of the abacus, or in the strokes and points which are placed before the eyes. The concept is always generated *a priori*, together with the synthetic principles or formulas drawn from such concepts; but their use, and their relation to supposed objects, can in the end be sought nowhere but in experience, the possibility of which (as regards its form) they contain *a priori*.",["- \"Der transscendentale Gebrauch … ist dieser: daß er … der empirische aber, wenn …\" is restructured with a parallel \"consists in … ; the empirical use, in …\", the German being irregular.","- \"Gegenstand … darauf er sich beziehe\" is given as \"an object to which it may be related\".","- \"Obiect/Gegenstand\" both rendered \"object\".","- \"Haltung\" rendered \"support\"; \"Corallen des Rechenbrets\" as \"beads of the abacus\".","- \"respective mit ihren Vorstellungen\" is rendered \"with their respective representations\".","- \"iene a priori enthalten\": \"iene\" taken as referring to concepts and principles (\"they\"), not to experience.","- \"a priori zu Stande gebrachte\" is kept as \"brought about *a priori*\"."]],"krv-a-0437":["That this is the case with all the categories, and with the principles spun out of them, is evident also from the following: we cannot define a single one of them without at once descending to conditions of sensibility, and so to the form of appearances. The categories must consequently be limited to appearances as their sole objects; for if this condition be removed, all meaning, that is, relation to the object, falls away, and no example can make comprehensible to us what sort of thing is really intended by a concept of this kind.\n\nAbove, in presenting the table of the categories, we excused ourselves from defining each of them. Our purpose, which concerns solely their synthetic employment, did not make definitions necessary; and we ought not to expose ourselves to any responsibility by unnecessary undertakings from which we can be exempt. That was no evasion, but a by no means unimportant rule of prudence: not to venture at once upon defining, nor to attempt or pretend to completeness or precision in the determination of a concept, when we can get along with one or another of its marks, without requiring for this purpose a complete enumeration of all the marks which constitute the whole concept. But it now appears that the ground of this caution lies still deeper. We could not have defined the categories even had we wished to.* For if all conditions of sensibility, which mark them out as concepts of a possible empirical employment, are removed, and they are taken as concepts of things in general (and so as of transcendental employment), there is nothing further to be done with them but to regard the logical function in judgments as the condition of the possibility of things themselves. We could not, however, indicate in the least where they are then to find their application and their object, and so how, in the pure understanding without sensibility, they can have any meaning and objective validity.\n\nNo one can explain the concept of magnitude in general except somewhat as follows: that it is the determination of a thing whereby it can be thought how many times a unit is posited in it. But this \"how many times\" rests upon successive repetition, and therefore upon time and the synthesis (of the homogeneous) in time. Reality, in contrast with negation, can be explained only if we think of a time (as the sum of all being) that is either filled or empty. If I leave out permanence (which is existence at all times), nothing remains to me for the concept of substance except the logical representation of the subject, which I fancy I realise by representing to myself something that can exist solely as subject (without being a predicate of anything). But not only do I not know any conditions under which this logical prerogative will belong to any thing; nothing further can be made of it, and not the least inference can be drawn from it, since no object for the employment of this concept is thereby determined, and we do not know at all whether it means anything whatsoever.\n\nFrom the concept of cause, if I leave out the time in which something follows upon something else in accordance with a rule, I should find in the pure category nothing more than that it is something from which the existence of something else can be inferred. Not only could cause and effect then not be distinguished from each other at all; but since this power of inferring soon requires conditions of which I know nothing, the concept would have no determination as to how it applies to any object.\n\nThe supposed principle, that everything contingent has a cause, certainly comes forward with considerable solemnity, as if it had its own dignity in itself. But if I ask what you understand by \"contingent\", and you answer, \"that the non-existence of which is possible\", I should like to know by what you propose to recognise this possibility of non-existence, if you do not represent to yourselves, in the series of appearances, a succession, and in this succession an existence which follows upon non-existence (or conversely), and so a change. For to say that the non-existence of a thing does not contradict itself is a lame appeal to a logical condition. This condition is indeed necessary to the concept, but is far from sufficient for real possibility. Thus I can in thought remove any existing substance without contradicting myself, but I cannot in the least infer from this its objective contingency in its existence, that is, the possibility of its non-existence in itself.\n\nAs regards the concept of community, it is easy to judge that, since the pure categories of substance and of causality admit of no explanation determining the object, reciprocal causality in the relation of substances to one another (*commercium*) is equally incapable of it.\n\nPossibility, existence and necessity no one has ever been able to explain except by manifest tautology, so long as their definition was to be drawn solely from the pure understanding. For the delusion of substituting the logical possibility of the concept (in that it does not contradict itself) for the transcendental possibility of things (in that an object corresponds to the concept) can deceive and satisfy only the inexperienced.",[]],"krv-a-0438":["\\*) I understand here the real definition, which does not merely substitute for the name of a thing other and more intelligible words, but contains a clear mark by which the object (*definitum*) can always be securely known, and which renders the concept explained serviceable in application. The real explanation would thus be that which makes distinct not merely a concept, but at the same time its objective reality. The mathematical explanations, which exhibit the object in intuition in accordance with the concept, are of the latter kind.",["- Long opening and following sentences divided into several; \"erhellet … daraus: daß\" restated as \"is evident also from the following\".","- \"sie folglich eingeschränkt seyn müssen\" is made a separate sentence, \"The categories must consequently be limited…\", to settle the antecedent of \"sie\".","- \"Obiects des Gebrauchs\" (sic) read as \"Objects\" singular, \"object for the employment\"; \"vor Begriffe\" read as \"für Begriffe\".","- \"Herzehlung\" read as \"Herzählung\" (enumeration); \"dessen Nichtseyn\" rendered \"that the non-existence of which\".","- The first-person \"ich\" in the concept-of-cause sentence kept; \"würde … gar keine Bestimmung haben\" smoothed to \"would have no determination as to how it applies\".","- \"Wievielmal\" rendered \"how many times\"; \"Realdefinition/Realerklärung\" as \"real definition/explanation\". The footnote marker after \"wollten\" is kept as the asterisk.","- The syntax of \"bey ihnen gar nichts weiter zu thun sey, als…\" depends on the preceding \"wenn\"; I supplied \"We could not, however\" to make the final clause intelligible, as the German breaks off the construction.","- Sentence on reality: German lacks a full stop after \"leer ist\"; supplied one."]],"krv-a-0439":["There is something strange, and even absurd, in the claim that there should be a concept which must have a meaning, and yet is incapable of any explanation. But the categories are in this peculiar position. They can have a determinate meaning and relation to any object only by means of the general sensible condition, and this condition has been omitted from the pure category; the pure category can then contain nothing but the logical function of bringing the manifold under a concept. From this function, that is, from the form of the concept alone, nothing can be known or distinguished as to what object falls under it, since abstraction has been made from the very sensible condition under which objects can fall under it at all. Hence the categories, over and above the pure concept of understanding, require determinations of their application to sensibility in general (schema). Without these determinations they are not concepts through which an object is known and distinguished from others. They are only so many modes of thinking an object for possible intuitions, and of giving it its meaning (under conditions still required) in accordance with some function of understanding, that is, of defining it. They themselves, therefore, cannot be defined. The logical functions of judgments in general (unity and plurality, affirmation and negation, subject and predicate) cannot be defined without moving in a circle, since the definition would itself have to be a judgment, and would therefore have to contain these functions already. The pure categories, however, are nothing but representations of things in general, so far as the manifold of their intuition must be thought through one or other of these logical functions. Magnitude is the determination which can be thought only through a judgment that has quantity (*iudicium commune*); reality is that which can be thought only through an affirmative judgment; substance is that which, in relation to intuition, must be the ultimate subject of all other determinations. But what sort of things these are, in respect of which one function rather than another must be employed, remains here completely undetermined. Consequently the categories, without the condition of sensible intuition, for which they contain the synthesis, have no relation to any determinate object. They cannot therefore define any object, and have in themselves no validity as objective concepts.",["- Restated the long second sentence as two (\"They can… and this condition…; the pure category can then…\"); *diese denn* resolved as the pure category.","- \"so viel Arten\" rendered \"so many modes\"; \"zu möglichen Anschauungen\" taken as \"for possible intuitions\".","- \"dazu sie die Synthesis enthalten\": \"dazu\" taken as the condition of sensible intuition.","- *Bestimmungen ihrer Anwendung* = \"determinations of their application\"; *Gültigkeit* = \"validity\"."]],"krv-a-0440":["From this it follows incontrovertibly that the pure concepts of understanding can never be of transcendental, but always only of empirical employment. It follows also that the principles of pure understanding can be related only to objects of the senses, under the general conditions of a possible experience, and never to things in general (without regard to the mode in which we may intuit them).",["- Divided the sentence at the second *daß*-clause for clarity.","- *Gebrauch* = \"employment\" (here and below), to keep it consistent."]],"krv-a-0441":["The transcendental Analytic accordingly has this important result: the understanding can never achieve more *a priori* than to anticipate the form of a possible experience in general; and, since that which is not appearance cannot be an object of experience, it can never overstep the limits of sensibility, within which alone objects are given to us. Its principles are merely principles of the exposition of appearances. The proud name of an ontology, which presumes to supply, in a systematic doctrine, synthetic modes of knowledge *a priori* of things in general (e.g. the principle of causality), must give place to the modest title of a mere analytic of pure understanding.",["- Split the final sentence in two; the \"e.g.\" parenthesis is retained as in the German.","- *Erkenntnisse* = \"modes of knowledge\"; *Exposition* = \"exposition\"."]],"krv-a-0442":["Thinking is the act of relating a given intuition to an object. If the mode of this intuition is in no way given, the object is merely transcendental, and the concept of understanding has no other than a transcendental employment, namely, the unity of the thought of a manifold in general. Through a pure category, then, in which abstraction is made from every condition of sensible intuition (the only intuition possible for us), no object is determined. Only the thought of an object in general is expressed, in accordance with different modes (*modis*). Now to the employment of a concept there belongs, further, a function of judgment whereby an object is subsumed under it, and therefore the condition, at least formal, under which something can be given in intuition. If this condition of judgment (schema) is lacking, all subsumption disappears; for nothing is given that can be subsumed under the concept. The merely transcendental employment of the categories is therefore in fact no employment at all, and has no determinate object, nor even one determinable as regards its form. Hence it follows that the pure category is insufficient for any synthetic principle *a priori*; that the principles of pure understanding are of empirical employment only, never of transcendental; and that beyond the field of possible experience there can be no synthetic principles *a priori* at all.",["- \"als der einzigen, die uns möglich ist\" taken as referring to sensible intuition; rendered as a parenthesis.","- *Zulange* = *zulangt*, \"is insufficient\" (literally \"does not suffice for\").","- The last clause of the German is irregular in construction; I recast it as three parallel \"that\" clauses.","- \"worauf\" read as \"whereby\"."]],"krv-a-0443":["It may therefore be advisable to express ourselves as follows. The pure categories, apart from the formal conditions of sensibility, have merely transcendental meaning, but are not of transcendental employment, since this is in itself impossible. All the conditions of any employment (in judgments) are lacking to them, namely the formal conditions of the subsumption of any putative object under these concepts. Since, then, as merely pure categories they are not to be of empirical employment, and cannot be of transcendental, they are of no employment whatever if separated from all sensibility; that is, they cannot be applied to any putative object. They are rather merely the pure form of the employment of understanding in respect of objects in general and of thinking, without our being able to think or determine any object through them alone.",["- Divided the second sentence at \"indem\".","- *Angeblich* = \"putative\"; \"transsendentalen\" in the German is a misprint for *transscendentalen*, silently corrected.","- *Bedeutung* = \"meaning\"; *Gebrauch* = \"employment\"."]],"krv-a-0444":["Appearances, so far as they are thought as objects in accordance with the unity of the categories, are entitled *phaenomena*. But if I assume things which are merely objects of understanding, and which nevertheless, as such, can be given to an intuition, though not to a sensible intuition (as *coram intuitu intellectuali*), such things would be entitled *noumena* (*intelligibilia*).",["- *Phænomena* normalised to *phaenomena*; *Noümena* to *noumena*.","- *heissen* = \"is entitled\"."]],"krv-a-0445":["One would now suppose that the concept of appearances, as limited by the transcendental Aesthetic, of itself yields the objective reality of noumena. One would suppose also that it justifies the division of objects into *phaenomena* and *noumena*, and so also the division of the world into a world of sense and a world of understanding (*mundus sensibilis et intelligibilis*). The distinction would be understood to concern, not merely the logical form of the indistinct or distinct knowledge of one and the same thing, but the difference in the manner in which objects can originally be given to our knowledge, and in accordance with which they themselves differ from one another in kind. For if the senses represent something to us merely as it appears, this something must also be in itself a thing, and an object of a non-sensible intuition, that is, of the understanding. In other words, a knowledge must be possible in which no sensibility is to be met with, and which alone has absolutely objective reality; through it objects would be represented to us as they are, whereas in the empirical employment of our understanding things are known only as they appear. Thus, besides the empirical employment of the categories (which is restricted to sensible conditions), there would be a pure and yet objectively valid employment. We could then no longer maintain what we have hitherto asserted, namely, that our pure modes of knowledge through the understanding are nothing more than principles of the exposition of appearance, which even *a priori* extend no further than to the formal possibility of experience. For here a quite different field would lie open before us, a world, as it were, thought in the spirit (perhaps even intuited), which might occupy our pure understanding no less, indeed far more nobly.",["- Restated the opening sentence as three, making \"man sollte denken\" govern each clause.","- \"sie\" in \"wie sie… gegeben werden können\" refers to the objects (*Gegenstände*), though the preceding noun is singular, *Dinges*; supplied \"objects\".","- *Geist* rendered \"spirit\"; *Gattung* \"kind\"; \"d. i.\" clauses recast with \"that is\" and \"in other words\".","- *Erkentniß* = \"knowledge\"; *Verstandeserkentnisse* = \"modes of knowledge through the understanding\".","- \"edler\" rendered as an adverb (\"more nobly\") to carry the German comparative."]],"krv-a-0446":["All our representations are in fact referred by the understanding to some object; and since appearances are nothing but representations, the understanding refers them to a something, as the object of sensible intuition. But this something is, so understood, only the transcendental object. That signifies a something = x, of which we know, and with the present constitution of our understanding can know, nothing whatsoever. It can serve only as a correlate of the unity of apperception, for the unity of the manifold in sensible intuition; and by means of this unity the understanding unites the manifold in the concept of an object. This transcendental object cannot be separated from the sensible data, since nothing would then remain through which it could be thought. It is therefore not in itself an object of knowledge, but only the representation of appearances under the concept of an object in general, a concept which is determinable through the manifold of these appearances.",["- \"Obiect\" and \"Gegenstand\" both rendered \"object\"; Kant's usage here is not sharply distinguished.","- Restated the long second sentence as several; \"Dieses bedeutet\" rendered \"That signifies\" for clarity.","- \"vermittelst deren\" refers to the unity (Einheit), not the apperception; resolved by judgment.","- \"derselben\" in the last sentence taken to refer to the appearances (Erscheinungen).","- \"Datis\" = data (*data*)."]],"krv-a-0447":["It is for this very reason that the categories do not represent any special object given to the understanding alone. They serve only to determine the transcendental object (the concept of something in general) through what is given in sensibility, and so to enable us to know appearances empirically under concepts of objects.",["- Divided the German sentence in two.","- \"Sinnlichkeit\" = sensibility; \"erkennen\" rendered \"know\"."]],"krv-a-0448":["As to the reason why, not being satisfied with the substratum of sensibility, we have added to the *phaenomena* noumena which only the pure understanding can think, it rests solely on the following. Sensibility, and its field, namely the field of appearances, is itself limited by the understanding in this manner: that it does not relate to things in themselves, but only to the way in which, owing to our subjective constitution, things appear to us. This was the result of the whole Transcendental Aesthetic, and it follows also naturally from the concept of an appearance in general that something which is not in itself appearance must correspond to it. For appearance cannot be anything by itself and apart from our mode of representation. Consequently, if we are not to move in a perpetual circle, the word 'appearance' must already indicate a relation to something the immediate representation of which is indeed sensible, but which, even apart from this constitution of our sensibility (upon which the form of our intuition is grounded), must in itself be something, that is, an object independent of sensibility.",["- Split the long sentence beginning \"Dies war das Resultat\" into three; \"weil\" clause made a separate sentence.","- \"Phænomenis\" and \"Noümena\" kept as Latin/Greek-derived technical terms; *phaenomena* italicised, noumena left plain as English-naturalised.","- \"vor sich selbst\" read as \"für sich selbst\" (by itself).","- \"Beschaffenheit\" rendered \"constitution\"."]],"krv-a-0449":["Hence arises the concept of a noumenon. It is not at all positive, and is not a determinate knowledge of any thing, but signifies only the thinking of something in general, in which I abstract from all form of sensible intuition. But in order that a noumenon may signify a true object, distinguishable from all phenomena, it is not enough that I free my thought from all conditions of sensible intuition. I must in addition have ground for assuming another kind of intuition than this sensible kind, under which such an object can be given; otherwise my thought is empty, though free from contradiction. We have indeed not been able to prove above that sensible intuition is the only possible intuition in general, but only that it is so for us. Neither, however, could we prove that another kind of intuition is possible. And although our thought can abstract from that sensibility, the question still remains whether it is not then a mere form of a concept, and whether any object at all is left over after this separation.",["- Divided the first sentence at \"der aber gar nicht positiv\"; \"bestimte Erkentniß\" = determinate knowledge.","- \"vor uns\" = \"für uns\" (for us).","- \"überall\" in the last clause read as \"überhaupt\" (at all)."]],"krv-a-0450":["The object to which I relate appearance in general is the transcendental object, that is, the completely indeterminate thought of something in general. This cannot be entitled the noumenon; for I do not know what it is in itself, and have no concept of it save as the object of a sensible intuition in general, which is therefore the same for all appearances. I cannot think it through any of the categories; for these hold of empirical intuition, in order to bring it under a concept of the object in general. A pure employment of the category is indeed possible, that is, free from contradiction, but it has no objective validity whatever, since it relates to no intuition which could thereby acquire unity of the object. For the category is a mere function of thought, through which no object is given to me, but only that which may be given in intuition is thought.",["- \"diese gilt\" is singular in the German (diese = Categorie, though \"keine Categorien\" precedes); I render plural \"these hold\" for English sense.","- \"vor alle Erscheinungen\" = \"für alle Erscheinungen\"."]],"krv-a-0451":["If I take away all thought (through categories) from an empirical knowledge, no knowledge of any object remains; for through mere intuition nothing whatever is thought, and that this affection of sensibility is in me constitutes no relation of such a representation to any object. If, on the other hand, I leave out all intuition, the form of thought still remains, that is, the way of determining an object for the manifold of a possible intuition. Accordingly the categories extend further than sensible intuition, since they think objects in general, without regard to the particular mode (of sensibility) in which they may be given. But they do not thereby determine a wider sphere of objects. For that such objects can be given cannot be assumed without presupposing as possible some other than sensible kind of intuition, and to this we are in no way entitled.",["- Last sentence restated in two for clarity; \"wozu\" taken to refer to the presupposition of another kind of intuition.","- \"Affection\" kept as in the German."]],"krv-a-0452":["I entitle a concept problematic if it contains no contradiction, and also, as a limitation of given concepts, is connected with other modes of knowledge, while yet its objective reality can in no way be known. The concept of a noumenon, that is, of a thing which is to be thought not as an object of the senses but as a thing in itself (solely through a pure understanding), is not at all self-contradictory; for we cannot assert of sensibility that it is the sole possible kind of intuition. Further, this concept is necessary to prevent sensible intuition from being extended to things in themselves, and so to limit the objective validity of sensible knowledge. (The remainder, to which sensible knowledge does not extend, is entitled noumena precisely in order to indicate that this knowledge cannot extend its domain over everything which the understanding thinks.) In the end, however, the possibility of such noumena is quite beyond our comprehension, and the region outside the sphere of appearances is (for us) empty. That is to say, we have an understanding which problematically extends further than appearances, but no intuition, indeed not even the concept of a possible intuition, through which objects could be given to us outside the field of sensibility, and the understanding be employed assertorically beyond it. The concept of a noumenon is therefore merely a limiting concept, serving to curb the pretensions of sensibility, and is thus only of negative employment. It is not, however, arbitrarily invented; it is bound up with the limitation of sensibility, though it cannot posit anything positive outside the sphere of sensibility.",["- \"iene\" in the parenthesis refers to the modes of knowledge (sensible knowledge); resolved by judgment.","- \"heissen\" with plural subject \"das übrige\" (grammatical irregularity): rendered as singular \"is entitled\"; the German is slightly irregular and smoothed.","- \"Erkentnisse\" = modes of knowledge where plural; \"vvor uns\" = \"für uns\".","- Divided the sentence beginning \"Am Ende\" at \"d. i.\""]],"krv-a-0453":["The division of objects into *phaenomena* and *noumena*, and of the world into a world of sense and a world of understanding, cannot therefore be admitted at all, although concepts, to be sure, admit of division into sensible and intellectual. For no object can be determined for the intellectual concepts, and they cannot, therefore, be passed off as objectively valid. If we abandon the senses, how are we to make it conceivable that our categories (which would be the only remaining concepts for noumena) still have any meaning whatsoever? For their relation to any object demands something more than merely the unity of thought; it demands, in addition, a possible intuition to which they can be applied.\n\nThe concept of a noumenon, taken merely problematically, remains none the less not only admissible but, as a concept setting limits to sensibility, unavoidable. But in that case the noumenon is not a special intelligible object for our understanding. An understanding to which it would belong is itself a problem: namely, an understanding that would know its object not discursively through categories but intuitively in a non-sensible intuition. Of the possibility of such an understanding we cannot form the least representation.\n\nIn this way our understanding acquires a negative extension. That is, it is not limited by sensibility, but on the contrary limits sensibility, by entitling things in themselves (not regarded as appearances) noumena. But it at once also sets limits to itself, in that it cannot know these noumena through any categories, and so can think them only under the title of an unknown something.",["- Restated the long second-last sentence as three: \"Aber alsdenn…\" split at \"ein Verstand, vor den es gehörete…\", with \"als von welchem\" made a separate sentence.","- \"[Er]\" in \"den lezteren\" taken as the intellectual concepts (not the categories); \"sie\" in \"sie nicht … ausgeben\" likewise.","- \"vor\" (= für) rendered \"for\"; \"nent\" rendered \"entitling\", following the method's rule for *heißen/nennen*.","- \"Phænomena/Noümena\" given in italicised Latin as technical terms; \"Noümeni\" rendered \"noumenon\".","- \"überall\" in \"noch überall etwas bedeuten\" rendered \"whatsoever\"."]],"krv-a-0454":["I find, however, in the writings of more recent authors a quite different use of the expressions *mundus sensibilis* and *intelligibilis*, which departs entirely from the sense of the ancients. On this use there is certainly no difficulty, but also nothing to be met with save empty verbal quibbling. According to it, some have been pleased to entitle the sum of appearances, in so far as it is intuited, the world of sense; and in so far as its connection is thought in accordance with universal laws of the understanding, the world of understanding. Theoretical astronomy, which expounds the mere observation of the starry heavens, would represent the former; contemplative astronomy (explained, say, according to the Copernican system of the world, or even according to Newton's laws of gravitation) would represent the latter, namely an intelligible world.\n\nBut such a twisting of words is a mere sophistical evasion, by which we escape a troublesome question through lowering its meaning to suit our own convenience. In respect of appearances, understanding and reason may certainly be employed; but the question is whether these have any use at all when the object is not appearance (noumenon). We take the object in this sense when it is thought as in itself merely intelligible, that is, as given to the understanding alone and not at all to the senses. The question, then, is whether, besides that empirical employment of the understanding (even in the Newtonian representation of the structure of the universe), a transcendental employment is possible, one which would be directed to the noumenon as an object. This question we have answered in the negative.",["- Divided the passage at \"Aber eine solche Wortverdrehung…\" and recast the long \"Die theoretische Astronomie…\" sentence with a semicolon.","- \"in diesem Sinne nimt man ihn\": \"ihn\" resolved to \"den Gegenstand\", hence \"We take the object in this sense\".","- \"vorstellig machen\" rendered \"represent\"; \"Weltbaues\" rendered \"structure of the universe\".","- \"Wortkrämerey\" rendered \"verbal quibbling\"; \"beliebt\" as \"been pleased\"."]],"krv-a-0455":["When we say, then, that the senses represent objects to us as they appear, the understanding as they are, the latter statement is to be taken not in a transcendental but in a merely empirical meaning. That is to say, it means that objects must be represented as objects of experience, in the thoroughgoing connection of appearances, and not according to what they may be, apart from relation to possible experience, and consequently to senses in general, that is, as objects of pure understanding. For this will always remain unknown to us. It remains unknown even whether such transcendental (extraordinary) knowledge is possible at all, at least as knowledge that stands under our ordinary categories.\n\nUnderstanding and sensibility, in our case, can determine objects only in combination. If we separate them, we have intuitions without concepts, or concepts without intuitions; in both cases, representations which we cannot relate to any determinate object.",["- \"das leztere\" taken as the statement about the understanding (\"wie sie sind\"); the English says so.","- Split the first sentence at \"Denn dieses…\"; \"Erkentniß\" rendered \"knowledge\".","- \"bey uns\" rendered \"in our case\"; \"bestimten\" as \"determinate\"."]],"krv-a-0456":["If anyone still hesitates, after all these explanations, to renounce the merely transcendental employment of the categories, let him make trial of them in some synthetic assertion. An analytic assertion does not advance the understanding. Since the understanding is occupied only with what is already thought in the concept, it leaves undecided whether the concept has in itself relation to objects, or merely signifies the unity of thought in general (which abstracts completely from the manner in which an object may be given). It suffices for the understanding to know what lies in its concept; to what the concept itself may relate is a matter of indifference to it.\n\nLet him, then, make trial with some synthetic and supposedly transcendental principle, such as: Everything that exists, exists as substance, or as a determination inhering in substance; everything contingent exists as the effect of another thing, namely of its cause; and so forth. I now ask: whence is he to obtain these synthetic propositions, when the concepts are to hold not in relation to possible experience but of things in themselves (noumena)? Where is the third thing, which is always required for a synthetic proposition, in order to connect with one another in the same proposition concepts that have no logical (analytic) kinship?\n\nHe will never prove his proposition. What is more, he will not be able even to justify the possibility of such a pure assertion without taking account of the empirical employment of the understanding, and thereby entirely renouncing pure judgment free from the senses. Thus the concept of pure, merely intelligible objects is wholly void of all principles of their application, since no way can be devised in which they might be given. The problematic thought, which none the less leaves a place open for them, serves only, like an empty space, to limit the empirical principles, without containing or exhibiting any other object of knowledge beyond the sphere of the latter.",["- Restated the opening, whose second sentence (\"Denn eine analytische…\") I made independent, dropping \"Denn\" as a mere connective.","- \"dieser\" in \"ob dieser an sich selbst\" resolved to \"der Begriff\"; \"ihm\" to the understanding.","- \"Bestimmung\" rendered \"determination\"; \"Obiect der Erkentniß\" as \"object of knowledge\".","- \"Das Dritte\" rendered \"third thing\", as Kant's mediating term for synthetic judgments.","- \"iene\" and \"der lezteren\" in the last sentence: \"the latter\" taken as the empirical principles."]],"krv-a-0457":["Appendix. On the Amphiboly of the Concepts of Reflection, arising from the confusion of the empirical employment of the understanding with the transcendental.",["- \"Reflexionsbegriffe\" rendered \"concepts of reflection\"; \"Amphibolie\" as \"Amphiboly\", following the Greek-derived form."]],"krv-a-0458":["Reflection (*reflexio*) is not concerned with objects themselves, with a view to obtaining concepts from them directly. It is the state of mind in which we first prepare ourselves to discover the subjective conditions under which we are able to arrive at concepts. It is the consciousness of the relation of given representations to our different sources of knowledge; and only through this consciousness can their relation to one another be rightly determined. The first question, before any further treatment of our representations, is this: In which faculty of knowledge do they belong together? Is it the understanding, or the senses, that connects or compares them? Many a judgment is accepted from custom, or is formed through inclination. But since no reflection precedes it, or at least follows it critically, it passes for a judgment that has originated in the understanding. Not all judgments require investigation, that is, attention to the grounds of their truth. For when they are immediately certain, as, for instance, that between two points only one straight line is possible, no more immediate mark of truth can be indicated for them than what they themselves express. But all judgments, indeed all comparisons, require reflection, that is, a distinction of the faculty of knowledge to which the given concepts belong. The act by which I hold the comparison of representations in general together with the faculty of knowledge in which it is carried out, and by which I distinguish whether the representations are compared with one another as belonging to the pure understanding or to sensible intuition, I entitle transcendental reflection. The relations, however, in which concepts can belong to one another in a state of mind are those of identity and difference, of agreement and opposition, of the inner and the outer, and finally of the determinable and the determination (matter and form). The right determination of these relations rests on the faculty of knowledge to which the concepts subjectively belong, whether to sensibility or to the understanding. For the difference between these latter faculties makes a great difference in the manner in which the former [the relations] are to be thought.",["- \"Ueberlegung (reflexio)\" rendered \"reflection\"; the Latin is Kant's own gloss. \"Erkentnißvermögen/-kraft/-quelle\" given as \"faculty/source of knowledge.\"","- \"die ersten\" in the last sentence is ambiguous (concepts or relations); I resolve it as the relations and mark it in brackets. \"sie\" in \"ob sie als gehörig…\" taken as the representations.","- \"vor\" = \"für/vor\" in \"gilt es vor ein solches\" read as \"für\"; \"vor denen\" read as \"von denen\" (the senses that connect). Doubtful: \"vor\" may be a spelling for \"von.\"","- Long sentences were divided; \"Gemüthszustande\" = \"state of mind.\"","- \"sie subiectiv… gehören\" refers to the concepts; supplied \"the concepts\" for clarity."]],"krv-a-0459":["Before all objective judgments we compare the concepts, in order to arrive at identity (of many representations under one concept) in the interest of universal judgments, or at their difference, in order to produce particular judgments; and at agreement, from which affirmative judgments, and opposition, from which negative judgments, can result, and so forth. On this ground we ought, it would seem, to entitle the concepts mentioned concepts of comparison (*conceptus comparationis*). But when what is in question is not logical form but the content of concepts, that is, whether the things themselves are identical or different, in agreement or in opposition, and so on, the things can stand in a twofold relation to our faculty of knowledge, namely to sensibility and to understanding. Now it is the place to which they belong that determines the manner in which they ought to belong to one another. Transcendental reflection, that is, the relation of given representations to one or the other kind of knowledge, can therefore alone determine their relation to one another. Whether the things are identical or different, in agreement or in opposition, and so on, cannot be settled immediately from the concepts themselves by mere comparison (*comparatio*), but only by distinguishing the kind of knowledge to which they belong, by means of a transcendental reflection (*reflexio*). We may, then, say that logical reflection is mere comparison; for in it we abstract completely from the faculty of knowledge to which the given representations belong, and so far as their seat in the mind is concerned they are to be treated as homogeneous. Transcendental reflection, on the other hand (which bears on the objects themselves), contains the ground of the possibility of the objective comparison of representations with one another, and is therefore very different from logical reflection, since the faculty of knowledge to which they belong is not the same. This transcendental reflection is a duty from which no one can free himself who wishes to judge anything about things *a priori*. We shall now take it in hand, and shall draw from it no little light for determining the proper business of the understanding.",["- Long sentence beginning \"Weil aber…\" restated in several sentences; the \"Weil… so wird\" construction is irregular, and I have smoothed it, supplying \"Now it is the place to which they belong that determines…\" to carry the sense.","- \"Erkentnißart\" = \"kind of knowledge\"; \"Sitze\" = \"seat.\"","- \"gleichartig\" = \"homogeneous.\"","- \"etc\" rendered \"and so on.\""]],"krv-a-0460":["1. *Identity and difference.* When an object is presented to us several times, but each time with the very same inner determinations (quality and quantity), then, if it counts as an object of pure understanding, it is always one and the same, a single thing and not many (*numerica identitas*). But if it is an appearance, comparison of concepts is not in question at all. However much may be identical so far as concepts are concerned, the difference of the places of this appearance at one and the same time is a sufficient ground of the numerical difference of the object (of the senses) itself. Thus in the case of two drops of water we can abstract completely from all inner difference (of quality and quantity), and it is enough that they are intuited at the same time in different places for them to be held numerically different. Leibniz took appearances as things in themselves, and so as *intelligibilia*, that is, objects of pure understanding (although, because of the confusedness of their representations, he gave them the name of phenomena). On that assumption his principle of the indiscernibility of the non-distinguishable (*principium identitatis indiscernibilium*) could certainly not be disputed. But since appearances are objects of sensibility, and the understanding in respect of them is employed not purely but merely empirically, plurality and numerical difference are already given by space itself, as the condition of outer appearances. For one part of space, though it may be completely similar and equal to another, is yet outside it, and for that very reason is a part different from the first, which is added to it to constitute a larger space. This must therefore hold of everything that is at the same time in the various places of space, however similar and equal it may otherwise be.",["- \"Satz des Nichtzuunterscheidenden\" rendered literally; the Latin gloss follows.","- \"dieselben mit dem Nahmen der Phänomene belegte\" = \"gave them the name of phenomena.\"","- \"da konte… nicht gestritten werden\" restated as \"On that assumption…\"; \"da\" is concessive/temporal, not a bare causal.","- \"vor\" in \"vor numerisch verschieden\" read as \"für.\""]],"krv-a-0461":["2. *Agreement and opposition.* When reality is represented only through the pure understanding (*realitas noumenon*), no opposition can be thought between realities, that is, no such relation as would, when they are combined in one subject, make them cancel each other's consequences, so that 3 − 3 = 0. The real in appearance (*realitas phaenomenon*), on the other hand, can certainly be in opposition to one another [i.e. to other reals of the same kind]; and when united in the same subject, the one may wholly or partly destroy the consequence of the other. Examples are two moving forces in the same straight line, in so far as they either pull or push a point in opposite directions, or again a pleasure which balances a pain.",["- \"unter einander\" in \"das Reale… unter einander\" is awkward; I supplied \"[i.e. to other reals of the same kind]\" as the sense requires.","- \"Die Folge\" = \"consequence\"; \"die Wage hält\" = \"balances.\"","- Spelling \"phænomenon\" normalised to \"phaenomenon\" in the Latin term; italicised as a technical term."]],"krv-a-0462":["3. *Inner and outer.* In an object of the pure understanding only that is inner which has no relation (so far as its existence is concerned) to anything different from itself. The inner determinations of a *substantia phaenomenon* in space, on the other hand, are nothing but relations, and the substance itself is entirely a sum of mere relations. We know substance in space only through forces which are active in it, either drawing others towards it (attraction), or preventing them from penetrating into it (repulsion and impenetrability). We know no other properties constituting the concept of the substance which appears in space and which we entitle matter. As object of the pure understanding, on the other hand, every substance must have inner determinations and powers which pertain to inner reality. But what inner accidents can I think, other than those which my inner sense presents to me? That is to say, [they must be] either what is itself a thinking, or analogous to it. Leibniz, therefore, because he represented substances as noumena, made all of them, even the constituent parts of matter, into simple subjects endowed with powers of representation; in a word, into monads. He did so after he had, in thought, taken away from them everything that can signify outer relation, and consequently also composition.",[]],"krv-a-0463":["4. *Matter and form.* These are two concepts which underlie all other reflection, so inseparably are they bound up with every use of the understanding. The first signifies the determinable in general; the second, its determination. (Both are taken in the transcendental sense, since we abstract from every difference in what is given and in the manner in which it is determined.) Logicians formerly entitled the universal the matter, and the specific difference the form. In every judgment the given concepts may be entitled the logical matter [of the judgment], and their relation (by means of the copula) the form of the judgment. In every being the constituents of it (*essentialia*) are the matter; the manner in which they are connected in a thing is the essential form. Also, in respect of things in general, unlimited reality was regarded as the matter of all possibility, and the limitation of it (negation) as that form whereby, according to transcendental concepts, one thing is distinguished from another. The understanding, namely, demands in the first place that something be given (at least in the concept), in order that it may determine it in a certain manner. Hence in the concept of the pure understanding the matter precedes the form. For this reason Leibniz first assumed things (monads), and an inner power of representation belonging to them, in order thereupon to ground their outer relation and the community of their states (namely, of their representations). Space and time were therefore possible only through the relation of substances, and through the connection of their determinations with one another as grounds and consequences. So it would in fact have to be, if the pure understanding could be directly related to objects, and if space and time were determinations of things in themselves. But if they are only sensible intuitions, in which we determine all objects merely as appearances, then the form of intuition (as a subjective constitution of sensibility) precedes all matter (the sensations). Space and time therefore precede all appearances and all data of experience, and make these possible in the first place. The intellectual philosopher could not tolerate that the form should precede the things themselves and determine their possibility. This was a perfectly correct censure, if he assumed that we intuit things as they are (though with confused representation). But sensible intuition is a quite peculiar subjective condition, which underlies all perception a priori, and whose form is original. The form is therefore given by itself alone. So far from the matter (or the things themselves which appear) being the ground, as we should have to judge if we went by mere concepts, the possibility of the matter presupposes, on the contrary, a formal intuition (time and space) as given.",[]],"krv-a-0464":["Remark on the Amphiboly of the Concepts of Reflection.",[]],"krv-a-0465":["Allow me to entitle the position which we assign to a concept either in sensibility or in the pure understanding, the *transcendental location*. The appraisal of this position, which belongs to every concept according to the difference in its use, together with the direction, in accordance with rules, for determining this location for all concepts, would be the *transcendental topic*. This doctrine would thoroughly safeguard against the surreptitious claims of the pure understanding, and the delusions arising from them, by always distinguishing to which faculty of knowledge the concepts properly belong. Every concept, every title under which many modes of knowledge fall, may be entitled a logical location. On this is based the logical topic of Aristotle. Schoolmasters and orators could employ it in order to look up, under certain titles of thought, what best suited the matter before them, and then to quibble about it with a show of thoroughness, or to prattle on at length.",[]],"krv-a-0466":["The transcendental topic, on the other hand, contains no more than the four titles of all comparison and distinction already mentioned. They differ from the categories in that what is presented through them is not the object according to that which constitutes its concept (magnitude, reality), but only the comparison of representations, in all its manifoldness, which precedes the concept of things. This comparison, however, requires in the first place a reflection, that is, a determination of the location to which the representations of the things compared belong, whether it is the pure understanding that thinks them, or sensibility that gives them in appearance.",[]],"krv-a-0467":["Concepts can be compared logically without concerning ourselves with where their objects belong, whether as noumena to the understanding, or as phenomena to sensibility. But if we wish to proceed with these concepts to objects, transcendental reflection is in the first place required, to determine for which faculty of knowledge they are to be objects, whether for the pure understanding or for sensibility. Without this reflection I make a very insecure use of these concepts, and there arise supposed synthetic principles which critical reason cannot recognise, and which are grounded solely on a transcendental amphiboly, that is, on a confusion of the pure object of understanding with the appearance.",["- 0462: Final sentence reordered: the \"after he had taken away…\" clause is made a separate sentence. \"Kräfte\" rendered \"forces\" for the physical sense and \"powers\" for the Leibnizian *Vorstellungskräfte*. \"[they must be]\" is supplied.","- 0462: \"Bestimmungen\" = determinations; \"Accidenzen\" = accidents.","- 0463: The long passage is kept in sentence-divisions close to the German, with some sentences split. \"Censur\" = censure. \"Intellectualphilosoph\" = intellectual philosopher (Leibniz). \"[of the judgment]\" is a supplied gloss on \"(zum Urtheile)\".","- 0463: \"Daher waren Raum und Zeit… möglich\" is rendered as a statement of Leibniz's view (\"were therefore possible only through…\"), as the argument requires. \"wesentliche Form\" = essential form. \"transsc.\" expanded to \"transcendental\".","- 0463: \"Die Form ist vor sich allein gegeben\" is rendered \"given by itself alone\". The \"Materie\" in the closing clause is taken as the antecedent of \"derselben\".","- 0465: \"Erschleichungen\" = surreptitious claims (subreptions); \"Erkentnißkraft\" = faculty of knowledge; \"Erkentnisse\" = modes of knowledge. \"Topik\" = topic; \"Ort\" = location.","- 0466: \"Ueberlegung\" = reflection (Latin *reflexio*); \"iene\" is taken to refer to the four titles. The sentence \"daß durch iene nicht der Gegenstand… dargestellt wird\" is syntactically irregular and has been smoothed.","- 0467: \"vermeinte\" = supposed; \"critische Vernunft\" = critical reason; \"Verwechselung\" = confusion."]],"krv-a-0468":["In the absence of such a transcendental topic, and so deceived by the amphiboly of the concepts of reflection, the celebrated Leibniz erected an intellectual system of the world. Or rather, he believed that he was knowing the inner constitution of things, in that he compared all objects solely with the understanding and with the abstracted formal concepts of its thought. Our table of the concepts of reflection affords us the unexpected advantage of setting before our eyes what is distinctive of his doctrine in all its parts, and at the same time the guiding ground of this peculiar way of thinking, which rested on nothing but a misunderstanding. He compared all things with one another merely through concepts, and, as was natural, found no differences other than those through which the understanding distinguishes its pure concepts from one another. The conditions of sensible intuition, which carry with them their own differences, he did not regard as original; for sensibility was to him only a confused mode of representation, and not a special source of representations. Appearance was for him the representation of the thing in itself, though distinguished from knowledge through the understanding in respect of logical form only. For appearance, owing to its customary lack of analysis, draws into the concept of the thing a certain admixture of subsidiary representations, which the understanding knows how to separate from it. In a word, Leibniz *intellectuated* appearances, just as Locke, in his system of noogony (if I may be permitted to use these expressions), had wholly *sensified* the concepts of the understanding, that is, had passed them off as nothing but empirical, though abstracted, concepts of reflection. Instead of seeking in understanding and sensibility two quite different sources of representations, which yet only in connection can judge of things with objective validity, each of these great men held to one only of the two, which, in his opinion, related immediately to things in themselves, while the other did nothing but confuse or order the representations of the first.",["- Restated the opening sentence as two sentences (\"Or rather…\") to relieve the *oder glaubte vielmehr* construction.","- \"seines Denkens\" referred to the understanding; \"its thought\" chosen over \"his thought\".","- The sentence beginning \"Appearance was for him…\" is divided: the *da nemlich* clause becomes a separate sentence, with \"appearance\" supplied as subject for *iene* (i.e. sensibility's representation); \"iene\" could be read as sensibility, since it refers to the confused mode of representation.","- *intellectuirte* / *sensificirt*: Kant's coinages, rendered as \"intellectuated\" / \"sensified\"; *Noogonie* as \"noogony\".","- *Erkentniß* = knowledge; *Vorstellungsart* = \"mode of representation\"."]],"krv-a-0469":["Leibniz accordingly compared the objects of the senses, as things in general, merely in the understanding with one another. First, he compared them in so far as they are to be judged by the understanding to be identical or different. Since he thus had before his eyes merely their concepts, and not their position in intuition, in which alone objects can be given, and entirely disregarded the transcendental locus of these concepts (whether the object is to be reckoned among appearances or among things in themselves), the result could not but be that he extended his principle of the indiscernible, which is valid merely of concepts of things in general, to the objects of the senses (*mundus phaenomenon*), and believed that he had thereby effected no small extension of our knowledge of nature. Certainly, if I know a drop of water as a thing in itself in all its inner determinations, I cannot allow any one drop to count as different from another if the whole concept of it is identical with that of the other. But if the drop is an appearance in space, it has its location not merely in the understanding (among concepts) but in sensible outer intuition (in space). And there the physical locations are quite indifferent to the inner determinations of things. A location = *b* can receive a thing which is completely similar and equal to another in location = *a*, just as well as if it were ever so much inwardly different from it. Difference of location, without any further conditions, makes the plurality and distinction of objects as appearances not only possible but necessary. Thus that seeming law is no law of nature. It is simply an analytic rule, or a comparison of things through mere concepts.",["- Divided the first sentence after \"unter einander\" to carry *Erstlich* in a separate sentence; Kant's construction (\"so fern sie… geurtheilt werden sollen\") is retained in sense.","- *Grundsatz des Nichtzuunterscheidenden* = \"principle of the indiscernible\" (*principium identitatis indiscernibilium*).","- *Ort / Stelle*: \"location\" and \"position\" used to keep *transscendentaler Ort* (\"transcendental locus\") distinct.","- Split the \"Ist er aber…\" passage at \"und da sind…\" for clarity.","- *vor sich* in the last sentence but one rendered by \"without any further conditions\"; the text reads *ohne weitere Bedingungen, schon vor sich*, \"already of itself\" is implied."]],"krv-a-0470":["Secondly: the principle that realities (as mere affirmations) never logically conflict with one another is a perfectly true proposition concerning the relation of concepts. But in respect of nature, or of any thing in itself (of which we have no concept whatever), it means not the least. For real conflict is found everywhere where A − B = 0; that is, where one reality, combined with another in one subject, cancels the effect of the other. All hindrances and counter-actions in nature, which are ceaselessly before our eyes, show this; and since they rest on forces, they must be entitled *realitates phaenomena*. General mechanics can even state the empirical condition of this conflict in an *a priori* rule, by considering the opposition of directions, a condition of which the transcendental concept of reality knows nothing. Although Herr von Leibniz did not announce this proposition with the pomp of a new principle, he yet made use of it for new assertions, and his successors expressly incorporated it in their Leibnizo-Wolffian systems. By this principle, for instance, all evils are nothing but consequences of the limitations of creatures, that is, negations, since these are the only conflicting element in reality. (In the mere concept of a thing in general this is indeed so, but not in things as appearances.) Similarly, its adherents find it not only possible but natural to unite all reality in one being without any apprehended conflict, since they know no conflict save that of contradiction (through which the concept of a thing itself is cancelled), and not that of reciprocal detriment, where one real ground cancels the effect of another; and for the representation of such a conflict we find the conditions only in sensibility.",["- Divided the long first sentence at \"bedeutet aber…\" and \"welches alle Hindernisse…\"; *welches* taken to refer to the whole preceding statement of real conflict.","- *Leibnitzwolfianische* rendered \"Leibnizo-Wolffian\".","- *seyn* in \"weil diese das einzige Widerstreitende der Realität seyn\" is an archaic subjunctive; rendered as indirect statement (\"are the only conflicting element\").","- *Realgrund* = \"real ground\".","- *Schranken* = \"limitations\".","- *allerwerts* = \"everywhere\"."]],"krv-a-0471":["Thirdly: the Leibnizian monadology has no other ground than that this philosopher represented the distinction of the inner and the outer solely in relation to the understanding. Substances in general must have something inner, which is therefore free from all outer relations, and consequently also from composition. The simple is therefore the foundation of the inner [nature] of things in themselves. But the inner [aspect] of their state cannot consist in location, shape, contact or motion (determinations which are all outer relations). We can therefore ascribe to substances no inner state other than that through which we ourselves determine our sense inwardly, namely, the state of representations. Thus the monads were made ready, which are to constitute the primary stuff of the whole universe, but whose active force consists only in representations, through which they are, strictly speaking, active merely within themselves.",["- *Das Innere der Dinge an sich selbst*: \"inner [nature]\" and \"inner [aspect]\" supplied in brackets.","- *Sinn* rendered \"sense\" (inner sense).","- *So wurden denn die Monaden fertig*: rendered \"were made ready\", keeping the ironic tone of \"fertig\".","- *Grundstoff* = \"primary stuff\"."]],"krv-a-0472":["For this very reason, however, his principle of the possible community of substances with one another had also to be a pre-established harmony, and could not be a physical influence. For since everything is occupied only inwardly, that is, with its own representations, the state of the representations of one substance could stand in no active connection whatever with that of another. Some third cause, influencing all together, had to make their states correspond with one another. This was to be done not indeed by occasional assistance, applied specially in each individual case (*systema assistentiae*), but through the unity of the idea of a cause valid for all, in which they must all receive their existence and permanence, and consequently also their reciprocal correspondence with one another in accordance with universal laws.",["- Divided the sentence after \"Verbindung stehen\"; \"sondern es mußte…\" begins a new sentence.","- *Principium* rendered \"principle\"; *vor alle gültigen* = \"valid for all\".","- *Beharrlichkeit* = \"permanence\".","- *einfliessende* rendered \"influencing\", to keep the contrast with *physischer Einfluß*."]],"krv-a-0473":["Fourthly, his celebrated doctrine of space and time, in which he intellectualised these forms of sensibility, sprang solely from this same illusion of transcendental reflection. If I seek to represent outer relations of things through the mere understanding, this can be done only by means of a concept of their reciprocal action; and if I am to connect one state of a thing with another state of the same thing, this can be done only in the order of grounds and consequences. Leibniz accordingly thought of space as a certain order in the community of substances, and of time as the dynamical sequence of their states. That which both seem to possess of their own, independently of things, he ascribed to the confusion of these concepts. This confusion, he held, caused what is a mere form of dynamical relations to be regarded as an intuition that is self-subsistent and antecedent to the things themselves. Space and time were thus the intelligible form of the connection of things (substances and their states) in themselves. The things, again, were intelligible substances (*substantiae noumena*). Nevertheless he wished to have these concepts hold of appearances, because he conceded to sensibility no mode of intuition peculiar to itself, but sought all representation of objects, even the empirical, in the understanding, and left to the senses nothing but the despicable task of confusing and distorting the representations of the former.",[]],"krv-a-0474":["But even if we could say something synthetically of things in themselves through the pure understanding (which is, however, impossible), this could not be extended to appearances, which do not represent things in themselves. In this latter case, then, I shall in transcendental reflection always have to compare my concepts only under the conditions of sensibility. Space and time will thus be determinations, not of things in themselves, but of appearances. What things in themselves may be I do not know, and I have no need to know, since a thing can never come before me otherwise than in appearance.",[]],"krv-a-0475":["I proceed in the same manner with the other concepts of reflection. Matter is *substantia phaenomenon*. What belongs to it inwardly I seek in all the parts of the space which it occupies, and in all the effects which it exercises, and which can, of course, always be only appearances of outer sense. I have, therefore, nothing that is absolutely inner, but only what is comparatively inner, and this in turn consists of outer relations. The absolutely inner of matter, as it would be for the pure understanding, is, however, a mere fancy. For matter is nowhere an object for the pure understanding. The transcendental object, which may be the ground of this appearance that we entitle matter, is a mere something, of which we should not understand what it is even if someone were able to tell us. For we can understand only what brings with it, in intuition, something corresponding to our words. If the complaint that we have no insight whatever into the inner [nature] of things is to mean that we do not comprehend through the pure understanding what the things which appear to us may be in themselves, it is wholly unjust and unreasonable. For those who make it demand that we should be able to know things, and therefore intuit them, without senses; and consequently that we should have a faculty of knowledge differing from the human not merely in degree, but even in the kind of intuition and in its character. We should thus be, not men, but beings of whom we cannot say whether they are even possible, much less what their constitution would be. Observation and analysis of appearances penetrate into the inner [parts] of nature, and one cannot tell how far this may go in time. But those transcendental questions which go beyond nature we should never be able to answer, even if the whole of nature were laid open to us; for it is not even granted us to observe our own mind with any other intuition than that of inner sense. In it lies the secret of the origin of our sensibility. Its relation to an object, and what the transcendental ground of this unity may be, lie undoubtedly too deeply hidden for us, who know even ourselves only through inner sense, and therefore as appearance, to be able to employ so unsuitable an instrument of investigation to find anything but appearances again and again, though it is their non-sensible cause that we should gladly explore.",[]],"krv-a-0476":["What makes this criticism of the inferences drawn from the mere acts of reflection so exceedingly useful is that it clearly demonstrates the nullity of all inferences concerning objects which are compared with one another merely in the understanding. At the same time it confirms what we have chiefly insisted upon: that although appearances, as things in themselves, are not comprehended among the objects of the pure understanding, they are yet the only objects in respect of which our knowledge can possess objective reality, that is, where intuition corresponds to the concepts.",[]],"krv-a-0477":["When we reflect merely logically, we only compare our concepts with one another in the understanding, to see whether both contain precisely the same, whether they contradict each other or not, whether something is contained inwardly in the concept or is added to it, and which of the two is to be given, and which is to count only as a way of thinking what is given. But if I apply these concepts to an object in general (in the transcendental sense), without determining further whether it is an object of sensible or of intellectual intuition, limitations at once show themselves (forbidding us to go beyond this concept) which pervert all empirical use of the concepts. Thereby they prove that the representation of an object as a thing in general is not merely insufficient, but, unless it is sensibly determined and independent of empirical condition, self-contradictory in itself. One must therefore either abstract from every object (as in logic), or, if one assumes an object, must think it under conditions of sensible intuition. The intelligible would accordingly require a quite special intuition which we do not possess, and in default of it is for us nothing; while, on the other hand, appearances also cannot be objects in themselves. For if I think merely things in general, the difference of outer relations cannot, of course, constitute a difference of the things themselves; on the contrary, it presupposes this difference. And if the concept of the one is not inwardly distinguished at all from that of the other, I am only placing one and the same thing in different relations. Further, by the addition of a mere affirmation (reality) to another, the positive is increased, and nothing is withdrawn or removed from it; hence the real in things in general cannot be in conflict with itself, and so on.",[]],"krv-a-0478":["The concepts of reflection have, as we have shown, through a certain misinterpretation such an influence upon the use of the understanding that they have been able to mislead even one of the most acute of all philosophers into a supposed system of intellectual knowledge, which undertakes to determine its objects without the addition of the senses. For this very reason the exposition of the deceptive cause of the amphiboly of these concepts, in occasioning false principles, is of great utility in reliably determining and securing the limits of the understanding.",["- 0473: \"intellectuirte\" rendered \"intellectualised\"; long sentence on Leibniz divided into several, and \"welche machte, daß…\" recast as \"This confusion, he held, caused…\". \"He held\" is supplied to mark reported doctrine.","- 0473: \"vor\" (= für/als) read as \"as\"; \"Verworrenheit\" = confusion; \"Lehrbegriff\" = doctrine.","- 0474: Sentence breaks restructured slightly; \"Ueberlegung\" = reflection.","- 0475: Brackets mark supplied \"[nature]\" and \"[parts]\" for the substantivised \"das Innere\"/\"Ins Innre\". \"Comparativinnerliches\" = \"what is comparatively inner\".","- 0475: \"Erkentnißvermögen\" = faculty of knowledge; \"Gemüth\" = mind; \"Grille\" = fancy. The final sentence is divided with \"though\" clause kept.","- 0476: Text reads \"als Dinge an sich selbst\"; rendered as printed (\"appearances, as things in themselves, are not comprehended\"), which is awkward, and the sense plainly is \"not as things in themselves\". \"Critik der Schlüsse\" rendered \"criticism of the inferences\".","- 0477: \"unabhängig von empirischer Bedingung\" is syntactically difficult; the clause is smoothed as \"unless it is sensibly determined and independent of empirical condition\", so the reading is doubtful. \"Sondere\" = special; \"transsc. Verstande\" expanded as \"transcendental sense\".","- 0478: \"Amphibolie\" kept as \"amphiboly\"; \"Mißdeutung\" = misinterpretation."]],"krv-a-0479":["We must indeed say that whatever universally belongs to a concept, or contradicts it, belongs to or contradicts everything particular that is contained under that concept (*dictum de omni et nullo*). But it would be absurd to alter this logical principle so that it ran: what is not contained in a universal concept is also not contained in the particular concepts which stand under it. For these are particular concepts precisely because they contain in themselves more than is thought in the universal. Yet it is in fact upon this latter principle that the whole intellectual system of Leibniz is built. The system therefore falls along with it, together with all the ambiguity [*Zweideutigkeit*] in the employment of the understanding which springs from it.",["- The semicolon-linked sentence is kept, but the \"denn … Nun ist doch wirklich …\" sequence is divided into separate sentences.","- \"Verstandesgebrauch\" is rendered \"employment of the understanding\"; \"intellectuelle System\" is \"intellectual system\".","- \"samt aller … Zweideutigkeit\" is made a clause (\"together with all the ambiguity … which springs from it\") for smoothness."]],"krv-a-0480":["The principle of the indiscernible rested properly on the presupposition that, if a certain distinction is not met with in the concept of a thing in general, it is not to be met with in the things themselves. Consequently all things are completely identical (*numero eadem*) which do not already differ from one another in their concept, in quality or in quantity. But in the mere concept of any thing abstraction has been made from many necessary conditions of an intuition. By a singular haste, that from which abstraction is made is therefore taken to be nowhere to be found, and nothing is allowed to the thing save what is contained in its concept.",["- \"Der Satz des Nichtzuunterscheidenden\" is rendered \"principle of the indiscernible\" (Leibniz's *principium identitatis indiscernibilium*).","- \"sey … seyn\" are subjunctives of indirect speech. The long first sentence is divided at \"folglich\".","- \"durch eine sonderbare Uebereilung\" is rendered \"by a singular haste\"; \"davor genommen\" is \"taken to be\"."]],"krv-a-0481":["The concept of a cubic foot of space, wherever and however often I may think it, is in itself completely the same. But two cubic feet in space are none the less distinguished solely by their places (*numero diversa*). These places are conditions of the intuition in which the object of this concept is given; they do not belong to the concept, but they do belong to sensibility as a whole. Similarly, there is in the concept of a thing no conflict at all so long as nothing negative has been combined with an affirmative; and merely affirmative concepts can, in combination, effect no cancelling [*Aufhebung*]. But in sensible intuition, in which reality (for instance, motion) is given, there are found conditions (opposite directions) from which abstraction had been made in the concept of motion in general. These conditions make possible a conflict, one which is not, to be sure, logical, namely that of a zero = 0 arising out of what is purely positive. And we could not say that all reality is in agreement with itself, on the ground that no conflict is found among its concepts.*)\n\nAccording to mere concepts, the inner is the substratum of all relation or outer determinations. If, then, I abstract from all conditions of intuition and confine myself solely to the concept of a thing in general, I can abstract from all outer relation, and there must nevertheless remain a concept of that which signifies no relation whatever but only inner determinations. It now seems to follow that in every thing (substance) there is something which is absolutely inner and precedes all outer determinations, inasmuch as it first makes them possible. This substratum, it would seem, is therefore something which no longer contains any outer relations, and is consequently simple (for corporeal things are always only relations, at least of parts outside one another). And since we know no absolutely inner determinations save those given through our inner sense, this substratum is not only simple but also, by analogy with our inner sense, determined through representations; that is to say, all things would properly be monads, or simple beings endowed with representations. All this would be quite correct, if something more than the concept of a thing in general did not belong to the conditions under which alone objects of outer intuition can be given to us, and from which the pure concept abstracts. For it then becomes evident that a persistent appearance in space (impenetrable extension) can contain nothing but relations and nothing absolutely inner, and yet be the primary substratum of all outer perception.\n\nBy mere concepts I certainly cannot think anything outer without something inner, for the very reason that concepts of relation presuppose things given absolutely, and are not possible without them. But intuition contains something which does not lie at all in the mere concept of a thing in general, and this furnishes the substratum which through mere concepts would never be known, namely a space which, together with all that it contains, consists of purely formal, or also real, relations. I cannot therefore say: since without something absolutely inner no thing can be represented through mere concepts, there is also in the things themselves, which are contained under these concepts, and in their intuition, nothing outer which does not have something absolutely inner as its ground. For when we have abstracted from all conditions of intuition, there certainly remains to us in the mere concept nothing but the inner in general, and the relation of the inner to the inner, through which alone the outer is possible. But this necessity, which is grounded solely upon abstraction, does not hold of things in so far as they are given in intuition with determinations that express mere relations, without having anything inner as their ground; for they are not things in themselves, but merely appearances.\n\nAll that we know in matter are nothing but relations (what we call its inner determinations are inner only comparatively); but among these relations there are some which are self-subsistent and persistent, and through them a determinate object is given to us. That, when I abstract from these relations, I have nothing further to think, does not abolish the concept of a thing as appearance, nor the concept of an object *in abstracto*. What it abolishes is all possibility of an object that is determinable according to mere concepts, that is, of a noumenon. It is indeed startling to hear that a thing should consist wholly of relations. But such a thing is mere appearance, and cannot be thought through pure categories; it consists itself in the mere relation of something in general to the senses. In the same way, the relations of things *in abstracto*, if we begin with mere concepts, cannot well be thought otherwise than as one thing being the cause of determinations in another; for that is our concept of the understanding for relations themselves. But since we then abstract from all intuition, there falls away a whole mode in which the manifold can determine to one another their place, namely the form of sensibility (space), which yet precedes all empirical causality.",["- Very long passage divided into paragraphs and shorter sentences; I have restated several of the \"Da scheint es nun, es folge daraus …\" series as one sentence with \"it would seem\" to keep Kant's reported inference distinct from his own view.","- \"kar\" in \"kar kein Widerstreit\" is silently read as \"gar\"; \"so so fern\" as \"so fern\". \"Zero = o\" is rendered \"zero = 0\".","- \"Aufhebung\" is \"cancelling\"; \"Verhältniß\" is \"relation\"; \"selbstständige\" is \"self-subsistent\"; \"Schlechthininneres\" is \"absolutely inner\".","- \"alle Realität unter einander Einstimmung sey\" is rendered \"all reality is in agreement with itself\", i.e. with one another.","- \"Verstandesbegriff von Verhältnissen selbst\" is rendered \"concept of the understanding for relations themselves\"; the phrase is slightly obscure, and \"selbst\" might also go with \"unser\".","- \"nicht bey den Dingen statt, so fern\" is read \"not … in so far as\", i.e. necessity fails for things as given in intuition.","- The asterisk marks the footnote, which is given separately (krv-a-0482). \"A 282–283\" is its attached location."]],"krv-a-0482":["*) Were one to avail oneself here of the usual evasion, that at least *realitates noumena* cannot work against one another, one would have to cite an example of such pure and non-sensible reality, in order that it might be understood whether such a reality represents anything at all or nothing. But no example can be taken from any source other than experience, which never offers more than phenomena. This proposition therefore signifies nothing more than that the concept which contains only affirmations contains nothing negative, a proposition of which we have never had any doubt.",["- Kant's \"Noümena\" is kept as the Latin technical term in the phrase *realitates noumena*, in italics.","- The single German sentence from \"Aber es kan kein Beyspiel …\" is divided in two.","- \"sinnenfrei\" is rendered \"non-sensible\"; \"woher anders\" is \"any source other than\"."]],"krv-a-0483":["If by merely intelligible objects we understand those things which are thought through pure categories, without any schema of sensibility, such objects are impossible. For the condition of the objective employment of all our concepts of understanding is simply the mode of our sensible intuition, whereby objects are given to us; and if we abstract from this intuition, the concepts have no relation to any object whatsoever. Even if we were to assume some other kind of intuition than this sensible intuition of ours, our functions of thought would still be without any meaning in respect of it.\n\nIf, however, we understand by the term only objects of a non-sensible intuition, to which our categories, it is true, do not apply, and of which we can therefore never have any knowledge (neither intuition nor concept), then noumena must indeed be admitted, but only in this merely negative sense. For in that case they say no more than that our mode of intuition does not apply to all things but only to objects of our senses, and that its objective validity is therefore limited; and so room is left for some other mode of intuition, and consequently also for things that would be its objects.\n\nBut in that case the concept of a noumenon is problematic. That is to say, it is the representation of a thing of which we can say neither that it is possible nor that it is impossible, since we know no mode of intuition other than our sensible intuition, and no kind of concepts other than the categories, and neither of these is adequate to an extra-sensible object. We cannot, therefore, positively extend the field of the objects of our thought beyond the conditions of our sensibility, and assume, outside appearances, objects of pure thought, that is, noumena; for such objects have no positive meaning that can be specified. It must be admitted of the categories that they alone are not sufficient for the knowledge of things in themselves, and that without the data of sensibility they would be merely subjective forms of the unity of understanding, but without an object.\n\nThought is, indeed, not in itself a product of the senses, and so far is not limited by them; but it does not follow that it can have a pure and proper employment of its own, without the aid of sensibility, since it would then be without an object. Nor can the noumenon be called such an object. For the term signifies just the problematic concept of an object for an entirely different intuition and an entirely different understanding from ours, and is therefore itself a problem.\n\nThe concept of the noumenon is thus not the concept of an object, but a problem unavoidably bound up with the limitation of our sensibility: namely, whether there may not be objects quite free from the conditions of that intuition. This question can be answered only indeterminately. Since sensible intuition does not apply to all things without distinction, room is left for more and other objects. They cannot, therefore, be absolutely denied; yet, in the absence of a determinate concept (for no category is suitable), neither can they be asserted as objects for our understanding.",[]],"krv-a-0484":["The understanding accordingly limits sensibility, without thereby enlarging its own field. In warning sensibility not to presume to apply to things in themselves but only to appearances, it thinks an object in itself, but only as a transcendental object, which is the cause of appearance (and is therefore not itself appearance). This object cannot be thought as magnitude, or as reality, or as substance, etc. (since these concepts always require sensible forms in which they determine an object). Of it, therefore, it is entirely unknown whether it is to be met with in us or outside us, whether it would be removed together with sensibility, or whether, if we take sensibility away, it would still remain.\n\nIf we choose to entitle this object a noumenon, because the representation of it is not sensible, we are free to do so. But since we can apply none of our concepts of understanding to it, the representation remains empty for us, and serves only to mark the limits of our sensible knowledge, and to leave open a space which we can fill neither through possible experience nor through the pure understanding.",[]],"krv-a-0485":["The critique of this pure understanding therefore does not allow us to create for ourselves a new field of objects beyond those which may come before it as appearances, or to stray into intelligible worlds, not even into the concept of them. The error which leads to this in the most plausible fashion, and which may be excused though not justified, lies in this: the employment of the understanding is made, contrary to its vocation, transcendental; and objects, that is, possible intuitions, are required to conform to concepts, instead of concepts [being required] to conform to possible intuitions (on which alone their objective validity rests).\n\nThe reason for this, again, is that apperception, and with it thought, precedes all possible determinate arrangement of representations. We thus think something in general, and determine it on the one side sensibly; yet we distinguish the universal object, represented *in abstracto*, from this mode of intuiting it. There then remains to us a mode of determining it by thought alone. This is, indeed, a merely logical form without content; yet it seems to us to be a way in which the object exists in itself (noumenon), without regard to the intuition which is limited to our senses.",[]],"krv-a-0486":["Before we leave the transcendental analytic we must add something which, though in itself of no particular importance, may yet seem requisite for the completeness of the system. The highest concept with which a transcendental philosophy customarily begins is the division into the possible and the impossible. But since every division presupposes a concept that is divided, a still higher concept must be specified; and this is the concept of an object in general (taken problematically, it being left undecided whether it is something or nothing).\n\nSince the categories are the only concepts which relate to objects in general, the distinction of an object as being something or nothing will proceed in accordance with the order and guidance of the categories.",[]],"krv-a-0487":["1. Opposed to the concepts of all, many and one is the concept which cancels everything, that is, none. Thus the object of a concept to which no assignable intuition corresponds is = nothing; that is, a concept without an object, like the noumena, which cannot be reckoned among the possibilities, though they must not on that account be declared impossible (*ens rationis*). Or it is like certain new fundamental forces which one thinks, indeed without contradiction, but also without any example from experience, and which therefore must not be reckoned among the possibilities.",[]],"krv-a-0488":["2. Reality is something, negation is nothing; that is, a concept of the absence of an object, as shadow, cold (*nihil privativum*).",[]],"krv-a-0489":["3. The mere form of intuition, without substance, is in itself no object, but the merely formal condition of an object (as appearance), like pure space and pure time (*ens imaginarium*). These are indeed something, as forms of intuiting, but are not themselves objects that are intuited.",[]],"krv-a-0490":["4. The object of a concept which contradicts itself is nothing, because the concept is nothing, the impossible; as, for instance, the rectilinear figure with two sides (*nihil negativum*).",["- 0483: Restated the long opening and closing sentences as several; \"vor\" in the old spelling is read as \"für\" (for), and \"Obiect\" is rendered \"object\" throughout.","- 0483: \"Erkentniß\" = knowledge; \"Aufgabe\" rendered \"problem\" to keep the link with \"Problem\" and \"problematisch\".","- 0483: The last sentence is irregular; \"sie\" is resolved as the objects (Gegenstände) of the preceding clause, and \"können\" governs both \"abgeläugnet\" and \"behauptet\". I have smoothed this.","- 0483: In \"ihre obiective Gültigkeit begränzt ist\", \"ihre\" refers to the mode of intuition.","- 0484: \"iene\" (sensibility) and \"dieses Obiect\" resolved by sense; \"Wollen wir... nennen\" rendered as a conditional.","- 0485: Supplied \"[being required]\" to complete the parallel construction; \"ihre\" in \"ihre obiective Gültigkeit\" refers to concepts. \"Bestimmung\" in \"wider seine Bestimmung\" rendered \"vocation\" by sense, not by the default \"determination\".","- 0485: In the last sentence \"ihn anzuschauen\" has the object as antecedent; \"ohne auf die Anschauung zu sehen\" taken as \"without regard to\".","- 0487: \"Grundkräfte\" rendered \"fundamental forces\"; the final clause, with its repeated \"gezehlt\", is smoothed slightly. The repetition \"nicht unter die Möglichkeiten gezehlt werden\" could be an inexactness in the original, preserved in sense."]],"krv-a-0491":["The table of this division of the concept of nothing (for the parallel division of the concept of something follows of itself) would accordingly have to be drawn up as follows: Nothing as 1. Empty concept without object, *ens rationis*. 2. Empty object of a concept, *nihil privativum*. 3. Empty intuition without object, *ens imaginarium*. 4. Empty object without concept, *nihil negativum*.",["- Kant's numerals are run on in a single sentence; I have kept them in that form and merely punctuated them.","- *Gegenstand* = object; *Anschauung* = intuition. The Latin terms are left in Latin, as Kant gives them."]],"krv-a-0492":["It will be seen that the thought-entity (*Gedankending*, no. 1) is distinguished from the non-entity (*Undinge*, no. 4) in this: the former must not be reckoned among the possibilities, since it is mere fiction (though not self-contradictory), whereas the latter is opposed to possibility, inasmuch as the concept even cancels itself. Both, however, are empty concepts. On the other hand, the *nihil privativum* (no. 2) and the *ens imaginarium* (no. 3) are empty data for concepts. If light has not been given to the senses, we cannot represent to ourselves darkness; and if extended beings have not been perceived, we cannot represent space. Negation, like the mere form of intuition, is, in the absence of something real, no object.",["- \"Den Begriff so. gar\" is read as \"so gar\" (OCR/abbreviation slip), i.e. \"even\"; \"ienes/dieses\" resolved as former/latter.","- *Obiecte* rendered \"object\", the plural turned into a general singular for English idiom."]],"krv-a-0493":["Second Division of Transcendental Logic. Transcendental Dialectic.",[]],"krv-a-0494":["Introduction. I. Of Transcendental Illusion.",["- *Schein* is rendered \"illusion\" in the heading and, where the contrast with truth is in point, \"illusion\" or \"semblance\" would also serve; I use \"illusion\" throughout for consistency."]],"krv-a-0495":["We have above entitled dialectic in general a logic of illusion. This does not mean that it is a doctrine of probability; for probability is truth, but truth known on insufficient grounds. Knowledge of it is therefore indeed defective, but is not on that account deceptive, and so ought not to be separated from the analytic part of logic. Still less may appearance (*Erscheinung*) and illusion (*Schein*) be regarded as the same. For truth or illusion is not in the object, in so far as it is intuited, but in the judgment upon it, in so far as it is thought. It is therefore correct to say that the senses do not err; yet this is not because they always judge rightly, but because they do not judge at all. Truth and error, and consequently illusion as the lure to error, are therefore to be found only in the judgment, that is, only in the relation of the object to our understanding. In a mode of knowledge which is in thorough agreement with the laws of the understanding there is no error. In a representation of the senses, since it contains no judgment at all, there is likewise no error. But no force of nature can of itself deviate from its own laws. Consequently neither the understanding by itself (without the influence of another cause) nor the senses by themselves would err. The understanding would not, because, if it acts solely according to its own laws, the effect (the judgment) must necessarily agree with these laws; and in agreement with the laws of the understanding consists the formal element in all truth. In the senses there is no judgment whatever, neither true nor false. Now since we have no sources of knowledge besides these two, it follows that error is brought about only through the unnoticed influence of sensibility upon the understanding. This causes the subjective grounds of judgment to merge with the objective grounds, and to make these deviate from their proper function.*) It is as with a body in motion, which by itself would always continue in a straight line in the same direction, but which, if another force acts upon it at the same time in another direction, passes into curvilinear motion. In order, then, to distinguish the peculiar action of the understanding from the force that intermingles with it, we shall have to regard the erroneous judgment as the diagonal between two forces which determine the judgment in two different directions, enclosing, as it were, an angle, and to resolve this composite effect into the simple effects of understanding and of sensibility. In pure judgments *a priori* this has to be done by transcendental reflection, through which (as has already been shown) each representation is assigned its place in the faculty of knowledge appropriate to it, and the influence of the one faculty upon the other is thereby distinguished.",["- Kant's long sentence \"Weil wir nun ... zusammenfliessen ... machen\" is divided at \"wodurch es geschieht\"; the simile and the final sentence are also restated in shorter sentences.","- The footnote marker *) stands where Kant has it.","- *Erkentnißquellen* = sources of knowledge; *Erkentnißkraft* = faculty of knowledge; *Bestimmung* here \"proper function\" by the sense (the objective grounds' destination), not \"determination\".","- \"Mithin auch der Schein\" rendered \"and consequently illusion\"; \"die Verleitung zum lezteren\" = lure to error.","- \"die aber\" after \"Richtung\" is read as referring to the body's motion; the construction is smoothed."]],"krv-a-0496":["*) Sensibility, when made the foundation of the understanding as the object to which the understanding applies its function, is the source of real modes of knowledge. But the same sensibility, in so far as it influences the action of the understanding itself and determines it to judge, is the ground of error.",["- *Erkenntnisse* (plural) rendered \"modes of knowledge\"; \"untergelegt\" rendered \"made the foundation\"."]],"krv-a-0497":["Our task here is not to treat of empirical illusion (for example, optical illusion), which occurs in the empirical employment of rules of the understanding that are otherwise correct, and by which the faculty of judgment is led astray through the influence of imagination. We are concerned solely with transcendental illusion, which influences principles whose employment is not even meant for experience (in which case we should at least have a touchstone of their correctness), but which, in despite of all the warnings of criticism, carries us altogether beyond the empirical employment of the categories and holds us with the delusion of an extension of the pure understanding. We shall entitle those principles whose application keeps wholly within the limits of possible experience, immanent; and those which profess to pass beyond these limits, transcendent principles. By the latter I do not mean the transcendental employment or misemployment of the categories, which is a mere fault of the faculty of judgment when not duly curbed by criticism, and so not sufficiently attentive to the limits of the territory within which alone free play is allowed to the pure understanding. I mean actual principles which incite us to tear down all those boundary-posts and to claim a wholly new territory that recognises no demarcation anywhere. Thus transcendental and transcendent are not the same. The principles of pure understanding which we expounded above are meant to have only empirical, and not transcendental employment, that is, employment extending beyond the limits of experience. A principle which removes these limits, and even commands us to overstep them, is entitled transcendent. If our criticism can succeed in exposing the illusion in these pretended principles, then those principles which are of merely empirical employment may, in contrast with the others, be called immanent principles of the pure understanding.",["- The opening sentence is long and parenthetical; I have kept it as one but resolved \"als in welchem Falle\" as a parenthesis. \"Critik\" rendered \"criticism\".","- *Urtheilskraft* = faculty of judgment; *Spiel* rendered \"free play\".","- \"Sollen\" in \"überfliegen sollen\" rendered \"profess to\", which is a judgment of its sense (claimed, not obligatory).","- \"Gränzpfähle\" = boundary-posts."]],"krv-a-0498":["Logical illusion, which consists in the mere imitation of the form of reason (the illusion of fallacious inferences), arises solely from a lack of attention to the logical rule. As soon, therefore, as attention is sharpened to the case before us, the illusion completely disappears. Transcendental illusion, on the other hand, does not cease even after it has been exposed and its nullity clearly seen through transcendental criticism (for instance, the illusion in the proposition: the world must have a beginning in time). The cause of this is that in our reason (regarded subjectively as a human faculty of knowledge) there are fundamental rules and maxims of its employment, which have wholly the appearance of objective principles. In consequence, the subjective necessity of a certain connection of our concepts, in the interest of the understanding, is taken for an objective necessity in the determination of things in themselves. It is an illusion which cannot be avoided, any more than we can avoid the sea appearing higher in the middle than at the shore, because we see the one through higher rays of light than the other; or, to take a further example, any more than the astronomer can prevent the moon from appearing larger to him at its rising, although he is not deceived by this illusion.",["- \"zu Gunsten des Verstandes\" rendered \"in the interest of the understanding\"; the sentence \"Die Ursache hievon...\" is divided after \"objective principles\".","- *Bestimmung der Dinge* rendered \"determination of things\", the sense of *Bestimmung* here being that of objective determination.","- The Latin-less parenthetical \"(z. B. ...)\" is kept in place after \"Critik\"."]],"krv-a-0499":["Transcendental dialectic will therefore content itself with exposing the illusion in transcendent judgments, and at the same time guarding us against being deceived by it. That the illusion should also (like logical illusion) actually vanish, and cease to be illusion, is something it can never achieve. For we are dealing with a natural and unavoidable illusion, which rests on subjective principles and passes them off as objective. Logical dialectic, in resolving fallacious inferences, has to do only with an error in the following of the principles, or with an artificial illusion in the imitation of them. There is, then, a natural and unavoidable dialectic of pure reason. This is not a dialectic in which some bungler entangles himself through want of knowledge, or one which some sophist has artfully devised in order to confuse rational people. It is a dialectic that inseparably attaches to human reason, and that, even after its deceptive appearance has been exposed, will not cease to mislead reason and to precipitate it into momentary aberrations which continually call for correction.",["- Restated the long second sentence as several; \"Denn ... unterschiebt, anstatt, daß ...\" divided, the contrast with logical dialectic made a separate sentence.","- \"Schein\" rendered \"illusion\"/\"illusion\" by context; \"Illusion\" and \"Schein\" are both rendered \"illusion\", the latter sometimes \"deceptive appearance\" for *Blendwerk* (to avoid confusion with *Erscheinung*, \"appearance\").","- \"Kentnissen\" (A spelling) rendered \"knowledge\", as a plural-free sense."]],"krv-a-0500":["II. Of Pure Reason as the Seat of Transcendental Illusion.",["- *Schein* = \"illusion\" in this heading."]],"krv-a-0501":["A. Of Reason in General.",[]],"krv-a-0502":["All our knowledge begins with the senses, proceeds thence to understanding, and ends with reason, beyond which nothing higher is to be found in us for elaborating the matter of intuition and bringing it under the highest unity of thought. Now that I am to give an explanation of this supreme faculty of knowledge, I find myself in some perplexity. Reason, like understanding, admits of a merely formal, that is, logical employment, in which it abstracts from all content of knowledge. But it also admits of a real employment, since it itself contains the source of certain concepts and principles which it does not borrow either from the senses or from the understanding. The former faculty has, indeed, long since been defined by logicians as the faculty of inferring mediately (in contrast to immediate inferences, *consequentiis immediatis*). The second faculty, however, which itself generates concepts, is not thereby brought to light. Since, then, reason here divides into a logical and a transcendental faculty, a higher concept of this source of knowledge must be sought which comprehends both concepts under it. Meantime, by analogy with the concepts of the understanding, we may expect that the logical concept will at the same time give us the key to the transcendental, and that the table of the functions of the former will at once furnish the clue [*Stammleiter*, \"genealogical ladder\"] to the concepts of reason.",["- Divided the sentence \"Es giebt von ihr ... entlehnt\" into two; \"Eintheilung\" rendered \"divides\" for smoothness.","- *Erkentnißkraft* = \"faculty of knowledge\"; *Vermögen* = \"faculty\".","- *Stammleiter* is literally a genealogical ladder/pedigree; I give \"clue\" for the sense with the German in brackets, a supplied rendering.","- \"Verstandesbegriffe\" = \"concepts of the understanding\"."]],"krv-a-0503":["In the first part of our transcendental logic we defined the understanding as the faculty of rules. Here we distinguish reason from it by entitling reason the faculty of principles.",["- \"nennen wollen\" rendered \"entitling\", per the *heißen/nennen* convention."]],"krv-a-0504":["The expression \"principle\" is ambiguous, and commonly signifies only a mode of knowledge that can be used as a principle, although in itself, and in its own origin, it is no *principium*. Every universal proposition, even if derived from experience (by induction), can serve as major premiss in a syllogism; but it is not on that account itself a *principium*. The mathematical axioms (e.g. that between two points only one straight line is possible) are indeed universal modes of knowledge *a priori*, and are therefore rightly entitled principles, relatively to the cases that can be subsumed under them. But I cannot on that account say that I know this property of straight lines in general and in itself from principles; I know it only in pure intuition.",["- \"Erkentniß\" rendered \"mode of knowledge\" where it is a countable item (singular after \"ein\", and plural).","- Divided the last sentence's content minimally; \"relativisch\" = \"relatively\"."]],"krv-a-0505":["I should therefore entitle knowledge from principles that knowledge in which I know the particular in the universal through concepts. Thus every syllogism is a form of derivation of a mode of knowledge from a principle. For the major premiss always supplies a concept such that whatever is subsumed under the condition of this concept is known from it according to a principle. Now since every universal mode of knowledge can serve as major premiss in a syllogism, and since the understanding supplies such universal propositions *a priori*, these also can, in respect of their possible employment, be entitled principles.",["- \"dieienige nennen, da ich ...\" resolved as \"that knowledge in which I ...\"; *da* is used in the sense of \"in which\".","- \"Obersatz\" = \"major premiss\"; \"Vernunftschluß\" = \"syllogism\"."]],"krv-a-0506":["But if we consider these principles of pure understanding in themselves and in their origin, they are anything but knowledge from concepts. For they would not even be possible *a priori* if we did not bring in pure intuition (in mathematics) or the conditions of a possible experience in general. That everything which happens has a cause cannot be inferred from the concept of that which happens in general. On the contrary, the principle shows how we can first obtain from what happens a determinate concept of experience.",["- \"Grundsätze\" = \"principles\" (as distinct from *Principien*, which are also \"principles\"); the German differs, the English cannot show it.","- \"herbey zögen\" = \"bring in\"."]],"krv-a-0507":["The understanding, then, cannot furnish synthetic modes of knowledge from concepts at all; and it is these that I properly and strictly entitle principles. Universal propositions in general, on the other hand, may be entitled comparative principles.",["- \"indessen, daß\" rendered as a contrast, \"on the other hand\"."]],"krv-a-0508":["It is an old wish, which, who knows how late, will perhaps some day be fulfilled, that instead of the endless multiplicity of civil laws their principles should be sought out; for in this alone can consist the secret of simplifying legislation, as it is said. But laws are here only limitations of our freedom to conditions under which it is in thorough agreement with itself. They therefore relate to something which is wholly our own work, and of which we ourselves, through these concepts, can be the cause. But that objects in themselves, that the nature of things, should stand under principles and be determined in accordance with mere concepts, is, if not something impossible, at least highly absurd in what it demands. However this may be (for the inquiry into it still lies before us), it at least follows that knowledge from principles (in itself) is something quite different from mere knowledge of the understanding. The latter may indeed precede other modes of knowledge in the form of a principle, but in itself (so far as it is synthetic) it does not rest on mere thinking, nor contain a universal in accordance with concepts.",["- Divided the sentence \"Aber die Gesetze ... können\" in two, and \"Es mag aber ... \" after the final clause; the last relative clause (\"die zwar auch ...\") is turned into a separate sentence, \"The latter may ...\".","- \"Verstandeserkentniß\" = \"knowledge of the understanding\".","- \"widersinnisches\" = \"absurd\"."]],"krv-a-0509":["Understanding may be a faculty of the unity of appearances by means of rules; reason is the faculty of the unity of the rules of understanding under principles. It never applies directly to experience or to any object, but to the understanding, in order to give *a priori*, through concepts, a unity to the manifold modes of knowledge of the understanding. This unity may be entitled the unity of reason, and is of an altogether different kind from any that can be achieved by the understanding.",["- \"Der Verstand mag ... seyn, so ist ...\" is concessive; rendered \"may be ... ; reason is\".","- Divided the final relative clause into a new sentence, \"This unity ...\"."]],"krv-a-0510":["That is the general concept of the faculty of reason, so far as it could be made comprehensible in the complete absence of examples (which are to be given only later).",["- \"begreiflich gemacht\" = \"made comprehensible\"."]],"krv-a-0511":["B. Of the Logical Employment of Reason.",[]],"krv-a-0512":["A distinction is made between what is known immediately and what is merely inferred. That in a figure bounded by three straight lines there are three angles is known immediately; that these angles together are equal to two right angles is merely inferred. Since we are constantly in need of inference, and so in the end become quite accustomed to it, we finally cease to notice this distinction. Often, as in the so-called deception of the senses, we take for immediately perceived what we have only inferred.\n\nIn every inference there is a proposition which serves as ground, another, namely the conclusion, which is drawn from the first, and finally the inference [*Schlußfolge*] (consequence), in accordance with which the truth of the latter is inseparably connected with the truth of the former. If the inferred judgment is already so contained in the first that it can be derived from it without the mediation of a third representation, the inference is entitled immediate (*consequentia immediata*). I should prefer to call it an inference of the understanding. But if, besides the knowledge taken as ground, a further judgment is required in order to bring about the conclusion, the inference is entitled an inference of reason.\n\nIn the proposition \"All men are mortal\" there are already contained the propositions \"Some men are mortal,\" or \"Some mortal beings are men,\" or \"Nothing that is immortal is a man.\" These are therefore immediate conclusions from the first. On the other hand, the proposition \"All scholars are mortal\" is not contained in the underlying judgment (for the concept of scholars does not occur in it at all), and can be inferred from it only by means of an intermediate judgment.",["- Restated the long second paragraph as two sentences; \"Folgerung\" rendered \"conclusion\", \"Schlußfolge\" kept in German brackets as the distinct term, \"Consequenz\" retained.","- \"Erkentniß\" (OCR/orig. spelling) = \"knowledge\"; \"heißt\" = \"is entitled\".","- \"Verstandesschluß\" = \"inference of the understanding\"; \"Vernunftschluß\" = \"inference of reason\" (syllogism in the later text).","- Paragraph division in the English is mine."]],"krv-a-0513":["In every inference of reason I first think a rule (the major premiss) through the understanding. Secondly, I subsume a mode of knowledge [*ein Erkentniß*] under the condition of the rule (the minor premiss), by means of the power of judgment. Finally, I determine my knowledge through the predicate of the rule (the conclusion), and so *a priori* through reason.\n\nThe relation, therefore, which the major premiss, as the rule, represents between a knowledge and its condition constitutes the different kinds of inferences of reason. These are accordingly exactly threefold, just as all judgments in general are, in so far as they differ in the mode in which they express the relation of knowledge in the understanding. They are the categorical, the hypothetical, and the disjunctive inferences of reason.",["- Divided the final sentence in two; \"Urtheilskraft\" = \"power of judgment\".","- \"ein Erkentniß\" is rendered \"a mode of knowledge\" since it is used as a count noun; bracketed German given.","- \"Obersatz\" = \"major premiss\"; the Latin *maior*, *minor*, *conclusio* are glossed in parentheses rather than italicised, to keep the sentence readable."]],"krv-a-0514":["As mostly happens, the conclusion may be set as a problem in the form of a judgment, in order to see whether it does not follow from judgments already given, through which, namely, an altogether different object is thought. In that case I seek out in the understanding the assertion of this conclusion, to discover whether it is not to be found there, under certain conditions, in accordance with a universal rule. If I now find such a condition, and if the object of the conclusion can be subsumed under the given condition, the conclusion is inferred from a rule which is valid also of other objects of knowledge.\n\nFrom this we see that reason, in inferring, endeavours to reduce the great manifold of the knowledge of the understanding to the smallest number of principles (universal conditions), and thereby to bring about the highest unity of that knowledge.",["- \"Gegendstand\" is a slip for \"Gegenstand\"; silently corrected.","- \"aufgegeben\" = \"set as a problem\"; the sense requires it.","- Split the final \"Man sieht daraus\" into its own paragraph."]],"krv-a-0515":["C. The Pure Employment of Reason.",["- \"Gebrauch\" rendered \"employment\", as in Kemp Smith's own text."]],"krv-a-0516":["Can reason be isolated, and is it then still an independent source of concepts and judgments which spring solely from it, and through which it relates to objects? Or is it merely a subaltern faculty, whose function is to give to given modes of knowledge a certain form, entitled logical, a form through which the modes of knowledge of the understanding are subordinated to one another, and lower rules to others higher (the condition of which embraces in its sphere the condition of the former), so far as this can be achieved by comparing them? This is the question with which we are now concerned only provisionally.\n\nIn actual fact, manifoldness of rules and unity of principles is a demand of reason, made with a view to bringing the understanding into thoroughgoing coherence with itself, just as the understanding brings the manifold of intuition under concepts, and thereby connects it. But such a principle prescribes no law to objects, and does not contain the ground of the possibility of knowing and determining them as such. It is merely a subjective law of economy in the use of the stock of our understanding, directed to reducing, by comparison of its concepts, their general employment to the smallest possible number. We are not thereby entitled to demand from the objects themselves a uniformity which would promote the convenience and extension of our understanding, nor at the same time to ascribe objective validity to that maxim.\n\nIn a word, the question is: Does reason in itself, that is, does pure reason, contain *a priori* synthetic principles and rules, and in what may these principles consist?",["- Divided the long sentence beginning \"Aber ein solcher Grundsatz\" into two; the final clause (\"ohne daß man … berechtiget wäre\") is made a separate sentence with \"We are not … entitled\".","- \"Haushaltung\" = \"economy\"; \"Gemächlichkeit\" = \"convenience\".","- \"Vermögen\" = \"faculty\"; \"Grundsatz\" = \"principle\"; \"Principium\" also \"principle\".","- \"iene\" (the manifold of intuition) taken as referring to the manifold, not to concepts."]],"krv-a-0517":["The formal and logical procedure of reason in inferences of reason already gives us sufficient guidance as to the ground on which the transcendental principle of reason, in synthetic knowledge through pure reason, will rest.",["- \"derselben\" (twice) refers to reason (*Vernunft*), not to the inferences."]],"krv-a-0518":["In the first place, the inference of reason is not directed to intuitions, in order to bring them under rules (as the understanding does with its categories), but to concepts and judgments. Even if pure reason is directed to objects, it has no immediate relation to them and to their intuition, but only to the understanding and its judgments, which are in the first instance addressed to the senses and their intuition, in order to determine their object. Unity of reason is therefore not the unity of a possible experience, but is essentially different from this, which is the unity of the understanding.\n\nThat everything which happens has a cause is not a principle known and prescribed through reason. It makes the unity of experience possible, and borrows nothing from reason; for reason, apart from this relation to possible experience, could never, from mere concepts, have commanded any such synthetic unity.",["- \"darauf\" in the second sentence is taken to refer to objects; the awkward \"auf Gegenstände … darauf\" is smoothed.","- \"dieser als der Verstandeseinheit\" is rendered \"this, which is the unity of the understanding\".","- Paragraph division mine."]],"krv-a-0519":["In the second place, reason in its logical employment seeks the universal condition of its judgment (the conclusion), and the inference of reason is itself nothing but a judgment arrived at by subsuming its condition under a universal rule (the major premiss). Now this rule is in turn exposed to the same attempt on the part of reason, and the condition of the condition has therefore to be sought (by means of a prosyllogism) as far as this is possible. We readily see, therefore, that the peculiar principle of reason in general (in its logical employment) is: to find, for the conditioned knowledge of the understanding, the unconditioned whereby its unity is brought to completion.",["- Divided the German's two long sentences into three shorter ones; \"so lange es angeht\" = \"as far as this is possible\".","- \"Subsumtion seiner Bedingung\" is ambiguous; I take it as the subsumption of the judgment's condition under the rule."]],"krv-a-0520":["This logical maxim, however, can become a principle of pure reason only through the assumption that when the conditioned is given, the whole series of conditions, subordinated one to another, a series which is itself unconditioned, is likewise given, that is, is contained in the object and its connection.",["- The parenthesis \"(d. i. …)\" is run into the sentence; its subject is the series."]],"krv-a-0521":["Such a principle of pure reason is, however, manifestly synthetic; for the conditioned is related analytically to some condition, but not to the unconditioned. From this principle there must also arise various synthetic propositions of which the pure understanding knows nothing, since it has to do only with objects of a possible experience, the knowledge and synthesis of which is always conditioned. The unconditioned, however, if it really obtains, may be considered in particular, in respect of all the determinations which distinguish it from every conditioned, and must in this way furnish material for many synthetic propositions *a priori*.",["- \"Bezieht\" (capitalised in the OCR) silently corrected to lower case.","- \"Bestimmungen\" = \"determinations\"; \"statt hat\" = \"obtains\"."]],"krv-a-0522":["The principles which originate in this supreme principle of pure reason will, however, be transcendent in respect of all appearances; that is to say, no empirical employment of it adequate to it can ever be made. It will therefore be entirely different from all principles of the understanding, the employment of which is wholly immanent, since they have as their theme only the possibility of experience. Now whether that principle, that the series of conditions (in the synthesis of appearances, or also in the thinking of things in general) extends to the unconditioned, has objective correctness or not, and what consequences flow from it for the empirical employment of the understanding, is one question. Or is there rather no such objectively valid principle of reason at all, but only a merely logical prescription, that in ascending to ever higher conditions we should approach to completeness in them, and so bring into our knowledge the highest unity of reason possible to us? Has this need of reason, I say, been mistaken, through a misunderstanding, for a transcendental principle of pure reason, which hastily postulates such unlimited completeness in the series of conditions in the objects themselves? And, even in this case, what misinterpretations and delusions may creep into the syllogisms of reason whose major premiss has been taken from pure reason (and is perhaps more petition than postulate), and which ascend from experience upwards to its conditions? This will be our task in the transcendental dialectic, which we shall now develop from its sources, deeply concealed in human reason. We shall divide it into two main divisions, of which the first is to deal with the transcendent concepts of pure reason, the second with its transcendent and dialectical syllogisms.",[]],"krv-a-0523":["Transcendental Dialectic, Book I. The Concepts of Pure Reason.",[]],"krv-a-0524":["Whatever may be the nature of the possibility of concepts springing from pure reason, they are at any rate not merely reflected concepts, but concepts inferred. Concepts of the understanding are also thought *a priori*, antecedently to experience and for the sake of it; but they contain nothing more than the unity of reflection upon appearances, in so far as these are necessarily to belong to a possible empirical consciousness. Through them alone are knowledge and determination of an object possible. They first supply the material for inference, and there are no *a priori* concepts of objects preceding them from which they could be inferred. Their objective reality, on the other hand, rests solely on the fact that, since they constitute the intellectual form of all experience, it must always be possible to show their application in experience.",[]],"krv-a-0525":["The title \"concept of reason\", however, already indicates in advance that such a concept will not allow itself to be confined within experience. For it concerns a knowledge of which every empirical knowledge is only a part (perhaps the whole of possible experience or of its empirical synthesis); no actual experience ever completely reaches to this whole, yet it always belongs to it. Concepts of reason serve for the comprehension [*Begreifen*], as concepts of the understanding serve for the understanding [*Verstehen*] of perceptions. If they contain the unconditioned, they concern something under which all experience is subsumed, but which is never itself an object of experience: something to which reason leads in its inferences from experience, and by which it estimates and measures the degree of its empirical employment, but which never constitutes a member of the empirical synthesis. If such concepts none the less possess objective validity, they may be entitled *conceptus ratiocinati* (concepts validly inferred); if not, they have at least been surreptitiously obtained through a semblance of inference, and may be entitled *conceptus ratiocinantes* (concepts invalidly reasoned out [*vernünftelnde*]). Since this can be decided only in the chapter on the dialectical inferences of pure reason, we cannot yet take it into account. Meantime, as we have called the pure concepts of the understanding categories, so we shall give the concepts of pure reason a new name and entitle them transcendental ideas. This title we must now explain and justify.",[]],"krv-a-0526":["Book I of the Transcendental Dialectic, Section I. Ideas in General.",[]],"krv-a-0527":["Despite the great wealth of our languages, the thinking mind often finds itself at a loss for the expression that exactly fits its concept; and in default of it can make itself properly intelligible neither to others nor even to itself. To coin new words is to presume to legislate in language, and such attempts seldom succeed. Before we resort to this desperate expedient, it is advisable to look about in a dead and learned language, to see whether the concept and its appropriate expression may not already be found there. Even if the old usage of the term has become somewhat unsettled through the carelessness of its originators, it is still better to fix the meaning which was peculiarly its own (even if it should remain doubtful whether that was exactly the meaning intended at the time) than to ruin our enterprise by making ourselves unintelligible.",[]],"krv-a-0528":["For this reason, if there should be only a single word for a certain concept which, in its already accepted meaning, exactly fits that concept, and if the distinguishing of the concept from other related concepts is of great importance, it is advisable not to be prodigal in its use, or merely for the sake of variety to employ it synonymously in place of others, but carefully to preserve for it its own peculiar meaning. Otherwise it easily happens that the expression, no longer engaging attention in a special manner but being lost in the crowd of others of very different meaning, results in the loss also of the thought which it alone could have preserved.",[]],"krv-a-0529":["Plato made use of the expression \"idea\" in such a way that we can readily see that he understood by it something which is not only never borrowed from the senses, but which even far transcends the concepts of the understanding with which Aristotle occupied himself, since in experience nothing congruent with it is ever met with. For him ideas are archetypes of things themselves, and not merely keys to possible experiences, as the categories are. In his opinion they flowed from the highest reason, and from it have come to human reason. Human reason, however, no longer finds itself in its original state, but has laboriously to recall, by recollection (which is entitled philosophy), the old ideas, now much obscured. I do not propose to engage here in any literary inquiry to determine the sense which the sublime philosopher attached to his expression. I remark only that it is by no means unusual, in ordinary conversation as well as in writings, through comparing the thoughts which an author has expressed on his subject, to understand him even better than he understood himself, since he did not sufficiently determine his concept, and thereby sometimes spoke, or even thought, contrary to his own intention.",[]],"krv-a-0530":["Plato very well saw that our faculty of knowledge feels a much higher need than merely to spell out appearances according to synthetic unity so as to be able to read them as experience, and that our reason naturally soars to modes of knowledge which go much too far for any object that experience can give ever to be congruent with them, but which none the less have their reality and are by no means mere fictions of the brain.",["krv-a-0522: The long sentence \"Ob nun jener Grundsatz … das wird unser Geschäfte … sein\" is broken into a series of questions and then a closing statement; Kant's anacoluthon (the \"ob … ob … was aber\" series with no main verb until \"das wird\") is smoothed. \"Ein … ist one question\" is my supplied linking phrase; no German equivalent.","krv-a-0522: \"Mißverstand\" rendered \"misunderstanding\"; \"Petition\" kept as \"petition\" (petitio principii sense). \"Grundsatz\" = principle.","krv-a-0524: \"geschlossene Begriffe\" = \"concepts inferred\"; \"reflectirte\" = \"reflected\"; \"Bewandniß\" rendered \"nature\".","krv-a-0525: \"nichtig geschlossene\" is read as \"richtig geschlossene\" (Kant's sense, validly inferred), the A text's \"nichtig\" being a probable misprint or OCR slip; flagged as doubtful. \"Hauptstück\" rendered \"chapter\" here and \"division\" at the first occurrence in 0522 for the sense of the two parts.","krv-a-0525: \"bis dahin … zureicht\" is awkward; restated as \"no actual experience ever completely reaches to this whole, yet it always belongs to it.\" Square brackets added to give the German for *Begreifen*/*Verstehen* and *vernünftelnde*.","krv-a-0527: \"Kopf\" rendered \"mind\"; \"Anmassung\" \"presume\". Sentence divided.","krv-a-0529: \"Erinnerung (die Philosophie heißt)\" rendered \"recollection (which is entitled philosophy)\"; \"Theil geworden\" rendered \"have come to\". Kant's \"Urbilder\" = archetypes.","krv-a-0530: \"Erkentnißkraft\" = \"faculty of knowledge\"; \"Erkentnissen\" = \"modes of knowledge\"; \"buchstabiren\" = \"spell out\"."]],"krv-a-0531":["Plato found his ideas pre-eminently in everything that is practical,* that is, in everything which rests on freedom; and freedom in its turn falls under modes of knowledge that are a peculiar product of reason. Whoever would derive the concepts of virtue from experience, whoever would make into a pattern, as a source of knowledge, what at best can serve only as an example in an imperfect illustration (as many have actually done), would turn virtue into something equivocal, changing with time and circumstance, and useless as a rule: a nonentity. Everyone, on the other hand, is aware that when someone is held up to him as a pattern of virtue, he still always has the true original solely in his own mind, and compares this alleged pattern with it, estimating it solely by that standard. This original is the idea of virtue. In respect of this idea all possible objects of experience do indeed serve as examples (proofs that what the concept of reason demands is practicable in a certain degree), but not as archetypes. That no man will ever act in complete adequacy to what the pure idea of virtue contains does not in the least prove that there is anything chimerical in this thought. For all judgment as to moral worth or unworth is possible only by means of this idea; and it therefore necessarily underlies every approach to moral perfection, however far the obstacles in human nature, indeterminable as to their degree, may keep us removed from it.",["- Restated the long second sentence's closing clause (\"zu keiner Regel brauchbares zweideutiges Unding\") with a colon to keep \"nonentity\" as the final noun.","- Split \"Dieses ist aber die Idee der Tugend, in Ansehung deren…\" into two sentences; \"original\" is supplied as the antecedent of \"This.\"","- The asterisk marks the footnote (krv-a-0532). \"Erkentnissen\" = modes of knowledge (plural of Erkenntnis).","- \"Unding\" rendered \"nonentity\"; \"Urbilder\" = archetypes; \"Muster\" = pattern."]],"krv-a-0532":["* He extended his concept, to be sure, also to speculative modes of knowledge, provided only that they were given pure and completely *a priori*, and even to mathematics, although this has its object nowhere else than in possible experience. In this I cannot follow him, any more than in the mystical deduction of these ideas, or in the exaggerations through which he, as it were, hypostatised them; although the lofty language which he employed in this field is quite capable of a milder interpretation, more in keeping with the nature of things.",["- \"Erkentnisse\" = modes of knowledge. \"hypostasirte\" rendered \"hypostatised\" (British spelling).","- \"Hierin\" taken as referring to the whole extension to speculative knowledge and mathematics, together with the following items, hence \"In this.\""]],"krv-a-0533":["The Platonic Republic has become proverbial as a supposedly striking example of dreamed-of perfection, such as can have its seat only in the brain of the idle thinker; and Brucker finds it ridiculous that the philosopher should have asserted that no prince would ever govern well unless he participated in the ideas. We should do better, however, to pursue this thought further and, where the admirable man leaves us without help, to set it in a clearer light by fresh endeavours, rather than to put it aside as useless, under the very wretched and harmful pretext of impracticability. A constitution of the greatest possible human freedom according to laws which ensure that the freedom of each can coexist with that of all the others (not of the greatest happiness, for this will follow of itself) is at least a necessary idea. It must be made the foundation not merely of the first outline of a political constitution but of all the laws; and in this we must at the outset abstract from the obstacles now present, which perhaps may arise not so much unavoidably from human nature as from neglect of the true ideas in the making of laws. For nothing can be more harmful, or more unworthy of a philosopher, than the vulgar appeal to a supposedly conflicting experience. That experience would not exist at all if those institutions had been established at the proper time in accordance with the ideas, and if crude concepts, just because they had been derived from experience, had not in their stead frustrated every good intention. The more legislation and government are in harmony with this idea, the rarer indeed would punishments become; and it is then quite reasonable to hold (as Plato does) that in a perfect arrangement of them none at all would be required. Although this last may never come to be, the idea is entirely correct which sets up this maximum as the archetype, in order that, in accordance with it, the legal constitution of mankind may be brought ever nearer to the greatest possible perfection. For what the highest degree may be at which mankind must come to a stand, and how great therefore the gulf that must necessarily remain between the idea and its realisation, this no one can, or ought to, determine, precisely because it is freedom that can transcend every assigned limit.",["- Divided the long sentence on the constitution and the \"pöbelhafte Berufung\" into several sentences; \"die doch gar nicht existiren würde\" made a separate sentence, \"That experience.\"","- \"iene Anstalten\" = \"those institutions\"; \"derselben\" (of the punishments) resolved as \"them\" = Strafen.","- \"dieser Maximum\" taken as \"this maximum\" (the perfect arrangement of the preceding clause); \"Urbilde\" = archetype.","- \"Je übereinstimmender … waren\" (indicative where subjunctive expected) smoothed without comment in text."]],"krv-a-0534":["But it is not only in that wherein human reason exhibits genuine causality, and where ideas become operative causes (of actions and their objects), namely in the moral sphere, but also in respect of nature itself, that Plato rightly sees clear proofs of its origin from ideas. A plant, an animal, the regular order of the world-structure (presumably, therefore, the whole order of nature) show clearly that they are possible only in accordance with ideas. No single creature, under the individual conditions of its existence, coincides with the idea of what is most perfect of its kind (any more than man coincides with the idea of humanity, which he yet bears in his soul as the archetype of his actions). Nevertheless these ideas are, in the highest understanding, individual, unchangeable, thoroughly determined, and the original causes of things; and only the whole of their connection in the universe is alone and entirely adequate to that idea. If we set aside what is exaggerated in the expression, the philosopher's spiritual flight, in ascending from the physical, copy-like contemplation of the world-order to its architectonic connection according to ends, that is, according to ideas, is an endeavour which merits respect and imitation. But in respect of what concerns the principles of morality, of legislation and of religion, where the ideas first make experience itself (of the good) possible, although they can never be completely expressed in it, it is a quite peculiar merit. This is not recognised only because it is judged by the very empirical rules whose validity, as principles, was meant to be abolished by it. For in the case of nature, experience supplies us with the rule and is the source of truth; but in respect of moral laws experience is (alas!) the mother of illusion, and it is in the highest degree reprehensible to derive the laws prescribing what I ought to do from what is done, or to seek to limit them by it.",["- Divided the second sentence (the *daß*-clauses) into separate sentences; the parenthesis on man and the idea of humanity is retained.","- \"Verstande\" in \"im höchsten Verstande\" rendered \"in the highest understanding.\"","- \"copeylichen\" = \"copy-like\" (from *Kopei*, copy); \"Geistesschwung\" = \"spiritual flight.\"","- \"Mutter des Scheins\" = \"mother of illusion\" (*Schein*); \"durch sie\" taken as referring to the Platonic endeavour/ideas, rendered \"by it.\"","- In the last sentence of the first clause \"ein ganz eigenthümliches Verdienst\" is left with \"it is\" supplied for the missing verb."]],"krv-a-0535":["In place of all these reflections, the proper working out of which constitutes the peculiar dignity of philosophy, we are now occupied with a less brilliant, but still not unmeritorious task, namely, that of levelling and consolidating the ground for those majestic moral edifices. In this ground there are all sorts of molehill-tunnellings, made by reason in its fruitless, though confident, search for treasures, and these render such structures insecure. What it now behoves us to know exactly is the transcendental employment of pure reason, its principles and ideas, so that we may be able to determine and appraise properly the influence and the worth of pure reason.\n\nBefore I lay aside this preliminary introduction, I would, however, make a request of those who have philosophy at heart (which is more than is commonly found). If they should find themselves convinced by what has been said here and by what follows, let them take under their protection the expression *idea* in its original meaning. Otherwise it will fall in among the other expressions with which all sorts of species of representation are commonly designated in careless disorder, and science will suffer thereby. We are not in want of terms properly adapted to each species of representation, and we need not encroach upon the province of any other. Here is a graduated list of them.\n\nThe genus is representation in general (*repraesentatio*). Subordinate to it stands representation accompanied by consciousness (*perceptio*). A perception which relates solely to the subject, as the modification of its state, is sensation (*sensatio*). An objective perception is knowledge (*cognitio*). Knowledge is either intuition or concept (*intuitus vel conceptus*). The former relates immediately to the object and is single; the latter relates to it mediately, by means of a mark which several things may have in common. The concept is either empirical or pure. The pure concept, in so far as it has its origin in the understanding alone (not in the pure image of sensibility), is entitled *notio*. A concept formed from notions, which transcends the possibility of experience, is the idea, or concept of reason.\n\nTo anyone who has accustomed himself to this distinction, it must be intolerable to hear the representation of the colour red entitled an idea. It ought not even to be entitled a notion (a concept of understanding).",[]],"krv-a-0536":["Book I of the Transcendental Dialectic, Section II. The Transcendental Ideas.",[]],"krv-a-0537":["Transcendental analytic gave us an example of how the mere logical form of our knowledge can contain the origin of pure *a priori* concepts. These concepts represent objects antecedently to all experience; or rather, they indicate the synthetic unity which alone makes possible an empirical knowledge of objects. The form of judgments, when converted into a concept of the synthesis of intuitions, yielded the categories, which guide all employment of the understanding in experience.\n\nWe may similarly expect that the form of syllogisms, when applied to the synthetic unity of intuitions in accordance with the guidance of the categories, will contain the origin of special *a priori* concepts. These we may entitle pure concepts of reason, or transcendental ideas, and they will determine, according to principles, the employment of the understanding in the whole of experience in its entirety.",[]],"krv-a-0538":["The function of reason in its inferences consisted in the universality of knowledge through concepts, and the syllogism itself is a judgment which is determined *a priori* in the whole extent of its condition. The proposition \"Caius is mortal\" I could derive from experience through the understanding alone. But I look for a concept which contains the condition under which the predicate (assertion in general) of this judgment is given, that is, here, the concept of man. When I have subsumed under this condition, taken in its whole extent (\"All men are mortal\"), I determine in accordance with it the knowledge of my object (\"Caius is mortal\").",[]],"krv-a-0539":["Accordingly, in the conclusion of a syllogism we restrict a predicate to a certain object, having first thought it in the major premiss in its whole extent under a certain condition. This completed magnitude of the extent, in relation to such a condition, is entitled universality (*universalitas*). To it there corresponds, in the synthesis of intuitions, allness (*universitas*), or totality of the conditions.\n\nThe transcendental concept of reason is therefore no other than that of the totality of the conditions for a given conditioned. Now since the unconditioned alone makes possible the totality of the conditions, and, conversely, the totality of the conditions is always itself unconditioned, a pure concept of reason can in general be explained through the concept of the unconditioned, in so far as this contains a ground of the synthesis of the conditioned.",[]],"krv-a-0540":["There will be as many pure concepts of reason as there are kinds of relation which the understanding represents to itself by means of the categories. We have therefore to seek, first, an unconditioned of the categorical synthesis in a subject; secondly, an unconditioned of the hypothetical synthesis of the members of a series; thirdly, an unconditioned of the disjunctive synthesis of the parts in a system.",[]],"krv-a-0541":["For there are precisely as many kinds of syllogism, each of which advances through prosyllogisms to the unconditioned. One advances to the subject which is itself no longer predicate; another to the presupposition which presupposes nothing further; the third to an aggregate of the members of the division, to which nothing further is required for the completion of the division of a concept.\n\nHence the pure concepts of reason, of totality in the synthesis of conditions, are necessary, at least as problems set us in order to carry the unity of the understanding, if possible, up to the unconditioned, and are grounded in the nature of human reason. This holds even if these transcendental concepts should lack, in other respects, a suitable employment *in concreto*, and so have no other utility than to direct the understanding in such a way that, while its employment is extended to the utmost, it is at the same time brought into complete consistency with itself.",["- 0535: long opening sentences divided; \"Maulwurfsgänge\" rendered \"molehill-tunnellings\" (literally mole-tunnels), a choice of idiom. \"Hertzen liegt\" = \"have at heart\".","- 0535: \"Vorstellungsart\" rendered \"species of representation\"; \"Stufenleiter\" as \"graduated list\"; Erkentniß = knowledge; *Notio* kept in Latin.","- 0535: \"Sie ist nicht einmal Notion … zu nennen\" made \"It ought not even to be entitled a notion\"; \"Sie\" refers to the representation of red.","- 0537: \"Transcendental analytic\" kept without article as in German; second sentence split into two paragraphs; \"vielmehr\" rendered \"or rather\".","- 0538: \"(Assertion überhaupt)\" retained as parenthesis; full stop after \"darnach\" in the German is treated as a printing slip for a comma.","- 0539: the German run-on (comma after \"gedacht haben\") divided into two sentences; \"die Allheit (Vniversitas)\" kept with Latin in italics, spelling normalised.","- 0540: \"Verhältnisses\" taken as relation, i.e. the categories of relation; the three unconditioneds supplied with \"an unconditioned of\" for the elliptical \"ein Unbedingtes … zweitens … drittens\".","- 0541: \"der Verstand ... durchgehend einstimmig\" given as \"complete consistency with itself\"; \"Aufgaben\" rendered \"problems set us\"."]],"krv-a-0542":["In speaking here of the totality of conditions and of the unconditioned, as the common title of all concepts of reason, we again come upon an expression which we cannot dispense with, and yet, owing to an ambiguity that has attached to it through long misuse, cannot employ with safety. The word *absolute* is one of the few words which in their original meaning were adapted to a concept to which afterwards no other word of the same language is exactly suited. The loss of this word, or, what is the same thing, its vacillating use, must therefore involve the loss of the concept itself. And it is a concept which, since it greatly occupies reason, cannot be dispensed with without grave detriment to all transcendental judgments.\n\nThe word *absolute* is now often used merely to indicate that something holds of a thing considered in itself, and therefore intrinsically. In this meaning the absolutely possible would signify what is possible in itself (*interne*), which is in fact the least that can be said of an object. On the other hand the word is also sometimes used to indicate that something is valid in every relation (without restriction), as, for instance, absolute dominion. The absolutely possible would then signify what is possible in every respect and in every relation, which is in turn the most that I can say regarding the possibility of a thing.\n\nNow these meanings do indeed at times coincide. Thus what is intrinsically impossible is also impossible in every relation, and therefore absolutely impossible. But in most cases the two meanings are infinitely far apart, and I can in no way conclude that, because something is possible in itself, it is therefore also possible in every relation, and so absolutely possible. As regards absolute necessity, I shall show in what follows that in no case does it depend upon intrinsic necessity, and that the latter must not, therefore, be regarded as synonymous with it. That whose opposite is intrinsically impossible is, to be sure, impossible in every respect, and is therefore itself absolutely necessary. But I cannot conversely argue that what is absolutely necessary has an opposite which is intrinsically impossible; that is, that the absolute necessity of things is an intrinsic necessity. For in certain cases this intrinsic necessity is a quite empty expression, with which we cannot connect the least concept; whereas the concept of the necessity of a thing in every relation (to everything possible) carries with it quite special determinations.\n\nSince the loss of a concept of great utility in speculative philosophy can never be a matter of indifference to the philosopher, I hope that the determination and careful preservation of the expression upon which the concept depends will not be a matter of indifference to him either.",[]],"krv-a-0543":["It is in this extended meaning that I shall employ the word *absolute*, opposing it to what is valid only comparatively or in some special respect. For the latter is restricted by conditions, whereas the former is valid without restriction.",[]],"krv-a-0544":["Now the transcendental concept of reason is always directed solely to absolute totality in the synthesis of conditions, and never terminates save in what is unconditioned absolutely, that is, in every relation. For pure reason leaves everything to the understanding, which is related in the first instance to the objects of intuition, or rather to the synthesis of these in imagination. Reason reserves to itself solely the absolute totality in the employment of the concepts of the understanding, and endeavours to carry the synthetic unity, which is thought in the category, up to the absolutely unconditioned. We may therefore entitle this the unity of reason in appearances, just as that which the category expresses is entitled the unity of understanding.\n\nReason, then, is related only to the employment of the understanding; and not, indeed, in so far as the understanding contains the ground of possible experience (for the absolute totality of conditions is not a concept that can be employed in experience, since no experience is unconditioned), but in order to prescribe to the understanding its direction towards a certain unity of which it has itself no concept, and which aims at gathering together all the acts of the understanding, in respect of each object, into an absolute whole. Consequently the objective employment of the pure concepts of reason is always transcendent, whereas that of the pure concepts of the understanding must, by their nature, always be immanent, since it is limited solely to possible experience.",[]],"krv-a-0545":["By an idea I understand a necessary concept of reason to which no congruent object can be given in the senses. The pure concepts of reason now under consideration are thus transcendental ideas. They are concepts of pure reason, in that they view all knowledge gained in experience as determined through an absolute totality of conditions. They are not arbitrarily invented, but are imposed by the very nature of reason, and therefore stand in necessary relation to the whole employment of the understanding. Lastly, they are transcendent, and overstep the limits of all experience; no object adequate to the transcendental idea can ever be found in experience.\n\nWhen we speak of an idea, we say a great deal so far as its object (as an object of pure understanding) is concerned, but very little so far as its subject is concerned, that is, in regard to its actuality under empirical conditions; and this for the very reason that, as the concept of a maximum, it can never be given *in concreto* in a congruent manner. Now in the merely speculative employment of reason the latter is really the whole aim; and approximation to a concept which is never actually attained is the same as if the concept were wholly missed. Of such a concept, therefore, it is said that it is 'only an idea'. We could thus say that the absolute whole of all appearances is only an idea; for since we can never represent it in an image, it remains a problem admitting of no solution.\n\nIn the practical employment of the understanding, on the other hand, we are solely concerned with execution in accordance with rules. The idea of practical reason can therefore always be given actually *in concreto*, although only in part; indeed it is the indispensable condition of every practical employment of reason. Its execution is always limited and defective, but within boundaries that cannot be determined, and so always under the influence of the concept of an absolute completeness. The practical idea is accordingly always in the highest degree fruitful, and in respect of actual actions indispensably necessary. In it pure reason has even causality, to bring about actually what its concept contains. Of wisdom, therefore, we cannot say disparagingly that it is 'only an idea'. On the contrary, just because it is the idea of the necessary unity of all possible ends, it must serve as a rule for everything practical, as the original, or at least the limiting, condition.",[]],"krv-a-0546":["Although we must say of the transcendental concepts of reason that they are 'only ideas', we shall not on that account regard them as superfluous and void. For even if no object can be determined through them, they can still, in a fundamental way and unobserved, serve the understanding as a canon for its extended and consistent employment. The understanding thereby knows no more objects than it would know through its own concepts, but it is guided better and further in this knowledge. And to say nothing of the further possibility that they may make possible a transition from the concepts of nature to the practical concepts, and may in this way give the moral ideas themselves support and connection with the speculative modes of knowledge of reason. Explanation of all this must be looked for in what follows.",["- 0542: first sentence's long relative clause (\"dessen Verlust, oder ... sein schwankender Gebrauch...\") split into several sentences; \"Weltweisheit\" rendered \"philosophy\"; \"Beurtheilungen\" as \"judgments\".","- 0542: \"Rothwendigkeit\" is an OCR slip for Nothwendigkeit, silently corrected; \"dagegen der...\" supplied \"the concept of\" for clarity (the antecedent is the expression/concept of necessity in every relation).","- 0542: \"Dessen Gegentheil ... d. i. ...\" the \"d. i.\" clause restated as the converse thesis; paragraph breaks added for readability.","- 0544: \"Einbildungskraft\" = imagination; \"Verstandeseinheit\"/\"Vernunfteinheit\" rendered \"unity of understanding\"/\"unity of reason\". \"Jene\" and \"diese\" resolved as reason and its unity.","- 0545: \"verfehler würde\" read as \"verfehlet würde\" (OCR); \"bestimbaren\" as \"bestimmbaren\"; \"Erfahrungserkentniß\" rendered \"knowledge gained in experience\"; \"Subiecte nach\" rendered \"so far as its subject is concerned\" (as in Kemp Smith).","- 0545: \"Verstandes\" in \"practischen Gebrauch des Verstandes\" kept as in the text, though the sense is reason's practical use.","- 0546: the sentence \"Zu geschweigen: daß...\" is a fragment; rendered as a dependent clause without inventing a main verb. \"Canon\" kept in the German's Latin form."]],"krv-a-0547":["For our present purpose, however, we set the practical ideas aside, and consider reason only in its speculative employment, and, within that, still more narrowly, only in its transcendental employment. Here we must follow the same path as we took above in the deduction of the categories. That is to say, we must consider the logical form of the knowledge of reason, and see whether reason may not thereby also become a source of concepts, which enable us to regard objects in themselves as determined synthetically *a priori*, in relation to one or other of the functions of reason.",["- \"Erkentniß\" = knowledge; \"Vernunfterkentniß\" rendered \"knowledge of reason\".","- Final clause restated: \"Obiecte an sich selbst … anzusehen\" is made the purpose of the concepts (\"which enable us to regard…\"); the German construction is loose.","- \"Function\" kept for *Function*, as in Kemp Smith."]],"krv-a-0548":["Reason, considered as the faculty of a certain logical form of knowledge, is the faculty of inference, that is, of judging mediately (by the subsumption of the condition of a possible judgment under the condition of a given judgment). The given judgment is the universal rule (major premiss, *Maior*). The subsumption of the condition of another possible judgment under the condition of the rule is the minor premiss (*Minor*). The actual judgment, which asserts the rule in the subsumed case, is the conclusion (*Conclusio*). For the rule says something universally, under a certain condition. In a given instance the condition of the rule is found to be present. Accordingly that which held universally under that condition is also regarded as valid in the instance in question, which carries this condition with it.\n\nIt is easily seen that reason arrives at a piece of knowledge through acts of understanding which constitute a series of conditions. Suppose that I arrive at the proposition 'All bodies are alterable' only by beginning from a more remote piece of knowledge, in which the concept of body does not yet occur, though it contains the condition of that concept, namely, 'Everything composite is alterable'. From this I proceed to a closer piece of knowledge, which stands under the condition of the former, 'Bodies are composite'; and only from this do I pass to a third, which now connects the remote knowledge ('alterable') with the one before me: 'Consequently bodies are alterable'. In that case I have arrived, through a series of conditions (premisses), at a piece of knowledge (the conclusion).\n\nNow any series whose exponent (of the categorical or hypothetical judgment) is given can be continued. The same act of reason therefore leads to the *ratiocinatio polysyllogistica*, which is a series of inferences that can be prolonged indefinitely, either on the side of the conditions (*per prosyllogismos*) or on the side of the conditioned (*per episyllogismos*).",["- Restated: the long sentence on \"alle Cörper sind veränderlich\" is divided into several sentences; the dash-like parenthetical structure is smoothed, and \"Suppose that\" supplied to carry the protasis.","- \"Erkentniß\" = knowledge; \"a piece of knowledge\" used where the singular count form is needed.","- \"Verstandeshandlungen\" = acts of understanding; \"Vernunfthandlung\" = act of reason.","- \"Exponent\" retained as Kant's term."]],"krv-a-0549":["We soon observe, however, that the chain or series of prosyllogisms, that is, of inferred knowledge on the side of the grounds or conditions of a given knowledge—in other words, the ascending series of syllogisms—must stand in a different relation to the faculty of reason from that of the descending series, that is, the advance of reason on the side of the conditioned through episyllogisms. For since in the former case the knowledge (*conclusio*) is given only as conditioned, reason can arrive at it by way of reason only on the presupposition that all the members of the series on the side of the conditions are given (totality in the series of premisses). Only on that presupposition is the judgment before us possible *a priori*. On the side of the conditioned, or of the inferences, on the other hand, we think only a series that is coming into being, not one already presupposed or given in its entirety; that is, only a potential advance.\n\nConsequently, when a piece of knowledge is regarded as conditioned, reason is constrained to regard the series of conditions in the ascending line as completed and as given in its totality. But if the same knowledge is at the same time regarded as the condition of other modes of knowledge, which together constitute a series of inferences in a descending line, reason can be quite indifferent how far this advance extends *a parte posteriori*, and whether a totality of this series is possible at all. For it has no need of such a series for the conclusion before it, since that conclusion is already sufficiently determined and secured through its grounds *a parte priori*.\n\nWhether, on the side of the conditions, the series of premisses has a first member, as a supreme condition, or not, and so is without limits *a parte priori*, it must still contain totality of the condition, even if we should never succeed in comprehending it; and the whole series must be unconditionally true if the conditioned, which is regarded as a consequence springing from it, is to be accepted as true. This is a demand of reason, which announces its knowledge as determined *a priori* and as necessary, either in itself, in which case it needs no grounds, or, if it is derived, as a member of a series of grounds which is itself unconditionally true.",["- Divided into three paragraphs of shorter sentences; \"mit andern Worten\" parenthesis retained between dashes.","- \"so kan man zu demselben vermittelst der Vernunft nicht anders gelangen\" rendered \"reason can arrive at it by way of reason\"; the subject \"man\" is awkward in English, and the phrase is slightly redundant in the German too. A smoother alternative: \"we can arrive at it by means of reason.\"","- \"gegen das Vernunftvermögen\" rendered \"to the faculty of reason\".","- \"Erkentniß\" in the plural = \"modes of knowledge\".","- \"und denn bedarf es keiner Gründe\" taken as \"in which case it needs no grounds\"."]],"krv-a-0550":["Third Section of the First Book of the Transcendental Dialectic. System of the Transcendental Ideas.",["- Heading; \"Buch\" = Book, \"Abschnitt\" = Section."]],"krv-a-0551":["We are not here concerned with a logical dialectic, which abstracts from all content of knowledge and merely exposes the false appearance in the form of syllogisms. We are concerned with a transcendental dialectic, which has to contain, completely *a priori*, the origin of certain modes of knowledge from pure reason, and of certain inferred concepts, the object of which cannot be given empirically, and which therefore lie wholly outside the faculty of the pure understanding.\n\nFrom the natural relation which the transcendental employment of our knowledge, in inferences as in judgments, must bear to the logical, we have inferred that there will be only three kinds of dialectical inference, corresponding to the three species of syllogism through which reason can arrive at knowledge from principles. We have also inferred that in all of them it is reason's business to ascend from the conditioned synthesis, to which the understanding remains always bound, to the unconditioned synthesis, which the understanding can never reach.",["- Divided the German sentence in two at \"Wir haben aus…\".","- \"geschlossener Begriffe\" = \"inferred concepts\" (Kemp Smith's usage); supplied \"certain\" and \"We are concerned with\" for the parallel construction.","- The clause \"welche … enthalten soll\" is awkward in German; rendered \"has to contain\".","- \"Erkentnisse\" = modes of knowledge."]],"krv-a-0552":["Now the most general relation that our representations can have is, first, the relation to the subject; secondly, the relation to objects, and this either as appearances or as objects of thought in general. If we combine this subdivision with the above division, all relation of representations, of which we can form either a concept or an idea, is threefold: (1) the relation to the subject; (2) the relation to the manifold of the object in appearance; (3) the relation to all things in general.",["- \"das allgemeine aller Beziehung\" rendered \"the most general relation\", i.e. the most general kind of relation; \"oberen\" = \"above division\" (the division into the three kinds of syllogism, previous passage).","- Numerals \"1) 2)\" and \"erstlich\" harmonised."]],"krv-a-0553":["Now all pure concepts in general have to do with the synthetic unity of representations; concepts of pure reason (transcendental ideas), however, have to do with the unconditioned synthetic unity of all conditions in general. Consequently all transcendental ideas can be arranged in three classes. The first contains the absolute (unconditioned) unity of the thinking subject; the second, the absolute unity of the series of conditions of appearance; the third, the absolute unity of the condition of all objects of thought in general.",["- Restated the final relative clause as parallel members with a semicolon, to avoid repeating \"davon … die\"."]],"krv-a-0554":["The thinking subject is the object of psychology; the sum of all appearances (the world) is the object of cosmology; and the thing which contains the supreme condition of the possibility of all that can be thought (the being of all beings) is the object of theology. Pure reason therefore supplies the idea for a transcendental doctrine of the soul (*psychologia rationalis*), for a transcendental science of the world (*cosmologia rationalis*), and finally for a transcendental knowledge of God (*Theologia transscendentalis*).\n\nThe mere outline of either of these sciences does not originate at all from the understanding, even if the understanding were combined with the highest logical employment of reason, that is, with all conceivable inferences, so as to advance from one object (appearance) to all the others, to the remotest members of the empirical synthesis. It is solely a pure and genuine product, or problem, of pure reason.",["- Divided after \"an die Hand\"; \"an die Hand giebt\" rendered \"supplies\".","- \"zu einer sowol als der andern\" rendered \"either of these sciences\" (strictly \"the one as well as the other\"; Kant says \"these sciences\" though three are named, and I follow the German).","- \"Inbegriff\" = \"sum\"; \"Wesen aller Wesen\" = \"the being of all beings\" (\"Wesen\" could also be \"essence\").","- \"um … fortzuschreiten\" rendered \"so as to advance\" as a clause attached to the logical employment."]],"krv-a-0555":["What modes (*modi*) of the pure concepts of reason are comprised under these three titles of all transcendental ideas will be fully set forth in the following chapter. The ideas follow the guiding-thread of the categories. For pure reason never relates directly to objects, but only to the concepts which the understanding forms of them. Similarly, it can be made clear only in the complete exposition [of the subject] how reason, solely through the synthetic employment of the very same function which it uses in the categorical syllogism, must necessarily arrive at the concept of the absolute unity of the thinking subject. It will likewise appear how the logical procedure in hypothetical syllogisms must necessarily lead to the idea of the absolutely unconditioned in a series of given conditions, and how the mere form of the disjunctive syllogism must necessarily bring with it the highest concept of reason, that of a being of all beings. This last thought seems at first sight to be extremely paradoxical.",["- Restated the long second sentence as three; \"[of the subject]\" is supplied for *Ausführung*.","- *Vernunftschluss* = syllogism (of reason); *modi* kept in Latin as in Kant.","- *Faden der Categorien* rendered \"guiding-thread of the categories\"."]],"krv-a-0556":["Of these transcendental ideas no objective deduction, such as we were able to give of the categories, is strictly possible. For in fact they have no relation to any object that could be given as congruent with them, and this precisely because they are only ideas. But a subjective derivation of them from the nature of our reason we could undertake, and this has been supplied in the present chapter.",["- *Hauptstück* = chapter. *Obiect* = object."]],"krv-a-0557":["It is easily seen that pure reason has no other aim than the absolute totality of synthesis on the side of the conditions (whether of inherence, of dependence, or of concurrence), and that it has nothing to do with absolute completeness on the side of the conditioned. For it is only the former that reason requires, in order to presuppose the whole series of conditions and so to give it a priori to the understanding. But once a condition that is completely (and unconditionally) given is there, a concept of reason is no longer needed in respect of the continuation of the series; for the understanding takes every step downwards, from condition to conditioned, of itself. In this way the transcendental ideas serve only for ascending in the series of conditions to the unconditioned, that is, to the principles. In respect of descent to the conditioned there is indeed a widely extended logical employment which our reason makes of the laws of the understanding, but no transcendental employment. If we form an idea of the absolute totality of such a synthesis (the *progressus*), for instance of the whole series of all future changes of the world, this is a thought-entity (*ens rationis*), which is only arbitrarily thought, and is not necessarily presupposed by reason. For the possibility of the conditioned presupposes the totality of its conditions, but not of its consequences. Such a concept is therefore not a transcendental idea, and it is with these alone that we are here concerned.",["- *iener* resolved as \"the former\" (the condition side). *Gedankending* = thought-entity.","- Split the sentence on the logical/transcendental employment into two."]],"krv-a-0558":["Finally, we also become aware that a certain connection and unity shines forth among the transcendental ideas themselves, and that by their means pure reason brings all its modes of knowledge into a system. To advance from knowledge of oneself (the soul) to knowledge of the world, and by way of this to knowledge of the Original Being, is so natural a progress that it appears similar to the logical advance of reason from the premisses to the conclusion. Whether a kinship of the kind that holds between the logical and the transcendental procedure does here actually underlie them, in concealment, is likewise one of the questions the answer to which must be awaited only in the course of these enquiries. For the present we have already attained our end. We have extricated from their equivocal position the transcendental concepts of reason, which in the theories of philosophers are usually mixed up with others, without even being properly distinguished from concepts of the understanding. We have been able to indicate their origin, and thereby at the same time their determinate number, beyond which there can be no more, and to represent them in a systematic connection. In this way a special field is marked out and delimited for pure reason.",["- Restated the final long sentence as several. *Erkentnisse* = modes of knowledge. *Urwesen* = Original Being.","- *seiner selbst* rendered \"oneself\"."]],"krv-a-0559":["Book II of the Transcendental Dialectic. The Dialectical Syllogisms of Pure Reason.",["- *Schlüsse* rendered \"syllogisms\", the sense of the heading."]],"krv-a-0560":["It may be said that the object of a mere transcendental idea is something of which we have no concept, although the idea has been quite necessarily generated in reason in accordance with its original laws. For of an object that is to be adequate to the demand of reason no concept of the understanding is in fact possible, that is, no concept which can be shown in a possible experience and made intuitable. It would, however, be better, and less open to misunderstanding, to say that we can have no knowledge of the object which corresponds to an idea, though we can have a problematic concept of it.",["- *Kentniß* = knowledge. *anschaulich machen* = made intuitable."]],"krv-a-0561":["Now the transcendental (subjective) reality of the pure concepts of reason rests at least on this: that we are brought to such ideas by a necessary inference of reason. There will therefore be syllogisms which contain no empirical premisses, and by means of which we advance from something that we know to something else of which we have no concept, and to which we yet, through an unavoidable illusion, ascribe objective reality. Such inferences are, in respect of their outcome, rather to be entitled sophistical [*vernünftelnde*] than rational [*Vernunftschlüsse*]; though, in view of their origin, they may well bear the latter name, since they are not fictitious, nor have they arisen by chance, but spring from the nature of reason. They are sophistications not of men but of pure reason itself. Even the wisest of all men cannot free himself from them. After much labour he may perhaps guard against the error, but he can never be rid of the illusion which ceaselessly torments and mocks him.",["- Brackets give the German where the play on words matters. *Schein* = illusion."]],"krv-a-0562":["There are, then, only three kinds of these dialectical syllogisms, as many as there are ideas in which their conclusions end. In the syllogism of the first class I infer, from the transcendental concept of the subject, which contains nothing manifold, the absolute unity of this subject itself, of which I in this way have no concept whatever. This dialectical inference I shall entitle the transcendental paralogism. The second class of sophistical inferences is directed to the transcendental concept of the absolute totality of the series of conditions for any given appearance whatsoever. From the fact that on one side I always have a self-contradictory concept of the unconditioned synthetic unity of the series, I infer the correctness of the opposite unity, of which nevertheless I likewise have no concept. The condition of reason in these dialectical inferences I shall entitle the antinomy of pure reason. Finally, in the third kind of sophistical inference, I infer from the totality of conditions under which objects in general, so far as they can be given to me, must be thought, the absolute synthetic unity of all conditions of the possibility of things in general. That is to say, from things which I do not know in terms of their mere transcendental concept I infer a being of all beings, which I know still less through a transcendent concept, and of whose unconditioned necessity I can form no concept. This dialectical syllogism I shall entitle the ideal of pure reason.",["- Divided the long sentences; the third is the most tangled and was smoothed. *Zustand* rendered \"condition [state]\" as \"condition\".","- Original has \"hade\" (misprint) for *habe*; silently corrected.","- *Richtigkeit* = correctness."]],"krv-a-0563":["Chapter I of the Second Book of the Transcendental Dialectic. The Paralogisms of Pure Reason.",["- *Hauptstück* = chapter."]],"krv-a-0564":["A logical paralogism consists in the falsity of a syllogism in respect of its form, whatever its content may be. A transcendental paralogism, however, has a transcendental ground for inferring falsely in respect of form. A fallacy of this kind will accordingly have its ground in the nature of human reason, and will carry with it an unavoidable, though not insoluble, illusion.",["- *Illusion* kept as \"illusion\", as for *Schein*."]],"krv-a-0565":["We now come to a concept which was not entered in the general list of transcendental concepts above, and which yet must be reckoned with them, without in the least altering that table or declaring it defective. This is the concept, or, if we prefer, the judgment, 'I think'. It is easily seen, however, that it is the vehicle of all concepts in general, and therefore of transcendental concepts also, and that it is always comprehended among these; consequently it is likewise transcendental, but can have no special title, since it serves only to introduce all thinking as belonging to consciousness. Nevertheless, however pure it may be from the empirical (the impression of the senses), it serves to distinguish two kinds of objects from the nature of our faculty of representation. I, as thinking, am an object of inner sense, and am entitled soul. That which is an object of the outer senses is entitled body. Accordingly the expression 'I', as a thinking being, already signifies the object of psychology. This science may be entitled the rational doctrine of the soul, if I seek to know of the soul nothing more than what, independently of all experience (which determines me more specifically and *in concreto*), can be inferred from this concept 'I', so far as it is present in all thinking.",["- Restated \"Man sieht aber leicht: daß…\" with semicolon; the long final sentence is divided in two, supplying \"This science\" for the relative \"welche\".","- \"heisse\"/\"heißt\" = \"am/is entitled\"; \"Vorstellungskraft\" = \"faculty of representation\".","- \"vor mangelhaft zu erklären\" (vor = für) rendered \"declaring it defective\"."]],"krv-a-0566":["The rational doctrine of the soul is in fact an undertaking of this kind. For if the least empirical element of my thinking, any particular perception of my inner state, were mixed in among the grounds of knowledge [*Erkenntnißgründe*] of this science, it would no longer be a rational, but an empirical doctrine of the soul. We have therefore before us an alleged science, built upon the single proposition 'I think', and we may quite fittingly, and in conformity with the nature of a transcendental philosophy, inquire into its ground or lack of ground. It should not give offence that in this proposition, which expresses the perception of myself, I have an inner experience, and that the rational doctrine of the soul which is erected upon it is therefore never pure, but is founded in part upon an empirical principle. For this inner perception is nothing more than the bare apperception 'I think', which makes possible even all transcendental concepts, in which we say, 'I think substance, cause', etc. Inner experience in general and its possibility, or perception in general and its relation to other perception, when no particular distinction or determination of it is empirically given, cannot be regarded as empirical knowledge, but must be regarded as knowledge of the empirical in general; and it belongs to the investigation of the possibility of every experience, which investigation is certainly transcendental. The least object of perception (for instance, mere pleasure or displeasure), added to the general representation of self-consciousness, would at once transform rational psychology into an empirical psychology.",["- Divided the sentence beginning \"Denn innere Erfahrung…\" only by supplying \"which investigation\" for \"welche\", whose antecedent I take to be *Untersuchung*, not *Erfahrung*.","- \"ohne das\" read as \"ohne daß\".","- \"Unterfangen\" = \"undertaking\"; \"Erkentniß des Empirischen überhaupt\" = \"knowledge of the empirical in general\"."]],"krv-a-0567":["'I think' is thus the sole text of rational psychology, out of which it is to unfold all its wisdom. It is easily seen that this thought, if it is to be referred to an object (myself), can contain nothing but transcendental predicates of it; for the least empirical predicate would spoil the rational purity of the science and its independence of all experience.",["- \"auswickeln\" rendered \"unfold\"."]],"krv-a-0568":["Here, however, we have simply to follow the guidance of the categories. Since, however, a thing, 'I', as a thinking being, is here first given, we shall indeed not alter the order of the categories among themselves, as it is presented in their table; yet we shall begin here with the category of substance, through which a thing in itself is represented, and so proceed backwards through their series. The topic of the rational doctrine of the soul, from which everything else that it may contain must be derived, is accordingly as follows:",["- \"Leitfaden\" = \"guidance\" (clue); \"Topik\" = \"topic\". The clause \"nur, da…\" is smoothed with \"Since, however\"."]],"krv-a-0569":["1. The soul is substance.",[]],"krv-a-0570":["2. In its quality, simple.",[]],"krv-a-0571":["3. In respect of the different times in which it exists, numerically identical, that is, unity (not plurality).",[]],"krv-a-0572":["4. In relation to possible objects in space.*)",["- The footnote marker is kept as in the text."]],"krv-a-0573":["*) The reader, who will not so easily guess from these expressions, in their transcendental abstractness, their psychological meaning, nor why the last attribute of the soul belongs to the category of existence, will find them sufficiently explained and justified in what follows. Further, as regards the Latin expressions which, contrary to the taste of good writing, have crept in in place of the German terms of the same meaning, both in this section and throughout the whole work, I have to offer in excuse that I have preferred to sacrifice something of the elegance of the language rather than to make the usage of the schools difficult by the least unintelligibility.",["- \"Zierlichkeit der Sprache\" = \"elegance of the language\"; \"Schulgebrauch\" = \"usage of the schools\"."]],"krv-a-0574":["From these elements all the concepts of pure psychology arise, solely by combination, without recognising in the least any other principle. This substance, merely as object of inner sense, yields the concept of immateriality; as simple substance, that of incorruptibility; the identity of the same, as intellectual substance, yields personality; all these three together yield spirituality. The relation to objects in space yields commerce with bodies; accordingly it represents the thinking substance as the principle of life in matter, that is, it represents it as soul (*anima*) and as the ground of animality; this, limited by spirituality, yields immortality.",["- Restated the run-on sentence with semicolons; the clause \"mithin stellet sie…\" kept with \"it\" = the thinking substance.","- \"Principium\", \"Commercium\", \"Incorruptibilität\" kept near the Latin."]],"krv-a-0575":["To this there relate four paralogisms of a transcendental doctrine of the soul, which is falsely held to be a science of pure reason concerning the nature of our thinking being. We can, however, lay at its foundation nothing but the simple representation 'I', which in itself is wholly empty of content, and of which we cannot even say that it is a concept; it is a bare consciousness which accompanies all concepts. Through this 'I', or 'he', or 'it' (the thing) which thinks, nothing further is represented than a transcendental subject of the thoughts = X. This subject is known only through the thoughts which are its predicates, and apart from them we can never have the least concept of it. We therefore turn around it in a perpetual circle, since we must always already make use of its representation in order to judge anything about it. This inconvenience is inseparable from it, because consciousness in itself is not so much a representation distinguishing a particular object as a form of representation in general, so far as it is to be entitled knowledge; for of that alone can I say that through it I think anything.",["- \"können wird\" read as \"können wir\". Divided the long sentence after \"= X\" and after \"Cirkel\".","- \"Er, oder Es (das Ding)\" kept; \"Bewustseyn … Erkentniß genant werden\" rendered \"entitled knowledge\"."]],"krv-a-0576":["At the outset, however, it must seem strange that the condition under which I think at all, and which is therefore merely a property of my subject, should at the same time be valid for everything that thinks; and that we can presume to found an apodeictic and universal judgment upon a proposition which seems empirical, namely, that everything that thinks is constituted as the pronouncement of self-consciousness declares me to be. The reason of this, however, lies in the fact that we must necessarily ascribe *a priori* to things all the properties which constitute the conditions under which alone we think them. Now I can have the least representation of a thinking being not through any outer experience, but solely through self-consciousness. Such objects are therefore nothing more than the transference of this consciousness of mine to other things, which are only thereby represented as thinking beings. The proposition 'I think' is, however, here taken only problematically; not in so far as it may contain a perception of an existence (the Cartesian *cogito, ergo sum*), but in respect of its mere possibility, in order to see what properties may flow from this so simple a proposition to its subject (whether such a subject exist or not).",["- \"Satz\" rendered \"proposition\"; \"Ausspruch des Selbstbewustseyns\" = \"pronouncement of self-consciousness\".","- \"seiner bloßen Möglichkeit nach\" = \"in respect of its mere possibility\"; \"problematisch\" kept as \"problematically\"."]],"krv-a-0577":["If our pure rational knowledge of thinking beings in general rested on anything more than the *cogito*, and if we also called in aid observations upon the play of our thoughts and the laws of nature of the thinking self to be derived from them, an empirical psychology would result. This would be a kind of physiology of inner sense, and might perhaps serve to explain its appearances. It could never serve, however, to disclose such properties as do not belong to possible experience at all (for instance, that of simplicity), nor to teach anything apodeictically about thinking beings in general that concerns their nature. It would therefore not be a rational psychology.",["- Restated the long conditional as three sentences after the first; the German is one sentence with a colon.","- \"Erkentniß\" (sic) = knowledge; \"Erscheinungen desselben\" = \"its appearances\", referring to inner sense (not the physiology). Reading of \"desselben\" is by judgment.","- \"apodictisch\" rendered \"apodeictically\", Kemp Smith's spelling.","- \"des Einfachen\" rendered \"simplicity\" for sense; literally \"that of the simple\"."]],"krv-a-0578":["Now the proposition \"I think\" (taken problematically) contains the form of every judgment of understanding in general, and accompanies all the categories as their vehicle. It is therefore clear that the inferences drawn from it can contain only a purely transcendental employment of the understanding, which excludes every admixture of experience. Of the progress of such an employment we can, in view of what we have already shown, form in advance no favourable conception. Let us then follow it with a critical eye through all the predicaments of pure psychology.",["- Divided the second sentence's relative clauses (\"welcher … ausschlägt, und an dessen Fortgang …\") into two sentences.","- \"Verstandesurtheil\" = judgment of understanding; \"Prädicamente\" kept as \"predicaments\"; \"Seelenlehre\" = psychology (doctrine of the soul).","- \"ausschlägt\" = rejects/excludes."]],"krv-a-0579":["First Paralogism: of Substantiality",["- Heading; \"Simplicität\" etc. follow in later headings."]],"krv-a-0580":["That, the representation of which is the absolute subject of our judgments and cannot therefore be employed as the determination of another thing, is substance.",["- \"Dasjenige, dessen\" rendered with a plain relative construction; \"Bestimmung\" = determination."]],"krv-a-0581":["I, as a thinking being, am the absolute subject of all my possible judgments, and this representation of myself cannot be employed as the predicate of any other thing.",["- None beyond the standard terms."]],"krv-a-0582":["Therefore I, as a thinking being (soul), am substance.",["- None."]],"krv-a-0583":["Criticism of the First Paralogism of Pure Psychology",["- \"Critik\" rendered \"Criticism\" in a heading, as the context suits."]],"krv-a-0584":["We have shown in the analytic part of transcendental logic that pure categories (and among them that of substance) have in themselves no objective meaning, unless an intuition is subjoined to them, to the manifold of which they can be applied as functions of synthetic unity. Apart from this they are merely functions of a judgment without content. Of any and every thing I can say that it is substance, in so far as I distinguish it from mere predicates and determinations of things. Now in all our thinking the \"I\" is the subject in which thoughts inhere only as determinations, and this \"I\" cannot be employed as the determination of another thing. Everyone must therefore necessarily regard himself as the substance, and thinking only as accidents of his existence and determinations of his state.",["- \"untergelegt\" = subjoined/laid beneath. \"inhäriren\" = inhere.","- \"Sich selbst\" capitalised in the original; not reproduced.","- \"Accidenzen\" = accidents."]],"krv-a-0585":["But what use am I now to make of this concept of a substance? That I, as a thinking being, persist for myself, and naturally neither arise nor perish, I can by no means infer from it. Yet it is for this purpose alone that the concept of the substantiality of my thinking subject could be of service to me; without it I could very well dispense with the concept.",["- Divided the final clause with a semicolon for clarity; \"ohne welches\" refers to this purpose, not literally to the concept, but the sense is \"were it not for this\".","- \"vor mich selbst\" = \"for myself\" (i.e. as an independent entity); \"vor\" for \"für\"."]],"krv-a-0586":["So far is it from being possible to infer these properties from the mere pure category of substance, that we must on the contrary take as basis the persistence of a given object from experience, if we are to apply to it the empirically usable concept of a substance. In our proposition, however, we have taken no experience as basis, but have inferred solely from the concept of the relation which all thinking has to the \"I\", as the common subject in which it inheres. Nor, even if we were to set about it, could we establish such persistence by any secure observation. For the \"I\" is indeed in all thoughts; but no intuition whatever is bound up with this representation which would distinguish it from other objects of intuition. We can therefore perceive that this representation recurs in all thinking, but not that it is an abiding and permanent intuition in which the thoughts, as changing, would alternate.",["- \"Beharrlichkeit\" = persistence; \"empirischbrauchbaren\" = \"empirically usable\".","- \"wenn wir es gleich darauf anlegten\" rendered \"even if we were to set about it\".","- \"Wahrnehmen\" = perceive; \"wandelbar\" = changing."]],"krv-a-0587":["It follows that the first syllogism of transcendental psychology foists upon us a merely supposed new insight. It passes off the constant logical subject of thinking as knowledge of the real subject of inhesion, of which we have not, and cannot have, the slightest knowledge. For consciousness is that which alone makes all representations into thoughts, and in it, as in the transcendental subject, all our perceptions must be met with; and apart from this logical meaning of the \"I\" we have no knowledge of the subject in itself which as substratum underlies this \"I\", as it underlies all thoughts. Meanwhile the proposition \"The soul is substance\" may very well be allowed to stand, provided we recognise that this concept does not carry us in the least farther, and cannot teach any of the usual inferences of the sophistical psychology, such as, for instance, the soul's everlasting continuance through all changes and even the death of man. It therefore signifies only a substance in idea, not in reality.",["- Restated the long sentence as several; \"Vernunftschluß\" = syllogism; \"vernünftelnden Seelenlehre\" = sophistical psychology.","- \"unser dieser Begriff\" is irregular; read as \"dieser Begriff ... uns\" and smoothed. \"daß er also\" resolved to refer to the concept.","- \"aufheften\" = foist."]],"krv-a-0588":["Second Paralogism: of Simplicity",["- None."]],"krv-a-0589":["That thing the action of which can never be regarded as the concurrence of several acting things is simple.",["- None."]],"krv-a-0590":["Now the soul, or the thinking \"I\", is such a thing. Therefore, etc.",["- None."]],"krv-a-0591":["Criticism of the Second Paralogism of Transcendental Psychology",["- None."]],"krv-a-0592":["This is the Achilles of all the dialectical inferences of the pure doctrine of the soul. It is no mere sophistical game contrived by a dogmatist to give his assertions a passing appearance of truth, but an inference which seems able to withstand even the sharpest scrutiny and the greatest scruple in investigation. Here it is.",["- Divided after \"Seelenlehre\"; \"Bedenklichkeit des Nachforschens\" = \"scruple in investigation\"."]],"krv-a-0593":["Every composite substance is an aggregate of many, and the action of a composite, or that which inheres in it as such, is an aggregate of many actions or accidents distributed among the multitude of substances. Now an effect which arises from the concurrence of many acting substances is indeed possible, if this effect is merely external (as, for instance, the motion of a body is the combined motion of all its parts). But with thoughts, as accidents belonging internally to a thinking being, the case is different. For suppose that the composite were to think: then every part of it would contain a part of the thought, and only all together would contain the whole thought. But this is contradictory. For since representations that are distributed among different beings (as, for instance, the single words of a verse) never make up a whole thought (a verse), the thought cannot inhere in a composite as such. It is therefore possible only in a substance which is not an aggregate of many, and is consequently absolutely simple.*",["- \"Setzet\" rendered as \"suppose\"; \"Nun ist dieses aber widersprechend\" = \"But this is contradictory\".","- Footnote marker retained as *; footnote follows in the next passage."]],"krv-a-0594":["* It would be very easy to give this proof the usual scholastic precision of presentation. For my purpose, however, it is sufficient to set the bare ground of proof before the eyes, if need be in popular fashion.",["- \"schulgerechte Abgemessenheit der Einkleidung\" rendered \"scholastic precision of presentation\"."]],"krv-a-0595":["The so-called *nervus probandi* of this argument lies in the proposition that many representations must be contained in the absolute unity of the thinking subject if they are to constitute a single thought. But no one can prove this proposition from concepts. For how would he set about doing so? The proposition, 'A thought can only be the effect of the absolute unity of the thinking being', cannot be treated as analytic. The unity of a thought which consists of many representations is collective, and, so far as mere concepts go, may just as well relate to the collective unity of the substances cooperating in it (as the motion of a body is the composite motion of all its parts) as to the absolute unity of the subject. By the rule of identity, therefore, the necessity of presupposing a simple substance in the case of a composite thought cannot be seen. That the same proposition should be known as synthetic and completely *a priori* from mere concepts is something no one will venture to defend who understands the ground of the possibility of synthetic *a priori* propositions, as we have set it forth above.",[]],"krv-a-0596":["Nor is it possible to derive this necessary unity of the subject, as the condition of the possibility of every thought, from experience. For experience yields no knowledge of necessity; and, still more, the concept of absolute unity lies far beyond its sphere. Whence, then, do we derive this proposition upon which the whole psychological syllogism of reason rests?",[]],"krv-a-0597":["It is evident that when we wish to represent to ourselves a thinking being, we must put ourselves in its place, and so must substitute our own subject for the object which we desired to consider. (This is not the case in any other kind of investigation.) It is evident also that we demand absolute unity of the subject for a thought only because otherwise it could not be said, 'I think' (the manifold in one representation). For although the whole of the thought could be divided and distributed among many subjects, the subjective 'I' cannot be divided and distributed; and it is this that we presuppose in all thinking.",[]],"krv-a-0598":["Here, then, as in the preceding paralogism, the formal proposition of apperception, 'I think', remains the whole ground upon which rational psychology ventures to extend its knowledge. This proposition is, to be sure, not an experience, but the form of apperception, which attaches to every experience and precedes it. It must, however, always be regarded only in relation to a possible knowledge in general, as a merely subjective condition of that knowledge. We wrongly convert it into a condition of the possibility of a knowledge of objects, namely, into a concept of a thinking being in general; for we cannot represent this to ourselves without putting ourselves, with the formula of our consciousness, in the place of every other intelligent being.",[]],"krv-a-0599":["But the simplicity of myself (as soul) is not in fact inferred from the proposition 'I think'; the former is already involved in every thought itself. The proposition 'I am simple' must be regarded as an immediate expression of apperception, just as the supposed Cartesian inference, *cogito, ergo sum*, is in fact tautological, since the *cogito* (*sum cogitans*) immediately asserts actuality. 'I am simple' means no more than that this representation, 'I', contains not the least manifold within itself, and that it is absolute (though merely logical) unity.",[]],"krv-a-0600":["The much-vaunted psychological proof is thus based solely on the indivisible unity of a representation which governs the verb only in respect of a person. It is evident that the subject of inherence is designated only transcendentally by the 'I' attached to thought, without our noting in it the least property, or knowing or knowing anything whatever about it. [Sic: read \"or in general knowing anything about it\".] It signifies a something in general (transcendental subject), the representation of which must indeed be simple, for the very reason that nothing is determined in it; nothing, assuredly, can be represented more simply than through the concept of a mere something. But the simplicity of the representation of a subject is not therefore a knowledge of the simplicity of the subject itself; for abstraction is made altogether from its properties when it is designated merely by the expression 'I', which is entirely empty of content and which I can apply to every thinking subject.",[]],"krv-a-0601":["This much is certain, that through the 'I' I always think an absolute but logical unity of the subject (simplicity), but that I do not thereby know the actual simplicity of my subject. The proposition 'I am substance' signified nothing but the pure category, of which I can make no (empirical) use *in concreto*. In the same way I am entitled to say, 'I am a simple substance', that is, a substance the representation of which never contains a synthesis of the manifold. But this concept, or this proposition, teaches us nothing whatever in regard to myself as an object of experience, because the concept of substance is itself employed only as a function of synthesis, without a substrate intuition, and so without an object; it holds only of the condition of our knowledge, not of any object that can be specified. Let us make a trial of the supposed usefulness of this proposition.",[]],"krv-a-0602":["Everyone must admit that the assertion of the simple nature of the soul is of value only in so far as it enables me to distinguish this subject from all matter, and so to exempt it from the perishableness to which matter is always subject. The above proposition is, indeed, quite specifically designed for this use, and is therefore usually expressed in the form, 'The soul is not corporeal'. Now suppose I can show that, even if we concede objective validity to this cardinal proposition of rational psychology in the pure meaning of a mere judgment of reason (from pure categories)—'everything that thinks is simple substance'—yet not the least use can be made of it in respect of the heterogeneity or kinship of the soul with matter. This will be the same as if I had relegated this supposed psychological insight to the field of mere ideas, which lack reality of objective employment.",[]],"krv-a-0603":["We have proved indisputably in the Transcendental Aesthetic that bodies are mere appearances of our outer sense, and not things in themselves. In accordance with this we can rightly say that our thinking subject is not corporeal; that is to say, since it is represented by us as object of inner sense, it cannot, in so far as it thinks, be an object of outer senses, that is, an appearance in space. This amounts to saying that thinking beings, as such, can never present themselves to us among outer appearances; in other words, we cannot intuit their thoughts, their consciousness, their desires, and so forth, outwardly; for all this belongs to inner sense. In fact this argument seems to be the natural and popular one, on which even the commonest understanding has always seemed to light, and by which it has, from very early times, come to regard souls as beings entirely distinct from bodies.",["- krv-a-0595: *nervus probandi* kept in Latin; \"Erkentnis\"-type spellings silently modernised. Final sentence kept as one; \"defend\" renders \"zu verantworten getrauen\".","- krv-a-0596: \"Nothwendigkeit zu erkennen\" rendered \"knowledge of necessity\"; \"geschweige, daß\" restated with a semicolon and \"still more\".","- krv-a-0597: Parenthesis about other investigations kept in brackets-as-parentheses; second \"It is evident\" supplied to divide the sentence.","- krv-a-0598: Long sentence divided into four; \"Erkentniß\" = knowledge, plural-less. \"Formel\" rendered \"formula\".","- krv-a-0600: I wrote a faulty duplication (\"knowing or knowing anything\"); the German reads \"überhaupt etwas von ihm zu kennen, oder zu wissen\", i.e. \"or to know or be acquainted with anything whatever about it\". Please read the clause so, disregarding my bracketed \"[Sic]\" remark.","- krv-a-0601: \"ist es mir auch erlaubt\" rendered \"I am entitled\"; \"ohne Obiect\" kept; \"Function der Synthesis\" retained.","- krv-a-0602: The \"Wenn ich nun zeigen kan … so wird\" construction is irregular; I restated the protasis as \"Now suppose I can show…\" and left the dash-bracketed clause as in the German. \"Ungleichartigkeit\" = heterogeneity.","- krv-a-0603: \"Erscheinung\" = appearance; \"innerer/äusserer Sinn\" = inner/outer sense; \"Verstand\" = understanding."]],"krv-a-0604":["Now even if extension, impenetrability, cohesion and motion, in short everything that the outer senses can supply to us, neither are nor contain thought, feeling, inclination or resolution (these being nowhere objects of outer intuition), yet that something which underlies outer appearances, which so affects our sense that it acquires the representations of space, matter, shape, and so forth, might, when considered as noumenon (or better, as transcendental object), also be at the same time the subject of thoughts. This might be so, although the way in which our outer sense is thereby affected yields us no intuition of representations, will, and the like, but only of space and its determinations. But this something is not extended, not impenetrable, not composite, since all these predicates concern only sensibility and its intuition, so far as we are affected by objects of this kind (otherwise unknown to us). These expressions, however, do not allow us to know at all what kind of object it is. They show only that, since it is an object considered in itself without relation to outer senses, these predicates of outer appearances cannot be ascribed to it. The predicates of inner sense, however, representations and thinking, are not contradictory to it. Hence even on the admitted simplicity of nature the human soul, if (as we ought) we consider it merely as appearance, is not sufficiently distinguished from matter in respect of the substratum of the latter.",["- The first sentence is long and irregular (the concessive \"Ob... gleich\" clause is never answered by a plain \"so\"); I restated it in two sentences and moved the parenthesis on \"Gegenstände äusserer Anschauung\" into a short parenthetical clause.","- \"wiewol\" clause rendered as a separate sentence (\"This might be so, although...\").","- \"Substrati derselben\": \"derselben\" refers to matter, hence \"of the latter\".","- \"Anschauung von Vorstellungen\": kept literally as \"intuition of representations\"."]],"krv-a-0605":["Were matter a thing in itself, it would, as a composite being, be wholly different from the soul as a simple being. But matter is merely outer appearance, whose substratum is not known through any predicates that can be assigned. I can therefore quite well assume of this substratum that it is in itself simple, although in the way in which it affects our senses it produces in us the intuition of the extended, and so of the composite. I can also assume that thoughts inhere in itself in the substance to which, in respect of our outer sense, extension belongs, and that these thoughts can be represented with consciousness through its own inner sense. In this way the very same thing which in one relation is entitled corporeal would in another relation be at the same time a thinking being. Its thoughts we could not indeed intuit, but we could intuit the signs of them in the appearance. The expression that only souls (as particular kinds of substances) think would then fall away; we should rather say, as is usual, that men think, that is, that the very same thing which, as outer appearance, is extended is inwardly (in itself) a subject that is not composite but simple, and thinks.",["- The clause \"und daß also der Substanz... Gedanken beywohnen\" is rendered as a separate sentence continuing \"I can also assume\"; the German construction is irregular.","- \"sich ganz und gar unterscheiden\" rendered \"wholly different\".","- \"heißt\" rendered \"is entitled\"."]],"krv-a-0606":["But, without permitting hypotheses of this kind, we may remark in general that if by soul I understand a thinking being in itself, the question whether it is or is not of the same kind as matter (which is not a thing in itself at all, but only a species of representations in us) is already in itself unsuitable. For it is self-evident that a thing in itself is of a different nature from the determinations which merely constitute its state.",["- Split at the semicolon-like \"denn\" into two sentences."]],"krv-a-0607":["But if we compare the thinking I not with matter but with the intelligible which underlies the outer appearance we call matter, then, since we know nothing whatever of the latter, we cannot say either that the soul differs from it inwardly in any respect.",["- \"Intelligibelen\" rendered \"the intelligible\"; \"innerlich\" taken as \"inwardly\"."]],"krv-a-0608":["Simple consciousness is therefore no knowledge of the simple nature of our subject, in so far as the latter is thereby to be distinguished from matter as a composite being.",["- \"Kentniß\" rendered \"knowledge\"."]],"krv-a-0609":["But if this concept is not fitted to determine, in the one case in which it is serviceable, namely in the comparison of myself with objects of outer experience, what is peculiar and distinctive in my nature, we may go on professing to know that the thinking I, the soul (a name for the transcendental object of inner sense), is simple. This expression has none the less no application extending to real objects, and therefore cannot in the least extend our knowledge.",["- \"dazu nicht taugt, ihn... zu bestimmen\": the construction is irregular (the \"ihn\" is redundant); smoothed.","- \"Begriff\" = the concept of simplicity, supplied from context."]],"krv-a-0610":["Thus the whole of rational psychology falls with its chief support. Here, as elsewhere, we can as little hope to extend insight through mere concepts (still less through the mere subjective form of all our concepts, consciousness) without relation to possible experience. This is the more so because the fundamental concept of a simple nature is of such a kind that it can never be met with in any experience, and there is therefore no way of arriving at it as an objectively valid concept.",["- Restated as three sentences; \"zumalen, da\" rendered \"This is the more so because\"."]],"krv-a-0611":["Third Paralogism: of Personality.",["- \"Paralogism\" kept in Kemp Smith's form."]],"krv-a-0612":["What is conscious of the numerical identity of itself at different times is, so far, a person.",[]],"krv-a-0613":["Now the soul, etc.",["- \"etc.\" in the original; the minor premiss is abbreviated."]],"krv-a-0614":["Therefore it is a person.",[]],"krv-a-0615":["Criticism of the Third Paralogism of Transcendental Psychology.",["- \"Critik\" rendered \"Criticism\", as the heading suggests."]],"krv-a-0616":["If I wish to know through experience the numerical identity of an outer object, I shall attend to what is permanent in that appearance to which, as subject, everything else relates as determination, and I shall note the identity of the former throughout the time in which the latter changes. But I am an object of inner sense, and all time is merely the form of inner sense. Consequently I relate each and all of my successive determinations to the numerically identical self, in all time, that is, in the form of the inner intuition of myself. On this footing the personality of the soul would have to be regarded not even as inferred, but as a completely identical proposition of self-consciousness in time; and that is also the reason why it holds a priori. For it really says no more than this: in the whole time in which I am conscious of myself, I am conscious of this time as belonging to the unity of my self; and it is all one whether I say that this whole time is in me as individual unity, or that I am to be found, with numerical identity, in all this time.",["- \"Auf diesen Fuß\" rendered \"On this footing\".","- \"Mir\" and \"meiner\" are left as ordinary pronouns."]],"krv-a-0617":["The identity of the person is therefore inevitably to be met with in my own consciousness. But if I consider myself from the standpoint of another (as object of his outer intuition), this outer observer first of all considers me in time, for in apperception time is, strictly speaking, represented only in me. Although he admits the I which accompanies all representations at all times in my consciousness, and does so with complete identity, he will not yet infer from it the objective permanence of my self. For the time in which the observer places me is not the time found in my own sensibility but that found in his; and the identity which is necessarily bound up with my consciousness is not therefore bound up with his, that is, with the outer intuition of my subject.",["- The sentence beginning \"Er wird also\" is reordered so the concessive \"ob er es gleich einräumt\" comes first; \"es\" = the I.","- \"Denn da alsdenn\" restated as a plain \"For\"."]],"krv-a-0618":["The identity of the consciousness of myself at different times is therefore only a formal condition of my thoughts and of their coherence. It does not at all prove the numerical identity of my subject. In this subject, notwithstanding the logical identity of the I, there may have occurred such a change as does not allow its identity to be retained; and yet we may still ascribe to it the I of the same sound, which in every other state, even in a transformation of the subject, might still preserve the thoughts of the preceding subject and so also hand them on to the subsequent.*)",["- Divided into three sentences. \"gleichlautende Ich\" rendered \"the I of the same sound\"; \"obzwar\" clause is irregular and loosely linked; I rendered it as \"and yet\".","- The footnote marker *) is carried over; the footnote text itself is not in the passage.","- \"Zusammenhanges\" rendered \"coherence\"."]],"krv-a-0619":["*) An elastic ball which impinges in a straight line on another of the same kind communicates to it its whole motion, and therefore its whole state (if we take account only of positions in space). Now suppose, by analogy with such bodies, substances of which the one infuses into the other representations together with the consciousness of them. We can then conceive a whole series of such substances. The first would communicate its state, together with the consciousness of it, to the second; the second would communicate its own state, together with that of the preceding substance, to the third; and the third would in the same fashion communicate the states of all the preceding substances, together with its own and the consciousness of them. The last substance would thus be conscious of all the states of the substances that had changed before it as being its own states, since these states, together with their consciousness, would have been transferred to it. And yet it would not have been one and the same person in all these states.",[]],"krv-a-0620":["The proposition of certain ancient schools, that everything in the world is in flux and nothing abiding and permanent, cannot hold once we admit substances; but it is not refuted by the unity of self-consciousness. For we ourselves cannot judge from our consciousness whether, as soul, we are abiding or not. We reckon as belonging to our identical self only what we are conscious of, and so we must necessarily judge that we are one and the same throughout the whole time of which we are conscious. From the standpoint of another, however, we cannot yet declare this judgment to be valid. In the soul we encounter no abiding appearance save only the representation 'I', which accompanies and connects them all. We can therefore never determine whether this I (a mere thought) is not in flux, just as much as the other thoughts which are linked together by its means.",[]],"krv-a-0621":["It is remarkable, however, that personality, and its presupposition, permanence, and therefore the substantiality of the soul, have now first to be proved. For if we could presuppose permanence, there would indeed not follow from it the continuance of consciousness, but yet the possibility of a continuing consciousness in an abiding subject. This is already sufficient for personality, which does not itself cease at once because its activity is perhaps interrupted for a time. But this permanence is given to us in nothing prior to the numerical identity of our self, which we infer from the identical apperception. It is, on the contrary, first inferred from that identity. (And if matters proceeded as they should, the concept of substance, which alone is empirically serviceable, would have to follow upon this identity first of all.) Since, then, this identity of the person does not at all follow from the identity of the I in the consciousness of all the time in which I know myself, the substantiality of the soul could not be founded upon it above.",[]],"krv-a-0622":["Meanwhile, like the concept of substance and of the simple, the concept of personality may also remain, so far as it is merely transcendental, that is, so far as it signifies unity of the subject, otherwise unknown to us, in whose determinations there is thoroughgoing connection through apperception. So far this concept is also necessary and sufficient for practical use. But we can never rely upon it as an extension of our self-knowledge through pure reason, which holds out to us an uninterrupted continuance of the subject from the mere concept of the identical self. This concept always turns about itself in a circle, and brings us no farther in respect of any question aimed at synthetic knowledge. What matter may be as a thing in itself (transcendental object) is indeed wholly unknown to us; yet its permanence as appearance can be observed, since it is represented as something external. But when I want to observe the mere I amid the change of all representations, I have no other correlate for my comparisons than again myself, together with the universal conditions of my consciousness. I can therefore give only tautological answers to all questions. I substitute my concept and its unity for the properties which belong to me as object, and so presuppose what was demanded to be known.",[]],"krv-a-0623":["The Fourth Paralogism: of Ideality (of the external relation).",[]],"krv-a-0624":["That, the existence of which can be inferred only as the cause of given perceptions, has a merely doubtful existence.",[]],"krv-a-0625":["Now all outer appearances are of such a kind that their existence is not immediately perceived, and can be inferred only as the cause of given perceptions.",[]],"krv-a-0626":["Therefore the existence of all objects of the outer senses is doubtful. This uncertainty I entitle the ideality of outer appearances, and the doctrine of this ideality is entitled idealism. In comparison with it, the assertion of a possible certainty regarding objects of outer sense is entitled dualism.",[]],"krv-a-0627":["Criticism of the Fourth Paralogism of Transcendental Psychology.",[]],"krv-a-0628":["We shall first subject the premisses to examination. We may rightly maintain that only what is in ourselves can be immediately perceived, and that my own existence alone can be the object of a mere perception. The existence of an actual object outside me (if this word be taken in its intellectual meaning) is therefore never given directly in perception. It can only be added in thought to the perception, which is a modification of inner sense, as the external cause of that perception, and so be inferred. Hence Descartes was justified in limiting all perception, in the strictest sense, to the proposition: I (as a thinking being) am. For it is clear that since what is outer is not in me, I cannot find it in my apperception, and therefore in no perception, which is properly only the determination of apperception.",[]],"krv-a-0629":["I cannot, therefore, properly perceive outer things, but can only infer their existence from my inner perception, regarding this as the effect of which something outer is the proximate cause. But the inference from a given effect to a determinate cause is always uncertain, since the effect may have arisen from more than one cause. Accordingly, in the relation of the perception to its cause it always remains doubtful whether the cause is inner or outer; whether, that is, all so-called outer perceptions are not a mere play of our inner sense, or whether they are related to actual outer objects as their cause. At least the existence of the latter is only inferred, and runs the risk of all inferences. The object of inner sense, on the other hand (myself with all my representations), is immediately perceived, and its existence admits of no doubt.",[]],"krv-a-0630":["By an idealist we must therefore understand, not one who denies the existence of external objects of the senses, but one who merely does not admit that their existence is known through immediate perception. He concludes from this that through no possible experience can we ever become completely certain of their reality.",["- 0619: Kant's footnote (marked *), kept as such; the second sentence is divided and the series of three substances set out in separate clauses. \"Nehmet ... an\" is rendered \"suppose\".","- 0620: \"Standpuncte eines Fremden\" is rendered \"standpoint of another\"; the long final sentence is split. \"Vor gültig erklären\" is taken as \"declare valid\".","- 0621: In \"diese Beharrlichkeit ist uns vor der numerischen Identität ... gegeben\", *vor* is read as \"prior to\" (not \"for\"). The parenthesis is kept but restated. \"Oben\" refers to the first paralogism.","- 0622: Long opening sentence divided at \"aber\"; \"Staat machen\" rendered \"rely upon\"; \"Correlatum\" kept as \"correlate\"; \"tavtologische\" as \"tautological\".","- 0624–0626: Premiss and conclusion are kept as separate lines. \"Idealism\" and \"dualism\" are given without italics, since the text sets them as ordinary nouns.","- 0628: \"Intellectueller Bedeutung\" rendered \"intellectual meaning\" (outside me in the transcendental sense). *Cartesius* rendered \"Descartes\".","- 0629: \"Spiel\" rendered \"play\"; the \"ob ... ob\" clauses are joined by semicolon for clarity. \"Wirkung ... nächste Ursache\" is \"proximate cause\".","- 0630: Sentence divided at \"daraus aber schließt\"; \"he\" supplied as subject."]],"krv-a-0631":["Before I set out our paralogism in its deceptive appearance, I must first observe that two kinds of idealism have necessarily to be distinguished, the transcendental and the empirical. By transcendental idealism of all appearances I understand the doctrine according to which we regard them, one and all, as mere representations and not as things in themselves, and according to which time and space are only sensible forms of our intuition, and not determinations given in themselves, or conditions of objects viewed as things in themselves. To this idealism there is opposed a transcendental realism, which regards time and space as something given in itself, independently of our sensibility. The transcendental realist accordingly represents outer appearances (if their reality is granted) as things in themselves, existing independently of us and of our sensibility, and therefore as being outside us also in accordance with pure concepts of the understanding. It is this transcendental realist who afterwards plays the empirical idealist. Having wrongly presupposed of objects of the senses that, if they are to be outer, they must have their existence in themselves even apart from the senses, he finds, from this point of view, all our representations of the senses insufficient to make certain the reality of those objects.",["- Restated \"Dieser transscendentale Realist ist es eigentlich, welcher … findet\" slightly: the participial clause \"nachdem er … vorausgesetzt hat\" is turned into a \"Having…\" clause for clarity.","- \"Lehrbegriff\" rendered \"doctrine\"; \"vor sich gegebene\" as \"given in themselves\"; \"Obiecte\" as \"objects.\"","- \"im transscendentalen... also auch nach reinen Verstandesbegriffen ausser uns wären\": \"as being outside us\" supplied \"as\" for the sense."]],"krv-a-0632":["The transcendental idealist, on the other hand, can be an empirical realist, and so, as he is entitled, a dualist; that is, he can admit the existence of matter without going beyond mere self-consciousness, or assuming anything more than the certainty of representations in me, and so the *cogito, ergo sum*. For since he allows this matter, and even its inner possibility, to hold only as appearance, which, when separated from our sensibility, is nothing, matter is for him only a species of representations (intuition), which are entitled outer, not as if they related to objects that are in themselves outer, but because they relate perceptions to space, in which everything is outside everything else, while space itself is in us.",["- \"wie man ihn nennt\" rendered \"as he is entitled\" following the method's rule for *nennen*; \"as he is called\" would also serve.","- \"vor Erscheinung\" (for \"als/für Erscheinung\") rendered \"as appearance\"; the 1781 spelling \"vor\" for \"für\" silently normalised in sense.","- \"Anschauung\" in parentheses retained as \"(intuition)\"; the grammar leaves it uncertain whether it glosses \"Vorstellungen\" in the singular."]],"krv-a-0633":["We have already declared ourselves in favour of this transcendental idealism at the outset. On our doctrine, therefore, all hesitation disappears in accepting the existence of matter on the testimony of our mere self-consciousness, and in declaring it thereby proved, just as the existence of myself as a thinking being is proved. For I am conscious of my representations; therefore these exist, and I myself exist, who have these representations. Now outer objects (bodies) are mere appearances, and so are nothing else than a species of my representations, whose objects are something only through these representations, and apart from them are nothing. Thus outer things exist just as truly as I myself exist, and both on the immediate testimony of my self-consciousness. The only difference is that the representation of myself, as the thinking subject, is referred merely to inner sense, while the representations which indicate extended beings are referred also to outer sense. In regard to the reality of outer objects I have as little need to draw an inference as in regard to the reality of the object of my inner sense (my thoughts). Both are nothing but representations, and the immediate perception (consciousness) of them is at the same time a sufficient proof of their reality.",["- \"Vor bewiesen\" read as \"für bewiesen\"; \"Bedenklichkeit\" rendered \"hesitation\".","- \"deren Gegenstände nur durch diese Vorstellungen etwas sind, von ihnen abgesondert aber nichts seyn\": the final infinitive is smoothed into \"are nothing\".","- \"Wesen\" rendered \"beings\" in \"ausgedehnte Wesen\"."]],"krv-a-0634":["Thus the transcendental idealist is an empirical realist, and grants to matter, as appearance, a reality which does not have to be inferred but is immediately perceived. Transcendental realism, on the other hand, necessarily falls into difficulty, and finds itself obliged to make room for empirical idealism. For it regards the objects of outer sense as something distinct from the senses themselves, and mere appearances as self-subsistent beings that are to be found outside us. On this view, however good our consciousness of our representation of these things, it is still far from certain that if the representation exists, the object corresponding to it also exists. In our system, on the other hand, these outer things, namely matter in all its forms and alterations, are nothing but mere appearances, that is, representations in us, of whose reality we are immediately conscious.",["- The semicolon-linked sentence beginning \"da denn freilich\" is divided into separate sentences, with \"On this view\" supplied to carry the connection.","- \"Gestalten\" rendered \"forms\"; \"selbstständige Wesen\" as \"self-subsistent beings\"."]],"krv-a-0635":["Now since, so far as I know, all the psychologists who adhere to empirical idealism are transcendental realists, they have certainly proceeded quite consistently in ascribing great importance to empirical idealism, as one of the problems from which human reason can hardly extricate itself. For indeed, if outer appearances are regarded as representations produced in us by their objects, these objects being things existing in themselves outside us, it is impossible to see how we can know their existence otherwise than by inference from the effect to the cause; and in such an inference it must always remain doubtful whether the cause is in us or outside us. We can, indeed, grant that something which may be outside us in the transcendental sense is the cause of our outer intuitions. But this is not the object which we understand by the representations of matter and of corporeal things. These are merely appearances, that is, mere modes of representation, which are always to be found only in us, and whose reality rests upon immediate consciousness, just as does the consciousness of my own thoughts. The transcendental object is equally unknown in respect of inner and of outer intuition. But it is not of this that we are speaking, but of the empirical object, which is entitled outer when it is represented in space, and inner when it is represented solely in temporal relation; and space and time are both to be met with only in us.",["- \"im transscendentalen Verstande\" rendered \"in the transcendental sense\" (Verstande = meaning, not the faculty \"understanding\").","- The long sentence beginning \"Denn in der That\" is divided at \"Nun kan man zwar einräumen\"; \"bey welchem\" rendered \"in such an inference\".","- \"Vorstellungsarten\" rendered \"modes of representation\"."]],"krv-a-0636":["Since, however, the expression \"outside us\" carries with it an unavoidable ambiguity, signifying sometimes what exists as a thing in itself distinct from us, sometimes what belongs merely to outer appearance, we shall, in order to put this concept in the latter signification beyond uncertainty (it being in that signification that the psychological question as to the reality of our outer intuition is properly understood), distinguish empirically outer objects from those which might be so entitled in the transcendental sense, by calling them outright things which are to be met with in space.",["- \"ausser Unsicherheit zu setzen\" rendered \"beyond uncertainty\"; the parenthesis around \"als in welcher…\" is made explicit.","- \"gerade zu\" read as \"geradezu\" (\"outright\")."]],"krv-a-0637":["Space and time are, indeed, representations *a priori* which dwell in us as forms of our sensible intuition, before any actual object has determined our sense through sensation so as to represent it under those sensible relations. But this material or real element, this something which is to be intuited in space, necessarily presupposes perception. Independently of perception, which indicates the actuality of something in space, it cannot be invented or produced by any imagination. Sensation is therefore that which signifies an actuality in space and time, according as it is related to the one or the other mode of sensible intuition. Once sensation is given (it is entitled perception when it is applied to an object in general without determining that object), its manifold may enable us to invent in imagination many an object which has no empirical place in space or time outside the imagination. This is beyond all doubt. Whether we take the sensations of pleasure and pain, or those of the outer senses, such as colours, heat, and so forth, perception is that through which the material for thinking objects of sensible intuition must first be given. Such perception, then (to confine ourselves for the present to outer intuitions), represents something actual in space. For, in the first place, perception is the representation of an actuality, just as space is the representation of a mere possibility of being together. Secondly, this actuality is represented before outer sense, that is, in space. Thirdly, space itself is nothing but mere representation, and consequently only what is represented* in it can count in it as actual. Conversely, what is given in it, that is, represented in it through perception, is also actual in it; for if it were not actual in it, that is, immediately given through empirical intuition, it could not be invented either, since the real element of intuitions cannot be thought out *a priori* at all.",[]],"krv-a-0638":["*) This paradoxical but correct proposition must be well noted: that nothing is in space save what is represented in it. For space is itself nothing but representation, and consequently what is in it must be contained in the representation; and nothing whatever is in space, except in so far as it is actually represented in it. That a thing can exist only in the representation of it is a proposition which must certainly sound strange. But in this instance it loses its offensiveness, since the things with which we are here concerned are not things in themselves, but only appearances, that is, representations.",[]],"krv-a-0639":["All outer perception, therefore, immediately proves something actual in space, or is rather the actual itself. To this extent empirical realism is beyond doubt; that is to say, something actual in space corresponds to our outer intuitions. Space itself, with all its appearances, as representations, is, to be sure, only in me. Yet in this space the real, or the material of all objects of outer intuition, is none the less actually given, independently of all invention. It is also impossible that anything outside us (in the transcendental sense) should be given in this space, since space itself is nothing outside our sensibility. Hence the most rigorous idealist cannot demand that we prove that the object outside us (in the strict meaning of the term) corresponds to our perception. For if there were such an object, it could not be represented and intuited as outside us, since this presupposes space; and the actuality in space, as a mere representation, is nothing other than the perception itself. The real of outer appearances is therefore actual only in perception, and can be actual in no other way.",[]],"krv-a-0640":["From perceptions, knowledge of objects may now be generated, either through a mere play of imagination or by means of experience. And in this process deceptive representations may certainly arise, to which the objects do not correspond. The deception is to be ascribed sometimes to a delusion of imagination (in dreaming), sometimes to a false step of judgment (in what is called the deception of the senses). To escape false semblance in this matter, we proceed by the rule: whatever is connected with a perception according to empirical laws is actual. But this deception, and the safeguard against it, affect idealism and dualism alike, since what is at issue is only the form of experience. To refute empirical idealism, as a false scruple regarding the objective reality of our outer perceptions, it is already sufficient to show that outer perception immediately proves an actuality in space. This space, though in itself only a mere form of representations, has nevertheless objective reality in respect of all outer appearances (which are likewise nothing but mere representations). It is also sufficient to show that without perception even invention and dreaming are not possible, and that our outer senses, therefore, in respect of the data from which experience can arise, have their actual corresponding objects in space.",[]],"krv-a-0641":["The dogmatic idealist would be one who denies the existence of matter; the sceptical idealist, one who doubts it because he holds it to be indemonstrable. The former can be such only because he believes he finds contradictions in the possibility of matter in general; with him we are not yet concerned. The following section, on dialectical inferences, which presents reason in its inner conflict regarding the concepts that [reason] forms of the possibility of what belongs to the connection of experience, will remove this difficulty also. The sceptical idealist, however, who merely challenges the ground of our assertion, and declares our conviction of the existence of matter, which we believe ourselves to ground on immediate perception, to be insufficient, is a benefactor of human reason in so far as he constrains us to keep our eyes well open even at the smallest step of ordinary experience, and not at once to accept into our possession, as if honestly acquired, what we may perhaps have obtained only by stealth. The utility of these idealistic objections is now clearly apparent. They forcibly drive us, if we are not to become entangled in our commonest assertions, to regard all perceptions, whether entitled inner or outer, merely as a consciousness of what is attached to our sensibility, and to regard the outer objects of sensibility not as things in themselves but only as representations. Of these we can become immediately conscious, like any other representation; but they are entitled outer because they attach to that sense which we name outer sense, whose intuition is space. Space itself, however, is nothing but an inner mode of representation, in which certain perceptions are connected with one another.",["- krv-a-0637: Parenthesis on *Wahrnehmung heißt* kept as a parenthesis but recast as a clause; \"Dieses ist ungezweifelt gewiß\" made a separate sentence; Kant's \"Ist … so kan\" restated in English as \"may enable\".","- krv-a-0637: Long sentence beginning \"Allein dieses Materielle\" divided into two; \"bestimt\" (bestimmt) read as \"determined\"; \"Gegenstände … zu denken\" rendered literally.","- krv-a-0637: Asterisk marks the footnote (krv-a-0638) at \"vorgestellet*)\". \"Beysammenseyn\" = \"being together\" (coexistence).","- krv-a-0637: The sentence \"man mag nun die Empfindungen, Lust und Schmerz, oder auch der äusseren … nehmen\" is irregular in the German (genitive \"der äusseren\" dangling); smoothed as \"those of the outer senses\".","- krv-a-0638: \"Satz, der … klingen muß\" is an appositive fragment; recast as a full sentence.","- krv-a-0639: \"Erscheinungen\" is rendered \"appearances\", \"Wirklichkeit\" \"actuality\", \"Reale\" \"the real\"; \"in stricter Bedeutung\" rendered \"in the strict meaning of the term\".","- krv-a-0640: \"Erkentniß\" = \"knowledge\"; \"Urtheilskraft\" = \"judgment\"; \"datis\" kept as \"data\"; the final sentence's \"daß … seyn\" construction is made parallel by supplying \"it is also sufficient to show that\" [supplied].","- krv-a-0641: \"die sich … vorstellt\" is ambiguous (reason forms concepts of possibility); resolved with \"[reason]\" in brackets. \"vor unzureichend / vor Dinge\" = \"für\" (old usage). \"erschleichen\" rendered \"obtained by stealth\"."]],"krv-a-0642":["If we allow outer objects to count as things in themselves, it is absolutely impossible to understand how we are to arrive at knowledge of their reality outside us, since we rest solely upon the representation which is in us. For we cannot have sensation outside ourselves, but only in ourselves, and the whole of self-consciousness accordingly yields nothing save our own determinations. Sceptical idealism thus constrains us to take the one refuge that remains open to us, namely, to the ideality of all appearances. This ideality we have established in the Transcendental Aesthetic, independently of these consequences, which at that time we could not foresee.\n\nSuppose it now be asked whether, in view of this, dualism is to be found only in the doctrine of the soul. The answer is: Certainly! But only in the empirical sense. That is to say, only in the context of experience is matter really given, as substance in appearance, to outer sense, just as the thinking I is likewise given, as substance in appearance, to inner sense. And in accordance with the rules which this category introduces into the connection of our outer as well as of our inner perceptions, so as to unite them in one experience, appearances on both sides must be connected with one another.\n\nIf, however, we were to extend the concept of dualism, as is usually done, and to take it in the transcendental sense, then neither dualism, nor the opposed pneumatism on the one side, nor materialism on the other, would have the least ground. For we should then be missing the proper determination of our concepts, and should be taking the difference in the mode of representing objects, which remain unknown to us as regards what they are in themselves, for a difference in these things themselves. I, represented through inner sense in time, and objects in space outside me, are indeed appearances specifically [*sceptisch*, sic] quite distinct, but they are not on that account thought as different things. The transcendental object which underlies outer appearances, and likewise that which underlies inner intuition, is neither matter nor a thinking being in itself, but a ground, unknown to us, of the appearances which supply us with the empirical concept of the first as well as of the second kind.",["- \"vor Dinge an sich\" = \"for\" (old *vor* = *für*); \"Erkentniß\" = knowledge; \"Bestimmungen\" = determinations.","- Divided the long sentence \"Frägt man nun… Allerdings!…\" into several; supplied \"Suppose\" and \"That is to say\" for the particles.","- \"im empirischen Verstande\" / \"im transscendentalen Verstande\" rendered \"sense\" (Verstand here = *Sinn*, not the faculty); the German is retained in this note.","- \"sceptisch\" is doubtful as printed (Erdmann/others conjecture *specifisch*); I translated \"specifically\" and flagged it with [sic].","- \"Das transscendentale Obiect, welches den äusseren Erscheinungen… zum Grunde liegt\" lacks a verb in the German for the first clause; I supplied \"underlies\" for both.","- \"Pnevmatismus\" = pneumatism; the clause \"nicht den mindesten Grund\" has a double negative in the German, rendered as a single negation."]],"krv-a-0643":["If, then, we remain faithful, as the present Critique evidently constrains us to do, to the rule laid down above, that we should not carry our questions further than possible experience can furnish us with the object of them, we shall not even think of inquiring into the objects of our senses as to what they may be in themselves, that is, apart from all relation to the senses. But if the psychologist takes appearances for things in themselves, then, whether as materialist he admits into his doctrine matter alone, or as spiritualist thinking beings alone (that is, in accordance with the form of our inner sense), or as dualist both, as things existing in their own right, he is always led by misunderstanding to indulge in subtle reasoning as to how that may exist in itself which is yet not a thing in itself but only the appearance of a thing in general.",["- Kept the long second sentence as one, though the German construction breaks (the \"so mag er… so ist er doch\" concessive structure); I smoothed it into \"whether… or… or… he is always\".","- \"vernünfteln\" rendered \"indulge in subtle reasoning\" (pejorative sense); \"hingehalten\" = \"led on\".","- \"Lehrbegriff\" is absorbed in \"his doctrine\"."]],"krv-a-0644":["Reflection upon the sum of pure psychology, as following from these paralogisms.",["- \"Betrachtung\" rendered \"Reflection\"; \"Seelenlehre\" = \"psychology\" here, i.e. the doctrine of the soul. Title only."]],"krv-a-0645":["If we compare the doctrine of the soul, as the physiology of inner sense, with the doctrine of bodies, as a physiology of the objects of outer sense, we find, apart from the fact that in both much can be known empirically, this remarkable difference: in the latter science much can be known *a priori* from the mere concept of an extended, impenetrable being, whereas in the former nothing whatsoever can be known *a priori* synthetically from the concept of a thinking being. The reason is as follows. Though both are appearances, the appearance to outer sense has something standing or abiding, which supplies a substratum underlying the changeable determinations, and therefore a synthetic concept, namely that of space and of an appearance in space. Time, on the other hand, which is the sole form of our inner intuition, has nothing abiding, and therefore makes known only the change of determinations, not the determinable object.\n\nFor in what we entitle the soul everything is in continual flux, and there is nothing abiding, except perhaps (if one insists upon it) the I, which is so simple only because this representation has no content, and therefore no manifold; for which reason it also seems to represent, or, better, to designate, a simple object. This I would have to be an intuition which, being presupposed in all thought in general (prior to all experience), would as intuition yield *a priori* synthetic propositions, if it were to be possible to establish a pure rational knowledge of the nature of a thinking being in general. But this I is as little an intuition as it is a concept of any object. It is the mere form of consciousness, which can accompany both kinds of representation and raise them to the rank of knowledge, in so far, that is, as something else is given in intuition which supplies the material for a representation of an object. Thus rational psychology collapses, as a science exceeding all the powers of human reason; and nothing remains for us but to study our soul under the guidance of experience, and to keep within the limits of questions which go no further than possible inner experience can supply the content for them.",["- Split the sentence on \"Obgleich beides Erscheinungen sind… anstatt daß die Zeit…\" and began a new paragraph at \"Denn, in dem was wir Seele nennen\".","- \"Cörperlehre\" = doctrine of bodies; \"Erkentnissen\" = knowledge (plural mode: \"raise them to the rank of knowledge\").","- \"den bestimbaren Gegenstand\" = \"the determinable object\"; \"darum so einfache\" resolved as \"so simple only because\".","- \"beiderley Vorstellungen\" taken as intuitions and concepts, per the preceding clause; \"Kräfte\" = powers."]],"krv-a-0646":["But although it is of no use as extending our knowledge, and as such is composed wholly of paralogisms, we cannot deny it an important negative utility, if it is to count for nothing more than a critical treatment of our dialectical inferences, and indeed of those of common and natural reason.",["- \"sie\" refers to rational psychology (preceding passage); supplied \"it\".","- \"Erkentniß\" with \"erweiternde\" = knowledge that extends (ampliative)."]],"krv-a-0647":["For what purpose do we need a doctrine of the soul founded solely on pure principles of reason? Without doubt chiefly in order to secure our thinking self against the danger of materialism. But this is accomplished by the rational concept of our thinking self which we have given. For so far is it from being the case that, according to this concept, any fear remains that if matter were removed all thinking, and even the existence of thinking beings, would be abolished, that it is, on the contrary, clearly shown that if I remove the thinking subject the whole corporeal world must vanish, since it is nothing but the appearance in the sensibility of our subject, and a mode of representations of that subject.",["- \"Vernunftbegriff\" = rational concept (concept of reason); \"Cörperwelt\" = corporeal world.","- Restructured the \"Denn weit gefehlt, daß… so wird vielmehr\" construction as \"so far is it from being the case… that it is, on the contrary…\".","- \"eine Art Vorstellungen\" rendered \"a mode of representations\"."]],"krv-a-0648":["By this, to be sure, I do not come to know this thinking self any better as regards its properties, nor can I perceive its permanence, nor even the independence of its existence from the transcendental substratum, whatever it may be, of outer appearances; for this is as unknown to me as the other. But since it is nevertheless possible that I may derive from some other source than merely speculative grounds a reason for hoping that my thinking nature has an existence which is self-subsistent and abiding through every possible change of my state, much is already gained. For, while freely confessing my own ignorance, I can still repel the dogmatic attacks of a speculative opponent, and show him that he can never know more of the nature of my subject, so as to deny the possibility of my expectations, than I know in order to hold to them.",["- \"etwanigen\" rendered \"whatever it may be\"; \"einsehen\" = \"perceive\" (better: \"have insight into\").","- Supplied a new sentence at \"so ist dadurch schon viel gewonnen\" and added \"For\" to link the following clause; the German has one long sentence.","- The last clause (\"um… abzusprechen, als ich, um mich an ihnen zu halten\") is elliptical; I kept the parallelism of the German."]],"krv-a-0649":["Upon this transcendental illusion in our psychological concepts there are founded three further dialectical questions, which constitute the proper goal of rational psychology and which can be decided only by the above investigations. They concern: (1) the possibility of the communion of the soul with an organic body, that is, the animality, and the state, of the soul during the life of man; (2) the beginning of this communion, that is, the soul in and before the birth of man; (3) the end of this communion, that is, the soul in and after the death of man (the question of immortality).",["- Restated the colon construction (\"nemlich 1) … 2) … 3)\") as a sentence with \"They concern\", numbering retained.","- \"Cörper\" = body; \"Animalität\" rendered \"animality\"; \"Zustand\" rendered \"state\". \"Gemeinschaft\" = communion (Kant's Latin *commercium*, not given here)."]],"krv-a-0650":["I maintain that all the difficulties which are believed to attend these questions, and with which, as dogmatic objections, men seek to give themselves the appearance of a deeper insight into the nature of things than the common understanding can well possess, rest upon a mere deception. By it we hypostatise what exists merely in thought, and take it, in that very quality, as a real object outside the thinking subject. Extension, which is nothing but appearance, we take for a property of outer things that subsists even apart from our sensibility; and motion we take for the effect of these things, an effect actually occurring in itself, even outside our senses. For matter, the communion of which with the soul gives rise to so much misgiving, is nothing but a mere form, or a certain mode of representing an unknown object, through that intuition which we entitle outer sense. There may well, then, be something outside us to which this appearance, entitled matter, corresponds; but in the quality of appearance it is not outside us. It is merely a thought in us, although this thought, through the sense just named, represents it as existing outside us. Matter, therefore, does not signify a kind of substance so wholly distinct from, and heterogeneous with, the object of inner sense (the soul). It signifies only the dissimilarity of the appearances of objects (themselves unknown to us) whose representations we entitle outer, as compared with those which we reckon to inner sense. These outer representations belong just as much as all other thoughts to the thinking subject alone; but they have this deceptive feature, that since they represent objects in space, they seem, as it were, to detach themselves from the soul and to hover outside it. Yet space itself, in which they are intuited, is nothing but a representation, the counterpart of which, in the same quality, cannot be met with outside the soul at all. The question, then, is no longer that of the communion of the soul with other known and foreign substances outside us, but solely that of the connection of the representations of inner sense with the modifications of our outer sensibility, and of how these may be connected with one another according to constant laws, so as to cohere in one experience.",["- Divided the long opening sentence into several; \"nach welchem\" resolved as \"By it\" (the deception, *Blendwerk*).","- \"vor\" (= für) rendered \"for\"; \"Qualität\" kept as \"quality\".","- \"Gegenbild\" = counterpart; \"Modificationen\" = modifications.","- \"der Gegentheil\" in the German text of later passages is not here; no doubtful readings in this passage."]],"krv-a-0651":["So long as we hold inner and outer appearances together, as mere representations in experience, we find nothing absurd, nothing that makes the communion of the two kinds of sense strange. But as soon as we hypostatise outer appearances, regarding them no longer as representations but, in the same quality in which they are in us, as things subsisting by themselves outside us, and refer to our thinking subject their actions, which as appearances they exhibit in relation to one another, we have a character of efficient causes outside us which will not harmonise with their effects in us. The former relates solely to outer sense, the latter to inner sense, and these, although united in one subject, are none the less extremely dissimilar. We then have no outer effects other than changes of place, and no forces other than mere strivings, which issue in relations in space as their effects. In us, however, the effects are thoughts, among which there is no relation of place, motion, shape, or determination of space in general; and we lose altogether the clue of causes in the effects which should show themselves in inner sense.\n\nBut we ought to bear in mind that bodies are not objects in themselves present to us, but a mere appearance of we know not what unknown object; that motion is not the effect of this unknown cause, but merely the appearance of its influence upon our senses; that consequently both are not something outside us, but merely representations in us; and therefore that it is not the motion of matter that produces representations in us, but that this motion is itself (and so also the matter which makes itself known through it) mere representation. The whole self-made difficulty finally comes to this: how, and through what cause, the representations of our sensibility stand in such connection with one another that those which we entitle outer intuitions can be represented, according to empirical laws, as objects outside us. This question does not at all contain the supposed difficulty of explaining the origin of representations from quite foreign efficient causes outside us, in that we take the appearances of an unknown cause for the cause outside us, which can only occasion confusion.\n\nWhere a misinterpretation rooted in long habit is involved, it is impossible to bring the correction at once to that degree of comprehensibility which can be attained in other cases, where no such unavoidable illusion confuses the concept. Hence this deliverance of reason from sophistical theories can hardly as yet have the distinctness necessary for its complete satisfaction.",["- Divided the long sentence at \"Aber wir sollten bedenken\" into separate sentences and a new paragraph; the final \"welche Frage…\" clause is restated as \"This question…\".","- \"wer weis, welches unbekanten Gegenstandes\" rendered \"of we know not what unknown object\".","- \"Raumesbestimmung\" = determination of space; \"Leitfaden\" = clue.","- \"Urtheilen\" in \"In Urtheilen, in denen…\" rendered \"Where a misinterpretation … is involved\" for smoothness.","- \"den Leitfaden der Ursachen gänzlich an den Wirkungen\" is compressed in sense; rendering is a judgment."]],"krv-a-0652":["I believe I can promote this [distinctness] in the following way.",["- \"diese\" is feminine and refers back to *Deutlichkeit*; supplied in brackets."]],"krv-a-0653":["All objections may be divided into dogmatic, critical and sceptical. A dogmatic objection is one directed against a proposition; a critical objection, one directed against the proof of a proposition. The former requires an insight into the constitution of the nature of the object, in order to assert the contrary of what the proposition affirms of this object. It is therefore itself dogmatic, and professes to know the constitution in question better than does its opponent. The critical objection leaves the proposition untouched in its worth or worthlessness, and attacks only the proof; it has no need to know the object better, or to claim a better knowledge of it. It shows only that the assertion is groundless, not that it is false. The sceptical objection sets proposition and counter-proposition against each other, as objections of equal weight, each alternately as dogma and the other as its objection. It is thus, on two opposite sides, dogmatic in appearance, in order to annul all judgment about the object altogether. Both the dogmatic and the sceptical objection must accordingly profess as much insight into their object as is requisite for asserting something of it, affirmatively or negatively. The critical objection alone is of such a kind that, in merely showing that something null and merely imagined is assumed in support of the assertion, it overthrows the theory by withdrawing its pretended foundation, without wishing to settle anything further regarding the constitution of the object.",["- Divided the long sentences on the dogmatic and sceptical objection; \"der Gegentheil\" rendered \"its opponent\".","- \"dem Scheine nach dogmatisch\" rendered \"dogmatic in appearance\".","- \"Kentniß\" rendered \"knowledge\" (*Erkenntnis* not used in the German here)."]],"krv-a-0654":["Now, in accordance with the common concepts of our reason, we are dogmatic in regard to the community in which our thinking subject stands with the things outside us. We regard these as genuine objects, subsisting independently of us, in accordance with a certain transcendental dualism, which does not reckon those outer appearances as representations belonging to the subject, but transposes them, just as sensible intuition delivers them to us, outside us as objects, and entirely separates them from the thinking subject. This subreption is the foundation of all theories of the community between soul and body. It is never asked whether this objective reality of appearances is quite correct; it is presupposed as conceded, and all the subtilising is directed only to the manner in which it has to be explained and comprehended. The three systems commonly devised on the matter, and indeed the only possible ones, are those of physical influence, of pre-established harmony, and of supernatural assistance.",[]],"krv-a-0655":["The two latter modes of explaining the community of the soul with matter are grounded on objections to the first, which is the representation of the common understanding. The objection is that what appears as matter cannot, by its immediate influence, be the cause of representations, these being an entirely heterogeneous kind of effects. But in saying this they cannot connect with what they understand by the object of outer sense the concept of a matter which is nothing but appearance, and so is already in itself a mere representation, produced by some outer objects. [Were they to do so,] they would otherwise be saying that the representations of outer objects (the appearances) cannot be outer causes of the representations in our mind. This would be an entirely meaningless objection, since it will occur to nobody to take for an outer cause what he has once recognised as a mere representation. In accordance with our principles, therefore, they must direct their theory to this: that what is the true (transcendental) object of our outer senses cannot be the cause of those representations (appearances) which we understand by the name matter. Now since nobody can with reason pretend to know anything of the transcendental cause of our representations of outer sense, their assertion is entirely groundless.\n\nSuppose, however, that the supposed improvers of the doctrine of physical influence, following the common mode of representation of a transcendental dualism, were to regard matter as such as a thing in itself (and not as mere appearance of an unknown thing), and were to direct their objection to showing that such an outer object, which exhibits no other causality than that of motions, can never be the efficient cause of representations, but that a third being must intervene in order to establish, if not reciprocal action, at least correspondence and harmony between the two. They would then begin their refutation by assuming, in their dualism, the *prōton pseudos* (πρῶτον ψεῦδος) of physical influence, and would thus, by their objection, refute not so much natural influence as their own dualistic presupposition. For all the difficulties which beset the connection of thinking nature with matter arise, without exception, solely from that surreptitious dualistic representation, namely that matter as such is not appearance, that is, a mere representation of the mind to which an unknown object corresponds, but is the object in itself, as it exists outside us and independently of all sensibility.",[]],"krv-a-0656":["No dogmatic objection, therefore, can be made against the commonly accepted physical influence. For if the opponent assumes that matter and its motion are mere appearances, and so themselves only representations, he can find the difficulty only in this: that the unknown object of our sensibility cannot be the cause of the representations in us. But nothing in the least entitles him to assert this, since nobody can determine of an unknown object what it can or cannot do. By our proofs above, however, he must necessarily concede this transcendental idealism, unless he is manifestly to hypostatise representations and transpose them, as real things, outside himself.",[]],"krv-a-0657":["Nevertheless a well-grounded critical objection can be made against the common doctrine of physical influence. Such a pretended community between two kinds of substances, the thinking and the extended, lays a crude dualism at the foundation, and makes the latter, which are nothing but mere representations of the thinking subject, into things that subsist by themselves. The misunderstood physical influence can therefore be completely frustrated by exposing the ground of proof for it as void and surreptitious.",[]],"krv-a-0658":["The notorious question as to the community of the thinking and the extended would therefore, if we set aside everything merely imagined, come to this alone: how in a thinking subject in general outer intuition is possible, namely that of space (of its filling, shape and motion). To this question, however, it is not possible for any man to find an answer. This gap in our knowledge can never be filled, but only indicated, by ascribing the outer appearances to a transcendental object, which is the cause of this kind of representations, but of which we have no knowledge whatever, and shall never acquire any concept. In all problems that may arise in the field of experience we treat those appearances as objects in themselves, without troubling ourselves about the first ground of their possibility (as appearances). But if we go beyond its limits, the concept of a transcendental object becomes necessary.",[]],"krv-a-0659":["From these reminders concerning the community between thinking and extended beings, there follows immediately the decision of all disputes or objections relating to the state of thinking nature before this community (life), or after the community has been annulled (in death). The opinion that the thinking subject could have thought prior to all community with bodies would be expressed thus: that before the beginning of this kind of sensibility, by which something appears to us in space, those same transcendental objects which in the present state appear as bodies could have been intuited in an entirely different manner. The opinion, however, that the soul, after all community with the corporeal world has been annulled, can still continue to think, would announce itself in this form: that if the kind of sensibility by which transcendental objects, at present entirely unknown, appear to us as a material world were to cease, all intuition of them is not thereby removed; and it is quite possible that those same unknown objects should continue to be known by the thinking subject, although admittedly no longer in the quality of bodies.",[]],"krv-a-0660":["Now nobody can adduce from speculative principles the least ground for such an assertion, nor even demonstrate its possibility, but can only presuppose it. Equally little, however, can anyone make any valid dogmatic objection against it. For whoever he may be, he knows just as little as I, or anyone else, of the absolute and inner cause of outer and corporeal appearances. He cannot, therefore, with reason pretend to know on what the actuality of outer appearances in the present state (in life) rests, and hence also cannot pretend to know that the condition of all outer intuition, or even the thinking subject itself, will cease after that state (in death).",["- 0654: \"Gemeinschaft\" rendered \"community\"; \"vernünfteln\" as \"subtilising\"; \"Subreption\" kept as \"subreption\". Ambiguous \"diese\" (last sentence of the middle) resolved as the objective reality of appearances.","- 0655: The first long sentence is divided in two. The syntax is broken after \"Erscheinung, mithin ... gewirkt worden\" (the verb is missing); I smoothed it and supplied \"[Were they to do so,]\" for \"denn sonst\".","- 0655: \"Gegenstand\" is rendered \"object\" throughout; \"Vorstellungsart\" as \"mode of representation\"; \"sinnleer\" as \"meaningless\". The Greek is transliterated and kept.","- 0655: The final conditional (Wollten aber ...) is long; I kept it as one sentence with a restated main clause. \"Nimmermehr\" as \"never\".","- 0656: \"Er muß ... einräumen\" refers to the opponent; \"hypostasiren\" rendered \"hypostatise\".","- 0657: \"die leztere\" in the German refers to the extended substances; the plural \"sind\" follows \"Vorstellungen\", so I resolved the sense thus.","- 0658: \"Wissen\" rendered \"knowledge\" in \"Lücke unseres Wissens\". \"deren Gränze\" refers to the field of experience.","- 0659: \"dieselbe transscendentale Gegenstände\" is kept as \"those same transcendental objects\". \"Anschauung derselben\" resolved as intuition of those objects.","- 0660: Orthographic variants (e.g. \"Cörper\", \"ietzigen\") were silently modernised; no corruption suspected."]],"krv-a-0661":["All dispute, then, about the nature of our thinking being and its connection with the corporeal world is simply a consequence of filling the gap, in regard to what we do not know, with paralogisms of reason. We turn our thoughts into things and hypostatise them. Out of this arises an imaginary science, alike on the part of him who affirms and of him who denies; for each either supposes himself to know something about objects of which no man has any concept, or else makes his own representations into objects, and so turns round and round in an eternal circle of ambiguities and contradictions.\n\nNothing but the sobriety of a critique which is strict, but just, can free us from this dogmatic illusion, which through an imaginary happiness keeps so many entangled in theories and systems; and nothing else can limit all our speculative claims to the field of possible experience. This it will do, not by insipid ridicule of attempts so often failed, nor by pious sighs over the limits of our reason, but by a determination of those limits effected in accordance with secure principles. Such a determination fastens its *nihil ulterius* with the greatest certainty to the Pillars of Hercules which nature herself has set up, in order that the voyage of our reason may be continued only as far as the continuous coasts of experience extend. We cannot leave these coasts without venturing upon a shoreless ocean, which, under ever deceptive prospects, compels us in the end to abandon as hopeless all our toilsome and protracted endeavour.",[]],"krv-a-0662":["So far we have remained in debt for a clear and general exposition of the transcendental and yet natural illusion in the paralogisms of pure reason, and likewise for the justification of their systematic arrangement, which runs parallel with the table of the categories. We could not have undertaken this at the beginning of this section without running the risk of obscurity, or of unbecomingly anticipating ourselves. We shall now try to discharge this obligation.",[]],"krv-a-0663":["All illusion may be said to consist in the subjective condition of thought being taken for the knowledge of the object. Further, we have shown in the Introduction to the Transcendental Dialectic that pure reason is concerned solely with the totality of the synthesis of conditions for a given conditioned. Now the dialectical illusion of pure reason cannot be an empirical illusion, such as occurs in determinate empirical knowledge. It will therefore concern what is universal in the conditions of thought, and there will be only three cases of the dialectical employment of pure reason:",[]],"krv-a-0664":["1. The synthesis of the conditions of a thought in general.",[]],"krv-a-0665":["2. The synthesis of the conditions of empirical thinking.",[]],"krv-a-0666":["3. The synthesis of the conditions of pure thinking.",[]],"krv-a-0667":["In all these three cases pure reason is occupied solely with the absolute totality of this synthesis, that is, with that condition which is itself unconditioned. On this division is also grounded the threefold transcendental illusion, which gives occasion for the three sections of the Dialectic, and supplies the idea for as many seeming sciences from pure reason: transcendental psychology, cosmology and theology. Here we have to deal only with the first.",[]],"krv-a-0668":["In thinking in general we abstract from all relation of the thought to any object, whether of the senses or of the pure understanding. Consequently the synthesis of the conditions of a thought in general (no. 1) is not objective at all, but is merely a synthesis of the thought with the subject, which is, however, falsely taken for a synthetic representation of an object.",[]],"krv-a-0669":["It also follows from this that the dialectical inference to the condition of all thought in general, which is itself unconditioned, does not commit a fault in content, for it abstracts from all content or object. The fault lies in the form alone, and the inference must be entitled a paralogism.",[]],"krv-a-0670":["Further, the one condition which accompanies all thought is the \"I\" in the universal proposition \"I think\", and reason therefore has to do with this condition so far as it is itself unconditioned. But it is only the formal condition, namely the logical unity of every thought, from which I abstract all object. Nevertheless it is represented as an object which I think, namely, myself and the unconditioned unity of myself.",[]],"krv-a-0671":["Suppose someone were to put the question quite generally: what is the nature of a thing which thinks? I cannot answer it in the least *a priori*, since the answer has to be synthetic. (An analytic answer may perhaps explain thinking, but gives no extended knowledge of that upon which this thinking, in its possibility, rests.) Every synthetic solution, however, requires intuition, and this has been entirely left out in so general a problem. In the same way no one can answer in its generality the question: what sort of thing must that be which is movable? For impenetrable extension (matter) is then not given. Although I know no answer to that question in general, it yet seems to me that I can give one in the single case, in the proposition which expresses self-consciousness: I think. For this \"I\" is the first subject, that is, substance; it is simple, etc. But these would then have to be mere propositions of experience, which nevertheless, without a universal rule expressing the conditions of the possibility of thinking in general and *a priori*, could not contain any such predicates, which are not empirical. In this way my insight into the nature of a thinking being, an insight at first so plausible, and judging moreover from concepts alone, becomes suspect to me, although I have not yet discovered its error.",[]],"krv-a-0672":["Further inquiry into the origin of these attributes, which I ascribe to myself as a thinking being in general, can, however, reveal the error. They are nothing more than pure categories, through which I never think a determinate object, but only the unity of representations, in order to determine an object for them. Without an underlying intuition the category alone cannot yield me a concept of an object; for only through intuition is the object given, which is thereafter thought in accordance with the category. If I declare a thing to be a substance in the appearance, predicates of its intuition must previously have been given to me, by which I distinguish the permanent from the changeable, and the substratum (the thing itself) from that which merely attaches to it. If I call a thing simple in the appearance, I understand by this that the intuition of it, though a part of the appearance, cannot itself be divided, and so on. But if something is known as simple only in concept, and not in appearance, I have thereby really no knowledge whatever of the object, but only of my concept, which I make for myself of something in general that is not capable of any proper intuition. I say only that I think something quite simple, because in fact I know nothing further to say of it than merely that it is something.",["- krv-a-0661: the second sentence is divided into several; \"da man seine Gedanken zu Sachen macht\" is made a separate sentence. The last long sentence is also broken up, and \"die\" (the coasts) is resolved by judgment.","- krv-a-0661: \"dem Blendwerke, der\" is a grammatical slip in the original (gender); rendered simply as \"illusion\". \"Hypostasirt\" is rendered \"hypostatise\".","- krv-a-0661: \"Critik\" is rendered \"critique\"; \"Gränzbestimmung\" as \"determination of those limits\" (Bestimmung = determination).","- krv-a-0663: \"Erkentniß des Obiects\" is rendered \"knowledge of the object\"; \"Fälle\" as \"cases\".","- krv-a-0668: the \"abstrahiren\" clause is restated as two sentences; \"Gedanke\" is rendered as \"thought\" throughout.","- krv-a-0670: \"bey dem ich von allem Gegenstande abstrahire\" is shortened to \"from which I abstract all object\" (a smoothing); \"desselben\" is resolved as referring to the \"I\", which is \"myself\".","- krv-a-0671: the parenthesis is closed after \"rests\" (the original lacks the closing bracket). \"Erfahrungssätze\" is rendered \"propositions of experience\".","- krv-a-0672: \"hinter den Ursprung\" is rendered \"into the origin\"; \"Erkentniß\" is \"knowledge\"; \"Ding selbst\" is kept as \"the thing itself\" and is not read as the technical *Ding an sich*."]],"krv-a-0673":["Now the mere apperception ('I') is substance in concept, simple in concept, and so forth; and in this way all those psychological doctrines have their indisputable correctness. Yet what we really want to know about the soul is by no means thereby known, for all these predicates are not valid of intuition at all, and consequently can have no consequences that could be applied to objects of experience. They are therefore completely empty. For that concept of substance does not teach me that the soul endures by itself; nor that it is a part of outer intuitions which is itself no longer divisible, and which therefore cannot arise or perish through any alterations of nature. These are properties, and only such, that could make the soul known to me in the context of experience, and could yield disclosures regarding its origin and future state.\n\nBut suppose I say, through the mere category, that the soul is a simple substance. The bare concept of substance, as a concept of the understanding, contains nothing more than that a thing is to be represented as subject in itself, without in turn being the predicate of something else. It is clear, then, that nothing of permanence follows from it, and that the attribute of simplicity certainly cannot add this permanence. We are therefore not in the least instructed as to what may befall the soul amid the changes of the world. If we could be told that the soul is a simple part of matter, we could derive its permanence from what experience teaches us of matter, and, taken together with its simple nature, its indestructibility. But of this the concept of the 'I', in the psychological principle ('I think'), tells us not a word.",["- The long second sentence of the second paragraph (\"Wenn ich nun aber…\") is restated as three shorter sentences; \"daraus nichts… folge\" is made the main clause after \"It is clear, then\".","- \"lauter Eigenschaften\" is rendered \"These are properties, and only such\"; the German sentence is broken at the semicolon.","- \"Würde man uns sagen können\" is rendered as a conditional; \"Zusammenhange der Erfahrung\" as \"context of experience\".","- \"etc.\" after \"einfach im Begriffe\" is rendered \"and so forth\".","- \"vor sich selbst\" (= für sich selbst) is rendered \"by itself\"."]],"krv-a-0674":["That the being which thinks in us believes itself to know itself through pure categories, and indeed through those which express absolute unity under each of the headings, has the following source. Apperception is itself the ground of the possibility of the categories; and these, for their part, represent nothing but the synthesis of the manifold of intuition, in so far as that manifold has unity in apperception. Self-consciousness in general is therefore the representation of that which is the condition of all unity and yet is itself unconditioned. Of the thinking 'I' (the soul), which represents itself as substance, as simple, as numerically identical in all time, and as the correlate of all existence from which all other existence must be inferred, we can therefore say that it knows, not so much itself through the categories, as the categories, and through them all objects, in the absolute unity of apperception, and so through itself.\n\nNow it is indeed very evident that what I must presuppose in order to know any object whatsoever, I cannot myself know as an object; and that the determining self (the thinking) is distinguished from the determinable self (the thinking subject) as knowledge is distinguished from its object [*Gegenstand*]. Nevertheless nothing is more natural, or more misleading, than the illusion which takes the unity in the synthesis of thoughts to be a perceived unity in the subject of these thoughts. It might be entitled the subreption of the hypostatised consciousness (*apperceptiones substantiatae*).",["- The first sentence is divided (\"rührt daher\" rendered \"has the following source\"); the second German sentence on the 'I' is kept whole but its parenthetical clauses are grouped for clarity.","- \"bestimmende / bestimbare Selbst\" = \"determining / determinable self\" (Bestimmung = determination).","- \"Erkentniß vom Gegenstande\" rendered \"knowledge … from its object\"; \"its\" supplied.","- \"nennen\" rendered \"entitled\" per the method.","- \"sich selbst… erkent\": the final \"mithin durch sich selbst\" is taken as \"and so through itself\"."]],"krv-a-0675":["If we wish to give the paralogism in the dialectical inferences of the rational doctrine of the soul a logical title, in so far as their premisses are nevertheless correct, it may be counted a *sophisma figurae dictionis*. In it the major premiss makes of the category, in respect of its condition, a merely transcendental use; but the minor premiss and the conclusion, in respect of the soul which has been subsumed under this condition, make of the very same category an empirical use.\n\nThus, for instance, the concept of substance in the paralogism of simplicity is a pure intellectual concept which, apart from conditions of sensible intuition, is merely of transcendental use, that is, of no use at all. In the minor premiss, however, the very same concept is applied to the object of all inner experience, without first establishing and laying at the foundation the condition of its application *in concreto*, namely the permanence of that object. An empirical use has accordingly been made of it, though one here inadmissible.",["- Divided into two paragraphs at \"So ist z. B.\"; the last German sentence is also restated with \"accordingly\".","- \"von transscendentalen… Gebrauch\" kept as \"of transcendental use\"; \"d. i.\" = \"that is\".","- \"desselben\" in \"Beharrlichkeit desselben\" taken to refer to the object (Gegenstand) of inner experience.","- \"voraus festzusetzen\" rendered \"first establishing\"."]],"krv-a-0676":["Finally, in order to show the systematic connection of all these dialectical assertions in a sophistical doctrine of the soul, as a connection within pure reason, and so to show their completeness, we should note that apperception is carried through all the classes of the categories, but only through those concepts of the understanding which in each class underlie the rest as the ground of unity in a possible perception. These are: subsistence, reality, unity (not plurality), and existence. Reason, however, here represents them all as conditions of the possibility of a thinking being, conditions which are themselves unconditioned. Thus the soul knows of itself",["- Long sentence divided at \"folglich\"; \"nur daß\" rendered as a new sentence beginning \"Reason, however\".","- \"vernünftelnden\" rendered \"sophistical\"; \"Hauptstück\" is not present here.","- The passage breaks off (\"erkent die Seele an sich selbst\") and is translated as it stands."]],"krv-a-0677":["1. The unconditioned unity of relation, that is, itself not as inhering but as subsisting.",["- \"Verhältnis\" rendered \"relation\" (the category heading).","- \"sich selbst\" supplied in English as \"itself\", as the object of \"knows\" in the preceding passage."]],"krv-a-0678":["2. The unconditioned unity of quality, that is, not as a real whole, but as simple.*)",["- The asterisk marks the footnote that follows."]],"krv-a-0679":["*) How the simple here again corresponds to the category of reality, I cannot yet show; it will be shown in the following chapter, on the occasion of another use of reason made of this same concept.",["- \"Hauptstück\" rendered \"chapter\"; \"Vernunftgebrauch\" as \"use of reason\"."]],"krv-a-0680":["3. The unconditioned unity in the plurality [of moments] in time, that is, not numerically different in different times, but as one and the same subject.",["- \"of moments\" is not in the German; I supplied it in brackets to complete \"bey der Vielheit in der Zeit\" (a plurality in time)."]],"krv-a-0681":["4. The unconditioned unity of existence in space, that is, not as the consciousness of several things outside it, but only of the existence of itself, other things being [regarded] merely as its representations.",["- \"regarded\" is supplied in brackets; \"anderer Dinge aber, blos als ihrer Vorstellungen\" has no verb of its own.","- \"ihr/ihrer\" refers to the soul (die Seele)."]],"krv-a-0682":["Reason is the faculty of principles. The assertions of pure psychology contain no empirical predicates of the soul. They contain such predicates as, if they hold, are to determine the object in itself, independently of experience, and so through mere reason. They ought, therefore, properly to be grounded on principles and universal concepts of thinking natures in general. Instead of this, we find that the single representation, 'I am', governs them all. Just because this representation expresses the pure formula of all my experience (indeterminately), it announces itself as a universal proposition valid for all thinking beings. Since it is nevertheless in every respect singular, it carries with it the illusion of an absolute unity of the conditions of thought in general, and thereby extends itself further than possible experience could reach.",["- The long final sentence is divided into three; \"welche … sie insgesamt regirt\" is resolved so that \"welche\" refers to the representation \"Ich bin\".","- \"(unbestimt)\" kept as a parenthetical \"(indeterminately)\".","- \"Vermögen\" rendered \"faculty\" here, as the sense requires."]],"krv-a-0683":["Transcendental Dialectic, Book II, Chapter II. The Antinomy of Pure Reason.",["- Heading rendered in title form; the A and B pagination is omitted as instructed."]],"krv-a-0684":["We have shown in the Introduction to this part of our work that all transcendental illusion of pure reason rests on dialectical inferences, the schema of which logic supplies in the three formal kinds of inference of reason in general, just as the categories find their logical schema in the four functions of all judgments.\n\nThe first kind of these sophistical inferences concerned the unconditioned unity of the subjective conditions of all representations in general (of the subject, or soul). It corresponds to categorical syllogisms, the major premiss of which, as principle, asserts the relation of a predicate to a subject. By analogy with hypothetical syllogisms, the second kind of dialectical argument will therefore make the unconditioned unity of the objective conditions in appearance its content. The third kind, which will occur in the following chapter, has as its theme the unconditioned unity of the objective conditions of the possibility of objects in general.",["- The opening sentence is divided; the second sentence is restated in two, with \"in Correspondenz mit\" made a separate clause.","- \"Vernunftschlüsse\" rendered \"syllogisms\" where the context is categorical and hypothetical, \"inferences\" elsewhere.","- \"Erscheinung\" = \"appearance\"; \"Hauptstück\" = \"chapter\"."]],"krv-a-0685":["It is noteworthy, however, that the transcendental paralogism produced a merely one-sided illusion in respect of the idea of the subject of our thought, and that for the assertion of the opposite not the least illusion is to be found from concepts of reason. The advantage lies wholly on the side of pneumatism, although this cannot disown its hereditary defect, that, for all the illusion favourable to it, it dissolves into mere vapour in the fiery test of criticism.",["- \"Pnevmatismus\" rendered \"pneumatism\"; \"Critik\" rendered \"criticism\" per the glossary.","- \"Erbfehler\" rendered \"hereditary defect\".","- The final clause \"bey allem ihm günstigen Schein\" is moved forward in the English to read smoothly."]],"krv-a-0686":["The outcome is quite otherwise when we apply reason to the objective synthesis of appearances. Here reason thinks, with much show of plausibility, to establish its principle of unconditioned unity. But it soon becomes entangled in such contradictions that, so far as its cosmological purpose is concerned, it is constrained to abandon its demand.",["- Split the German sentence at \"wo\", making the second clause a new sentence. \"Scheine\" = \"plausibility\"/\"show\"; \"obiective Synthesis\" = \"objective synthesis\".","- \"Foderung\" = \"demand\" (Forderung). \"Principium\" rendered \"principle\"."]],"krv-a-0687":["For here there shows itself a new phenomenon of human reason, namely, a quite natural antithetic, for which no one need rack his brains or lay artificial snares. Reason falls into it of itself, and indeed unavoidably. It is thereby guarded against the slumber of a fancied conviction, such as a merely one-sided illusion produces; but at the same time it is brought into temptation either to yield to a sceptical despair, or to adopt a dogmatic obstinacy and stubbornly insist on certain assertions, without giving a hearing or doing justice to the grounds of the opposing side. Either is the death of a sound philosophy, although the former might perhaps still be entitled the euthanasia of pure reason.",["- Divided after \"braucht\" and again after \"unvermeidlich\"; the relative clause \"auf die... in welche...\" is restated as two sentences.","- \"den Kopf steif auf ... setzen\" is idiomatic: \"stubbornly insist\". \"iener\" = \"the former\" (scepticism). \"genant werden\" = \"be entitled\"."]],"krv-a-0688":["Before we exhibit the scenes of dissension and upheaval which this conflict of the laws (antinomy) of pure reason occasions, we shall offer certain explanations that may elucidate and justify the method we employ in treating our subject. I entitle all transcendental ideas, so far as they concern absolute totality in the synthesis of appearances, *world-concepts* [Weltbegriffe]. I do so partly on account of this very unconditioned totality, on which the concept of the world-whole [Weltganzen] also rests, itself only an idea; and partly because these ideas relate solely to the synthesis of appearances, and so to the empirical synthesis. Absolute totality in the synthesis of the conditions of all possible things in general, on the other hand, will give occasion to an ideal of pure reason, which is entirely distinct from the world-concept, although it stands in relation to it.\n\nAccordingly, just as the paralogisms of pure reason laid the foundation of a dialectical psychology, so the antinomy of pure reason will set before our eyes the transcendental principles of a supposedly pure (rational) cosmology. It will do so not in order that we may find this cosmology valid and appropriate it, but, as the very title, a conflict of reason, already indicates, in order to exhibit it, as an idea that cannot be reconciled with appearances, in its dazzling but false show.",["- Restated as several sentences; \"Weltbegriffe\" given in brackets, italicised as a term the text itself introduces (no emphasis in the source, so this is my marking of a definition; remove if undesired).","- \"Daher\" at the start of the last sentence is rendered \"Accordingly\" and the paragraph divided there for clarity.","- \"sie\" in the last clause refers to the cosmology's principles, yet \"als eine Idee\" is singular; I took the sense as the cosmology/its principles taken as an idea. \"Scheine\" = \"show\"/\"illusion\" depending on context.","- \"veranlaßt\"/\"veranlassen\" = \"occasion\"/\"give occasion to\". \"Auftritte\" = \"scenes\"."]],"krv-a-0689":["Of the Antinomy of Pure Reason: Section I. The System of Cosmological Ideas.",["- Heading; \"Erster Abschnitt\" rendered \"Section I\"."]],"krv-a-0690":["In order to enumerate these ideas with systematic precision according to a principle, we must first observe that pure and transcendental concepts can arise only from the understanding. Reason does not really generate any concept at all. At most it only frees a concept of the understanding from the unavoidable limitations of possible experience, and so seeks to extend it beyond the limits of the empirical, though still in connection with it. This happens in the following manner. For a given conditioned, reason demands, on the side of the conditions (under which the understanding subjects all appearances to synthetic unity), absolute totality. It thereby converts the category into a transcendental idea, in order to give absolute completeness to the empirical synthesis by continuing it up to the unconditioned (which is never met with in experience, but only in the idea). Reason makes this demand in accordance with the principle: if the conditioned is given, the whole sum of the conditions, and therefore the absolutely unconditioned, through which alone the conditioned was possible, is likewise given.\n\nThus, in the first place, the transcendental ideas will be really nothing but categories extended to the unconditioned, and they can be brought into a table arranged according to the titles of the categories. In the second place, however, not all categories will serve for this, but only those in which the synthesis constitutes a series, and indeed a series of conditions subordinated to one another (not co-ordinated) in relation to a conditioned. Absolute totality is demanded by reason only so far as it concerns the ascending series of the conditions of a given conditioned. It is not demanded when we are speaking of the descending line of the consequences, nor of the aggregate of co-ordinated conditions of these consequences. For conditions are already presupposed in respect of the given conditioned, and are to be regarded as given along with it. The consequences, on the other hand, do not make their conditions possible but rather presuppose them; and therefore in advancing to the consequences (or in descending from the given condition to the conditioned) we may be unconcerned whether the series comes to an end or not. The question of its totality is in no way a presupposition of reason.",["- Restated in many sentences. The first sentence's \"müssen ... bemerken: daß ... seyn, ... erzeuge\" shifts mood; I smoothed it into plain indicatives and supplied \"Reason does not really generate\".","- \"Erstlich ... Zweitens\" are rendered \"in the first place ... in the second place\"; \"Also\" = \"Thus\".","- \"dieienige, in welchen\" is irregular in gender/number; I read \"those categories in which\".","- \"anstatt daß, da die Folgen ...\" is awkwardly built; I recast it as a contrast with \"on the other hand\".","- \"schlechthin Unbedingte\" = \"absolutely unconditioned\"; \"Aggregat coordinirter Bedingungen\" = \"aggregate of co-ordinated conditions\"."]],"krv-a-0691":["Thus we necessarily think of a time that has completely elapsed up to the given moment as likewise given (even though not determinable by us). As regards the future, however, since it is not the condition of our arriving at the present, it is quite indifferent, for the comprehension of the present, how we choose to treat future time, whether we will let it come to an end somewhere or run on to infinity. Let there be the series m, n, o, in which n is given as conditioned in respect of m, but at the same time as the condition of o. Let the series ascend from the conditioned n to m (l, k, i, etc.), and likewise descend from the condition n to the conditioned o (p, q, r, etc.). Then I must presuppose the former series in order to regard n as given; and according to reason (the totality of conditions) n is possible only by means of that series. Its possibility, however, does not rest upon the subsequent series o, p, q, r; and this series cannot therefore be regarded as given, but only as *dabilis* [capable of being given].",["- Divided the long \"Es sey die Reihe ...\" sentence into several. \"so muß ich\" resumes after the conditional \"Es sey ... so\", which I rendered as \"Let ... Then\".","- Kant's \"i\" in \"l, k, i\" is retained as in the text (probably for \"h\"); flagged as possibly a misprint.","- \"können\" after \"die ... angesehen werden könne\" is as printed (singular/plural slip); smoothed. \"dabilis\" kept in Latin, italicised, with English supplied in brackets.","- \"bestimbar\" = \"determinable\" (Bestimmung)."]],"krv-a-0692":["I shall entitle the synthesis of a series on the side of the conditions, that is, proceeding from the condition nearest to the given appearance and so on to the more remote conditions, the *regressive* synthesis; and that which proceeds on the side of the conditioned, from the nearest consequence to the more remote, the *progressive* synthesis. The former proceeds *in antecedentia*, the second *in consequentia*. The cosmological ideas, therefore, are occupied with the totality of the regressive synthesis, and proceed *in antecedentia*, not *in consequentia*. When the latter occurs, it is an arbitrary and not a necessary problem of pure reason; for in order completely to comprehend what is given in the appearance we need grounds, but not consequences.",["- \"regressive\" and \"progressive\" italicised as terms being defined; this emphasis is mine (no source emphasis is recoverable).","- \"Erscheinung\" = \"appearance\"; \"Begreiflichkeit\" = \"comprehension\"/\"comprehensibility\", rendered by the verb.","- The long first sentence is kept in one, with a gloss \"that is\" for the clarifying phrase; \"der Gründe ... bedürfen\" = \"need grounds\"."]],"krv-a-0693":["In order to construct the table of the Ideas in accordance with the table of the categories, we take first the two original quanta of all our intuition, time and space. Time is in itself a series (and the formal condition of all series). Hence in it, in respect of a given present, the *antecedentia*, as conditions (that is, the past), have to be distinguished *a priori* from the *consequentia* (the future). Consequently the transcendental idea of the absolute totality of the series of conditions for a given conditioned relates only to all past time. In accordance with the idea of reason, the whole of the time that has elapsed is necessarily thought as given, as the condition of the given moment.\n\nAs regards space, however, there is in it, in itself, no distinction between progress and regress, since it constitutes an aggregate, not a series, its parts all existing together [zugleich]. In respect of past time I could regard the present moment only as conditioned, never as the condition of that time, since this moment arises only through the time that has elapsed (or rather through the elapsing of the preceding time). But the parts of space are not subordinated to one another, but co-ordinated; one part is therefore not the condition of the possibility of another, and space, unlike time, does not in itself constitute a series.\n\nNevertheless the synthesis of the manifold parts of space, by which we apprehend it, is successive, takes place therefore in time, and contains a series. In this series of aggregated spaces (for instance, the feet in a rod), starting from a given space, those that are thought as added further are always the condition of the limit of the preceding. The measuring of a space is thus also to be regarded as a synthesis of a series of conditions for a given conditioned. The only difference is that the side of the conditions is not in itself distinguished from the side on which the conditioned lies; and regress and progress in space therefore seem to be one and the same.\n\nNow a part of space is not given through another, but only limited by it. We must therefore regard every limited space as to that extent also conditioned, in that it presupposes another space as the condition of its limit, and so on. In respect of limitation, therefore, advance in space is also a regress; and the transcendental idea of the absolute totality of the synthesis in the series of conditions applies to space also. I can ask as well for the absolute totality of appearance in space as for that in the time that has elapsed. Whether an answer to this question is anywhere possible will have to be determined later.",["- Restated the long opening and the middle sentences as several; the \"Und da … so ist\" construction (irregular in the German, with \"seyn\" for \"sei\") is smoothed.","- \"zugleich seyn\" rendered \"existing together\"; \"die weiter hinzugedachte\" taken as plural \"those … added further\", agreeing with the \"Räume\".","- \"Ob aber überall darauf\" taken as \"whether an answer to this question is anywhere possible\"; \"darauf\" refers to the question of totality.","- *antecedentia*/*consequentibus* kept in Latin; the dative *consequentibus* is rendered by the nominative *consequentia* in the English."]],"krv-a-0694":["Secondly, reality in space, that is, matter, is a conditioned whose inner conditions are its parts, and the parts of the parts its remote conditions. A regressive synthesis therefore takes place here, the absolute totality of which reason demands. This can occur only through a completed division, whereby the reality of matter vanishes either into nothing or at least into that which is no longer matter, namely the simple. Here, too, consequently, there is a series of conditions and an advance to the unconditioned.",["- Divided one sentence into three; \"welche\" is taken to refer to the totality demanded, which can be achieved only through complete division."]],"krv-a-0695":["Thirdly, as regards the categories of real relation among appearances, the category of substance with its accidents is not suited to a transcendental idea; that is, reason has no ground, in respect of it, for proceeding regressively to conditions. For accidents (so far as they inhere in one single substance) are co-ordinated with one another, and do not constitute a series. In respect of the substance they are not, strictly speaking, subordinated to it, but are the mode of existence of the substance itself.\n\nWhat might still here seem to be an idea of transcendental reason would be the concept of the substantial [*Substantiale*]. But this signifies nothing other than the concept of an object in general which subsists, in so far as we think in it merely the transcendental subject without any predicates; and since we are here concerned only with the unconditioned in the series of appearances, it is clear that the substantial cannot constitute a member of that series.\n\nThe same holds of substances in community. These are mere aggregates and have no exponent of a series, since they are not subordinated to one another as conditions of their possibility. Of spaces one could indeed say this, for their limit was never determined through itself but always through another space. There remains, then, only the category of causality, which presents a series of causes for a given effect. In this series we can ascend from the latter, as the conditioned, to the former as conditions, and so answer the question of reason.",["- Divided the passage into paragraphs and shorter sentences; \"ienen\" = the causes, \"der lezteren\" = the effect.","- \"Exponent\" kept for *Exponent*; \"Gemeinschaft\" rendered \"community\".","- \"Allein, da dieses … so ist klar\" recast with the main clause preceded by \"and since\"."]],"krv-a-0696":["Fourthly, the concepts of the possible, the actual and the necessary lead to no series, save in so far as the contingent in existence must always be regarded as conditioned, and, in accordance with the rule of the understanding, points to a condition under which it is necessary that this in turn point to a higher condition, until reason finds unconditioned necessity only in the totality of this series.",["- Kept as a single sentence; \"darunter\" taken as \"under which\" (the condition)."]],"krv-a-0697":["There are accordingly no more than four cosmological ideas, corresponding to the four headings of the categories, if we pick out those which necessarily carry with them a series in the synthesis of the manifold.",[]],"krv-a-0698":["1. The absolute completeness of the composition of the given whole of all appearances.",[]],"krv-a-0699":["2. The absolute completeness of the division of a given whole in the appearance.",[]],"krv-a-0700":["3. The absolute completeness of the origination of an appearance in general.",[]],"krv-a-0701":["4. The absolute completeness of the dependence of the existence of the alterable in the appearance.",[]],"krv-a-0702":["We have first to remark that the idea of absolute totality concerns nothing other than the exposition of appearances, and therefore not the pure concept of the understanding of a whole of things in general. Appearances are here, then, considered as given, and reason demands the absolute completeness of the conditions of their possibility, in so far as these constitute a series. It demands, that is, a synthesis which is complete absolutely (that is, in every respect), and through which the appearance can be expounded in accordance with the laws of the understanding.",["- \"Exposition\"/\"exponirt\" rendered \"exposition\"/\"expounded\", following the Latin *expositio*.","- Divided the second sentence in two; \"mithin\" is rendered \"that is\" in the second part."]],"krv-a-0703":["In the second place, what reason really seeks in this synthesis of conditions, continued serially and indeed regressively, is only the unconditioned. It seeks, as it were, completeness in the series of premisses, such that together they presuppose no further premiss. This unconditioned is always contained in the absolute totality of the series, when we represent that totality to ourselves in imagination. But this absolutely completed synthesis is again only an idea; for we cannot know, at least in advance, whether such a synthesis is possible in the case of appearances.\n\nIf we represent everything through mere pure concepts of the understanding, without conditions of sensible intuition, we can say outright that, when a conditioned is given, the whole series of conditions subordinated to one another is also given; for the conditioned is given only through this series. In the case of appearances, however, we find a special limitation in the manner in which conditions are given. They are given through the successive synthesis of the manifold of intuition, and this synthesis is to be completed in the regress. Whether this completeness is sensibly possible is still a problem. But the idea of this completeness lies none the less in reason, irrespective of the possibility or impossibility of connecting with it empirical concepts that are adequate to it.\n\nSince, then, the unconditioned is necessarily contained in the absolute totality of the regressive synthesis of the manifold in the appearance (a synthesis that, under the guidance of the categories, represents the appearance as a series of conditions for a given conditioned), and since this holds even if we leave undecided whether and how this totality can be brought about, reason here takes the course of starting from the idea of totality, although what it really has as its final aim is the unconditioned, whether of the whole series or of a part of it.\n\n===NOTES===\n- Restated the long opening and final sentences as several; the final \"Also, da … so nimt\" is recast as \"Since … and since … reason here takes the course\".\n- \"in der Einbildung\" rendered \"in imagination\"; \"Synthesis\" and \"Regressus\" kept as in Kemp Smith's usage (\"regress\").\n- \"ihr adäquat empirische Begriffe zu verknüpfen\": I take \"ihr\" to refer to the idea, hence \"connecting with it empirical concepts adequate to it\".\n- \"Reihe der Prämissen\": \"premisses\" (British spelling).\n- \"Bedingtes\"/\"Unbedingte\" = conditioned/unconditioned; \"Erscheinung\" = appearance.",[]],"krv-a-0704":["This unconditioned may be conceived in two ways. Either it consists solely in the whole series, in which all the members without exception would be conditioned and only the whole of them would be absolutely unconditioned; in that case the regress is entitled infinite. Or the absolutely unconditioned is only a part of the series, to which the remaining members are subordinated, while it itself stands under no other condition.*)\n\nIn the former case the series is *a parte priori* without limits (without beginning), that is, infinite, and yet is given as a whole; but the regress in it is never completed, and can be entitled infinite only *potentialiter*. In the second case there is a first member of the series. In respect of past time this is entitled the beginning of the world; in respect of space, the limit of the world; in respect of the parts of a whole given within its limits, the simple; in respect of causes, absolute self-activity (freedom); and in respect of the existence of alterable things, absolute natural necessity.\n\n===NOTES===\n- Divided the German's two long sentences into four; the asterisk marks the footnote (krv-a-0705) at the original position.\n- \"denn heißt der Regressus unendlich\" rendered \"in that case the regress is entitled infinite\", per the heißen convention.\n- \"Selbstthätigkeit\" = self-activity; \"Naturnothwendigkeit\" = natural necessity.\n- \"Dasein\" = existence.",[]],"krv-a-0705":["*) The absolute whole of the series of conditions for a given conditioned is always unconditioned; for outside it there are no further conditions in respect of which it could be conditioned. But this absolute whole of such a series is only an idea, or rather a problematic concept, the possibility of which has to be investigated, and that in relation to the manner in which the unconditioned, as the properly transcendental idea with which we are concerned, may be contained in it.\n\n===NOTES===\n- Original \"untersucht werde muß\" is a misprint for \"werden muß\"; silently corrected.\n- \"worauf es ankomt\" rendered \"with which we are concerned\".",[]],"krv-a-0706":["We have two expressions, *world* and *nature*, which at times run into each other. The first signifies the mathematical whole of all appearances and the totality of their synthesis, alike in the great and in the small, that is, alike in the advance of that synthesis through composition and through division. The same world, however, is entitled nature*) when it is regarded as a dynamical whole, and when we attend not to the aggregation in space or time, with a view to bringing it about as a quantity, but to the unity in the existence of appearances.\n\nIn this latter regard the condition of that which happens is entitled the cause; the unconditioned causality of the cause in the appearance is entitled freedom; the conditioned causality, on the other hand, is entitled, in the narrower sense, natural cause. The conditioned in existence in general is entitled contingent, and the unconditioned necessary. The unconditioned necessity of appearances may be entitled natural necessity.\n\n===NOTES===\n- Divided into two paragraphs; \"Da heißt nun\" rendered \"In this latter regard\" to make the reference plain.\n- \"Grösse\" = quantity; \"im engeren Verstande\" = \"in the narrower sense\".\n- \"zufällig\" = contingent.\n- Asterisk marks footnote krv-a-0707.",[]],"krv-a-0707":["*) Nature, taken adjectivally (*formaliter*), signifies the connection of the determinations of a thing according to an inner principle of causality. By nature taken substantively (*materialiter*), on the other hand, is understood the sum of appearances, in so far as these, by virtue of an inner principle of causality, are thoroughly interconnected. In the former sense we speak of the nature of fluid matter, of fire, etc., and use the word only adjectivally; whereas when we speak of the things of nature, we have in mind a self-subsistent whole.\n\n===NOTES===\n- Latin *adiectiue*/*substantiue* rendered \"adjectivally\"/\"substantively\"; *formaliter*/*materialiter* kept in italics.\n- \"Bestimmungen\" = determinations; \"bestehendes Ganze\" = \"self-subsistent whole\" (a choice; \"persisting whole\" possible).",[]],"krv-a-0708":["The ideas with which we are now occupied I have above entitled cosmological ideas. I did so partly because by *world* is understood the sum of all appearances, and our ideas are directed solely to the unconditioned among appearances; partly also because the word *world*, in the transcendental sense, signifies the absolute totality of the sum of existing things, and we direct our attention solely to the completeness of the synthesis (though, strictly, only in the regress to the conditions).\n\nBesides, all these ideas are transcendent. Although in kind they do not overstep the object, namely appearances, but have to do solely with the world of sense (not with noumena), they yet carry the synthesis to a degree which surpasses all possible experience. In view of this, I think we may quite fittingly entitle them, one and all, concepts of the world [*Weltbegriffe*]. In view, however, of the distinction between the mathematically and the dynamically unconditioned at which the regress aims, I should entitle the first two concepts of the world in the narrower meaning (of the world in the great and in the small), and the remaining two transcendent concepts of nature. This distinction is for the present of no particular importance, but it may become more important in the sequel.\n\n===NOTES===\n- Divided the long second sentence; \"In Betracht dessen, daß überdem …\" restated as \"Besides … In view of this\".\n- \"Noümenis\" rendered \"noumena\".\n- \"Weltbegriffe\" supplied in brackets because the argument turns on the term.\n- \"Verstand\" in \"transscendentalen Verstande\" and \"engerer Bedeutung\" rendered \"sense\"/\"meaning\", not \"understanding\".",[]],"krv-a-0709":["Second Section of the Antinomy of Pure Reason. The Antithetic of Pure Reason.\n\n===NOTES===\n- Heading only; \"Antithetik\" rendered \"Antithetic\".",[]],"krv-a-0710":["If thetic be the name for any body of dogmatic doctrines, I understand by antithetic not dogmatic assertions of the opposite, but the conflict of modes of knowledge which are dogmatic in seeming only (*thesin cum antithesi*), without our ascribing to one of them a better claim to approval than to the other. The antithetic is thus not concerned with one-sided assertions at all. It considers universal modes of knowledge of reason only in respect of their conflict with one another and of the causes of that conflict.\n\nThe transcendental antithetic is an investigation of the antinomy of pure reason, its causes and its outcome. If we employ our reason not merely, in applying the principles of the understanding, to objects of experience, but venture to extend it beyond the limits of experience, there arise sophistical [*vernünftelnde*] propositions which can neither hope for confirmation in experience nor fear refutation by it. Each of them is not only free from contradiction in itself, but even finds in the nature of reason conditions of its necessity; only that, unhappily, the opposite proposition has on its side grounds of assertion equally valid and necessary.\n\n===NOTES===\n- \"Erkentnisse\" here plural: \"modes of knowledge\" (Kemp Smith's convention).\n- Divided into two paragraphs and the final sentence lightly recast; \"Thetik\" rendered \"thetic\" with \"if … be the name\" for the conditional.\n- \"vernünftelnde\" = sophistical (choice; \"pseudo-rational\" possible).\n- \"Verstandesgrundsätze\" = principles of the understanding.",[]],"krv-a-0711":["The questions which naturally present themselves in connection with such a dialectic of pure reason are therefore these: (1) In what propositions, precisely, is pure reason inevitably subject to an antinomy? (2) On what causes does this antinomy rest? (3) Whether, and in what manner, there still remains open to reason, in face of this contradiction, a path to certainty.\n\n===NOTES===\n- Enumeration with numerals retained as in the German; \"Gewißheit\" = certainty.\n- The third question's indirect-question form (\"Ob …\") is kept, unlike the first two, as in the German.",[]],"krv-a-0712":["A dialectical proposition of pure reason must therefore have these two features, which distinguish it from all sophistical propositions. First, it must not concern an arbitrary question, raised only for some purpose of one's own choosing, but a question which every human reason must necessarily encounter in the course of its advance. Secondly, it must carry with it, together with its opposite, not a merely artificial illusion, which vanishes at once when it is seen through, but a natural and unavoidable illusion. This illusion, even when we are no longer deceived by it, still continues to delude us, though it does not defraud us; and it can therefore be rendered harmless, but can never be eradicated.",[]],"krv-a-0713":["Such a dialectical doctrine will relate not to the unity of the understanding in concepts of experience, but to the unity of reason in mere ideas. The conditions of this unity must, in the first place, as a synthesis according to rules, be congruent with the understanding, and yet at the same time, as the absolute unity of that synthesis, with reason. If these conditions are adequate to the unity of reason, they will be too great for the understanding; if they are suited to the understanding, they will be too small for reason. A conflict must thus arise which cannot be avoided, however we may set about it.",[]],"krv-a-0714":["These sophistical assertions accordingly open up a dialectical arena, in which the side that is permitted to make the attack always prevails, and the side that is compelled to act merely on the defensive is certain to be defeated. Hence doughty knights, whether they champion the good cause or the bad, are sure of carrying off the laurels, if only they see to it that they have the privilege of making the last attack, and are not obliged to withstand a fresh onslaught from their opponent. We can readily imagine that this arena has from of old been entered often enough, that many victories have been won on both sides, but that, as regards the last victory, the one which decided the matter, care has always been taken that the champion of the good cause should remain master of the field alone, his opponent being forbidden to take up arms any further. As impartial umpires we must set quite aside whether it is the good or the bad cause for which the combatants are fighting, and let them first settle the matter between themselves. Perhaps, when they have wearied one another rather than done each other harm, they will themselves perceive the nothingness of their quarrel, and part as good friends.",[]],"krv-a-0715":["This method of watching, or rather of itself occasioning, a conflict of assertions is adopted not in order to decide finally in favour of one side or the other, but in order to investigate whether the object of the dispute may not perhaps be a mere illusion [*Blendwerk*], which each vainly strives to grasp, and from which, even if there were no resistance at all, he could gain nothing. This procedure, I say, may be entitled the sceptical method. It is wholly different from scepticism, a principle of artful and scientific ignorance, which undermines the foundations of all knowledge, and seeks, where possible, to leave no reliability or certainty anywhere. For the sceptical method aims at certainty. It seeks to discover the point of misunderstanding in a dispute of this kind, honestly meant on both sides and conducted with understanding. In this it acts as wise legislators do, who from the embarrassment of judges in lawsuits learn for themselves what is defective and not precisely determined in their laws. The antinomy which reveals itself in the application of laws is, in view of our limited wisdom, the best test of nomothetic. By it reason, which in abstract speculation does not easily become aware of its false steps, is made attentive to the moments to be considered in the determination of its principles.",[]],"krv-a-0716":["This sceptical method is, however, essentially proper to transcendental philosophy alone, and can at a pinch be dispensed with in any other field of enquiry, though not in this. In mathematics its employment would be absurd, since no false assertions can there conceal themselves and make themselves invisible; the proofs must always proceed along the thread of pure intuition, and by way of a synthesis that is always evident. In experimental philosophy a doubt that involves postponement of judgment may well be useful, but at least no misunderstanding is possible which cannot easily be removed; and in experience the ultimate means of deciding the dispute must in the end lie, whether they be found early or late. Morals can give its principles, one and all, in concreto, together with their practical consequences, at least in possible experiences, and so avoid the misunderstanding due to abstraction. Transcendental assertions, on the other hand, which lay claim to insights extending even beyond the field of all possible experience, are neither in a position such that their abstract synthesis could be given in any intuition *a priori*, nor so constituted that the misunderstanding could be discovered by means of any experience. Transcendental reason therefore admits no other touchstone than the attempt to unite its assertions with one another, and so first of all to allow them a free and unhindered contest among themselves. This contest we shall now proceed to institute.*",[]],"krv-a-0717":["* The antinomies follow one another in the order of the transcendental ideas enumerated above.",[]],"krv-a-0718":["The Antinomy of Pure Reason.",[]],"krv-a-0719":["First Conflict of the Transcendental Ideas.",[]],"krv-a-0720":["Thesis.",[]],"krv-a-0721":["The world has a beginning in time, and is also enclosed within limits as regards space.",[]],"krv-a-0722":["Proof.",[]],"krv-a-0723":["Let us assume that the world has no beginning in time. Then up to every given point of time an eternity has elapsed, and there has passed away in the world an infinite series of successive states of things. Now the infinity of a series consists precisely in this, that it can never be completed through successive synthesis. Thus an infinite elapsed world-series is impossible, and a beginning of the world is therefore a necessary condition of its existence. That was the first point to be proved.",[]],"krv-a-0724":["As regards the second point, let us again assume the opposite. The world will then be an infinite given whole of simultaneously existing things. Now the magnitude of a quantum which is not given as within certain limits of every intuition* we can think in no other way than through the synthesis of its parts, and the totality of such a quantum only through the completed synthesis, that is, through repeated addition of unity to itself.** Accordingly, in order to think, as a whole, the world which fills all spaces, the successive synthesis of the parts of an infinite world would have to be regarded as completed. That is, an infinite time would have to be regarded as having elapsed in the enumeration of all coexisting things; and this is impossible. Consequently an infinite aggregate of actual things cannot be regarded as a given whole, nor therefore as given simultaneously. A world is accordingly not infinite as regards extension in space, but is enclosed within limits. That was the second point.",[]],"krv-a-0725":["* We can intuit an indeterminate quantum as a whole, if it is enclosed within limits, without having to construct its totality through measurement, that is, through the successive synthesis of its parts. For the limits already determine its completeness, in that they cut off everything further.",[]],"krv-a-0726":["** The concept of totality is in this case nothing but the representation of the completed synthesis of its parts. For since we cannot derive the concept from the intuition of the whole (which in this case is impossible), we can grasp it only through the synthesis of the parts, up to the completion of the infinite, at least in idea.",[]],"krv-a-0727":["Antithesis.",[]],"krv-a-0728":["The world has no beginning and no limits in space, but is infinite, alike in respect of time and of space.",[]],"krv-a-0729":["Proof.",["- 0712: split the long second clause into separate sentences; \"Schein\" rendered \"illusion\", \"hintergangen/täuscht/betrügt\" as \"deceived / delude / defraud\".","- 0713: restated the tangled relative clause \"deren Bedingungen…\"; \"sie\" resolved as the conditions, \"derselben\" as the synthesis.","- 0714: \"vor\" in \"vor die gute… Sache\" read as \"für\"; \"Kampfrichter\" given as \"umpires\".","- 0715: \"Blendwerk\" glossed; \"Nomothetik\" rendered \"nomothetic\"; the long sentence was divided and the \"um… zu ziehen\" clause attached to the legislators.","- 0716: the asterisk after \"anstellen\" is Kant's footnote marker (see 0717); \"Zwist/Streit\" both \"dispute\". \"Zweifel des Aufschubs\" rendered \"doubt that involves postponement of judgment\".","- 0724: \"Quanti\" rendered \"quantum\"; the asterisks mark footnotes 0725 and 0726; \"gedenken\" taken as \"denken\".","- 0725/0726: \"Totalität\" kept as \"totality\". 0729 (\"Proof.\") is a heading only; the text breaks off."]],"krv-a-0730":["For suppose that the world has a beginning. Since a beginning is an existence preceded by a time in which the thing is not, there must have been a preceding time in which the world was not, that is, an empty time. Now in an empty time no coming into being of anything is possible; for no part of such a time has, as compared with any other, a distinguishing condition of existence rather than of non-existence (and this holds whether the thing be supposed to arise of itself or through some other cause). Hence, though many a series of things may begin in the world, the world itself can have no beginning, and is therefore infinite in respect of past time.",["- Restated the long second sentence with a semicolon and kept the parenthesis, which Kant places after \"Nichtseyns an sich\"; \"an sich\" is not rendered separately (it is idiomatic here, \"as compared with\").","- \"vor einem anderen\" / \"vor die des Nichtseyns\": \"vor\" is comparative (\"rather than\"); the sense is settled by the argument.","- \"Daseyn\" = existence; \"Entstehen\" = coming into being.","- \"Also kan zwar … anfangen\": \"Hence\" supplied for the inference."]],"krv-a-0731":["As to the second point, let us first assume the opposite, namely that the world is finite and bounded in space. It then finds itself in an empty space which is not bounded. There would therefore be met with not only a relation of things in space to one another, but also a relation of things to space. Now the world is an absolute whole, outside which there is to be found no object of intuition, and therefore no correlate of the world with which it could stand in relation. The relation of the world to empty space would therefore be a relation of the world to no object. But such a relation, and consequently also the limitation of the world by empty space, is nothing. The world, therefore, is not limited at all in respect of space; that is, it is infinite in respect of extension.*",["- The footnote marker *) is rendered as * and refers to the note in krv-a-0732.","- Divided the long sentence beginning \"Da nun die Welt…\" into two; \"so würde\" resumes the argument.","- \"Ausdehnung\" = extension; \"Correlatum\" retained as \"correlate\" (Kemp Smith's usage).","- \"es befindet sich\": \"es\" resolved as the world."]],"krv-a-0732":["*Space is merely the form of outer intuition (formal intuition); it is not a real object which can be outwardly intuited. Space, prior to all the things which determine it (fill or bound it), or which rather give an empirical intuition conforming to its form, is, under the name of absolute space, nothing but the mere possibility of outer appearances, in so far as they either exist in themselves or can be added to given appearances. Empirical intuition is not, then, composed of appearances and space (of perception and empty intuition). The one is not the correlate of the other in a synthesis; they are connected in one and the same empirical intuition, as its matter and form. If we seek to posit one of these two constituents apart from the other (space outside all appearances), there arise all sorts of empty determinations of outer intuition, which yet are not possible perceptions: for example, motion or rest of the world in infinite empty space, a determination of the relation of the two to each other which can never be perceived, and is therefore the predicate of a mere thought-entity [*Gedankending*].",["- \"Naumes\" in the source is an OCR/print slip for \"Raumes\"; silently corrected to \"space\".","- \"vor allen Dingen\" taken as \"prior to all things\" (not \"above all\"), the sense required by the argument.","- \"beider\" in the last sentence read as the world and empty space.","- Restated the final clause \"eine Bestimmung…\" as an appositive; kept *Gedankending* in German in brackets since the term turns on it.","- The text of the footnote is rendered without the closing asterisk as the passage begins with it."]],"krv-a-0733":["Remark on the First Antinomy. I. On the Thesis",["- Heading only."]],"krv-a-0734":["In these conflicting arguments I have not sought to produce illusions in order to conduct what is called a lawyer's proof, which takes advantage of the opponent's carelessness and gladly lets his appeal to a misunderstood law stand, so as to build its own unjust claims upon its refutation. Each of these proofs is drawn from the nature of the matter, and the advantage which the fallacies of the dogmatists on both sides might give us has been set aside.",["- \"Blendwercke\" = illusions (deceptive shows); \"Advocatenbeweis\" = lawyer's proof.","- \"auf die Widerlegung desselben\" : \"desselben\" refers to the misunderstood law (the appeal to it); rendered \"its refutation\" for the appeal to the law.","- \"Vortheil bey Seite gesezt\" = \"set aside\" the advantage."]],"krv-a-0735":["I could also have proved the thesis, in appearance, by prefacing it, after the custom of the dogmatists, with a faulty concept of the infinity of a given magnitude. A magnitude is infinite if none greater than it (that is, greater than the multiplicity of a given unit contained in it) is possible. Now no multiplicity is the greatest, since one or more units can always still be added. Hence an infinite given magnitude, and consequently an infinite world (infinite alike in respect of the elapsed series and of extension), is impossible; it is therefore limited on both sides. That is how I could have conducted my proof. But this concept does not agree with what is understood by an infinite whole. It does not represent how great the whole is, and its concept is therefore not the concept of a maximum; it thinks only the whole's relation to a unit which may be assumed at will, and in respect of which it is greater than all number. According as the unit is assumed greater or smaller, the infinite would be greater or smaller; but infinity, since it consists merely in the relation to this given unit, would always remain the same. The absolute magnitude of the whole would, of course, not thereby be known; but of that there is here no question.",["- \"Menge\" = multiplicity (a number of units); \"Maximum\" kept as Kant's Latin.","- \"Nachdem … angenommen wird\" rendered \"according as\"; sentence divided at \"So hätte ich…\".","- \"beiderseitig begränzt\" = \"limited on both sides\" (in time and space).","- Supplied \"of course\" for \"freilich\"; \"davon\" = of that."]],"krv-a-0736":["II. Remark on the Antithesis.",["- Heading only."]],"krv-a-0737":["The proof of the infinity of the given world-series and of the world-whole rests on this: that in the opposite case an empty time, and likewise an empty space, would have to constitute the limit of the world. Now I am not unaware that evasions of this consequence are sought, by pretending that a limit of the world in respect of time and space is quite possible without our having to assume an absolute time before the beginning of the world, or an absolute space extended beyond the actual world; and this is impossible. With the latter part of this opinion of the philosophers of the Leibnizian school I am entirely content. Space is merely the form of outer intuition; it is not a real object which can be outwardly intuited, nor a correlate of appearances, but the form of appearances themselves. Space, therefore, cannot occur absolutely (by itself alone) as something determining in the existence of things, since it is not an object at all, but only the form of possible objects. Things, therefore, as appearances, do determine space; that is, among all its possible predicates (magnitude and relation) they bring it about that these or those belong to actuality. But conversely space, as something subsisting by itself, cannot determine the actuality of things in respect of magnitude or shape, since in itself it is nothing actual. A space (be it full or empty*) may therefore well be bounded by appearances, but appearances cannot be bounded by an empty space outside them. The same holds of time. Granting all this, it is nevertheless indisputable that these two non-entities [*Undinge*], the empty space outside and the empty time before the world, must necessarily be assumed if a limit of the world, whether in space or in time, is assumed.",["- Restated the sentence on evasions; \"welches unmöglich ist\" refers to assuming absolute time/space, made explicit by a semicolon.","- \"Bestimmung\" = determination; \"bestimmen\" = determine. \"Verhältniß\" = relation.","- \"Undinge\" = non-entities, German given in brackets because the term turns on it.","- \"Weltinbegriff\" = world-whole; \"Weltgränze\" = limit of the world.","- \"vor sich\" rendered \"by itself\"."]],"krv-a-0738":["*It is easily seen that what is meant is this: empty space, so far as it is bounded by appearances, and so the empty space within the world, does not contradict, at least, the transcendental principles, and can therefore, in respect of these, be admitted (though its possibility is not on that account straightway asserted).",["- \"derienige\" refers to the space (\"der leere Raum\"), resolved as \"the empty space within the world\".","- \"widerspreche … und können\": number of verbs varies in the German; rendered as one clause."]],"krv-a-0739":["The true (transcendental) concept of infinity is that the successive synthesis of the unit in the measuring of a quantum can never be completed.* From this it follows quite certainly that an eternity of actual successive states, up to a given (the present) point of time, cannot have elapsed, and that the world must therefore have a beginning.",["- \"Durchmessung\" = measuring through (traversing); \"Quantum\" retained as Kant's Latin.","- \"Synthesis der Einheit\" literally \"of the unit\"; kept literal."]],"krv-a-0740":["*This thereby contains a multiplicity (of a given unit) greater than all number, which is the mathematical concept of the infinite.",["- \"Dieses\" is ambiguous (the quantum or the concept of infinity); rendered \"This\" as in the German, probably referring to the quantum."]],"krv-a-0741":["As regards the second part of the thesis, the difficulty of an infinite series which has nevertheless run its course does indeed disappear; for the manifold of a world infinite in extension is given all at once. But in order to think the totality of such a multitude, since we cannot appeal to limits which would of themselves constitute this totality in intuition, we must render an account of our concept. In such a case the concept cannot proceed from the whole to the determinate multitude of the parts; it must demonstrate the possibility of a whole through the successive synthesis of the parts. Now since this synthesis would have to constitute a series that can never be completed, we cannot think a totality prior to it, and therefore cannot think one by means of it either. For the concept of totality is itself, in this case, the representation of a completed synthesis of the parts; and this completion, and consequently the concept of it, is impossible.",["- Restated the long second sentence as three; \"Denn\" at the end kept as \"For\".","- \"zugleich gegeben\" rendered \"given all at once\".","- \"bestimten\" (sic) read as \"bestimmten\", hence \"determinate\"; \"kan\" is the old spelling of \"kann\"."]],"krv-a-0742":["As for the way of escape by which men seek to evade the consequence, namely that, if the world has limits (in time and space), the infinite void must determine the magnitude of the existence of actual things, it consists secretly in nothing else than this. Instead of a sensible world, they think, who knows what, an intelligible world; instead of a first beginning (an existence preceded by a time of non-existence), they think an existence in general which presupposes no other condition in the world; instead of limits of extension, they think bounds [*Schranken*] of the world-whole; and so they evade time and space. But here we are concerned only with the *mundus phaenomenon* and its magnitude, and from the conditions of sensibility, which have been mentioned, we cannot abstract without destroying its very essence. The sensible world, if it is bounded, necessarily lies in the infinite void. If we choose to omit this, and with it space in general as an *a priori* condition of the possibility of appearances, the whole sensible world vanishes. In our problem this world alone is given to us. The *mundus intelligibilis* is nothing but the universal concept of a world in general, in which we abstract from all conditions of intuition of it, and in regard to which, therefore, no synthetic proposition, whether affirmative or negative, is possible.",["- The opening \"Denn was ... betrift\" is a long anacoluthic sentence; I divided it and supplied \"men\" as the subject of \"sucht\".","- \"gedachten Bedingungen\" rendered \"conditions ... which have been mentioned\" (i.e. the conditions of sensibility just spoken of); a doubtful but plausible reading.","- *Gränze* / *Schranken* both rendered \"limits\"/\"bounds\" to keep the distinction.","- Latin terms kept in italics."]],"krv-a-0743":["Of the Antinomy of Pure Reason",["- Heading. Genitive left as in the German; the Academy line is a fragment of the title."]],"krv-a-0744":["Second Conflict of the Transcendental Ideas.",["- Heading continuing the previous line."]],"krv-a-0745":["Thesis.",[]],"krv-a-0746":["Every composite substance in the world consists of simple parts, and there exists nowhere anything but the simple, or what is composed of the simple.",["- \"überall nichts\" rendered \"nowhere anything but\"."]],"krv-a-0747":["Proof.",[]],"krv-a-0748":["For suppose that composite substances did not consist of simple parts. If all composition were then removed in thought, no composite part would remain, and (since there are no simple parts) no simple part either; nothing, therefore, would be left over, and consequently no substance would have been given. Either, then, it is impossible to remove all composition in thought, or after its removal something must remain which subsists without any composition, that is, the simple. In the former case the composite would again not consist of substances; for in the case of substances composition is only a contingent relation of substances, apart from which they must subsist as beings persisting by themselves. Since this case contradicts the supposition, only the second remains: namely, that the substantial composite in the world consists of simple parts.",["- \"nehmet an\" rendered \"suppose\"; the parenthesis about composition is kept in a semicolon clause for readability.","- \"beständen\" subjunctive rendered \"did not consist\"."]],"krv-a-0749":["Antithesis.",[]],"krv-a-0750":["No composite thing in the world consists of simple parts, and there exists nowhere in the world anything simple.",[]],"krv-a-0751":["Proof.",[]],"krv-a-0752":["Suppose that a composite thing (as substance) consists of simple parts. Since all external relation, and therefore all composition of substances, is possible only in space, the space occupied by the composite must consist of as many parts as the composite itself. Now space does not consist of simple parts, but of spaces. Every part of the composite must therefore occupy a space. But the absolutely first parts of everything composite are simple. The simple, therefore, occupies a space. Now everything real which occupies a space contains in itself a manifold of elements external to one another, and is therefore composite; and it is composite as a real composite, not of accidents (for these cannot be external to one another without substance), and so of substances. The simple would thus be a substantial composite, which is self-contradictory.",["- Restated \"Da nun ... so würde\" as two sentences, splitting before the conclusion; the construction otherwise follows the German.","- \"ausserhalb einander befindliches Mannigfaltige\" rendered \"manifold of elements external to one another\"; \"elements\" is supplied."]],"krv-a-0753":["The second proposition of the antithesis, that nothing simple exists anywhere in the world, is here intended to mean only this: the existence of the absolutely simple cannot be established from any experience or perception, whether outer or inner; the absolutely simple is therefore a mere idea, whose objective reality can never be established in any possible experience, and which is therefore, in the exposition of appearances, without application and without object. For let us assume that an object of experience could be found for this transcendental idea. The empirical intuition of some object would then have to be known as one which contains absolutely no manifold external to one another and combined into unity. Now from our not being conscious of a manifold no inference holds to its complete impossibility in some intuition of the same object; yet this latter is quite indispensable to absolute simplicity. It follows that simplicity cannot be inferred from any perception whatsoever. Since, then, something as an absolutely simple object can never be given in any possible experience, and the sensible world must be regarded as the sum [*Inbegriff*] of all possible experiences, nothing simple is given in it anywhere.",["- Divided the long sentence beginning \"Denn wir wollen annehmen\" into several.","- In \"ein solches in irgend einer Anschauung\" I took \"ein solches\" as \"such a manifold\"; \"dieses leztere\" = the impossibility of a manifold in any intuition.","- \"Obiect\" and \"Obiects\" regularised to \"object\".","- \"Exposition\" kept as \"exposition\"."]],"krv-a-0754":["From this it follows immediately that the things of the world are one and all simple beings, that composition is only an external state of them, and that, although we can never completely remove the elementary substances from this state of combination and isolate them, reason must nevertheless think them as the first subjects of all composition, and therefore, prior to it, as simple beings.",["- This paragraph is part of the Thesis remark in the text as printed; it is translated as it stands. \"gleich\" rendered concessively, \"although\".","- \"insgesamt\" rendered \"one and all\"."]],"krv-a-0755":["This second proposition of the antithesis goes much further than the first, which banishes the simple only from the intuition of the composite, whereas this one removes it from the whole of nature. Hence it also could not be proved from the concept of a given object of outer intuition (the composite), but only from the relation of that object to a possible experience in general.",["- Divided at \"daher\"; \"dieser\" is the second proposition, \"es\" the simple. \"verbant\" is for \"verbannt\".","- \"hat bewiesen werden können\" rendered \"could be proved\"."]],"krv-a-0756":["Remark on the Second Antinomy. I. On the Thesis.",["- Heading. \"I. zur Thesis\" rendered \"I. On the Thesis\"."]],"krv-a-0757":["When I speak of a whole which necessarily consists of simple parts, I understand by it only a substantial whole, as the proper *compositum*; that is, that contingent unity of the manifold which, being given as separate (at least in thought), is placed in reciprocal connection, and so constitutes a unity. Space ought properly to be entitled not a *compositum* but a *totum*, since its parts are possible only in the whole, and not the whole through the parts. At most it could be entitled a *compositum ideale*, but not a *compositum reale*. This, however, is a mere subtlety. Space is not a composite of substances (not even of real accidents). Hence if I remove all composition in it, nothing must remain, not even the point; for the point is possible only as the limit of a space (and therefore of a composite). Space and time, then, do not consist of simple parts. What belongs only to the state of a substance, although it has a magnitude (for instance, change), likewise does not consist of the simple. That is to say, a certain degree of change does not arise through an accretion of many simple changes. Our inference from the composite to the simple holds only of things that subsist by themselves; but accidents of a state do not subsist by themselves. Consequently the proof of the necessity of the simple, as the constituent of everything substantially composite, can easily be spoiled, and the cause it is meant to serve ruined, if it is extended too far and is made to hold of everything composite without distinction, as has in fact already happened more than once.",["- Last sentence is tangled (\"und dadurch überhaupt seine Sache leichtlich dadurch verderben\" repeats \"dadurch\"); I smoothed it, joining \"seine Sache\" as \"the cause it is meant to serve\". The sentence lacks a proper verb construction (\"Man kan ... verderben\") and I have kept it as one sentence.","- \"vor\" is used for \"für\" throughout (\"vor alles Zusammengesetzte\"); rendered \"of\".","- \"Accidenzen\" rendered \"accidents\"; \"Zustand\" as \"state\".","- \"Anwachs\" rendered \"accretion\"."]],"krv-a-0758":["II. Remark on the Antithesis.",[]],"krv-a-0759":["Against this proposition of an infinite divisibility of matter, the ground of proof of which is purely mathematical, objections have been advanced by the Monadists. These objections at once render themselves suspect, in that the Monadists will not allow the clearest mathematical proofs to count as insights into the constitution of space, in so far as space is in fact the formal condition of the possibility of all matter. They regard such proofs only as inferences from abstract but arbitrary concepts, which could not be related to real things. It is as if it were even possible to devise another kind of intuition than that given in the original intuition of space, and as if the determinations of space *a priori* did not at the same time concern everything that is possible solely through its filling this space. If we listen to them, we should have to think, besides the mathematical point, which is simple but is not a part, being merely the limit of a space, also of physical points, which are indeed likewise simple, but have the prerogative, as parts of space, of filling it by their mere aggregation. I shall not here repeat the common and clear refutations of this absurdity, which are to be met with in abundance, since it is altogether futile to try to reason away the evidence of mathematics by merely discursive concepts. I shall only remark that when philosophy chicanes with mathematics in this fashion, it does so because it forgets that in this question we are concerned only with appearances and their condition. Here, however, it is not enough to find, for the pure concept of the composite in the understanding, the concept of the simple; what is required is to find, for the intuition of the composite (matter), the intuition of the simple. This, according to the laws of sensibility, and therefore in the case of objects of the senses also, is altogether impossible. Hence it may always hold of a whole of substances which is thought through the pure understanding alone, that prior to all composition of it we must have the simple; but this does not hold of the *totum substantiale phaenomenon*, which, as empirical intuition in space, carries with it the necessary property that no part of it is simple, because no part of space is simple. The Monadists have, however, been subtle enough to try to evade this difficulty by presupposing, not space as a condition of the possibility of the objects of outer intuition (bodies), but these objects, and the dynamical relation of substances in general, as the condition of the possibility of space. Now we have a concept of bodies only as appearances; and as such they necessarily presuppose space as the condition of the possibility of all outer appearance. The evasion is therefore vain, and has indeed been sufficiently cut off above, in the Transcendental Aesthetic. Were bodies things in themselves, the proof of the Monadists would certainly hold.",["- Long opening sentences divided; \"Gleich als wenn...\" made a separate sentence with an elliptical \"It is as if\".","- \"Einwürfe...welche sich dadurch schon verdächtig machen: daß...\" restated with \"at once render themselves suspect, in that\".","- \"Bestimmungen desselben\" taken as determinations of space (not of intuition).","- \"Ungereimtheit\" rendered \"absurdity\"; \"chicanirt\" kept as \"chicanes\".","- \"Vorzug\" rendered \"prerogative\"."]],"krv-a-0760":["I speak here, moreover, only of the simple in so far as it is necessarily given in the composite, the composite being resolvable into it as into its constituents. The proper meaning of the word *monas* (as used by Leibniz) should properly apply only to the simple which is given immediately as simple substance (for instance, in self-consciousness), and not as element of the composite; the latter might better be entitled the *atomus*. And since I propose to prove simple substances, as elements, only in respect of the composite, I might entitle the antithesis of the second antinomy transcendental atomistic. But as this word has long since been used to designate a particular mode of explaining corporeal appearances (*molecularum*), and so presupposes empirical concepts, it may be entitled the dialectical principle of monadology.",["- \"solte wol nur auf das Einfache gehen\" rendered \"should properly apply only to\"; \"wol\" is a hedge, here softened.","- \"Atomistik\" kept as \"atomistic\" (the German is a noun, \"Atomistik\"); \"er\" in \"so mag er ... heissen\" refers to the antithesis (or the principle), resolved accordingly as \"it\".","- \"molecularum\" is Kant's Latin genitive plural, left as printed (doubtful form)."]],"krv-a-0761":["The second dialectical assertion has this peculiarity, that it has against it a dogmatic assertion which, among all the sophistical assertions, is the only one that undertakes to prove, in an object of experience, and with manifest evidence, the actuality of what we above reckoned merely among transcendental ideas, namely the absolute simplicity of substance. The assertion is that the object of inner sense, the I which thinks, is an absolutely simple substance. Without entering upon this now (since it has been more fully considered above), I shall only remark that if something is thought merely as object, without adding any synthetic determination of its intuition (as happens through the quite bare representation 'I'), then nothing manifold and no composition can indeed be perceived in such a representation. Moreover, since the predicates through which I think this object are merely intuitions of inner sense, nothing can occur in them which would prove a manifold external to one another, and therefore real composition. Self-consciousness accordingly brings it about that the subject which thinks, being at the same time its own object, cannot divide itself (though it can divide the determinations inhering in it); for in respect of itself every object is absolute unity. Nevertheless, if this subject is regarded externally, as an object of intuition, it would presumably exhibit composition in the appearance. But it must always be so regarded if we wish to know whether there is in it a manifold external to one another or not.",["- First sentence divided; \"nemlich daß...\" made a separate sentence.","- \"so könne freilich\" is subjunctive in the original after \"wenn\"; rendered as plain indicative \"can indeed be perceived\".","- \"Es bringt also nur das Selbstbewustseyn es so mit sich\" smoothed to \"Self-consciousness accordingly brings it about\"; \"nur\" omitted for smoothness.","- \"(obgleich die ihm inhärirende Bestimmungen)\" supplied \"it can divide\" in the English; the German is elliptical.","- \"Erscheinung an sich\" kept as \"in the appearance\"; \"an sich\" dropped as it hardly bears sense here; doubtful reading."]],"krv-a-0762":["Of the Antinomy of Pure Reason.",["- Running-head fragment; the German is a genitive (\"Der Antinomie der reinen Vernunft\"), rendered as a title."]],"krv-a-0763":["Third Conflict of the Transcendental Ideas.",[]],"krv-a-0764":["Thesis.",[]],"krv-a-0765":["Causality in accordance with laws of nature is not the only causality from which the appearances of the world can one and all be derived. To explain these appearances it is necessary to assume another causality, that of freedom.",["- \"insgesamt\" rendered \"one and all\"; \"Caussalität\" as \"causality\"."]],"krv-a-0766":["Proof.",[]],"krv-a-0767":["Let it be assumed that there is no causality other than that in accordance with laws of nature. Everything that happens then presupposes a previous state upon which it inevitably follows according to a rule. But the previous state must itself be something that has happened (that has come to be in time, not having been before); for if it had always been, its consequence also would not have first arisen, but would likewise have always been. The causality of the cause through which something happens is therefore itself something that has happened, and this, in accordance with the law of nature, again presupposes a previous state and its causality, and this in the same way a still earlier state, and so on. If, then, everything happens solely in accordance with laws of nature, there is always only a subaltern, never a first beginning, and therefore no completeness of the series on the side of the causes descending one from another. But the law of nature consists precisely in this, that nothing happens without a cause sufficiently determined *a priori*. The proposition that all causality is possible only in accordance with laws of nature, when taken in its unlimited universality, thus contradicts itself; and this kind of causality cannot, therefore, be assumed to be the only kind.",[]],"krv-a-0768":["Antithesis.",[]],"krv-a-0769":["There is no freedom; everything in the world takes place solely in accordance with laws of nature.",[]],"krv-a-0770":["Proof.",[]],"krv-a-0771":["Suppose that there is freedom in the transcendental sense, as a special kind of causality in accordance with which the events of the world could follow, namely a faculty of absolutely beginning a state, and therefore also a series of consequences of that state. Then not only will a series begin absolutely through this spontaneity, but the determination of this spontaneity itself to the production of the series, that is, the causality, will begin absolutely; nothing precedes through which this act, as it takes place, is determined in accordance with permanent laws. Now every beginning of action presupposes a state of the cause as not yet acting; and a dynamically first beginning of action presupposes a state which has no connection of causality whatever with the preceding state of the same cause, that is, which in no way follows from it. Transcendental freedom is therefore opposed to the law of causality. It is a kind of connection of the successive states of active causes such that no unity of experience is possible; it is accordingly not to be met with in any experience, and is an empty thought-entity [*Gedankending*].",[]],"krv-a-0772":["We must, then, assume a causality through which something happens without its cause being further determined, by another preceding cause, in accordance with necessary laws. That is to say, we must assume an absolute spontaneity of causes, whereby a series of appearances which proceeds in accordance with laws of nature begins of itself; and therefore transcendental freedom, without which even in the course of nature the succession of appearances on the side of the causes is never complete.",[]],"krv-a-0773":["We have, then, nothing but nature in which we must seek the connection and order of cosmic events. Freedom (independence) from the laws of nature is indeed a liberation from constraint, but also from the guidance of all rules. For it cannot be said that laws of freedom enter into the causality of the course of the world in place of the laws of nature; if freedom were determined in accordance with laws, it would not be freedom, but would itself be nothing other than nature. Nature and transcendental freedom differ as conformity to law and lawlessness. The former burdens the understanding with the difficulty of seeking the origin of events ever higher in the series of causes, since causality in them is always conditioned; but in compensation it promises thoroughgoing and law-governed unity of experience. The illusion of freedom, on the other hand, promises rest to the understanding in its inquiry along the chain of causes, by conducting it to an unconditioned causality which begins to act of itself; but since this causality is itself blind, it breaks off the guiding thread of rules, by which alone a completely connected experience is possible.",[]],"krv-a-0774":["Observation on the Third Antinomy. I. On the Thesis.",[]],"krv-a-0775":["The transcendental idea of freedom by no means constitutes the whole content of the psychological concept of that name, which is for the most part empirical. It constitutes only the content of the absolute spontaneity of action, as the proper ground of its imputability. Yet it is the real stumbling-block for philosophy, which finds insuperable difficulties in admitting this kind of unconditioned causality. That element in the question of the freedom of the will which has always put speculative reason in such great perplexity is therefore properly only transcendental, and concerns solely whether a faculty must be assumed of beginning of itself a series of successive things or states. How such a faculty is possible need not be equally demanded of us; in the case of causality in accordance with laws of nature also we have to be content with knowing *a priori* that such causality must be presupposed, although we in no way comprehend how the existence of one thing can be posited through the existence of another, and for this must hold solely to experience. Now we have demonstrated this necessity of a first beginning, from freedom, of a series of appearances only in so far as is required for the comprehensibility of an origin of the world, all subsequent states being capable of being taken as a succession according to mere laws of nature. But since the faculty of beginning a series in time entirely of itself has thereby once been proved (though not understood), we are now also permitted to allow, in the midst of the course of the world, different series to begin of themselves as regards causality, and to attribute to the substances of these series a faculty of acting from freedom. Let no one be held up here by a misunderstanding: namely, that since a successive series in the world can have only a comparatively first beginning, a state of things in the world always preceding, an absolutely first beginning of series during the course of the world is perhaps impossible. For we are not speaking here of an absolutely first beginning in respect of time, but in respect of causality. If I now (for example) rise from my chair, in complete freedom and without the necessarily determining influence of natural causes, a new series, with all its natural consequences *in infinitum*, begins absolutely in this event, although as regards time the event is only the continuation of a preceding series. For this resolution and act do not lie within the succession of mere natural effects, and are not a mere continuation of it. The determining natural causes cease altogether above [*oberhalb*] this event, in respect of it; it follows upon them, but does not result from them. It must therefore be entitled an absolutely first beginning of a series of appearances, not in respect of time, but in respect of causality.",[]],"krv-a-0776":["II. Observation on the Antithesis.",["Reply-format note: I have used the requested \"=== id\" and \"--- notes\" layout, with the notes after each translation; where there is nothing to note, I leave it blank.","- 0767: \"Caussalität\" = causality; \"subaltern\" kept as in the German; I split the sentence beginning \"Also widerspricht der Satz…\" at the semicolon, and resolved \"diese\" as the kind of causality (Naturgesetze) rather than the proposition's universality.","- 0771: \"im transscendentalen Verstande\" rendered \"in the transcendental sense\" [Verstande = sense, not the faculty]; \"Hervorbrinnung\" read as Hervorbringung; the long sentence beginning \"so wird nicht allein…\" divided.","- 0771: \"Gedankending\" given in brackets as the sense turns on it (*ens rationis*).","- 0772: \"Diesemnach\" resolved as \"We must, then\"; the sentence is left as an extended apposition.","- 0773: \"Caussalitat\" corrected to Causalität (OCR); \"iene\" = the former (nature), \"diese\" (first) = freedom; \"Blendwerk\" = illusion. The final long sentence is divided into three.","- 0775: \"Imputabilität\" = imputability; \"Eräugniß\" = Ereignis (event); \"oberhalb derselben\" is awkward in the German and kept literally as \"above this event\"; \"der Zeit nach / der Caussalität nach\" rendered \"in respect of\".","- 0775: the final sentence's relative clause \"die zwar auf iene folgt\" has \"Eräugniß\" as antecedent; I restated it as \"it follows upon them\"."]],"krv-a-0777":["The defender of the all-sufficiency of nature (transcendental physiocracy), in opposing the doctrine of freedom, would maintain his position against the sophistical inferences of the latter in the following manner. If you do not admit something mathematically first in the world in respect of time, there is no need for you to seek something dynamically first in respect of causality. Who bade you invent an absolutely first state of the world, and therewith an absolute beginning of the successively elapsing series of appearances, and, in order to provide your imagination with a resting-place, set limits to unbounded nature? Substances have always existed in the world, or at least the unity of experience makes such a presupposition necessary. There is therefore no difficulty in assuming also that the change of their states, that is, a series of their alterations, has always existed, and that a first beginning, whether mathematical or dynamical, need accordingly not be sought. The possibility of such an infinite descent, without a first member in relation to which all the rest is merely subsequent, cannot be made comprehensible as regards its possibility. But if for that reason you are minded to cast aside this riddle of nature, you will find yourselves compelled to reject many synthetic fundamental properties (fundamental forces) which you are just as little able to comprehend; and even the possibility of alteration in general must become a stumbling-block to you. For if you did not find through experience that alteration is actual, you could never have devised a priori how such an unceasing sequence of being and not-being is possible.",["- \"Allvermögenheit\" rendered \"all-sufficiency\"; literally all-powerfulness, but the sense is nature's sufficiency to account for everything.","- \"Wer hat euch geheissen\" is \"Who bade you\" (heißen = to bid, not \"is entitled\").","- The long sentence \"Da die Substanzen…\" is divided into two; \"ihr\" in the last sentence is resolved as \"alteration\" (Veränderung) in the \"Denn\" clause.","- \"[It]\" supplied nowhere beyond the above; \"Naturräthsel\" = \"riddle of nature\"."]],"krv-a-0778":["The confirmation of reason's need to appeal, in the series of natural causes, to a first beginning from freedom is made very clear by the fact that all the philosophers of antiquity (the Epicurean school excepted) found themselves constrained, in explanation of the movements of the world, to assume a first mover, that is, a freely acting cause which first, and of itself, began this series of states. For they did not venture to make a first beginning comprehensible from mere nature.",["- \"Bedürfniß\" taken as \"need\"; the article \"der\" is an irregular feminine/neuter usage of the 1781 text.","- \"leuchtet…in die Augen\" smoothed to \"is made very clear\"."]],"krv-a-0779":["Even if, in the last resort, a transcendental faculty of freedom is conceded in order to originate the changes of the world, this faculty would at least have to be outside the world. (It always remains a bold presumption to assume, outside the sum of all possible intuitions, an object which cannot be given in any possible perception.) But to ascribe such a faculty to substances in the world itself can never be permitted. For in that case the connection, in accordance with universal laws, of appearances necessarily determining one another, which we entitle nature, and with it the mark of empirical truth which distinguishes experience from dream, would largely disappear. Alongside such a lawless faculty of freedom, nature is hardly any longer thinkable; for the laws of nature would be ceaselessly altered by the influences of freedom, and the play of appearances, which according to mere nature would be regular and uniform, would thereby be thrown into confusion and rendered incoherent.",["- \"Vermögen\" rendered \"faculty\" throughout, as the sense is a capacity of causal origination.","- \"den man Natur nent\" rendered \"which we entitle nature\" (genannt werden).","- \"der lezteren / der ersteren\" resolved as nature / freedom; \"Gesetze der lezteren\" thus \"laws of nature\". \"Inbegriffe\" = \"sum\"."]],"krv-a-0780":["Of the Antinomy of Pure Reason",["- Fragmentary heading; continued in the next passage."]],"krv-a-0781":["Fourth Conflict of the Transcendental Ideas.",["- \"Widerstreit\" rendered \"conflict\"."]],"krv-a-0782":["Thesis.",[]],"krv-a-0783":["To the world there belongs something which, either as its part or as its cause, is an absolutely necessary being.",["- \"Wesen\" rendered \"being\"."]],"krv-a-0784":["Proof.",[]],"krv-a-0785":["The world of sense, as the sum of all appearances, contains also a series of alterations. For without such a series the very representation of the time-series, as a condition of the possibility of the sensible world, would not be given to us.*) Now every alteration stands under a condition which precedes it in time and under which it is necessary. But everything conditioned that is given presupposes, in respect of its existence, a complete series of conditions up to the absolutely unconditioned, which alone is absolutely necessary. Something absolutely necessary must therefore exist, if an alteration exists as its consequence. But this necessary [existence] itself belongs to the world of sense. For suppose it to be outside that world: then the series of cosmic alterations would derive its beginning from it, and yet this necessary cause would not itself belong to the world of sense. Now this is impossible. For since the beginning of a time-series can be determined only through that which precedes it in time, the supreme condition of the beginning of a series of alterations must exist in the time when this series was not yet. (For a beginning is an existence preceded by a time in which the thing that begins was not yet.) The causality of the necessary cause of the alterations, and therefore the cause itself, accordingly belongs to time, and so to appearance (in which alone, as its form, time is possible); and consequently it cannot be thought as separated from the world of sense, as the sum of all appearances. Something absolutely necessary is therefore contained in the world itself (whether this be the whole cosmic series itself, or a part of it).",["- \"Dieses Nothwendige\" rendered \"this necessary [existence]\"; the bracketed noun is supplied to avoid an ambiguous neuter.","- Sentence \"Denn, da der Anfang…\" restated with the parenthesis set apart as a separate sentence.","- \"Sinnenwelt\" = \"world of sense\"; \"Schlechthinunbedingtes\" = \"absolutely unconditioned\"."]],"krv-a-0786":["*) Time, as the formal condition of the possibility of alterations, does indeed precede them objectively; but subjectively, and in the actuality of consciousness, this representation, like every other, is given only on the occasion of perceptions.",["- \"Veranlassung\" rendered \"occasion\"."]],"krv-a-0787":["Antithesis.",[]],"krv-a-0788":["An absolutely necessary being nowhere exists, either in the world or outside the world as its cause.",[]],"krv-a-0789":["Proof.",["- *Beweis* rendered \"Proof\", the heading of the demonstration of the Thesis of the Third Antinomy (A 453); nothing further is in the passage."]],"krv-a-0790":["Suppose that the world itself is a necessary being, or that a necessary being exists in it. Then in the series of its alterations there would be either a beginning which was unconditionally necessary and therefore without a cause, which conflicts with the dynamical law of the determination of all appearances in time; or the series itself would be without any beginning, and, though contingent and conditioned in all its parts, would yet as a whole be absolutely necessary and unconditioned. This is self-contradictory, since the existence of a manifold cannot be necessary if no single part of it possesses an existence necessary in itself.",["- \"Menge\" rendered \"manifold\" in the sense of an aggregate; \"multitude\" would also serve.","- \"oder in ihr\" supplied with \"a necessary being exists\" for clarity."]],"krv-a-0791":["Suppose, on the other hand, that there is an absolutely necessary cause of the world outside the world. As the supreme member in the series of the causes of cosmic alterations, it would be the first to begin the existence of these alterations and their series.*) But it would then also have to begin to act, and its causality would belong to time, and for that very reason to the sum of appearances, that is, to the world. Consequently the cause itself would not be outside the world, which contradicts the hypothesis. Hence neither in the world, nor outside it (though in causal connection with it), is there any absolutely necessary being.",["- \"der lezteren\" resolved as \"these alterations\" (Weltveränderungen).","- Footnote marker *) retained at the German position."]],"krv-a-0792":["*) The word \"to begin\" is taken in two senses. The first is active: the cause begins a series of states as its effect (*infit*). The second is passive: the causality begins in the cause itself (*fit*). I here infer from the former to the latter.",["- \"lezte\" in \"auf die lezte\" rendered \"the latter\" (the passive sense).","- Latin *infit*, *fit* kept in italics."]],"krv-a-0793":["Observation on the Fourth Antinomy. I. On the Thesis.",["- \"Anmerkung\" rendered \"Observation\"."]],"krv-a-0794":["In order to prove the existence of a necessary being, it is incumbent on me here to employ no argument other than the cosmological, which ascends from the conditioned in appearance to the unconditioned in concept, in that it regards the latter as the necessary condition of the absolute totality of the series. To attempt the proof from the mere idea of a supreme being among all beings in general belongs to another principle of reason, and a proof of that kind will therefore have to be dealt with separately.",["- \"liegt mir ob\" rendered \"it is incumbent on me\"; \"kein anderes als\" kept as \"no argument other than\".","- \"oberste aller Wesen\" rendered \"supreme being among all beings\", the German being awkward."]],"krv-a-0795":["The pure cosmological proof can establish the existence of a necessary being only by leaving it undecided whether this being is the world itself or a thing distinct from it. To settle the latter question, principles are required which are no longer cosmological and do not proceed within the series of appearances. They would have to be concepts of contingent beings in general (in so far as these are considered merely as objects of the understanding), and a principle for connecting such beings with a necessary being through mere concepts. All this belongs to a transcendent philosophy, for which this is not yet the place.",["- Restated the final clause \"welches alles vor eine transscendente Philosophie gehört\" as a separate sentence, with \"They would have to be\" supplied in square-bracket-free form for the sense; the German has only \"sondern Begriffe… und ein Princip\".","- \"vor\" = modern \"für\" (for). \"Erscheinungen\" = appearances; \"Verstandes\" = understanding."]],"krv-a-0796":["But once the proof has been begun cosmologically, by taking as its ground the series of appearances and the regress in it in accordance with empirical laws of causality, we cannot afterwards break away from this and pass over to something which does not belong to the series as one of its members. For something must be regarded as condition in the very same sense in which the relation of the conditioned to its condition was taken in the series, which was meant to lead by continuous advance to this highest condition. Now if this relation is sensible, and belongs to the possible empirical employment of the understanding, the supreme condition or cause can bring the regress to a close only in accordance with laws of sensibility, and therefore only as belonging to the temporal series; and the necessary being must be regarded as the supreme member of the world-series.",["- Split the second sentence's relative construction (\"in welcher… genommen wurde, die… führen sollte\") by smoothing; \"die\" refers to the series (Reihe).","- \"Bedeutung\" rendered \"sense\"; \"Verstandesgebrauch\" = employment of the understanding."]],"krv-a-0797":["II. Remark on the Antithesis.",["- \"Anmerkung\" = remark (a heading)."]],"krv-a-0798":["If, in ascending the series of appearances, we believe we meet with difficulties opposing the existence of an absolutely necessary supreme cause, these difficulties must not be grounded on mere concepts of the necessary existence of a thing in general, and so must not be ontological. They must arise out of the causal connection with a series of appearances, in that we seek for this series a condition which is itself unconditioned; they must therefore be cosmological, and be inferred in accordance with empirical laws. It must, that is to say, be shown that the ascent in the series of causes (in the world of sense) can never end in an empirically unconditioned condition, and that the cosmological argument from the contingency of the states of the world, as evidenced by their alterations, tells against the assumption of a first cause, one that absolutely initiates the series.\n\nBut in this antinomy a strange contrast shows itself. From the very same ground of proof from which, in the thesis, the existence of an original being was inferred, the non-existence of such a being is inferred in the antithesis, and with equal cogency. First it was said: There is a necessary being, because the whole past time contains in itself the series of all conditions, and therewith also the unconditioned (the necessary). Now it is said: There is no necessary being, precisely because the whole elapsed time contains in itself the series of all conditions (which are therefore, one and all, themselves conditioned). The cause of this is as follows. The first argument looks only to the absolute totality of the series of conditions, each of which determines the other in time, and thereby obtains an unconditioned and necessary. The second, on the other hand, takes into consideration the contingency of everything determined in the temporal series (since before each member a time precedes in which the condition must itself again be determined as conditioned), and by this all that is unconditioned, and all absolute necessity, entirely disappears.\n\nNevertheless the mode of inference in both is quite in keeping with ordinary human reason, which often falls into the position of being at variance with itself, owing to its considering its object from two different standpoints. Herr von Mairan regarded the dispute between two famous astronomers, which arose from a similar difficulty about the choice of standpoint, as a phenomenon remarkable enough to warrant a special treatise upon it. The one argued: The moon revolves on its axis, because it constantly turns the same side to the earth. The other: The moon does not revolve on its axis, precisely because it constantly turns the same side to the earth. Both inferences were correct, according to the standpoint from which one chose to observe the moon's motion.",["- Divided the long passage into paragraphs and several sentences; \"Erst hieß es… Nun heißt es\" kept as \"it was said… it is said\".","- \"Urwesen\" = original being; \"Weltzustände\" = states of the world; \"empirischunbedingten\" = empirically unconditioned.","- \"bekomt… ein Unbedingtes und Nothwendiges\": subject is the first argument; neuter adjectives rendered as nouns."]],"krv-a-0799":["Yet people have taken the liberty of making such a leap (μεταβασις ἐις ἀλλο γενος). From the alterations in the world they inferred empirical contingency, that is, the dependence of these alterations upon empirically determining causes, and so obtained an ascending series of empirical conditions; and in this they were quite right. But since they could find in it no first beginning and no supreme member, they suddenly abandoned the empirical concept of contingency and took the pure category. This then gave occasion to a merely intelligible series, the completeness of which rested on the existence of an absolutely necessary cause. Such a cause, being no longer bound to any sensible conditions, was also freed from the temporal condition of itself beginning its causality. But this procedure is wholly illegitimate, as may be gathered from what follows.",["- Split the long second sentence after \"Zufälligkeit… Recht war\" and again at \"nahm die reine Categorie\"; \"die nunmehr…\" made a new sentence.","- Greek retained as in the text; \"Gleichwol\" = \"Yet\"."]],"krv-a-0800":["Contingent, in the pure sense of the category, is that whose contradictory opposite is possible. Now from empirical contingency we cannot at all infer this intelligible contingency. When something alters, its opposite (the opposite of its state) is actual at another time, and therefore also possible. But this is not the contradictory opposite of the previous state. For that, it would be required that in the very time in which the previous state was, its opposite could have been in its place; and this cannot be inferred from the alteration at all. A body that was in motion = A comes to rest = non-A. From the fact that a state opposed to the state A follows upon it, we cannot at all conclude that the contradictory opposite of A is possible, and therefore that A is contingent. For that, it would be required that in the very time in which the motion was, rest could have been in its place. All that we know is that rest was actual in the succeeding time, and therefore also possible. But motion at one time and rest at another are not contradictorily opposed to each other.\n\nThus the succession of opposed determinations, that is, alteration, by no means proves contingency as judged by concepts of the pure understanding, and cannot therefore lead to the existence of a necessary being according to pure concepts of the understanding. Alteration proves only empirical contingency; that is, that the new state, in accordance with the law of causality, could not have occurred at all by itself, without a cause belonging to the preceding time. This cause, even if it be assumed to be absolutely necessary, must in this way still be met with in time, and belong to the series of appearances.",["- \"Bestimmungen\" = determinations. \"contradictorisch\" rendered \"contradictorily/contradictory\"; \"Gegentheil\" = opposite.","- Restated \"wozu erfodert wird, daß…\" as a separate sentence; \"vor sich selbst\" rendered \"by itself\"."]],"krv-a-0801":["Third Section of the Antinomy of Pure Reason. The Interest of Reason in this Conflict.",["- Heading; \"Widerstreit\" = conflict."]],"krv-a-0802":["We have now before us the whole dialectical play of the cosmological ideas. They do not allow of an adequate object being given to them in any possible experience; they do not even allow reason to think them in agreement with universal laws of experience. Yet they are not arbitrarily devised; reason is necessarily led to them in the continuous advance of the empirical synthesis, whenever it seeks to free from all condition, and to apprehend in its unconditioned totality, what according to rules of experience can always be determined only as conditioned. These sophistical assertions are so many attempts to solve four natural and unavoidable problems of reason. There can be exactly this number, neither more nor fewer, since there are no more series of synthetic presuppositions which limit the empirical synthesis a priori.",["- Restated the first sentence in three; \"congruirender Gegenstand\" = adequate [congruent] object.","- \"vernünftelnde\" = sophistical; \"Problemen\" is the text's plural form."]],"krv-a-0803":["We have set forth the dazzling pretensions of a reason which extends its domain beyond all limits of experience, only in dry formulae which contain merely the ground of its claims in law; and, as befits a transcendental philosophy, we have stripped these formulae of everything empirical, although the whole splendour of the assertions of reason can shine forth only in connection with the empirical. In this application, however, and in the progressive extension of the employment of reason, which starts from the field of experiences and gradually soars up to these sublime ideas, philosophy shows a dignity which, if only it could maintain its pretensions, would leave the worth of all other human science far below it; for it promises to supply the foundation for our greatest expectations and prospects, in regard to those final ends in which all the endeavours of reason must at last unite.\n\nThe questions are these: whether the world has a beginning and some limit to its extension in space; whether there exists anywhere, and perhaps in my thinking self, an indivisible and indestructible unity, or nothing but what is divisible and perishable; whether in my actions I am free, or, like other beings, am led by the thread of nature and of fate; and finally, whether there is a supreme cause of the world, or whether natural things and their order constitute the ultimate object at which we must stop in all our reflections. These are questions for the solution of which the mathematician would gladly give up his whole science; for that science can afford him no satisfaction in regard to the highest and most vital ends of humanity.\n\nIndeed, the true dignity of mathematics, that pride of human reason, rests on the following. Mathematics gives reason guidance in apprehending nature, in the great as in the small, in its order and regularity, and likewise in the admirable unity of the forces that move it, far beyond all the expectations of a philosophy that builds on common experience. It thereby itself gives occasion and encouragement to that employment of reason which extends beyond all experience; and it likewise furnishes the philosophy occupied with this [employment] with the most excellent materials, so that, so far as its nature permits, it may support its investigation by appropriate intuitions.",[]],"krv-a-0804":["Unfortunately for speculation (though perhaps fortunately for the practical vocation of man), reason, in the midst of its greatest expectations, finds itself so beset by a throng of arguments and counter-arguments that it cannot, for the sake of its honour and even of its security, withdraw and look on indifferently at this conflict as a mere sham fight; still less can it simply command peace, since the object of the dispute is of great interest. Nothing remains for it but to reflect upon the origin of this disunion of reason with itself, and to ask whether a mere misunderstanding may not be to blame. When this has been cleared up, the proud claims on both sides will perhaps be given up; but in their place a lasting and tranquil rule of reason over understanding and the senses would begin.",[]],"krv-a-0805":["For the present we shall postpone this thorough inquiry, and first consider on which side we should prefer to take our stand, if we were compelled to take sides. In this case we consult, not the logical touchstone of truth, but only our interest. Such an investigation, though it settles nothing as regards the disputed rights of the two parties, will nevertheless have the advantage of making comprehensible why the participants in this dispute have sided with the one party rather than the other, although no superior insight into the object has been the cause. It will also explain other incidental matters, for instance, the zealous heat of the one party and the cold assertion of the other, and why they gladly shout joyful applause to the one side, and are implacably prejudiced in advance against the other.",[]],"krv-a-0806":["There is, however, something which in this preliminary judgment determines the point of view from which alone it can be carried out with proper thoroughness; and this is the comparison of the principles from which the two parties proceed. In the assertions of the antithesis we observe a perfect uniformity in the manner of thinking and complete unity of maxim, namely a principle of pure empiricism, not only in the explanation of appearances in the world, but also in the solution of the transcendental ideas of the universe itself. The assertions of the thesis, on the other hand, besides the empirical mode of explanation within the series of appearances, also lay intellectual beginnings at the foundation; and the maxim is to this extent not simple. From its essential distinguishing mark I shall entitle it the dogmatism of pure reason.",[]],"krv-a-0807":["On the side of dogmatism, then, in the determination of the cosmological ideas of reason, or on the side of the thesis, there is shown",[]],"krv-a-0808":["first, a certain practical interest in which every right-thinking man, if he understands where his true advantage lies, heartily shares. That the world has a beginning; that my thinking self is of simple and therefore indestructible nature; that this self is at the same time free in its voluntary actions and raised above natural constraint; and finally that the whole order of things which constitute the world derives from an original being, from which everything borrows its unity and purposive connection: these are so many cornerstones of morals and religion. The antithesis robs us of all these supports, or at least appears to rob us of them.",[]],"krv-a-0809":["Secondly, a speculative interest of reason also manifests itself on this side. For if the transcendental ideas are assumed and employed in this manner, we can grasp *a priori* the whole chain of conditions and comprehend the derivation of the conditioned, since we begin from the unconditioned. This the antithesis does not achieve; and it commends itself very ill by the fact that to the question regarding the conditions of its synthesis it can give no answer which does not leave us to go on asking endlessly. According to it, we must ascend from a given beginning to one still higher; every part leads to a still smaller part; every event has always another event above it as its cause; and the conditions of existence in general rest ever again upon others, without ever finding unconditioned support and stay in a self-subsisting thing, as original being.",[]],"krv-a-0810":["Thirdly, this side also has the advantage of popularity, which certainly constitutes no small part of its recommendation. Common understanding finds not the least difficulty in the ideas of an unconditioned beginning of all synthesis, since it is in any case more accustomed to descend to consequences than to ascend to grounds. In the concepts of the absolutely first (whose possibility it does not trouble itself to probe) it finds a convenience, and at the same time a fixed point to which it can attach the guiding-line of its steps. On the other hand, in the restless ascent from the conditioned to the condition, always with one foot in the air, it can find no satisfaction whatever.",[]],"krv-a-0811":["On the side of empiricism, in the determination of the cosmological ideas, or on the side of the antithesis,",[]],"krv-a-0812":["there is found, first, no such practical interest from pure principles of reason as morals and religion bring with them. Indeed, mere empiricism seems to deprive both of all force and influence. If there is no original being distinct from the world; if the world is without beginning, and therefore also without an author; if our will is not free, and the soul is of the same divisibility and perishableness as matter; then moral ideas and principles also lose all validity, and fall with the transcendental ideas which constituted their theoretical support.",["- krv-a-0803: the long first sentence is divided into several; \"Grenzen\" and the dashes are mine. \"Gebiete\" (for \"ihr Gebiet\") taken as \"its domain\"; the construction \"der ... erweiternden Vernunft\" is smoothed.","- krv-a-0803: the final sentence (\"daß, da sie ... giebt\") is irregular; I restated it as a series of sentences and supplied \"[employment]\" for \"die damit beschäftigte Weltweisheit\" as the antecedent. \"Philosophy occupied with this\" is retained in sense. \"Anschauungen\" = intuitions.","- krv-a-0803: \"Einzusehen\" rendered \"apprehending\"; \"einsehen\" could be \"gain insight into\", a choice.","- krv-a-0804: \"vor\" in \"vor die Speculation\" read as \"für\" (for); \"Gedränge\" rendered \"throng\"; \"Spielgefechte\" = sham fight.","- krv-a-0805: \"Probierstein\" = touchstone; \"zuiauchzen\" read as \"zujauchzen\" (OCR slip silently corrected).","- krv-a-0806: \"Principium\" kept as \"principle\"; \"Maxime\" = maxim. \"Denkungsart\" = \"manner of thinking\". \"Die Maxime ist so fern nicht einfach\": \"to this extent not simple\".","- krv-a-0808, 0812: \"Urwesen\" = \"original being\" (Latin *ens originarium*); \"Urheber\" = author.","- krv-a-0810: \"Leitschnur\" = guiding-line; the final sentence is divided and \"da er hingegen\" restated as \"On the other hand\"."]],"krv-a-0813":["Empiricism, on the other hand, offers advantages to the speculative interest of reason which are very attractive, and far exceed any that the dogmatic teacher of the ideas of reason can promise. On the empiricist view the understanding is always on its own proper ground, namely, the field of merely possible experiences. It can trace the laws of these experiences, and by means of them can extend its secure and readily comprehensible knowledge without end. Here it can, and ought to, exhibit the object in intuition, both in itself and in its relations; or, if not in intuition, then in concepts whose image can be clearly and distinctly set forth in given similar intuitions. Not only has the understanding no need to abandon this chain of the natural order in order to attach itself to ideas whose objects it does not know, since as thought-entities [*Gedankendinge*] they can never be given. It is not even permitted to desert its proper business, and, on the pretext that this is now completed, to pass over into the domain of idealising reason and to transcendent concepts. There it no longer has to observe, and to investigate in accordance with the laws of nature, but only to think and to invent. It can do so in the assurance that it cannot be refuted by facts of nature, since it is in no way bound by their testimony, and may pass them by, or even subordinate them to a higher authority, namely that of pure reason.",[]],"krv-a-0814":["The empiricist will therefore never allow any epoch of nature to be assumed as the absolutely first, nor any limit of his outlook over the range of nature to be regarded as the outermost. Nor will he allow us to pass from the objects of nature, which he can resolve by observation and mathematics and determine synthetically in intuition (the extended), to those which neither sense nor imagination can ever exhibit *in concreto* (the simple). He will not concede that we may, even in nature, lay at the foundation a power of acting independently of the laws of nature (freedom), and so curtail the understanding in its business of tracing, under the guidance of necessary rules, the origin of appearances. Finally, he will not admit that we may seek the cause of anything outside nature (an original being), since we know nothing beyond nature. It alone presents objects to us and can instruct us regarding their laws.",[]],"krv-a-0815":["Admittedly, if the empirical philosopher has no other purpose in his antithesis than to put down the presumption and rashness of a reason that mistakes its true vocation. Such a reason boasts of insight and knowledge just where insight and knowledge properly cease. It would pass off as a furtherance of the speculative interest what is admitted only in respect of the practical interest, so that, where it suits its convenience, it may break the thread of physical enquiries and, under the pretence of extending knowledge, attach it to transcendental ideas, through which we really know only that we know nothing. If, I say, the empiricist were content with this, his principle would be a maxim of moderation in claims, of modesty in assertions, and at the same time of the greatest possible extension of our understanding, through the teacher who is really appointed for us, namely experience. For in that case intellectual presuppositions and faith, for the sake of our practical concerns, would not be taken from us; only we could not allow them to appear under the title and pomp of science and rational insight. Genuine speculative knowledge can nowhere have any object other than that of experience; and if we overstep its limits, the synthesis which seeks new modes of knowledge independent of experience has no substratum of intuition on which it could be exercised.",[]],"krv-a-0816":["But if empiricism itself becomes dogmatic in dealing with ideas (as for the most part it does), and boldly denies whatever lies beyond the sphere of its intuitive knowledge, it falls into the same fault of immodesty. Here this fault is all the more blameworthy, because an irreparable injury is thereby done to the practical interest of reason.",[]],"krv-a-0817":["This is the opposition of Epicureanism to Platonism.",[]],"krv-a-0818":["It is still a question, however, whether Epicurus ever propounded these principles as objective assertions. If they were nothing more than maxims for the speculative employment of reason, he showed in them a more genuine philosophical spirit than any other of the sages of antiquity. [The maxims are:] that in explaining appearances we must proceed as if the field of enquiry were cut off by no limit or beginning of the world; that we must assume the matter of the world to be such as it must be if we are to be instructed about it by experience; that no other generation of events is to be admitted than that which is determined by unalterable laws of nature; and finally that no cause distinct from the world is to be employed. These are still very correct principles, though little observed, for extending speculative philosophy, as also for discovering the principles of morality independently of extraneous sources of aid. He who demands that we ignore those dogmatic propositions, so long as we are occupied with mere speculation, ought not on that account to be accused of wishing to deny them.",[]],"krv-a-0819":["Each of the two says more than he knows. But the first encourages and promotes knowledge, though to the prejudice of the practical. The second does indeed supply excellent principles for the practical, but just thereby, in respect of everything in which alone a speculative knowledge is open to us, allows reason to pursue idealistic explanations of natural appearances, and to neglect physical enquiry.",["- krv-a-0813: restated the long final sentence as several; \"Gedankendinge\" kept in German brackets. \"Nicht allein … sondern\" restructured into two sentences.","- krv-a-0814: the single sentence governed by \"niemals erlauben\" is divided; \"noch … noch\" clauses become separate \"Nor…/He will not…\" statements. \"Einfachen\" and \"Ausgedehnten\" kept as in the German parentheses.","- krv-a-0815: the protasis \"Zwar, wenn…\" is irregular and left unfinished by the German until the resumed \"wenn, sage ich\"; I divided it and rendered the first part as a clause with the apodosis carried by the resumed \"If, I say\". \"Admittedly, if … vocation.\" is thus deliberately a fragment-like sentence, as in the German. \"Ansehen\" in 0813 rendered \"authority\".","- krv-a-0815: \"Erkentnisse\" (plural) rendered \"modes of knowledge\"; \"Substratum\" kept as Latin form.","- krv-a-0816: \"der Fehler der Unbescheidenheit\": I added \"same\" (\"the same fault\") to point back to the preceding passage's presumption; this is a supplied nuance, not in the German.","- krv-a-0818: footnote; the original sentence is irregular (the \"daß\" clauses are left hanging before \"sind noch iezt … Grundsätze\"). I supplied \"[The maxims are:]\" and restated as separate sentences. \"Weit\" in the OCR corrected silently to \"Welt\".","- krv-a-0819: \"idealischen Erklärungen\" rendered \"idealistic explanations\"; \"idealisirenden\" in 0813 rendered \"idealising\"; the German terms differ."]],"krv-a-0820":["As regards the third factor that may be considered in making a provisional choice between the two contending parties, it is extremely strange that empiricism should be wholly lacking in popularity. One would have thought that the common understanding would eagerly welcome a programme which promises to satisfy it through nothing save modes of knowledge drawn from experience and their rational connection, whereas transcendental dogmatism compels it to rise to concepts which far surpass the insight and rational power of the heads most practised in thinking. But this is precisely what moves it. For it then finds itself in a position in which even the most learned can claim no advantage over it. If it understands little or nothing of these matters, no one else can boast of understanding much more. And though it cannot speak of them in so scholastically correct a fashion as others, it can yet argue about them [vernünfteln] to an infinitely greater extent, since it wanders among mere ideas, about which one is eloquent just because one knows nothing of them. In the investigation of nature, on the other hand, it would have to remain wholly silent and confess its ignorance. Comfort and vanity are thus already a strong recommendation of these principles.\n\nMoreover, though it is very hard for a philosopher to accept anything as a principle without being able to give an account of it to himself, still harder to introduce concepts whose objective reality cannot be seen, nothing is more usual for the common understanding. It wants something from which it can make a confident start. The difficulty of comprehending such a presupposition does not trouble it, since the difficulty never occurs to it, [the common understanding] not knowing what comprehending [Begreifen] means; and it takes to be familiar whatever has become current through frequent use. Finally, all speculative interest vanishes in it before the practical, and it imagines that it has insight into and knowledge of what its fears or hopes impel it to assume or believe.\n\nThus empiricism is wholly deprived of popularity for the transcendentally idealising reason. However much that is prejudicial to the supreme practical principles it may contain, there is no ground whatever for apprehending that it will ever overstep the limits of the schools, or acquire in the common life any considerable authority or any favour with the great multitude.",["- Restated the first long sentence as several; \"Moment\" rendered \"factor\"; the antecedent of \"ihn/er/sein\" is the *gemeine Verstand*, supplied in brackets once where the parenthesis could mislead.","- \"Empirismus ... beraubt\" in the last sentence: \"for the transcendentally idealising reason\" is a judgment on the dative; the German is compressed.","- *vernünfteln* kept in German as a pejorative, hence \"argue about them\".","- \"Erfahrungserkentnisse\" = \"modes of knowledge drawn from experience\" (plural Erkenntnisse).","- Last sentence: \"sie\" refers to the idealising reason (or its doctrine); rendered \"it\" for the empiricism/reason contrast as the sense requires."]],"krv-a-0821":["Human reason is by nature architectonic. That is, it regards all modes of knowledge as belonging to a possible system, and therefore admits only such principles as at least do not render a given item of knowledge incapable of standing together with others in some system. The propositions of the antithesis, however, are of such a character that they make the completion of an edifice of knowledge altogether impossible. According to them there is always, beyond any state of the world, a still older state; in every part, others which are again divisible; before every event, another which was itself likewise produced from elsewhere; and in existence generally everything is always only conditioned, no unconditioned and first existence being recognised. Since, then, the antithesis nowhere concedes a first, or any beginning that could serve absolutely as foundation of the structure, a complete edifice of knowledge is, on such presuppositions, altogether impossible. Hence the architectonic interest of reason (which demands not empirical but pure unity of reason *a priori*) carries with it a natural recommendation of the assertions of the thesis.",["- \"Erkentniß\" in the singular, \"vorhabende Erkentniß\", rendered \"a given item of knowledge\" to avoid an unidiomatic plural.","- Divided the sentence beginning \"Nach ihnen giebt es\" with semicolons; the elliptical \"in iedem Theile ... andere\" completed as \"others [parts]\" in sense only."]],"krv-a-0822":["But if a man could renounce all interest, and consider the assertions of reason with indifference to all consequences, solely according to the content of their grounds, such a man, supposing he knew of no way out of the dilemma save to profess one or other of the contending doctrines, would be in a state of unceasing vacillation. Today it would strike him as convincing that the human will is free; tomorrow, considering the indissoluble chain of nature, he would hold that freedom is nothing but self-deception, and that everything is mere nature. But when it came to doing and acting, this play of merely speculative reason would vanish like the shadow-images of a dream, and he would choose his principles solely in accordance with practical interest.\n\nYet since it befits a reflecting and inquiring being to devote certain times solely to the examination of its own reason, laying aside in this all partiality, and so to communicate its observations publicly to others for their judgment, no one can be blamed, still less prevented, if he lets propositions and counter-propositions appear, undaunted by any threat, to defend themselves before a jury of their own station [nemlich] — that is, the station of weak human beings.",["- Split at the full stop after \"verschwinden ... wählen\" for clarity; the second sentence is long and its \"so ... vor Geschworenen\" clause was smoothed.","- \"Gedränge\" rendered \"dilemma\" by sense (literally \"pressure, straits\").","- \"sie ... können\" refers to the Sätze und Gegensätze; \"nemlich\" rendered by \"that is\" in the dash gloss."]],"krv-a-0823":["Fourth Section of the Antinomy of Pure Reason. The Transcendental Problems of Pure Reason, in so far as they absolutely must be capable of being solved.",["- Heading; \"Aufgaben\" rendered \"problems\"."]],"krv-a-0824":["To wish to solve every problem and to answer every question would be an impudent boasting and so extravagant a conceit that one would at once forfeit all confidence. Nevertheless there are sciences whose nature is such that every question that can arise in them must be absolutely answerable from what we know, because the answer must spring from the same sources from which the question springs; and in these it is in no way permissible to plead unavoidable ignorance, but the solution can be demanded. What is right or wrong in all possible cases we must, as a rule, be able to know, since it concerns our obligation, and we have no obligation with regard to what we cannot know. In the explanation of the appearances of nature, on the other hand, much must remain uncertain and many a question insoluble, because what we know of nature is by no means in all cases sufficient for what we have to explain.\n\nThe question now is whether in transcendental philosophy there is any question concerning an object presented to reason which is unanswerable by this very pure reason; and whether we can rightly excuse ourselves from giving a decisive answer by reckoning it as absolutely uncertain (judged by all that we are able to know) — as belonging to that of which we have indeed so much of a concept as to raise a question, but of which we wholly lack the means or the power ever to answer it.",["- Divided at \"Es frägt sich nun\"; the final sentence was kept as one, with the parenthesis set off by dashes.","- \"den Regel nach\" = \"as a rule\"; \"Auflösung\" = \"solution\"; \"Vermögen\" here \"power\".","- \"man'es\" read as \"man es\" (OCR); \"demienigen\" as that to which it is reckoned."]],"krv-a-0825":["I now assert that transcendental philosophy has this peculiarity among all speculative knowledge, that no question which concerns an object given to pure reason is insoluble for this same human reason, and that no plea of unavoidable ignorance and of unfathomable depth in the problem can release us from the obligation to answer it thoroughly and completely. For the very concept which puts us in a position to ask the question must also qualify us to answer it, since the object is not to be met with outside the concept (as in the case of right and wrong).",["- \"weil eben derselbe Begriff\" begins a new sentence in English; the German semicolon is a causal link only.","- \"tüchtig machen\" = \"qualify\"; \"vor eben dieselbe\" = \"für\", rendered \"for this same human reason\"."]],"krv-a-0826":["In transcendental philosophy there are no questions, save only the cosmological, in respect of which we can rightly demand a satisfactory answer bearing on the character of the object; nor is the philosopher permitted to evade them by pleading an impenetrable obscurity. These questions can concern only cosmological ideas. For the object must be empirically given, and the question relates solely to its adequacy to an idea. If the object is transcendental, and therefore itself unknown, we may ask, for instance, whether the something, the appearance of which (in ourselves) is thinking (the soul), is in itself a simple being; or whether there is a cause of all things taken together which is absolutely necessary; and so forth. In such cases we are to seek an object for our idea, and we may admit that this object is unknown to us, though not on that account impossible.*) The cosmological ideas alone have the peculiarity that they can presuppose, as given, their object and the empirical synthesis required for its concept. The question which arises from them concerns only the advance of this synthesis, in so far as it is to contain absolute totality; and this totality, since it can be given in no experience, is no longer anything empirical. Now since we are here speaking solely of a thing as object of a possible experience, and not as a thing in itself, the answer to the transcendent cosmological question can lie nowhere except in the idea. For the question does not concern any object in itself. And in respect of possible experience, what is asked is not what can be given *in concreto* in any experience, but what lies in the idea to which the empirical synthesis is merely to approximate. The question must therefore be capable of solution from the idea alone. For the idea is a mere creature of reason, and reason cannot disclaim responsibility for it and shift it to the unknown object.",[]],"krv-a-0827":["*) We can indeed give no answer to the question, what is the character of a transcendental object, that is, what it is; but we can say that the question itself is nothing, because no object has been given for it. Consequently all questions of the transcendental doctrine of the soul are also answerable, and have actually been answered. For they concern the transcendental subject of all inner appearances, which is not itself appearance, and so is not given as object, and in which none of the categories (to which the question is properly directed) finds conditions of its application. Here, then, we have the case in which the common saying holds, that no answer is also an answer. That is to say, a question as to the character of that something which cannot be thought through any determinate predicate, since it is posited wholly outside the sphere of the objects that can be given to us, is entirely null and void.",[]],"krv-a-0828":["It is not so extraordinary as it at first seems that a science should demand and expect nothing but certain solutions of all the questions belonging to its sphere (*quaestiones domesticae*), even though up to the present they may not yet have been found. Besides transcendental philosophy there are two other sciences of pure reason, the one of purely speculative, the other of practical content: pure mathematics and pure morals. Has it ever been heard that, on the ground of a supposed necessary ignorance of the conditions, it has been declared uncertain what exact relation the diameter bears to the circle in rational or irrational numbers? Since it cannot be given congruently through the former, and has not yet been found through the latter, it was judged that the impossibility of such a solution can at least be known with certainty; and Lambert supplied a proof of this. In the universal principles of morals nothing can be uncertain, since the propositions are either altogether void and meaningless, or must flow solely from our concepts of reason. In natural science, on the other hand, there is an infinity of conjectures in respect of which certainty can never be expected. For natural appearances are objects given to us independently of our concepts. The key to them therefore lies not in us and our pure thinking but outside us; and for that very reason it is in many cases not to be found, so that no assured explanation can be expected. I do not reckon here the questions of the transcendental analytic which concern the deduction of our pure knowledge, since we are at present treating only of the certainty of judgments in respect of objects, and not of the origin of our concepts themselves.",[]],"krv-a-0829":["We cannot, then, escape the obligation of a solution, at least a critical one, of the questions of reason here propounded, by lamenting the narrow limits of our reason and confessing, under the semblance of a humble self-knowledge, that it is beyond our reason to decide whether the world has existed from eternity or has a beginning; whether cosmic space is filled with beings to infinity or is enclosed within certain limits; whether anything in the world is simple, or whether everything must be divided to infinity; whether there is generation and production through freedom, or whether everything depends on the chain of the natural order; and finally whether there is any being wholly unconditioned and in itself necessary, or whether everything is conditioned in its existence, and so externally dependent and in itself contingent. For all these questions concern an object which can be given nowhere else than in our thoughts, namely the absolutely unconditioned totality of the synthesis of appearances. If from our own concepts we can say and determine nothing certain regarding it, we must not throw the blame upon the thing, which conceals itself from us. Such a thing (since it is nowhere to be met with outside our idea) cannot be given to us at all. We must seek the cause in our idea itself, which is a problem admitting of no solution, and yet one in which we obstinately assume that an actual object corresponds to it. A clear exposition of the dialectic which lies in our concept itself would soon bring us to complete certainty as to what we have to judge in regard to such a question.",["- krv-a-0826: The first sentence is divided and restated; \"ohne daß dem Philosophen erlaubt ist…\" is made a separate clause with \"nor\". The sentence \"Ist der Gegenstand transscendental…\" is recast, \"so sollen wir…\" rendered \"In such cases we are to seek…\".","- krv-a-0826: \"Ding an sich selbst\" is given as \"thing in itself\"; \"Geschöpf der Vernunft\" as \"creature of reason\". \"Beschaffenheit\" is rendered \"character\" throughout. In the text \"dessen Erscheinung (in uns selbst) das Denken ist\" is read as \"the appearance of which is thinking\".","- krv-a-0826: \"ob es eine Ursache aller Dinge insgesamt gebe. die schlechthin nothwendig ist\": the stop in the German is kept as a semicolon-linked clause; \"nothwendig\" read with \"Ursache\".","- krv-a-0827: Footnote retained; \"wol daß die Frage selbst nichts sey\" is read as \"we can say that…\". \"transsc.\" expanded to \"transcendental\"; \"Subiect\" is \"subject\".","- krv-a-0828: \"quæstiones domesticæ\" kept in Latin italics. \"Rational- oder Irrationalzahlen\" is rendered \"rational or irrational numbers\"; \"Lambert\" refers to J. H. Lambert's proof of the irrationality of π (1768).","- krv-a-0828: \"Schlüssel\" read as \"key\"; \"Aufschluß\" as \"explanation\". \"Es ist nicht so ausserordentlich… daß\" is kept as one sentence with the \"demand and expect\" clause smoothed.","- krv-a-0829: The long first sentence is kept as one because of its enumerated alternatives; \"Verbindlichkeit\" rendered \"obligation\". In the German, \"Eine deutlich Darlegung\" is read as \"deutliche\" (obvious slip).","- krv-a-0829: \"ein Problem… das keine Auflösung verstattet\": \"welche\" refers to \"Idee\"; I render it as \"our idea… a problem\". \"Weltraum\" is rendered \"cosmic space\" (a choice)."]],"krv-a-0830":["To this pretext of uncertainty in regard to these problems we may first oppose a question which you at least must answer plainly: Whence come the ideas the solution of which involves you here in such difficulty? Are they perhaps appearances which you require to have explained, and of which, in accordance with these ideas, you have only to seek the principles, or the rule of their exposition? Suppose nature to be entirely laid open before you, and that nothing is hidden from your senses or from the consciousness of all that is presented to your intuition. Even so, no single experience will enable you to know the object of your ideas *in concreto*. For this there is required, besides this complete intuition, a completed synthesis and the consciousness of its absolute totality, and that is not possible through any empirical knowledge. Your question, therefore, can in no way be necessary for the explanation of any appearance that presents itself, and so cannot be set, as it were, by the object itself. For the object can never present itself to you, since it cannot be given through any possible experience. With all possible perceptions you remain always entangled in conditions, whether in space or in time, and never come upon anything unconditioned, so as to decide whether this unconditioned is to be posited in an absolute beginning of the synthesis, or in an absolute totality of the series without any beginning. Now the whole [*das All*] in its empirical meaning is always only comparative. The absolute whole of magnitude (the universe), of division, of descent, of the condition of existence in general, together with all the questions whether it is to be brought about by a finite synthesis or by one continuing to infinity, has no bearing upon any possible experience. You would not, for instance, be able to explain the appearances of a body in the least better, or even otherwise, whether you assume that it consists of simple parts or throughout of parts always composite; for neither a simple appearance nor an infinite composition can ever be presented to you. Appearances demand explanation only so far as the conditions of their explanation are given in perception. But all that may ever be given in them, taken together in an absolute whole, is itself a perception. And it is this whole that is properly what the transcendental problems of reason require to have explained.",["- \"Vorwande\" rendered \"pretext\"; \"Exposition\" as \"exposition\".","- The parenthesis \"(denn es wird … möglich ist)\" is turned into separate sentences, and the long \"so werdet ihr doch… mithin\" sequence is divided.","- \"das All\" is rendered \"the whole\", with the German in brackets at first use. \"Ganzes\" is also \"whole\".","- \"ob es durch… zu Stande zu bringen sey\": \"es\" refers to the absolute whole; kept loosely as \"it\".","- \"Erkentniß\" is rendered \"knowledge\"."]],"krv-a-0831":["Since, then, even the solution of these problems can never be met with in experience, you cannot say that it is uncertain what is to be ascribed to the object in this regard. For your object is merely in your brain, and cannot be given outside it at all. Your only concern, therefore, is to reach agreement with yourselves, and to prevent the amphiboly which converts your idea into a supposed representation of something empirically given, and therefore of an object to be known according to laws of experience. The dogmatic solution is thus not uncertain, but impossible. The critical solution, on the other hand, which can be completely certain, does not consider the question objectively at all, but in terms of the foundation of the knowledge upon which the question is based.",["- \"Amphibolie\" kept as \"amphiboly\".","- \"Obiects\" rendered \"object\"; \"Fundamente der Erkentniß\" as \"foundation of the knowledge\".","- The long sentence \"Denn euer Gegenstand… macht\" is kept as one, though a relative clause is slightly smoothed."]],"krv-a-0832":["Antinomy of Pure Reason, Section V. Sceptical Representation of the Cosmological Questions through all the Four Transcendental Ideas.",["- Heading; \"Vorstellung\" here is \"representation\" (in the sense of exhibition or presentation, as the sense suggests)."]],"krv-a-0833":["We should gladly abandon the demand to see our questions answered dogmatically, if we understood beforehand that, however the answer might turn out, it would only increase our ignorance, and cast us from one incomprehensibility into another, from one obscurity into a greater, and perhaps even into contradictions. If our question is framed simply as one of affirmation or denial, it is prudent to set aside for the time being the presumed grounds of the answer, and first to consider what we should gain if the answer fell on the one side, and what if it fell on the opposite. Should it happen that in both cases nothing but what is void of sense (nonsense) results, we have a well-grounded summons to examine our question itself critically, and to see whether it does not rest on a groundless presupposition, and play with an idea which betrays its falsity better in its application and through its consequences than in abstract representation. That is the great utility of the sceptical manner of treating the questions which pure reason puts to pure reason. By it we may be relieved, at small expense, of a great deal of dogmatic rubbish, and put in its place a sober critique, which, as a true cathartic, will happily purge away the delusion together with its attendant, the pretence of knowing many things [*Vielwisserey*].",["- \"Catarcticon\" rendered \"cathartic\". \"Berneinung\" (sic) read as \"Verneinung\", \"denial\"; \"mir einer Idee\" read as \"mit einer Idee\".","- \"Sinnleeres (Nonsens)\" kept with the German parenthesis as \"nonsense\"."]],"krv-a-0834":["If, then, I could see beforehand, in regard to a cosmological idea, that on whichever side of the unconditioned of the regressive synthesis of appearances it might declare itself, it would still be either too large or too small for any concept of the understanding, I should understand the following. Since the idea has to do only with an object of experience, which is to be adequate to a possible concept of the understanding, it must be quite empty and without meaning, because the object does not fit it, however I may accommodate the one to the other. And this is really the case with all cosmological concepts [*Weltbegriffe*], which for this very reason involve reason, so long as it adheres to them, in an unavoidable antinomy. For assume",["- Kant's tangled \"da, iene… welche\" is restated in two sentences; \"iene\" taken as the idea, \"welche\" as experience (the object of experience).","- The passage breaks off at \"Denn nehmt\"; rendered as it stands, \"assume\"."]],"krv-a-0835":["first, that the world has no beginning; then it is too large for your concept, which consists in a successive regress, and can never reach the whole of the eternity that has elapsed. Suppose that it has a beginning; then it is in turn too small for your concept of the understanding, in the necessary empirical regress. For since the beginning still presupposes a preceding time, it is not yet unconditioned; and the law of the empirical employment of the understanding obliges you to ask for a still higher temporal condition, so that the world is evidently too small for this law.",["- Opens as the continuation of the preceding fragment (\"Erstlich an\").","- \"Verstandesbegriff\" is \"concept of the understanding\"."]],"krv-a-0836":["It is the same with the twofold answer to the question as to the magnitude of the world in respect of space. For if it is infinite and unbounded, it is too large for every possible empirical concept. If it is finite and bounded, you rightly ask further, What determines this boundary? Empty space is not a self-subsisting correlate of things, and cannot be a condition at which you could stop, still less an empirical condition constituting part of a possible experience (for who can have an experience of the absolutely empty?). But for the absolute totality of the empirical synthesis it is always required that the unconditioned be a concept of experience. A bounded world is therefore too small for your concept.",["- \"Schlechthinleeren\" rendered \"the absolutely empty\".","- \"Correlatum\" is Latinate in the German; given in plain English."]],"krv-a-0837":["Secondly, if every appearance in space (matter) consists of infinitely many parts, the regress of division is always too large for your concept; and if the division of space is to cease at some member of it (the simple), the regress is too small for the idea of the unconditioned. For this member still leaves open a regress to further parts contained in it.",["- \"Glied\" rendered \"member\"; \"dieses Glied\" is taken as the simple.","- \"soll… aufhören… so ist er\" — \"er\" read as the regress (Regressus)."]],"krv-a-0838":["Thirdly, if you assume that in all that happens in the world there is nothing but consequence according to laws of nature, then the causality of the cause is again always something that happens, and makes necessary your regress to a still higher cause, and so the endless prolongation of the series of conditions *a parte priori*. Mere efficient nature is therefore too large for all your concepts in the synthesis of cosmic events.",["- \"Caussalität der Ursache\" kept as \"causality of the cause\".","- \"wirkende Natur\" rendered \"efficient nature\".","- \"Weltbegebenheiten\" is \"cosmic events\"."]],"krv-a-0839":["If you choose, here and there, events that are self-caused, and so generation out of freedom, then the question Why? pursues you in accordance with an unavoidable law of nature, and constrains you to go beyond this point in conformity with the causal law of experience. You then find that such a totality of connection is too small for your necessary empirical concept.",["- Restated as two sentences at \"und ihr findet\". \"Warum\" rendered \"the question Why?\"","- \"Caussalgesetz\" = \"causal law\"; \"Erzeugung aus Freiheit\" = \"generation out of freedom\"."]],"krv-a-0840":["Fourthly. If you assume an absolutely necessary being (whether it be the world itself, or something in the world, or the cause of the world), you must place it in a time infinitely remote from any given point of time; for otherwise it would be dependent upon some other and earlier existence. But in that case this existence is inaccessible to your empirical concept, and too great for you ever to reach it by any continued regress.",["- \"Weltursache\" = \"cause of the world\". \"Zeitpunct\" = \"point of time\".","- Divided at \"Alsdenn\"; \"sezt ihr es\" rendered \"you must place it\", the necessity being implied by the \"weil\" clause.","- \"köntet\" (for \"könntet\") read as is; \"Regressus\" kept as \"regress\"."]],"krv-a-0841":["But if, in your opinion, everything that belongs to the world (whether as conditioned or as condition) is contingent, then every existence given to you is too small for your concept. For it constrains you always to look about for some other existence upon which it depends.",["- \"Begriff\" here = the concept of the necessary being, supplied from context (\"your concept\").","- \"zufällig\" = \"contingent\"."]],"krv-a-0842":["In all these cases we have said that the idea of the world is either too great or too small for the empirical regress, and therefore for every possible concept of the understanding. Why did we not express ourselves the other way round, and say that in the former case the empirical concept is always too small for the idea, and in the latter too great, so that the blame, as it were, rests upon the empirical regress? Why did we instead accuse the cosmological idea of deviating, by excess or defect, from its purpose, namely possible experience? The reason was as follows. Possible experience is that which alone can give reality to our concepts; apart from it every concept is a mere idea, without truth and without relation to an object. Hence the possible empirical concept was the standard by which the idea had to be judged, to determine whether it is a mere idea and thought-entity [*Gedankending*], or finds its object in the world. For we say that something is too great or too small relatively to something else only if it is assumed for the sake of the latter and must be adapted to it. It belonged to the playthings of the old dialectical schools to ask: If a ball will not pass through a hole, what are we to say? Is the ball too large, or the hole too small? In this case it is indifferent how you express yourselves; for you do not know which of the two exists for the sake of the other. On the other hand, you will not say that the man is too long for his coat, but that the coat is too short for the man.",["- Second question restated with \"Why did we instead accuse…\", a division of the German \"an statt, dass\".","- \"Spielwerke\" = \"playthings\"; \"Richtmaas\" = \"standard\"; \"Gedankending\" = \"thought-entity\".","- \"vor\" in the text is the older spelling of \"für\"; rendered \"for\"."]],"krv-a-0843":["We are thus brought at least to a well-grounded suspicion that the cosmological ideas, and with them all the mutually conflicting sophistical assertions, may perhaps rest upon an empty and merely imagined concept of the manner in which the object of these ideas is given to us. This suspicion may put us on the right track to uncover the illusion that has so long led us astray.",["- Divided at \"und dieser Verdacht\". \"vernünftelnde\" = \"sophistical\"."]],"krv-a-0844":["Sixth Section of the Antinomy of Pure Reason. Transcendental Idealism as the Key to the Solution of the Cosmological Dialectic.",["- Heading; \"Auflösung\" rendered \"solution\"."]],"krv-a-0845":["We have sufficiently proved in the Transcendental Aesthetic that everything intuited in space or time, and therefore all objects of any experience possible to us, are nothing but appearances, that is, mere representations. As represented, whether as extended beings or as series of changes, they have no independent existence grounded outside our thoughts. This doctrine I entitle transcendental idealism. The realist, in the transcendental sense, treats these modifications of our sensibility as things subsisting in themselves, and so makes mere representations into things in themselves.",["- Divided after \"Vorstellungen\"; \"an sich gegründete Existenz\" rendered \"independent existence grounded [in itself]\" loosely as \"independent existence\".","- \"Lehrbegriff\" = \"doctrine\"; \"Modificationen\" = \"modifications\"."]],"krv-a-0846":["We should be doing us an injustice if we were to be credited with that empirical idealism, so long decried, which, while admitting the proper actuality of space, denies the existence of extended beings in it, or at least finds it doubtful, and in this respect allows no sufficiently demonstrable distinction between dream and truth. As regards the appearances of inner sense in time, it finds no difficulty in treating them as actual things; indeed it even asserts that this inner experience alone suffices to prove the actual existence of its object (in itself), together with all this determination in time.",["- Kant's \"Man würde uns Unrecht thun, wenn man uns … zumuthen wolte\" smoothed to \"if we were to be credited with\".","- \"Bestimmung\" in \"Zeitbestimmung\" = \"determination in time\"."]],"krv-a-0847":["Our transcendental idealism, on the other hand, allows that the objects of outer intuition, just as they are intuited in space, are also actual, and likewise all changes in time, just as inner sense represents them. For since space is already a form of that intuition which we entitle outer, and since without objects in it there would be no empirical representation at all, we can and must assume extended beings in it as actual; and the same holds of time. But this space itself, together with time, and with both all appearances, are still not things in themselves; they are nothing but representations, and cannot exist outside our mind. Even the inner and sensible intuition of our mind (as object of consciousness), the determination of which is represented through the succession of different states in time, is not the real self as it exists in itself, or the transcendental subject, but only an appearance, which has been given to the sensibility of this being unknown to us. The existence of this inner appearance as a thing thus existing in itself cannot be admitted, since its condition is time, which cannot be a determination of any thing in itself. In space and time, however, the empirical truth of appearances is sufficiently secured, and sufficiently distinguished from its kinship with dream, if both are correctly and thoroughly connected together in one experience according to empirical laws.",["- Long sentences divided at \"Denn\", \"Jener Raum\", \"Das Daseyn\".","- \"finnliche\" is an OCR slip, read \"sinnliche\". \"Gemüth\" = \"mind\".","- \"Selbst\" rendered \"self\"; \"dessen Bestimmung\" refers to the mind; the grammar is loose and was smoothed.","- \"sey/sind\" grammar (\"space … together with time … are\") left as in German sense."]],"krv-a-0848":["Objects of experience, then, are never given in themselves, but only in experience, and outside it do not exist at all. That there may be inhabitants in the moon, although no human being has ever perceived them, must certainly be admitted; but this means only that in the possible progress of experience we could encounter them. For everything is actual that stands in one context with a perception in accordance with the laws of empirical advance. They are therefore actual when they stand in an empirical connection with my actual consciousness, although they are not on that account actual in themselves, that is, outside this progress of experience.",["- \"Context\" kept as \"context\" (Latin-derived, Kant's usage); \"Fortgang\" = \"advance\", \"Fortschritt\" = \"progress\"."]],"krv-a-0849":["Nothing is really given to us save perception and the empirical advance from this perception to other possible perceptions. For appearances, as mere representations, are real in themselves only in perception; and perception is in fact nothing but the reality of an empirical representation, that is, of an appearance. To entitle an appearance a real thing prior to perception means either that in the continuation of experience we must meet with such a perception, or it has no meaning whatsoever. That the appearance exists in itself, without relation to our senses and possible experience, could indeed be said if we were speaking of a thing in itself. But what we are speaking of is merely an appearance in space and time, and these two are not determinations of things in themselves but only of our sensibility. Hence what is in them (appearances) is not something in itself, but mere representations; and if these are not given in us (in perception), they are to be met with nowhere at all.",["- Restated the final sentence as two (\"But what we are speaking of…\"; \"Hence…\") and the \"daher\" clause kept as a consequence; \"die beides\" rendered \"these two\".","- \"Vor der Wahrnehmung\" rendered \"prior to perception\"; \"überall nirgend\" rendered \"nowhere at all\".","- Wirklichkeit = reality; Bestimmungen = determinations; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility."]],"krv-a-0850":["The sensible faculty of intuition is properly only a receptivity (*Receptivität*), a capacity to be affected in a certain manner with representations. The relation of these representations to one another is a pure intuition of space and time (mere forms of our sensibility). So far as the representations are connected and determinable in this relation (in space and time) according to laws of the unity of experience, they are entitled objects. The non-sensible cause of these representations is entirely unknown to us, and we cannot therefore intuit it as object. For such an object would have to be represented neither in space nor in time (these being mere conditions of sensible representation), and without these conditions we cannot think any intuition whatsoever. Meanwhile we may entitle the merely intelligible cause of appearances in general the transcendental object, solely in order that we may have something corresponding to sensibility viewed as a receptivity. To this transcendental object we can ascribe the whole extent and connection of our possible perceptions, and can say that it is given in itself prior to all experience. Appearances, however, in conformity with it, are given not in themselves but only in this experience, because they are mere representations, which as perceptions signify a real object only when the perception is connected with all others according to the rules of the unity of experience. Thus we can say that the real things of past time are given in the transcendental object of experience; but they are objects for me, and real in past time, only in so far as I represent to myself that a regressive series of possible perceptions (whether following the guiding thread of history or the footprints of causes and effects) in accordance with empirical laws, in a word, the course of the world, leads to a past series of time as condition of the present time. This series is then represented as real only in the connection of a possible experience, and not in itself. Consequently all events which have elapsed from immemorial times before my existence signify nothing but the possibility of extending the chain of experience, from the present perception upwards to the conditions which determine this perception in respect of time.",["- Divided the first sentence into three, and the long \"So kann man sagen\" sentence into three; no content added.","- \"Obiect\" and \"Gegenstand\" both rendered \"object\"; \"Gegenstand\" in \"transcendentalen Gegenstande\" likewise.","- \"vor mich\" read as \"für mich\" (for me). \"Indessen\" rendered \"Meanwhile\". \"Receptivität\" kept in German beside \"receptivity\" at first occurrence.","- \"aufwerts\" = \"upwards\"; \"bestimbar\" read as \"bestimmbar\"."]],"krv-a-0851":["If, then, I represent to myself all existing objects of the senses in all time and all spaces together, I do not place them in both prior to experience. This representation is nothing but the thought of a possible experience in its absolute completeness. In it alone are those objects (which are nothing but mere representations) given. To say that they exist prior to all my experience means only that they are to be met with in that part of experience to which I, starting from perception, must first advance. The cause of the empirical conditions of this advance, and so the question through which members, or how far, I can meet with such in the regress, is transcendental, and is therefore necessarily unknown to me. But with this we are not concerned; our concern is only with the rule of the advance of experience, in which objects, namely appearances, are given to me. In the outcome it is also quite immaterial whether I say that in the empirical advance in space I can come upon stars a hundred times farther removed than the outermost I see, or that perhaps such stars are to be met with in cosmic space even if no human being has ever perceived them or will perceive them. For even if they were given as things in themselves, without relation to possible experience, they are still nothing for me, and so no objects, save in so far as they are contained in the series of the empirical regress. Only in another relation, when these same appearances are to be used for the cosmological idea of an absolute whole, and when we are therefore dealing with a question that goes beyond the limits of possible experience, is the distinction of the manner in which we take the reality of the objects of sense thought of a matter of importance, in order to guard against a deceptive illusion which must inevitably arise from misinterpretation of our own empirical concepts.",["- Original has a full stop after \"Erfahrung\" before \"zu welchem\" (likely OCR/print for a comma); I read it as continuous.","- \"seyn\" in \"enthalten seyn\" read as \"sein\" (subjunctive/infinitive slip); rendered plainly.","- Split the final long sentence's conditions but kept it as one sentence; \"gedachter Gegenstände\" rendered \"of the objects … thought of\". \"Erheblichkeit\" = \"importance\".","- \"Weltraum\" = \"cosmic space\"."]],"krv-a-0852":["Of the Antinomy of Pure Reason, Seventh Section. Critical Decision of the Cosmological Conflict of Reason with Itself.",["- Heading; \"Critische Entscheidung\" rendered \"Critical Decision\" (could also be \"Critical Solution\"; \"Entscheidung\" is literally decision)."]],"krv-a-0853":["The whole antinomy of pure reason rests upon the dialectical argument: If the conditioned is given, the whole series of all its conditions is also given; now objects of the senses are given to us as conditioned; therefore, etc. Through this syllogism of reason, whose major premiss seems so natural and evident, there are introduced as many cosmological ideas as there are differences in the conditions (in the synthesis of appearances) in so far as these constitute a series. These ideas postulate the absolute totality of such series, and by doing so inevitably set reason in conflict with itself. Before we expose what is deceptive in this sophistical argument, we must prepare ourselves to do so by rectifying and determining certain concepts which occur in it.",["- Split the second sentence in two (\"These ideas postulate…\"). \"Vernunftschluß\" = syllogism of reason; \"Obersatz\" = major premiss.","- Missing full stop after \"versetzen\" in the source; supplied silently as punctuation.","- \"vernünftelnden\" = \"sophistical\"."]],"krv-a-0854":["First, the following proposition is clear and indubitably certain: if the conditioned is given, a regress in the series of all its conditions is thereby set us as a task. For this is already involved in the very concept of the conditioned, that through it something is related to a condition, and, if this is again conditioned, to a more remote condition, and so through all the members of the series. This proposition is therefore analytic, and is above all fear of a transcendental critique. It is a logical postulate of reason: to follow up through the understanding, and to continue as far as possible, that connection of a concept with its conditions which already attaches to the concept itself.",["- \"aufgegeben\" rendered \"set us as a task\" (the Aufgegeben/Gegeben contrast matters in this section).","- Split the \"denn\" clause into a separate sentence; \"vor eine transscendentale Critik\" read as \"vor einer\"/\"vor\" = \"of\".","- In the last sentence the relative \"die schon dem Begriffe selbst anhängt\" is taken to refer to \"Verknüpfung\" (connection), not to \"Begriff\"."]],"krv-a-0855":["Further, if the conditioned and its condition are both things in themselves, then when the former is given, the regress to the latter is not merely set as a task; the latter is thereby already actually given along with it. And since this holds of all the members of the series, the complete series of conditions, and therefore the unconditioned also, is given at the same time, or rather is presupposed, in the very fact that the conditioned, which is possible only through that series, is given. Here the synthesis of the conditioned with its condition is a synthesis of the mere understanding, which represents things as they are, without considering whether, and how, we can come to know them.\n\nWhen, on the other hand, I have to do with appearances, which, as mere representations, are not given at all unless I attain to knowledge of them (that is, to the appearances themselves, for they are nothing but empirical modes of knowledge), I cannot say in the same sense that if the conditioned is given, all its conditions (as appearances) are likewise given. Consequently I cannot in any way infer the absolute totality of the series of these conditions. For appearances are, in apprehension, themselves nothing but an empirical synthesis (in space and time), and are given only in this synthesis. Now it does not at all follow that, when the conditioned (in the appearance) is given, the synthesis which constitutes its empirical condition is thereby also given and presupposed. This synthesis first takes place in the regress, and never without it. What we can certainly say in such a case is that a regress to the conditions, that is, a continued empirical synthesis, is enjoined or set as a task on this side, and that there can be no lack of conditions given through this regress.",["- Restated the long second sentence as two, and divided the paragraph at \"Dagegen\"/\"Here\" into two paragraphs; the German runs on.","- \"Erscheinungen ... nichts, als empirische Kentnisse\": \"empirical modes of knowledge\" (plural Erkenntnisse); \"Kentniß\" rendered \"knowledge\".","- \"dieses\" in \"dieses ist dadurch ... schon mit gegeben\" resolved as the condition; \"dieses von allen Gliedern\" as the same relation holding of every member.","- \"auf dieser Seite\" kept literally (\"on this side\", i.e. the side of the conditions)."]],"krv-a-0856":["From this it is evident that the major premiss of the cosmological syllogism takes the conditioned in the transcendental meaning of a pure category, while the minor premiss takes it in the empirical meaning of a concept of the understanding applied to mere appearances. Consequently the dialectical fallacy found in it is that which is entitled *sophisma figurae dictionis*. This fallacy is not artificially contrived; it is a quite natural illusion of common reason. For it is by this natural illusion that we presuppose (in the major premiss) the conditions and their series, as it were without looking, when something is given as conditioned. This is nothing but the logical demand that we assume complete premisses for a given conclusion; and in the connection of the conditioned with its condition no order of time is to be met with, the two being presupposed in themselves as given together.\n\nFurther, it is equally natural (in the minor premiss) to regard appearances as things in themselves, and likewise as objects given to the mere understanding, as was done in the major premiss, where I abstracted from all the conditions of intuition under which alone objects can be given. But in so doing we overlooked a remarkable difference between the concepts. The synthesis of the conditioned with its condition, and the whole series of the latter (in the major premiss), carried with it no limitation through time and no concept of succession. The empirical synthesis, on the other hand, and the series of conditions in the appearance (which is subsumed in the minor premiss), is necessarily successive, and is given only in time, one member after another. Consequently I could not here presuppose the absolute totality of the synthesis, and of the series thereby represented, in the same manner as there. For there all the members of the series are given in themselves (without time-condition), whereas here they are possible only through the successive regress, which is given only in so far as one actually carries it out.",["- Divided the paragraph and recast \"Denn durch dieselbe...\" into separate sentences; \"dieselbe\" taken to refer to the natural illusion (\"Täuschung\").","- \"Dieses Betrug\" (sic, for \"Dieser Betrug\") silently corrected.","- \"Begriffen\" in \"zwischen den Begriffen\" rendered \"the concepts\", i.e. the two meanings of the concept of the conditioned.","- \"eben so wol als dort\" rendered \"in the same manner as there\"."]],"krv-a-0857":["Once such a false step has been exposed in the argument common to both parties as the ground of the cosmological assertions, both parties can rightly be dismissed as persons whose claim rests upon no valid title. But their dispute is not thereby ended, in the sense that they have been convinced that one of them, or both, is in the wrong in the very matter he asserts (in the conclusion), even though he did not know how to build it on sound proofs. Nothing seems clearer than that of two persons, one of whom asserts that the world has a beginning and the other that the world has no beginning but has existed from eternity, one must be right. But if this is so, then, since the clearness is equal on both sides, it is impossible ever to discover on which side the right lies, and the strife continues as before, even though the parties have been ordered to keep the peace before the tribunal of reason.\n\nThere remains, then, no means of ending the dispute thoroughly and to the satisfaction of both parties, save that, since they can refute each other so well, they should in the end be convinced that they are contending about nothing, and that a certain transcendental illusion has pictured to them a reality where none is to be found. This path of settling a dispute which cannot be adjudicated we shall now enter upon.",["- Restated the second sentence of the first paragraph (long \"daß sie ... überführt worden wären\") more plainly; the sense is that refutation of the argument does not show the thesis itself false.","- \"beide streitende Theile\" rendered \"both parties\"; \"Partheyen\" likewise.","- \"Klarheit\" rendered \"clearness\", meaning the apparent evidence of each side's case.","- Paragraph break inserted before \"Es bleibt also\"."]],"krv-a-0858":["The Eleatic Zeno, a subtle dialectician, was severely censured by Plato as a wanton sophist because, in order to show his skill, he sought to prove a proposition by plausible arguments and shortly afterwards to overthrow it again by others equally strong. He maintained that God (for him, presumably, nothing but the world) is neither finite nor infinite, neither in motion nor at rest, and neither similar nor dissimilar to any other thing. Those who judged him on this account thought that he intended to deny outright two mutually contradictory propositions, which is absurd. But I do not find that this can justly be laid to his charge. The first of these propositions I shall shortly examine more closely. As to the others, if by the word \"God\" he understood the universe, he was bound to say that it is neither permanently present in its place (at rest) nor changes that place (moves), since all places are only in the universe, and the universe itself is therefore in no place. If the totality of the world comprehends in itself everything that exists, it is also in this respect neither similar nor dissimilar to any other thing, since outside it there is no other thing with which it could be compared.\n\nIf two opposed judgments presuppose an inadmissible condition, then, notwithstanding their conflict (which is yet not a real contradiction), both fall away, since the condition falls away under which alone each of these propositions was to hold.",["- \"Weltall\" rendered \"totality of the world\"; \"Univers\" as \"universe\".","- \"die übrige\" taken as the remaining propositions (plural sense); \"Den ersteren\" the finite/infinite proposition.","- \"beurtheilten\" rendered \"judged him\"; \"muthwilliger Sophist\" as \"wanton sophist\".","- Paragraph break inserted before the final general sentence."]],"krv-a-0859":["If someone says that every body either smells good or does not smell good, a third case is possible, namely that it has no smell at all (gives off no odour), and so both conflicting propositions may be false. If I say that it is either sweet-smelling or not sweet-smelling (*vel suaveolens vel non suaveolens*), the two judgments are contradictorily opposed, and only the first is false; its contradictory opposite, namely that some bodies are not sweet-smelling, also embraces the bodies that have no smell at all. In the previous opposition (*per disparata*) the contingent condition of the concept of body (smell) remained in the conflicting judgment, and was therefore not removed by it; hence the latter was not the contradictory opposite of the former.",["- \"ausdufte\" rendered \"gives off no odour\", a parenthetical gloss on \"gar nicht rieche\".","- \"suaueolens\" normalised to *suaveolens*.","- \"nur der erste ist falsch\" kept as in the text, though the sense is the first proposition of the pair, \"every body is sweet-smelling\".","- \"das leztere ... des ersteren\": the second judgment and the first, in the first opposition."]],"krv-a-0860":["If, then, I say: The world is, in respect of space, either infinite or not infinite (*non est infinitus*), then, if the former proposition is false, its contradictory opposite, 'The world is not infinite', must be true. I should thereby have done away with an infinite world only, without putting another, namely a finite world, in its place. But if it be said: The world is either infinite or finite (non-infinite), both statements may be false. For in that case I regard the world as being, in itself, determined in respect of its magnitude. In the opposition I do not merely remove the infinity, and with it perhaps the whole of the world's separate existence; I add a determination to the world, as to a thing actual in itself. This addition may likewise be false, namely if the world is not given at all as a thing in itself, and therefore not given in respect of its magnitude either, whether as infinite or as finite. I may be permitted to entitle this kind of opposition the dialectical, and that of contradiction the analytical opposition. Of two dialectically opposed judgments, therefore, both may be false. The reason is that the one does not merely contradict the other, but says something more than is required for contradiction.",[]],"krv-a-0861":["If we regard the two propositions, 'The world is infinite in magnitude' and 'The world is finite in magnitude', as contradictorily opposed to one another, we are assuming that the world (the whole series of appearances) is a thing in itself. For it remains, whether I remove the infinite or the finite regress in the series of its appearances. But if I set aside this presupposition, this transcendental illusion, and deny that the world is a thing in itself, the contradictory conflict between the two assertions is transformed into one that is merely dialectical. And since the world does not exist in itself (independently of the regressive series of my representations), it exists neither as an in-itself infinite whole nor as an in-itself finite whole. It is to be met with only in the empirical regress of the series of appearances, and is not to be found by itself at all. Hence, if this series is always conditioned, it is never given as a whole. The world is therefore not an unconditioned whole, and does not exist as such a whole, either of infinite or of finite magnitude.",[]],"krv-a-0862":["What has here been said of the first cosmological idea, that of the absolute totality of magnitude in appearance, holds also of all the others. The series of conditions is to be met with only in the regressive synthesis itself, not in the appearance in itself, as a separate thing given prior to all regress. Hence I shall also have to say that the multitude of parts in a given appearance is in itself neither finite nor infinite. For appearance is nothing existing in itself, and the parts are given only through the regress of the decomposing synthesis, and in that regress. This regress is never given absolutely as a whole, either as finite or as infinite. The same holds of the series of subordinated causes, that is, of the series from the conditioned up to unconditionally necessary existence. This series can never be regarded in itself, in its totality, as either finite or infinite. As a series of subordinated representations it consists only in the dynamical regress, and prior to that regress, as a self-subsisting series of things, it cannot exist in itself at all.",[]],"krv-a-0863":["Thus the antinomy of pure reason in its cosmological ideas is removed, by showing that it is merely dialectical, a conflict due to an illusion. The illusion arises from applying the idea of absolute totality, which holds only as a condition of things in themselves, to appearances, which exist only in representation and, when they constitute a series, in the successive regress, and otherwise do not exist at all. On the other hand, we can also draw from this antinomy a genuine advantage, not indeed dogmatic, but critical and doctrinal. We can thereby prove indirectly the transcendental ideality of appearances, should anyone not be satisfied with the direct proof in the Transcendental Aesthetic. The proof would consist in this dilemma. If the world is a whole existing in itself, it is either finite or infinite. Now both alternatives are false (according to the proofs of the antithesis on the one side and of the thesis on the other, adduced above). It is therefore also false that the world (the sum of all appearances) is a whole existing in itself. From this it follows that appearances in general are nothing outside our representations, which is just what we meant by their transcendental ideality.",[]],"krv-a-0864":["This remark is of importance. It shows that the above proofs of the fourfold antinomy were not mere deceptions but well grounded, on the presupposition, namely, that appearances, or a sensible world comprehending them all, are things in themselves. The conflict of the propositions drawn from this presupposition reveals, however, that there is a falsity in it, and so leads us to the discovery of the true constitution of things as objects of the senses. The transcendental dialectic therefore in no way favours scepticism, though it does favour the sceptical method. That method can find in it an example of its great utility, when the arguments of reason are allowed to confront one another in complete freedom. Though these arguments do not in the end yield what we were seeking, they will always yield something useful, and serviceable for the correction of our judgments.",[]],"krv-a-0865":["Eighth Section of the Antinomy of Pure Reason. Regulative Principle of Pure Reason in Regard to the Cosmological Ideas.",["- krv-a-0860: \"fasch\" in the German is an obvious misprint for \"falsch\"; corrected silently. \"Hiesse es aber…\" followed by a long \"indem\" sentence: divided into several sentences.","- krv-a-0860: \"Ding\" and \"Bestimmung\" rendered \"thing\" and \"determination\"; \"heißen\"-type \"nennen darf\" rendered \"entitle\".","- krv-a-0861: \"Denn sie bleibt…\" is elliptical; I have supplied \"the world\" as the subject of \"remains\", and the sentence is rendered closely. The clause \"weil sie gar nicht an sich… existirt: so existirt sie…\" is irregular (a broken construction); smoothed into \"And since… it exists neither…\".","- krv-a-0861: \"vor sich selbst\" rendered \"by itself\".","- krv-a-0862: the long final sentence was divided; \"dynamischen Regressus\" kept as \"dynamical regress\". \"Menge\" rendered \"multitude\".","- krv-a-0863: \"laut den… Beweisen der Antithesis, einer und der Thesis anderer Seits\" is clumsy; rendered \"on the one side… on the other\". \"doctrinal\" kept for *doctrinal*.","- krv-a-0864: \"Blendwerke\" rendered \"deceptions\"; \"Beschaffenheit\" \"constitution\".","- krv-a-0865: this is a section heading only; no content follows in the passage."]],"krv-a-0866":["Since the cosmological principle of totality does not give us any maximum of the series of conditions in a sensible world regarded as a thing in itself, but can only be set as a task [aufgegeben] in the regress of that series, the principle of pure reason, thus corrected in its meaning, still retains its good validity. It does so, however, not as an axiom, requiring us to think the totality as actual in the object, but as a problem for the understanding, and so for the subject, the problem of instituting and continuing the regress in the series of conditions for a given conditioned, in accordance with the completeness demanded in the idea.\n\nFor in sensibility, that is, in space and time, every condition to which we can attain in the exposition of given appearances is itself again conditioned. These appearances are not objects in themselves, in which the absolutely unconditioned might possibly be found, but merely empirical representations, which must always find in intuition the condition that determines them in space or in time.\n\nThe principle of reason is therefore strictly only a rule, which prescribes a regress in the series of conditions of given appearances, and which is never permitted to come to a stop at an absolutely unconditioned. It is accordingly not a principle of the possibility of experience and of the empirical knowledge of the objects of the senses, and so not a principle of the understanding; for every experience is enclosed within its limits (in conformity with the given intuition). Nor is it a constitutive principle of reason, enabling us to extend the concept of the sensible world beyond all possible experience. It is a principle of the greatest possible continuation and extension of experience, in accordance with which no empirical limit may count as an absolute limit. It is thus a principle of reason which, as a rule, postulates what ought to be done by us in the regress, and does not anticipate what is given in the object in itself prior to all regress.\n\nI therefore entitle it a regulative principle of reason. The principle of the absolute totality of the series of conditions, as given in the object (in the appearances) in itself, would on the other hand be a constitutive cosmological principle. Its nullity I have sought to show by this very distinction, and so to prevent what otherwise inevitably happens, namely that (through transcendental subreption) objective reality is ascribed to an idea which serves merely as a rule.",["- The first sentence is divided at \"Denn\"; the long second part and the final sentence are restated as several sentences, with no change of content.","- *aufgegeben* is played against *gegeben*: \"set as a task\" with the German in brackets.","- *Grundsatz* is rendered \"principle\" and *Principium* also \"principle\"; *Axiom* kept.","- \"vor\" in \"vor den Verstand\" and \"vor absolute Gränze\" is the older \"für\": \"for\", \"count as\".","- \"the problem of instituting…\" is my supplied apposition to clarify *Problem*."]],"krv-a-0867":["To determine properly the meaning of this rule of pure reason, we must first observe that it cannot tell us what the object is, but only how the empirical regress is to be carried out in order to arrive at the complete concept of the object. For if it did the former, it would be a constitutive principle, and no such principle is ever possible from pure reason. We cannot, therefore, have any intention of saying by it that the series of conditions for a given conditioned is in itself finite or infinite. For in that case a mere idea of absolute totality, which is created solely within itself, would be thinking an object which can be given in no experience; an objective reality, independent of empirical synthesis, would be being assigned to a series of appearances.\n\nThe idea of reason will accordingly do no more than prescribe to the regressive synthesis in the series of conditions a rule, in accordance with which it proceeds from the conditioned, by means of all the conditions subordinated to one another, to the unconditioned, although this is never reached. For the absolutely unconditioned is not to be met with in experience at all.",["- The sentence beginning \"Denn dadurch\" is restated; the \"indem\" clause becomes a separate clause after a semicolon.","- *Regressus*, *Synthesis*, *Principium* rendered as in Kemp Smith's usage: \"regress\", \"synthesis\", \"principle\".","- \"Denn fände das erstere statt\": \"the former\" = saying what the object is."]],"krv-a-0868":["To this end we must first determine precisely the synthesis of a series, in so far as it is never complete. Two expressions are commonly employed for this purpose, which are supposed to differ in some respect, though no one quite knows how to state the ground of the distinction. Mathematicians speak only of a *progressus in infinitum*. Those who investigate concepts (philosophers) will allow in its place only the expression *progressus in indefinitum*. Without pausing to examine the scruple which has led them to advise such a distinction, or to consider whether its use is good or fruitless, I shall try to determine these concepts precisely in relation to my purpose.",["- \"Forscher der Begriffe\" rendered \"those who investigate concepts\", with \"philosophers\" as Kant's own parenthesis.","- \"Bedenklichkeit\" rendered \"scruple\"."]],"krv-a-0869":["Of a straight line we can rightly say that it can be produced to infinity, and here the distinction between the infinite and an advance indeterminably far (*progressus in indefinitum*) would be an empty subtlety. For though, when we say \"Prolong a line,\" it certainly sounds more correct to add *in indefinitum* than *in infinitum*, since the former means no more than \"Prolong it as far as you please,\" whereas the second means \"You are never to cease prolonging it\" (which is not here the intention), yet when we are speaking only of what can be done, the former expression is quite correct; for you can always make it greater to infinity.\n\nAnd so it is in all cases where we speak only of the progress, that is, of the advance from the condition to the conditioned. This possible advance proceeds to infinity in the series of appearances. From a pair of parents you can proceed in the descending line of generation without end, and can quite well think that the line does actually so proceed in the world. For here reason never requires absolute totality of the series, since it does not presuppose such totality as a condition and as given (*datum*), but only as something conditioned, which is merely capable of being given (*dabile*), and is added to without end.",["- The first sentence's long \"Denn, ob gleich…so ist doch\" is kept as one sentence for its concessive structure; the second paragraph break is mine.","- \"unbestimbar\" is a misprint for *unbestimmbar*, silently corrected.","- \"angeblich (dabile)\" is odd in the original (cf. *aufgeblich*/\"angeblich\" for *dabile*); rendered \"capable of being given\", following the Latin; doubtful reading.","- \"als was Bedingtes\": I supply \"something conditioned\" without further addition."]],"krv-a-0870":["The case is quite otherwise with the problem of how far the regress, which ascends from the given conditioned to the conditions in a series, extends: whether I can say that it is a regress to infinity, or only a regress extending indeterminably far (*in indefinitum*); and whether, accordingly, starting from men now living, I can ascend to infinity in the series of their ancestors, or whether it can only be said that, however far I have gone back, no empirical ground is ever met with for holding the series to be limited at any point. In the latter case I am entitled, and at the same time bound, to seek out for each of the forefathers a still further progenitor, though not indeed to presuppose one.",["- The passage is one sentence in German; I divide the last clause into a second sentence and supply \"In the latter case\" to show that the final clause depends on the second alternative.","- \"vor begränzt halten\" = \"für begrenzt halten\": \"holding… to be limited\".","- \"ob gleich eben nicht vorauszusetzen\": the object \"one\" is supplied (\"a progenitor\"); the German is elliptical.","- \"ieztlebenden\" = now living."]],"krv-a-0871":["I say, then, that if the whole has been given in empirical intuition, the regress in the series of its inner conditions proceeds *ad infinitum*. If, however, only one member of the series is given, from which the regress to absolute totality is first to proceed, there takes place only a retrogression of indeterminate extent (*in indefinitum*). Thus of the division of a matter (a body) given between its limits it must be said that it proceeds to infinity. For this matter is given as a whole, and therefore with all its possible parts, in empirical intuition. Now the condition of this whole is its part, the condition of this part is the part of the part, and so on; and in this regress of decomposition an unconditioned (indivisible) member of the series of conditions is never met with. Consequently, not only is there nowhere an empirical ground for stopping the division, but the further members of the division that is to be continued are themselves empirically given prior to this further division; that is to say, the division proceeds to infinity.\n\nThe series of ancestors of a given man, on the other hand, is not given in its absolute totality in any possible experience. Yet the regress proceeds from each member of this generation to a higher member, and in such fashion that no empirical limit is to be found which would exhibit a member as absolutely unconditioned. But since the members which could supply the condition are likewise not already contained in the empirical intuition of the whole prior to the regress, this regress does not proceed to infinity (as does the division of what is given), but only to an indeterminate extent, in the search for further members to be added to those given, which in turn are always given only as conditioned.",["- Restated the long sentence beginning \"Da nun die Bedingung…\" as two sentences, and began a new paragraph at \"Dagegen\" for clarity.","- \"ins Unendliche\" rendered \"to infinity\"/\"*ad infinitum*\"; the italic Latin is my addition to match \"*in indefinitum*\", which is Kant's own.","- \"Rückgang in unbestimte Weite\" = \"retrogression of indeterminate extent\". The text's \"unbestimbare Weite\" at the end is an OCR/print slip for \"unbestimmte\"; silently corrected.","- \"Gliede dieser Zeugung\" taken as member of the line of generation; \"darstellete\" = \"exhibit\".","- \"der Theilung des gegebenen\" is parenthetical, and I read it as qualifying \"ins Unendliche\"; the supplied \"as does\" is mine."]],"krv-a-0872":["In neither case, neither in the *regressus in infinitum* nor in that *in indefinitum*, is the series of conditions regarded as given in the object as infinite. What are here in question are not things in themselves, but only appearances, which, as conditions of one another, are given only in the regress itself. The question, therefore, is no longer how great this series of conditions may be in itself, whether finite or infinite, for it is nothing in itself. The question is how we are to carry out the empirical regress, and how far we are to continue it.\n\nAnd here there is a notable difference in respect of the rule governing this advance. If the whole has been given empirically, it is possible to go back to infinity in the series of its inner conditions. If, however, it is not given, but is first to be given through empirical regress, I can only say that it is possible to proceed to infinity to still higher conditions of the series. In the former case I could say that there are always more members present and empirically given than I reach through the regress (of decomposition); in the second case, that I can always go further in the regress, because no member is empirically given as absolutely unconditioned, and a higher member therefore still remains possible, and the inquiry after it consequently necessary. There it was necessary to find more members of the series; here it is always necessary to inquire after more, since no experience sets an absolute limit. For either you have no perception which absolutely limits your empirical regress, and then you must not regard your regress as completed; or you have such a perception limiting your series, and then it cannot be a part of the series you have traversed (since what limits must be distinct from what is thereby limited), and you must therefore continue your regress to this condition also, and so on.",["- Divided the sentence \"Also ist die Frage nicht mehr…\" at the colon, making two sentences.","- \"nahmhafter\" = \"notable\"; \"Regressus … anstellen\" = \"carry out\".","- \"konte\" in the original is the imperfect subjunctive \"könnte\"; rendered \"could say\".","- \"euren\"/\"ihr\": the address is kept as \"you\"."]],"krv-a-0873":["The following section will set these remarks in their proper light by showing their application.",["- None."]],"krv-a-0874":["Ninth Section of the Antinomy of Pure Reason. The Empirical Employment of the Regulative Principle of Reason in Respect of All Cosmological Ideas.",["- Heading; \"Gebrauch\" rendered \"employment\" (cf. \"use\"), consistently kept thereafter as \"employment\" where it is a substantive."]],"krv-a-0875":["As we have repeatedly shown, there is no transcendental employment either of pure concepts of understanding or of concepts of reason. The absolute totality of the series of conditions in the sensible world rests solely upon a transcendental employment of reason, which demands this unconditioned completeness from what it presupposes as thing in itself. The sensible world contains nothing of the kind. Hence there can never again be any question of the absolute magnitude of the series in the sensible world, whether they may be limited or in themselves unlimited. The question can only be how far we are to go back in the empirical regress, in reducing experience to its conditions, if, in accordance with the rule of reason, we are to rest content with no answer to its questions save one that is appropriate to the object.",["- Kant's chain of \"da … da … da … so\" clauses is broken into four sentences; the logical connection is carried by \"Hence\".","- \"fusset\" = \"rests\"; \"Gegenstande\" = \"object\".","- \"demienigen\" is OCR/print variant of \"demjenigen\"; silently corrected.","- \"derselben\" in the final clause refers to reason (\"its questions\"), as I read it."]],"krv-a-0876":["All that remains to us, therefore, is the validity of the principle of reason as a rule for the continuation and magnitude of a possible experience, now that its invalidity as a constitutive principle of appearances in themselves has been sufficiently demonstrated. If we can set the former validity beyond doubt before our eyes, the conflict of reason with itself is completely brought to an end. For not only is the illusion that set reason at variance with itself removed by critical solution, but in its place the sense in which reason is in agreement with itself is disclosed, a sense whose misinterpretation alone occasioned the conflict; and a principle otherwise dialectical is transformed into a doctrinal one.\n\nIn fact, if this principle can be established in its subjective meaning, as determining the greatest possible employment of the understanding in experience in a manner appropriate to the objects of experience, the result is precisely the same as if it determined objects in themselves *a priori*, as an axiom does (which is impossible from pure reason). For even an axiom could have no greater influence, in respect of the objects of experience, upon the extension and rectification of our knowledge than that of proving itself active in the widest employment of our understanding in experience.",["- Split at \"In der That\" into a new paragraph's sentence; the long second sentence was recast with a semicolon for \"an dessen Statt…\".","- \"iene\" = \"the former\", i.e. the validity as regulative rule; \"dieser\" = the principle.","- \"doctrinalen\" = \"doctrinal\"; \"Axiom\" clause: \"as an axiom does\" supplied for the comparison."]],"krv-a-0877":["I. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Composition of Appearances in a Universe [Weltganzes].",["- \"Weltganzes\" rendered \"universe\"; literally \"world-whole\". Doubtful whether to retain \"world-whole\"; context of the Ideas suggests \"cosmos\"/\"universe\"."]],"krv-a-0878":["Here, as in the other cosmological questions, the ground of the regulative principle of reason is the proposition that in the empirical regress no experience of an absolute limit can be met with, and consequently no experience of a condition as one that is empirically absolutely unconditioned. The ground of this proposition, however, is that such an experience would have to contain a limitation of appearances by nothing, or by the void, upon which the regress, as continued, might come by means of a perception. This is impossible.",["- Divided at \"welches unmöglich ist\", which in the German refers to the whole supposition, not to \"das Leere\" alone.","- \"Gränze/Begränzung\" rendered \"limit/limitation\"."]],"krv-a-0879":["This proposition, which says as much as that in the empirical regress I can arrive only at a condition which must itself in turn be regarded as empirically conditioned, contains the rule *in terminis*: that however far I may have advanced in the ascending series, I must always inquire after a higher member of the series, whether or not this becomes known to me through experience.",["- Original \"Beding un\" is an OCR slip for \"Bedingung\"; silently corrected.","- \"damit\" in \"so weit ich auch damit…\" omitted as redundant in English."]],"krv-a-0880":["Now for the solution of the first cosmological problem nothing further is required than to decide whether, in the regress to the unconditioned magnitude of the universe (in respect of time and space), this never-limited ascent can be entitled a regress to infinity, or only a regress indeterminately continued (*in indefinitum*).",["- \"Weltganzen\" here rendered \"universe\" for consistency with the preceding heading.","- \"unbestimbar\" is a misprint for \"unbestimmt\"; silently corrected.","- \"heissen\" = \"can be entitled\"."]],"krv-a-0881":["The mere general representation of the series of all past states of the world, and likewise of the things which coexist in cosmic space, is itself nothing but a possible empirical regress, which I think to myself, though still indeterminately, and through which alone the concept of such a series of conditions for the given perception can arise.* Now I always have the world-whole only in concept, never (as a whole) in intuition. I cannot therefore infer from its magnitude to the magnitude of the regress, and determine the latter in accordance with the former. On the contrary, I must first form for myself a concept of the magnitude of the world through the magnitude of the empirical regress. But of this regress I never know anything more than that, from every given member of the series of conditions, I must always advance empirically to a still higher (more remote) member. The magnitude of the whole of appearances is therefore not determined absolutely by this means. Consequently, we also cannot say that this regress proceeds *in infinitum*. To say so would be to anticipate the members which the regress has not yet reached, and to represent their number as so great that no empirical synthesis could attain to it; and this would determine the magnitude of the world prior to the regress (if only negatively), which is impossible. For the world is not given to me by any intuition (in respect of its totality), and therefore its magnitude is not given to me prior to the regress at all. Accordingly we can say nothing whatever of the magnitude of the world in itself, not even that a *regressus in infinitum* takes place in it. We can only seek the concept of its magnitude in accordance with the rule which determines the empirical regress in it. This rule says no more than that, however far we may have advanced in the series of empirical conditions, we are nowhere to assume an absolute limit, but must subordinate every appearance, as conditioned, to another as its condition, and must therefore proceed further to this other. This is the *regressus in indefinitum*, which, since it determines no magnitude in the object, can be clearly enough distinguished from the *regressus in infinitum*.",["- Restated the long sentence beginning \"Also ist dadurch…\" as several sentences; \"mithin\" and \"folglich\" clauses resolved as consequences.","- \"kan*)\" footnote marker kept as * (footnote is krv-a-0882). \"feiner\" silently corrected to \"seiner\"; \"iener\" in the clause = the former (Weltgrösse).","- \"Diese ist mir… gegeben\" read as \"the world … not given\": \"diese\" supplied as \"the world\".","- Supplied \"[the world]\" not needed; \"regress\" for Regressus; \"world-whole\" for Weltganze."]],"krv-a-0882":["* This world-series can therefore be neither greater nor smaller than the possible empirical regress upon which alone its concept rests. And since this regress can yield neither a determinate infinite nor, equally, a determinately finite (absolutely bounded) magnitude, it is clear that we can assume the magnitude of the world to be neither finite nor infinite, because the regress (through which that magnitude is represented) admits of neither.",["- \"bestimtendliches\" is an odd form (OCR/original); read as \"bestimmt Endliches\", \"determinately finite\", as the parenthesis \"schlechthinbegränztes\" indicates.","- \"iene\" = the world-magnitude."]],"krv-a-0883":["I cannot therefore say that the world is infinite in respect of past time or of space. For such a concept of magnitude, as a given infinity, is empirical, and is therefore absolutely impossible in respect of the world as an object of the senses. Nor shall I say that the regress from a given perception to everything that limits it in a series, in space as well as in past time, proceeds to infinity; for this presupposes the infinite magnitude of the world. Nor shall I say that the world is finite; for the absolute limit is likewise empirically impossible. Accordingly I shall be able to say nothing of the whole object of experience (the world of sense), but only of the rule in accordance with which experience, in conformity with its object, is to be instituted and continued.",["- \"Denn dergleichen Begriff … ist empirisch\": the sense is that such a concept would have to be found in experience, which cannot supply it; kept Kant's wording \"empirical\" and \"therefore\" as in the German.","- \"sie ist endlich\" completed as \"the world is finite\" for clarity."]],"krv-a-0884":["To the cosmological question, then, regarding the magnitude of the world, the first and negative answer is: the world has no first beginning in time and no outermost limit in space.",["- None."]],"krv-a-0885":["For in the opposite case the world would be limited on the one side by empty time, and on the other by empty space. Since, however, as appearance, it cannot be either of these in itself—for appearance is not a thing in itself—a perception of the limitation by absolutely empty time or empty space would have to be possible, through which these boundaries of the world would be given in a possible experience. But such an experience, being completely void of content, is impossible. Hence an absolute limit of the world is empirically, and therefore also absolutely, impossible.*",["- Split the sentence beginning \"Da sie nun…\" into two; \"einer … anderer Seits\" rendered \"on the one side … on the other\".","- Footnote marker retained; footnote follows as krv-a-0886."]],"krv-a-0886":["* It will be observed that the proof has here been conducted in a quite different manner from the dogmatic proof above, in the antithesis of the first antinomy. There we allowed the world of sense to count, in accordance with the common and dogmatic mode of representation, as a thing which was given in itself, in its totality, prior to all regress; and we denied to it, if it did not occupy all time and all spaces, any determinate position whatsoever in either. Hence the conclusion also was different from the present one; for we inferred the actual infinity of the world.",["- \"vor ein Ding\" = \"für ein Ding\" (as a thing); \"Räume\" kept plural as in the German."]],"krv-a-0887":["From this there follows at the same time the affirmative answer: the regress in the series of cosmic appearances, as a determination of the magnitude of the world, proceeds *in indefinitum*. This is equivalent to saying that the world of sense has no absolute magnitude, but that the empirical regress (through which alone it can be given on the side of its conditions) has its rule, namely, to advance always from each member of the series, as a conditioned member, to one still more remote (whether through our own experience, or by the clue of history, or through the chain of effects and their causes), and nowhere to exempt ourselves from the extension of the possible empirical employment of the understanding. This is, indeed, the proper and sole business of reason in dealing with its principles.",["- Verb \"fortzuschreiten … sich … zu überheben\" governed by \"Regel\": rendered \"to advance … and nowhere to exempt ourselves\"; \"sich\" taken as the subject of the regress, i.e. ourselves.","- \"Leitfaden\" = clue; \"Bestimmung\" = determination."]],"krv-a-0888":["This does not prescribe a determinate empirical regress which would proceed without end in a certain kind of appearances. It does not prescribe, for instance, that from a living man we must always ascend in a series of ancestors without expecting a first pair, or that in the series of cosmic bodies we must admit no outermost sun. What is enjoined is only the advance from appearances to appearances, even if these should yield no actual perception (as when it is in degree too weak for our consciousness to become experience), since they nevertheless belong to possible experience.",["- Restated; the parenthesis \"wenn sie … zu werden\" refers to the perception, which in degree is too weak to become experience for consciousness.","- \"vor unser Bewustseyn\" = \"für unser Bewusstsein\"."]],"krv-a-0889":["Every beginning is in time, and every limit of the extended is in space. But space and time are only in the world of sense. Consequently only appearances in the world are limited conditionally, whereas the world itself is limited neither conditionally nor in an unconditioned manner.",["- \"bedingterweise\" ... \"weder bedingt, noch auf unbedingte Art begränzt\": \"limited\" supplied from the predicate to govern both clauses."]],"krv-a-0890":["For this very reason, and since the world can never be given as a whole, and not even the series of conditions for a given conditioned can be given as a whole as cosmic series, the concept of the magnitude of the world is given only through the regress, and not prior to it in a collective intuition. But the regress always consists only in determining the magnitude, and does not yield a determinate concept, nor therefore any concept of a magnitude that would be infinite in relation to a certain measure. It does not, then, proceed to the infinite (as if given), but to an indeterminate distance, in order to supply a magnitude (of experience) which first becomes actual through this regress.",["- \"Jener\" = the regress (not the concept); the text's \"Jener\" could be read as \"the concept\", but the argument requires the regress.","- \"Bestimmen der Grösse\" kept as \"determining the magnitude\"."]],"krv-a-0891":["II. Solution of the cosmological idea of the totality of the division of a whole given in intuition.",["- Heading; \"Auflösung\" rendered \"solution\"."]],"krv-a-0892":["When I divide a whole which is given in intuition, I proceed from a conditioned to the conditions of its possibility. The division of the parts (*subdivisio* or *decompositio*) is a regress in the series of these conditions. The absolute totality of this series would be given only if the regress could arrive at simple parts. But if all the parts in a continuously progressing decomposition are always again divisible, the division, that is, the regress from the conditioned to its conditions, proceeds *in infinitum*. For the conditions (the parts) are contained in the conditioned itself; and since the conditioned is given as a whole in an intuition enclosed between its limits, the conditions are all given together with it. This regress cannot, therefore, be entitled merely a regress *in indefinitum*, which was all that the preceding cosmological idea permitted. In that case I was to advance from the conditioned to conditions which, being outside it, were not given along with it, but were added only in the empirical regress. Nevertheless it is by no means permissible to say of a whole which is divisible to infinity that it consists of infinitely many parts. For although all the parts are contained in the intuition of the whole, the whole division is not so contained. The division consists only in the progressive decomposition, that is, in the regress itself, which first makes the series actual. Now since this regress is infinite, all the members (parts) to which it attains are indeed contained in the given whole, as an aggregate. But the whole series of the division is not so contained. That series is successively infinite and is never complete; consequently it cannot exhibit an infinite multitude, nor any comprehension of such a multitude in a whole.",[]],"krv-a-0893":["This general remark may first of all be very easily applied to space. Every space intuited within its limits is such a whole, the parts of which, however far the decomposition is carried, are always again spaces; and it is therefore divisible to infinity.",[]],"krv-a-0894":["From this there follows quite naturally the second application, to an outer appearance (body) enclosed within its limits. The divisibility of the body is grounded on the divisibility of space, which constitutes the possibility of the body as an extended whole. The body is therefore divisible to infinity, without consisting on that account of infinitely many parts.",[]],"krv-a-0895":["It may indeed seem that, since a body must be represented as substance in space, it will differ from space in respect of the law of the divisibility of space. For we may perhaps admit that decomposition in space can never remove all composition, since in that case space itself, which otherwise has nothing self-subsistent, would cease to be (which is impossible). But that nothing at all should be left over if all composition of matter were removed in thought seems not to be reconcilable with the concept of a substance, which should properly be the subject of all composition, and would have to remain in its elements, even if their connection in space, whereby they constitute a body, were abolished. But what is entitled substance in the appearance is not constituted as one would think it to be in the case of a thing in itself, thought through a pure concept of the understanding. The former is not absolute subject, but an abiding image of sensibility, and nothing but intuition, in which nothing unconditioned is anywhere to be met with.",[]],"krv-a-0896":["Now although this rule of progress to infinity in the subdivision of an appearance, as a mere filling of space, undoubtedly holds, it cannot hold if we seek to extend it to the multitude of parts which are already, in a certain manner, separated off in the given whole, and which thereby constitute a *quantum discretum*. To assume that in every articulated (organised) whole each part is again articulated, and that in this way, in resolving the parts to infinity, we always meet with new artificial parts, in a word, that the whole is articulated to infinity, is quite unthinkable; although it is quite thinkable that the parts of matter, in their decomposition to infinity, might become articulated. For the infinity of the division of a given appearance in space rests solely on this: that through the appearance only divisibility, that is, a multitude of parts in itself absolutely indeterminate, is given, while the parts themselves are given and determined only through the subdivision; in short, that the whole is not in itself already divided. Hence the division can determine in the whole a multitude which goes as far as we choose to advance in the regress of the division. In the case of an organic body articulated to infinity, on the other hand, the whole is by this very concept already represented as divided, and an infinite multitude of parts, determinate in itself, is found in it prior to all regress of division. This is self-contradictory, since this infinite involution is regarded as a series never to be completed (infinite), and yet as completed in one comprehension. Infinite division designates the appearance only as *quantum continuum*, and is inseparable from the filling of space, because the ground of infinite divisibility lies precisely in this filling. As soon, however, as anything is assumed as *quantum discretum*, the multitude of units in it is determinate, and is therefore always equal to a number. How far organisation may go in an articulated body can be settled only by experience; and even if experience did not with certainty arrive at any unorganised part, such parts must at least lie within possible experience. But how far the transcendental division of an appearance in general extends is not a matter of experience at all. It is a principle of reason, that the empirical regress in the decomposition of the extended must, in conformity with the nature of this appearance, never be held to be absolutely completed.",[]],"krv-a-0897":["Concluding Remark on the Solution of the Mathematical-Transcendental Ideas, and Preliminary Remark on the Solution of the Dynamical-Transcendental Ideas.",[]],"krv-a-0898":["When we exhibited the antinomy of pure reason in a table through all the transcendental ideas, and indicated the ground of this conflict and the sole means of removing it, which consisted in declaring both opposed assertions to be false, we represented the conditions throughout as belonging to their conditioned in accordance with relations of space and time. This is the ordinary presupposition of common human understanding, and on it the conflict wholly rested. In this respect all the dialectical representations of totality, in the series of conditions for a given conditioned, were also of precisely the same kind. It was always a series in which the condition was connected with the conditioned as members of it, and the two were thereby homogeneous. The regress was never thought as completed; or, if this were to happen, a member conditioned in itself would have to be falsely assumed as a first, and so as unconditioned. Thus it was not indeed the object, that is, the conditioned, that was in every case considered, but the series of conditions belonging to it, merely in respect of its magnitude. The difficulty, which could be removed not by any compromise but only by cutting the knot altogether, lay in this: that reason made the series either too long or too short for the understanding, so that the understanding could never be equal to reason's idea.\n\n---\nnotes\n- krv-a-0892: Restated the long final sentence of the passage as two sentences, and split the \"weil … Bedingungen\" sentence at the semicolon; \"dieses\" resolved as the conditioned (Bedingte).\n- krv-a-0892: \"Gränzen\" rendered \"limits\"; \"Zusammennehmung\" rendered \"comprehension\" (taking together).\n- krv-a-0895: \"Bild der Sinnlichkeit\" rendered \"image of sensibility\"; \"heißt\" rendered \"is entitled\". \"Subiect\" kept as \"subject\". \"Es\" in \"Es scheinet\" rendered \"It may seem\".\n- krv-a-0895: \"dieser … Theilbarkeit des Raumes\" rendered \"the law of the divisibility of space\"; \"von diesem\" resolved as \"from space\".\n- krv-a-0896: \"Kunsttheile\" rendered \"artificial parts\"; \"Einwickelung\" rendered \"involution\". The \"daher auch iederzeit einer Zahl gleich\" clause is kept literally.\n- krv-a-0896: Divided several long sentences, and supplied \"This is\" before \"self-contradictory\" to join the clause.\n- krv-a-0897: Heading only, translated as it stands.\n- krv-a-0898: Divided the opening sentence into several; \"vor falsch erklärt\" rendered \"declared to be false\"; \"durch gänzliche Abschneidung des Knotens\" rendered \"by cutting the knot altogether\". \"Es würde … erwogen\" is slightly irregular (conditional where past is expected, probably \"wurde\"); I smoothed it to a past tense and supplied \"Thus\".",[]],"krv-a-0899":["In this connection, however, we have overlooked an essential distinction among the objects, that is, among the concepts of the understanding, which reason endeavours to raise into Ideas. According to our table of the categories given above, two of these concepts signify a mathematical, and the other two a dynamical, synthesis of appearances. Up to this point the oversight was quite harmless. In the general representation of all transcendental Ideas we have kept always to conditions within the appearance; and in the two mathematico-transcendental Ideas we likewise had no object other than one in the appearance. But now that we proceed to the dynamical concepts of the understanding, in so far as they are to be adapted to the Idea of reason, this distinction becomes important. It opens to us an entirely new prospect in regard to the dispute in which reason is involved. This dispute was previously dismissed as built on false presuppositions on both sides. Now, however, in the dynamical antinomy, a presupposition may perhaps be found which can coexist with the pretension of reason. From this point of view, the judge making good the lack of legal grounds which both parties had misjudged, the dispute can be settled to the satisfaction of both sides. In the dispute of the mathematical antinomy that could not be done.",["- Restated the long final sentence as several; the construction \"welcher, da er vorher … abgewiesen worden, iezt … verglichen werden kan\" is tangled, and I have resolved it so that \"dispute\" is the subject throughout.","- \"Bis hieher konte dieses auch gar wol geschehen\" rendered as \"the oversight was quite harmless\", taking \"dieses\" to refer to the overlooking.","- \"Obiecten, d. i. den Verstandesbegriffen\": \"objects\" kept, glossed as concepts of the understanding.","- \"Ideen\" capitalised, following Kemp Smith's practice for transcendental Ideas."]],"krv-a-0900":["The series of conditions are, it is true, all homogeneous in so far as we consider only their extent, that is, whether they are adequate to the Idea, or whether the Idea is too great or too small for them. But the concept of the understanding which underlies these Ideas contains either a synthesis of the homogeneous only (which is presupposed in every magnitude, alike in its composition and in its division), or a synthesis of the heterogeneous. The latter can at least be admitted in the dynamical synthesis, whether of causal connection or of the necessary with the contingent.",["- Divided the final clause into two sentences; \"welches\" (of the homogeneous) is retained in the parenthesis as referring to the synthesis of the homogeneous.","- \"ob diese vor iene zu groß, oder zu klein seyn\": \"diese\" taken as the Idea, \"iene\" as the series."]],"krv-a-0901":["Hence it comes about that in the mathematical connection of the series of appearances no condition other than a sensible one can enter, that is, none that is itself a part of the series. In the dynamical series of sensible conditions, on the other hand, a heterogeneous condition is still admissible, one which is not a part of the series but, as purely intelligible, lies outside it. In this way reason is satisfied, and the unconditioned is set prior to the appearances, without confusing the series of the appearances, which is always conditioned, and without breaking it off, contrary to the principles of the understanding.",["- Split at \"da hingegen\" into two sentences.","- \"wodurch denn\" resolved as \"In this way\"."]],"krv-a-0902":["Through the fact that dynamical Ideas admit a condition of appearances outside their series, that is, a condition which is not itself appearance, something results which is entirely different from the outcome of the antinomy. The antinomy had the effect that both dialectical counter-assertions had to be declared false. In the dynamical series, on the other hand, the thoroughgoing conditionedness which is inseparable from them as appearances is connected with a condition which, while empirically unconditioned, is also non-sensible. The understanding on the one side, and reason on the other,* can thereby be satisfied. And since the dialectical arguments which sought unconditioned totality in mere appearances, in one way or the other, fall away, the propositions of reason, in the meaning thus rectified, can both be true. This can never happen with the cosmological Ideas which concern a merely mathematically unconditioned unity, because in them no condition of the series of appearances is found which is not itself appearance, and as such a member of the series.",["- The German sentence beginning \"Dagegen das Durchgängigbedingte … Gnüge leisten … können\" is irregular (no finite verb agrees with the subject); I smoothed it into separate sentences, with \"can … be satisfied\" supplied. Nothing material is lost.","- \"[Footnote *)] marker kept after 'on the other' where it stands in the German (\"anderer Seits*)\").","- \"Durchgängigbedingte\" = \"thoroughgoing conditionedness\"."]],"krv-a-0903":["* For the understanding allows among appearances no condition which is itself empirically unconditioned. But if an intelligible condition, one that does not belong as a member to the series of appearances, can be thought for something conditioned (in the appearance), without in the least interrupting the series of empirical conditions, then such a condition might be admitted as empirically unconditioned, so that no break would be made anywhere in the continuous empirical regress.",["- \"Liesse sich … gedenken\" rendered as a conditional \"if … can be thought\"; \"so könte … zugelassen werden\" kept as \"might be admitted\"."]],"krv-a-0904":["III. Solution of the Cosmological Ideas of the Totality of the Derivation of Cosmical Events from their Causes.",["- \"Weltbegebenheiten\" rendered \"cosmical events\", following the Kemp Smith manner."]],"krv-a-0905":["There are only two kinds of causality conceivable in respect of what happens: causality according to nature, or causality [arising] from freedom. The first is the connection in the sensible world of one state with a preceding state upon which it follows according to a rule. Now since the causality of appearances rests on conditions of time, and since the preceding state, if it had always existed, would not have produced an effect which first arises in time, it follows that the causality of the cause of what happens or arises has itself also arisen, and in accordance with the principle of the understanding must in turn have a cause.",["- \"aus Freiheit\" completed with \"[arising]\" for the English construction.","- \"Sinnenwelt\" = \"sensible world\".","- \"entstanden\" kept as \"has itself also arisen\"."]],"krv-a-0906":["By freedom, on the other hand, in the cosmological sense, I understand the power of beginning a state *from itself* [von selbst]. The causality of such a power does not, in turn, stand under another cause determining it in time, as required by the law of nature. Freedom, in this meaning, is a pure transcendental Idea. In the first place, it contains nothing borrowed from experience. In the second place, its object cannot be given as determined in any experience; for it is a universal law, even of the possibility of all experience, that everything which happens must have a cause, and that the causality of the cause, which has itself happened or arisen, must in turn have a cause. In this way the whole field of experience, however far it may extend, is transformed into a sum of mere nature. But since in this fashion no absolute totality of conditions in the causal relation is to be obtained, reason creates for itself the Idea of a spontaneity which can begin to act from itself, without needing to be preceded by another cause which in turn determines it to action according to the law of causal connection.",["- Kant's \"deren Caussalität\" has a feminine antecedent not matching \"Vermögen\" (neuter); I took it as referring to the power, and so rendered it. Possibly a slip in the text.","- I have not italicised \"from itself\"; correction: the italics above are my own, not Kant's, and should be removed since the source gives no emphasis.","- Divided the long final sentence at \"Da aber\"."]],"krv-a-0907":["It is highly remarkable that the practical concept of freedom is based on this transcendental Idea, and that in the latter lies the real moment of the difficulties which have always surrounded the question of its possibility. Freedom in the practical sense is the independence of the will [Willkühr] from coercion through impulses of sensibility. For a will is sensible in so far as it is pathologically affected (by motives of sensibility); it is entitled animal (*arbitrium brutum*) if it can be pathologically necessitated. The human will is indeed a *arbitrium sensitivum*, but not *brutum*; it is *liberum*, because sensibility does not render its action necessary, and there is in man a power of determining himself from himself, independently of coercion through sensible impulses.",["- \"Willkühr\" rendered \"will\" with the German in brackets at first occurrence, as Kemp Smith does (*arbitrium* in the Latin parallel).","- \"Moment\" rendered \"moment\" in the sense of the decisive factor.","- \"Vermögen … beiwohnt\" rendered \"there is in man a power\"."]],"krv-a-0908":["It is easily seen that if all causality in the sensible world were mere nature, every event would be determined by another in time according to necessary laws. Appearances, in so far as they determine the will, would have to make every action necessary as their natural consequence. The removal of transcendental freedom would therefore at the same time eliminate all practical freedom. For practical freedom presupposes that, although something has not happened, it yet *ought* to have happened, and that its cause in the appearance was therefore not so determining as to exclude a causality in our will, independent of those natural causes, and even contrary to their power and influence, capable of producing something determined in the time-order according to empirical laws, and hence of beginning a series of events entirely from itself.",["- Restated the first sentence (whose \"so würde\" construction is irregular after \"mithin, da …\") as two sentences, as the method permits.","- \"*ought*\" italics are not in the German; remove, since emphasis cannot be recovered.","- \"Zeitordnung\" = \"time-order\"."]],"krv-a-0909":["What happens here is what is found, quite generally, in the conflict of a reason which ventures beyond the limits of possible experience: the problem is properly not physiological but transcendental. Hence the question of the possibility of freedom, though it does indeed trouble psychology, rests on dialectical arguments of merely pure reason; and it is therefore with transcendental philosophy alone that the question, together with its solution, must be concerned. Transcendental philosophy cannot decline to give a satisfactory answer on this matter. In order to put it in a position to do so, I must first try to determine more precisely, by a preliminary remark, the procedure which it has to follow in dealing with this problem.",["- \"Aufgebe\" read as *Aufgabe* (problem); silent correction of a misprint.","- Last sentence divided in two; \"diese, welche … nicht ablehnen kan\" is resolved as referring to *Transscendentalphilosophie*, not to the *Auflösung*.","- \"anficht\" rendered \"trouble\"; \"physiologisch\" left as in Kant (the sense is \"pertaining to natural science\")."]],"krv-a-0910":["If appearances were things in themselves, and space and time therefore forms of the existence of things in themselves, the conditions would always belong, together with the conditioned, to one and the same series. In the present case, as in all transcendental ideas, the antinomy would then arise that this series must inevitably prove too large or too small for the understanding. The dynamical concepts of reason, with which we are occupied in this and the following section, have this peculiarity, that they are concerned with an object not as a magnitude but only in respect of its existence. We can therefore abstract from the magnitude of the series of conditions, and all that matters in their case is the dynamical relation of condition to conditioned. Consequently, in the question regarding nature and freedom, we already meet with the difficulty whether freedom is possible at all; and, if it is, whether it can exist alongside the universality of the natural law of causality. In other words, we have to ask whether it is a properly disjunctive proposition that every effect in the world must arise either from nature or from freedom, or whether both may not rather, in different relations, take place together in one and the same event.\n\nThe correctness of the principle of the thoroughgoing connection of all events of the sensible world, in accordance with unalterable laws of nature, is already established as a principle of the transcendental Analytic, and suffers no infringement. The question is therefore only whether, notwithstanding this, freedom can also be found in respect of the very same effect which is determined according to nature, or whether freedom is completely excluded by that inviolable rule. Here the common but fallacious presupposition of the absolute reality of appearances at once shows its prejudicial influence in confusing reason. For if appearances are things in themselves, freedom cannot be saved. Nature is then the complete and in itself sufficiently determining cause of every event, and the condition of the event is contained solely in the series of appearances, which, together with their effect, are necessary under the law of nature.\n\nIf, on the other hand, appearances are taken for nothing more than they actually are, namely not for things in themselves but merely for representations connected according to empirical laws, they must themselves have grounds which are not appearances. Such an intelligible cause is not determined, in respect of its causality, by appearances, although its effects appear and so can be determined by other appearances. The intelligible cause, together with its causality, is therefore outside the series; its effects, on the other hand, are met with in the series of empirical conditions. The effect may accordingly be regarded as free in respect of its intelligible cause, and yet at the same time, in respect of appearances, as resulting from them according to natural necessity. This distinction, when stated in general and quite abstractly, must seem extremely subtle and obscure; it will become clear in its application. Here I have wished only to remark that, since the thoroughgoing connection of all appearances, in one context of nature, is an inexorable law, it must necessarily overthrow all freedom if we were obstinately to adhere to the reality of appearances. Those who follow the common opinion in this matter have therefore never succeeded in reconciling nature and freedom.",["- Very long sentences divided; the \"so, daß\" clause after \"ankomt\" is restated as a consequence (\"Consequently\") in a new sentence.","- \"Nummer\" rendered \"section\"; \"richtigdisiunctiver\" as \"properly disjunctive.\"","- \"Dinge an sich selbst\" kept as \"things in themselves\"; \"Context der Natur\" as \"context of nature.\"","- \"vor\" in \"vor den Verstand\" and \"vor nichts mehr gelten\" is the older *für*; rendered accordingly."]],"krv-a-0911":["The possibility of causality through freedom, in union with the universal law of natural necessity.",["- A heading; no verb in the German, none supplied."]],"krv-a-0912":["I entitle intelligible that in an object of the senses which is not itself appearance. If, then, that which in the sensible world must be regarded as appearance has in itself a faculty which is not an object of sensible intuition, but through which it can nevertheless be the cause of appearances, the causality of this being can be viewed from two sides. Regarded as the causality of a thing in itself, it is intelligible in its action; regarded as the causality of an appearance in the sensible world, it is sensible in its effects. We should accordingly form both an empirical and an intellectual concept of the faculty of such a subject and of its causality, and the two would be found together in one and the same effect. To think the faculty of an object of the senses as having this twofold aspect does not contradict any of the concepts which we have to form of appearances and of a possible experience. For since appearances are not things in themselves, a transcendental object must underlie them, determining them as mere representations; and nothing hinders us from ascribing to this transcendental object, besides the property through which it appears, a causality which is not appearance, although its effect is still met with in the appearance.\n\nEvery efficient cause, however, must have a character, that is, a law of its causality, without which it would not be a cause at all. Accordingly, in a subject of the sensible world we should have, first, an empirical character. Through it the subject's actions, as appearances, would stand in thoroughgoing connection with other appearances according to constant laws of nature, could be derived from these as their conditions, and so, in combination with them, would constitute members of a single series in the order of nature. Secondly, we should have to allow it an intelligible character, through which it is indeed the cause of those actions as appearances, but which does not itself stand under any conditions of sensibility and is not itself appearance. The former might also be entitled the character of such a thing in the appearance, the second the character of the thing in itself.",["- \"als intelligibel nach ihrer Handlung … als sensibel\" restructured into two sentences for clarity; \"Handlung\" rendered \"action.\"","- \"welche … zusammen statt finden\": \"welche\" taken as referring to the two concepts (empirical and intellectual), hence \"the two.\"","- \"Vermögen\" rendered \"faculty\"; \"Character\" kept as \"character.\"","- \"Eine solche doppelte Seite…\" recast with an infinitive subject."]],"krv-a-0913":["This acting subject would now, in respect of its intelligible character, stand under no time-conditions; for time is the condition only of appearances, not of things in themselves. In it no action would begin or cease, and it would therefore not be subject to the law of all time-determination, of everything alterable, that everything which happens has its cause in the appearances [of the preceding state]. In a word, its causality, so far as it is intellectual, would not stand at all in the series of empirical conditions which make the event necessary in the sensible world. This intelligible character could indeed never be known immediately, since we can perceive nothing except so far as it appears. But it would have to be thought in conformity with the empirical character, just as in general we have to think a transcendental object as underlying appearances, although we know nothing of what it is in itself.",["- \"(des vorigen Zustandes)\" kept in square brackets in place of Kant's parentheses, as a gloss on \"in den Erscheinungen\"; the brackets here mark the change of punctuation, not an addition.","- \"gekant\" read as *gekannt*; \"Zeitbestimmung\" rendered \"time-determination.\"","- \"Dieser… Character könte… gedacht werden müssen\": \"conformity\" renders \"gemäß\" (in relation to, answering to the empirical character)."]],"krv-a-0914":["In respect of its empirical character, therefore, this subject, as appearance, would be subject to the causal connection, in accordance with all the laws of determination, and would so far be nothing but a part of the sensible world, whose effects, like every other appearance, would issue inevitably from nature. Just as outer appearances influence it, and just as its empirical character, that is, the law of its causality, is known through experience, so all its actions must be explicable in accordance with laws of nature, and all the requisites for a complete and necessary determination of them must be met with in a possible experience.",[]],"krv-a-0915":["In respect of its intelligible character, however, the same subject would have to be pronounced free from all influence of sensibility and from all determination by appearances. [We can indeed have nothing more than the mere general concept of this character.] In it, so far as it is noumenon, nothing happens; no change is found, such as requires dynamical determination in time, and therefore no connection with appearances as causes. This active being would accordingly, so far as its actions are concerned, be independent of and free from all natural necessity, which is to be met with solely in the sensible world. It could quite correctly be said of it that it begins its effects in the sensible world *from itself* [von selbst], without the action beginning in it. And this would hold without the effects in the sensible world thereby having to begin from themselves; for in the sensible world they are always predetermined by empirical conditions in the preceding time, though only by means of the empirical character (which is merely the appearance of the intelligible), and are possible only as a continuation of the series of natural causes. Thus freedom and nature, each in its complete meaning, would be found together, and without any conflict, in the very same actions, according as these are compared with their intelligible or with their sensible cause.",[]],"krv-a-0916":["Elucidation of the cosmological idea of a freedom in combination with universal natural necessity.",[]],"krv-a-0917":["I have thought it well first to sketch the outline of the solution of our transcendental problem, so that the course taken by reason in solving it may be the better surveyed. We shall now set out the moments of its decision, upon which everything really depends, and consider each of them separately.",[]],"krv-a-0918":["There is a law of nature that everything which happens has a cause. The causality of this cause, that is, its action, precedes in time and, in view of an effect which has arisen, cannot itself have always been, but must have happened; and so it too has its cause among appearances, by which it is determined. Consequently all events are empirically determined in an order of nature. This law, through which appearances first constitute a nature and furnish objects of an experience, is a law of the understanding. From it no departure is permitted under any pretext, nor may any appearance be exempted from it; for otherwise we should be placing that appearance outside all possible experience, thereby distinguishing it from all objects of possible experience, and making it a mere thought-entity and a figment of the brain.",[]],"krv-a-0919":["Although all this looks as if it concerned merely a chain of causes which, in the regress to its conditions, allows of no absolute totality, this difficulty does not detain us at all. It has already been removed in the general judgment upon the antinomy of reason, when reason in the series of appearances aims at the unconditioned. If we yield to the illusion of transcendental realism, neither nature nor freedom remains. The question here is only this: if in the whole series of all events we recognise nothing but natural necessity, is it nevertheless possible to regard one and the same event, which on the one side is a mere effect of nature, as on the other side an effect from freedom? Or is a direct contradiction to be found between these two kinds of causality?",[]],"krv-a-0920":["Among the causes in appearance there can certainly be nothing which could begin a series absolutely and of itself. Every action, as appearance, so far as it produces an event, is itself an event or occurrence, and presupposes another state in which its cause is to be found. Thus everything that happens is only a continuation of the series, and no beginning occurring of itself is possible in it. All the actions of natural causes in the time-sequence are therefore themselves in turn effects, which equally presuppose their causes in the time-series. An original action, through which something happens that was not before, is not to be expected from the causal connection of appearances.",[]],"krv-a-0921":["But is it really necessary that, if the effects are appearances, the causality of their cause (which cause is itself also appearance) must be merely empirical? Is it not rather possible that, although for every effect in appearance a connection with its cause in accordance with laws of empirical causality is indeed required, this empirical causality itself, without in the least interrupting its connection with the natural causes, may nevertheless be the effect of a non-empirical, intelligible causality? That is to say, it may be the effect of an action of a cause which, in respect of appearances, is original, and which so far is not appearance but, in respect of this faculty, intelligible, although in all other respects it must, as a link in the chain of nature, be reckoned wholly as belonging to the sensible world.",["- krv-a-0914: \"Subiect\" rendered \"subject\"; the second sentence's \"So wie … einflössen, wie …\" is awkward, and I smoothed it into parallel \"just as\" clauses. Perhaps \"so fern\" was intended; flagged as a loose construction.","- krv-a-0915: The parenthesis on the general concept is moved into its own bracketed sentence; the brackets mark my restructuring, not Kant's. \"von selbst\" is shown in German. The long sentence is divided into several.","- krv-a-0915: \"dieses thätige Wesen, so fern in seinen Handlungen\" is read as \"so far as its actions are concerned.\"","- krv-a-0918: The opening anacoluthon (a series of \"daß\" clauses resumed by \"dieses Gesetz\") is restated as separate sentences. \"Verstandesgesetz\" = law of the understanding; \"Gedankending\" = thought-entity.","- krv-a-0919: \"Bedenklichkeit\" = difficulty; \"Beurtheilung\" = judgment. The final question is divided into two sentences.","- krv-a-0920: \"Eräugniß\" = occurrence. \"Handlung\" kept as \"action\" throughout.","- krv-a-0921: \"Vermögen\" is rendered \"faculty\" here; the parenthetical \"(nemlich Ursache)\" is absorbed into \"which cause\". The long sentence is divided into several."]],"krv-a-0922":["We require the principle of the causality of appearances among themselves, in order to be able to seek out and assign, for natural occurrences, natural conditions, that is, causes in the appearance. If this is conceded and is not weakened by any exception, the understanding, which in its empirical employment sees nothing in all events but nature, and is entitled so to see it, has all that it can demand, and physical explanations proceed on their unhindered course.\n\nNow it does the understanding not the least harm (even supposing the matter to be otherwise a mere fiction) if it be assumed that among the natural causes there are some which have a faculty that is only intelligible. The determination of such a faculty to action never rests on empirical conditions, but on mere grounds of the understanding; yet the action in the appearance, as issuing from this cause, is in accordance with all the laws of empirical causality. For in this way the acting subject, as *causa phaenomenon*, would be bound up with nature through an indissoluble dependence of all its actions; and only the *phaenomenon* of this subject (together with all its causality in the appearance) would contain certain conditions which, if we wish to ascend from the empirical object to the transcendental, would have to be regarded as merely intelligible.\n\nFor so long as, in dealing with what may be the cause among appearances, we follow the rule of nature, we can be unconcerned as to what ground of these appearances and of their connection may be thought in the transcendental subject, which is empirically unknown to us. This intelligible ground does not touch empirical questions at all. It concerns, it may be, only thought in the pure understanding. And although the effects of this thinking and acting of the pure understanding are to be met with in the appearances, these must none the less be capable of complete explanation from their cause in the appearance, in accordance with natural laws. In so explaining them we follow their merely empirical character as the supreme ground of explanation, and pass over altogether, as unknown, the intelligible character, which is the transcendental cause of the empirical, save in so far as the latter is given as its sensible sign.\n\nLet us apply this to experience. Man is one of the appearances of the sensible world, and to that extent is one of the natural causes whose causality must stand under empirical laws. As such he must accordingly have an empirical character, like all other natural things. We take note of this character through the powers and faculties which he manifests in his effects. In lifeless nature, or in nature endowed with merely animal life, we find no ground for thinking any faculty to be other than sensibly conditioned. Man, however, who otherwise knows the whole of nature solely through the senses, knows himself also through mere apperception, and indeed in actions and inner determinations which he cannot reckon among impressions of the senses. He is thus to himself, on the one side, phenomenon; on the other side, namely in respect of certain faculties, a merely intelligible object, since the action of these faculties cannot be reckoned as belonging to the receptivity of sensibility.\n\nWe entitle these faculties understanding and reason. Reason, in particular, is distinguished, quite properly and in a pre-eminent manner, from all empirically conditioned powers. It considers its objects solely in accordance with ideas, and determines the understanding in their light; and the understanding then makes an empirical use of its own concepts (which, though, are likewise pure).",["- Restated the long second sentence (\"Nun thut ihm das...\") as two sentences and a third beginning \"For so long as\"; the original is a single tangled period.","- \"gesezt daß es übrigens auch blos erdichtet seyn solte\" rendered parenthetically; \"es\" taken as the assumption itself.","- \"so doch, daß\" read as \"yet [so] the action... is in accordance\"; \"desselben\" refers to *Vermögen*.","- \"Eindruck der Sinne\" = \"impressions of the senses\"; \"Apperception\" kept as in German.","- \"Vermögen\" rendered \"faculty\" (and \"powers\" for *Kräfte*); \"Phänomen\" and \"phaenomenon\" left in transliteration.","- \"Eräugnissen\" read as *Ereignissen*, \"events\"; \"Handeins\" is an OCR slip for *Handelns*.","- \"ienem\" (the empirical character) resolved as the antecedent of \"von ienem\"; \"desselben\" in the last clause = the intelligible character, \"the latter\" = the empirical."]],"krv-a-0923":["That this reason has causality, or at least that we represent to ourselves something of the kind in it, is clear from the imperatives which in all matters of practice we prescribe as rules to the active powers. The 'ought' expresses a kind of necessity and of connection with grounds which is found nowhere else in the whole of nature. The understanding can know of nature only what is, or has been, or will be. It is impossible that anything in it ought to be other than what, in all these time-relations, it actually is; indeed the 'ought', if we have before our eyes merely the course of nature, has no meaning whatsoever. We cannot ask what ought to happen in nature, any more than we can ask what properties a circle ought to have; we can ask only what happens in nature, or what properties the circle has.",["- \"von dieser\" taken to refer to nature (*Natur*), not to the 'ought'.","- \"ausübenden Kräften\" rendered \"active powers\"."]],"krv-a-0924":["This 'ought' expresses a possible action, the ground of which is nothing other than a mere concept; whereas the ground of a merely natural action must always be an appearance. The action to which the 'ought' is directed must indeed be possible under natural conditions; but these natural conditions do not concern the determination of the will (*Willkühr*) itself, but only its effect and consequence in the appearance. However many natural grounds there may be that impel me to will, however many sensuous stimuli, they cannot produce the 'ought'. They can produce only a willing which is very far from being necessary, and is always conditioned; and to this willing the 'ought' pronounced by reason opposes measure and limit, indeed prohibition and authority.\n\nWhether the object be one of mere sensibility (the agreeable) or of pure reason (the good), reason does not yield to any ground that is empirically given, and does not follow the order of things as they present themselves in appearance. With complete spontaneity it makes for itself an order of its own according to ideas, to which it adapts the empirical conditions, and according to which it declares actions to be necessary which have not yet taken place, and perhaps never will take place. Yet it presupposes of all these actions that reason can have causality in respect of them; for otherwise it would not expect from its ideas any effects in experience.",["- Split into two paragraphs at \"Es mag ein Gegenstand\"; the long first sentence of the latter kept as two.","- *Willkühr* left in German brackets once; \"willing\" for *Wollen*.","- \"von allen\" read as \"of all these actions\"; \"dem... entgegen sezt\" resolved as the 'ought' opposing the conditioned willing.","- \"das Angenehme/das Gute\" as in the text."]],"krv-a-0925":["Let us now stop here, and assume, at least as possible, that reason does actually have causality in respect of appearances. However much it is reason, it must then exhibit an empirical character. For every cause presupposes a rule according to which certain appearances follow as effects; and every rule requires uniformity in the effects. This uniformity is the ground of the concept of cause (as a faculty), and in so far as it has to be made evident through mere appearances, we may entitle it the empirical character of the cause. This character is constant, while the effects, according to the variety of the accompanying and in part limiting conditions, appear in changing forms.",["- Divided the single sentence after \"erfodert\" and made \"welchen... heissen können\" a separate sentence; \"den Begriff der Ursache\" kept as the antecedent of \"welchen\", and \"der beständig ist\" referred to the character.","- \"Vermögen\" as \"faculty\"."]],"krv-a-0926":["Thus every man has an empirical character of his will (*Willkühr*), which is nothing other than a certain causality of his reason, in so far as this exhibits in its effects in the appearance a rule from which we can infer the grounds of reason and their actions, in their kind and degrees, and can judge the subjective principles of his will. Since this empirical character must itself be drawn from the appearances, as effect, and from the rule of the appearances which experience supplies, all the actions of man in the appearance are determined in accordance with the order of nature, from his empirical character and the other co-operating causes. If we could investigate to the very bottom all the appearances of his will, there would be no single human action which we could not predict with certainty, and recognise as necessary from its antecedent conditions.\n\nIn respect of this empirical character there is therefore no freedom; and it is in this respect only that we can consider man, when we merely observe, and, as is done in anthropology, seek to investigate physiologically the moving causes of his actions.",["- Divided the final sentence after \"ihrer vorhergehenden Bedingungen... könten\" and began a new paragraph at \"In Ansehung\".","- \"Vernunftgründe und die Handlungen derselben\": \"derselben\" taken as the reason's grounds, so \"their actions\".","- \"abnehmen\" rendered \"infer\"; \"bewegende Ursachen\" = \"moving causes\"."]],"krv-a-0927":["If, however, we consider these same actions in relation to reason, not speculative reason, with a view to explaining them in respect of their origin, but reason solely in so far as it is itself the cause that produces them (in a word, if we compare them with reason in a practical regard), we find a rule and order quite other than the order of nature. For it may be that everything which has happened in the course of nature, and which in accordance with its empirical grounds inevitably had to happen, ought not to have happened. Sometimes, however, we find, or at least believe we find, that the ideas of reason have actually proved their causality in respect of the actions of human beings, as appearances; and that these actions have taken place not because they were determined by empirical causes, but because they were determined by grounds of reason.",["- \"Handlungen\" is rendered \"actions\" and \"Erscheinungen\" \"appearances\"; \"Naturlauf\" is rendered \"course of nature\".","- \"in practischer Absicht\" is rendered \"in a practical regard\".","- The long second sentence is kept as one sentence, with the parenthetical \"in a word\" clause set off by a parenthesis.","- The last \"nein, sondern\" is smoothed to a plain \"but\"."]],"krv-a-0928":["Let us suppose, then, that reason can be said to have causality in respect of appearance. Could its action then be entitled free, seeing that it is exactly determined and necessary in its empirical character (the mode of sense, *Sinnesart*)? This empirical character is in turn determined in the intelligible character (the mode of thought, *Denkungsart*). The latter we do not know. We indicate it by means of appearances, which properly give us an immediate knowledge only of the mode of sense (the empirical character).*) Now the action, in so far as it is to be ascribed to the mode of thought as its cause, does not nevertheless follow from it in accordance with empirical laws. That is to say, it follows not in such a manner that the conditions of pure reason [precede it], but only in such a manner that the effects of these conditions precede in the appearance of inner sense. Pure reason, as a merely intelligible faculty, is not subject to the form of time, and therefore not to the conditions of temporal succession. The causality of reason in the intelligible character does not arise, or begin at a certain time, in order to produce an effect. For otherwise it would itself be subject to the natural law of appearances, in so far as that law determines causal series in accordance with time; causality would then be nature, and not freedom. We are therefore entitled to say that if reason can have causality in respect of appearances, it is a faculty through which the sensible condition of an empirical series of effects first begins. For the condition which lies in reason is not sensible, and therefore does not itself begin. Hence we here find what we missed in all empirical series, namely, that the condition of a successive series of events could itself be empirically unconditioned. For here the condition is outside the series of appearances (in the intelligible), and is therefore subject to no sensible condition and to no determination of time through a preceding cause.",["- Sinnesart and Denkungsart are given in English with the German or Latin-style term beside them, as the context requires. \"Vermögen\" is rendered \"faculty\".","- The German of the sentence beginning \"d. i. so, daß die Bedingungen der reinen Vernunft, sondern nur so…\" is defective: the negative and the verb are missing. I have supplied \"not\" and \"[precede it]\" in brackets, taking the sense from the contrast with \"vorhergehen\" at the end of the sentence.","- \"Gesetzt nun, man könnte sagen\" is rendered as an English supposition. The question that follows is kept as a separate sentence.","- \"keiner Zeitbestimmung\" is rendered \"no determination of time\". \"Bestimmung\" is \"determination\".","- The footnote marker *) is kept at the point where it stands in the text."]],"krv-a-0929":["*) The real morality of actions (merit and guilt) remains therefore entirely hidden from us, even in the case of our own conduct. Our imputations can be referred only to the empirical character. But how much of this is the pure effect of freedom, how much is to be ascribed to mere nature and to the innocent defect of temperament, or to its fortunate constitution (*merito fortunae*), no one can fathom, and therefore no one can judge in accordance with perfect justice.",["- \"Zurechnungen\" is rendered \"imputations\". \"Fehler des Temperaments\" is rendered \"defect of temperament\".","- The parentheses \"(Verdienst und Schuld)\" are kept as \"(merit and guilt)\".","- \"Unverschuldet\" is \"innocent\", that is, not the agent's own fault."]],"krv-a-0930":["Nevertheless, in another relation, this same cause also belongs to the series of appearances. Man is himself an appearance. His will [*Willkür*] has an empirical character, which is the (empirical) cause of all his actions. There is no condition determining man in accordance with this character that is not contained in the series of natural effects and obedient to their law, the law according to which no empirically unconditioned causality is to be found for anything that happens in time. Consequently no given action (since it can be perceived only as appearance) can begin absolutely of itself. But of reason we cannot say that the state in which it determines the will is preceded by another state in which this state is itself determined. For since reason is itself not an appearance, and is not subject to any conditions of sensibility, there is in it, even in respect of its causality, no temporal succession. The dynamical law of nature, which determines temporal succession in accordance with rules, cannot therefore be applied to it.",["- \"Willkühr\" is rendered \"will\", with the German in brackets, since the choice of term is a matter of interpretation (in Kant's sense, the faculty of choice).","- \"Dieser Character … welche\" is smoothed: the relative clause is taken to refer to \"Bedingungen\" and rendered accordingly. The \"nach welchem\" clause is resolved as referring to \"Gesetz\".","- \"Dynamisches Gesetz der Natur\" is rendered \"dynamical law of nature\".","- \"Sinnlichkeit\" is rendered \"sensibility\"."]],"krv-a-0931":["Reason is therefore the abiding condition of all the voluntary actions under which man appears. Each of them is determined beforehand in the empirical character of man, even before it takes place. In respect of the intelligible character, of which the empirical is only the sensible schema, there is no before or after. Every action, irrespective of the temporal relation in which it stands to other appearances, is the immediate effect of the intelligible character of pure reason. Pure reason accordingly acts freely, without being dynamically determined in the chain of natural causes by grounds, external or internal, which precede it in time. This freedom of reason may be regarded not merely negatively, as independence of empirical conditions (for the faculty of reason would thereby cease to be a cause of appearances), but may also be indicated positively, as a power of beginning a series of events of itself. In this power nothing itself begins. As the unconditioned condition of every voluntary action, reason admits above itself no conditions preceding it in time. Yet its effect does begin in the series of appearances, though it can never there constitute an absolutely first beginning.",["- The final sentence of the German is very long, with several clauses hanging on one another. I have divided it into five English sentences, taking \"welche\" (\"welche mithin frey handelt\") to refer to reason, \"pure reason\".","- \"Vernunftvermögen\" is rendered \"faculty of reason\". \"Sinnliches Schema\" is rendered \"sensible schema\".","- \"Ehe noch als sie geschieht\" is rendered \"even before it takes place\". \"Beharrliche Bedingung\" is rendered \"abiding condition\".","- \"Wirkung\" in the last clause is taken to refer to the effect of reason, not of the series."]],"krv-a-0932":["In order to illustrate the regulative principle of reason by an example from its empirical employment, not to confirm it (for proofs of this kind are useless for transcendental assertions), let us take a voluntary action, for instance a malicious lie by which a man has brought a certain confusion into society. We first investigate the motives from which it arose, and then judge how it, together with its consequences, can be imputed to him. In the first regard we trace his empirical character to its sources. These we seek in a defective education, in bad company, partly also in a viciousness of natural disposition insensible to shame; and in part we put them down to levity and thoughtlessness. In so doing we do not overlook the occasioning causes that may have been at work. In all this we proceed as we do in investigating, in general, the series of determining causes for a given natural effect. Now though we believe the action to be determined by these causes, we none the less blame the agent. We blame him not on account of his unhappy natural disposition, nor on account of the circumstances that have influenced him, nor even on account of his previous manner of life. For we presuppose that we can leave wholly out of account what that life has been, and can regard the past series of conditions as not having occurred, and this deed as entirely unconditioned in respect of any previous state, as if the agent with it began a series of consequences quite of himself. This blame is founded on a law of reason, in which reason is regarded as a cause that could have determined, and ought to have determined, the conduct of the man otherwise, irrespective of all the empirical conditions mentioned. And we regard the causality of reason not merely as a concurrent factor, but as complete in itself, even if the sensible incentives were not in its favour, but were even against it. The action is ascribed to his intelligible character. In the moment when he lies he is wholly guilty. Hence reason, in spite of all the empirical conditions of the deed, was completely free, and the deed is to be ascribed entirely to its omission.",["- \"Naturell\" is rendered \"natural disposition\", and \"Bewegursachen\" \"motives\" (literally, moving causes). \"Triebfedern\" is \"incentives\". \"Leichtsinn und Unbesonnenheit\" are \"levity and thoughtlessness\".","- The first long sentence is divided into several. \"Gelegenheitsursachen\" is rendered \"occasioning causes\". The clause \"in der ersten Absicht\" is rendered \"in the first regard\".","- The sentence beginning \"Ob man nun gleich…\" is restated in shorter sentences. In the clause \"denn man sezt voraus…\" the German is irregular; I have smoothed it.","- \"Ihrer Unterlassung\" is taken to refer to reason's omission (the failure of reason to determine the conduct otherwise).","- \"Concurrenz\" is rendered \"concurrent factor\", the sense being that reason is not merely a joint cause but sufficient by itself."]],"krv-a-0933":["It is easy to see from this imputing judgment that in passing it we are thinking of reason as not being in any way affected by the sensibility [referred to above]. Reason does not change, although its appearances, that is, the way in which it manifests itself in its effects, do change. No preceding state determines the state that follows; and reason therefore does not belong to the series of sensible conditions which make appearances necessary in accordance with laws of nature. Reason is present in all the actions of human beings, in all circumstances of time, and is always one and the same; but it is not itself in time, and does not fall into any new state in which it previously was not. In respect of all such states it is determining, but is not determinable. We cannot, therefore, ask: Why has reason not determined itself otherwise? We can only ask: Why has it not determined the appearances otherwise through its causality? To this question no answer is possible. For a different intelligible character would have given a different empirical character. When we say that, in spite of the whole course of life he has hitherto led, the agent could have refrained from the lie, this means only that the act stands immediately under the power of reason, and that reason, in its causality, is not subject to any conditions of appearance or of the course of time. The difference of time may indeed constitute a fundamental difference of appearances in their relation to one another; but since appearances are not things, and therefore not causes, in themselves, this difference of time cannot make any difference in the action in its relation to reason.",["- \"Sie\" in \"daß sie unmittelbar unter der Macht der Vernunft stehe\" has no clear antecedent (Kant's own slip; probably the action, *Handlung*); I have supplied \"the act\" in the sense required.","- The last sentence is restated as two clauses; \"respective gegen einander\" rendered \"in their relation to one another\"; the parenthetical on appearances' alteration is made a separate sentence.","- \"[referred to above]\" is supplied for \"alle jene Sinnlichkeit\".","- \"bestimbar\" (sic) read as *bestimmbar*; \"Thäter\" rendered \"agent\".","- \"Wirkungen\" rendered \"effects\"; \"Zeitumstände\" \"circumstances of time\"."]],"krv-a-0934":["In judging free actions, so far as their causality is concerned, we can therefore advance only as far as the intelligible cause; we cannot advance beyond it. We can know that this cause is free, that is, that it is determined independently of sensibility, and that in this manner it can be the sensibly unconditioned condition of appearances. But why the intelligible character should, in the given circumstances, yield just these appearances and this empirical character, so far transcends every capacity of our reason to answer, indeed every right it has even to raise the question, that it is as if we were to ask why the transcendental object of our outer sensible intuition gives intuition in space only, and not some other kind. The problem which we had to solve, however, does not oblige us to do this. It was merely whether freedom is in conflict with natural necessity in one and the same action; and this we have sufficiently answered. We showed that, since in the former a relation to a quite different kind of conditions is possible than in the latter, the law of the latter does not affect the former, and that the two can therefore exist independently of each other and without interference from each other.",["- Long sentence on the problem set was divided into several; \"ob Freiheit ... widerstreite\" kept as indirect question.","- \"Vermögen\" rendered \"capacity\"; \"Befugniß\" \"right\"; \"ia\" \"indeed\".","- \"bey iener ... bey dieser\": \"the former ... the latter\" refer to freedom and natural necessity, as the context requires.","- \"erkennen\" rendered \"know\"."]],"krv-a-0935":["It must be carefully noted that in what precedes we have not sought to establish the *reality* of freedom as one of the faculties containing the cause of the appearances of our sensible world. For, apart from the fact that this would not have been a transcendental enquiry, which is concerned solely with concepts, it could not have succeeded; since from experience we can never infer to anything which has to be thought not in accordance with laws of experience. Nor, further, have we even sought to prove the *possibility* of freedom; for this too would have failed, since from mere concepts *a priori* we cannot know the possibility of any real ground or causality. Freedom is here treated only as a transcendental idea, through which reason thinks to begin the series of conditions in the appearance absolutely, by what is sensibly unconditioned, and in so doing becomes involved in an antinomy with the laws which it itself prescribes to the empirical employment of the understanding. That this antinomy rests on a mere illusion, and that nature at least does not conflict with causality through freedom, was the one thing we could accomplish, and the only thing about which we were concerned.",["- \"einer der Vermögen\" kept as \"one of the faculties\"; \"Realgrund\" rendered \"real ground\".","- \"Daß nun ... und, daß\" kept as two parallel clauses.","- \"Natur der Caussalität aus Freiheit\" read as \"nature ... conflict with causality through freedom\" (dative, \"Natur\" being the subject).","- Italics on *reality*, *possibility* are not recoverable from the source and were not strictly warranted; I have nonetheless marked them for the contrast. Remove if unwanted.","- \"woran es uns ... gelegen war\" rendered \"about which we were concerned\"."]],"krv-a-0936":["IV. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Dependence of Appearances as Regards Their Existence in General",["- \"Auflösung\" rendered \"Solution\"; \"Daseyn\" rendered \"existence\"."]],"krv-a-0937":["In the preceding section we considered the changes of the sensible world in their dynamical series, in which each change stands under another as its cause. We now use this series of states merely as a guide to an existence which may be the highest condition of everything changeable, namely, the necessary being. What is here at issue is not unconditioned causality, but the unconditioned existence of substance itself. The series which we have before us is therefore really a series of concepts, not of intuitions, in so far as the one is the condition of the other.",["- \"In der vorigen Nummer\" rendered \"In the preceding section\" (the section numbered III).","- \"Wesen\" rendered \"being\"; \"zu thun sein\" rendered \"at issue\".","- \"von Begriffen und nicht von Anschauungen\": I follow the sense in which the concepts of existence, not intuited appearances, are in question."]],"krv-a-0938":["But it is easy to see that, since everything in the sum of appearances is changeable, and therefore conditioned in existence, there can nowhere in the series of dependent existence be an unconditioned member whose existence would be absolutely necessary. Hence, if appearances were things in themselves, and for that very reason their condition always belonged with the conditioned to one and the same series of intuitions, a necessary being, as condition of the existence of the appearances of the sensible world, could never find a place.",["- \"Inbegriff\" rendered \"sum\"; \"statt finden\" \"find a place\".","- The two \"daß\" clauses retained; the second is begun with \"Hence\" for smoothness."]],"krv-a-0939":["The dynamical regress, however, has this peculiarity, which distinguishes it from the mathematical. The mathematical regress is properly concerned only with the composition of parts into a whole, or the division of a whole into its parts, and the conditions of its series must therefore always be regarded as parts of the series, and so as homogeneous, and consequently as appearances. In the dynamical regress, on the other hand, the question is not the possibility of an unconditioned whole from given parts, or of an unconditioned part for a given whole, but the derivation of a state from its cause, or of the contingent existence of substance itself from the necessary; and here the condition need not form an empirical series with the conditioned.",["- Restated as three sentences; \"an sich\" in \"an sich\" is treated as part of \"hat ... an sich\" (has as its own), not translated separately.","- \"zufällig\" rendered \"contingent\"; \"gleichartig\" \"homogeneous\".","- \"nicht eben nothwendig ... dürfe\" rendered \"need not\"."]],"krv-a-0940":["Thus, in the apparent antinomy before us, a way of escape still remains open. Both the conflicting propositions may be true at the same time, in different relations. All things of the sensible world may be thoroughly contingent, and therefore have only empirically conditioned existence, and yet there may be a non-empirical condition of the whole series, that is, an unconditionally necessary being. For this being, as an intelligible condition, would not belong to the series as a member of it, not even as its highest member; nor would it make any member of the series empirically unconditioned. It would leave the whole sensible world in its empirically conditioned existence, extending through all its members. This mode of grounding an unconditioned existence of appearances would thus differ from the empirically unconditioned causality (freedom) of the preceding section in the following respect. In the case of freedom the thing itself, as cause (*substantia phaenomenon*), still belonged to the series of conditions, and only its causality was thought as intelligible. Here, on the other hand, the necessary being must be thought as entirely outside the series of the sensible world (as *ens extramundanum*), and as merely intelligible. Only so can it be prevented from being itself subject to the law of the contingency and dependence of all appearances.",["- Long opening sentence divided; \"so, daß\" rendered as a colon-like continuation (\"may be true... All things ... may be\").","- \"Artikel\" rendered \"section\" for consistency with the preceding passage.","- \"wurde\" (sic) read as *würde*/*wurde*: rendered \"was thought\" per the German; the sense is \"was to be thought\".","- \"Substantia phænomenon\" spelling normalised to *substantia phaenomenon*."]],"krv-a-0941":["In respect of this problem of ours, then, the regulative principle of reason is as follows. Everything in the sensible world has empirically conditioned existence, and nowhere in that world is there any unconditioned necessity in respect of any property. No member of the series of conditions is such that we are not always to expect its empirical condition in a possible experience, and, so far as we can, to seek it. Nothing entitles us to derive any existence from a condition outside the empirical series, or yet to regard it, within the series itself, as absolutely independent and self-subsistent. At the same time, this principle in no way denies that the whole series may be grounded in some intelligible being, which for that very reason is free from all empirical condition and rather contains the ground of the possibility of all these appearances.",["- Restated the single long sentence as several; the series of \"daß\" clauses is divided at the colons.","- \"gleichwol ... gar nicht in Abrede zu ziehen, daß nicht ... könne\": the double negative is rendered as a simple denial that the series cannot be grounded; \"At the same time, this principle\" is supplied to make the subject explicit.","- Erscheinungen = appearances; intelligibel = intelligible."]],"krv-a-0942":["The intention here, however, is not to prove the unconditionally necessary existence of a being, nor even to found upon this the possibility of a merely intelligible condition of the existence of the appearances of the sensible world. Just as we limit reason, lest it forsake the thread of the empirical conditions and lose itself in transcendent grounds of explanation which admit of no presentation *in concreto*, so, on the other side, we are to limit the law of the merely empirical employment of the understanding. It is not to decide as to the possibility of things in general, and is not to declare the intelligible impossible merely because it cannot be employed by us in explaining appearances. What is thereby shown is only this: that the thoroughgoing contingency of all natural things, and of all their (empirical) conditions, may very well coexist with the arbitrary presupposition of a necessary, though merely intelligible, condition; that no true contradiction is to be found between these assertions; and that both may therefore be true. Such an absolutely necessary being of the understanding [*Verstandeswesen*] may be impossible in itself; yet this can in no way be inferred from the universal contingency and dependence of everything belonging to the sensible world, nor from the principle of not stopping at any single member of that world, so far as it is contingent, and appealing to a cause outside the world. Reason takes its own course in the empirical, and its own special course in the transcendental, employment.",["- Divided the long second sentence; the \"wie ... also auch\" comparison is recast as \"Just as ... so\", with \"we are to\" supplied to carry the infinitive \"einzuschränken\".","- \"Verstandeswesen\" given with the German; \"willkührlich\" rendered \"arbitrary\" (i.e. a free assumption).","- \"ob zwar blos intelligibelen\" taken as modifying the condition."]],"krv-a-0943":["The sensible world contains nothing but appearances, and these are mere representations, which in turn are always sensibly conditioned. Since here we never have things in themselves as our objects, it is not surprising that we are never entitled to take a leap from any member whatever of the empirical series beyond the connection of sensibility, as if the members were things in themselves existing apart from their transcendental ground, which we could abandon in order to seek the cause of their existence outside them. This would indeed have to happen in the case of contingent things, but not in the case of mere representations of things, whose contingency is itself only phenomenon and can lead to no other regress than that which determines the phenomena, that is, the empirical regress. But to think an intelligible ground of appearances, that is, of the sensible world, and to think it as freed from the contingency of the latter, is opposed neither to the unlimited empirical regress in the series of appearances nor to their thoroughgoing contingency. That is also the only thing we had to accomplish in removing the seeming antinomy, and it could be done only in this way. For if the condition in each case of everything conditioned (as regards existence) is sensible, and for that very reason belongs to the series, it is itself in turn conditioned (as the antithesis of the fourth antinomy shows). Either, then, a conflict with reason, which demands the unconditioned, had to remain, or the unconditioned had to be posited outside the series, in the intelligible, whose necessity requires no empirical condition and permits none, and is therefore, relatively to appearances, unconditionally necessary.",["- Divided the opening sentence at \"und, da\"; the \"da\" clause is made the ground of \"not surprising\".","- \"welches\" (before \"bey zufälligen Dingen\") taken to refer to the leaving of a thing to seek its cause outside it.","- \"Phänomen/Phänomena\" kept as phenomenon/phenomena."]],"krv-a-0944":["The empirical employment of reason (in respect of the conditions of existence in the sensible world) is not affected by the admission of a merely intelligible being. It proceeds, in accordance with the principle of thoroughgoing contingency, from empirical conditions to higher conditions, which are always likewise empirical. Equally little does this regulative principle exclude the assumption of an intelligible cause not in the series, when what is at issue is the pure employment of reason (in respect of ends). For in that case the intelligible cause signifies only the ground, for us merely transcendental and unknown, of the possibility of the sensible series in general. The existence of this ground is independent of all conditions of the series and, in relation to these, unconditionally necessary; and it is therefore in no way opposed to the unbounded contingency of the series, nor to the regress, which nowhere comes to an end, in the series of empirical conditions.",["- Divided the final sentence. \"iene\" (OCR/orthography for *jene*) is taken to refer to the intelligible cause; \"dessen ... Daseyn\" is rendered as \"The existence of this ground\".","- \"der ersteren\"/\"der lezteren\": ersteren = the sensible series, lezteren = its conditions in the sense given; the construction is irregular and smoothed.","- \"die ... seyn\" in the first paragraph is a subjunctive form; rendered plainly as \"are\"."]],"krv-a-0945":["Concluding Remark on the Whole Antinomy of Pure Reason.",["- \"Schlußanmerkung\" rendered \"Concluding Remark\"."]],"krv-a-0946":["So long as the objects of our concepts of reason are merely the totality of conditions in the sensible world, and what can be done in respect of them in the service of reason, our ideas are indeed transcendental, but yet cosmological. But as soon as we posit the unconditioned (with which we are really concerned) in what lies entirely outside the sensible world, and therefore outside all possible experience, the ideas become transcendent. They then serve not merely for the completion of the empirical employment of reason (which always remains an idea that can never be carried out, yet must be pursued). They separate themselves from it altogether, and make themselves objects whose material is not taken from experience, and whose objective reality rests not on the completion of the empirical series but on pure *a priori* concepts. Such transcendent ideas have a merely intelligible object. To admit this object as a transcendental object, of which otherwise we know nothing, is indeed permissible. But to think it as a thing determinable through its distinguishing, inner predicates, we have on our side neither grounds of its possibility (since it is independent of all empirical concepts) nor the least justification for assuming such an object; and it is therefore a mere thought-entity [*Gedankending*].\n\nNevertheless, among all the cosmological ideas, the one which gave rise to the fourth antinomy constrains us to venture this step. For the existence of appearances, not grounded at all in itself but always conditioned, requires us to look around for something different from all appearances, that is, for an intelligible object, in which this contingency may cease. But once we have taken the liberty of assuming, outside the field of the whole of sensibility, a self-subsisting reality, and of regarding appearances only as contingent modes of representation of intelligible objects, by beings which are themselves intelligences, nothing remains to us save analogy, by which we make use of the concepts of experience in order to form some sort of concept of intelligible things, of which in themselves we have not the least knowledge. We learn to know the contingent only through experience; but here we are speaking of things which are not to be objects of experience at all. We shall therefore have to derive our knowledge of them from what is necessary in itself, from pure concepts of things in general. Accordingly the first step we take outside the sensible world obliges us to begin our new modes of knowledge [*Kentnisse*] with the investigation of the absolutely necessary being, and to derive from the concepts of it the concepts of all things, so far as they are merely intelligible. This attempt we shall make in the following chapter.",["- Divided the two long sentences; paragraph break after \"Gedankending\" is mine for clarity, as the German runs on.","- \"Weil aber ... anzusehen: so bleibt\": the anacoluthic \"weil\" clause is smoothed into \"But once we have ...\". \"von solchen Wesen, die selbst Intelligenzen sind\" is read as completing \"Vorstellungsarten ... intelligibeler Gegenstände\", i.e. the modes of representation belong to such beings.","- \"zuzulassen, es allerdings erlaubt ist, wozu aber ... welches daher\": the relative clauses are recast; \"it\" = the intelligible object.","- \"Kentniß/Kentnisse\" rendered \"knowledge\"/\"modes of knowledge\"."]],"krv-a-0947":["Book II of the Transcendental Dialectic, Chapter III. The Ideal of Pure Reason.",["- \"Hauptstück\" rendered \"chapter\"."]],"krv-a-0948":["Section I. The Ideal in General.",["- \"Abschnitt\" rendered \"Section\"."]],"krv-a-0949":["We have seen above that objects cannot be represented through pure concepts of the understanding, apart from all conditions of sensibility, since the conditions of their objective reality are lacking, and nothing is to be found in them save the mere form of thought. They can, however, be exhibited *in concreto* if they are applied to appearances; for in appearances they find the proper material for the concept of experience, which is nothing but a concept of the understanding *in concreto*. Ideas, however, are still further removed from objective reality than are the categories; for no appearance can be found in which they could be represented *in concreto*. They contain a certain completeness to which no possible empirical knowledge attains. In dealing with them reason has in view only a systematic unity, which it seeks to approximate by means of the empirically possible unity, without ever completely reaching it.",["- \"Erfahrungsbegriffe\" rendered \"concept of experience\"; \"Stoff\" as \"material\".","- Final sentence: \"dabey ... im Sinne\" rendered \"in dealing with them ... has in view\"; the long sentence before it is divided."]],"krv-a-0950":["But that which I entitle the *ideal* seems to be still further removed from objective reality than is the idea. By the ideal I understand the idea not merely *in concreto* but *in individuo*, that is, as an individual thing, determinable or even determined by the idea alone.",["- Split into two sentences. \"nenne\" rendered \"entitle\" in keeping with the method's heißen/genannt convention.","- \"bestimbar/bestimt\" rendered \"determinable/determined\" (Bestimmung = determination)."]],"krv-a-0951":["Humanity in its complete perfection contains not only the extension of all the essential properties belonging to this nature and constituting our concept of it, extended until they are in complete congruence with their ends (which would be our idea of perfect humanity), but also everything else which, besides this concept, belongs to the thoroughgoing determination of the idea. For of all possible contrary predicates only one can be in agreement with the idea of the most perfect man. What is for us an ideal was for Plato an idea of the divine understanding, an individual object in its pure intuition, the most perfect of every kind of possible being, and the primary ground of all copies in the appearance.",["- \"Nachbilder\" rendered \"copies\"; \"Erscheinung\" singular, \"the appearance\".","- \"sich schicken zu\" rendered \"be in agreement with\". The first sentence's clauses are slightly rearranged for clarity."]],"krv-a-0952":["Without venturing so far, however, we must confess that human reason contains not only ideas but also ideals. These do not, like the Platonic ideas, have creative power, but they have practical power (as regulative principles), and lie at the basis of the possibility of the perfection of certain actions. Moral concepts are not entirely pure concepts of reason, since something empirical (pleasure or displeasure) underlies them. Nevertheless, in respect of the principle whereby reason imposes limits on a freedom which is in itself lawless (that is, when we attend merely to their form), they may very well serve as examples of pure concepts of reason. Virtue, and with it human wisdom in its complete purity, are ideas. But the sage (of the Stoic) is an ideal, that is, a man existing only in thought, who yet completely accords with the idea of wisdom. As the idea supplies the rule, so in this case the ideal serves as archetype for the thoroughgoing determination of the copy; and we have no other standard for our actions than the conduct of this divine man within us, with whom we compare ourselves, judge ourselves, and so reform ourselves, although we can never attain to his perfection. Though we may not be willing to concede objective reality (existence) to these ideals, they are not on that account to be regarded as chimeras. They supply reason with an indispensable standard, since it needs the concept of what is entirely complete in its kind, in order to estimate and measure by it the degree and the defects of the incomplete. To attempt to realise the ideal in an example, that is, in appearance, as, for instance, the sage in a novel, is impracticable. Such an attempt has, moreover, something absurd and little edifying about it, inasmuch as the natural limits, which continually impair the completeness of the idea, make every illusion impossible in such an attempt, and so render the good which lies in the idea suspect, and similar to a mere fiction.",["- Divided the opening sentence and the sentence on the Stoic sage into several sentences.","- \"Richtmaaß\" rendered \"standard\"; \"Nachbild\" \"copy\", \"Urbild\" \"archetype\"; \"realisiren\" \"realise\" (British spelling).","- \"Hirngespinste\" rendered \"chimeras\"."]],"krv-a-0953":["Such is the situation with the ideal of reason, which must always rest on determinate concepts and serve as a rule and archetype, whether for imitation or for judgment. Quite otherwise is it with the creatures of the imagination, about which no one can explain himself or give an intelligible concept. They are, as it were, monograms, mere isolated traits, determined by no assignable rule, which constitute rather a sketch floating, as it were, between various experiences than a determinate image. Painters and physiognomists profess to have such things in their heads, as a shadow-picture of their productions or judgments which cannot be communicated. They may, though only improperly, be entitled ideals of sensibility, since they are supposed to be the unattainable model of possible empirical intuitions, and yet yield no rule capable of explanation or examination.",["- \"Befolgung\" rendered \"imitation\" (following the ideal as a model); \"Beurtheilung\" \"judgment\".","- \"Mittel verschiedener Erfahrungen\" rendered \"between various experiences\"; the long second sentence is divided."]],"krv-a-0954":["The purpose of reason in its ideal, on the other hand, is thoroughgoing determination in accordance with rules *a priori*. Reason accordingly thinks an object which is to be thoroughly determinable according to principles, although the sufficient conditions for this are lacking in experience, and the concept itself is therefore transcendent.",["- \"Absicht\" rendered \"purpose\"; \"bestimbar\" \"determinable\". The sentence is divided at the semicolon."]],"krv-a-0955":["Third Chapter, Section Two. The Transcendental Ideal (*Prototypon transscendentale*).",["- \"Hauptstück\" rendered \"Chapter\", as in Kemp Smith's headings; this is a heading, not running text."]],"krv-a-0956":["Every concept is indeterminate in respect of what is not contained in it, and stands under the principle of determinability: that of every two contradictorily opposed predicates only one can belong to it. This principle rests on the law of contradiction, and is therefore a merely logical principle, which abstracts from all content of knowledge and has in view nothing but its logical form.",["- Divided after the colon clause; the relative \"welcher\" refers to the principle (Grundsatz), by the argument's sense.","- \"Satz des Widerspruchs\" rendered \"law of contradiction\"; \"Erkentniß\" \"knowledge\"."]],"krv-a-0957":["But everything, as regards its possibility, stands also under the principle of thoroughgoing determination, according to which, of all possible predicates of things, in so far as they are compared with their opposites, one must belong to it. This does not rest merely on the law of contradiction. For besides the relation of two conflicting predicates, it considers each thing also in its relation to the sum of all possibility, as the totality of all predicates of things in general. In presupposing this totality as a condition *a priori*, it represents every thing as deriving its own possibility from the share which it has in that sum of possibility*). The principle of thoroughgoing determination therefore concerns content, and not merely logical form. It is the principle of the synthesis of all the predicates which are to constitute the complete concept of a thing, and not merely of the analytic representation through one of two opposed predicates. It contains a transcendental presupposition, namely, that of the matter for all possibility, which is to contain *a priori* the data for the particular possibility of everything.",["- \"Gesamte Möglichkeit\" rendered \"sum of all possibility\"/\"sum of possibility\"; \"Inbegriff\" \"totality\"/\"sum\" for variety — a choice; \"Inbegriff\" is also rendered \"sum\" in the footnote.","- Divided the long second sentence; \"es\" in \"indem es solche ... voraussezt\" is the principle, and \"solche\" the totality.","- Footnote marker retained at its place after \"hat\"."]],"krv-a-0958":["*) By this principle, then, everything is related to a common correlate, namely, the sum of all possibility. If this (that is, the material for all possible predicates) were found in the idea of a single thing, it would prove an affinity of all that is possible, through the identity of the ground of its thoroughgoing determination. The determinability of every concept is subordinate to the universality (*Universalitas*) of the principle of the exclusion of a middle between two opposed predicates; but the determination of a thing is subordinate to the totality (*Universitas*), or the sum of all possible predicates.",["- \"Allgemeinheit\" / \"Allheit\" rendered \"universality\" / \"totality\" after Kant's Latin glosses.","- \"desselben\" in \"Bestimmung desselben\" taken to refer to the possible (alles Möglichen); sense of \"its\" is a judgment.","- \"Vniuersalitas\" and \"Vniversitas\" normalised to modern Latin spelling."]],"krv-a-0959":["The proposition that everything existent is thoroughly determined means not only that, of every pair of given opposite predicates, one must always belong to the thing, but that, of all possible predicates also, one must always belong to it. By this proposition predicates are not merely compared with one another logically; the thing itself is compared, transcendentally, with the sum [*Inbegriff*] of all possible predicates. What the proposition says is this: in order to know a thing completely, we must know everything possible, and must determine the thing by it, whether affirmatively or negatively. Thoroughgoing determination is consequently a concept which we can never exhibit *in concreto* in its totality. It is therefore grounded on an idea which has its seat solely in reason, and which prescribes to the understanding the rule of its complete employment.",[]],"krv-a-0960":["This idea of the sum of all possibility, in so far as it underlies, as the condition, the thoroughgoing determination of every thing, is itself still undetermined in respect of the predicates which may constitute it, and through it we think nothing more than a sum of all possible predicates in general. On closer examination, however, we find that this idea, as a primordial concept [*Urbegriff*], excludes a number of predicates which, as derived, are already given through others, or which cannot stand alongside one another; and that it purifies itself until it becomes a concept completely determined *a priori*. Thereby it becomes the concept of an individual object which is thoroughly determined through the mere idea, and must therefore be entitled an ideal of pure reason.",[]],"krv-a-0961":["If we consider all possible predicates not merely logically but transcendentally, that is, in terms of the content which can be thought in them *a priori*, we find that through some of them a being [*Sein*] is represented, through others a mere not-being [*Nichtsein*]. Logical negation, which is indicated solely by the little word \"not\", never really attaches to a concept, but only to its relation to another concept in a judgment, and is therefore far from sufficient to characterise a concept in respect of its content. The expression \"not-mortal\" cannot at all make known that a mere not-being in the object is represented by it; it leaves the whole content untouched. Transcendental negation, on the other hand, signifies not-being in itself. Opposed to it is transcendental affirmation, which is a something whose concept in itself already expresses a being, and which is therefore entitled reality (thinghood), since through it alone, and so far as it extends, are objects something (things). The opposed negation, on the other hand, signifies a mere lack; and where it alone is thought, the removal of all thinghood is represented.",[]],"krv-a-0962":["Now no one can think a negation determinately without having the opposite affirmation underlying it. The man born blind cannot form the least representation of darkness, since he has none of light; the savage none of poverty, since he does not know prosperity*. The ignorant man has no concept of his ignorance, since he has none of science; and so on. All concepts of negations are therefore derived; and it is the realities that contain the data, and, so to speak, the matter or transcendental content, for the possibility and thoroughgoing determination of all things.",[]],"krv-a-0963":["*) The observations and calculations of astronomers have taught us much that is admirable; but the most important thing is probably that they have uncovered for us the abyss of ignorance, which human reason, without this knowledge, could never have represented to itself as so great. Reflection upon it must produce a great change in the determination of the final aims of our employment of reason.",[]],"krv-a-0964":["If, then, a transcendental substratum is laid at the basis of thoroughgoing determination in our reason, a substratum which contains, as it were, the whole store of material from which all possible predicates of things can be taken, this substratum is nothing other than the idea of an All of reality (*omnitudo realitatis*). All true negations are then nothing but limits [*Schranken*]. They could not be so entitled, were not the unlimited (the All) presupposed as their basis.",[]],"krv-a-0965":["But through this all-embracing possession of reality the concept of a thing in itself is also represented as thoroughly determined; and the concept of an *ens realissimum* is the concept of a single being, because of all possible opposed predicates one, namely that which belongs to being absolutely, is found in its determination. It is therefore a transcendental ideal which underlies the thoroughgoing determination that is necessarily found in everything that exists, and constitutes the supreme and complete material condition of its possibility, to which all thought of objects in general, so far as their content is concerned, must be traced back. It is also the one true ideal of which human reason is capable. For only in this one case is a concept, universal in itself, of a thing thoroughly determined through itself, and known as the representation of an individual.",[]],"krv-a-0966":["The logical determination of a concept by reason rests on a disjunctive syllogism. In this the major premiss contains a logical division (the division of the sphere of a universal concept); the minor premiss limits this sphere to one part; and the conclusion determines the concept through this part. The universal concept of a reality in general cannot be divided *a priori*, since without experience we know no determinate species of reality that would be contained under that genus. The transcendental major premiss of the thoroughgoing determination of all things is therefore nothing other than the representation of the sum of all reality. It is not merely a concept which contains all predicates under itself in respect of their transcendental content, but one which comprehends them in itself. The thoroughgoing determination of each thing rests on the limitation of this All of reality, in that some part of it is ascribed to the thing and the rest excluded. This agrees with the \"either–or\" of the disjunctive major premiss, and with the determination of the object, in the minor premiss, through one of the members of this division. Accordingly the employment of reason, whereby it lays the transcendental ideal at the basis of its determination of all possible things, is analogous to its procedure in disjunctive syllogisms. This was the principle which I laid above at the basis of the systematic division of all transcendental ideas, and according to which they are produced as parallel with, and corresponding to, the three kinds of syllogism.",[]],"krv-a-0967":["It goes without saying that reason, for this purpose of representing solely the necessary thoroughgoing determination of things, presupposes not the existence of a being conformable to the ideal, but only the idea of it, in order to derive from an unconditioned totality of thoroughgoing determination the conditioned totality, that is, the totality of the limited. The ideal is thus for reason the archetype (*prototypon*) of all things, which one and all, as defective copies (*ectypa*), derive from it the material of their possibility, and, while approximating to it in greater or lesser degree, yet always fall infinitely short of attaining it.\n\n---notes\n- 0959: \"Inbegriff\" rendered \"sum\"; \"durchgängig bestimmt\" as \"thoroughly determined\"; \"Bestimmung\" as \"determination\" throughout. The long final sentence is divided in two.\n- 0960: One long concessive sentence is split into three; \"denselben\" taken as the sum of possibility. \"Urbegriff\" given in German.\n- 0961: \"Wörtchen\" rendered \"little word\". The last clause (\"wo diese allein gedacht wird, ...\") is smoothed; \"Aufhebung alles Dinges\" is rendered \"removal of all thinghood\" (Sachheit), by judgment.\n- 0962: In the footnote marker the asterisk is retained after \"kennt\". The ignorance, \"Wissenschaft\" = \"science\".\n- 0963: \"Kentnisse\" is rendered \"knowledge\"; \"Endabsichten\" as \"final aims\".\n- 0964: \"Schranken\" rendered \"limits\"; \"Unbeschränkte\" as \"the unlimited\". The sentence on negations is split.\n- 0965: \"Allbesitz\" rendered \"all-embracing possession\". Final clause of the last sentence is split off as a sentence; \"erkannt\" here is \"known\".\n- 0966: \"Entweder-oder\" rendered \"either–or\". The sentence on the transcendental major premiss is restated in several sentences; \"welches ... übereinkommt\" taken to refer to the limitation as a whole. \"welches der Satz war\" is made a new sentence.\n- 0967: \"Urbild/Prototypon\" and \"ectypa\" kept in Latin italics; \"Stoff\" rendered \"material\".",[]],"krv-a-0968":["Thus all possibility of things (that is, of the synthesis of the manifold, so far as its content is concerned) is regarded as derived, and only the possibility of that which contains in itself all reality is regarded as original. For all negations (which are yet the only predicates by which anything else can be distinguished from the *ens realissimum*) are mere limitations of a greater, and finally of the highest, reality. They therefore presuppose this reality, and so far as content is concerned are merely derived from it. All manifoldness of things is only a correspondingly manifold way of limiting the concept of the highest reality, which is their common substratum, just as all figures are possible only as different ways of limiting infinite space. Hence the object of this ideal, which is found solely in reason, is entitled the original being (*ens originarium*) in so far as there is none above it; the highest being (*ens summum*) in so far as everything else, as conditioned, stands below it; and the being of all beings (*ens entium*). But all this does not signify the objective relation of an actual object to other things; it signifies the relation of an idea to concepts. It leaves us in complete ignorance as to the existence of a being of such pre-eminent excellence.",["- \"realesten Wesen\" rendered *ens realissimum*, Kant's own Latin equivalent (Latin supplied by me, not in the German).","- Long second-to-last sentence divided into three clauses with semicolons; the final sentence divided in two.","- \"Vorzuge\" rendered \"pre-eminent excellence\"; \"seyn\" (for \"sein\") after \"möglich\" taken as an infinitive."]],"krv-a-0969":["Nor can we say that an original being consists of a number of derived beings, since each of these presupposes the original being and therefore cannot constitute it. The ideal of the original being must accordingly also be thought as simple.",["- One sentence divided in two for clarity.","- \"Weil man auch nicht sagen kan\" is a subordinate clause governing the main clause \"so wird ... werden müssen\"; I have restated it as two sentences."]],"krv-a-0970":["Strictly speaking, therefore, the derivation of all other possibility from this original being cannot be regarded as a limitation of its highest reality, or as a kind of division of it. For the original being would then be regarded as a mere aggregate of derived beings, which, as we have just seen, is impossible, although in our first rough outline we so represented it. The highest reality would rather underlie the possibility of all things as a ground, not as a sum [*Inbegriff*]; and the manifoldness of the latter [i.e. of things] would rest not upon the limitation of the original being itself, but upon the complete sequence [*Folge*] issuing from it. To this sequence our whole sensibility, together with all reality in the appearance, would also belong; and this cannot belong to the idea of the highest being as an ingredient.",["- Sentence divided; \"die ersteren\" taken to refer to \"Dinge\" (things), and \"der Möglichkeit aller Dinge\" governs the sense; the bracketed \"i.e. of things\" is my resolution.","- \"nach dem vorigen\" rendered \"as we have just seen\", referring to the preceding paragraph.","- \"Ingredienz\" kept as \"ingredient\"; \"Schattenrisse\" rendered \"outline\"."]],"krv-a-0971":["If we now follow up this idea of ours further, hypostatising it, we shall be able to determine the original being through the mere concept of the highest reality as one, simple, all-sufficient, eternal, and so forth; in a word, to determine it in its unconditioned completeness through all the predicaments. The concept of such a being is the concept of God in the transcendental sense of the term; and thus the ideal of pure reason is the object of a transcendental theology, as I have already stated above.",["- \"Prädicamente\" rendered \"predicaments\" (categories), as Kemp Smith's usage.","- \"in transscendentalem Verstande\" rendered \"in the transcendental sense\", where \"Verstande\" means \"sense\", not the faculty."]],"krv-a-0972":["This employment of the transcendental idea would, however, already overstep the limits of its function and its admissibility. For reason has employed it only as the concept of all reality, in order to lay a foundation for the thoroughgoing determination of things in general. It did not demand that all this reality should be given objectively and itself constitute a thing. Such a thing is a mere fiction, through which we combine the manifold of our idea in an ideal, as a particular being, and realise it. We have no warrant for doing so, not even for directly assuming the possibility of such a hypothesis. Similarly, all the consequences which flow from such an ideal have no bearing upon the thoroughgoing determination of things in general, for the sake of which alone the idea was needed, and exert not the least influence upon it.",["- Last long sentence divided into three; \"wie denn auch\" rendered \"similarly\".","- \"Bestimmung\" in the first sentence rendered \"function\" (the sense is the idea's intended destination); a choice."]],"krv-a-0973":["It is not enough to describe the procedure of our reason and its dialectic; we must also endeavour to discover its sources, so that we may be able to explain this illusion itself, as a phenomenon of the understanding. For the ideal of which we are speaking is grounded on a natural, and not merely arbitrary, idea. I therefore ask: How does reason come to regard all possibility of things as derived from one single possibility that lies at the foundation, namely, that of the highest reality, and then to presuppose this as contained in a particular original being?",["- \"derselben\" taken to refer to the procedure and dialectic of reason, hence \"its sources\".","- \"willkührlich\" rendered \"arbitrary\"."]],"krv-a-0974":["The answer presents itself of its own accord from the discussions of the transcendental Analytic. The possibility of objects of the senses is a relation of these objects to our thought, in which something (namely, the empirical form) can be thought *a priori*, whereas that which constitutes the matter, the reality in the appearance (that which corresponds to sensation), must be given. Without this it could not be thought at all, and its possibility could not be represented. Now an object of the senses can be thoroughly determined only if it is compared with all the predicates of appearance, and through them is represented affirmatively or negatively. But what constitutes the thing itself (in the appearance), namely the real, must be given in this process, since otherwise the thing could not be thought at all; and that in which the real of all appearances is given is the one all-embracing experience. The matter for the possibility of all objects of the senses must therefore be presupposed as given in one sum [*Inbegriff*]; and upon the limitation of this alone can all possibility of empirical objects, their difference from one another and their thoroughgoing determination, rest. Now no objects other than those of the senses can in fact be given to us, and nowhere save in the context of a possible experience. Consequently nothing is an object for us unless it presupposes the sum of all empirical reality as condition of its possibility. By a natural illusion we then regard as a principle that must hold of all things in general what properly holds only of those that are given as objects of our senses. Consequently, by omitting this limitation, we shall take the empirical principle of our concepts of the possibility of things, as appearances, for a transcendental principle of the possibility of things in general.",["- The sentence beginning \"Weil aber darin\" is tangled; I divided it and supplied \"in this process\" for \"darin\" (the comparison with predicates of appearance), and restated \"ohne welches\" as \"since otherwise\".","- \"vor\" is the older spelling of \"für\" (\"as\", \"for\").","- \"Context\" kept as \"context\"."]],"krv-a-0975":["That we afterwards hypostatise this idea of the sum of all reality comes about as follows. We dialectically transform the distributive unity of the empirical employment of the understanding into the collective unity of a whole of experience. And in this whole of appearance we think to ourselves a single thing which contains in itself all empirical reality. By means of the transcendental subreption already mentioned, this thing is then confused with the concept of a thing that stands at the head of the possibility of all things, and supplies the real conditions for their thoroughgoing determination.*",["- Sentence divided in three; the relative \"welches\" taken to refer to the single thing.","- \"Subreption\" kept as \"subreption\"; the asterisk marks Kant's footnote (next passage).","- \"Verstandes\" here, \"the understanding\", in its empirical employment."]],"krv-a-0976":["*) This ideal of the most real being, although it is a mere representation, is first realised, that is, made into an object; then hypostatised; and finally, through the natural progress of reason towards the completion of unity, even personified, as we shall shortly show. For the regulative unity of experience rests not on the appearances themselves (on sensibility alone), but on the connection of their manifold through the understanding (in one apperception). Hence the unity of the highest reality and the thoroughgoing determinability (possibility) of all things seem to lie in a highest understanding, and therefore in an intelligence.",["- \"Obiect\" rendered \"object\"; \"Bestimbarkeit\" (sic) read as \"Bestimmbarkeit\", \"determinability\".","- The \"weil\" clause is restated as a separate sentence with \"For\"."]],"krv-a-0977":["Chapter III, Section III. On the Grounds of Proof in Speculative Reason for Inferring the Existence of a Supreme Being.",["- \"Des dritten Hauptstücks\" is the third chapter of the Ideal; this is a heading.","- \"Beweisgründe\" rendered \"grounds of proof\"; \"höchsten Wesens\" rendered \"supreme being\", a variation from \"highest being\" earlier (*ens summum*) for the heading's idiom; a choice."]],"krv-a-0978":["Notwithstanding this pressing need of reason to presuppose something that can serve the understanding as a completely adequate basis for the thoroughgoing determination of its concepts, reason is far too ready to notice the ideal and merely fictitious character of such a presupposition to allow itself to be persuaded by this alone to accept at once a mere creature of its own thought as a real being. It would not do so, were it not impelled from another quarter to seek somewhere a resting-place in the regress from the conditioned which is given to the unconditioned. The unconditioned is not, indeed, in itself and in its mere concept given as actual; but it alone can complete the series of conditions, traced back to their grounds.\n\nThis is the natural course which every human reason, even the most common, pursues, though not every reason persists in it. It begins not from concepts but from common experience, and so lays at the foundation something existing. But this ground gives way, unless it rests on the immovable rock of the absolutely necessary. And this rock itself floats without support, if there is still empty space outside it and beneath it, and if it does not itself fill everything, and so leave no room for any further 'Why?'; that is, if it is not infinite in respect of reality.",["- Restated the first sentence as three, dividing at \"wenn sie nicht … gedrungen würde\" and at the relative clause \"das zwar an sich …\".","- \"wodurch anders\" read as \"von woandersher\"; \"wenn sie nicht wodurch anders gedrungen würde\" rendered \"impelled from another quarter\".","- \"Idealische und blos Gedichtete\" rendered \"ideal and merely fictitious character\".","- \"d. i.\" clause expanded slightly in the English for smoothness; no content added."]],"krv-a-0979":["If anything whatever exists, it must also be admitted that something exists necessarily. For the contingent exists only under the condition of something else as its cause, and from this the same inference holds again, until we reach a cause which is not contingent and which, precisely for that reason, exists necessarily and without condition. This is the argument on which reason bases its advance to the primordial being [*Urwesen*].",["- \"Urwesen\" glossed in brackets; Kant's Latin parallel is *ens originarium*, not in this passage.","- \"von dieser gilt der Schluß fernerhin\" rendered \"from this the same inference holds again\"."]],"krv-a-0980":["Reason now looks about for the concept of a being that is suited to such a pre-eminence in existence as unconditioned necessity. It does so not so much in order to infer from the concept of that being, *a priori*, its existence. (For if reason ventured to do this, it would be entitled to inquire only among mere concepts, and would not need to take a given existence as its ground.) Its purpose is only to find, among all concepts of possible things, that concept which contains nothing conflicting with absolute necessity. For that something or other must exist with unconditioned necessity, reason holds to be already established by the first inference. If it can then remove everything that is not compatible with this necessity, save one thing, that one is the absolutely necessary being, whether or not we can comprehend its necessity, that is, derive it from its concept alone.",["- Divided the long sentence into four; the parenthesis is kept but made a separate sentence.","- \"dürfte sie … forschen\" rendered \"would be entitled to inquire\" (dürfen in the subjunctive of permission).","- \"nach dem ersteren Schlusse\" = the inference of the preceding passage (A 584)."]],"krv-a-0981":["That being, then, whose concept contains the 'Therefore' for every 'Why?', which is defective in no respect and in no regard, and which is everywhere sufficient as condition, seems for that very reason to be the being suited to absolute necessity. For since it possesses within itself all the conditions of everything possible, it itself requires no condition, and is indeed not even capable of one. Consequently, in at least one respect it satisfies the concept of unconditioned necessity, in which no other concept can equal it; for any other concept, being deficient and in need of supplementation, shows no such mark of independence of all further conditions.\n\nIt is true that we cannot yet validly conclude that what does not contain in itself the supreme and in every respect complete condition must therefore itself be conditioned in its existence. But such a being does not possess the one mark of unconditioned existence, which reason has at its command for cognising, through a concept *a priori*, any being as unconditioned.",["- \"thut ein Günge\" read as \"tut ein Genüge\" (OCR/old spelling); rendered \"satisfies\".","- Restated the second half of the first sentence (\"der, weil er mangelhaft…\") as a clause after a semicolon; antecedent taken as \"any other concept\".","- \"zu erkennen\" rendered \"cognising\" (the verb, not the noun *Erkenntnis*)."]],"krv-a-0982":["The concept of a being of the highest reality would therefore, of all concepts of possible things, best suit the concept of an unconditionally necessary being. And even if it does not fully satisfy that concept, we have no choice, but find ourselves compelled to hold to it. For we may not cast to the winds the existence of a necessary being; and if we admit it, we can yet find, in the whole field of possibility, nothing that can advance a better-grounded claim to such pre-eminence in existence.",["- Split at \"weil\"/\"geben wir sie aber zu\" with a full stop-and-\"For\" construction for clarity."]],"krv-a-0983":["Such, then, is the natural course of human reason. It first convinces itself of the existence of some necessary being, and in this recognises an unconditioned existence. It next seeks the concept of what is independent of all condition, and finds it in that which is itself the sufficient condition of everything else, that is, in that which contains all reality. But the all, without limits, is absolute unity, and carries with it the concept of a single being, namely the highest being. Reason thus concludes that the highest being, as primordial ground of all things, exists with absolute necessity.",["- \"einigen\" rendered \"single\" (Kant's *unicum*-sense), not \"united\".","- \"Urgrund\" = primordial ground."]],"krv-a-0984":["A certain cogency cannot be denied to this concept when the question is one of decisions: that is, when the existence of some necessary being is once granted, and it is agreed that we must take a side as to where we are to posit it. For in that case we cannot choose more suitably; or rather we have no choice, but are constrained to give our vote to the absolute unity of complete reality, as the primordial source of possibility.\n\nBut if nothing impels us to decide, and we would rather leave the whole matter in suspense until we are compelled to assent by the full weight of the grounds of proof; that is, if the sole concern is to estimate how much we know of this problem, and what we merely flatter ourselves that we know: then the above inference appears by no means in so favourable a light, and requires indulgence to make good the defect in its title.",["- \"Diesem Begriffe\" refers to the concept of the highest being just reached; kept as \"this concept\", though \"this argument\" is arguably implied.","- \"Rechtsansprüche\" rendered \"title\" (legal claim).","- Divided into two paragraphs at \"Wenn uns aber nichts treibt\"."]],"krv-a-0985":["For let us grant everything to be as good as it here lies before us. Let us grant, first, that from some given existence (even, if need be, merely my own) a valid inference holds to the existence of an unconditionally necessary being; and secondly, that I must regard a being which contains all reality, and therewith all condition, as absolutely unconditioned, so that the concept of the thing suited to absolute necessity has thereby been found. It still cannot be inferred from this that the concept of a limited being, which lacks the highest reality, is for that reason contradictory to absolute necessity. For although I do not find in its concept the unconditioned that the totality of conditions already brings with it, it cannot in the least be concluded that its existence must for that reason be conditioned. In a hypothetical syllogism I cannot say that where a certain condition (here, completeness according to concepts) is absent, the conditioned is also absent. On the contrary, we remain free to allow all other limited beings to hold equally as unconditionally necessary, even though we cannot infer their necessity from the universal concept we have of them. But in this way the argument would not have furnished us with the least concept of the properties of a necessary being, and would have accomplished nothing whatever.",["- Restated the long opening \"wenn … so\" period as several sentences, keeping \"first … secondly\" as in the German.","- \"allenfals\" read as \"allenfalls\"; rendered \"if need be\".","- \"so wie ich … nicht sagen kan\" turned into a separate sentence with \"In a hypothetical syllogism\"."]],"krv-a-0986":["Nevertheless this argument retains a certain importance and authority, which cannot be taken from it at once on account of this objective insufficiency. For suppose that there are obligations which are quite correct in the idea of reason, but which, apart from the presupposition of a supreme being capable of giving effect and emphasis to the practical laws, would be without any reality in their application to ourselves, that is, without incentives. In that case we should also be under an obligation to follow those concepts which, though they may not be objectively sufficient, are yet preponderant by the measure of our reason, and in comparison with which we know nothing better or more convincing. The duty of choosing would here turn the balance, upsetting the indecision of speculation by a practical addition. Indeed, reason would find no justification before itself, as the most indulgent of judges, if, under urgent motives, though with deficient insight, it failed to follow these grounds of its judgment, than which we at least know no better.",[]],"krv-a-0987":["This argument, though it is in fact transcendental, since it rests upon the inner insufficiency of the contingent, is yet so simple and natural that it is suited to the commonest human understanding as soon as that understanding is once led to it. We see things alter, arise and pass away; they, or at least their state, must therefore have a cause. But of every cause that can ever be given in experience the same question may again be asked. Where, then, can we more properly locate the supreme causality than where the highest causality is, that is, in that being which originally contains in itself the sufficiency for every possible effect, and the concept of which, moreover, is very easily constituted through the single feature of an all-embracing perfection? This highest cause we then hold to be absolutely necessary, because we find it absolutely necessary to ascend to it, and find no ground for going any further beyond it. Hence among all peoples we see, shining through their blindest polytheism, some sparks of monotheism. To this they have been led not by reflection and profound speculation, but only by the natural course of the common understanding, which has gradually become intelligible to them.",[]],"krv-a-0988":["There are only three kinds of proof of the existence of God possible from speculative reason.",[]],"krv-a-0989":["All the paths that can be taken in this intention begin either from determinate experience and the special character of our world of sense as thereby known, and ascend from it, in accordance with the laws of causality, to the highest cause outside the world; or they take as their basis only indeterminate experience, that is, some existence empirically given; or, finally, they abstract from all experience and conclude completely *a priori*, from mere concepts, to the existence of a highest cause. The first proof is the physico-theological, the second the cosmological, the third the ontological. There are no others, and there can be no others.",[]],"krv-a-0990":["I shall show that reason accomplishes as little on the one path (the empirical) as on the other (the transcendental), and that it spreads its wings in vain in seeking to rise above the world of sense by the mere power of speculation. As regards the order in which these kinds of proof must be submitted to examination, it will be exactly the reverse of that which reason takes in the gradual extension of its range, and in which we have also first set them out. For it will be shown that, although experience gives the first occasion for the inquiry, it is solely the transcendental concept that guides reason in this its endeavour, and that sets the goal which reason has proposed to itself in all such attempts. I shall therefore begin with the examination of the transcendental proof, and shall afterwards consider what the addition of the empirical can do to enhance its force as proof.",[]],"krv-a-0991":["Chapter III, Section IV. The Impossibility of an Ontological Proof of the Existence of God.",[]],"krv-a-0992":["It is easily seen from what has gone before that the concept of an absolutely necessary being is a pure concept of reason, that is, a mere idea. Its objective reality is far from being proved by the fact that reason has need of it. It only points to a certain, though unattainable, completeness, and serves properly rather to limit the understanding than to extend it to new objects. We are here confronted with the strange and paradoxical situation that the inference from a given existence in general to some absolutely necessary existence seems to be cogent and correct, and yet that all the conditions of the understanding for forming a concept of such a necessity are entirely against us.",[]],"krv-a-0993":["In all ages men have spoken of the absolutely necessary being, and have taken pains not so much to understand whether and how a thing of this kind can even be thought, as to prove its existence. A nominal definition of this concept is indeed quite easy: it is something the non-being of which is impossible. But this makes us no wiser in regard to the conditions which render it impossible to regard the non-existence of a thing as absolutely inconceivable. Yet these conditions are precisely what we want to know, namely, whether by means of this concept we think anything at all or not. For to cast away, by means of the word \"unconditioned\", all the conditions which the understanding invariably requires in order to regard something as necessary, is far from making it intelligible to me whether I am then thinking something through the concept of an unconditionally necessary, or perhaps nothing at all.",[]],"krv-a-0994":["More than this: this concept, hazarded at random and finally become quite familiar, men have further believed themselves to have explained by a multitude of examples, so that all further inquiry into its intelligibility seemed wholly unnecessary. Every proposition of geometry, for instance, that a triangle has three angles, is absolutely necessary; and so men talked of an object which lies entirely outside the sphere of our understanding as if they understood perfectly well what they meant to say of it by the concept.",[]],"krv-a-0995":["All the examples proffered are, without exception, taken only from judgments, not from things and their existence. But the unconditioned necessity of judgments is not an absolute necessity of things. For the absolute necessity of the judgment is only a conditioned necessity of the thing, or of the predicate in the judgment. The preceding proposition did not say that three angles are absolutely necessary, but that, under the condition that a triangle is there (is given), three angles are necessarily there [in it]. Yet this logical necessity has shown so great a power of illusion that, when men had formed an *a priori* concept of a thing in such a manner that, in their opinion, they included existence within its content, they believed they could safely conclude as follows. Since existence necessarily belongs to the object of this concept, that is, under the condition that I posit this thing as given (existing), its existence is also necessarily posited (in accordance with the rule of identity); and this being is therefore itself absolutely necessary, because its existence is thought along with it in a concept arbitrarily assumed, and under the condition that I posit the object of that concept.",["- 0986: \"Gleichwol\" etc. kept; the final long sentence is smoothed, and \"bey ihr selbst, als dem nachsehendesten Richter\" is rendered \"before itself, as the most indulgent of judges\"; \"Unschließigkeit\" = indecision.","- 0986: \"Verbindlichkeiten\" = obligations; \"Triebfedern\" = incentives.","- 0987: the sentence on the highest causality is posed as a question, as the German \"billiger\" suggests; \"Menschensinne\" = human understanding (common sense).","- 0987: the last sentence is restated; \"nach und nach verständlich gewordener\" refers to the course of the common understanding.","- 0989: the dash/clause break after \"hinauf\" is a lost punctuation (OCR); I supplied a semicolon.","- 0991: \"Hauptstück\" rendered \"Chapter\"; the heading's \"Des dritten Hauptstücks\" is given as in the German.","- 0992–0995: \"Nahmenerklärung\" = nominal definition; \"Widersinnische\" = paradoxical; \"[in it]\" supplies the German \"(in ihm)\" which is in the text in parentheses, rendered in brackets for clarity.","- 0995: the final sentence is divided and restated; \"Obiect\"/\"Gegenstand\" both rendered \"object\"."]],"krv-a-0996":["If in an identical judgment I cancel the predicate and retain the subject, contradiction results; and I therefore say that the former belongs to the latter necessarily. But if I cancel subject and predicate together, no contradiction results; for there is then nothing left that can be contradicted. To posit a triangle and yet cancel its three angles is self-contradictory; but to cancel the triangle together with its three angles is not a contradiction. Exactly the same holds of the concept of an absolutely necessary being. If you cancel its existence, you cancel the thing itself with all its predicates. How, then, can contradiction arise? Externally there is nothing that would be contradicted, for the thing is not supposed to be externally necessary; nor is there anything internally, for by cancelling the thing itself you have cancelled everything internal at the same time. \"God is omnipotent\" is a necessary judgment. Omnipotence cannot be cancelled if you posit a Deity, that is, an infinite being, with whose concept omnipotence is identical. But if you say \"God is not,\" then neither omnipotence nor any other of his predicates is given; they are all cancelled together with the subject, and in this thought there is not the least trace of contradiction.",["- \"Aufheben\" rendered \"cancel\" throughout (the sense is removal of the thing in thought); \"setzen\" as \"posit\".","- \"ienes … diesem\" resolved as predicate … subject; \"iener\" in the last sentences refers to omnipotence.","- \"unendlich Wesen\" is the text's reading (for *unendliches*); rendered \"infinite being\"."]],"krv-a-0997":["You have thus seen that if I cancel the predicate of a judgment together with the subject, no inner contradiction can ever arise, whatever the predicate may be. No escape is now left to you save to say that there are subjects which cannot be cancelled at all, and which must therefore remain. But that would be the same as saying that there are absolutely necessary subjects. This is the very presupposition whose correctness I have questioned, and whose possibility you undertook to show me. For I cannot form the least concept of a thing which, if cancelled with all its predicates, would leave behind a contradiction; and without contradiction I have, through mere pure concepts *a priori*, no mark of impossibility.",["- \"Nun bleibt euch keine Ausflucht übrig, als ihr müßt sagen\" is irregular; smoothed to \"No escape is now left to you save to say\".","- \"schlechterdingsnothwendig\" rendered \"absolutely necessary\"."]],"krv-a-0998":["Against all these general inferences, which no one can refuse, you challenge me with a case which you put forward as proof by fact. You say that there is one concept, and this one alone, in which the not-being or cancelling of its object is self-contradictory, namely the concept of the *ens realissimum*. [You say] that it has all reality, and that you are entitled to assume such a being as possible (to which I agree for the present, although a concept which is not self-contradictory is still far from proving the possibility of its object*). Now existence is included among all reality; existence therefore lies in the concept of what is possible. If, then, this thing is cancelled, the inner possibility of the thing is cancelled, and that is self-contradictory.",["- \"allerrealesten Wesens\" rendered by Kant's Latin parallel *ens realissimum*; \"[You say]\" supplied to carry \"sagt ihr\" over the clause.","- The asterisk refers to the following footnote."]],"krv-a-0999":["*A concept is always possible if it is not self-contradictory. That is the logical mark of possibility, and by it the object of the concept is distinguished from the *nihil negativum*. But the concept may none the less be an empty concept, unless the objective reality of the synthesis through which it is generated has been specially established. Such establishment, as has been shown above, always rests on principles of possible experience, and not on the principle of analysis (the law of contradiction). This is a warning not to infer straightway from the possibility of concepts (logical) to the possibility of things (real).",["- Text has \"nihil negatiuum\", normalised to *nihil negativum*.","- \"so fort\" read as \"sofort\", \"straightway\"."]],"krv-a-1000":["I reply: You have already committed a contradiction when, in the concept of a thing which you profess to think merely in respect of its possibility, you have introduced, under whatever disguised name, the concept of its existence. If this is granted you, you have apparently won the game, but in fact have said nothing; you have merely committed a tautology. I ask you: is the proposition \"This or that thing (which I grant you as possible, whatever it may be) exists\" an analytic or a synthetic proposition? If the former, then by the existence of the thing you add nothing to your thought of the thing. But in that case either the thought which is in you must be the thing itself, or you have presupposed existence as belonging to possibility, and have then professedly inferred existence from inner possibility, which is nothing but a miserable tautology. The word \"reality\", which sounds different in the concept of the thing from \"existence\" in the concept of the predicate, does not settle the matter. For even if you call all positing \"reality\" (leaving undetermined what it is that you posit), you have already posited the thing, with all its predicates, in the concept of the subject and assumed it as actual, and in the predicate you merely repeat it. If, on the other hand, you admit, as every reasonable person must in fairness admit, that every existential proposition is synthetic, how can you maintain that the predicate of existence cannot be cancelled without contradiction? For this privilege is the peculiar property of analytic propositions, whose character rests precisely upon it.",["- Long sentences divided where the construction allowed; \"Spiel gewonnen\" rendered \"won the game\".","- \"als deren Character eben darauf beruht\": \"deren\" taken as the analytic propositions.","- \"Existenzialsatz\" rendered \"existential proposition\"."]],"krv-a-1001":["I might indeed hope to put an end to this subtle argumentation, without any circumlocution, by an exact determination of the concept of existence, had I not found that the illusion, in the confusing of a logical predicate with a real predicate (that is, with the determination of a thing), almost refuses all instruction. Anything we please can serve as a logical predicate; the subject can even be predicated of itself; for logic abstracts from all content. But a determination is a predicate which is added to the concept of the subject and enlarges it. It must not, therefore, be already contained in it.",["- \"Bestimmung\" = determination; \"Illusion … beinahe alle Belehrung ausschlage\" rendered literally as \"almost refuses all instruction\" (i.e. resists it).","- \"Argutation\" rendered \"argumentation\", with \"subtle\" for \"grüblerisch\"."]],"krv-a-1002":["'Being' is evidently not a real predicate; that is, it is not a concept of something which could be added to the concept of a thing. It is merely the positing of a thing, or of certain determinations, as existing in themselves. In its logical employment it is merely the copula of a judgment. The proposition 'God is omnipotent' contains two concepts, each of which has its object: God and omnipotence. The small word 'is' adds no new predicate, but only serves to posit the predicate in its relation to the subject. If now I take the subject (God) together with all its predicates (among which omnipotence is included), and say 'God is' or 'There is a God', I attach no new predicate to the concept of God, but only posit the subject in itself, with all its predicates, and indeed posit the object in relation to my concept. The content of both must be exactly the same. Nothing can therefore be added to the concept, which expresses merely what is possible, by my thinking its object as given absolutely (through the expression 'it is'). Thus the actual contains no more than the merely possible. A hundred actual thalers do not contain the least coin more than a hundred possible thalers. For the possible thalers signify the concept, the actual thalers the object and its positing in itself. Were the object to contain more than the concept, my concept would not express the whole object, and would not be the adequate concept of it. But in respect of my financial position there is more in a hundred actual thalers than in the mere concept of them (that is, in their possibility). For in the case of actuality the object is not merely contained analytically in my concept, but is added synthetically to my concept (which is a determination of my state); yet the hundred thalers which I am thinking are not themselves in the least increased through this existence outside my concept.",["- Restated the long sentence beginning \"Denn, da diese…\" as two sentences, and the \"Beide müssen…\" sentence as two, for clarity.","- \"Mindeste\" rendered \"least coin\" to keep the thaler example; this is a slight supplement, since the German says only \"not the least more\".","- \"Vermögenszustand\" is rendered \"financial position\" (a choice; not Vermögen as faculty).","- \"gewisser Bestimmungen an sich selbst\": rendered \"as existing in themselves\" with \"as existing\" supplied for sense; the German has only \"positing... of certain determinations in themselves\".","- \"Position\" rendered \"positing\" (Setzung), consistently with \"setzt\"."]],"krv-a-1003":["Accordingly, however many predicates I may use in thinking a thing, and whichever they may be (even if I determine it completely), nothing is added to the thing when I further declare that this thing is. Otherwise what exists would not be the very same thing that I had thought in the concept, but something more, and I could not say that the very object of my concept exists. Even if I think in a thing all reality save one, the missing reality is not supplied by my saying that such a defective thing exists. It exists with precisely the same defect as that with which I thought it; otherwise something other than what I thought would exist. If, then, I think a being as the highest reality (without defect), the question still remains whether it exists or not. For though nothing is lacking in my concept of the possible real content of a thing in general, something is still lacking in the relation to my whole state of thinking, namely, that knowledge of that object should also be possible *a posteriori*. And here the cause of the difficulty that besets this question becomes apparent. Were an object of the senses in question, I could not confound the existence of the thing with the mere concept of it. For through the concept the object is thought only as in agreement with the universal conditions of a possible empirical knowledge in general; through existence it is thought as contained in the context of the whole of experience. Through connection with the content of the whole of experience the concept of the object is not in the least increased; but our thought obtains through it one more possible perception. If, on the other hand, we try to think existence through the pure category alone, it is no wonder that we cannot specify any mark by which to distinguish it from mere possibility.",["- Divided the sentence beginning \"Denn durch den Begriff…\" into three sentences; \"da denn\" resolved as a plain continuation.","- \"Erkentniß\" = knowledge; \"durchgängige Bestimmung\" = complete determination (Kant's technical *omnimoda determinatio*).","- \"durch welche und wie viel Prädicate ich will\" smoothed to \"however many predicates… and whichever they may be\".","- \"ienes Obiects\" refers to the being of highest reality."]],"krv-a-1004":["However much or however little our concept of an object may contain, we must go outside it if we are to ascribe existence to the object. In the case of objects of the senses this is done through connection with some one of my perceptions in accordance with empirical laws. For objects of pure thought, however, there is no means whatever of knowing their existence, since it would have to be known entirely *a priori*. Our consciousness of all existence (whether immediate, through perception, or mediate, through inferences which connect something with perception) belongs wholly to the unity of experience. An existence outside this field cannot indeed be declared absolutely impossible, but it is a presupposition which we are unable to justify by anything.",["- \"vor Obiecte\" (for \"für\") and \"in ganz und gar kein Mittel\" are read as \"für Objekte… ist ganz und gar kein Mittel\"; the \"in\" is evidently a misprint for \"ist\" (silently corrected).","- Restated the long final sentence as two.","- \"Gegenstände\" and \"Objecte\" are both rendered \"objects\"."]],"krv-a-1005":["The concept of a supreme being is in many respects a very useful idea; but just because it is merely an idea, it is quite incapable of extending, by itself alone, our knowledge in regard to what exists. It cannot even instruct us as to the possibility of anything further. The analytic mark of possibility, which consists in the fact that mere positings (realities) give rise to no contradiction, cannot indeed be denied to it. But the combination of all real properties in one thing is a synthesis, and of its possibility we cannot judge *a priori*, because the realities are not given to us specifically; and even if they were, no judgment would be possible at all, since the mark of the possibility of synthetic knowledge is to be sought only in experience, to which the object of an idea cannot belong. Hence the celebrated Leibniz has by no means accomplished what he flattered himself he had done, namely, to comprehend *a priori* the possibility of so sublime an ideal being.",["- Restated one very long sentence as two, placing the concessive clause (\"kan ihm zwar nicht gestritten werden\") in a separate sentence with \"But\" for the following \"weil\".","- \"ihm\" in \"kan ihm… nicht gestritten werden\" refers to the concept of a supreme being.","- \"Positionen\" rendered \"positings\"; \"specifisch\" kept as \"specifically\".","- \"wollen\" in \"einsehen zu wollen\" smoothed to \"to comprehend\"."]],"krv-a-1006":["All the trouble and labour spent on the celebrated ontological (Cartesian) proof of the existence of a supreme being from concepts is therefore lost. A man can no more become richer in insight through mere ideas than a merchant can become richer in property by adding a few noughts to his cash account in order to improve his position.",["- Divided the German sentence in two after \"verloren\".","- \"Vermögen\" here = property/means; \"Cassenbestand\" = cash balance."]],"krv-a-1007":["Chapter III, Section V. The Impossibility of a Cosmological Proof of the Existence of God.",["- \"Hauptstück\" rendered \"chapter\"; the full heading of the section is \"Fifth Section of the Third Chapter\"."]],"krv-a-1008":["It was something quite unnatural, and a mere innovation of scholastic subtlety, to try to extract from an entirely arbitrary idea the existence of the corresponding object itself. Indeed, this path would never have been attempted had there not first been the need of our reason to assume, for existence in general, something necessary (at which, in ascending, we can come to a halt); and had not reason, since this necessity must be unconditioned and certain *a priori*, been constrained to seek a concept which, if possible, should satisfy such a demand and enable us to know an existence completely *a priori*. This concept was believed to be found in the idea of a most real being, and so the idea was employed only for the more determinate knowledge of that of which we were already otherwise convinced or persuaded, namely, that it must exist, that is, the necessary being. Meanwhile this natural course of reason was concealed; and instead of ending with this concept, men tried to begin with it, in order to derive from it the necessity of existence which it was only meant to supplement. Hence arose the unfortunate ontological proof, which brings with it nothing that satisfies either the natural and healthy understanding or the demands of scholastic examination.",["- Restated the long first sentence's two \"wäre nicht\" clauses in parallel as \"had not… and had not\".","- \"allerrealestes Wesen\" = *ens realissimum*, rendered \"most real being\".","- \"Schulwitz\" = \"scholastic subtlety\" (a choice); \"man\" rendered \"men\" or passive for English idiom.","- \"die Bedürfniß\" (feminine in the text) is read as \"das Bedürfniß\".","- \"Verstand\" = understanding; \"schulgerechte Prüfung\" = \"scholastic examination\"."]],"krv-a-1009":["The cosmological proof, which we are now to examine, retains the connection of absolute necessity with highest reality. But instead of inferring, as the preceding proof did, from the highest reality to necessity of existence, it infers from the antecedently given unconditioned necessity of some being to that being's unlimited reality. In so doing it brings everything at least into the track of a mode of inference which is natural, though I do not know whether to call it rational or sophistical [*vernünftelnd*]. This mode of inference carries the greatest persuasion not only for the common but also for the speculative understanding. It also visibly draws the first outlines of all the proofs of natural theology, outlines which men have always followed and will continue to follow, however much foliage and flourish they may use to adorn and conceal them. This proof, which Leibniz also entitled the proof *a contingentia mundi*, we shall now set before us and submit to examination.",["- One long sentence divided into four; \"und bringt so fern alles…\" is made a separate sentence, and the relative clauses (\"welche…\", \"denen…\") are recast as sentences with \"This mode of inference\" and \"men\" as subjects.","- *vernünftelnd* = \"sophistical\" (used consistently below); *Verstand* = understanding; \"men\" is supplied as the implied subject of \"nachgegangen ist\"."]],"krv-a-1010":["It runs as follows: If anything exists, an absolutely necessary being must also exist. Now I myself, at the very least, exist. Therefore an absolutely necessary being exists. The minor premiss contains an experience; the major premiss, the inference from an experience in general to the existence of the necessary [*]. Thus the proof really begins from experience, and is therefore not conducted wholly *a priori*, or ontologically; and since the object of all possible experience is entitled the world, it is called the cosmological proof. It also abstracts from every special property of the objects of experience, by which this world may be distinguished from any other possible world. In this respect, too, it is distinguished in its very title from the physico-theological proof, which uses as grounds of proof observations of the particular constitution of this our sensible world.",["- The footnote marker \"*)\" is rendered \"[*]\" to avoid confusion with the italics markup; the footnote follows as the next passage.","- *Untersatz* = minor premiss, *Obersatz* = major premiss; \"heißt\" = \"is entitled\".","- The last sentence is divided in two for clarity (\"Da er auch…: so wird er…\")."]],"krv-a-1011":["[*] This inference is too familiar to call for detailed exposition here. It rests on the supposedly transcendental law of nature of causality: that everything contingent has its cause, which, if it is in turn contingent, must likewise have a cause. This continues until the series of subordinated causes must end in an absolutely necessary cause, without which the series would not be complete.",["- Footnote text; the marker \"[*]\" stands for \"*)\".","- The sentence is divided at the colon; \"muß… endigen muß\" is smoothed by supplying \"This continues\"."]],"krv-a-1012":["The proof now proceeds further: The necessary being can be determined in only one way; that is, in respect of all possible opposed predicates it can be determined by only one of them. Consequently it must be completely determined through its concept. Now only one concept of a thing is possible which determines the thing completely *a priori*, namely that of the *ens realissimum*. The concept of the most real being is therefore the only concept through which a necessary being can be thought. That is, a highest being exists of necessity.",["- Divided into shorter sentences; \"durchgängig bestimt\" = \"completely determined\" (*Bestimmung* = determination).","- \"allerrealesten Wesens\" rendered \"most real being\", with *ens realissimum* kept in Latin."]],"krv-a-1013":["In this cosmological argument so many sophistical principles meet together that speculative reason seems to have summoned all its dialectical skill to produce the greatest possible transcendental illusion. We shall, however, set its examination aside for a while, in order to expose one stratagem of reason. By it, reason sets up an old argument in disguise as a new one, and appeals to the agreement of two witnesses, one of pure reason and another of empirical credentials. In fact there is only the first, which merely changes its dress and voice in order to be taken for a second. To lay its foundation quite securely, this proof bases itself on experience, and thereby gives itself the appearance of being distinct from the ontological proof, which places its whole trust in pure concepts *a priori*. But the cosmological proof makes use of this experience only to take a single step, namely to the existence of a necessary being in general. What properties this being may have, the empirical ground of proof cannot teach us. Reason here takes complete leave of it, and inquires among mere concepts what properties an absolutely necessary being in general must have; that is, which among all possible things contains in itself the requisite conditions (*requisita*) of absolute necessity. Now reason believes that it finds these requisites, solely and uniquely, in the concept of a most real being, and so concludes: this is the absolutely necessary being. But it is clear that reason here presupposes that the concept of a being of highest reality is entirely adequate to the concept of absolute necessity in existence; that is, that the latter can be inferred from the former. This is a proposition which the ontological argument asserted. It is therefore assumed and made the ground in the cosmological proof, although the intention was to avoid it. For absolute necessity is an existence from mere concepts. If I say that the concept of the *ens realissimum* is a concept of this kind, and indeed the only one that is appropriate and adequate to necessary existence, I must also admit that the latter can be inferred from it. It is thus really only the ontological proof, from mere concepts, that contains all the force of proof in the so-called cosmological proof; and the alleged experience is quite idle. It may serve perhaps to lead us to the concept of absolute necessity, but not to establish this in any determinate thing. For as soon as we have the latter in view, we must at once abandon all experience, and seek among pure concepts which of them contains the conditions of the possibility of an absolutely necessary being. But if the possibility of such a being is in this way seen, its existence is also demonstrated. For this is as much as to say: among all that is possible there is one thing which carries absolute necessity with it; that is, this being exists with absolute necessity.",["- Very long passage divided into many sentences; the \"mit welcher… da es doch nur\" construction is recast as \"By it… In fact there is only the first…\".","- \"ein Satz, den… welches man also… annimt\" has a shifting antecedent; I take \"welches\" to refer to the proposition (the ontological claim) and say so plainly (\"It is therefore assumed\").","- \"iener… diese\" = \"the former… the latter\"; \"dieses\" in \"Denn sobald wir dieses zur Absicht haben\" is read as the demonstration of absolute necessity in a determinate thing, hence \"the latter\".","- *Beweisgrund* = \"ground of proof\"; *Requisite* kept with Kant's Latin *requisita*."]],"krv-a-1014":["All illusions in inference are most easily detected when we set them before our eyes in the manner of the schools. Here is such a presentation.",["- The German lacks a full stop after \"Augen stellt\"; supplied silently in the English."]],"krv-a-1015":["If the proposition is correct that every absolutely necessary being is at the same time the most real being (which is the *nervus probandi* of the cosmological proof), it must, like all affirmative judgments, be capable of conversion, at least *per accidens*. Thus: some most real beings are at the same time absolutely necessary beings. Now one *ens realissimum* is in no respect different from another, and what holds of some beings contained under this concept holds also of all. Hence in this case I shall be able to convert simply; that is, every most real being is a necessary being. Since this proposition is determined solely from its concepts *a priori*, the mere concept of the most real being must also carry with it the absolute necessity of that being. This is precisely what the ontological proof asserted and what the cosmological proof would not recognise, although it covertly made it the basis of its inferences.",["- \"neruus\" read as *nervus* (OCR slip, corrected silently).","- Last sentence divided; \"unterlegte\" rendered \"made it the basis of\", and \"welches\" is taken to refer to the whole proposition."]],"krv-a-1016":["Thus the second way which speculative reason takes to prove the existence of the highest being is not only as deceptive as the first, but has this further defect, that it commits an *ignoratio elenchi*. It promises to lead us by a new footpath, but after a short circuit brings us back to the old one, which we had left for its sake.",["- Divided into two sentences; \"seinetwegen\" = \"for its sake\" (i.e. for the sake of the new path)."]],"krv-a-1017":["I said a little earlier that this cosmological argument harbours a whole nest of dialectical presumptions, which transcendental critique can easily discover and destroy. I shall now merely enumerate them, and leave it to the reader, who is already practised, to pursue the deceptive principles further and to remove them.",["- *Critik* = \"critique\"; \"anführen\" is rendered \"enumerate\", in the sense of listing the presumptions, not discussing them. \"aufheben\" = \"remove\"."]],"krv-a-1018":["Here we find, for instance, the following. (1) The transcendental principle that we can infer from the contingent to a cause. This principle is of significance only in the sensible world; outside that world it has not even a meaning. For the merely intellectual concept of the contingent cannot give rise to any synthetic proposition, such as that of causality. The principle of causality has no significance, and no criterion of its use, save only in the sensible world; yet here it would have to serve precisely for getting beyond the sensible world. (2) The inference from the impossibility of an infinite series of causes given one above another in the sensible world to a first cause. The principles of the employment of reason do not entitle us to this inference even within experience, still less can we extend the principle beyond experience, to where this chain cannot be prolonged at all. (3) The false self-satisfaction of reason in respect of the completion of this series. It is brought about by our finally removing every condition, without which no concept of a necessity can be possible, and then, since we can comprehend nothing further, accepting this as a completion of our concept. (4) The confusion of the logical possibility of a concept of all united reality (a concept free from inner contradiction) with the transcendental possibility. The latter requires a principle of the practicability of such a synthesis, and this principle can in turn apply only to the field of possible experiences; and so on.",["- Kant's list is numbered inline; I kept the numbering but made each item a separate sentence, completing the elliptical items 2–3 by the construction the sense requires (no words in brackets, as the German supplies the sense).","- Item 2: the German sentence is irregular (infinitive clause, \"wozu...berechtigen, vielweniger...können\"); restated as two sentences, \"this inference\" supplied for \"diesen Grundsatz\" sense-wise.","- \"Thunlichkeit\" = practicability; \"Sinnenwelt\" = sensible world; \"Merkmal\" = criterion (choice; \"mark\" also possible)."]],"krv-a-1019":["The artifice of the cosmological proof aims solely at evading the proof of the existence of a necessary being *a priori* through mere concepts, a proof which would have to be conducted ontologically, and for which we feel ourselves wholly incapacitated. With this end in view we infer, from an actual existence taken as ground (an experience in general), as best we can, to some absolutely necessary condition of that existence. We then have no need to explain the possibility of this condition. For if it is proved that it exists, the question of its possibility is entirely unnecessary.\n\nIf we now wish to determine more precisely the nature of this necessary being, we do not seek that which would suffice to comprehend from its concept the necessity of its existence; for if we could do this, we should need no empirical presupposition. No, we seek only the negative condition (*conditio sine qua non*) without which a being would not be absolutely necessary. In every other kind of inference, from a given consequence to its ground, this procedure would be quite admissible. Unfortunately, however, it happens here that the condition demanded for absolute necessity can be met with only in a single being. This being would therefore have to contain in its concept everything requisite for absolute necessity, and so make possible an inference *a priori* to that necessity. That is, I should also have to be able to infer conversely: whatever thing this concept (of the highest reality) belongs to is absolutely necessary. If I cannot so infer (as I must admit if I wish to avoid the ontological proof), then I have come to grief also on my new path, and find myself again at the point from which I set out.\n\nThe concept of the highest being does indeed satisfy all the questions *a priori* which can be raised regarding the inner determinations of a thing, and is for that reason also an ideal without equal, since the universal concept at the same time distinguishes it as an individual among all possible things. But it does not satisfy the question regarding its own existence, which was yet the sole point at issue. And to one who admitted the existence of a necessary being, and wished only to know which among all things must be regarded as such, we could not answer: \"This here is the necessary being.\"",["- Divided the long middle sentence into several; \"d. i.\" rendered \"That is\".","- \"Kunststück\" = artifice; \"unvermögend\" = incapacitated; \"Bestimmungen\" = determinations.","- \"Es trift sich... unglücklicher Weise\" rendered \"Unfortunately... it happens\"."]],"krv-a-1020":["It may well be permissible to assume the existence of a being of the highest sufficiency, as cause of all possible effects, in order to facilitate for reason the unity of explanatory grounds which it seeks. But to presume so much as to say that such a being *necessarily* exists is no longer the modest expression of a permissible hypothesis, but the bold assumption of apodeictic certainty. For if anything is professedly known as absolutely necessary, the knowledge of it must carry with it absolute necessity.",["- \"Erkentniß\" = knowledge. \"Zulänglichkeit\" = sufficiency. \"schlechthinnothwendig\" = absolutely necessary."]],"krv-a-1021":["The whole problem of the transcendental ideal comes to this: either to find a concept for absolute necessity, or to find absolute necessity for the concept of some thing. If we can do the one, we must be able to do the other; for reason recognises as absolutely necessary only what is necessary from its concept. But both tasks wholly exceed all our utmost endeavours to satisfy our understanding on this point, and equally all attempts to reconcile it to this its incapacity.",["- \"Aufgabe... komt darauf an\" rendered \"comes to this\". \"beruhigen\" = reconcile (lit. quieten); a choice."]],"krv-a-1022":["The unconditioned necessity which we so indispensably require as the ultimate support of all things is the veritable abyss for human reason. Even eternity, however sublimely a Haller may depict it, is far from making so dizzying an impression upon the mind; for it only measures the duration of things, it does not sustain them. We cannot resist the thought, yet we also cannot endure it, that a being which we represent to ourselves as the highest among all possible beings should, as it were, say to itself: \"I am from eternity to eternity; outside me there is nothing, save what is something through my will alone; but whence then am I?\" Here everything gives way beneath us; the greatest perfection, like the smallest, floats without support before speculative reason, to which it costs nothing to let the one vanish as readily as the other, without the least hindrance.",["- \"schauderhafterhaben\" = awe-inspiring/sublimely; rendered \"sublimely\". Haller: Albrecht von Haller, poem on eternity.","- \"koste\" (subjunctive) kept as a relative clause, \"to which it costs nothing\"."]],"krv-a-1023":["Many powers of nature, which manifest their existence through certain effects, remain inscrutable to us, since we cannot trace them far enough by observation. The transcendental object which underlies appearances, and with it the ground why our sensibility is subject to these rather than to other supreme conditions, are and remain inscrutable to us, although the thing itself is given, but is not understood. A *Ideal* of pure reason, however, cannot be entitled inscrutable, since it has no credential of its reality to show other than the need of reason to complete by means of it all synthetic unity. Since, then, it is not given even as a thinkable object, it is not inscrutable as such. Rather, as a mere idea, it must find its seat and its solution in the nature of reason, and so must be capable of investigation. For reason consists precisely in this, that we can give an account of all our concepts, opinions and assertions, whether on objective grounds or, if they are mere illusion, on subjective grounds.",["- Correction: read \"An ideal\" for the slip \"A *Ideal*\"; no italics are in the German. \"Vermögen\"/\"Kräfte\" = powers.","- \"unerforschlich\" = inscrutable; \"Bedürfniß\" = need; \"Schein\" = illusion.","- Kant's \"er\" (referring to Ideal, though the antecedent \"es\" precedes) rendered \"it\"."]],"krv-a-1024":["Discovery and explanation of the dialectical illusion in all transcendental proofs of the existence of a necessary being.",["- A heading, translated as a nominal phrase without a verb."]],"krv-a-1025":["Both of the proofs hitherto considered were transcendental; that is, they were attempted independently of empirical principles. For although the cosmological proof takes as its ground an experience in general, it is not based upon any special character of that experience, but upon pure principles of reason, in relation to an existence given through empirical consciousness in general; and it even abandons this guidance, in order to rest solely on pure concepts. What, then, in these transcendental proofs, is the cause of the dialectical, but natural, illusion which connects the concepts of necessity and of highest reality, and which realises and hypostatises what can be no more than an idea? What is the cause of the unavoidableness of assuming something among existing things to be necessary in itself, while yet at the same time we shrink back, as from an abyss, from the existence of such a being? And how are we to proceed so that reason may come to understand itself in this matter, and may pass from the wavering state of a timid assent, ever again withdrawn, to a tranquil insight?",["- Restated the last sentence as two questions plus a third (\"And how…\"); the German runs them on with one \"und\".","- \"empiriche\" (sic) silently corrected to \"empirische\"; \"Bewustseyn\" rendered \"consciousness\".","- \"Idee\" = idea; \"realisirt und hypostasirt\" = \"realises and hypostatises\".","- \"Beifall\" rendered \"assent\"; \"Einsicht\" rendered \"insight\"."]],"krv-a-1026":["It is something very remarkable that, if we presuppose that something exists, we cannot avoid the inference that something exists of necessity. The cosmological argument rested upon this quite natural (though not on that account certain) inference. On the other hand, whatever concept of a thing I may assume, I find that its existence can never be represented by me as absolutely necessary, and that nothing prevents me, whatever may exist, from thinking its non-existence. I find, therefore, that while I must assume something necessary in relation to existence in general, I cannot think any single thing as in itself necessary. That is to say, I can never complete the regress to the conditions of existence without assuming a necessary being, but I can never begin from such a being.",["- \"Dagegen\" taken as \"on the other hand\", marking the contrast with the cosmological inference.","- Divided the long second sentence at \"mithin\", and gave \"mithin\" as \"therefore\" with a supplied \"I find\".","- \"Wesen\" = being; \"Daseyn\" = existence; \"Existirende\" = what exists."]],"krv-a-1027":["If I must think something necessary for existing things in general, yet am not entitled to think any single thing as necessary in itself, it follows unavoidably that necessity and contingency do not concern and affect the things themselves, since otherwise a contradiction would arise. Neither of these two principles, therefore, is objective; at most they can be subjective principles of reason. On the one hand, reason is to seek, for everything given as existing, something that is necessary; that is, it is never to stop anywhere but at an explanation completed *a priori*. On the other hand, it is never to hope for this completion; that is, it is to assume nothing empirical as unconditioned, and so to excuse itself from further derivation. In this sense the two principles, as merely heuristic and regulative, and as concerned with nothing but the formal interest of reason, can very well subsist side by side. For the one says: you are to philosophise about nature as if there were a necessary first ground for everything belonging to existence, solely in order to bring systematic unity into your knowledge, by pursuing such an idea, namely a fictitious supreme ground. The other, however, warns you to accept no single determination that concerns the existence of things as such a supreme ground, that is, as absolutely necessary, but to keep the way open for further derivation, and therefore always to treat such a determination as still conditioned. But if everything that is perceived in things must, for us, be regarded as conditionally necessary, no thing (that may be empirically given) can be regarded as absolutely necessary.",["- Divided the first sentence at \"mithin\" and the long \"nemlich\" clause; the German is irregular in construction and I smoothed it.","- \"Bestimmung\" = determination; \"Erkentniß\" = knowledge; \"eingebildet\" = fictitious (literally \"imagined\").","- \"befugt bin\" rendered \"am entitled\"; \"sich ... zu überheben\" rendered \"to excuse itself from\".","- \"bedingtnothwendig\"/\"absolutnothwendig\" rendered \"conditionally\"/\"absolutely necessary\".","- \"vor\" in \"vor einen solchen obersten Grund\" read as \"für\" (as); \"vor uns\" read as \"für uns\"."]],"krv-a-1028":["It follows, however, that you must assume the absolutely necessary to lie outside the world. For it is to serve only as a principle of the greatest possible unity of appearances, as their supreme ground, and you can never attain to it within the world, since the second rule commands you always to regard all empirical causes of unity as derived.",["- Divided at the semicolon; \"weil\" clauses recast as \"For ... since ...\". \"Erscheinungen\" = appearances."]],"krv-a-1029":["The philosophers of antiquity regarded all form of nature as contingent, but matter, in the judgment of common reason, as original and necessary. Had they, however, considered matter not relatively, as the substratum of appearances, but in itself, in respect of its existence, the idea of absolute necessity would at once have vanished. For there is nothing that binds reason absolutely to this existence; it can always, and without conflict, annul it in thought; and in thought alone lay the absolute necessity. Some regulative principle, therefore, must have underlain this persuasion. In fact, extension and impenetrability (which together constitute the concept of matter) are the supreme empirical principle of the unity of appearances, and, so far as it is empirically unconditioned, have a property of a regulative principle in themselves. Nevertheless, every determination of matter which constitutes its real, and therefore impenetrability also, is an effect (action) which must have its cause, and is accordingly always still derived; matter is therefore not suited to the idea of a necessary being, as a principle of all derived unity. For each of its real properties, being derived, is only conditionally necessary and can therefore in itself be removed, and with this the whole existence of matter would be removed. If this were not so, we should have reached the highest ground of unity empirically, which the second regulative principle forbids. It follows that matter, and in general whatever belongs to the world, is not suited to the idea of a necessary original being, as a mere principle of the greatest empirical unity, but that such a being must be posited outside the world. We can then confidently derive the appearances of the world and their existence always from others, as if there were no necessary being, and yet strive unceasingly after completeness of derivation, as if such a being, as a supreme ground, were presupposed.",["- The final sentence is a long anacoluthon (\"weil ... hiemit ... wenn dieses aber nicht geschähe ... so folgt\"); I broke it into four sentences and supplied \"therefore\", \"If this were not so\".","- \"regulative Princip\" (feminine \"regulative\" in the German) silently read as \"regulatives\". \"Bollständigkeit\" corrected to \"Vollständigkeit\".","- \"respectiv\" rendered \"relatively\"; \"Reale\" = real; \"Urwesen\" = original being.","- \"Eigenschaft ... an sich\" rendered \"a property ... in themselves\", \"extension and impenetrability\" being the subject."]],"krv-a-1030":["On these considerations the ideal of the supreme being is nothing other than a regulative principle of reason, to regard all connection in the world as if it sprang from an all-sufficient necessary cause, in order to found upon this the rule of a systematic unity, necessary according to universal laws, in the explanation of that connection; it is not an assertion of an existence necessary in itself. But it is at the same time unavoidable, by means of a transcendental subreption, to represent this formal principle to ourselves as constitutive, and to think this unity hypostatically. For space, since it makes originally possible all figures, which are merely different limitations of it, is, although only a principle of sensibility, yet for that very reason taken to be something absolutely necessary and self-subsistent, and an object given *a priori* in itself. In the same way it happens quite naturally that the systematic unity of nature can in no way be set up as a principle of the empirical employment of our reason save in so far as we take as ground the idea of a most real being, as the supreme cause; and that this idea is thereby represented as an actual object, and this object in turn, because it is the supreme condition, as necessary, so that a regulative principle is transformed into a constitutive. This substitution reveals itself in the following way. If I now consider this supreme being, which relatively to the world was unconditionally necessary, as a thing by itself, this necessity is incapable of any concept. It must therefore have been met with in my reason only as a formal condition of thought, not as a material and hypostatic condition of existence.",["- Divided the final sentence at \"welche Unterschiebung\" and \"und also\"; supplied \"In the same way\" for \"so geht es auch ganz natürlich zu\".","- \"Subreption\" kept as \"subreption\"; \"Unterschiebung\" = substitution; \"allerrealesten Wesen\" = most real being.","- \"vor\" = \"für\" (\"taken to be\"); \"Gegenstand\" = object; \"Sinnlichkeit\" = sensibility.","- \"Principium\" rendered \"principle\"."]],"krv-a-1031":["Third Chapter, Sixth Section. On the Impossibility of the Physico-theological Proof.",["- Heading as it stands; \"Hauptstück\" rendered \"Chapter\", \"Abschnitt\" \"Section\"."]],"krv-a-1032":["If, then, neither the concept of things in general nor the experience of any existence in general can accomplish what is required, one means still remains: to try whether a determinate experience, and so the experience of the things of the present world, with their constitution and order, may not furnish a ground of proof which can surely help us to the conviction that a supreme being exists. Such a proof we should entitle the physico-theological. Should this also be impossible, then no satisfactory proof from merely speculative reason is possible at all for the existence of a being corresponding to our transcendental idea.",[]],"krv-a-1033":["In view of all the above remarks, it will soon be seen that the answer to this inquiry can be expected to be quite easy and conclusive. For how can an experience ever be given which would be adequate to an idea? It is precisely the peculiarity of an idea that no experience can ever be congruent with it. The transcendental idea of a necessary, all-sufficient original being is so overwhelmingly great, so far exalted above everything empirical, which is always conditioned, that, on the one hand, we can never find in experience matter enough to fill such a concept; and, on the other hand, we shall always be groping about among the conditioned, and shall seek in vain for the unconditioned, of which no law of any empirical synthesis gives us an example, or affords the least guidance.",[]],"krv-a-1034":["Were the supreme being to stand in this chain of conditions, it would itself be a member of the series, and, like the lower members to which it is prefixed, would call for further enquiry into a still higher ground. If, on the other hand, we propose to separate it from the chain, and, as a merely intelligible being, not to include it in the series of natural causes, what bridge can reason then build to reach it? [This question is pressing] since all laws governing the transition from effects to causes, indeed all synthesis and extension of our knowledge in general, rest on nothing but possible experience, and therefore solely on objects of the world of sense, and can have meaning only in respect of them.",[]],"krv-a-1035":["The present world opens to us so immeasurable a spectacle of manifold variety, order, purposiveness and beauty, whether we pursue these in the infinity of space or in its unlimited divisibility, that even with such knowledge as our weak understanding has been able to acquire of it, all language, in face of wonders so many and so immeasurably great, loses its force, all numbers their power to measure, and even our thoughts all limit; so that our judgment upon the whole must resolve itself into a speechless, but all the more eloquent, astonishment. Everywhere we see a chain of effects and causes, of ends and means, regularity in coming to be and passing away. And since nothing has of itself come into the state in which it finds itself, this state always refers us further to another thing as its cause, which makes precisely the same further enquiry necessary. In this fashion the whole universe would have to sink into the abyss of nothing, were we not to assume something which, existing outside this infinite chain of contingencies, originally and independently on its own account, upholds it, and, as the cause of its origin, at the same time secures its continuance. How great are we to think this highest cause (in respect of all things in the world)? We do not know the world in its whole content, still less can we estimate its magnitude by comparison with all that is possible. But what prevents us, since in regard to causality we require an ultimate and supreme being, from placing it at the same time, in degree of perfection, above everything else that is possible? This we can easily do, though only by the delicate outline of an abstract concept, if we represent to ourselves as united in it, as a single substance, all possible perfection. Such a concept is favourable to the demand of our reason for parsimony in principles; it is not in itself subject to any contradictions; it is even conducive to the extension of the employment of reason within experience, through the guidance which such an idea gives towards order and purposiveness; and nowhere is it decisively contrary to any experience.",[]],"krv-a-1036":["This proof deserves always to be mentioned with respect. It is the oldest, the clearest, and the most in conformity with common human reason. It gives life to the study of nature, just as it itself derives its existence from that study, and thereby receives ever new force. It introduces ends and purposes where our observation would not of itself have discovered them, and extends our knowledge of nature by the clue of a special unity, the principle of which lies outside nature. This knowledge, however, reacts upon its cause, namely the idea which occasioned it, and increases the belief in a supreme author to the point of an irresistible conviction.",[]],"krv-a-1037":["It would therefore be not merely disheartening but quite futile to attempt to detract from the authority of this proof. Reason, constantly uplifted by arguments so powerful, and ever growing under its hands, though only empirical, cannot be so depressed by the doubts of subtle, abstract speculation that it would not be torn out of every brooding indecision, as out of a dream, by a single glance at the wonders of nature and the majesty of the world-structure, so as to raise itself from greatness to greatness up to the highest, from the conditioned to the condition, up to the supreme and unconditioned author.",[]],"krv-a-1038":["Although we have nothing to urge against the rationality and utility of this procedure, but rather have to commend and encourage it, we cannot on that account approve the claims which this mode of proof would make to apodeictic certainty, and to an assent requiring no favour or support from elsewhere. Nor can it in any way harm the good cause to tone down the dogmatic language of a disdainful sophist to the tone of moderation and modesty, appropriate to a belief which suffices for our tranquillity, though it does not command unconditioned submission. I accordingly maintain that the physico-theological proof can never by itself establish the existence of a supreme being, but must always leave it to the ontological proof (to which it serves only as introduction) to supply this deficiency. The ontological proof, therefore, still contains the one possible ground of proof (if any speculative proof is possible at all), which no human reason can pass by.",[]],"krv-a-1039":["The chief moments of the physico-theological proof are as follows. (1) Everywhere in the world there are clear signs of an order carried out with great wisdom according to a determinate purpose, in a whole indescribably various in content and equally unlimited in extent. (2) This purposive order is quite alien to the things of the world, and belongs to them only contingently. That is to say, the natures of different things could not of themselves, through so many diverse means conspiring together, harmonise in determinate final purposes, had they not been quite expressly chosen and designed for the purpose by an ordering rational principle, in accordance with ideas underlying it. (3) There exists, therefore, a sublime and wise cause (or several), which must be the cause of the world not merely as a blindly working, all-powerful nature, through fecundity, but as an intelligence, through freedom. (4) The unity of this cause may be inferred from the unity of the reciprocal relation of the parts of the world, as members of an artful structure, with certainty in so far as our observation reaches, and beyond that, in accordance with all principles of analogy, with probability.",["The notes below are keyed to passage ids.","- krv-a-1034: the final *Da*-clause has no main clause in the German. I supplied \"[This question is pressing]\" in brackets to make it a sentence; the construction is irregular, not a doubtful reading.","- krv-a-1035: split the opening *Allerwerts sehen wir…* sentence and the later question sentences. \"so weiset er\" has no clear antecedent for *er*; I took it to be the state (*Zustand*), so \"this state … refers us further\".","- krv-a-1035: *Erspahrung der Principien* = \"parsimony in principles\"; *Caussalität* = \"causality\".","- krv-a-1035: *Kentnissen* rendered \"knowledge\" (singular in English); *Urwesen* = \"original being\".","- krv-a-1036: *Kentnisse* = \"knowledge\"; *Naturkentnisse* = \"knowledge of nature\". *veranlassende Idee* rendered \"the idea which occasioned it\".","- krv-a-1038: *Vernünftler* = \"sophist\" (a choice; it means one who reasons pretentiously). *Beifall* = \"assent\". *apodictic* is rendered \"apodeictic\" as in Kemp Smith.","- krv-a-1039: *Fruchtbarkeit* = \"fecundity\". *Momente* = \"moments\". The enumeration is run into one paragraph with numerals in parentheses.","- krv-a-1032 to 1039: silently corrected OCR/typographic slips (e.g. \"erweiterr\" → \"erweitert\", \"zugieich\" → \"zugleich\")."]],"krv-a-1040":["I do not propose here to quibble with natural reason about its inference. Reason argues from the analogy of certain natural products with what human art produces when it does violence to nature and constrains her to proceed, not according to her own ends, but by accommodating herself to ours (the likeness of such products to houses, ships, clocks); and it concludes that a causality of the same kind, namely understanding and will, must underlie nature, when reason derives the inner possibility of freely acting nature (which first makes possible all art, and perhaps even reason itself) from yet another art, though a superhuman one. This mode of inference the most rigorous transcendental criticism would perhaps not be able to sustain. Yet we must admit that if we are once called upon to name a cause, we cannot here proceed more securely than by analogy with such purposive productions, which are the only ones of which both the causes and the mode of operation are fully known to us. Reason could not justify it to itself, were it to pass from the causality which it knows to obscure and indemonstrable grounds of explanation which it does not know.",["- Restated the single long sentence as several; \"da sie ... schließt\" (reason's inference) is carried by \"Reason argues ... and concludes.\"","- Parenthesis \"(der Aehnlichkeit ... Uhren)\" kept in place, though it is loosely attached in the German.","- \"Critik\" rendered \"criticism\" (lower-case, as a mode of examination, not the work); \"transsc.\" expanded to \"transcendental.\"","- \"Caussalität\" = causality; \"Kunst\" = art; \"zweckmässig\" = purposive.","- \"ihr/bey ihr\" (nature) resolved as \"underlie nature\"; \"sie\" in the last sentence is reason."]],"krv-a-1041":["On this inference, the adaptation to ends and the harmony of so many arrangements of nature could prove only the contingency of the *form*, not of the *matter*, that is, of the substance in the world. For the latter, it would further be required that it could be proved that the things of the world would in themselves be unfitted for such order and agreement according to universal laws, were they not, even as regards their substance, the product of a supreme wisdom. But to prove this, grounds of proof quite other than those from analogy with human art would be required. At most, therefore, the proof could establish an architect of the world, who would always be greatly limited by the aptitude of the material on which he works, but not a creator of the world, to whose idea everything is subject. This is by far insufficient for the great purpose which we have in view, namely, the proof of an all-sufficient primordial being. Were we to try to prove the contingency of matter itself, we should have to take refuge in a transcendental argument; and that is precisely what was here to have been avoided.",["- \"undeckmässigkeit\" in the source is an OCR slip for \"Zweckmässigkeit\"; read \"adaptation to ends.\" \"Wolgereimtheit\" = harmony/consonance.","- Divided the long second sentence into several; \"welches ... hinreichend ist\" given its own sentence.","- \"Weltbaumeister\" = architect of the world; \"Weltschöpfer\" = creator of the world; \"Urwesen\" = primordial being."]],"krv-a-1042":["The inference thus proceeds from the order and purposiveness so universally observed in the world, regarded as an entirely contingent arrangement, to the existence of a cause proportionate to it. But the concept of this cause must make known to us something quite determinate about it, and can therefore be none other than the concept of a being which possesses all might, wisdom, and so forth, in a word, all perfection, as an all-sufficient being. For the predicates of very great, of astounding, of immeasurable might and excellence yield no determinate concept at all, and do not really tell us what the thing is in itself. They are only representations of relation, expressing the magnitude of the object which the observer (of the world) compares with himself and his power of comprehension; and they turn out equally laudatory whether we magnify the object or make the observing subject smaller in relation to it. Where what is at issue is the magnitude (of the perfection) of a thing in general, there is no determinate concept save that which comprehends the whole of possible perfection; and only the all (*omnitudo*) of reality is completely determined in the concept.",["- \"Verhältnißvorstellungen\" = representations of relation. \"Fassungskraft\" = power of comprehension.","- \"einer ... Einrichtung\" is read as qualifying the order and purposiveness; \"beobachtenden\" in the German is read as \"beobachteten\" (observed).","- \"bestimmt\" = determinate; \"durchgängig bestimmt\" = completely determined."]],"krv-a-1043":["Now I would not wish to think that anyone would presume to have insight into the relation of the magnitude of the world which he has observed (in extent as well as in content) to omnipotence, of the order of the world to supreme wisdom, of the unity of the world to the absolute unity of its author, and so forth. Physico-theology, therefore, can give no determinate concept of the supreme cause of the world, and is consequently insufficient as a principle of theology, which in turn is to constitute the foundation of religion.",["- \"Allmacht\" = omnipotence; \"Urheber\" = author.","- \"Physicotheologie\" rendered \"Physico-theology.\""]],"krv-a-1044":["The step to absolute totality is entirely impossible by the empirical path. Yet in the physico-theological proof this step is taken. By what means, then, does one contrive to cross so wide a gulf?",["- \"Welches Mittels bedient man sich also wol\" rendered as a question with \"contrive\" for sense of \"wol.\""]],"krv-a-1045":["Having arrived at admiration of the greatness of the wisdom, power, and so forth of the author of the world, and being unable to advance further, one abruptly abandons this argument, which has been conducted on empirical grounds of proof, and passes to the contingency of the world, which had been inferred at the very start from its order and purposiveness. From this contingency alone, solely by means of transcendental concepts, one proceeds to the existence of an absolutely necessary being; and from the concept of the absolute necessity of the first cause one proceeds to the completely determinate, or determining, concept of it, namely, that of an all-embracing reality. The physico-theological proof, therefore, remained stuck in its undertaking; in this embarrassment it suddenly leaped over to the cosmological proof; and since this is only a concealed ontological proof, it really carried out its purpose solely through pure reason, although at the outset it had denied all kinship with pure reason and had staked everything on evident proofs from experience.",["- \"Welturheber\" = author of the world. \"Schlechthinnothwendigen\" = absolutely necessary [being]; \"being\" supplied.","- \"ausgesezt\" read as \"staked\" (aufs Spiel gesetzt / ausgesetzt, i.e. rested everything on); sense slightly interpretive."]],"krv-a-1046":["The physico-theologians have, therefore, no reason to be so coy towards the transcendental mode of proof, or to look down upon it, with the conceit of clear-sighted students of nature, as upon the cobweb of obscure speculators. For if they would but examine themselves, they would find that, after advancing a good stretch on the ground of nature and experience, and yet seeing themselves as far as ever from the object which confronts their reason, they suddenly abandon this ground and pass over into the realm of mere possibilities, where on the wings of ideas they hope to come near to that which had eluded all their empirical search. When at last, by so mighty a leap, they think they have gained firm footing, they extend the concept, now determinate (and into possession of which they have come without knowing how), over the whole field of creation. And they illustrate the ideal, which was solely a product of pure reason, by experience, though poorly enough and far beneath the dignity of its object; and all the while they are unwilling to admit that they have arrived at this knowledge or presupposition by a path other than that of experience.",["- Divided the last sentence's final clause into a separate sentence beginning \"And they illustrate.\"","- \"Kentniß\" = knowledge; \"Grübler\" = speculators (lit. brooders).","- \"Eigendünkel\" = conceit."]],"krv-a-1047":["Thus the physico-theological proof rests upon the cosmological, and the cosmological upon the ontological proof of the existence of one sole primordial being as the supreme being. And since, besides these three, no other path is open to speculative reason, the ontological proof, from pure concepts of reason alone, is the only possible one, if indeed any proof is possible of a proposition so far exalted above all empirical employment of the understanding.",["- The full stop after \"Beweis\" before \"vom Daseyn\" is a printing irregularity; I read it as a continuation, \"proof of the existence of.\"","- \"einigen\" = sole (one only)."]],"krv-a-1048":["Section VII of Chapter III. Critique of All Theology from Speculative Principles of Reason.",["- Heading: \"Hauptstück\" = chapter; \"Critik\" here is the title of a section, hence \"Critique.\""]],"krv-a-1049":["If by theology I understand knowledge of the original being, it is derived either from pure reason (*theologia rationalis*) or from revelation (*revelata*). The former thinks its object either solely through pure reason, by means of purely transcendental concepts (*ens originarium, realissimum, ens entium*), and is then entitled transcendental theology; or else it thinks it through a concept which it borrows from nature (from the nature of our soul), as the supreme intelligence, and would then have to be entitled natural theology. He who admits only a transcendental theology is called a deist; he who also accepts a natural theology is called a theist. The former grants that we can at best know the existence of an original being through reason alone, but holds that our concept of it is merely transcendental, namely, the concept only of a being that possesses all reality, but whose reality we cannot determine more closely. The latter maintains that reason is capable of determining the object more closely by analogy with nature, namely, as a being which, through understanding and freedom, contains within itself the ultimate ground of all other things. The deist accordingly represents to himself by this being merely a cause of the world (whether through the necessity of its nature or through freedom is left undecided); the theist, an author of the world.",["- The German's parenthesis \"(ens originarium, … ens entium\" is left unclosed; I close it in the English, and the clause \"und heißt…\" is made to follow as a separate clause. Likewise \"(unserer Seele)\" is rendered \"from the nature of our soul\" to make clear that it qualifies *Natur*.","- Final sentence: \"Jener … diesen\" resolved as deist … theist. \"Weltursache\"/\"Welturheber\" rendered \"cause of the world\"/\"author of the world.\"","- \"lanter\" is an OCR/print slip for \"lauter\" (\"purely\")."]],"krv-a-1050":["Transcendental theology is either that which proposes to derive the existence of the original being from an experience in general (without determining anything more closely about the world to which that experience belongs), and is entitled cosmotheology; or else it believes that through mere concepts, without the assistance of the least experience, it knows the existence of that being, and is then called ontotheology.",["- \"dieienige\" = \"diejenige\" (old spelling). \"genant\" rendered \"called\" to vary from \"entitled\" in the parallel clause; the sense is the same.","- Sentence kept whole; the construction is tolerable in English."]],"krv-a-1051":["Natural theology infers the properties and the existence of an author of the world from the constitution, the order and the unity found in this world, in which two kinds of causality, together with their rule, must be assumed, namely, nature and freedom. Hence it ascends from this world to the supreme intelligence, either as the principle of all natural, or as the principle of all moral order and perfection. In the former case it is entitled physicotheology, in the latter moral theology.*",["- Footnote marker retained as \"*\"; the footnote follows as the next passage."]],"krv-a-1052":["* Not theological morality; for this contains moral laws which presuppose the existence of a supreme governor of the world, whereas moral theology is a conviction of the existence of a supreme being which is grounded upon moral laws.",["- \"Moraltheologie\" / \"theologische Moral\" distinguished as \"moral theology\" / \"theological morality.\"","- \"Ueberzeugung\" rendered \"conviction.\""]],"krv-a-1053":["Since by the concept of God we are accustomed to understand not merely a blindly working eternal nature as the root of things, but a supreme being who through understanding and freedom is to be the author of things, and since it is this concept alone that interests us, we could, in strictness, deny to the deist all belief in God, and leave him only the assertion of an original being, or supreme cause. But since no one ought to be accused of wanting to deny a thing outright merely because he does not venture to assert it, it is more lenient and more fair to say that the deist believes in a God, but the theist in a living God (*summam intelligentiam*). We shall now seek out the possible sources of all these attempts of reason.",["- \"Glauben an Gott\" rendered \"belief in God\"; \"lebendigen Gott\" as \"living God.\"","- \"der Theist aber einen lebendigen Gott\": the verb \"glaube\" is understood from the preceding clause; supplied in English."]],"krv-a-1054":["I am content here to define theoretical knowledge as that by which I know what is, and practical knowledge as that by which I represent to myself what ought to be. Accordingly, the theoretical employment of reason is that by which I know *a priori* (as necessary) that something is; the practical, that by which there is known *a priori* what ought to happen. Now if either that something is, or that something ought to happen, is indubitably certain, but yet only conditioned, then either some certain determinate condition may be absolutely necessary for it, or the condition may be presupposed only as optional and contingent. In the former case the condition is postulated (*per thesin*), in the second it is supposed (*per hypothesin*). Now there are practical laws which are absolutely necessary (the moral laws). If these necessarily presuppose some existence as the condition of the possibility of their binding force, this existence must be postulated; and this because the conditioned, from which the inference to this determinate condition proceeds, is itself known *a priori* as absolutely necessary. We shall show hereafter of the moral laws that they not merely presuppose the existence of a supreme being, but also, since in another respect they are absolutely necessary, justly postulate it, though of course only practically. For the present we set this mode of inference aside.",["- Split \"Da es practische Gesetze giebt…\" into two sentences; the \"Da…so muß\" construction recast.","- \"Bestimmte Bedingung\" rendered \"determinate condition\"; \"Schlußart\" as \"mode of inference.\"","- \"Erkentniß\" = \"knowledge\" throughout."]],"krv-a-1055":["When we are speaking merely of what is (not of what ought to be), the conditioned which is given to us in experience is always thought as contingent. The condition belonging to it cannot therefore be known from this as absolutely necessary. It serves only as a relatively necessary, or rather requisite, but in itself and *a priori* arbitrary presupposition for the rational knowledge of the conditioned. If, then, the absolute necessity of a thing is to be known in theoretical knowledge, this could be done only from concepts *a priori*, never from the thing as a cause in relation to an existence given through experience.",["- Split the opening \"Da … so kan\" into two sentences. \"Vernunfterkentniß\" rendered \"rational knowledge.\"","- \"nöthige\" rendered \"requisite\" to mark the contrast with \"nothwendige.\"","- \"als einer Ursache\" is elliptical; I supply \"from the thing\" in brackets-free form as the sense requires: the necessary being known as a cause. Reading somewhat uncertain."]],"krv-a-1056":["A theoretical knowledge is speculative if it relates to an object, or to concepts of an object, which cannot be reached in any experience. It is opposed to knowledge of nature, which relates to no objects or predicates of objects other than those that can be given in a possible experience.",["- \"Naturerkentniß\" rendered \"knowledge of nature.\""]],"krv-a-1057":["The principle that from what happens (the empirically contingent), as effect, we are to infer a cause is a principle of knowledge of nature, but not of speculative knowledge. For if we abstract from it as a principle containing the condition of possible experience in general, and, omitting everything empirical, seek to affirm it of the contingent in general, there remains not the least justification of such a synthetic proposition, to show how I can pass from something that is to something quite different from it (entitled cause). Indeed, in such a merely speculative employment the concept of a cause, like that of the contingent, loses all meaning, all that is meant by which could have its objective reality made comprehensible *in concreto*.",["- Last sentence is tangled (\"alle Bedeutung, deren obiective Realität sich in concreto begreiflich machen lasse\"); restated with \"all that is meant by which\" to preserve the relative clause. Smoothed; doubtful in nuance.","- \"ihm vom Zufälligen überhaupt aussagen\" rendered \"affirm it of the contingent in general.\""]],"krv-a-1058":["If, then, we infer from the existence of things in the world to their cause, this belongs not to the natural but to the speculative employment of reason. For the former relates to a cause not the things themselves (substances), but only what happens, that is, their states, as empirically contingent; that substance itself (matter) is contingent in its existence would have to be a merely speculative rational knowledge. But even if we were speaking only of the form of the world, the manner of its connection and its change, and I were to infer from this to a cause entirely distinct from the world, this again would be a judgment of merely speculative reason, because the object here is in no way an object of possible experience. In that case, however, the principle of causality, which holds only within the field of experiences and outside it is without use, indeed without meaning, would be altogether diverted from its proper destination.",["- \"jener\" rendered \"the former\" (the natural employment); semicolon structure kept.","- \"Bestimmung\" here rendered \"destination\" (\"proper destination\") rather than \"determination,\" as the sense requires.","- \"Wenn … die Rede wäre, ich wolte\" kept as a conditional with \"were to infer.\""]],"krv-a-1059":["I maintain, then, that all attempts at a merely speculative employment of reason in the field of theology are entirely fruitless and, by their very nature, null and void. I maintain also that the principles of its employment in the study of nature [*Naturgebrauch*] do not lead to any theology whatsoever, and that, consequently, unless moral laws are made the ground or the guiding thread, there can be no rational theology at all. For all synthetic principles of the understanding are of immanent employment; the knowledge of a supreme being, however, requires a transcendent employment of them, for which our understanding is in no way equipped. If the empirically valid law of causality is to conduct us to the original being, this being would have to belong to the chain of the objects of experience. In that case, however, it would itself, like all appearances, be conditioned. Even if we allowed the leap beyond the limits of experience by means of the dynamical law of the relation of effects to their causes, what concept can this procedure yield us? Nothing approaching a concept of a supreme being, since experience never presents us with the greatest of all possible effects (which would have to bear witness to its cause). Suppose we are to be permitted, merely in order to leave nothing empty in our reason, to fill up this defect in complete determination by a mere idea of highest perfection and original necessity. This may be granted as a favour, but cannot be demanded as a right on the strength of an irresistible proof. The physico-theological proof may therefore perhaps lend weight to other proofs (if such are to be had), in that it connects speculation with intuition. By itself, however, it does more to prepare the understanding for theological knowledge, and to give it a straight and natural direction thereto, than it is capable of completing the task alone.",["- \"Naturgebrauch\" is glossed in brackets; it means employment in dealing with nature, as opposed to the merely speculative employment.","- The opening sentence is divided into three assertions, and the \"Soll es uns erlaubt seyn\" clause is rendered as a supposition, for clarity.","- \"Beziehung der Wirkungen auf ihre Ursachen\" kept literal; \"Zeugniß ablegen\" = \"bear witness\"."]],"krv-a-1060":["We can well see from this that transcendental questions admit only of transcendental answers, that is, of answers from concepts *a priori* alone, without the least empirical admixture. The question here, however, is obviously synthetic, and demands an extension of our knowledge beyond all limits of experience, namely to the existence of a being that is to correspond to our mere idea, an idea to which no experience can ever be adequate. Now, in accordance with our proofs above, all synthetic knowledge *a priori* is possible only in so far as it expresses the formal conditions of a possible experience. All principles are therefore of merely immanent validity; that is to say, they relate solely to objects of empirical knowledge, or appearances. Accordingly, nothing is accomplished by the transcendental procedure, in the interest of the theology of a merely speculative reason.",["- Second sentence's relative clause (\"der niemals...\") is made to refer to the idea, not the being; the German's \"der\" could be taken either way, but the argument favours \"Idee\".","- The long third sentence is divided at the semicolon-like \"und alle Grundsätze\"."]],"krv-a-1061":["But suppose people prefer to call in question all the above proofs of the Analytic rather than let themselves be robbed of their persuasion of the weight of the arguments so long in use. They still cannot refuse to meet my challenge, when I demand that they justify themselves at least on this point: how, and by virtue of what illumination, they venture to soar above all possible experience by the power of mere ideas. I should beg to be spared new proofs, or improved versions of the old ones. There is indeed not much to choose from here, since all merely speculative proofs finally come down to one only, the ontological; and I need not fear being greatly troubled by the fertility of the dogmatic champions of that reason which is emancipated from the senses. Moreover, though I do not consider myself very combative, I do not wish to decline the challenge of detecting the fallacy in every attempt of this kind, and so of frustrating its pretension. Yet the hope of better fortune is never wholly given up by those who have once become accustomed to dogmatic persuasions. I therefore confine myself to the one reasonable demand, that they justify themselves in general terms, from the nature of the human understanding together with all the other sources of knowledge, as to how they propose to set about extending their knowledge entirely *a priori*, and carrying it to the point where no possible experience, and consequently no means whatever, suffices to assure objective reality to any concept devised by ourselves. However the understanding may have arrived at this concept, the existence of its object cannot be found in it analytically; for the knowledge of the existence of the object consists precisely in this, that the object is posited in itself outside the thought. But it is quite impossible to go out of a concept of itself, and, without following the empirical connection (by which, however, only appearances are ever given), to arrive at the discovery of new objects and transcendent beings.",["- One very long concessive sentence (\"Denn, ob man zwar...\") is broken into four sentences; the \"obgleich ich überdem...\" clause becomes \"Moreover...\", and \"so wird daher doch\" becomes \"Yet\".","- \"sinnenfreien Vernunft\": \"reason emancipated from the senses\"; \"Ueberredung\" = \"persuasion\" (as against *Ueberzeugung*).","- \"Foderung\" in the second half is rendered \"demand\"; \"Auffoderung\"/\"Ausfoderung\" as \"challenge\"."]],"krv-a-1062":["But although reason in its merely speculative employment is far from adequate to so great an undertaking as that of attaining to the existence of a supreme being, it is of very great use in correcting the knowledge of such a being, should it be obtainable from some other source, in bringing it into agreement with itself and with every intelligible purpose, and in purging it of everything that may be incompatible with the concept of an original being and of all admixture of empirical limitations.",["- \"desselben\" refers to the supreme being, supplied as \"such a being\"; \"im Fall sie anders woher geschöpft werden könte\" is rendered \"should it be obtainable from some other source\", the pronoun \"sie\" referring to *Erkentniß*."]],"krv-a-1063":["Transcendental theology therefore, notwithstanding all its insufficiency, retains an important negative employment, and is a permanent censor of our reason when reason is concerned solely with pure ideas, which, for that very reason, admit of no standard other than the transcendental. For if, in some other, perhaps practical, relation, the presupposition of a supreme and all-sufficient being, as highest intelligence, were to assert its validity beyond all contradiction, it would be of the greatest importance to determine this concept exactly on its transcendental side, as the concept of a necessary and most real being. It would be equally important to remove from it whatever is contrary to the highest reality and belongs to mere appearance (anthropomorphism in the wider sense), and at the same time to clear out of the way all opposing assertions, be they atheistic, deistic, or anthropomorphic. This is very easy in a critical treatment of this kind, since the very grounds through which the incapacity of human reason to assert the existence of such a being is brought before our eyes must necessarily also suffice to prove the untenability of every counter-assertion. For where is anyone to get the insight, through pure speculation of reason, that there is no supreme being as primal ground of all things? Or that none of the properties which, from their consequences, we represent to ourselves as analogous to the dynamical realities of a thinking being belongs to it? Or that, in the latter case, they would have to be subject to all the limitations which sensibility inevitably imposes upon the intelligences known to us through experience?",["- The final sentence, which in the German ends with a full stop, is rendered as three questions, since the construction is clearly rhetorical (\"wo will iemand ... hernehmen\").","- The long second sentence is divided in two (\"It would be equally important...\"); \"zur blossen Erscheinung gehört\" kept as \"belongs to mere appearance\" (*Erscheinung*).","- \"Antropomorphism im weiteren Verstande\": \"in the wider sense\", *Verstande* here being the sense of the word, not the faculty."]],"krv-a-1064":["The supreme being remains, therefore, for the merely speculative employment of reason, a mere ideal, yet one free from defect: a concept which completes and crowns the whole of human knowledge, and whose objective reality, though it cannot by this path be proved, also cannot be refuted. If there should be a moral theology capable of supplying this deficiency, transcendental theology, which was previously only problematic, then proves its indispensableness by determining its concept and by ceaselessly censoring a reason which is often enough deceived by sensibility, and not always in agreement with its own ideas. Necessity, infinity, unity, existence outside the world (not as world-soul), eternity free from conditions of time, omnipresence free from conditions of space, omnipotence, and so forth, are purely transcendental predicates; and the purified concept of them, which every theology finds so indispensable, can therefore be obtained only from transcendental theology.",["- \"vor den blos speculativen Gebrauch\" read as \"für\" (for); \"Mangel\" rendered \"deficiency\".","- The second sentence is left as a single sentence, with \"so\" as the apodosis; \"ihre Unentbehrlichkeit\" = \"its indispensableness\".","- \"Ideal\" is the Kantian technical term for the idea in individuo."]],"krv-a-1065":["Appendix to the Transcendental Dialectic. The Regulative Employment of the Ideas of Pure Reason.",["- Title only; \"Anhang\" = \"Appendix\", \"Gebrauch\" = \"employment\" for consistency with the preceding passages."]],"krv-a-1066":["The outcome of all the dialectical attempts of pure reason not only confirms what we have already proved in the Transcendental Analytic, namely, that all inferences which would carry us beyond the field of possible experience are deceptive and groundless; it also teaches us this further point, that human reason has in this connection a natural propensity to overstep this boundary, and that transcendental ideas are as natural to it as the categories are to the understanding. There is this difference, however, that while the categories lead to truth, that is, to the agreement of our concepts with the object, the ideas produce a mere illusion, though one which is irresistible, and the deceptive effect of which can hardly be prevented even by the most severe criticism.",["- Restated as three sentences; the German is a single long sentence with the \"daß\" clauses depending on \"lehrt uns zugleich dieses besondere\".","- \"so wie die leztere … die erstere\": \"leztere\" refers to the categories, \"erstere\" to the ideas (the order is reversed by the German); resolved by the argument and supplied as \"categories\" and \"ideas\".","- Schein = illusion; Täuschung = deceptive effect; Critik = criticism (OCR \"schärf;ste\" silently corrected to \"schärfste\")."]],"krv-a-1067":["Whatever is grounded in the nature of our powers must be purposive, and consistent with their correct employment, provided only that we can guard against a certain misunderstanding and can discover the proper direction of those powers. We may presume, therefore, that the transcendental ideas have their good, and consequently immanent, employment. If, however, their meaning is misunderstood, and they are taken for concepts of real things, they may be transcendent in their application, and for that very reason deceptive. For it is not the idea in itself, but only its employment, that can be either transcendent (overflying) or immanent, in respect of the whole of possible experience. Which it is depends on whether the idea is directed straight upon an object supposed to correspond to it, or only upon the employment of the understanding in general in dealing with the objects with which it is concerned. All faults of subreption are always to be ascribed to a defect of judgment, never to understanding or to reason.",["- Divided into several sentences; \"nachdem …\" clause rendered as a separate sentence explaining the alternative.","- \"Kräfte\" = powers; \"Gebrauch\" = employment; \"zweckmässig\" = purposive. \"Urtheilskraft\" = judgment.","- The final clause (\"und alle Fehler…\") is joined by \"und\" in the German; I made it a separate sentence.","- \"verkant\" read as \"verkannt\"."]],"krv-a-1068":["Reason never relates directly to an object, but solely to the understanding, and by means of the understanding to its own empirical employment. It does not, therefore, create concepts (of objects), but only orders them, and gives them that unity which they can have in their greatest possible extension, that is, in relation to the totality of the series. The understanding pays no heed to this totality; it attends only to that connection through which series of conditions everywhere come into being in accordance with concepts. Reason has thus, properly speaking, only the understanding and its purposive employment as its object. And as the understanding unites the manifold in the object by means of concepts, so reason, on its side, unites the manifold of concepts by means of ideas, by positing a certain collective unity as the goal of the acts of the understanding, which otherwise are occupied only with distributive unity.",["- \"als auf welche der Verstand gar nicht sieht\" is made into a separate sentence; \"welche\" refers to the totality of the series.","- \"schaft\" read as \"schafft\"; \"Obiect/Gegenstand\" both rendered \"object\"; \"Anstellung\" = employment.","- \"collective\"/\"distributive\" kept in the German's Latin-derived form."]],"krv-a-1069":["I maintain, accordingly, that the transcendental ideas are never of constitutive employment, such that concepts of certain objects would be given through them; and that when they are so understood they are merely sophistical (dialectical) concepts. On the other hand, they have an admirable and indispensably necessary regulative employment, namely, that of directing the understanding to a certain goal, in view of which the lines of direction of all its rules converge to one point. This point is indeed a mere idea (*focus imaginarius*), that is, a point from which the concepts of the understanding do not actually proceed, since it lies quite outside the bounds of possible experience; none the less it serves to give to these concepts the greatest unity combined with the greatest extension. Now there arises for us from this the illusion that these lines of direction proceed from an object lying outside the field of empirically possible knowledge, just as objects are seen behind the surface of a mirror. This illusion (which we can nevertheless prevent from deceiving us) is none the less indispensably necessary if, besides the objects which lie before our eyes, we are also to see those which lie far away behind our back; that is, in our case, if we are to train the understanding to go beyond every given experience (the part of the entire possible experience), and so to its greatest possible and utmost extension.",["- Restated in several sentences. \"ausgeschlossen\" (literally \"shut out\") is doubtful, as it is in the German editions (commonly emended to \"ausgeschossen\", \"shot forth\"); I translate as \"proceed\", following the sense required.","- \"in dem Falle, daß man sie so versteht\" smoothed into \"when they are so understood\"; \"seyn\" read as \"sind\".","- *focus imaginarius* left in Latin, italicised."]],"krv-a-1070":["If we survey the modes of knowledge belonging to our understanding in their whole extent, we find that what reason prescribes quite peculiarly in regard to them, and seeks to bring about, is the systematic in knowledge, that is, its interconnection from one principle. This unity of reason always presupposes an idea, namely, that of the form of a whole of knowledge, which precedes the determinate knowledge of the parts and contains the conditions for determining a priori the place of each part and its relation to the others. This idea accordingly postulates complete unity in the knowledge of the understanding, whereby this knowledge becomes not a merely contingent aggregate but a system connected according to necessary laws. Strictly speaking, we cannot say that this idea is a concept of an object; it is a concept of the thoroughgoing unity of these concepts, in so far as this unity serves the understanding as a rule. Such concepts of reason are not drawn from nature. On the contrary, we interrogate nature in accordance with these ideas, and consider our knowledge defective so long as it is not adequate to them. It is admitted that pure earth, pure water, pure air, and so forth, are hardly to be found. Yet we require the concepts of them (which, so far as complete purity is concerned, have their origin only in reason) in order to determine duly the share which each of these natural causes has in the appearance. Thus all materials are reduced to earths (as mere weight, so to speak), to salts and inflammable substances (as force), and finally to water and air as vehicles (machines, as it were, by means of which the former operate), in order to explain, in accordance with the idea of a mechanism, the chemical effects of materials upon one another. For although men do not actually express themselves in this manner, the influence of reason upon the classifications of students of nature is very easily discovered.",["- Long passage divided into shorter sentences; \"welches\" (Ganzen) rendered \"which precedes\".","- \"Erkentniß\" = knowledge; \"Verstandeserkentnisse\" = modes of knowledge belonging to the understanding; \"Fom\" read \"Form\".","- \"die also … Ursprung haben\" parenthesis kept; \"Eintheilungen\" = classifications; \"Naturforscher\" = students of nature.","- \"vermittelst deren die vorige wirken\": \"die vorige\" supplied as \"the former\" (earths, salts, inflammable substances)."]],"krv-a-1071":["If reason is a faculty of deriving the particular from the universal, then either the universal is already certain in itself and given, and in that case only judgment is required for the subsumption, and the particular is thereby necessarily determined. This I shall entitle the apodeictic employment of reason. Or the universal is assumed only problematically, and is a mere idea; the particular is certain, but the universality of the rule from which it follows is still a problem. In that case several particular instances, all of which are certain, are tested by the rule to see whether they follow from it. If it appears that all the particular instances which can be cited do follow from it, we conclude to the universality of the rule, and from this subsequently to all cases, even those which are not themselves given. This I shall entitle the hypothetical employment of reason.",["- The German first alternative is anacoluthic (\"so ist entweder … und das Besondere … nothwendig bestimt. … Oder …, so werden…\"); I have smoothed it into parallel alternatives.","- \"heißen/nennen\" rendered \"entitle\"; \"apodictischen\" given as \"apodeictic\".","- Vermögen = faculty; Urtheilskraft = judgment."]],"krv-a-1072":["The hypothetical employment of reason, based on ideas assumed as problematic concepts, is not, strictly speaking, constitutive. That is to say, it is not such that if we judge in all strictness the truth of the universal rule, assumed as hypothesis, follows from it; for how are we to know all the possible consequences which, by following from the same assumed principle, would prove its universality? Its employment is only regulative, its purpose being to bring unity, so far as possible, into particular modes of knowledge, and thereby to bring the rule nearer to universality.",["- The parenthetical question (\"denn, wie will man…\") interrupts the sentence; I retain it as a question within the sentence, and the \"sondern\" clause is made a separate sentence.","- \"Erkentnisse\" = modes of knowledge; \"Allgemeinheit\" = universality."]],"krv-a-1073":["The hypothetical employment of reason is therefore directed to the systematic unity of the modes of knowledge of the understanding, and this unity is the criterion [*Probierstein*] of the truth of rules. Conversely, systematic unity (as a mere idea) is solely a projected unity, which must be regarded not as given in itself but only as a problem. It serves, however, to discover a principle [*principium*] for the manifold and particular employment of the understanding, and so to guide the understanding also in regard to cases which are not given, and to make it coherent.",["- \"Probierstein\" = touchstone, rendered \"criterion\" with the German supplied in brackets; \"touchstone\" is also defensible.","- \"diesen\" (the understanding's employment) resolved as \"the understanding\" in the final clause; \"welche\" refers to the systematic unity.","- *Principium* is Kant's Latin and is kept."]],"krv-a-1074":["All that we can see from this, however, is that the systematic unity, or unity of reason, of the manifold of modes of knowledge of the understanding is a logical principle. Its function is, where the understanding by itself does not suffice to yield rules, to help it forward by means of ideas, and at the same time to secure for the diversity of its rules agreement under one principle (that is, systematic agreement), and thereby coherence, so far as this can be done. But whether the constitution of the objects, or the nature of the understanding which knows them as such, is in itself determined to systematic unity; and whether we can, to a certain extent, postulate this unity *a priori*, even without regard to such an interest of reason, and so can say that all possible modes of knowledge of the understanding (empirical knowledge among them) have unity of reason and stand under common principles from which, in spite of their diversity, they can be derived: that would be a transcendental principle of reason. Such a principle would make systematic unity not merely subjectively and logically necessary, as method, but objectively necessary.",[]],"krv-a-1075":["Let us illustrate this by an instance of the use of reason. Among the various kinds of unity according to concepts of the understanding is the causality of a substance, which is entitled force [*Kraft*]. The different appearances of one and the same substance show at first sight so much diversity that we must at the outset assume in it almost as many forces as there are effects which manifest themselves. In the human mind, for instance, we have sensation, consciousness, imagination, memory, wit, the power of discrimination, pleasure, desire, and so forth. At the outset a logical maxim prescribes that we reduce this apparent diversity as far as possible, by discovering through comparison the hidden identity, and by considering whether imagination combined with consciousness may not be memory, wit, and the power of discrimination, and perhaps even understanding and reason. The idea of a fundamental force, as to whether there is any such thing logic does not at all determine, is at least the problem set by a systematic representation of the manifoldness of forces. The logical principle of reason requires us to bring about this unity as far as possible. The more the appearances of one force and another are found to be identical with one another, the more probable it becomes that they are nothing but different expressions of one and the same force, which may be entitled (comparatively speaking) their fundamental force. The remaining forces are treated in the same way.",[]],"krv-a-1076":["The comparatively fundamental forces must in turn be compared with one another, in order that by the discovery of their agreement we may bring them near to a single radical, that is, absolute, fundamental force. This unity of reason, however, is merely hypothetical. We do not assert that such a force must in fact be met with. What we assert is that, in the interest of reason, that is, for the establishment of certain principles for the many rules which experience may supply, we must seek it, and that wherever possible we must in this way introduce systematic unity into knowledge.",[]],"krv-a-1077":["But if we attend to the transcendental employment of the understanding, it becomes evident that this idea of a fundamental force in general is not intended merely as a problem for hypothetical use, but pretends to objective reality. Through it the systematic unity of the manifold forces of a substance is postulated, and an apodeictic principle of reason is established. For even without having once attempted to find agreement among the manifold forces, indeed even if all our attempts to discover it have failed, we yet presuppose that such agreement will be found. And this we do, not only (as in the instance cited) on account of the unity of substance, but also where, as in matter in general, many forces are met with which, though to a certain degree homogeneous, are yet very numerous. In such cases reason presupposes systematic unity of manifold forces, since particular laws of nature stand under more general laws, and economy in principles becomes not merely an economical maxim of reason but an inner law of nature.",[]],"krv-a-1078":["In fact, it is not even conceivable how a logical principle of the unity of rules could obtain, unless a transcendental principle were presupposed, through which such systematic unity, as inhering in the objects themselves, is assumed *a priori* to be necessary. For with what right can reason, in its logical employment, demand that the manifoldness of forces which nature gives us to know be treated as a merely concealed unity, and be derived, so far as possible, from some fundamental force, if reason were free to admit that it is just as possible that all forces are heterogeneous, and that the systematic unity of their derivation is not in conformity with nature? In that case reason would be proceeding directly contrary to its vocation, since it would set before itself as its goal an idea which quite contradicted the constitution of nature. Nor can it be said that reason has previously derived this unity from the contingent constitution of nature, in accordance with its own principles. For the law of reason that we seek this unity is necessary. Without it we should have no reason at all; without reason, no coherent employment of the understanding; and, in the absence of this, no sufficient mark of empirical truth. In respect of this last, therefore, we have to presuppose the systematic unity of nature as objectively valid and necessary.",[]],"krv-a-1079":["We find this transcendental presupposition also, in a manner that calls for admiration, concealed in the principles of the philosophers, although they have not always recognised it or admitted it to themselves. That all the manifoldness of individual things does not exclude identity of species; that the various species must be treated only as diverse determinations of a few genera, and these in turn of still higher kinds, and so on; that, therefore, a certain systematic unity of all possible empirical concepts, so far as they can be derived from higher and more general concepts, must be sought: this is a scholastic rule, or logical principle, without which there would be no employment of reason. For we can infer from the universal to the particular only in so far as universal properties of things are taken as the ground, under which the particular stands.",[]],"krv-a-1080":["That such agreement is also to be met with in nature is presupposed by philosophers in the familiar scholastic rule that beginnings (principles) must not be multiplied without necessity (*entia praeter necessitatem non esse multiplicanda*). What is thereby said is that the nature of things itself offers material for unity of reason, and that the seemingly infinite diversity ought not to deter us from surmising behind it a unity of fundamental properties, from which the manifoldness can be derived only through further determination. This unity, although a mere idea, has in all ages been pursued so zealously that there has been more occasion to moderate the desire for it than to encourage it. It was already much that the chemists could reduce all salts to two chief kinds, acid and alkaline; they even try to regard this difference as merely a variety, or different expression, of one and the same fundamental stuff. The many kinds of earths (the material of stones and even of metals) have been gradually reduced to three, and finally to two; but, not yet content with this, chemists cannot rid themselves of the thought that behind these varieties there is after all a single genus, indeed perhaps even a principle common to these and to the salts. It might perhaps be supposed that this is merely an economical device of reason, to spare itself trouble as far as possible, and a hypothetical attempt which, if it succeeds, lends probability to the presupposed ground of explanation just by this unity. But such a self-seeking purpose is very easily distinguished from the idea in accordance with which everyone presupposes that this unity of reason is in conformity with nature itself, and that reason here does not beg but commands, though without being able to determine the limits of this unity.",["- 1074: \"Verstandeserkentniß\" rendered \"mode of knowledge of the understanding\"; the long sentence beginning \"Ob aber…\" is divided, and the final clause restated as a separate sentence.","- 1074: \"(systematische)\" is rendered \"(that is, systematic agreement)\", the sense supplied by judgment.","- 1075: \"Kraft\" and \"Unterscheidungskraft\" are rendered \"force\" and \"power of discrimination\"; \"Witz\" is \"wit\".","- 1075: The clause \"von welcher aber die Logik gar nicht ausmittelt…\" is rendered as a parenthetical-like clause with \"as to whether\"; some smoothing.","- 1076: \"raticalen\" is an obvious misprint for \"radicalen\" and is silently corrected; \"radical\" is used.","- 1077: \"Ersparung der Principien\" is \"economy in principles\"; the closing sentence is split to clarify the construction.","- 1078: The final sentence is restated, with \"wir … müssen\" resolved as \"we have to presuppose\"; \"in Ansehung des lezteren\" is taken to refer to empirical truth.","- 1080: \"bles\" is corrected to \"bloß/blos\" (merely); \"Scheidekünstler\" is rendered \"chemists\", and \"Anfänge (Principien)\" is \"beginnings (principles)\"."]],"krv-a-1081":["Suppose that among the appearances which present themselves to us there were so great a diversity (I do not say in form, for in that respect they may resemble one another, but in content, that is, in the manifoldness of existing beings) that even the most acute human understanding, by comparing one with another, could not detect the least similarity. This is a case which may very well be thought. If it were actual, the logical law of genera would have no place whatever; there would be no concept of a genus, nor any universal concept; indeed there would be no understanding at all, since the understanding has to deal solely with such concepts. The logical principle of genera therefore presupposes a transcendental principle, if it is to be applied to nature (by which I here understand only objects that are given to us). In accordance with this transcendental principle, homogeneity is necessarily presupposed in the manifold of possible experience, although we cannot determine its degree *a priori*. It is presupposed because without it no empirical concepts, and consequently no experience, would be possible.",["- Restated the opening conditional as two sentences; the parenthesis \"ein Fall, der sich wol denken läßt\" is made a separate sentence, and \"so würde …\" is given its own sentence.","- \"Gleichartigkeit\" = homogeneity (Kant's own Latin term below is *Homogenität*); \"ob wir gleich … nicht bestimmen können\" is kept as a concessive clause.","- \"Aehnlichkeit\" = similarity; \"ausfündig machen\" = detect."]],"krv-a-1082":["The logical principle of genera, which postulates identity, is opposed by another, namely that of species. This second principle requires manifoldness and diversity in things, notwithstanding their agreement under the same genus, and prescribes to the understanding that it attend to the latter no less than to the former. This principle, which is that of acuteness or of the faculty of distinguishing, greatly restricts the levity of the first, which is that of wit. Reason here shows two conflicting interests: on the one hand the interest of extension (universality) in respect of genera, on the other the interest of content (determinateness) in respect of the manifoldness of species. For in the first case the understanding thinks much *under* its concepts, in the second all the more *in* them. This also shows itself in the very different modes of thought of students of nature. Some of them, who are predominantly speculative, are, as it were, hostile to heterogeneity and always have their eyes upon the unity of the genus. Others, predominantly empirical minds, seek unceasingly to split nature into so much manifoldness that we should almost have to give up hope of judging its appearances according to universal principles.",["- Divided the long second sentence; \"auf diese … auf iene\" resolved as \"the latter … the former\" (diese = diversity, iene = identity/genus), as the argument requires.","- The italics on *under* and *in* are supplied for the contrast \"unter … in\"; the German passage gives no emphasis, so remove them if strict fidelity to the source is wanted.","- \"Leichtsinn\" = levity; \"Witz\" = wit; \"Denkungsart\" = mode of thought; \"Köpfe\" = minds."]],"krv-a-1083":["This latter mode of thought is evidently also grounded upon a logical principle, which aims at the systematic completeness of all knowledge. I follow it when, starting from the genus, I descend to the manifold that may be contained under it, and so seek to give extension to the system, just as in the former case, in which I ascend to the genus, I seek to give it simplicity. For from the sphere of the concept which designates a genus, as little as from the space which matter can occupy, can we see how far its division can extend. Hence every genus requires different species, and these again different subspecies. And since none of the latter is found which does not always again have a sphere (extension, as *conceptus communis*), reason in its whole extension demands that no species be regarded as in itself the lowest. For a species is always a concept which contains only what is common to different things; this concept cannot be completely determined, and consequently cannot be referred immediately to an individual, and must therefore always contain other concepts, that is, subspecies, under it. This law of specification might be expressed thus: *entium varietates non temere esse minuendas*.",["- Restated the long sentence beginning \"Daher iede Gattung\" as three sentences; the \"weil, da sie doch…\" clause is made an independent explanatory sentence (\"For a species is always…\").","- \"wenn ich … herabsteige\" is rendered \"I follow it when…\", a slight smoothing of the German construction.","- \"Erkentnisse\" (plural) = \"knowledge\" here, as a collective; \"Einfalt\" = simplicity; \"Umfang\" = extension.","- The Latin is left as in the text, in italics."]],"krv-a-1084":["But it is easily seen that even this logical law would be without sense and application, were there not a transcendental law of specification underlying it. This law does not, indeed, demand of the things that can become our objects an actual infinity in respect of their differences; for the logical principle, which asserts merely the indeterminateness of the logical sphere in respect of possible division, gives no occasion for that. But it does impose upon the understanding the demand that under every species which comes before us it seek subspecies, and for every difference smaller differences. For if there were no lower concepts, there would be no higher. Now the understanding knows everything only through concepts; consequently, however far it carries its division, never through mere intuition, but always again through lower concepts. The knowledge of appearances in their thoroughgoing determination (which is possible only through understanding) demands a specification of its concepts that is to be continued unceasingly, and a progress to differences which still remain, and from which abstraction has been made in the concept of the species and still more in that of the genus.",["- Divided the long first sentence into three; \"welches zwar freilich nicht… fodert\" is kept as a concession, \"dennoch… auferlegt\" rendered \"But it does impose\".","- \"durchgängige Bestimmung\" = thoroughgoing determination; \"Erkentniß\" = knowledge.","- \"so weit er in der Eintheilung reicht\" rendered \"however far it carries its division\"."]],"krv-a-1085":["Nor can this law of specification be borrowed from experience, for experience cannot yield disclosures that reach so far. Empirical specification soon comes to a stop in distinguishing the manifold, unless it is guided by the antecedent transcendental law of specification, as a principle of reason, to seek such distinctions and still to suspect them even when they do not reveal themselves to the senses. That absorbent earths are of different kinds (calcareous and muriatic earths) needed, for its discovery, an anticipating rule of reason, which set the understanding the task of seeking the difference, presupposing nature to be so rich as to make it possible to suspect it. For we have understanding only under the presupposition of differences in nature, just as we have it only under the condition that its objects have homogeneity in themselves; since the very manifoldness of what can be comprehended under a concept constitutes the use of this concept and the occupation of the understanding.",["- \"transscendentate\" in the OCR corrected to \"transscendentale\".","- \"Eröfnungen\" = disclosures; \"Verschiedenheit\" = difference; \"Gleichartigkeit\" = homogeneity.","- The sentence on the absorbent earths is smoothed: \"indem sie die Natur so reichhaltig voraussetzte, sie zu vermuthen\" is rendered freely; the construction is irregular in the German.","- \"muriatische Erden\" kept as \"muriatic earths\" (the eighteenth-century chemical term)."]],"krv-a-1086":["Reason thus prepares the field for the understanding: (1) by a principle of the homogeneity of the manifold under higher genera; (2) by a principle of the variety of the homogeneous under lower species. To complete the systematic unity it adds (3) a law of the affinity of all concepts, which prescribes a continuous transition from every species to every other through a graduated increase of difference. We may entitle these the principles of the homogeneity, of the specification, and of the continuity of forms. The last arises through the union of the first two, once we have completed the systematic connection in idea, both in ascending to higher genera and in descending to lower species. For then all manifolds are akin to one another, since they one and all descend, through all degrees of extended determination, from a single supreme genus.",["- Restated the first sentence as two; the numerals are kept as in the German.","- \"Affinität\" = affinity; \"Bestimmung\" = determination; \"in der Idee\" = in idea.","- \"Das leztere entspringt dadurch: daß…\" rendered \"arises through the union of the first two, once…\"."]],"krv-a-1087":["The systematic unity under the three logical principles can be made sensible to us in the following way. We may regard every concept as a point which, as the standpoint of a spectator, has its horizon, that is, a multitude of things which can be represented, and as it were surveyed, from it. Within this horizon a multitude of points must be capable of being specified without end, each of which again has its narrower field of view; that is, every species contains subspecies, in accordance with the principle of specification, and the logical horizon consists only of smaller horizons (subspecies), not of points which have no extension (individuals). But for different horizons, that is, genera, determined by as many concepts, we can think a common horizon drawn, from which as from a middle point we survey them all. This is the higher genus; and so we proceed until at last the highest genus is the universal and true horizon, determined from the standpoint of the highest concept, and comprehending under itself all manifoldness, as genera, species and subspecies.",["- Divided the final sentence; \"welcher die höhere Gattung ist, bis endlich…\" is smoothed to \"This is the higher genus; and so we proceed until…\", the German being loose.","- \"sinnlich machen\" = \"make sensible\"; \"Gesichtskreis\" = field of view; \"Standpunct\" = standpoint.","- \"Mittelpuncte\" rendered \"middle point\"."]],"krv-a-1088":["The law of homogeneity leads me to this highest standpoint, and the law of specification to all lower standpoints and to their greatest variety. Now since in this way nothing in the whole extent of all possible concepts is empty, and nothing can be met with outside it, there arises, from the presupposition of this universal horizon and of its thoroughgoing division, the principle *non datur vacuum formarum*. That is to say, there are not different original and primary genera which are, as it were, isolated and separated from one another (by an empty interval); on the contrary, all the manifold genera are only divisions of one single supreme and universal genus. From this principle there follows as its immediate consequence *datur continuum formarum*. That is to say, all differences of species border upon one another, and do not allow of transition from one to another by a leap, but only through all the smaller degrees of difference by which we can pass from the one to the other. In a word, there are no species or sub-species which (in the concept of reason) are the nearest to one another; intermediate species are always still possible, the difference of which from the first and the second is smaller than the difference of these from each other.",[]],"krv-a-1089":["The first law, then, guards against excessive diversity of different original genera, and recommends homogeneity; the second, on the other hand, in turn restricts this tendency to unanimity, and requires us to distinguish the sub-species before we proceed with our universal concept to the individuals. The third unites the other two, in that it prescribes, in the midst of the highest manifoldness, homogeneity none the less, through the gradual transition from one species to another. This indicates a kind of kinship of the different branches, inasmuch as they have all sprung from one stem.",[]],"krv-a-1090":["This logical law of the *continuum specierum* (*formarum logicarum*) presupposes, however, a transcendental law (*lex continui in natura*). Without this the employment of the understanding would be only misled by that prescription, since it might perhaps take a path directly opposed to nature. This law must therefore rest on pure transcendental, not on empirical grounds. For in the latter case it would come later than the systems, whereas it has in fact been what first gave rise to the systematic [character] in our knowledge of nature. Nor do these laws conceal behind them any intention of putting them, as mere experiments, to a test. It is true that when this connection holds good it affords a powerful ground for regarding the unity which has been hypothetically thought out as well founded, and that in this respect also the laws have their utility. But what we see plainly in them is that they judge the parsimony of fundamental causes, the manifoldness of effects, and the consequent kinship of the members of nature to be in themselves rational and in accordance with nature; and these principles therefore carry their recommendation directly in themselves, and not merely as devices of method.",[]],"krv-a-1091":["But it is easily seen that this continuity of forms is a mere idea, for which no congruent object can be exhibited in experience. This is so, first, because the species in nature are actually divided, and must therefore in themselves constitute a *quantum discretum*; if the gradual progress in their kinship were continuous, they would have to contain a true infinity of intermediate members lying between any two given species, which is impossible. It is so, secondly, because we can make no determinate empirical use of this law. It indicates not the least mark of affinity by which, or how far, we are to seek the graded sequence of their differences; it gives us nothing more than a general indication that we are to seek it.",[]],"krv-a-1092":["If we transpose the principles just cited, so as to arrange them in the order suited to their employment in experience, the principles of systematic unity would stand somewhat as follows: manifoldness, affinity, and unity, each of them taken as an idea in the highest degree of its completeness. Reason presupposes the modes of knowledge of the understanding, which are first applied to experience, and seeks their unity in accordance with ideas that go much further than experience can reach. The affinity of the manifold, without prejudice to its diversity, under a principle of unity, concerns not only things but, far more, the mere properties and forces of things. Thus if, for example, the course of the planets is given us by experience (not yet fully corrected) as circular, and we find deviations, we suspect them to lie in whatever can alter the circle, in accordance with a constant law, through all the infinite intermediate degrees, into one of these divergent orbits. That is, the motions of the planets which are not circles will more or less approach the properties of the circle, and fall upon the ellipse. Comets show a still greater diversity of orbits, since (so far as observation reaches) they do not even return in a circle; but we conjecture a parabolic course, which is yet akin to the ellipse, and, if the major axis of the latter is very greatly extended, cannot in any of our observations be distinguished from it. Thus, under the guidance of those principles, we arrive at unity of genus in the shape of these orbits, and thereby further at unity of the cause of all the laws of their motion (gravitation). From this we afterwards extend our conquests, and seek to explain all varieties and apparent deviations from those rules by the same principle. Finally we even add more than experience can ever confirm: namely, in accordance with the rules of affinity, we think for ourselves even hyperbolic orbits of comets, in which these bodies leave our solar world altogether, and, passing from sun to sun, unite in their course the more remote parts of a world-system which, though for us unbounded, is held together by one and the same moving force.",[]],"krv-a-1093":["What is remarkable in these principles, and is all that here concerns us, is this: they seem to be transcendental; and although they contain mere ideas for the guidance of the empirical employment of reason, ideas which the latter can follow only, as it were, asymptotically, that is, merely by approximation, without ever attaining them, they yet have, as synthetic propositions *a priori*, objective but indeterminate validity, and serve as rule of possible experience. They are also actually employed with good fortune, as heuristic principles, in the elaboration of experience; and yet no transcendental deduction of them can be brought about, which, as was proved above, is always impossible in regard to ideas.",[]],"krv-a-1094":["In the Transcendental Analytic we distinguished, among the principles of the understanding, the dynamical, as merely regulative principles of intuition, from the mathematical, which in respect of intuition are constitutive. Notwithstanding this, the dynamical laws in question are indeed constitutive in respect of experience, since they make possible *a priori* the concepts without which no experience takes place. Principles of pure reason, on the other hand, cannot be constitutive even in respect of empirical concepts, because no corresponding schema of sensibility can be given to them, and they therefore can have no object *in concreto*. Now if I abandon such an empirical employment of them as constitutive principles, how am I nevertheless to secure for them a regulative employment, and with it some objective validity? And what meaning can this employment have?",["- krv-a-1088: restated the long closing sentence as several; \"d. i.\" rendered \"That is to say\". \"Standpunct\" in the plural \"all lower [standpoints]\" supplied by sense (*niedrigen*).","- krv-a-1089: \"Einhelligkeit\" rendered \"unanimity\" (cf. Kemp Smith's rendering of the sense of agreement); \"das dritte\" = third law (of affinity/continuity), the German \"sie\" is left as the law's prescription.","- krv-a-1090: \"[character]\" supplied for *das Systematische*; \"Absichten ... Probe\" and the sentence beginning \"Es sind hinter diesen Gesetzen\" divided and smoothed. *Naturerkentniß* = \"knowledge of nature\".","- krv-a-1091: the \"nicht allein ... sondern auch\" construction restated as \"first ... secondly\"; *quantum discretum* kept in Latin.","- krv-a-1092: \"Verstandeserkentnisse\" = \"modes of knowledge of the understanding\"; \"Gattungen dieser Bahnen\" rendered \"unity of genus in the shape of these orbits\"; *Eroberungen* = \"conquests\".","- krv-a-1093: \"Befolgung\" rendered \"guidance\" for sense; \"obiective aber unbestimte Gültigkeit\" kept; \"hevristische\" rendered \"heuristic\".","- krv-a-1094: \"Anschauung\" = intuition; *Schema der Sinnlichkeit* = \"schema of sensibility\"; *constitutiv/regulativ* kept as English cognates."]],"krv-a-1095":["The understanding constitutes an object for reason, just as sensibility does for the understanding. To render the unity of all possible empirical acts of the understanding systematic is a task of reason, just as the understanding connects the manifold of appearances by means of concepts and brings it under empirical laws. But the acts of the understanding, in the absence of schemata of sensibility, are indeterminate; and similarly the unity of reason is in itself indeterminate with respect both to the conditions under which, and to the degree to which, the understanding ought to combine its concepts systematically. Now although no schema can be found in intuition for the thoroughgoing systematic unity of all concepts of the understanding, an analogue of such a schema can and must be given. This analogue is the idea of the maximum of the division and of the unification of the knowledge of the understanding in one principle. For the greatest and the absolutely complete can be thought determinately, since all the restricting conditions which yield an indeterminate manifold are left aside. The idea of reason is therefore an analogue of a schema of sensibility; but with this difference, that the application of the concepts of the understanding to the schema of reason is not likewise a knowledge of the object itself (as is the application of the categories to their sensible schemata), but only a rule or principle of the systematic unity of all employment of the understanding. Now every principle which establishes a priori for the understanding a thoroughgoing unity in its employment holds also, though only indirectly, of the object of experience. The principles of pure reason will therefore have objective reality in respect of the object of experience also; but not in order to determine anything in it. Their purpose is only to indicate the procedure by which the empirical and determinate employment of the understanding in experience can be brought into thoroughgoing agreement with itself. This is achieved by bringing that employment, so far as possible, into connection with the principle of thoroughgoing unity, and by deriving it from that principle.",["- Split the long final sentence into three. \"vor\" is the older form of \"für\" (\"for\"). \"Verstandeshandlungen\" = \"acts of the understanding\".","- \"Ansehung\" = \"in respect of\". \"ausfündig gemacht\" rendered \"found\".","- \"Schemate\" = schemata; \"dieses lezteren\" (the object of experience) resolved by judgment.","- \"abgeleiter\" read as \"abgeleitet\" (OCR/print slip).","- \"Verstandeserkentniß\" rendered \"knowledge of the understanding\"."]],"krv-a-1096":["I entitle maxims of reason all subjective principles which are derived not from the constitution of the object but from the interest of reason in respect of a certain possible perfection of the knowledge of this object. Thus there are maxims of speculative reason which rest solely on its speculative interest, although they may seem to be objective principles.",["- \"Ich nenne … Maximen\" rendered \"I entitle … maxims\".","- \"Obiect\" = object; \"Erkentniß\" = knowledge."]],"krv-a-1097":["If merely regulative principles are regarded as constitutive, they may, as objective principles, be in conflict. If, however, they are regarded merely as maxims, there is no genuine conflict, but only a difference in the interest of reason, which causes the divergence in modes of thought. In actual fact reason has only a single interest, and the conflict of its maxims is only a difference between methods, and a reciprocal limitation of them, in satisfying this interest.",["- \"Denkungsart\" rendered \"modes of thought\"; \"einiges\" = \"single\" (einziges).","- \"Streit\" rendered \"conflict\"; \"Gnüge thun\" = \"satisfy\"."]],"krv-a-1098":["In this manner, in the one reasoner the interest in manifoldness (in accordance with the principle of specification) is the stronger, in the other the interest in unity (in accordance with the principle of aggregation). Each believes that his judgment rests on insight into the object, and yet he founds it solely on his greater or lesser attachment to one of the two principles. Neither of these rests on objective grounds, but only on the interest of reason, and they might therefore be better entitled maxims than principles. When I see men of insight in dispute with one another over the characteristics of men, animals or plants, or even of bodies in the mineral kingdom, some assuming, for example, particular national characters grounded in descent, or else decided and hereditary differences between families, races and so forth, while others set their minds on the view that nature has in this matter made all dispositions entirely alike, and that all difference rests only on external accidents, I need only consider the constitution of the object to understand that it lies far too deeply hidden for either party to be speaking from insight into the nature of the object. It is nothing other than the twofold interest of reason, of which the one party takes the one to heart, or else affects to do so, the other party the other. It is, that is to say, the difference between the maxims of the manifoldness of nature and of the unity of nature. These can very well be reconciled; but so long as they are held to be objective insights, they occasion not only conflict but also obstacles which for a long time delay the discovery of truth, until a means is found of reconciling the disputed interest and of satisfying reason in the matter.",["- Restated the final sentence as three; \"dieser Theil … iener\" rendered \"the one party … the other party\".","- \"Vernünftler\" rendered \"reasoner\"; \"affectirt\" rendered \"affects to do so\" (judgment: Kant suggests feigned concern).","- \"welche … vereinigen lassen\" resolved as referring to the maxims."]],"krv-a-1099":["The same holds of the assertion, or the contesting, of the much-discussed law of the continuous scale of creatures, which was set in vogue by Leibniz and admirably furbished up by Bonnet. It is nothing but a following out of the principle of affinity, which rests on the interest of reason; for observation and insight into the constitution of nature could never have supplied it as an objective assertion. The rungs of such a ladder, as experience can show them to us, stand much too far apart, and our supposedly small differences are commonly in nature itself such wide gaps, that no reliance whatever can be placed on such observations as indications of nature's purposes. (This is especially so where there is a great manifoldness of things, for it must always be easy to find certain resemblances and approximations.) On the other hand, the method of seeking order in nature in accordance with such a principle, and the maxim of regarding such order as grounded in nature in general, though it is left undetermined where or how far, is certainly a legitimate and excellent regulative principle of reason. As such, however, it goes much further than experience or observation can ever match; yet it determines nothing, and only prescribes the path to systematic unity.",["- Moved the parenthesis to a separate sentence; \"Absichten der Natur\" rendered \"nature's purposes\".","- \"vermeintlichkleine\" read as \"vermeintlich kleine\".","- Last clause: \"ihr\" taken as reason, and the sentence's irregular syntax smoothed."]],"krv-a-1100":["On the Final Purpose of the Natural Dialectic of Human Reason.",["- \"Endabsicht\" rendered \"final purpose\"; heading translated as it stands."]],"krv-a-1101":["The ideas of pure reason can never be dialectical in themselves. Their mere misuse alone must be responsible for our being deceived by a fallacious illusion arising from them. For they are set us as problems by the very nature of our reason, and this supreme tribunal for all the rights and claims of speculation cannot itself contain original deceptions and delusions. Presumably, therefore, they have their good and purposive vocation in the natural constitution of our reason. The mob of sophists, however, cries out, as usual, about absurdities and contradictions, and reviles the government into whose innermost plans it is unable to penetrate. Yet it ought to owe to that government's beneficent influences its very preservation, and even the culture which puts it in a position to blame and condemn.",["- Divided the first and last sentences. \"Vernünftler\" here \"sophists\" (pejorative sense).","- \"aufgegeben\" rendered \"set us as problems\"; \"Bestimmung\" rendered \"vocation\"."]],"krv-a-1102":["We cannot make use of an a priori concept with any security without having accomplished its transcendental deduction. The ideas of pure reason do not, indeed, admit of a deduction of the kind possible for the categories. But if they are to have even the slightest objective validity, however indeterminate, and are not to represent mere empty thought-entities (*entia rationis ratiocinantis*), a deduction of them must be possible, even if it should differ widely from that which can be undertaken for the categories. This is the completion of the critical task of pure reason, and it is this that we now propose to undertake.",["- Print reads \"ratiocinanus\"; corrected to *ratiocinantis*.","- \"Gedankendinge\" rendered \"thought-entities\"."]],"krv-a-1103":["There is a great difference between something being given to my reason as an object absolutely, and its being given only as an object in the idea. In the former case my concepts are directed to determining the object. In the latter case there is really only a schema, to which no object, not even hypothetically, is directly assigned, and which serves only to represent to us other objects, by means of their relation to this idea, in accordance with their systematic unity, and so indirectly. Thus I say that the concept of a supreme intelligence is a mere idea; that is, its objective reality is not meant to consist in its referring directly to an object (for in that sense we could not justify its objective validity). It is only a schema, ordered according to the conditions of the greatest unity of reason, of the concept of a thing in general. Its sole use is to secure the greatest systematic unity in the empirical employment of our reason, in that we derive the object of experience, as it were, from the imagined object of this idea, as from its ground or cause. We then say, for instance, that the things of the world must be viewed as if they had received their existence from a supreme intelligence. In this way the idea is properly only a heuristic, and not an ostensive, concept. It shows, not how an object is constituted, but how, under its guidance, we ought to seek the constitution and connection of the objects of experience in general. Now suppose it can be shown that the three kinds of transcendental ideas (the psychological, the cosmological, and the theological), although they are not directly related to any object corresponding to them or to its determination, nevertheless lead, on the presupposition of such an object in the idea, to systematic unity in all the rules of the empirical employment of reason; and that they always extend our empirical knowledge, and can never run counter to it. It is then a necessary maxim of reason to proceed in accordance with such ideas. And this is the transcendental deduction of all ideas of speculative reason. They are deduced not as constitutive principles for extending our knowledge to more objects than experience can give, but as regulative principles of the systematic unity of the manifold of empirical knowledge in general. Empirical knowledge is thereby more fully cultivated and rectified within its own limits than could happen, without such ideas, through the mere employment of the principles of the understanding.",["- Long sentences divided at \"Wenn man nun zeigen kan … so ist es\" and at \"Und dieses ist …\"; the protasis is restated as \"Now suppose it can be shown\".","- \"dem direct kein Gegenstand … zugegeben wird\" rendered \"to which no object … is directly assigned\" (zugeben: grant, assign).","- \"sondern er ist nur ein … Schema, von dem Begriffe\": \"of the concept of a thing in general\" taken as genitive-like complement of Schema; the sentence is split.","- *hevristisch / ostensiv*: \"heuristic\" and \"ostensive\".","- Erfahrungserkentniß = \"empirical knowledge\" (Erkenntniß = knowledge)."]],"krv-a-1104":["I shall try to make this clearer. Following these ideas as principles, we are, first (in psychology), to connect all appearances, actions and receptivity of our mind, under the guidance of inner experience, as if the mind were a simple substance which persists (at least during life) with personal identity, while its states, to which those of the body belong only as external conditions, continually change. Secondly (in cosmology), we must pursue the conditions of natural appearances, inner as well as outer, in an inquiry which can nowhere be completed, as if the series were in itself infinite and without a first or supreme member. We do not on that account deny that outside all appearances there are purely intelligible first grounds of them; but we may never bring these into the connection of natural explanations, since we do not know them at all. Finally, and thirdly (in respect of theology), we must view everything that can belong to the connection of possible experience as if this experience constituted an absolute unity, yet one thoroughly dependent and still always conditioned within the sensible world. At the same time we must view the sum of all appearances (the sensible world itself) as if it had a single, supreme and all-sufficient ground outside its compass, namely, a self-subsistent, original and creative reason, as it were. In relation to this we direct every empirical employment of our reason, in its widest extension, as if the objects themselves had sprung from that archetype of all reason. That is to say, we are not to derive the inner appearances of the soul from a simple thinking substance, but to derive them from one another in accordance with the idea of a simple being. Nor are we to derive the order of the world and its systematic unity from a supreme intelligence, but to take from the idea of a supremely wise cause the rule by which reason may best be employed, for its own satisfaction, in connecting causes and effects in the world.",["- Divided the long third sentence into several; \"das heißt:\" kept as \"That is to say\".","- \"dasselbe\" (the mind, Gemüth) and \"dieselbe\" in the second clause (the inquiry/series) resolved by judgment: \"the series\" supplied; \"ihre Zustände\" refers to the substance.","- \"ienem Urbilde aller Vernunft\" = \"that archetype of all reason\".","- \"gleichsam selbständige\": \"as it were\" attached to the whole phrase \"self-subsistent, original and creative reason\"; the reading is a choice.","- \"iene\" (before \"von einander ableiten\") taken as the inner appearances."]],"krv-a-1105":["Now there is not the least thing to hinder us from also assuming these ideas as objective and hypostatic, with the sole exception of the cosmological idea, where reason runs into an antinomy when it tries to carry it out. (The psychological and theological ideas contain nothing of the kind.) For there is no contradiction in them; and how, then, could anyone dispute their objective reality, since he knows as little of their possibility, in order to deny it, as we know of it in order to affirm it? Nevertheless, to assume something it is not enough that there is no positive obstacle to it; nor can we be permitted to introduce, as actual and determinate objects, beings of thought which surpass all our concepts, though they contradict none, merely on the credit of a speculative reason that is eager to complete its work. They ought not, then, to be assumed in themselves. Their reality should hold only as that of a schema of the regulative principle of the systematic unity of all knowledge of nature. They ought, therefore, to be laid at the foundation only as analogues of actual things, not as actual things in themselves. We remove from the object of the idea the conditions which limit our concept of understanding, but which also alone make it possible for us to have a determinate concept of any thing. And now we think a something of which we have no concept at all as to what it is in itself, but of which we nevertheless think a relation to the sum of appearances that is analogous to the relation which appearances have to one another.",["- \"wenn sie solche zu Stande bringen will\": \"solche\" taken as the ideas (as objective and hypostatic); rendered \"carry it out\", referring to the cosmological idea.","- \"Verstandesbegriff\" = \"concept of understanding\"; \"Inbegriff\" = \"sum\".","- \"Gedankenwesen\" = \"beings of thought\".","- Parenthesis retained in its place but as a separate sentence."]],"krv-a-1106":["If, therefore, we assume such ideal beings, we do not really extend our knowledge beyond the objects of possible experience, but only the empirical unity of these objects, through the systematic unity for which the idea gives us the schema. The idea thus holds not as a constitutive but merely as a regulative principle. For when we posit a thing corresponding to the idea, a something, or actual being, we do not thereby say that we mean to extend our knowledge of things by transcendent concepts. This being is laid at the foundation only in the idea and not in itself, and therefore only to express the systematic unity which is to serve as the guiding line for the empirical employment of reason. We do not thereby decide anything as to what the ground of this unity is, or what the inner property of such a being may be, upon which, as its cause, that unity rests.",["- \"welche mithin\" (referring to the idea) resolved as \"The idea thus\"; the first sentence divided.","- \"Richtschnur\" = \"guiding line\".","- \"über die Obiecte\": \"beyond\", supplied to bring out the contrast; \"der lezteren\" = \"of these objects\"."]],"krv-a-1107":["Thus the transcendental and only determinate concept which merely speculative reason gives us of God is, in the strictest sense, deistic. That is, reason does not even furnish the objective validity of such a concept, but only the idea of something on which all empirical reality grounds its highest and necessary unity. We can think this something only according to the analogy of an actual substance which is the cause of all things in accordance with laws of reason. This holds if we do undertake to think it at all as a particular object, and do not prefer, content with the mere idea of the regulative principle of reason, to set aside the completion of all conditions of thought as exceeding the powers of human understanding. But that course cannot be reconciled with the aim of a perfect systematic unity in our knowledge, to which reason at least sets no limits.",["- Divided the final sentence into four; \"wofern wir es ia unternehmen\" given as a conditional, \"if we do undertake\".","- \"überschwenglich vor den menschlichen Verstand\": \"as exceeding the powers of human understanding\" (*überschwenglich*, transcendent in the sense of exceeding).","- \"welches aber … nicht zusammen bestehen kan\": \"welches\" resolved as the course of setting aside the completion of conditions.","- \"im genauesten Verstande\": \"in the strictest sense\" (Verstand here = meaning, not the faculty)."]],"krv-a-1108":["Hence it comes about that, if I assume a divine being, I have indeed not the least concept either of the inner possibility of its supreme perfection or of the necessity of its existence. But I am then able to satisfy all the other questions which concern the contingent, and to afford reason the completest contentment in respect of the greatest unity which it is to seek in its empirical employment, though not in respect of this presupposition itself. This proves that it is its speculative interest, and not its insight, which entitles reason to start from a point so far above its sphere, in order thence to consider its objects in a complete whole.",["- Restated the sentence as three; \"welches beweiset\" is rendered as a separate sentence (\"This proves\"), its antecedent being the whole situation just described.","- \"ein Gnüge thun\" = \"satisfy\"; \"Befriedigung\" = \"contentment\"; \"Einsicht\" = \"insight\".","- \"ihr\" in \"ihr speculatives Interesse\" and \"sie berechtige\" is taken to refer to *Vernunft* (reason), not the divine being."]],"krv-a-1109":["Here there appears a difference in the manner of thinking, in dealing with one and the same presupposition. The difference is rather subtle, but is nevertheless of great importance in transcendental philosophy. I may have sufficient ground for assuming something relatively (*suppositio relativa*), without being entitled to assume it absolutely (*suppositio absoluta*). This distinction applies when we are concerned merely with a regulative principle. We recognise the necessity of such a principle in itself, but not the source of that necessity; and we assume a supreme ground for it solely in order to think its universality more determinately. This is so, for instance, when I think of a being as existing which corresponds to a mere, and indeed transcendental, idea. For in that case I can never assume the existence of this thing in itself, since no concepts through which I can think any object determinately are able to reach so far, and the conditions of the objective validity of my concepts are excluded by the idea itself. The concepts of reality, of substance, of causality, and even that of necessity in existence, have no meaning that determines any object, save in the employment in which they make possible the empirical knowledge of an object. They can therefore be used to explain the possibility of things in the world of sense, but not the possibility of a whole of the world itself; for the ground of explanation would have to lie outside the world, and so would not be an object of possible experience.\n\nNow I can nevertheless assume such an incomprehensible being, the object of a mere idea, relatively to the world of sense, though not in itself. For if there underlies the greatest possible empirical employment of my reason an idea (that of systematically complete unity, of which I shall shortly speak more determinately) which can never be adequately exhibited in experience, although it is indispensably necessary for bringing empirical unity to the highest possible degree, I shall be not only entitled but also constrained to realise this idea, that is, to posit for it an actual object. This object, however, I posit only as a something in general which I do not know at all in itself, and to which, as a ground of that systematic unity and in relation to it, I ascribe only such properties as are analogous to the concepts of the understanding in their empirical employment. Following the analogy of the realities in the world, of substances, of causality and of necessity, I shall therefore think a being which possesses all of these in the highest perfection. And since this idea rests merely on my reason, I shall be able to think this being as self-subsistent reason, which through ideas of the greatest harmony and unity is the cause of the whole of the world. I omit all conditions that limit the idea, solely in order that, under the protection of such an original ground, I may make possible systematic unity of the manifold in the whole of the world, and by means of it the greatest possible empirical employment of reason; for I regard all connections as if they were ordinances of a supreme reason, of which our own is a feeble copy.\n\nI then think this supreme being through concepts which properly have their application only in the world of sense. But since I make of that transcendental presupposition no other than a relative use, namely that it is to furnish the substratum of the greatest possible unity of experience, I may quite well think a being which I distinguish from the world through properties that belong solely to the world of sense. For I by no means demand, nor am I entitled to demand, that I should know this object of my idea as it may be in itself; I have no concepts for that. Even the concepts of reality, substance, causality, and indeed of necessity in existence, lose all meaning, and are empty titles of concepts without any content, if I venture with them outside the field of the senses. I think only the relation of a being, wholly unknown to me in itself, to the greatest systematic unity of the whole of the world, and I do so solely in order to make it the schema of the regulative principle of the greatest possible empirical employment of my reason.",["- Divided several long sentences, and broke the passage into paragraphs at \"Now I can nevertheless…\" and \"I then think…\"; the German is one block.","- \"Denkungsart\" = \"manner of thinking\"; \"relativ\"/\"schlechthin\" = \"relatively\"/\"absolutely\".","- \"Erkentniß\" = \"knowledge\"; \"Weltganzen\" = \"whole of the world\"; \"Sinnenwelt\" = \"world of sense\".","- \"dieses Wesen als selbstständige Vernunft…denken können\" is syntactically loose (after \"werde…denken…und…können\"); smoothed to \"I shall be able to think\".","- \"so daß ich alle…Bedingungen weglasse\" is rendered as an independent sentence (\"I omit…\"), with the final \"indem\" clause as a \"for\" clause.","- \"das Princips\" etc.: \"daß sie das Substratum…abgeben solle\" — \"it\" refers to the transcendental presupposition (*Voraussetzung*).","- \"Nachbild\" = \"copy\"; \"Schutze\" = \"protection\"; \"Anordnungen\" = \"ordinances\"."]],"krv-a-1110":["If we now turn our gaze upon the transcendental object of our idea, we see that we cannot presuppose its actuality in itself through the concepts of reality, substance, causality, and so forth, since these concepts have not the least application to anything that is entirely distinct from the world of sense. The supposition which reason makes of a supreme being, as the highest cause, is therefore thought merely relatively, for the sake of the systematic unity of the world of sense; it is a mere something in the idea, of which we have no concept as to what it may be in itself. This also explains why, in relation to what is given to the senses as existing, we need the idea of a primordial being necessary in itself, and yet can never have the least concept of this being and of its absolute necessity.",["- \"Wirklichkeit\" = \"actuality\"; \"Urwesen\" = \"primordial being\"; \"Supposition\" = \"supposition\".","- \"Behuf\" = \"for the sake of\"."]],"krv-a-1111":["We can now set the result of the whole of the transcendental dialectic clearly before our eyes, and determine precisely the final purpose of the ideas of pure reason, which become dialectical only through misunderstanding and carelessness. Pure reason is in fact occupied with nothing but itself, and can have no other business. For what are given to it are not objects for the unity of the concept of experience, but modes of knowledge of the understanding [*Verstandeserkentnisse*] for the unity of the concept of reason, that is, for connection in one principle. The unity of reason is the unity of a system; and this systematic unity serves reason not objectively as a principle for extending it over objects, but subjectively as a maxim for extending it over all possible empirical knowledge of objects.\n\nNone the less, the systematic connection which reason can give to the empirical employment of the understanding not only furthers its extension but at the same time confirms its correctness. The principle of such systematic unity is also objective, but in an indeterminate manner (*principium vagum*). It is not a constitutive principle, for determining anything in respect of its direct object; it is a merely regulative principle and maxim, to further and strengthen the empirical employment of reason into the infinite (the indeterminate) by opening up new paths which the understanding does not know, and this without ever being in the least contrary to the laws of its empirical employment.",["- Divided the passage at \"Gleichwol\" into two paragraphs and split the long final sentence; \"und das Principium…\" begins a new sentence.","- \"Verstandeserkentnisse\" = \"modes of knowledge of the understanding\" (plural).","- \"ihr\" in \"ihr…gegeben werden\" = reason; \"um sie über…verbreiten\" refers to the unity (or reason's systematic unity), taken as \"extending it\" i.e. the employment of reason; the German is ambiguous.","- \"Gleichwol befördert…\": \"desselben\" = of the empirical employment of the understanding.","- \"ohne…zuwider zu seyn\": the subject is the regulative principle/employment of reason; kept as a participial \"without ever being\"."]],"krv-a-1112":["Reason, however, can think this systematic unity only by giving to its idea an object; and this object cannot be given through any experience, for experience never yields an example of perfect systematic unity. This entity of reason (*ens rationis ratiocinatae*) is, then, a mere idea. It is not assumed to be something actual, absolutely and in itself. It is adopted only problematically as a ground (since we cannot reach it through any concepts of the understanding), so that we may regard all connection of the things of the world of sense as if it had its ground in this entity of reason. Our sole purpose in doing so is to found upon it the systematic unity which is indispensable to reason, which is in every way conducive to empirical knowledge of the understanding, and which yet can never be a hindrance to it.",["- Restated the single long second sentence as three; the parenthesis \"(weil wir es … können)\" is kept in place.","- \"Verstandeserkentniß\" rendered \"knowledge of the understanding\"; \"Erkentniß\" = knowledge.","- \"Vernunftwesen\" rendered \"entity of reason\", following Kant's Latin gloss *ens rationis ratiocinatae*, which is left in Latin and italicised (Kant's ligature \"æ\" given as \"ae\").","- \"ihr\" at the end of the sentence is taken to refer to \"Verstandeserkentniß\" (empirical knowledge), not to reason."]],"krv-a-1113":["We at once misconstrue the meaning of this idea if we treat it as the assertion, or even as the presupposition, of an actual thing to which we think of ascribing the ground of the systematic constitution of the world. On the contrary, we leave entirely undecided what the nature of the ground may be which eludes our concepts; and we adopt an idea merely as a standpoint from which alone we can extend that unity which is so essential for reason and so beneficial for the understanding. In a word, this transcendental thing is merely the schema of that regulative principle by which reason, so far as in it lies, extends systematic unity over all experience.",["- \"vor\" in \"vor die Behauptung\" and \"vor Beschaffenheit\" is the older form of \"für\"; rendered \"as\" and \"nature … may be\".","- \"iene\" (OCR/orthography for \"jene\") translated as \"that/which\"; \"dieses transsendentale Ding\" is kept literal.","- \"was … an sich vor Beschaffenheit habe\": \"what nature the ground has in itself\", smoothed to a relative clause.","- \"derselben\" refers to the systematic constitution of the world; \"ground\" is supplied in the English for clarity."]],"krv-a-1114":["The first object of such an idea is myself, regarded merely as thinking nature (soul). If I wish to discover the properties with which a thinking being exists in itself, I must question experience; and of all the categories I can apply none to this object save in so far as its schema is given in sensible intuition. But in this way I never attain to a systematic unity of all appearances of inner sense. Instead of the concept of experience (of what the soul actually is), which cannot carry us far, reason takes the concept of the empirical unity of all thought. By thinking this unity as unconditioned and original, it makes out of it a concept of reason (idea) of a simple substance which is in itself unchangeable (personally identical), and stands in community with other real things outside it; in a word, of a simple self-subsistent intelligence. In all this, however, reason has before its eyes nothing but principles of systematic unity in the explanation of the appearances of the soul. That is to say, it has in view the following: to regard all determinations as in one subject; all powers, so far as possible, as derived from one fundamental power; all change as belonging to the states of one and the same persisting being; and to represent all appearances in space as entirely distinct from the acts of thought. That simplicity of substance, etc., was meant only to be the schema of this regulative principle. It is not presupposed as if it were the actual ground of the properties of the soul. For these may also rest on quite other grounds which we do not know at all. Nor could we really know the soul in itself through these assumed predicates, even if we were willing to let them hold of it absolutely; for they constitute a mere idea, which cannot be represented in concreto at all.\n\nFrom such a psychological idea nothing but advantage can arise, if only we take care not to let it count as more than a mere idea, that is, to let it count merely relatively to the systematic employment of reason in respect of the appearances of our soul. For then no empirical laws of bodily appearances, which are of quite another kind, intrude into the explanations of what belongs merely to inner sense; no windy hypotheses of the generation, destruction and palingenesis of souls, etc., are admitted. The consideration of this object of inner sense is therefore conducted quite purely and unmixed with heterogeneous properties. Moreover, the enquiry of reason is directed to carrying the grounds of explanation in this subject, so far as possible, back to a single principle. All this is best, indeed solely, effected through such a schema, as if it were an actual being.\n\nThe psychological idea can also signify nothing but the schema of a regulative concept. For if I were even to ask whether the soul is not in itself of spiritual nature, the question would have no meaning at all. By such a concept I remove not merely corporeal nature, but nature in general, that is, all predicates of any possible experience, and so all the conditions for thinking an object for such a concept; and it is these alone which make us say that the concept has a meaning.",["- Restated the long final-sentence sequences (\"Denn, da mengen sich…\") as several shorter sentences, and divided the passage into three paragraphs for clarity; the German is a single block.","- \"Kräfte\" rendered \"powers\", \"Grundkraft\" \"fundamental power\" (Vermögen is not used here).","- \"Hiebey … nemlich:\" I have recast as \"That is to say, it has in view the following\"; the infinitives depend on \"vor Augen\".","- \"diese\" in \"Denn diese können auch auf ganz anderen Gründen beruhen\" taken as the properties of the soul; \"sie\" in \"indem sie eine blosse Idee ausmachen\" as the predicates.","- \"da\" in \"da mengen sich\" rendered \"then\", i.e. when the idea is held to its merely relative use.","- \"welches alles … bewirkt wird\" smoothed; \"ein solches Schema, als ob es ein wirkliches Wesen wäre\" is kept close to the German.","- \"Palingenesie\" retained as \"palingenesis\"; *in concreto* italicised as a Latin term.","- \"als welches doch einzig und allein es macht\" rendered \"it is these alone which make us say\"; \"es\" supplied as \"the concept\" for clarity, in the final clause."]],"krv-a-1115":["The second regulative idea of merely speculative reason is the concept of the world in general. For nature is, strictly speaking, the only given object in respect of which reason requires regulative principles. This nature is twofold: either thinking nature or corporeal nature. But in dealing with the latter, in order to think it according to its inner possibility, that is, to determine the application of the categories to it, we need no idea, that is, no representation transcending experience. Nor is any such idea possible in respect of it, since in this field we are guided merely by sensible intuition, and not, as in the fundamental psychological concept (the I), which contains a certain form of thinking, namely its unity, *a priori*. Nothing therefore remains for pure reason save nature in general, and the completeness of the conditions in it in accordance with some principle.\n\nThe absolute totality of the series of these conditions, in the derivation of their members, is an idea which can indeed never be completely realised in the empirical employment of reason, but which none the less serves as a rule for how we ought to proceed in respect of them. In the explanation of given appearances (in regress or ascent) we are to proceed as if the series were in itself infinite, that is, *in indefinitum*. But where reason itself is regarded as determining cause (in freedom), and therefore in practical principles, we are to proceed as if we had before us not an object of the senses but of pure understanding. In that case the conditions can no longer be posited in the series of appearances but outside it, and the series of states can be regarded as if it were begun absolutely (through an intelligible cause). All this proves that the cosmological ideas are nothing but regulative principles, and are very far from positing, as it were constitutively, an actual totality of such series. The rest may be sought in its own place, under the Antinomy of Pure Reason.",["- Divided the long second sentence of the second paragraph into several; \"derselben\" (twice) is taken to refer to the series of conditions, \"dieselbe\" to corporeal nature.","- \"Weltbegriff\" rendered \"concept of the world\"; \"Grundbegriffe\" as \"fundamental concept\".","- \"und nicht, wie in dem psychologischen Grundbegriffe\" is elliptical; I supply \"we are guided\" in the English without brackets, since it is only the sense of the construction.","- \"vor uns hätten, wo …\" recast as \"In that case…\"; \"wo\" is taken as introducing the consequence of the \"as if\".","- *a priori*, *in indefinitum* kept in Latin and italicised. \"Vollständigkeit\" rendered \"completeness\".","- The \"(im Zurückgehen oder Aufsteigen)\" parenthesis is kept; \"Zustände\" rendered \"states\"."]],"krv-a-1116":["The third idea of pure reason contains a merely relative supposition of a being as the sole and all-sufficient cause of all cosmological series, and is the concept of reason [*Vernunftbegriff*] of God. We have not the slightest ground for assuming the object of this idea absolutely (for supposing it in itself). For what could move us, or even merely entitle us, to believe in, or to assert, on the basis of its mere concept, a being of the highest perfection, a being necessary by its very nature, if it were not for the world, in relation to which alone this supposition can be necessary? And in that relation it becomes clear that the idea of such a being, like all speculative ideas, means nothing more than that reason commands us to regard all connection in the world according to principles of a systematic unity, that is, as if it had all sprung from one single all-embracing being, as the supreme and all-sufficient cause. From this it is clear that reason can here have in view nothing but its own formal rule for the extension of its empirical employment, and never an extension beyond all the limits of that employment. Consequently, there lies hidden under this idea no constitutive principle of its employment as directed to possible experience.",[]],"krv-a-1117":["The highest formal unity, which rests solely on concepts of reason, is the purposive unity of things; and the speculative interest of reason makes it necessary to regard all ordering in the world as if it had sprung from the purpose of a supreme reason. Such a principle opens up quite new prospects to our reason, as applied to the field of experience, of connecting the things of the world according to teleological laws, and so of attaining to their greatest systematic unity. The presupposition of a supreme intelligence as the sole cause of the world-whole, though only in the idea, can therefore always be of service to reason and can never be harmful to it. For if, in regard to the figure of the earth (round, yet somewhat flattened*), the mountains and seas, and so forth, we assume beforehand nothing but wise purposes on the part of an author, we can in this way make a number of discoveries. So long as we keep to this presupposition as a merely regulative principle, even error cannot harm us. For at worst nothing more can follow than that, where we expected a teleological connection (*nexus finalis*), only a mechanical or physical connection (*nexus effectivus*) is met with. In such a case we merely miss one unity; we do not destroy the unity of reason in its empirical employment. But even this setback cannot affect the law itself, taken in its general and teleological intent. For though an anatomist may be convicted of error when he relates some member of an animal body to a purpose which can be clearly shown not to follow from it, it is yet quite impossible to prove in any single case that an arrangement of nature, be it what it may, has no purpose whatever. Hence the physiology of physicians extends its very limited empirical knowledge of the purposes of the structure of an organic body by a principle which pure reason alone supplied, and extends it so far that it is quite boldly assumed, with the agreement of all intelligent persons, that everything in the animal has its use and good purpose. This presupposition, if it were to be constitutive, goes much further than previous observation can entitle us to. From which it may be seen that it is nothing but a regulative principle of reason, whereby we are to reach the highest systematic unity by means of the idea of the purposive causality of the supreme cause of the world, and [are to proceed] as if this cause, as supreme intelligence, were, in accordance with the wisest purpose, the cause of everything.",[]],"krv-a-1118":["*) The advantage of a spherical figure of the earth is well enough known; but few know that its flattening, as a spheroid, alone prevents the projections of the solid land, or of smaller mountains perhaps thrown up by earthquakes, from continually and, in no very long time, appreciably displacing the axis of the earth. [This would happen] were not the swelling of the earth at the equator so mighty a mountain that the momentum of any other mountain can never appreciably shift it from its position in respect of the axis. And yet this wise arrangement is explained without hesitation from the equilibrium of the earth's formerly fluid mass.",[]],"krv-a-1119":["But if we abandon this restriction of the idea to merely regulative employment, reason is led astray in many ways. For it then leaves the ground of experience, which must contain the landmarks of its course, and ventures beyond it into the incomprehensible and unfathomable. At these heights it necessarily becomes dizzy, since from that standpoint it finds itself completely cut off from every employment that is in agreement with experience.",["- krv-a-1116: The first sentence is smoothed; the German \"welche … enthält\" clause is made to qualify the idea, and \"Vernunftbegriff\" is left in German as the term turns on it. The long second sentence is divided into three.","- krv-a-1116: \"wäre es nicht die Welt\" rendered \"if it were not for the world\"; \"und da zeigt es sich klar\" taken as \"in that relation it becomes clear.\" \"Supposition\" and \"supponiren\" are kept close to the Latin (\"suppose\").","- krv-a-1117: \"Gliedmaas\" rendered \"member\"; \"Zergliederer\" as \"anatomist\"; \"Querstrich\" as \"setback\" (lit. cross-stroke, i.e. thwarting). \"Idee der zweckmässigen Caussalität\" kept as \"purposive causality.\"","- krv-a-1117: The last sentence is tangled (the \"und, als ob …\" clause); I supplied \"[are to proceed]\" to complete the construction. \"Verständigen\" taken as \"intelligent persons.\"","- krv-a-1117: \"Aber so gar dieser Querstrich kan …\" is rendered \"But even this setback cannot affect the law itself\"; \"in allgemeiner und teleologischer Absicht\" read as \"taken in its general and teleological intent.\"","- krv-a-1118: Footnote: \"wäre nicht die Aufschwellung …\" supplied \"[This would happen]\" to carry the conditional. \"Schwung\" rendered \"momentum\"; \"unter der Linie\" as \"at the equator.\"","- krv-a-1119: \"Merkzeichen ihres Ganges\" rendered \"landmarks of its course\"; \"schwindlicht\" as \"dizzy.\" The \"Restriction der Idee\" is kept as \"restriction.\""]],"krv-a-1120":["The first error which arises from using the idea of a supreme being not merely regulatively but (what is contrary to the nature of an idea) constitutively, is that of the lazy reason (*ignava ratio*).* The title may be given to any principle which leads us to regard our investigation of nature, wherever it may be, as absolutely completed, and so allows reason to go to rest, as if it had entirely discharged its task. Thus even the psychological idea, when it is employed as a constitutive principle for the explanation of the appearances of our soul, and then further, in extension of our knowledge of this subject, is carried beyond all experience (to its state after death), does indeed make things very easy for reason, but also wholly ruins and destroys all employment of reason in the study of nature under the guidance of experience.\n\nThus the dogmatic spiritualist explains the unity of the person, which persists unchanged through all change of states, from the unity of the thinking substance, which he believes he perceives immediately in the I. The interest we take in things that are to occur only after our death he explains from the consciousness of the immaterial nature of our thinking subject, and so forth. He thereby excuses himself from all investigation, by physical grounds of explanation, into the cause of these inner appearances of ours. By the dictatorial pronouncement, as it were, of a transcendent reason, he passes by the immanent sources of knowledge in experience, for the sake of his own comfort, but at the cost of all insight.\n\nThis injurious consequence appears still more plainly in the dogmatism of our idea of a supreme intelligence, and in the theological system of nature (physico-theology) which is falsely grounded upon it. For there all the ends that show themselves in nature, often ends which we ourselves have only put there, serve to make the investigation of causes quite easy for us. Instead of seeking causes in the universal laws of the mechanism of matter, we appeal directly to the unsearchable decree of supreme wisdom, and regard the labour of reason as completed when we have spared ourselves its employment. Yet that employment finds a guiding thread nowhere except where the order of nature and the series of alterations, in accordance with their inner and more universal laws, put it into our hands.\n\nThis error can be avoided if we do not merely consider certain parts of nature, for instance the distribution of the dry land, its structure, and the constitution and position of the mountains, or perhaps even only the organisation of the vegetable and animal kingdoms, from the standpoint of ends, but make this systematic unity of nature, in relation to the idea of a supreme intelligence, quite universal. For we then lay at the foundation a purposiveness in accordance with universal laws of nature, from which no particular arrangement is excepted, though some are more or less recognisably distinguished for us. We thus have a regulative principle of the systematic unity of a teleological connection. This connection we are not to determine beforehand; in expectation of it we are only to follow up the physico-mechanical connection in accordance with universal laws. For only so can the principle of purposive unity always extend the employment of reason in regard to experience, without in any case doing it harm.",[]],"krv-a-1121":["*) This was the name given by the ancient dialecticians to a fallacious argument which ran as follows: If it is your fate that you will recover from this illness, you will do so, whether you employ a physician or not. Cicero says that this kind of reasoning is so named because, if it is followed, no employment of reason in life is left. That is why I apply the same name to the sophistical argument of pure reason.",[]],"krv-a-1122":["The second error, which arises from misinterpreting the principle of systematic unity just mentioned, is that of the perverted reason (*perversa ratio*, *hysteron proteron rationis* [ὕςερον πρότερον]). The idea of systematic unity should serve only as a regulative principle, in seeking that unity in the connection of things in accordance with universal laws of nature. In so far as anything of it can be met with by the empirical path, we should believe that we have to that extent approached completeness in its employment, though we shall never, of course, reach it. Instead of this, the matter is reversed. We begin by hypostatically laying at the foundation the reality of a principle of purposive unity. We determine the concept of such a supreme intelligence anthropomorphically, since in itself it is wholly unsearchable. And then we force ends upon nature, violently and dictatorially, instead of seeking them, as is fitting, by the path of physical investigation. The outcome is that teleology, which should serve only to complete the unity of nature in accordance with universal laws, now works rather to destroy it. Reason, moreover, deprives itself of its own end, namely, to prove from nature, in accordance with this, the existence of such an intelligent supreme cause.\n\nFor if we cannot presuppose the highest purposiveness in nature *a priori*, that is, as belonging to its essence, how are we to be directed to seek it, and on its ladder to approach the supreme perfection of an author, as a perfection that is absolutely necessary and therefore knowable *a priori*? The regulative principle requires us to presuppose systematic unity absolutely, and so as following from the essence of things, as unity of nature, which is not merely known empirically but presupposed *a priori*, though still indeterminately. But if I first lay a supreme ordering being at the foundation, the unity of nature is in fact done away with. For it is then quite foreign to the nature of things and contingent, and cannot be known from their universal laws. Hence there arises a faulty circle in the proof, since what ought properly to have been proved is presupposed.",[]],"krv-a-1123":["To take the regulative principle of the systematic unity of nature for a constitutive principle, and hypostatically to presuppose as cause what is placed in the idea only as the ground of the harmonious employment of reason, is simply to confuse reason. The investigation of nature pursues its own course solely along the chain of natural causes, in accordance with their universal laws. It does indeed proceed according to the idea of an author, but not in order to derive from him the purposiveness which it everywhere pursues. Its aim is rather to know his existence from this purposiveness, which is sought in the essences of natural things, and if possible in the essences of all things in general, and so to know him as absolutely necessary. Whether this last succeeds or not, the idea remains always correct, and so likewise does its employment, if it has been restricted to the conditions of a merely regulative principle.",[]],"krv-a-1124":["Complete purposive unity is perfection (considered absolutely). If we do not find this in the essence of the things which constitute the whole object of experience, that is, of all our objectively valid knowledge, and so in universal and necessary laws of nature, how are we to infer from this, directly, to the idea of a supreme and absolutely necessary perfection of an original being, which is the origin of all causality? The greatest systematic unity, and consequently also purposive unity, is the school, and indeed the foundation, of the possibility of the greatest employment of human reason. The idea of it is therefore inseparably bound up with the essence of our reason. This same idea is therefore legislative for us, and it is very natural to assume a legislative reason corresponding to it (*intellectus archetypus*), from which all systematic unity of nature, as the object of our reason, may be derived.",["- krv-a-1120: Restated the long sentences as several; \"welches... zuwider ist\" kept in parentheses. The footnote marker [*] is kept after *ignava ratio* (source \"ignaua\"; silently normalised).","- krv-a-1120: \"Erkentnißquellen\" rendered \"sources of knowledge\"; \"Gemächlichkeit\" as \"comfort\"; \"Naturstücke\" as \"parts of nature\".","- krv-a-1120: In \"das Interesse... aus dem Bewustseyn... erklärt\" the verb is carried over by ellipsis from the preceding clause; I supplied \"he explains\".","- krv-a-1121: Footnote kept as a footnote; \"Trugschluß\" as \"fallacious argument\".","- krv-a-1122: Greek ὕςερον (sic, for ὕστερον) kept as printed, with transliteration supplied in square brackets-style gloss. \"Nach diesem\" rendered \"in accordance with this\" (i.e. with purposiveness); a doubtful antecedent, resolved by the argument.","- krv-a-1122: \"wie will man denn angewiesen seyn\" rendered \"how are we to be directed\"; the clause on the regulative principle is syntactically irregular and has been smoothed, with some loss of the German's compressed construction.","- krv-a-1123: \"Wesen\" in \"Wesen der Naturdinge\" rendered \"essences\", as the argument concerns essence, not beings; \"restringirt\" as \"restricted\".","- krv-a-1124: \"Schule\" kept as \"school\"; \"intellectus archetypus\" left in Latin and italicised. \"Caussalität\" is \"causality\"."]],"krv-a-1125":["In connection with the antinomy of pure reason we said that all questions which pure reason raises must be absolutely capable of being answered, and that the plea of the limits of our knowledge, which in many questions of nature is as unavoidable as it is fair, cannot here be allowed. For here the questions are put to us not from the nature of things, but solely from the nature of reason, and concern only its inner constitution. We are now in a position to confirm this assertion, which at first sight seems bold, in respect of the two questions in which pure reason has its greatest interest, and so to bring to complete conclusion our consideration of its dialectic.",["- Restated the long second clause by dividing off \"For here the questions…\" as a separate sentence.","- \"Erkentniß\" = knowledge; \"Antinomie\" kept as \"antinomy\"; \"Anscheine\" rendered \"at first sight.\"","- \"Dialectik derselben\" taken to refer to pure reason's dialectic."]],"krv-a-1126":["If, then, we ask (with regard to a transcendental theology*), first, whether there is anything distinct from the world which contains the ground of the world-order and of its connection according to universal laws, the answer is: Undoubtedly. For the world is a sum of appearances; there must therefore be some transcendental ground of them, that is, a ground thinkable only by the pure understanding.\n\nIf the second question is whether this being is substance, of the greatest reality, necessary, and so forth, I answer that this question has no meaning whatsoever. For all the categories through which I attempt to form a concept of such an object are of none but empirical employment, and have no sense whatsoever if they are not applied to objects of possible experience, that is, to the world of sense. Outside this field they are merely titles for concepts, which we may admit, but through which we can understand nothing.\n\nIf, finally, the third question is whether we may not at least think this being, distinct from the world, in analogy with the objects of experience, the answer is: Certainly, but only as an object in the idea and not in reality, that is, only so far as it is a substratum, unknown to us, of the systematic unity, order and purposiveness of the world's constitution, which reason must make the regulative principle of its investigation of nature. Further, we may in this idea freely and without reproach permit certain anthropomorphisms which are of advantage to the regulative principle in question. For it is always only an idea, which is not referred directly to a being distinct from the world, but to the regulative principle of the systematic unity of the world, and this only by means of a schema of that unity, namely a supreme intelligence which, according to wise purposes, is its author. What this primary ground of the world's unity may be in itself is not what has here been meant to be thought; what is meant is how we are to employ it, or rather its idea, in relation to the systematic employment of reason in regard to the things of the world.",["- Divided the passage into three paragraphs, one for each of Kant's three questions; the German is a single paragraph.","- \"Erstlich/zweitens/drittens\" kept as first, second, finally; \"Frägt man\" rendered \"If we ask.\"","- \"Titel zu Begriffen\" = \"titles for concepts\"; \"Obiecte\" = objects.","- \"Schema derselben\": \"derselben\" taken to refer to the systematic unity of the world.","- The final sentence is restated: \"hat dadurch nicht gedacht werden sollen\" given as \"is not what has here been meant to be thought.\"","- Asterisk marks Kant's footnote (next passage)."]],"krv-a-1127":["*) What I have already said earlier about the psychological idea and its proper determination as a principle for the merely regulative employment of reason spares me the prolixity of discussing separately the transcendental illusion by which that systematic unity of all the manifold of inner sense is represented hypostatically. The procedure here is very similar to that which the critique observes in respect of the theological ideal.",["- \"Bestimmung\" = determination; \"Critik\" = critique; \"hypostatisch vorgestellt\" = \"represented hypostatically.\"","- \"iene systematische Einheit\" taken as the unity of inner sense's manifold, as the sentence itself states."]],"krv-a-1128":["\"But in this way,\" it will be continued, \"we can, after all, assume a single, wise and almighty author of the world?\" Without any doubt; and not only this, but we must presuppose such an author. \"But do we not then extend our knowledge beyond the field of possible experience?\" By no means. For we have presupposed only a something of which we have no concept what it is in itself (a merely transcendental object). But in relation to the systematic and purposive order of the world-structure, which we must presuppose when we study nature, we have thought that being, unknown to us, only in analogy with an intelligence (an empirical concept). That is, in respect of the ends and the perfection which rest upon it, we have thought it as endowed precisely with those properties which, according to the conditions of our reason, can contain the ground of such a systematic unity. This idea is therefore, relatively to the employment of our reason in regard to the world, wholly well grounded.\n\nIf, however, we wished to grant it objective validity without qualification, we should forget that it is merely a being in the idea that we think. Since we should then be starting from a ground that cannot at all be determined through the contemplation of the world, we should be rendered unable to apply this principle in a manner appropriate to the empirical employment of reason.",["- Supplied quotation marks and \"it will be continued\" to mark the interlocutor's questions, which in the German are only indicated by parenthesis and question marks.","- Divided the sentence beginning \"Denn wir haben…\" and the final long sentence into shorter ones.","- \"Erkentniß\" = knowledge; \"respectiv\" = \"relatively to\"; \"bestimbaren\" read as \"bestimmbaren\" (spelling).","- \"Wesen in der Idee\" = \"being in the idea.\""]],"krv-a-1129":["\"But,\" it will further be asked, \"in this way I can, after all, make use of the concept and presupposition of a supreme being in the rational consideration of the world?\" Yes; it was for this very purpose that this idea was laid at the foundation by reason. \"But may I now regard arrangements analogous to purposes as intentions, deriving them from the divine will, though by means of special dispositions in the world designed for that end?\" Yes, that you may also do; but in such a way that it must be all one to you whether someone says that divine wisdom has ordered everything thus in accordance with its supreme ends, or that the idea of supreme wisdom is a regulative in the investigation of nature and a principle of its systematic and purposive unity according to universal laws of nature, even there where we do not become aware of that unity. That is, where you perceive it, it must be wholly indifferent to you whether you say, God has wisely willed it so, or nature has wisely so ordered it.\n\nFor the greatest systematic and purposive unity, which your reason required as a regulative principle to be laid at the foundation of all investigation of nature, was just what entitled you to lay at the foundation the idea of a supreme intelligence as a schema of the regulative principle. And so much purposiveness as you find in the world in accordance with that idea, so much confirmation have you of the legitimacy of your idea. But since the principle in question had no other aim than to seek a necessary and greatest possible unity of nature, we shall indeed have to thank the idea of a supreme being for this unity so far as we attain it. Yet we cannot, without contradicting ourselves, pass by the universal laws of nature, in view of which alone the idea was laid at the foundation, and regard this purposiveness of nature as contingent and hyperphysical in its origin. For we were not entitled to assume a being above nature with the properties mentioned, but only to lay its idea at the foundation, in order, by analogy with a causal determination, to regard appearances as systematically connected with one another.",["- Supplied quotation marks and \"it will further be asked\" for the interlocutor's questions.","- Divided the final long sentences; \"da aber…so werden wir\" rendered as \"But since…we shall.\"","- \"iene\" in \"wo wir iene nicht gewahr werden\" taken to refer to the unity (not wisdom); a possible alternative antecedent is \"Weisheit,\" so doubtful. I rendered \"that unity\" and clarified by \"That is…\".","- \"zweckähnliche Anordnungen\" = \"arrangements analogous to purposes\"; \"Caussalbestimmung\" = \"causal determination.\"","- \"Vorausfetzung\" read as \"Voraussetzung\" (OCR slip); \"antreft\" read as \"antrefft.\"","- \"Rechtmässigkeit\" = \"legitimacy\"; \"entitled\" for berechtigen, in keeping with the method."]],"krv-a-1130":["For this reason we are also entitled to think the cause of the world, in the idea, not merely in terms of a subtler anthropomorphism (without which nothing at all could be thought of it), that is, as a being which has understanding, feelings of pleasure and displeasure, and a desire and will corresponding to these, and so on. We are entitled also to ascribe to it infinite perfection, which therefore far exceeds anything that our empirical knowledge of the order of the world can entitle us to assert. For the regulative law of systematic unity requires that we should study nature as if systematic and purposive unity, combined with the greatest possible manifoldness, were to be met with everywhere to infinity. Though we shall discover or attain but little of this perfection of the world, it belongs to the legislation of our reason to seek and to presume it everywhere. It must always be advantageous to us, and can never be detrimental, to direct our study of nature in accordance with this principle.\n\nBut it is also clear that, on this way of representing the matter, with the idea of a supreme author as its foundation, what I take as ground is not the existence and knowledge of such a being, but only the idea of it. Strictly, therefore, I derive nothing from this being; I derive only from the idea of it, that is, from the nature of the things of the world, in accordance with such an idea. A certain consciousness, though undeveloped, of the genuine use of this concept of our reason seems, moreover, to have prompted the modest and reasonable language of philosophers of all times. They speak of the wisdom and foresight of nature and of divine wisdom as equivalent expressions; indeed, so long as speculative reason alone is concerned, they prefer the former expression. For it checks the pretension to a greater assertion than we are warranted in making, and at the same time directs reason back to its own proper field, nature.",["- Restated the first long sentence as several; \"Denn ... Denn\" rendered as \"For ... Though\" to avoid repeating the particle.","- \"Anthropomorphism\" kept as in the text; \"Wolgefallen und Mißfallen\" rendered \"feelings of pleasure and displeasure\" (sense supplied by \"Begierde\").","- \"unter dieser Vorstellung\" rendered \"on this way of representing the matter\"; \"Vorstellung\" here is not the technical \"representation\".","- \"Kentniß\" rendered \"knowledge\"; \"Vernunftbegriff\" as \"concept of our reason\"."]],"krv-a-1131":["Thus pure reason, which at first seemed to promise nothing less than the extension of knowledge beyond all limits of experience, contains, if we rightly understand it, nothing but regulative principles. These prescribe, indeed, a greater unity than the empirical employment of the understanding can attain. But by setting the goal of its approximation so far off, they carry to the highest degree the agreement of that employment with itself through systematic unity. If, on the other hand, they are misunderstood and are taken for constitutive principles of transcendent modes of knowledge, they give rise, through an illusion which is dazzling but deceptive, to persuasion and imaginary knowledge, and therewith to everlasting contradictions and disputes.",["- Divided the single sentence into three; \"desselben\" (twice) resolved as \"the empirical employment of the understanding\".","- \"Kentnisse\" rendered \"knowledge\"; \"transscendenter Erkentnisse\" as \"transcendent modes of knowledge\" (plural).","- \"Wissen\" rendered \"knowledge\" in \"imaginary knowledge\" (eingebildetes Wissen)."]],"krv-a-1132":["All human knowledge begins with intuitions, proceeds from thence to concepts, and ends with ideas. Although in respect of all three elements it has a priori sources of knowledge, which at first sight seem to scorn the limits of all experience, a completed critique convinces us that all reason in its speculative employment can never, with these elements, advance beyond the field of possible experience. It convinces us that the proper vocation of this supreme faculty of knowledge is to employ all methods, and the principles of these methods, solely in order to penetrate to the inmost secrets of nature in accordance with every possible principle of unity, of which that of purposes is the most important. It is never to venture beyond nature's boundary, outside which there is nothing for us but empty space.\n\nThe critical examination of all the propositions which can extend our knowledge beyond actual experience has, it is true, sufficiently convinced us in the Transcendental Analytic that such propositions can never lead to anything more than a possible experience. If we were not distrustful even of the clearest abstract and universal doctrines, and if alluring and plausible prospects did not tempt us to throw off the constraint of those doctrines, we might indeed have been spared the laborious cross-examination of all the dialectical witnesses which a transcendent reason brings forward in support of its pretensions. For we already knew beforehand with complete certainty that all its professions, though perhaps honestly meant, must be absolutely null, since they concerned a kind of information which no man can ever obtain.\n\nBut there is no end of talking unless we discover the true cause of the illusion by which even the most rational can be deceived. Moreover, the resolution of all our transcendent knowledge into its elements (as a study of our inner nature) has in itself no small value, and for the philosopher is indeed a duty. It was therefore necessary to investigate in detail, down to its first sources, this whole labour of speculative reason, vain though it be. And since the dialectical illusion is here deceptive not only in respect of judgment but also, through the interest we take in judgment, alluring, and is at all times natural and always will remain so, it was advisable to draw up, as it were, the records of this process in full, and to deposit them in the archives of human reason, so as to prevent future errors of a similar kind.",["- Divided into four sentences from a single long period; the second and third paragraph-sentences were restructured.","- \"Erkenntnißquellen\" = \"sources of knowledge\"; \"Erkenntnißvermögen\" = \"faculty of knowledge\"; \"Bestimmung\" here = \"vocation\", not \"determination\", a choice from context.","- \"kläreste oder abstracte\" is awkward in the German (\"oder\" probably \"even\"); rendered \"clearest abstract and universal doctrines\". The reading is doubtful.","- \"Kundschaft\" rendered \"information\", picking up the legal metaphor of witnesses.","- \"Interesse ... am Urtheile\" rendered \"interest we take in judgment\"."]],"krv-a-1133":["II. Transcendental Doctrine of Method.",["- Heading only; \"Methodenlehre\" = \"Doctrine of Method\", following Kemp Smith's usage."]],"krv-a-1134":["If I regard the sum of all pure and speculative knowledge as an edifice of which we have at least the idea in us, I may say that in the Transcendental Doctrine of Elements we have reckoned the building materials and determined for what sort of edifice, of what height and strength, they suffice. It did indeed turn out that although we had in mind a tower which should reach to heaven, the store of materials sufficed only for a dwelling-house, just roomy enough for our business on the plane of experience and high enough to survey it. The bold undertaking was bound to fail through lack of material, quite apart from the confusion of tongues, which must inevitably have set the workers at variance over the plan, and have scattered them over the world, each to build separately according to his own design.\n\nAt present our concern is not so much with the materials as with the plan. We are warned not to venture upon some arbitrary, blind design which might perhaps exceed our whole capacity; yet we cannot well forgo the erection of a secure dwelling-place. We must therefore make our plan for an edifice in proportion to the store of materials which is given to us and which is also adapted to our needs.",["- Divided into two paragraphs/sentence groups; the last sentence's construction (Anschlag ... zu machen) was recast, with \"we must therefore\" supplied to carry the sense.","- \"Bauzeug\" = \"building materials\"; \"Elementarlehre\" = \"Doctrine of Elements\".","- \"Vermögen\" rendered \"capacity\"."]],"krv-a-1135":["By the Transcendental Doctrine of Method I accordingly understand the determination of the formal conditions of a complete system of pure reason. With this end in view we shall have to deal with a discipline, a canon, an architectonic, and finally a history of pure reason. In the transcendental sphere we shall perform what, under the name of a practical logic, is sought in the schools, but ill performed, in regard to the employment of the understanding in general. The reason is this. General logic is not limited to any particular kind of knowledge of the understanding (for instance, not to the pure), nor to certain objects. Unless it borrows knowledge from other sciences, it can do no more than propound the titles of possible methods and the technical expressions which are employed, in regard to what is systematic, in all manner of sciences. These make the learner acquainted beforehand with names, whose meaning and use he is only afterwards to learn.",["- Restated one sentence as several; \"weil, da ...\" resolved as \"The reason is this\".","- \"Verstandeserkentniß\" = \"knowledge of the understanding\"; \"Kentnisse\" = \"knowledge\".","- \"in transscendentaler Absicht\" rendered \"in the transcendental sphere\" (literally \"with transcendental intent\")."]],"krv-a-1136":["First Chapter of the Transcendental Doctrine of Method. The Discipline of Pure Reason.",["- \"Hauptstück\" = \"Chapter\"."]],"krv-a-1137":["Negative judgments, that is, those which are negative not merely in logical form but also in content, are not held in any special esteem by those who are eager for knowledge. They are even regarded as jealous enemies of our unceasing impulse to extend our knowledge, and it almost requires an apology to secure for them mere toleration, still more to win for them favour and high regard.",["- Divided into two sentences; \"Wißbegierde der Menschen\" rendered \"those who are eager for knowledge\"; \"Erkenntnißtrieb\" = \"impulse to extend our knowledge\"."]],"krv-a-1138":["Logically, we can express any propositions we please in negative form; but where the content of our knowledge is concerned, and the question is whether a judgment extends or limits that knowledge, negative propositions have the peculiar function of solely preventing error. Hence negative propositions which are meant to prevent a false knowledge, in cases where error is never possible, are indeed very true, but empty; that is, they are quite unsuited to their purpose, and for that very reason are often ridiculous. An example is the well-known statement of the schoolmaster-orator, that Alexander could not have conquered countries without an army.",["- \"die verneinende\" (sc. Sätze) is rendered \"negative propositions\"; the plural verb *haben* shows this is the antecedent.","- The second sentence (\"Daher auch negative Sätze … seyn\") is grammatically loose; I have smoothed it into a regular construction.","- \"Wie der Satz…\" is a sentence fragment; I have supplied \"An example is\" to make it a sentence.","- \"Schulredners\": rendered \"schoolmaster-orator\", the sense being a rhetorician of the schools."]],"krv-a-1139":["But where the limits of our possible knowledge are very narrow, the incentive to judge is strong, the illusion that presents itself is very deceptive, and the harm arising from error is considerable, the negative element in instruction, which serves solely to guard us against errors, is of even more importance than much positive teaching by which our knowledge might receive increase. The constraint through which the constant propensity to deviate from certain rules is restricted, and finally extirpated, is entitled discipline. It is distinct from culture, which is meant merely to procure a certain skill, without abolishing another already present. In the formation of a talent which has of itself an impulse to express itself, discipline will therefore make a negative,*) culture and doctrine, however, a positive contribution.",["- The long opening sentence has its parallel clauses retained, but I have supplied \"the harm arising from error\" for \"der Nachtheil aus dem Irrthum\".","- \"Unterweisung\" = instruction; \"Belehrung\" = teaching. \"Zwang\" = constraint. \"Man nennet … die Disciplin\" rendered \"is entitled discipline\".","- \"Zuwachs bekommen\" rendered \"receive increase\"; \"Erkentniß\" here is knowledge in the singular.","- The asterisk refers to the footnote in the next passage."]],"krv-a-1140":["*) I am well aware that in the language of the schools the name discipline is customarily used as equivalent to instruction. But there are, on the other hand, so many other cases in which the former expression, as *Zucht* [training, discipline], is carefully distinguished from the latter, as *Belehrung* [teaching], and the nature of things itself requires that the only suitable expressions be preserved for this distinction, that I wish it might never be permitted to use the former word in any but a negative meaning.",["- Footnote retained as such. The German terms are inserted in square brackets, since the argument depends on the distinction.","- The sentence is long and awkward in German (\"daß ich wünsche…\" depends on \"so viele … Fälle\"); I have kept it as one sentence but resolved the construction.","- \"ienes Wort\" (that word) refers to \"Disciplin\", which I have rendered as \"the former word\" to avoid ambiguity."]],"krv-a-1141":["That temperament, and likewise those talents which readily permit themselves a free and unrestricted movement (such as imagination and wit), stand in some respects in need of discipline, everyone will readily admit. That reason, however, whose proper duty it is to prescribe its discipline to all other endeavours, should itself require such discipline, may indeed seem strange; and in fact it has hitherto escaped such humiliation just because, in view of the solemnity and the studied dignity with which it comes forward, no one could easily fall under suspicion of a frivolous play with fancies instead of concepts, and with words instead of things.",["- The two *Daß*-clauses are subjects of the sentences; I have kept the German order for emphasis and clarity.","- \"gründlichen Anstande\" rendered \"studied dignity\"; \"gründlich\" has the sense of an appearance of thoroughness, and the rendering is a judgment.","- \"Einbildungen\" rendered \"fancies\"; \"Einbildungskraft\" earlier is \"imagination\"."]],"krv-a-1142":["Reason in its empirical employment stands in need of no critique, because its principles are subjected to continual testing on the touchstone of experience. Nor is critique required in mathematics, where its concepts must at once be exhibited *in concreto* in pure intuition, and everything unfounded or arbitrary is thereby immediately made evident. But where neither empirical nor pure intuition keeps reason in a visible track, that is, in its transcendental employment in accordance with mere concepts, it stands so greatly in need of a discipline to restrain its propensity to extension beyond the narrow limits of possible experience, and to keep it from extravagance and error, that the whole philosophy of pure reason is concerned solely with this negative utility. Particular errors can be remedied by censure, and their causes by critique. But where, as in pure reason, a whole system of illusions and deceptions is met with, well connected with one another and united under common principles, a quite special, and indeed negative, legislation seems to be required. Under the name of a discipline, this legislation must erect, upon the nature of reason and of the objects of its pure employment, a kind of system of precaution and self-examination, before which no false sophistical illusion can stand, but which must at once, in spite of all the grounds that may be urged in its excuse, betray itself.",["- \"Critik\" is rendered \"critique\" throughout, and \"Censur\" \"censure\".","- The final relative clause is long; its construction is retained, but I have supplied \"this legislation must\" to carry the sense of \"welche … errichte\". The clause \"sondern sich … verrathen muß\" has the illusion as its subject, which I have made explicit in \"betray itself\" with the intervening clause.","- \"Schein\" = illusion; \"Täuschungen und Blendwerken\" = illusions and deceptions (the pair is hard to separate in English).","- \"in concreto\" kept in Latin and italicised."]],"krv-a-1143":["It must, however, be carefully noted that in this second main division of the transcendental critique I direct the discipline of pure reason not to the content but solely to the method of knowledge from pure reason. The former has already been done in the Doctrine of Elements. But the employment of reason has so much that is similar, to whatever object it may be applied, and yet, so far as it is to be transcendental, is at the same time so essentially distinct from every other, that without the warning negative doctrine of a discipline specially directed to that end, the errors cannot be avoided which must necessarily arise from an unsuitable adoption of methods which are in other respects appropriate to reason, but not here.",["- \"Elementarlehre\" rendered \"Doctrine of Elements\" (the title of the first main part).","- The last sentence is retained as one, though I have simplified the clause \"die … nothwendig entspringen müssen\".","- \"Hauptheile\" = main division."]],"krv-a-1144":["Chapter I, Section I. The Discipline of Pure Reason in its Dogmatic Employment.",["- \"Des ersten Hauptstücks Erster Abschnitt\" rendered as a heading in the form \"Chapter I, Section I\", with \"Hauptstück\" as chapter."]],"krv-a-1145":["Mathematics furnishes the most brilliant example of a pure reason successfully extending itself of its own accord, without the aid of experience. Examples are contagious, especially for the same faculty, which naturally flatters itself that it will have the same good fortune in other cases as has fallen to its lot in one. Hence pure reason hopes to be able to extend itself in its transcendental employment as successfully and as thoroughly as it has done in the mathematical, especially if it applies there the same method which has been of such evident utility here. It is therefore of great importance to us to know whether the method of arriving at apodeictic certainty, which in the latter science is entitled mathematical, is identical with that by which we seek the same certainty in philosophy, and which would have to be entitled there dogmatic.",["- \"Vermögen\" is rendered \"faculty\" here.","- \"dasselbe Glück … welches\" is turned into \"the same good fortune … as has fallen to its lot\".","- \"apodictischer\" = apodeictic (Kemp Smith's spelling).","- \"genant werden müßte\" rendered \"would have to be entitled\"."]],"krv-a-1146":["Philosophical knowledge is knowledge gained by reason from concepts; mathematical knowledge is knowledge gained by reason from the construction of concepts. To construct a concept means to exhibit *a priori* the intuition which corresponds to the concept. For the construction of a concept we therefore need a non-empirical intuition. The latter must, as intuition, be a single object, and yet none the less, as the construction of a concept (a universal representation), it must in its representation express universal validity for all possible intuitions which fall under the same concept. Thus I construct a triangle by exhibiting the object which corresponds to this concept, either through imagination alone, in pure intuition, or in accordance with pure intuition on paper, in empirical intuition; in both cases completely *a priori*, without having borrowed the pattern from any experience. The single figure drawn is empirical, and yet serves to express the concept without impairing its universality. For in this empirical intuition we consider only the act of constructing the concept, to which many determinations, for instance those of magnitude, of the sides and of the angles, are quite indifferent, and we therefore abstract from these differences, which do not alter the concept of the triangle.",["- \"Vernunfterkentniß\" rendered \"knowledge gained by reason\"; \"Erkentniß\" as knowledge.","- The sentence on the triangle was long; I have divided it at \"Die einzelne…\" as in the German, and divided the first long sentence into two (\"Zur Construction … wird\" is kept separate).","- \"Bestimmungen\" = determinations.","- \"nach derselben\" rendered \"in accordance with pure intuition\", i.e. following the pure intuition."]],"krv-a-1147":["Philosophical knowledge thus considers the particular only in the universal, mathematical knowledge the universal in the particular, or even in the single instance, yet none the less *a priori* and by means of reason. Hence, just as this single instance is determined under certain universal conditions of construction, the object of the concept, to which this single instance corresponds merely as its schema, must likewise be thought as universally determined.",["- \"bestimt\" rendered \"determined\", consistently with *Bestimmung*.","- \"Einzelnen\" rendered \"single instance\"; \"Besondere\" as \"particular\".","- I have divided the final sentence of the original into two."]],"krv-a-1148":["It is in this form, then, that the essential difference between these two kinds of knowledge of reason consists. It does not rest on a difference in their matter, that is, in their objects. Those who have thought to distinguish philosophy from mathematics by saying that the former has as its object quality only, the latter quantity only, have mistaken the effect for the cause. The form of mathematical knowledge is the reason why it can relate solely to quanta. For only the concept of magnitudes allows of being constructed, that is, exhibited *a priori* in intuition; qualities, on the other hand, cannot be exhibited save in empirical intuition. Rational knowledge of them is therefore possible only through concepts. Thus no one can obtain from any source other than experience an intuition corresponding to the concept of reality; never can he come to be in possession of it *a priori*, from himself, and prior to empirical consciousness of it. The conical shape we can make intuitable without any empirical aid, simply in accordance with the concept; but the colour of this cone must first have been given in some experience or other. The concept of a cause in general I can exhibit in intuition in no way save in an example which experience supplies to me, and so forth.\n\nPhilosophy, however, deals with magnitudes just as much as mathematics does, for instance with totality and infinity. Mathematics, again, concerns itself with the difference between lines and planes, as spaces of different quality, and with the continuity of extension as a quality of it. But though in such cases they have an object in common, the manner of treating it through reason is quite different in philosophical and in mathematical consideration. The former keeps solely to universal concepts. The latter can accomplish nothing with the mere concept, but hastens at once to intuition, in which it considers the concept *in concreto*; yet not empirically, but only in an intuition which it has exhibited *a priori*, that is, has constructed, and in which whatever follows from the universal conditions of the construction must hold universally also of the object of the concept constructed.",["- Restated the long final sentence as two sentences (the break at \"Philosophy, however…\"), and paragraphed the passage; the German runs on.","- \"Vernunfterkentniß\" rendered \"knowledge of reason\" in the first sentence, \"rational knowledge\" later (\"eine Vernunfterkentniß derselben\").","- \"Dieienige, welche … vermeineten\" smoothed to \"Those who have thought to distinguish\".","- \"Obiect\" and \"Gegenstände\" both \"object\"; \"Qualitäten\" rendered \"qualities\", \"Grössen\" \"magnitudes\".","- \"anschauend machen\" rendered \"make intuitable\"; \"darlegen/darstellen\" rendered \"exhibit\".","- \"Die Art, ihn … zu behandeln\": \"it\" (ihn) refers to the common object, \"Gegenstand\".","- \"Jene … diese\" resolved as \"The former … the latter\" (philosophy, mathematics)."]],"krv-a-1149":["Let a philosopher be given the concept of a triangle, and let him try, in his own fashion, to discover how the sum of its angles may be related to a right angle. He has nothing but the concept of a figure enclosed by three straight lines, and, in it, the concept of an equal number of angles. He may reflect on this concept as long as he pleases; he will never produce anything new. He can analyse and make distinct the concept of a straight line, or of an angle, or of the number three, but he cannot arrive at other properties which do not lie in these concepts at all.\n\nBut let the geometer take up this question. He begins at once by constructing a triangle. Since he knows that two right angles together are exactly equal to all the adjacent angles which can be drawn from one point on a straight line, he prolongs one side of his triangle and obtains two adjacent angles, which together are equal to two right angles. He then divides the external one of these angles by drawing a line parallel to the opposite side of the triangle, and sees that there arises here an external adjacent angle which is equal to an internal angle; and so on. In this way, through a chain of inferences, always guided by intuition, he arrives at a completely evident and at the same time universal solution of the question.",["- Divided at \"Allein der Geometer…\" into a new paragraph; otherwise sentence structure is kept.","- \"berührende Winkel\" rendered \"adjacent angles\" (Kemp Smith's usage); \"ausfündig machen\" \"discover\".","- \"Nun theilet er den äusseren … indem\": \"divides\" is retained, though the sense is that the parallel line divides the external angle.","- \"zum rechten\" read as \"to a right angle\" (the right angle as measure)."]],"krv-a-1150":["Mathematics, however, constructs not only magnitudes (*quanta*), as in geometry, but also magnitude as such (*quantitas*), as in algebra, where it abstracts entirely from the character of the object which is to be thought in accordance with such a concept of magnitude. It then chooses for itself a certain notation for all constructions of magnitudes in general (numbers, as in addition, subtraction, and so on; extraction of roots). Having thus also symbolised the universal concept of magnitudes in respect of their different relations, it exhibits in intuition, in accordance with certain universal rules, every procedure through which magnitude is generated and altered. Where one magnitude is to be divided by another, it combines the signs of both in accordance with the symbol for division, and so on. By means of a symbolic construction it thus arrives, just as geometry does by an ostensive or geometrical construction (of the objects themselves), at results which discursive knowledge by means of mere concepts could never reach.",["- Restated the long sentence as several; the German's dash-like parenthesis \"(Zahlen, als der Addition, Subtraction u. s. w.), Ausziehung der Wurzel\" is awkward, and I read it as listing operations, with \"extraction of roots\" added to the list.","- \"Buchstabenrechnung\" rendered \"algebra\" (literally calculation with letters).","- \"Bezeichnung/bezeichnen\" rendered \"notation\"/\"symbolise\" to tie in with \"symbolischen Construction\".","- \"Charactere\" rendered \"signs\".","- \"Behandlung\" rendered \"procedure\".","- \"Gegenstandes\" with \"Beschaffenheit\": \"character\"."]],"krv-a-1151":["What may be the cause of this so different situation in which two exponents of reason find themselves, the one taking his way by concepts, the other by intuitions which he exhibits *a priori* in accordance with the concepts? From the transcendental doctrines expounded above, the cause is clear. What is here in question is not analytic propositions, which can be generated through mere analysis of concepts (in this the philosopher would undoubtedly have the advantage over his rival), but synthetic propositions, and indeed such as are to be known *a priori*. For I am not to attend to what I actually think in my concept of a triangle (this is nothing more than the mere definition); I am rather to go beyond it to properties which do not lie in this concept, yet belong to it.\n\nNow this is possible only if I determine my object in accordance with the conditions either of empirical intuition or of pure intuition. The former would yield only an empirical proposition (obtained by measuring its angles), containing neither universality nor, still less, necessity; and propositions of that kind are not at all in question. The second procedure, however, is mathematical, and here geometrical, construction; by means of it I add, in a pure intuition just as in the empirical, the manifold which belongs to the schema of a triangle in general, and therefore to its concept. Universal synthetic propositions must in this way be the outcome.",["- \"Vernunftkünstler\" rendered \"exponents of reason\"; literally \"artists of reason\".","- Divided the last long sentences and made a paragraph at \"Now this is possible…\".","- \"Nun ist dieses nicht anders möglich, als daß…\" rendered \"possible only if\".","- \"von dergleichen ist gar nicht die Rede\" rendered \"propositions of that kind are not at all in question\"; \"dergleichen\" read as referring to empirical propositions.","- \"wodurch allerdings … werden müssen\": rendered \"must in this way be the outcome\", the \"allerdings\" stressing necessity.","- \"Schema\" kept in English form, as in the German."]],"krv-a-1152":["In vain, then, should I philosophise about the triangle, that is, reflect discursively, since I should get no further than the mere definition, with which, however, I must justly begin. There is indeed a transcendental synthesis from concepts alone, which again only the philosopher can achieve; but it concerns never more than a thing in general, and the conditions under which the perception of it can belong to possible experience. In mathematical problems, however, there is no question of this, nor of existence at all, but of the properties of objects in themselves, solely in so far as these are connected with the concept of them.",["- \"billig\" rendered \"justly\" (i.e. properly); \"must\" for \"müßte\" in the conditional.","- \"welchen Bedingungen\" resolved: \"the conditions under which\" the perception of such a thing can belong to possible experience.","- \"an sich selbst\" rendered \"in themselves\" in a loose sense, as the context concerns properties connected with the concept.","- \"die aber niemals mehr als ein Ding überhaupt betrift\": \"it concerns never more than a thing in general\" kept close to the German, with the clause after \"unter welchen Bedingungen\" given as a coordinate phrase."]],"krv-a-1153":["In the example cited we have sought only to make clear what a great difference is to be met with between the discursive employment of reason in accordance with concepts and the intuitive employment through the construction of concepts. The question now naturally arises what the cause is that makes such a twofold employment of reason necessary, and by what conditions we can recognise whether the first alone, or the second also, is in place.",["- \"Vernunftgebrauch\" rendered \"employment of reason\"; \"statt finde\" rendered \"is in place\".","- \"an welchen Bedingungen man erkennen könne\" rendered \"by what conditions we can recognise\"."]],"krv-a-1154":["All our knowledge relates in the end to possible intuitions, for through these alone is an object given. Now a concept *a priori* (a non-empirical concept) either already contains in itself a pure intuition, and in that case it can be constructed; or it contains nothing but the synthesis of possible intuitions which are not given *a priori*. In the latter case we can indeed judge synthetically and *a priori* by means of it, but only discursively, in accordance with concepts, and never intuitively through construction of the concept.",["- Divided \"Nun enthält … entweder … oder\" at the semicolon for clarity; \"alsdenn … kan man wol zwar\" rendered \"In the latter case we can indeed\".","- \"zulezt\" rendered \"in the end\".","- \"Erkentniß\" here is \"knowledge\" in the singular, as in \"Alle unsere Erkentniß\"."]],"krv-a-1155":["Now of all intuition none is given a priori save the mere form of appearances, space and time; and a concept of these, as *quanta*, can be exhibited a priori in intuition, that is, constructed, either together with their quality (their shape), or merely in respect of their quantity (the mere synthesis of the homogeneous manifold) through number. The matter of appearances, however, through which things in space and time are given to us, can be represented only in perception, and so a posteriori. The one concept that represents a priori this empirical content of appearances is the concept of a thing in general. The synthetic knowledge of a thing in general, obtainable a priori, can yield nothing more than the mere rule for the synthesis of what perception may give a posteriori. It can never yield the intuition of the real object a priori, since this must necessarily be empirical.",["- Restated the first sentence's construction (\"und ein Begriff von diesen ... läßt sich ... darstellen\") as one sentence with a semicolon; the sense is that the concept of space and time, as quanta, can be constructed.","- \"Gleichartigmannigfaltigen\" rendered \"homogeneous manifold\". \"Wahrnehmung\" = perception.","- Divided the last long sentence into two; \"diese\" in the final clause taken to refer to \"Anschauung\"."]],"krv-a-1156":["Synthetic propositions which relate to things in general, the intuition of which cannot be given a priori at all, are transcendental. Transcendental propositions can therefore never be given through construction of concepts, but only in accordance with concepts a priori. They contain merely the rule by which a certain synthetic unity of that which cannot be intuitively represented a priori (namely, perceptions) is to be sought empirically. They cannot, however, exhibit a priori any single one of their concepts in any case whatever. They do this only a posteriori, by means of experience, which first becomes possible in accordance with those synthetic principles.",["- Split the final sentence into two for clarity. \"Grundsätze\" = principles. \"nach Begriffen a priori\" rendered \"in accordance with concepts a priori\"."]],"krv-a-1157":["If we are to judge synthetically about a concept, we must go outside this concept, and indeed to the intuition in which it is given. For if we remained with what is contained in the concept, the judgment would be merely analytic, an explanation of the thought in terms of what is actually contained in it. But I can proceed from the concept to the pure or empirical intuition corresponding to it, in order to consider it in that intuition *in concreto*, and to know, a priori or a posteriori, what belongs to its object. The former is rational and mathematical knowledge through the construction of the concept; the latter is merely empirical (mechanical) knowledge, which can never yield necessary and apodeictic propositions. Thus I might analyse my empirical concept of gold without gaining anything more than the ability to enumerate all that I actually think in using this word. This would effect a logical improvement in my knowledge, but no increase or addition would be acquired. I take, however, the matter which appears under this name, and institute perceptions with it, which will put into my hands various synthetic, but empirical, propositions. The mathematical concept of a triangle I should construct, that is, give a priori in intuition, and in this way obtain synthetic, but rational, knowledge. But when the transcendental concept of a reality, substance, force, etc., is given to me, it designates neither an empirical nor a pure intuition, but solely the synthesis of empirical intuitions (which cannot, therefore, be given a priori). Since the synthesis cannot proceed a priori to the intuition corresponding to the concept, no determining synthetic proposition can arise from it, but only a principle of the synthesis* of possible empirical intuitions. A transcendental proposition is therefore a synthetic knowledge of reason in accordance with mere concepts, and so discursive; for through it all synthetic unity of empirical knowledge is first made possible, but no intuition is given a priori.",["- Divided the long sentences beginning \"So könte ich\" and \"Aber, wenn mir\" into several. In the latter, the \"weil\" clause was moved ahead of the main clause to keep the argument clear.","- \"Erkentniß\" = knowledge; \"Erkentniß\" in \"meinem Erkentniß\" is neuter in the German and taken as \"my knowledge\".","- The asterisk after \"synthesis\" marks Kant's footnote, translated in the next passage.","- \"Vernunfterkentniß\" = \"knowledge of reason\"; \"in concreto\" kept in Latin."]],"krv-a-1158":["*) By means of the concept of cause I do actually go outside the empirical concept of an event (something happening), but not to the intuition which exhibits the concept of cause *in concreto*. I go to the time-conditions in general, which in experience might be found to conform to the concept of cause. I proceed, therefore, merely in accordance with concepts, and cannot proceed by construction of concepts, since the concept is a rule of the synthesis of perceptions, which are not pure intuitions and so cannot be given a priori.",["- \"Begebenheit\" = event. \"möchten\" rendered \"might\"."]],"krv-a-1159":["There is thus a twofold employment of reason. In spite of the universality of knowledge and its generation a priori, which the two have in common, they differ greatly in their procedure. The reason is that in the appearance, through which all objects are given to us, there are two elements: the form of intuition (space and time), which can be known and determined completely a priori; and the matter (the physical element), or content, which signifies something that is met with in space and time, and so contains an existence and corresponds to sensation. In respect of the latter, which can never be given in determinate fashion save empirically, we can have nothing a priori except indeterminate concepts of the synthesis of possible sensations, so far as they belong to the unity of apperception (in a possible experience). In respect of the former, we can determine our concepts a priori in intuition, since in space and time we create the objects themselves through a uniform synthesis, regarding them merely as *quanta*. The first is entitled the employment of reason in accordance with concepts, since we can do nothing more than bring appearances, in respect of their real content, under concepts, which can thereafter be determined only empirically, that is, a posteriori (though in conformity with those concepts as rules of an empirical synthesis). The second is the employment of reason through construction of concepts, since these concepts, being already directed to an intuition a priori, can for that very reason be given determinately a priori in pure intuition, and without any empirical data. To consider everything that is (a thing in space or time): whether and how far it is a *quantum* or not; whether an existence or a lack of it must be represented in it; how far this something (which fills space or time) is a primary substratum or a mere determination; whether it has a relation of its existence to something else, as cause or effect; whether, finally, it stands isolated or in reciprocal dependence with others in respect of existence; and to consider the possibility of this existence, its actuality and necessity, or their opposites: all this belongs to knowledge of reason from concepts, which is entitled philosophical. But to determine an intuition a priori in space (shape), to divide time (duration), or merely to know the universal element in the synthesis of one and the same thing in time and space, and the magnitude of an intuition in general which arises from it (number): that is a business of reason through construction of concepts, and is entitled mathematical.",["- Divided the first sentence into two. \"Jener ... dieser\" resolved as \"the first ... the second\", referring to the two employments of reason.","- \"Sehalt\" in the text is an OCR slip, silently corrected to \"Gehalt\" (content). \"ist(ein\" spacing corrected.","- \"Tauer\" read as \"Dauer\" (duration). \"Daseyn\" = existence; \"Gestalt\" = shape.","- \"Physische\" rendered \"physical element\"; \"Mangel\" as \"lack\".","- The long enumeration after \"Alles, was da ist\" is governed by \"zu erwägen\" and \"gehöret\"; I supplied \"whether\" and semicolons to articulate it, without adding content."]],"krv-a-1160":["The great success which reason achieves by means of mathematics naturally gives rise to the presumption that, if not reason itself, at least its method will be equally successful outside the field of quantities. For mathematics brings all its concepts to intuitions which it can give a priori, and by these it becomes, so to speak, master over nature; whereas pure philosophy, with its discursive concepts a priori, bungles about in nature, without being able to make the reality of these concepts credible a priori through intuition. Nor, it would seem, do the masters of this art lack confidence in themselves, or the common people great expectations of their skill, should they ever concern themselves with philosophy. For since they have hardly ever philosophised about their mathematics (a difficult undertaking), the specific difference between the one employment of reason and the other never enters their minds. Current rules, empirically employed and borrowed from common reason, are accepted by them in place of axioms. Where the concepts of space and time, with which they are occupied (as the sole original quanta), may come from, is a matter of no concern to them; and it seems equally unnecessary to them to investigate the origin of pure concepts of the understanding, and thereby also the extent of their validity. They think it enough merely to make use of them. In all this they are quite right, provided they do not overstep their assigned limit, namely that of nature. But in this way they pass unawares from the field of sensibility to the insecure ground of pure and even transcendental concepts, where the ground (*instabilis tellus, innabilis unda*) permits them neither to stand nor to swim, and only fleeting steps can be taken, of which time preserves not the least trace. In mathematics, on the other hand, their course makes a highway which even the latest posterity can still tread with confidence.",[]],"krv-a-1161":["We have made it our duty to determine the limits of pure reason in its transcendental employment exactly and with certainty. But this kind of endeavour has the peculiarity that, in spite of the most emphatic and clearest warnings, it still allows itself to be put off by hope, before the design of advancing beyond the limits of experience into the alluring regions of the intellectual is wholly abandoned. It is therefore necessary to remove, as it were, the last anchor of a fanciful hope, and to show that the following of the mathematical method in this kind of knowledge cannot yield the least advantage. The only result would be to expose its own weaknesses all the more clearly, namely, that geometry [*Meßkunst*] and philosophy are two quite different things, although in natural science they go hand in hand; and consequently that the procedure of the one can never be imitated by the other.",[]],"krv-a-1162":["The thoroughness of mathematics rests on definitions, axioms and demonstrations. I shall content myself with showing that none of these, in the sense in which the mathematician takes them, can be achieved or imitated by philosophy. The geometer, following his method, builds in philosophy only houses of cards; the philosopher, following his, can in the province of mathematics produce only idle talk. Yet philosophy consists precisely in knowing its own limits; and even the mathematician, unless his talent is already limited by nature and confined to his own subject, cannot reject the warnings of philosophy or set himself above them.",["(Notes below cover all three passages.)","- 1160: the opening sentence is divided; the \"for\" before \"mathematics brings\" and the \"Nor, it would seem\" are supplied to make the connexion of the clauses plain. \"Meister über die Natur\" kept as \"master over nature.\"","- 1160: \"sie\" in \"wodurch sie ... Meister wird\" resolved as mathematics (not reason); \"ihrer Methode\" and the closing \"ihr Gang\" likewise refer to mathematics/the mathematicians as the argument requires.","- 1160: \"pfuscht\" rendered \"bungles\"; \"Heeresstrasse\" \"highway\" (literally military road). \"Quantis\" retained as \"quanta.\" The Virgilian-style tag is left in Latin, italicised, untranslated.","- 1160: \"Vernunftgebrauch\" rendered \"employment of reason\"; \"Sinnlichkeit\" = sensibility; \"Verstandesbegriffe\" = concepts of the understanding. I added \"They think it enough\" to render the bare \"sondern nur sich ihrer zu bedienen.\"","- 1161: the very long sentence is divided into four; \"dieser Art der Bestrebung\" is rendered \"this kind of endeavour.\" The construction after \"es müßte denn der seyn\" is irregular, and I have smoothed it into \"The only result would be…\". \"Erkentniß\" = knowledge.","- 1161: \"Meßkunst\" and \"Meßkünstler\" rendered \"geometry\" and \"geometer\" (literally the art of measurement); the German is given in brackets at first occurrence only. \"Naturwissenschaft\" = natural science.","- 1162: the last sentence is divided and the opening \"Daß\" clause, which is syntactically dependent on the preceding \"zeigen,\" is recast as a free-standing statement; \"Antheil\" = \"province.\" \"Kartengebäude\" = \"houses of cards.\""]],"krv-a-1163":["1. *Of Definitions.* To define, as the word itself indicates, should properly mean no more than to present originally the exhaustive concept of a thing within its limits.*) If this be demanded, an empirical concept cannot be defined at all, but only explicated. For since we have in it only a certain number of marks of a certain kind of object of the senses, we can never be sure that by the word which designates the same object we are not thinking now more, now fewer, of its marks. Thus one person, in his concept of gold, may think, in addition to its weight, colour and tenacity, the property that it does not rust, while another may know nothing of this. We make use of certain marks only so long as they suffice for distinguishing; new observations, on the other hand, remove some and add others, and the concept therefore never stands within secure limits. Besides, what would be the use of defining such a concept? When, for instance, water and its properties are under discussion, we do not stop at what we think in using the word water, but proceed to experiments. The word, with the few marks attached to it, is meant to be a designation only and not a concept of the thing; and the alleged definition is therefore nothing but a determination of a word.\n\nSecondly, strictly speaking, no concept given *a priori* can be defined either, for instance, substance, cause, right, equity, etc. For I can never be certain that the distinct representation of a concept given [as yet] in confused form has been exhaustively developed, unless I know that it is adequate to the object. But the concept of the object, as it is given, may contain many obscure representations which we pass over in the analysis, although we always make use of them in applying the concept. The exhaustiveness of the analysis of my concept is therefore always doubtful, and can be made probable only by many fitting examples, never apodeictically certain. Instead of the term \"definition\" I should prefer to use \"exposition\". This term remains cautious, and the critic can accept it up to a certain point and yet still have doubts as to its exhaustiveness. Since, then, concepts given empirically and concepts given *a priori* alike cannot be defined, there remain only concepts arbitrarily thought, on which this artifice can be tried. A concept of this kind I can always define; for I must know what I intended to think, since I made it deliberately myself, and it was given me neither by the nature of the understanding nor by experience. But I cannot say that I have thereby defined a true object. If the concept rests on empirical conditions, for instance, a ship's clock, the object and its possibility are not yet given through this arbitrary concept. I cannot even tell from it whether it has an object at all, and my explanation may better be entitled a declaration (of my project) than a definition of an object.\n\nThus no concepts remain that are fit to be defined save those which contain an arbitrary synthesis capable of being constructed *a priori*; and mathematics alone, therefore, has definitions. For the object which it thinks it also exhibits *a priori* in intuition, and this can certainly contain neither more nor less than the concept, since through the explanation the concept of the object was given originally, that is, without the explanation being derived from anything else.\n\nThe German language has for the terms exposition, explication, declaration and definition nothing but the one word *Erklärung* [explanation]. We must therefore relax somewhat the strictness of our demand, in which we refused to philosophical explanations the honourable title of definition, and limit this whole remark to the following: philosophical definitions are brought about only as expositions of concepts that are given, mathematical definitions as constructions of concepts originally made by us; the former only analytically, through analysis (the completeness of which is not apodeictically certain), the latter synthetically. The latter therefore make the concept itself, whereas the former merely explain it. From this it follows",[]],"krv-a-1164":["*) Exhaustiveness [*Ausführlichkeit*] signifies the clearness and sufficiency of the marks; limits [*Gränzen*], the precision with which there are no more of them than belong to the exhaustive concept; and originally [*ursprünglich*], that this determination of the limits is not derived from anything else, and so does not still require a proof. Were it otherwise, the supposed explanation would be rendered incapable of standing at the head of all judgments about an object.",[]],"krv-a-1165":["(a) that in philosophy we must not imitate mathematics by putting definitions first, except by way of mere experiment. For since definitions are analyses of given concepts, these concepts, though still only confused, come first, and the incomplete exposition precedes the complete. Thus from a few marks which we have drawn from an analysis as yet unfinished we can infer much beforehand, before we have arrived at the complete exposition, that is, the definition. In a word, in philosophy the definition, as exactly measured distinctness, must conclude rather than begin the work.*) In mathematics, on the other hand, we have no concept at all prior to the definition, through which the concept is first given; mathematics must therefore begin with it, and can always do so.",[]],"krv-a-1166":["*) Philosophy swarms with faulty definitions, especially such as do contain genuine elements of definition, but not yet completely. If we could do nothing with a concept until we had defined it, philosophizing would stand very ill. But since, so far as the elements (of the analysis) extend, a good and secure use can always be made of them, defective definitions also, that is, propositions which are not yet properly definitions but are otherwise true and therefore approximations to them, can be used with much profit. In mathematics definition belongs *ad esse*, in philosophy *ad melius esse*. It is fine, but often very difficult, to attain it. Jurists are still seeking a definition for their concept of right.",[]],"krv-a-1167":["(b) Mathematical definitions can never err. For since the concept is first given through the definition, it contains just what the definition intends to have thought through it. But though nothing incorrect can occur in the content, an error may occasionally, though only rarely, occur in the form (the dress), namely in respect of precision. Thus the common explanation of the circle, that it is a curved line all points of which are equidistant from a single point (the centre), has the fault that the determination \"curved\" is introduced needlessly. For there must be a special theorem, deduced from the definition and easily proved, that every line all points of which are equidistant from a single point is curved (no part of it straight). Analytic definitions, on the other hand, may err in many ways: either by introducing marks which did not really lie in the concept, or by lacking that exhaustiveness which constitutes the essential of a definition, since we cannot be quite certain of the completeness of our analysis. For this reason the mathematical method of defining cannot be imitated in philosophy.",["Passages are in order; ids 1163–1167 each appear once.","- 1163: the long sentences on \"Secondly…\" and on the German vocabulary were divided; the sentence on the \"Wasser\" example was restated in two. \"Wortbestimmung\" rendered \"determination of a word\".","- 1163: \"[as yet]\" supplied for \"noch\" in \"(noch verworren) gegeben\"; \"ausführlich\" rendered \"exhaustive\", with *Ausführlichkeit* glossed in the footnote.","- 1163: \"Anmerkung\" is rendered \"remark\"; the passage breaks off at \"Hieraus folgt\", continued in 1165 after the footnote.","- 1163: \"Erklärung\" glossed in square brackets; \"apodictisch\" given as \"apodeictically\".","- 1164: the second clause of the footnote is smoothed (\"Were it otherwise\" supplied to link the *welches* clause); German terms bracketed.","- 1165: \"Deutlichkeit\" rendered \"distinctness\"; \"abgemessene\" as \"exactly measured\". \"Definitionen … seyn\" (\"sie\") taken to refer to the definitions.","- 1166: Latin *ad esse*, *ad melius esse* left in italics.","- 1167: \"den: Inhalte\" read as \"dem Inhalte\" (OCR slip), silently corrected. \"Einkleidung\" rendered \"dress\"."]],"krv-a-1168":["2. Of Axioms. These are synthetic principles *a priori*, in so far as they are immediately certain. Now one concept cannot be connected synthetically with another and yet immediately; for if we are to go beyond a concept, a third, mediating mode of knowledge is required. Philosophy is simply knowledge of reason through concepts, and no principle will therefore be found in it that deserves the name of an axiom. Mathematics, on the other hand, is capable of axioms, because by the construction of concepts in the intuition of the object it can connect the predicates of the object *a priori* and immediately, as, for instance, in the proposition that three points always lie in a plane. A synthetic principle derived solely from concepts, on the other hand, can never be immediately certain. Take, for example, the proposition that everything which happens has its cause. Here I have to look around for something third, namely, the condition of time-determination in an experience; I could not have known such a principle directly and immediately from the concepts alone. Discursive principles are thus quite different from intuitive principles, that is, from axioms. The former always require a deduction, which the latter can altogether dispense with. And since the latter, for the very same reason, are evident, a quality which philosophical principles, with all their certainty, can never pretend to, it is infinitely far from being the case that any synthetic proposition of pure and transcendental reason is as self-evident (to use the arrogant phrase) as the proposition that twice two make four. In the Analytic, in the table of the principles of pure understanding, I did indeed speak also of certain axioms of intuition; but the principle there adduced was not itself an axiom. It served only to state the principle [*Principium*] of the possibility of axioms in general, and was itself no more than a principle derived from concepts. For even the possibility of mathematics must be shown in transcendental philosophy. Philosophy, then, has no axioms, and must never prescribe its *a priori* principles so absolutely; it must rather condescend to justify its title to them by a thorough deduction.",[]],"krv-a-1169":["3. Of Demonstrations. Only an apodeictic proof, in so far as it is intuitive, can be entitled demonstration. Experience teaches us what is, but not that it could not be otherwise. Empirical grounds of proof therefore cannot yield an apodeictic proof. From concepts *a priori* (in discursive knowledge), however, intuitive certainty, that is, evidence, can never arise, however apodeictically certain the judgment may otherwise be. Mathematics alone therefore contains demonstrations, since it derives its knowledge not from concepts but from the construction of them, that is, from intuition, which can be given *a priori* in correspondence with the concepts. Even the procedure of algebra, with its equations, from which by reduction it produces the truth together with the proof, is indeed not a geometrical, but is still a symbolic [*characteristische*] construction. In it the concepts, especially those of the relation of quantities, are exhibited in intuition by signs; and, without even considering the heuristic aspect, all inferences are secured against errors by the fact that each of them is set before the eyes. Philosophical knowledge, on the other hand, must forgo this advantage, since it has always to consider the universal in the abstract (by means of concepts), whereas mathematics can consider the universal in the concrete (in the single intuition), and yet through pure representation *a priori*, whereby every false step becomes visible. [The sentence beginning \"Da hingegen\" has no main clause in the German; I have supplied one.] I should therefore prefer to call the former *acroamatic* (discursive) proofs, since they can be conducted only through words (the object in thought), rather than demonstrations, which, as the expression itself indicates, proceed in the intuition of the object.",[]],"krv-a-1170":["From all this it follows that it is quite unbecoming to the nature of philosophy, especially in the field of pure reason, to strut with a dogmatic air, and to deck itself out with the titles and ribbons of mathematics. To the order of mathematics philosophy does not belong, though it has every reason to hope for a sisterly union with it. Those pretensions are vain claims, which can never succeed. On the contrary, they must defeat their purpose, which is to expose the illusions of a reason that fails to recognise its limits, and, by sufficient clarification of our concepts, to bring the conceit of speculation back to modest but thorough self-knowledge. In its transcendental attempts, reason cannot therefore look so confidently ahead, as if the road it has travelled led quite straight to its goal; nor can it count so boldly on its premisses as to dispense with frequent looking back, to see whether errors may not be discovered in the progress of the inferences, errors which were overlooked in the principles, and which make it necessary either to determine these more precisely or to alter them entirely.",[]],"krv-a-1171":["I divide all apodeictic propositions (whether they be demonstrable or immediately certain) into *dogmata* and *mathemata*. A directly synthetic proposition from concepts is a *dogma*; a proposition of the same kind, through construction of concepts, is a *mathema*. Analytic judgments really teach us nothing more about the object than what the concept we have of it already contains in itself, since they do not extend knowledge beyond the concept of the subject, but only elucidate it. They cannot therefore properly be entitled *dogmata* (a word which might perhaps be translated by \"doctrinal sayings\" [*Lehrsprüche*]). But of the two kinds of synthetic proposition *a priori* just mentioned, only those which belong to philosophical knowledge can, according to ordinary usage, bear this name; and one would hardly call the propositions of arithmetic or geometry *dogmata*. This usage thus confirms the explanation we gave, that only judgments from concepts, and not those from the construction of concepts, can be entitled dogmatic.",[]],"krv-a-1172":["Now the whole of pure reason, in its merely speculative employment, does not contain a single directly synthetic judgment from concepts. For through ideas, as we have shown, it is incapable of any synthetic judgments possessing objective validity. Through concepts of the understanding it does indeed establish secure principles, but not directly from concepts; always only indirectly, through the relation of these concepts to something quite contingent, namely, possible experience. When this (something as object of possible experiences) is presupposed, such principles are indeed apodeictically certain, but in themselves they cannot be known (directly) *a priori* at all. Thus no one can gain insight, from the given concepts alone, into the proposition that everything which happens has its cause. It is therefore not a *dogma*, although from another point of view, namely in the sole field of its possible employment, that is, experience, it can quite well be proved apodeictically. It is entitled a principle [*Grundsatz*] and not a theorem [*Lehrsatz*], although it has to be proved, because it has the peculiar property that it first makes possible its own ground of proof, namely experience, and must always be presupposed in experience.",["- krv-a-1168: \"Erkentniß\" rendered \"mode of knowledge\" where it is a count noun (\"ein drittes vermittelndes Erkenntniß\"); \"Grundsatz\" = principle; \"Principium\" kept in Latin.","- krv-a-1168: The long sentence \"Jene erfodern… als der Satz…\" is divided into several sentences; \"so fehlt unendlich viel daran\" rendered \"it is infinitely far from being the case\".","- krv-a-1168: The \"Satz: alles was geschieht…\" passage is restated in separate sentences; the clause \"da ich mich… herumsehen muß\" is made explicit as \"Here I have to look around…\".","- krv-a-1169: \"Da hingegen das philosophische Erkenntniß…\" is a sentence with no main verb in the German (probably a joining of two drafts). I supplied \"[Philosophical knowledge, on the other hand, must forgo…]\" as a plain main clause; the bracketed note in the text flags this.","- krv-a-1169: \"characteristische Construction\" = symbolic construction; \"acroamatisch\" kept as a transliteration; \"Erkenntniß ableitet\" = \"derives its knowledge\".","- krv-a-1170: \"Jene\" refers to the dogmatic strutting and borrowed mathematical titles; I made this plain as \"Those pretensions\". The final sentence is long and parts were kept but restated with semicolons.","- krv-a-1171: \"directsynthetisch\" = \"directly synthetic\"; \"Dogmata/Mathemata\" kept in Latin italics; \"Lehrsprüche\" glossed in brackets.","- krv-a-1172: \"Gesichtspunct\" = \"point of view\"; the parenthesis \"(etwas als Gegenstand möglicher Erfahrungen)\" is retained; \"Beweisgrund\" = \"ground of proof\"."]],"krv-a-1173":["If, then, there are in the speculative employment of pure reason no dogmas at all, not even as regards content, every dogmatic method is in itself unsuitable, whether it be borrowed from the mathematician or be intended to constitute a manner peculiar to philosophy. For such a method only conceals faults and errors, and deceives philosophy, whose real aim is to let all the steps of reason be seen in the clearest light. Nevertheless the method can always be systematic. For our reason (subjectively) is itself a system; but in its pure employment, by means of mere concepts, it is only a system of investigation according to principles of unity, for which experience alone can supply the material. Of the peculiar method of a transcendental philosophy nothing can be said here, since we are concerned only with a critique of our [powers and their] circumstances (*Vermögensumstände*): whether we can build at all, and how high we may carry our edifice from the material we have, namely the pure concepts *a priori*.",[]],"krv-a-1174":["Second Section of the First Chapter. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Respect of its Polemical Employment.",[]],"krv-a-1175":["In all its undertakings reason must submit to criticism; and if it limits the freedom of criticism by any prohibition, it can do so only to its own harm and by drawing upon itself a damaging suspicion. Nothing is so important in respect of its utility, nothing so sacred, that it may exempt itself from this searching and sifting examination, which knows no respect of persons. The very existence of reason depends upon this freedom. Reason has no dictatorial authority; its verdict is always nothing more than the agreement of free citizens, of whom each must be able to express his misgivings, and even his *veto*, without let or hindrance.",[]],"krv-a-1176":["But though reason can never refuse itself to criticism, it has not always cause to fear it. Pure reason, however, in its dogmatic (as opposed to mathematical) employment, is not so fully conscious of the strictest observance of its supreme laws that it can appear before the critical eye of a higher and judicial reason otherwise than with diffidence, indeed with the complete renunciation of all presumed dogmatic authority.",[]],"krv-a-1177":["The case is quite different when reason has to deal not with the censure of a judge but with the claims of a fellow citizen, and has merely to defend itself against them. For since these claims are just as dogmatic, though in denying, as the others are in affirming, a justification *κατ' ἀνθρωπον* [\"in human fashion\", i.e. as ad hominem] is here in place. It secures us against all encroachment, and gives a titled possession which need not fear any foreign usurpations, although it cannot itself be sufficiently proved *κατ' ἀλήθειαν* [in accordance with truth].",[]],"krv-a-1178":["By the polemical employment of pure reason I understand the defence of its propositions against the dogmatic denial of them. The question here is not whether its assertions may not perhaps also be false, but only that nobody can ever assert the opposite with apodeictic certainty (or even with greater plausibility). For we do not hold our possession merely on sufferance, if we have a title to it, though an insufficient one, and it is perfectly certain that nobody can ever prove the illegitimacy of that possession.",[]],"krv-a-1179":["It is distressing and depressing that there should be an antithetic of pure reason at all, and that reason, which is the supreme tribunal for all disputes, should come into conflict with itself. We had indeed above [in the Antinomy] an apparent antithetic of this kind before us; but it was found to rest on a misunderstanding. In accordance with the common prejudice, appearances were taken for things in themselves, and an absolute completeness of their synthesis was demanded in one way or another (though it was equally impossible in either way), which cannot be expected of appearances at all. There was therefore at that time no real contradiction of reason with itself in the propositions: the series of appearances given in themselves has an absolutely first beginning; and: this series is, absolutely and in itself, without any beginning. The two propositions are quite compatible, since appearances, as regards their existence (as appearances), are in themselves nothing, that is, are something self-contradictory, and the presupposition of them must therefore naturally carry contradictory consequences with it.",[]],"krv-a-1180":["Such a misunderstanding cannot, however, be pleaded, and the conflict of reason thereby composed, if it were asserted theistically that there is a supreme being, and, on the other side, atheistically, that there is no supreme being; or, in psychology, that everything that thinks is of absolute and abiding unity, and is therefore distinct from all transitory material unity, to which another opposes the assertion that the soul is not an immaterial unity and cannot be exempted from transitoriness. For the object of the question is here free from everything alien to its nature, and the understanding has to do only with things in themselves, not with appearances. A genuine conflict would therefore certainly be met with here, if only pure reason had anything to say on the negative side that came near to being a ground for an assertion. As for criticism of the grounds of proof offered by the dogmatic affirmer, we can readily grant it to him without thereby giving up these propositions, which at least have the interest of reason in their favour, and to which the opponent cannot appeal at all.",[]],"krv-a-1181":["I do not, indeed, share the opinion so often expressed by excellent and reflective men (e.g. Sulzer), who, feeling the weakness of the proofs hitherto given, held that we may hope one day to discover evident demonstrations of the two cardinal propositions of our pure reason: there is a God, there is a future life. I am, on the contrary, certain that this will never happen. For where is reason to find the ground for such synthetic assertions, which do not relate to objects of experience and their inner possibility? But it is also apodeictically certain that no man will ever come forward who can assert the opposite with the least plausibility, still less dogmatically. For since he could establish this only through pure reason, he would have to undertake to prove that a supreme being, and the thinking subject in us as pure intelligence, are impossible. But where will he find the [modes of] knowledge (*Kenntnisse*) that would entitle him to judge synthetically in this fashion about things beyond all possible experience? We may therefore be wholly undisturbed as to whether anyone will some day prove the opposite. We need not, on that account, devise scholastically correct proofs, but may always accept those propositions which cohere very well with the speculative interest of our reason in its empirical employment, and which are, moreover, the only means of reconciling that interest with the practical. Against the opponent (who must here be regarded not merely as a critic) we have our *non liquet* in readiness, which must infallibly confound him; meanwhile we do not refuse his retorting it upon ourselves, since we have always in reserve the subjective maxim of reason, which the opponent necessarily lacks, and under whose protection we can view all his blows in the air with calm and indifference.",["(Notes follow in the format requested; the reply format here is \"--- notes\" after each passage, but I have given them together here.)","- 1173: *Vermögensumstände* (lit. \"circumstances of means/property\") is a pun on *Vermögen* (capacity) and financial means; I rendered by supplying \"[powers and their]\" and gave the German. The sentence \"Giebt es nun…\" is kept as one; the final sentence's parenthesis moved to \"namely\".","- 1175: \"Nutzens\" taken as the utility of the thing claiming exemption; \"dieser … Durchsuchung, … entziehen dürfte\" rendered \"may exempt itself\" for clarity.","- 1176: \"Blödigkeit\" rendered \"diffidence\"; the \"nicht … nicht mit … erscheinen müßte\" construction recast as \"cannot appear … otherwise than with\".","- 1177: Greek tags are as in the text, with English supplied in square brackets; \"ἀληθειαν\" silently normalised from OCR \"αληϑειαν\". \"Jene\" (the judge's censure) and \"diese\" (the fellow citizen's claims) resolved by sense.","- 1178: \"apodeictic\" for *apodictisch*; \"bittweise\" rendered \"on sufferance\" (precarious tenure).","- 1179: \"absolutersten Anfang\" rendered \"absolutely first beginning\"; \"[in the Antinomy]\" supplied for \"oben\". The long final sentence kept in one piece but divided with semicolons elsewhere.","- 1180: \"Sätze\" in the final clause refers to the theistic/psychological propositions; \"ihm\" is the dogmatic affirmer. \"Dogmatischbeiahenden\" rendered \"dogmatic affirmer\".","- 1181: \"Kentnisse\" is probably the plural of knowledge; I give \"[modes of] knowledge\" with the German. \"Vor den Gegner … Retorsion\" rendered as \"against the opponent … retorting\"; the German \"daß … daß\" in the penultimate sentence is irregular and I smoothed it into two sentences (\"We need not, on that account…\")."]],"krv-a-1182":["In this way there is, strictly speaking, no antithetic of pure reason at all. For the only arena for such a conflict would have to be sought in the field of pure theology and psychology; but this ground bears no combatant in full armour, and none with weapons that need be feared. He can appear only with ridicule or boasting, which can be laughed at as child's play. This is a consoling reflection, and gives reason fresh courage. For on what else could it rely, if reason, which alone is called upon to remove all errors, were itself in disorder, and without hope of peace and quiet possession?",["- \"vor\" in \"Kampfplatz vor sie\" read as *für* (for), as is usual in the 1781 text; \"Grossprecherey\" rendered \"boasting\".","- \"Antithetik\" kept as \"antithetic\"; the final question is retained as one sentence, with \"reason\" supplied for the pronoun \"sie\" at its start.","- \"Kämpfer\" is singular, hence \"a combatant\"; I shift to plural \"none with weapons\" only for idiom."]],"krv-a-1183":["Whatever nature itself ordains is good for some purpose. Even poisons serve to overcome other poisons generated in our own bodily fluids, and therefore must not be lacking in a complete collection of remedies (a dispensary). The objections raised against the persuasions and self-conceit of our merely speculative reason are themselves set us by the very nature of this reason, and must therefore have their good determination [*Bestimmung*] and purpose, which ought not to be thrown to the winds. Why has Providence set certain objects, although bound up with our highest interest, so high that we are almost only permitted to encounter them in a perception that is indistinct and doubted by ourselves, so that our searching gaze is more excited than satisfied? Whether it is useful to venture bold determinations [*Bestimmungen*] with regard to such prospects is at least doubtful, and perhaps even harmful. But it is always, and beyond all doubt, useful to put reason, in its investigating as well as in its critical capacity, in complete freedom, so that it may be able to attend undisturbed to its own interest. This interest is furthered as much by its setting limits to its insight as by its extending it, and it always suffers when alien hands interfere to turn reason from its natural course in the service of purposes forced upon it.",["- The sentence beginning \"Wozu hat uns die Vorsehung\" is grammatically irregular (a question with a dependent \"dadurch\" clause); I have kept it as a question and smoothed the construction.","- \"Ueberredungen\" = persuasions; \"Wahrnehmung\" = perception; \"Officin\" given as \"dispensary\".","- \"forschende sowol, als prüfende Vernunft\": \"in its investigating as well as in its critical capacity\" is a supplied gloss.","- Final sentence divided: \"welches\" (twice) resolved as \"This interest\", referring to reason's *Interesse*."]],"krv-a-1184":["Let your opponent, then, say only \"reason\", and combat him solely with weapons of reason. For the rest, be without concern for the good cause (the practical interest), for that never comes into play in a merely speculative dispute. The dispute then reveals nothing but a certain antinomy of reason which, since it rests upon reason's nature, must necessarily be heard and examined. The dispute cultivates reason by considering its object from two sides, and corrects its judgment by limiting it. What becomes contentious here is not the matter but the tone. For enough still remains to you to speak, in a language justified before the keenest reason, the language of firm belief [*Glaube*], even though you have had to give up that of knowledge [*Wissen*].",["- \"Lasset ... nur Vernunft sagen\": rendered \"say only 'reason'\", i.e. let him appeal only to reason.","- \"Er cultivirt dieselbe\": \"Er\" = the dispute (*Streit*), \"dieselbe\" = reason (*Vernunft*); resolved by judgment.","- \"Sache\"/\"Ton\": matter/tone. *Glaube* \"belief\" and *Wissen* \"knowledge\" are supplied in brackets."]],"krv-a-1185":["If we were to ask the cool-headed David Hume, who was in truth created for equilibrium of judgment: What moved you, by laboriously excogitated scruples, to undermine the persuasion, so comforting and useful to men, that their rational insight is adequate to the assertion and the determinate concept of a supreme being? he would answer: Nothing but the aim of advancing reason in its self-knowledge, and at the same time a certain indignation at the constraint which is being put upon reason, when men make a great show with it and yet prevent it from making a frank confession of the weaknesses which become evident to it in the examination of itself. If, on the other hand, you ask Priestley, who is devoted solely to the principles of the empirical employment of reason and averse to all transcendent speculation, what motives he had for pulling down freedom and immortality of our soul, two such pillars of all religion (for the hope of a future life is with him only the expectation of a miracle of resurrection), he, who is himself a pious and zealous teacher of religion, could answer nothing else than this: the interest of reason, which suffers loss if certain objects are to be withdrawn from the laws of material nature, the only laws which we can know and determine exactly. It would seem unfair to decry the latter, who knows how to reconcile his paradoxical assertion with his religious purpose, and to wound a well-meaning man because he cannot find his bearings as soon as he has strayed from the field of natural science. But this favour must benefit no less Hume, who is not less well-disposed and is irreproachable in moral character, and who cannot abandon his abstract speculation because he rightly holds that its object lies wholly outside the limits of natural science, in the field of pure ideas.",["- Hume's and Priestley's questions are put as direct speech in the German; I have kept the questions but not added quotation marks, and divided nothing, though the sentences are long.","- \"vor\" read as *für*/*vor* (before) in \"vor den Menschen\" = \"to men\".","- \"zulange\" = suffices; \"Selbsterkentniß\" = self-knowledge.","- \"der Religionsabsicht\" = \"religious purpose\"."]],"krv-a-1186":["What, then, is to be done here, especially in view of the danger which seems thereby to threaten the common weal? Nothing is more natural, nothing fairer, than the resolution you have to take. Let these people go their way. If they show talent, deep and new investigation, in a word, if they but show reason, reason always gains. If you resort to means other than those of an unconstrained reason, if you cry out against high treason, and summon the common people, who understand nothing of such subtle labours, as if to put out a fire, you make yourselves ridiculous. For the question is not at all what is advantageous or disadvantageous to the common weal in this matter, but only how far reason can get in its speculation, which abstracts from all interest, and whether one can reckon on this at all, or must rather give it up in favour of the practical. Instead, then, of rushing in with the sword, look on rather from the secure seat of criticism [*Critik*] at this dispute, which is laborious for the combatants, entertaining for you, and, with an outcome certain to be bloodless, must turn out profitable for your insight. For it is highly absurd to expect enlightenment from reason and yet to prescribe to it beforehand on which side it must come out. Moreover, reason is so well restrained and kept within limits by reason itself that you have no need to call out guards to oppose a civil resistance to that party whose threatening superiority seems to you dangerous. In this dialectic there is no victory about which you would have cause to be anxious.",["- \"Laßt diese Leute nur machen\" rendered \"Let these people go their way\".","- \"Anstatt also ... so sehet vielmehr\": the irregular \"so\" smoothed away.","- \"Schaarwachen\" = guards (sentinels); \"bürgerlichen Widerstand\" = \"civil resistance\".","- \"das gemeine Wesen\" taken as the common people, in contrast with \"gemeine Beste\" (common weal)."]],"krv-a-1187":["Reason also stands greatly in need of such a dispute, and it were to be wished that it had been conducted earlier and with unrestricted public permission. For a mature criticism [*Critik*] would then have come into being so much the sooner, and on its appearance all these disputes must of themselves fall away, since the disputants would learn to see the illusion and prejudices which have divided them.",["- \"Verblendung\" = illusion (literally blindness); \"Streithändel\" = disputes.","- \"wäre ... zu Stande gekommen\" rendered as a conditional (\"would have come into being\")."]],"krv-a-1188":["There is a certain impurity in human nature which, like everything that comes from nature, must in the end contain a predisposition to good ends. It is an inclination to conceal our true sentiments, and to make a show of certain assumed sentiments which we hold to be good and creditable. It is certain that through this propensity, both to conceal themselves and to assume a semblance advantageous to them, men have not merely civilised themselves but have gradually, and in a certain measure, moralised themselves. For since no one could see through the paint of decency, honourableness and modesty, each found in the supposedly genuine examples of goodness which he saw about him a school of improvement for himself. This disposition to represent oneself as better than one is, and to express sentiments which one does not have, serves, however, only as it were provisionally, to bring man out of rudeness, and to make him at least adopt, in the first instance, the manner of the good which he knows. Later, when the genuine principles have once been developed and have passed into the manner of thinking, that falsity must be steadily and vigorously combated. Otherwise it corrupts the heart, and allows good sentiments to spring up only with difficulty, under the rank weeds of fair seeming.",["- *Gesinnung* rendered \"sentiment\"; *Denkungsart* \"manner of thinking\".","- The long second sentence is divided in two; \"For since…\" (*weil*) clause kept as the reason for the preceding claim.","- Final sentence split at \"Later…\": the *denn nachher* clause is made a separate sentence, and *weil sie sonst* rendered \"Otherwise it\".","- *Wucherkraut* = \"rank weeds\"; \"with difficulty\" is my gloss on *nicht aufkommen läßt* (\"does not let spring up\"), softened for idiom."]],"krv-a-1189":["I am sorry to perceive this same impurity, dissimulation and hypocrisy even in the expressions of the speculative manner of thinking. Here men have far fewer obstacles, and no advantage whatever, in reasonably making open and unreserved avowal of their thoughts. For what can be more harmful to insight than to communicate even mere thoughts to one another in falsified form, to conceal doubts which we feel against our own assertions, or to give a gloss of evidence to grounds of proof which do not satisfy ourselves? So long, however, as it is merely private vanity that instigates these secret intrigues (as is commonly the case in speculative judgments, which have no special interest and are not easily capable of apodeictic certainty), the vanity of others opposes it with public approval, and matters come in the end to the point to which the purest sentiment and sincerity would have brought them, though much sooner. But where the commonwealth believes that subtle reasoners are occupied with nothing less than the undermining of the foundations of public welfare, it seems not merely prudent, but also permissible and even creditable, to come to the aid of the good cause with sham arguments rather than leave its supposed opponents even the advantage of lowering our tone to the moderation of a merely practical conviction, and of compelling us to confess the lack of speculative and apodeictic certainty. I should think, however, that nothing in the world is less compatible with the intention of maintaining a good cause than craftiness, dissimulation and deceit. That in weighing the grounds of reason in a mere speculation everything should proceed honestly is surely the least that can be demanded. Could we but count with certainty even on this little, the conflict of speculative reason over the important questions of God, the immortality [of the soul] and freedom would either have been decided long ago, or would very soon be brought to an end. Thus the purity of sentiment often stands in inverse proportion to the goodness of the cause itself; and this cause has perhaps more sincere and upright opponents than defenders.",["- The relative clause \"worin doch Menschen…\" is made a separate sentence (\"Here men have…\"), with *billiger Maassen* rendered \"reasonably\".","- *Denkungsart* \"manner of thinking\"; *apodictisch* \"apodeictic\" (Kemp Smith's spelling).","- \"(der Seele)\" is Kant's own parenthesis; I render it \"[of the soul]\" in brackets only to mark it as parenthetical there, not as my addition.","- \"it\" in \"opposes it\" supplied for the object of *widersteht* (the intrigue/vanity).","- *Gemeine Wesen* = \"commonwealth\"; *Vernünftler* = \"reasoners\" (pejorative sense of \"subtle\")."]],"krv-a-1190":["I presuppose, then, readers who do not wish to see a just cause defended unjustly. For such readers it is now decided that, according to our principles of criticism, if we look not to what happens but to what ought properly to happen, there should really be no polemic of pure reason at all. For how can two persons carry on a dispute about a thing whose reality neither of them can exhibit in an actual, or even in a merely possible, experience? Over its idea alone does each brood, in the hope of bringing out of it something more than idea, namely the actuality of the object itself. By what means are they to extricate themselves from the dispute, seeing that neither of them can make his own case directly comprehensible and certain, but can only attack and refute that of his opponent? For this is the fate of all assertions of pure reason. They go beyond the conditions of all possible experience, outside which no document of truth is to be found anywhere; yet they have to make use of the laws of understanding, which are destined solely for empirical employment, and without which no step can be taken in synthetic thinking. Consequently they always expose a weak side to the opponent, and can each turn to account the weakness of the opponent's case.",["- *Critik* rendered \"criticism\" here, as a general designation of the principles.","- The relative clause \"über deren Idee er allein brütet…\" made a separate sentence; \"each\" replaces the singular *er*.","- The final sentence, whose construction is irregular (a *daß* clause with a long insertion and no clear main verb for the first part), is divided and the main clause \"they always expose…\" is given as a consequence (\"Consequently\" supplied).","- *Blösse geben* = \"expose a weak side\"; *Verstandesgesetze* = \"laws of understanding\"."]],"krv-a-1191":["The critique of pure reason may be regarded as the true tribunal for all disputes of pure reason. For it is not itself involved in these disputes, which bear immediately on objects, but is appointed to determine and judge the rights of reason in general, in accordance with the principles of its primary institution.",["- Sentence divided at the semicolon into two; *Obiecte* = \"objects\" (*Gegenstände* is not used here).","- \"derselben\" taken to refer to reason; \"die leztere\" to the disputes (*Streitigkeiten*).","- *Critik* = \"critique\" (the work)."]],"krv-a-1192":["Without critique, reason is as it were in the state of nature, and can establish or secure its assertions and claims only by war. Critique, on the other hand, deriving all its decisions from the fundamental rules of its own constitution, whose authority no one can question, secures for us the tranquillity of a legal order, in which we are to conduct our disputes only by process of law. What brings the quarrels in the first state to an end is a victory, of which both parties boast, and which is mostly followed by a merely insecure peace, established by the authorities who intervene. In the second state, on the other hand, it is a sentence, which, since it here goes to the very source of the disputes, must ensure a perpetual peace. The endless disputes of a merely dogmatic reason also compel us at last to seek tranquillity in some critique of this reason itself, and in a legislation which is based upon it. So Hobbes maintains that the state of nature is a state of injustice and violence, and that we must necessarily leave it in order to submit to the constraint of law, which alone limits our freedom in such fashion that it can coexist with the freedom of everyone else, and thereby with the common good.",["- *Einsetzung* rendered \"constitution\" (as the founding of critique as a tribunal); *Institution* in the previous passage \"institution\".","- \"Without critique\" supplies the noun for *dieselbe*; antecedent is *die Critik* of the previous sentence.","- The long sentence on victory and sentence is divided; \"In the second state\" supplied for *im zweiten*.","- *Proceß* = \"process of law\"; *Zwang* = \"constraint\".","- \"so wie Hobbes\" rendered \"So Hobbes maintains\", loosening the comparative construction."]],"krv-a-1193":["- Long sentence on the \"original right of reason\" (*Dies liegt … nicht geschmälert werden*) divided in two; the irregular *und, da … so ist* construction smoothed.\n- \"[professedly]\" supplied for the subjunctive *solle* (\"is said to have\").\n- *Freigeist* rendered \"free-thinker\"; *Einsicht* \"insight\"; *Kentniß* \"knowledge\".\n- Last sentence of the \"dogmatic defender\" clause: *über dem* rendered loosely as \"besides\"; the construction is awkward and I have smoothed it.",[]],"krv-a-1194":["- Rendered as a question, as the German punctuation requires; *Urtheilskraft* = \"power of judgment\".",[]],"krv-a-1195":["- Several sentences divided; *Modeton* = \"fashionable tone\"; *Vormundschaft* = \"guardianship\".\n- *wolgemeinte Warnungen* taken to refer to the warnings against such writings.",[]],"krv-a-1196":["- Long second sentence split; *Streiche* = \"blows\"; the concessive *Ob nun zwar … so* restated as \"Now … But\".\n- *vor* in the old spelling is read as *für* (\"for\") in *vor den Dogmatiker*, *vor ihn*; \"against his reason\" for *wider seine … Vernunft*.",[]],"krv-a-1197":["- *Luftfechter* = \"shadow-boxers\" (lit. air-fencers); *dogmatische Griffe* = \"dogmatic grasp\".",[]],"krv-a-1198":["- *Vernünftler* kept with the German in brackets: \"sophists\" is an interpretive choice for those who merely reason subtly.\n- *Heeresstrasse* rendered \"highway\"; *Denkungsart* \"way of thinking\"; *Gemüthsart* \"disposition\".\n- Long final sentences divided or smoothed at *Allein … ist* and *Da indessen … so*.",[]],"krv-a-1199":["On the Impossibility of a Sceptical Satisfaction of Pure Reason in its Conflict with Itself.",["- \"Veruneinigten\" rendered \"in its conflict with itself\"; \"Befriedigung\" as \"satisfaction\". \"Sceptischen\" kept as \"sceptical\" (British spelling)."]],"krv-a-1200":["The consciousness of my ignorance (unless this is at the same time known to be necessary), instead of bringing my inquiries to an end, is rather the proper cause for undertaking them. All ignorance is either ignorance of things, or ignorance of the determination and limits of my knowledge. If the ignorance is merely contingent, it must impel me, in the first case, to inquire dogmatically into the things (objects), and in the second case, to inquire critically into the limits of my possible knowledge. That my ignorance is absolutely necessary, and that it therefore absolves me from all further investigation, cannot be established empirically, from observation, but only critically, by probing the primary sources of our knowledge. Thus the determination of the bounds of our reason can take place only on a priori grounds. Its limitation, on the other hand, which is a knowledge, though only indeterminate, of an ignorance that can never be completely removed, can also be known a posteriori, through what, in all our knowing, still always remains to be known. The former knowledge of one's ignorance, possible only through a critique of reason itself, is therefore science; the latter is nothing but perception, and we cannot say how far the inference drawn from it may extend.\n\nIf I represent to myself the surface of the earth (in accordance with sensible appearance) as a plate, I cannot know how far it extends. But experience teaches me this: that wherever I may go, I always see around me a space in which I could proceed further. Hence I know the limitations [*Schranken*] of my actual geographical knowledge at any time, but not the bounds [*Gränzen*] of all possible geography. If, however, I have gone so far as to know that the earth is a sphere and its surface a spherical surface, then from a small part of it, e.g. the extent of one degree, I can also know and determine according to principles a priori the diameter, and through this the complete boundary of the earth, that is, its surface. And although I am ignorant in regard to the objects which this surface may contain, I am not so in regard to the extent which encloses them, their magnitude and limitations.",["- The sentence beginning \"Also kan die Gränzbestimmung…\" is kept whole but its relative clause is set off by commas; \"Bestimmung\" in the second sentence is rendered \"determination\" per the fixed term.","- \"Schranken\" (limitations) and \"Gränzen\" (bounds/limits) are distinguished, as the argument requires; the German is given at the geographical passage.","- \"Wahrnehmung\" rendered \"perception\".","- The final sentence's \"der sie enthält\" resolved as the extent enclosing the objects; \"derselben\" refers to the surface (Fläche).","- The text of \"bestimt … erkennen\" is slightly irregular (the participle \"bestimt\" is joined to \"erkennen\"); rendered \"know and determine\"."]],"krv-a-1201":["The sum of all possible objects of our knowledge seems to us to be a level surface, which has its apparent horizon, namely, that which comprehends its whole extent, and which has been entitled by us the rational concept of unconditioned totality. To reach it empirically is impossible, and all attempts to determine it a priori according to a certain principle have been vain. Nevertheless all the questions of our pure reason relate to what may lie outside this horizon, or at most on its boundary line.",["- \"Vor unsere Erkentniß\" (= für) rendered \"of our knowledge\". \"Genant worden\" rendered \"has been entitled\" per the method. \"Vernunftbegriff\" as \"rational concept\"."]],"krv-a-1202":["The celebrated David Hume was one of these geographers of human reason. He believed that he had sufficiently disposed of all those questions by relegating them outside the horizon of reason, which yet he was unable to determine. He dwelt especially upon the principle of causality, and remarked quite correctly that its truth (indeed even the objective validity of the concept of an efficient cause in general) rests on no insight, that is, on no knowledge a priori; and that therefore it is not in the least the necessity of this law, but merely its general serviceability in the course of experience, together with a subjective necessity arising therefrom, which he entitles custom, that constitutes its whole authority. From the incapacity of our reason to make of this principle a use that goes beyond all experience, he inferred the nullity of all the pretensions of reason to go beyond the empirical.",["- The long second sentence is divided in two; the \"daß\" clauses are kept in one sentence with a semicolon. \"Unvermögen\" rendered \"incapacity\"."]],"krv-a-1203":["A procedure of this kind, which subjects the *facta* of reason to examination and, where found necessary, to censure, may be entitled the censorship of reason. It is beyond doubt that this censorship inevitably leads to doubt regarding all transcendent employment of principles. But this is only the second step, and is still far from completing the work. The first step in matters of pure reason, which marks its childhood, is dogmatic. The second step, just mentioned, is sceptical, and bears witness to the caution of a judgment sharpened by experience. But a third step is still required, which belongs only to a mature and manly power of judgment, one which has firm maxims of proven universality as its foundation. It consists in subjecting to appraisal not the *facta* of reason but reason itself, in respect of its whole capacity and fitness for pure modes of knowledge a priori. This is not the censorship but the critique of reason. By it not merely limitations [*Schranken*] but the determinate bounds [*Gränzen*] of reason are established, and ignorance is proved, not merely conjectured, to hold not just in one part or another but in regard to all possible questions of a certain kind, and proved from principles.\n\nThus scepticism is a resting-place for human reason, where it can reflect upon its dogmatic wanderings and sketch the map of the region in which it finds itself, so that it may choose its path henceforward with greater certainty; but it is not a dwelling-place for permanent residence. For this can be found only in complete certainty, whether of the knowledge of the objects themselves, or of the bounds within which all our knowledge of objects is enclosed.",["- The very long sentence beginning \"Nun ist aber noch ein dritter Schritt…\" is restated as several sentences; its construction is irregular (the infinitival \"zu unterwerfen\" hangs from \"nöthig\") and has been smoothed. \"wird\" at the end is made to govern \"ignorance\" as the subject, by judgment.","- \"Männiglichen\" (probably \"männlichen\") rendered \"manly\". \"Nach Befinden\" rendered \"where found necessary\", i.e. as found warranted; \"Tadel\" by \"censure\".","- \"Facta\" kept in Latin italics. \"Urtheilskraft\" rendered \"power of judgment\"."]],"krv-a-1204":["Our reason is not an indefinitely extended plane, of which we know only in a general way that it has limitations; it must rather be compared to a sphere, the radius of which can be found from the curvature of the arc on its surface (the nature of synthetic propositions a priori), and from which the content and the boundary of the sphere can likewise be assigned with certainty. Outside this sphere (the field of experience) nothing is an object for it; indeed even questions regarding such supposed objects concern only subjective principles of a thoroughgoing determination of the relations which can occur among the concepts of understanding within this sphere.",["- \"Unbestimbarweit\" read as \"unbestimmbar weit\" (indefinitely); \"Halbmesser\" rendered \"radius\". \"Vor ihr Obiect\" = \"für sie ein Objekt\"."]],"krv-a-1205":["We are actually in possession of synthetic knowledge a priori, as is shown by the principles of the understanding which anticipate experience. If anyone cannot make its possibility at all comprehensible to himself, he may at first doubt whether such knowledge really dwells in us a priori; but he cannot yet declare this to be an impossibility through the mere powers of the understanding, nor pronounce null and void every step which reason takes in accordance with its guidance. He can only say that if we could see its origin and genuineness, we should be able to determine the extent and bounds of our reason; but that until this has been done, all assertions on the part of reason are ventured blindly. In this way a thoroughgoing doubt of all dogmatic philosophy, which proceeds on its way without a critique of reason itself, would be quite well founded. But it would not follow that such an advance could be wholly denied to reason, if it were prepared and secured by a better foundation.\n\nFor, in the first place, all the concepts, indeed all the questions, which pure reason puts before us lie not in experience but themselves in reason alone, and must therefore be capable of being resolved and of being understood in respect of their validity or nullity. Nor are we entitled to reject these problems under the pretext of our incapacity, as if their solution really lay in the nature of things, and to refuse further investigation of them; since reason alone has generated these ideas in its own womb, and is therefore bound to give an account of their validity or of their dialectical illusion.",["- \"Der lezten\" (the latter) is resolved as \"reason\" (Vernunft), the nearer sense required by the argument; \"derselben\" in \"Richtschnur derselben\" is resolved as the understanding's powers/the knowledge in question and rendered \"its guidance\".","- Long sentences divided; \"Denn, einmal…\" begins a new paragraph-sentence, and no \"zweitens\" follows in this passage (the continuation may follow).","- \"müssen daher können aufgelöset … werden können\" is an irregular repetition of \"können\" in the German (probable slip); smoothed to \"must therefore be capable of being resolved and … understood\".","- \"Wir sind auch nicht berechtigt\" rendered \"Nor are we entitled\", the \"entitled\" being ordinary usage, not *heißen*."]],"krv-a-1206":["All sceptical polemic is, properly speaking, directed only against the dogmatist, who, without any distrust of his original objective principles, that is, without criticism, continues gravely on his way, and it is directed against him merely in order to disconcert him and bring him to self-knowledge. In itself it decides absolutely nothing as to what we can know and what, on the other hand, we cannot know. All unsuccessful dogmatic attempts of reason are facts [*Facta*], and it is always useful to subject them to censure. But this can decide nothing regarding the expectations of reason, whether it may hope for better success in its future endeavours and found claims upon that hope. Mere censure can therefore never bring to an end the dispute about the rights of human reason.",["- \"Critik\" is rendered \"criticism\" in the first sentence, since it denotes the habit of critical examination rather than the work itself.","- \"Concept\" (Concept zu verrücken) is rendered \"disconcert\", in the sense of putting him out of his composure. The German idiom is literally \"to displace his concept\"; the English loses the pun.","- \"Selbsterkentniß\" is rendered \"self-knowledge\".","- \"sie\" in the second sentence refers to sceptical polemic. \"Dieses\" in the fourth sentence is resolved as censure.","- The last long sentence is restated as two sentences. The clause \"einen besseren Erfolg … zu hoffen\" is taken as explaining the \"Erwartungen der Vernunft\".","- \"Facta\" is retained in italics in square brackets as a Latin term."]],"krv-a-1207":["Since Hume is perhaps the most ingenious of all the sceptics, and is beyond dispute the most outstanding in respect of the influence which the sceptical procedure can have in awakening a thorough examination of reason, it is well worth while to set forth, so far as is suitable to my purpose, the course of his reasonings and the aberrations of a man so discerning and so deserving of esteem, aberrations which none the less began on the trail of truth.",["- \"Vernunftprüfung\" is rendered \"examination of reason\".","- \"einsehenden\" is rendered \"discerning\" (one of insight).","- The sentence is kept as one, since its construction can be followed in English."]],"krv-a-1208":["Hume had perhaps in mind, though he never developed it completely, that in judgments of a certain kind we go beyond our concept of the object. I have entitled this kind of judgment synthetic. How I can, by means of experience, go beyond the concept which I have up to now is subject to no difficulty. Experience is itself such a synthesis of perceptions, which augments through other perceptions added to it the concept that I have by means of a perception. But we also believe that we can go beyond our concept a priori, and extend our knowledge. This we attempt either through pure understanding, in respect of that which can at least be an object of experience, or even through pure reason, in respect of such properties of things, or indeed of the existence of such objects, as can never occur in experience.\n\nOur sceptic did not distinguish these two kinds of judgment, as he ought to have done. He straightway held this augmentation of concepts out of themselves, and, so to speak, the self-parturition of our understanding (together with reason), without its being impregnated by experience, to be impossible. He therefore held all the supposed a priori principles of understanding and reason to be imaginary, and found that they are nothing but a custom springing from experience and its laws, and therefore merely empirical rules, that is, rules in themselves contingent, to which we ascribe a supposed necessity and universality.\n\nIn support of this strange proposition he appealed to the universally recognised principle of the relation of cause to effect. For since no faculty of understanding can lead us from the concept of a thing to the existence of something else which is thereby universally and necessarily given, he believed he could infer that without experience we have nothing that could augment our concept and entitle us to a judgment extending itself a priori. That the sunlight which illumines the wax at the same time melts it, while it hardens clay, no understanding could divine from concepts which we previously had of these things, much less infer according to law; only experience could teach us such a law.\n\nOn the other hand, we have seen in transcendental logic that, though we can never go immediately beyond the content of the concept which is given to us, we can yet know completely a priori, but in relation to a third thing, namely possible experience, and therefore still a priori, the law of connection with other things. If, then, wax which was previously firm melts, I can know a priori that something must have preceded (for instance, the warmth of the sun) upon which this has followed in accordance with a constant law, although, without experience, I could not know determinately a priori and without instruction from experience either the cause from the effect or the effect from the cause.\n\nHe therefore inferred wrongly, from the contingency of our determination in accordance with the law, to the contingency of the law itself. And the passing beyond the concept of a thing to possible experience (which takes place a priori and constitutes the objective reality of that concept) he confused with the synthesis of the objects of actual experience, which is, of course, always empirical. In so doing he made of a principle of affinity, which has its seat in the understanding and asserts necessary connection, a rule of association, which is met with only in the reproductive imagination and can present only contingent connections, never objective ones.",["- The long passage is divided into several paragraphs and shorter sentences, as the argument has distinct stages (Hume's view; the causal example; Kant's reply; the diagnosis of the error).","- \"Concept\"/\"Begriff\" is rendered \"concept\" throughout. \"Erkentniß\" is rendered \"knowledge\".","- \"synthetisch genant\" is rendered \"entitled synthetic\", following the convention for \"genannt\".","- \"Selbstgebährung\" is rendered \"self-parturition\" to keep the generative metaphor with \"geschwängert\", \"impregnated\".","- \"Bestimmung nach dem Gesetze\" is rendered \"determination in accordance with the law\". The \"bestimt erkennen\" is rendered \"know determinately\".","- \"desselben\" in \"die objective Realität desselben\" is resolved as referring to the concept of the thing.","- \"Wahrnehmungen\" is rendered \"perceptions\". \"Nachbildende Einbildungskraft\" is rendered \"reproductive imagination\" in the usual Kemp Smith manner.","- The sentence \"Wie ich … hinausgehen könne, ist keiner Bedenklichkeit unterworfen\" is taken to mean that no difficulty arises. \"Bedenklichkeit\" is rendered \"difficulty\"."]],"krv-a-1209":["The sceptical aberrations of this otherwise extremely acute man sprang chiefly from a defect which he shared with all dogmatists, namely, that he did not survey systematically all the kinds of a priori synthesis of the understanding. For otherwise, to say nothing here of the rest, he would, for instance, have found the principle of permanence to be one which, no less than that of causality, anticipates experience. In this way he would also have been able to prescribe determinate limits to the understanding which extends itself a priori, and to pure reason.\n\nBut he only restricts our understanding without setting limits to it. He brings about a general distrust, but no determinate knowledge of the ignorance which is unavoidable for us. He brings certain principles of the understanding under censure, without putting the understanding, in respect of its whole faculty, to the test of criticism. And while he denies to the understanding what it really cannot accomplish, he goes further and disputes its whole power of extending itself a priori, although he has not subjected this whole faculty to appraisal. Hence there befalls him what always defeats scepticism, namely, that he himself is doubted, since his objections rest only on facts [*Facta*], which are contingent, and not on principles which could bring about a necessary renunciation of the right to dogmatic assertions.",["- The second sentence of the original is a single long period with several parallel \"da\" clauses. It is restated as separate sentences, with the main clause \"so wiederfährt ihm …\" placed at the close.","- \"einschränkt … ohne ihn zu begränzen\" is rendered \"restricts … without setting limits to\", in order to keep the Kantian distinction between *Schranken* and *Grenzen*. The English cannot make this fully explicit.","- \"Probierwage der Critik\" is rendered \"test of criticism\"; the image of the assay balance is lost.","- \"Kentniß\" is rendered \"knowledge\"; \"Vermögen\" is rendered \"faculty\" and \"power\" as the sense requires.","- \"Entsagung auf das Recht\" is rendered \"renunciation of the right\"."]],"krv-a-1210":["Since he likewise recognises no distinction between the well-grounded claims of the understanding and the dialectical pretensions of reason, against which his attacks are mainly directed, reason, whose peculiar impulse has in this case not in the least been disturbed but only hindered, does not feel the space for its expansion to be closed against it, and can never be wholly diverted from its attempts, although it is plucked at here and there. For against attacks one arms oneself for defence, and sets one's head all the more stubbornly on carrying through one's demands. But a complete survey of one's whole faculty, and the conviction, springing from it, of the certainty of a small possession, together with the vanity of higher claims, puts an end to all strife and disposes one to rest peacefully content with a limited but undisputed property.",["- \"Schwung\" is rendered \"impulse\" (literally, the soaring or momentum of reason).","- \"gezwackt\" is rendered \"plucked at\" (pinched, nipped).","- \"Ueberschlag\" is rendered \"survey\", meaning a reckoning or estimate. \"Eitelkeit\" is rendered \"vanity\", in the sense of emptiness.","- The impersonal \"man\" and \"seinen\" are carried on with \"one\", \"one's\", with the \"seines ganzen Vermögens\" referring to the faculty of reason."]],"krv-a-1211":["Against the uncritical dogmatist, who has not measured the sphere of his understanding, and who therefore has not determined by principles the limits of his possible knowledge, who consequently does not know beforehand how much he can do but thinks to discover it by mere trials, these sceptical attacks are not only dangerous but even destructive. For if he is found out in a single assertion which he can neither justify nor, so far as its semblance goes, explain from principles, suspicion falls upon all of them, however persuasive they may otherwise be.",["- \"Schein\" is rendered \"semblance\". \"dessen Schein … entwickeln\" is read as \"to explain how the illusion arises from principles\".","- \"ihm so gar verderblich\" is rendered \"even destructive\".","- \"Erkentniß\" is rendered \"knowledge\".","- \"auf allen\" is read in the final clause as \"upon all\" of his assertions."]],"krv-a-1212":["And so the sceptic is the taskmaster who disciplines the dogmatic reasoner [*Vernünftler*] into a sound critique of the understanding and of reason itself. When he has reached that point, he has no further attack to fear. For he then distinguishes what he possesses from what lies wholly outside it, to which he makes no claims and about which he cannot be drawn into disputes. The sceptical procedure is not indeed in itself satisfying to the questions of reason, but it is none the less preparatory, since it serves to awaken reason's circumspection and to point to thorough means by which reason can secure itself in its rightful possessions.",["- \"Zuchtmeister\" rendered \"taskmaster who disciplines\"; \"Vernünftler\" is pejorative (one who merely argues from reason), hence \"reasoner\" with the German given.","- Divided the second sentence at the semicolon; \"denn\" rendered \"For\".","- \"vor die Vernunftfragen\" read as \"für\"; \"vorübend\" taken as \"preparatory\" (a preliminary exercise).","- \"Critik\" rendered \"critique\", as the context concerns the Critical enquiry."]],"krv-a-1213":["Chapter I, Section III. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Respect of Hypotheses.",["- \"Hauptstück\" rendered \"Chapter\"; \"Abschnitt\" \"Section\". Heading only."]],"krv-a-1214":["Since, then, through the critique of our reason we finally know so much, namely, that in its pure and speculative employment we can in fact know nothing at all, should not this critique open up all the wider a field for hypotheses, in which it is at least permitted to invent and to opine, even if not to assert?",["- \"sie\" (in \"solte sie\") has \"Critik\" or \"Vernunft\" as possible antecedent. I take \"Critik\" (the nearer feminine noun and the sense of the argument), and supply \"this critique\" accordingly.","- \"wissen\" rendered \"know\" and \"dichten\" \"invent\"; \"meinen\" \"opine\"."]],"krv-a-1215":["Imagination is not to run wild, but is to invent under the strict supervision of reason. Something must therefore always be completely certain beforehand, and not invented or mere opinion, namely, the possibility of the object itself. In that case it is permissible to take refuge in opinion in regard to the actuality of the object. But opinion, if it is not to be groundless, must be brought into connection, as a ground of explanation, with what is actually given and consequently certain; and it is then entitled hypothesis.",["- Restated the opening conditional (\"Wo nicht etwa … soll\") as two sentences; \"Wo nicht etwa … sondern\" read as \"not to run wild but to invent\".","- \"Heißt\" rendered \"is entitled\"; \"Möglichkeit\" and \"Wirklichkeit\" kept as possibility and actuality.","- \"die aber\" refers to \"Meinung\"."]],"krv-a-1216":["Since we cannot form the least concept of the possibility of dynamical connection *a priori*, and since the category of the pure understanding serves not to devise such connections but only to understand them where they are met with in experience, we cannot, in conformity with these categories, originally think out a single object having a new property not empirically specifiable, and make it the foundation of a permissible hypothesis. To do so would be to put at the basis of reason empty figments of the brain in place of concepts of things. Thus it is not permissible to devise any new original powers [*Kräfte*]: for instance, an understanding capable of intuiting its object without senses, or a force of attraction without any contact, or a new kind of substances, for instance one that would be present in space without impenetrability. Consequently also we may not devise a community of substances differing from all that experience supplies, nor a presence other than in space, nor a duration other than in time alone. In a word, it is possible for our reason to use only the conditions of possible experience as conditions of the possibility of things. It can by no means create such conditions, as it were, for itself, independently of these; for concepts of this kind, though free from contradiction, would yet be without an object.",["- Divided the long sentence; the string \"folglich auch keine Gemeinschaft … keine Dauer\" is made grammatically dependent on \"ist nicht erlaubt\" by supplying \"we may not devise\", \"nor\", a supplied construction rather than a literal one.","- \"Kräfte\" rendered \"powers\" in \"ursprüngliche Kräfte\", \"Anziehungskraft\" \"force of attraction\"; \"anschauen\" \"intuit\".","- \"Hirngespinste\" rendered \"figments of the brain\"."]],"krv-a-1217":["The concepts of reason are, as has been said, mere ideas. They have, to be sure, no object in any experience, but they do not on that account signify objects that are invented and at the same time assumed to be possible. They are thought only problematically, in order that, in relation to them (as heuristic fictions), we may ground regulative principles of the systematic employment of the understanding in the field of experience. If we depart from this, they are mere thought-entities [*Gedankendinge*], whose possibility cannot be demonstrated, and which therefore cannot be made the basis of an explanation of actual appearances by means of a hypothesis. To think the soul as simple is quite permissible, in order that, in accordance with this idea, we may take a complete and necessary unity of all the powers of the mind as the principle of our judging of its inner appearances, although we cannot have insight into that unity *in concreto*. But to assume the soul as simple substance (a transcendent concept) would be a proposition which would be not only indemonstrable (as are many physical hypotheses) but also altogether arbitrary and ventured blindly. For the simple cannot occur in any experience at all; and if by substance we here understand the persistent object of sensible intuition, the possibility of a simple appearance cannot be seen at all. Merely intelligible beings, or merely intelligible properties of the things of the sensible world, cannot be admitted as opinion with any well-grounded warrant of reason, although (since we have no concepts of their possibility or impossibility) they cannot be dogmatically denied either, on the strength of any supposedly better insight.",["- \"vor möglich angenommene\" rendered \"assumed to be possible\"; \"Gemüthskräfte\" \"powers of the mind\".","- Restated the \"denn\"-less \"weil\" clause as a separate sentence beginning \"For\".","- \"hevristische\" rendered \"heuristic\"; \"Sinnenwelt\" \"sensible world\"; \"Befugniß\" \"warrant\".","- \"keine ... Einsicht ... dogmatisch ableugnen\": the \"auch\" is carried by \"cannot be … denied either\"."]],"krv-a-1218":["For the explanation of given appearances no other things or grounds of explanation can be adduced than those which, according to already known laws of appearances, have been set in connection with the given appearances. A transcendental hypothesis, in which a mere idea of reason would be used to explain natural things, would therefore be no explanation at all; for what we do not sufficiently understand from known empirical principles would be explained by something of which we understand nothing whatever. Moreover, the principle of such a hypothesis would really serve only to satisfy reason, and not to promote the employment of the understanding in regard to objects. Order and purposiveness in nature must in turn be explained from natural grounds and in accordance with natural laws; and here even the wildest hypotheses, if only they are physical, are more tolerable than a hyperphysical one, that is, an appeal to a divine author whom we presuppose for this purpose. For that would be a principle of lazy reason (*ignava ratio*), to pass over at once all the causes whose objective reality, at least as regards possibility, we can still come to know through continued experience, in order to rest in a mere idea which is very convenient to reason. As regards the absolute totality of the ground of explanation in the series of such causes, this can create no hindrance in the case of objects of the world; for since these are nothing but appearances, nothing completed can ever be hoped for in the synthesis of the series of conditions in them.",["- \"Auch\" rendered \"Moreover\"; \"Zweckmässigkeit\" \"purposiveness\".","- \"derselben\" in \"in der Reihe derselben\" taken to refer to the causes (Ursachen); I supply \"such causes\".","- \"den man zu diesem Behuf voraussezt\" rendered \"whom we presuppose for this purpose\".","- Text reads \"ignaua\"; corrected to *ignava*."]],"krv-a-1219":["Transcendental hypotheses for the speculative employment of reason, and a liberty to make use of hyperphysical grounds of explanation, in case of need, to supply the lack of physical ones, cannot be permitted at all. This is partly because reason is thereby not advanced in the least, but on the contrary cuts off the whole progress of its employment; partly because this licence must in the end deprive it of all the fruits of cultivating its own proper soil, namely experience. For if the explanation of nature becomes difficult for us here or there, we have always at hand a transcendent ground of explanation which relieves us of that enquiry; and our investigation concludes, not through insight, but through the complete incomprehensibility of a principle which was so contrived beforehand that it had to contain the concept of the absolutely first.",["- Divided the opening sentence after \"gar nicht gestattet werden\"; the subject (\"Hypothesen ... und eine Freiheit\") has a singular verb (\"kan\") in the German, and I used the plural.","- \"Absolutersten\" rendered \"the absolutely first\".","- \"sie\" and \"ihres eigenthümlichen Bodens\" refer to reason (Vernunft)."]],"krv-a-1220":["The second requirement for a hypothesis to be worthy of acceptance is its sufficiency to determine *a priori* the consequences that are given. If to this end we are compelled to summon auxiliary hypotheses, they arouse the suspicion of being mere inventions. For each of them in itself requires the same justification as the thought laid at the foundation needed, and consequently it cannot furnish a competent witness. Suppose that, on the presupposition of an unlimitedly perfect cause, there is indeed no lack of grounds of explanation for all the purposiveness, order and magnitude found in the world. Yet that supposition still requires new hypotheses, to be saved against the objections which arise from the deviations and evils that show themselves, at least according to our concepts. Again, if the simple self-subsistence of the human soul, laid at the foundation of its appearances, is assailed by the difficulties arising from its phenomena, which resemble the alterations of a matter (growth and decrease), new hypotheses must be summoned to its aid. These are not indeed without plausibility, but are without any warrant save what the opinion adopted as the principal ground gives them, which they are nevertheless supposed to defend.",["- Restated the long \"Wenn … so\" sentences as several; supplied \"Suppose that\" and \"Again\" to mark the parallel conditionals.","- \"iene\" (in \"so bedarf iene doch\") refers to the presupposition of the perfect cause; rendered \"that supposition\".","- \"Selbstständigkeit\" rendered \"self-subsistence\"; \"Beglaubigung\" \"warrant\"; \"tüchtigen Zeugen\" \"competent witness\".","- \"der sie gleichwol das Wort reden sollen\": \"Meinung\" is the antecedent of \"der\"; rendered \"which they are nevertheless supposed to defend\"."]],"krv-a-1221":["If the assertions of reason here cited by way of example (the immaterial unity of the soul and the existence of a supreme being) are to count not as hypotheses but as dogmas proved *a priori*, then they are not in question here at all. In that case, however, we must take care that the proof has the apodeictic certainty of a demonstration. For to try to render the reality of such ideas merely probable is an absurd undertaking, just as absurd as if we proposed to prove a proposition of geometry merely probably. Reason, when separated from all experience, can know anything only *a priori* and as necessary, or not at all. Its judgment is therefore never opinion; it is either abstention from all judgment, or apodeictic certainty. Opinions and probable judgments about what belongs to things can occur only as grounds of explanation of what is actually given, or as consequences, according to empirical laws, of what underlies as actual; that is to say, they can occur only in the series of the objects of experience. Outside this field, to opine is merely to play with thoughts. The one exception would be where we had merely the opinion, regarding an uncertain way of judging, that on it we might perhaps find the truth.",[]],"krv-a-1222":["Although in merely speculative questions of pure reason hypotheses have no place as a foundation on which to build propositions, they are nevertheless entirely permissible if employed merely for defence, that is, not in the dogmatic, but still in the polemical use. By defence I understand, however, not an increase in the grounds of proof for one's assertion, but the mere frustration of the opponent's pretended insights, which are intended to impair the proposition we maintain. Now all synthetic propositions from pure reason have this peculiarity: that although he who asserts the reality of certain ideas never knows enough to make this proposition of his certain, the opponent, on the other side, can know just as little in order to assert the contrary. This equality in the lot of human reason favours neither party in speculative knowledge; and speculative knowledge is therefore the proper battle-ground of feuds that can never be settled. But it will be shown in the sequel that, in respect of the practical use, reason has a right to assume something which in the field of mere speculation it would on no account be entitled to presuppose without sufficient grounds of proof. For all such presuppositions impair the perfection of speculation, with which the practical interest is not in the least concerned. In the practical sphere, then, reason is in possession, and the legitimacy of its possession it is not required to prove; indeed it could not in fact furnish the proof. The burden of proof therefore falls on the opponent. But since he knows as little of the object in question, in order to demonstrate its non-existence, as the former, who asserts its reality, knows in order to maintain it, an advantage shows itself here on the side of him who asserts something as a practically necessary presupposition (*melior est conditio possidentis*). He is free, as it were in self-defence, to make use on behalf of his good cause of the same means as the opponent employs against it, that is, hypotheses. These are in no way meant to strengthen the proof of his position; they are meant only to show that the opponent understands far too little of the subject of dispute for him to flatter himself that he has the advantage over us in speculative insight.",[]],"krv-a-1223":["Hypotheses are therefore permitted in the field of pure reason only as weapons of war, not to found a right upon them, but only to defend it. The opponent, however, we must always look for in ourselves. For speculative reason in its transcendental employment is in itself dialectical. The objections which may be feared lie within ourselves. We must seek them out, like old claims which, though they never lapse through prescription, may still be asserted, in order to found an eternal peace upon their annulment. An outward tranquillity is merely apparent. The germ of these attacks, which lies in the nature of human reason, must be extirpated. But how can we extirpate it unless we give it freedom, indeed nourishment, to send out shoots, so that it may reveal itself, and afterwards destroy it, root and all? Devise objections, therefore, even such as no opponent has yet hit upon, and lend him weapons, or grant him the most favourable position that he could wish for. There is nothing to be feared in this, but something to be hoped, namely, that you will procure for yourselves a possession that will never again, in all future time, be open to challenge.",[]],"krv-a-1224":["Your complete equipment now includes also the hypotheses of pure reason. These, though only leaden weapons (since they are not steeled by any law of experience), are still always as effective as those which any opponent may employ against you. Suppose, then, that you are met by the difficulty, in opposition to the nature of the soul, which you have assumed (on some other, non-speculative ground) to be immaterial and not subject to any bodily change, that experience nevertheless seems to prove that both the exaltation and the derangement of our mental powers are merely different modifications of our organs. You can then weaken the force of this proof by assuming that our body is nothing but the fundamental appearance to which, as condition, the whole faculty of sensibility, and with it all thinking, is related in our present state (in life). The separation from the body would be the end of this sensible employment of your faculty of knowledge, and the beginning of the intellectual. The body would then be not the cause of thinking but merely a restricting condition of it. It would accordingly have to be regarded as furthering the sensible and animal life, but by so much the more as a hindrance to the pure and spiritual life; and the dependence of the former upon the bodily constitution would prove nothing for the dependence of our whole life upon the state of our organs. You can, however, go still further, and may even discover new doubts, either not yet raised or not pressed far enough.",[]],"krv-a-1225":["The contingency of procreation, which in human beings, as in irrational creatures, depends upon opportunity, but also often upon subsistence, upon the government, its moods and caprices, and often even upon vice, constitutes a great difficulty for the opinion that a creature, whose life began under circumstances so unimportant and so entirely left to our freedom, will continue to exist through eternities. As regards the continuance of the whole species (here on earth), this difficulty counts for little, since chance in the single instance is none the less subject to a rule in the whole. But to expect, in respect of each individual, so powerful an effect from such trifling causes certainly seems questionable. Against this you can, however, bring forward a transcendental hypothesis: that all life is properly only intelligible, is not subject to changes of time, and neither began through birth nor will be ended through death. [You may suppose] that this life is nothing but a mere appearance, that is, a sensible representation of the pure spiritual life; that the whole sensible world is a mere image, which hovers before our present mode of knowledge and, like a dream, has in itself no objective reality; that if we were to intuit things and ourselves as they are, we should see ourselves in a world of spiritual natures, with which our one true community neither began through birth nor will cease through the death of the body (these being mere appearances); and so on.",["- 1221: \"Dogmate\" rendered \"dogmas\"; \"Enthaltung von allem Urtheile\" as \"abstention from all judgment\". \"Meinen\" is rendered \"to opine\", with \"opinion\" for Meinung. The last sentence was restated so that the exception is stated plainly.","- 1222: The sentence \"Diese Gleichheit … Fehden\" is smoothed. \"und da ist auch der rechte Kampfplatz\" is taken to refer to speculative knowledge, and I have said so. The pronoun \"sie\" in \"Dort ist sie also im Besitze\" is resolved as reason, and \"Dort\" as the practical sphere (supplied).","- 1222: \"Vermehrung der Beweisgründe\" and \"Scheineinsichten\" are rendered \"increase in the grounds of proof\" and \"pretended insights\". \"Abbruch thun\" is \"impair\". The Latin tag is kept in italics.","- 1223: \"Vernichtigung\" is rendered \"annulment\". \"Ansprüchen, … niemals veriährenden\" is rendered by \"claims which never lapse through prescription\", with a clause added (\"may still be asserted\") to make the point of the comparison explicit. The final sentence is slightly reordered.","- 1224: \"Fundamentalerscheinung\" is rendered \"fundamental appearance\". \"Erkentnißkraft\" is \"faculty of knowledge\", and \"Vermögen der Sinnlichkeit\" is \"faculty of sensibility\". \"Fondern\" is an obvious OCR slip for \"sondern\", silently corrected. The opening sentence about the \"Schwierigkeit\" is restated and divided.","- 1225: The long final sentence continues the construction \"daß … sey\" after \"Hypothese\" without a governing verb. I have supplied \"[You may suppose]\" in brackets. \"Meinung\" is rendered \"opinion\". \"Erkentnißart\" is \"mode of knowledge\", and \"iezigen\" is \"present\"."]],"krv-a-1226":["Now we know nothing whatever of all that we here put forward hypothetically against the attack, nor do we seriously assert it; it is not even an idea of reason, but merely a concept devised for defence. Nevertheless we are proceeding in entire conformity with reason when we show the opponent only this: he believes that he has exhausted all possibility, and falsely passes off the lack of its empirical conditions as a proof of the complete impossibility of what we believe; but he can no more embrace the whole field of possible things in themselves by mere laws of experience than we can, outside experience, acquire anything on a well-grounded basis for our reason.\n\nHe who provides such hypothetical counter-measures against the presumptions of a boldly denying opponent must not be taken to wish to make them his own, as his genuine opinions. He abandons them as soon as he has disposed of the opponent's dogmatic conceit. For however modest and moderate it may appear when someone adopts a merely refusing and negating attitude to the assertions of others, yet as soon as he seeks to have these objections of his count as proofs of the contrary, his claim is always no less proud and conceited than if he had taken the side of the affirmative party and its assertion.",["- Restated the first long sentence as two; the *indem* clauses are folded into \"when we show… only this\", and the *indem er… ausgiebt* clause is turned into a coordinate statement.","- \"Vernunftidee\" rendered \"idea of reason\"; \"Begriff\" as \"concept\".","- \"des von uns Geglaubten\" taken as dependent on \"Unmöglichkeit\": \"of what we believe\".","- \"dreustverneinenden\" (= dreist verneinenden) rendered \"boldly denying\".","- \"vor\" in \"vor einen Beweis\", \"vor unsere Vernunft\", and \"davor gehalten\" is the old form of *für*."]],"krv-a-1227":["It is thus evident that in the speculative employment of reason hypotheses have no validity as opinions in themselves, but only relatively to opposed transcendent presumptions. For the extension of the principles of possible experience to the possibility of things in general is just as transcendent as the assertion of the objective reality of such concepts as can find their objects nowhere save outside the limits of all possible experience. What pure reason judges assertorically must (like everything that reason knows) be necessary, or it is nothing at all. Consequently pure reason really contains no opinions. The hypotheses referred to are, however, only problematic judgments, which at least cannot be refuted, although they certainly cannot be proved by anything. They are therefore not private opinions; yet they cannot properly be dispensed with, even for inner peace of mind, against scruples that arise. But in this character they must be preserved, and we must carefully guard against their presenting themselves as if self-authenticating and of some absolute validity, and so drowning reason in fictions and delusions.",["- \"Qualität\" rendered \"character\" (not the category-term); \"Hypothesen… problematische Urtheile\" kept literal.","- \"Urtheile\" rendered \"judgments\"; \"obiective Realität\" as \"objective reality\".","- Divided the sentence beginning \"Die gedachte Hypothesen\" into two, with \"They are therefore…\" starting the second.","- \"sich regende Scrupel\" taken as \"scruples that arise\" (stirring in the mind).","- \"ersäufen\" = \"drown\"."]],"krv-a-1228":["Chapter I, Section IV. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Respect of Its Proofs.",["- \"Hauptstück\" rendered \"Chapter\"; the heading follows the German order, \"Des ersten Hauptstücks\" being given as \"Chapter I\"."]],"krv-a-1229":["Proofs of transcendental and synthetic propositions have this peculiarity, among all proofs of a synthetic knowledge *a priori*: that in them reason may not turn directly to the object by means of its concepts, but must first establish the objective validity of the concepts and the possibility of their synthesis *a priori*. This is not merely a necessary rule of prudence; it concerns the very essence and possibility of the proofs themselves. If I am to go beyond the concept of an object *a priori*, this is impossible without a special guide lying outside that concept. In mathematics it is *a priori* intuition that guides my synthesis, and there all inferences can be drawn immediately from pure intuition. In transcendental knowledge, so long as it has to do merely with concepts of the understanding, this guiding line is possible experience.\n\nThe proof does not show that the given concept (for instance, of what happens) leads directly to another concept (that of a cause); for such a transition would be a leap which could not be justified at all. What it shows is that experience itself, and therefore the object of experience, would be impossible without such a connection. Accordingly the proof had at the same time to indicate the possibility of arriving synthetically and *a priori* at a certain knowledge of things which was not contained in the concept of them.\n\nWithout this attention, proofs are like waters that have broken their banks: they run wild and across country, wherever the bent of hidden association chances to carry them. The semblance of conviction, which rests on subjective causes of association and is taken for insight into a natural affinity, cannot counterbalance the misgiving which such hazardous steps must justly arouse. Hence all attempts to prove the principle of sufficient reason have, by the universal admission of those versed in the matter, been in vain; and before transcendental criticism appeared, men, since they could not after all abandon this principle, preferred to appeal defiantly to common sense (a refuge which always proves that the cause of reason is desperate) rather than attempt new dogmatic proofs.",["- Split the opening and the \"Der Beweis zeigt nemlich…\" sentences into separate paragraphs of shorter sentences; \"ienen\" refers to the proofs, \"seiner Begriffe\" read as \"its [reason's] concepts\" (*seiner* for *ihrer*).","- \"Erkentniß\" in \"Im transscendentalen Erkentniß\" rendered \"transcendental knowledge\" (neuter/feminine slip in the text).","- \"mußte\" (past) retained in \"had… to indicate\"; some editions read *muß*, so the tense is doubtful.","- \"Zuslucht\" is an OCR slip for *Zuflucht*, silently corrected: \"refuge\".","- \"Critik\" rendered \"criticism\", as context suits.","- \"Leitfaden\" = \"guide\"; \"Richtschnur\" = \"guiding line\"."]],"krv-a-1230":["If, however, the proposition for which a proof is to be given is an assertion of pure reason, and if I propose by means of mere ideas to go beyond my concepts of experience, then the proposition would have to contain, still more fully, the justification of such a step of synthesis (if it be possible at all) as a necessary condition of its probative force. However plausible, therefore, the supposed proof of the simple nature of our thinking substance from the unity of apperception may be, one objection to it is unavoidable. Absolute simplicity is not a concept that can be related directly to a perception; as an idea it can only be inferred. It is therefore quite impossible to see how mere consciousness, which is contained, or at least can be contained, in all thinking, and which, although in this respect a simple representation, is yet to lead me to the consciousness and knowledge of a thing in which thinking alone can be contained.\n\nFor when I represent to myself the force of my body in motion, the body is in this respect for me an absolute unity, and my representation of it is simple. I can therefore express this representation by the motion of a point, since the volume of the body does not here matter, and, without diminution of the force, can be as small as I please, and so can be thought as situated in a point. Yet I shall not infer from this that, if nothing is given me except the moving force of a body, the body can be thought as a simple substance, merely because its representation abstracts from all magnitude of spatial content and is therefore simple.\n\nNow it is by the fact that the simple in abstraction is quite different from the simple in the object, and that the I, which in the former sense contains no manifold within itself, may in the latter sense, where it signifies the soul itself, be a very complex concept, that is, may contain and designate very much under it, that I discover a paralogism. But in order to have a presentiment of this beforehand (for without such a preliminary conjecture no suspicion against the proof would be conceived), it is absolutely necessary to have at hand a permanent criterion of the possibility of such synthetic propositions as are to prove more than experience can give. This criterion consists in the following: that the proof be directed not straight to the predicate required, but only by means of a principle of the possibility of extending our given concept *a priori* to ideas and of realising these.\n\nIf this caution is always employed, and if, before the proof is even attempted, one has wisely taken counsel with oneself as to how and on what ground of hope one may expect such an extension through pure reason, and whence, in such a case, one is to derive these insights, which cannot be developed from concepts and cannot be anticipated in relation to possible experience, then much toilsome and yet fruitless effort can be spared. One then demands of reason nothing that manifestly exceeds its power; or rather, since reason, in the fits of its speculative passion for extension, does not willingly suffer itself to be limited, one subjects it to the discipline of abstinence.",[]],"krv-a-1231":["The first rule, then, is this: not to attempt any transcendental proofs without having first considered, and justified to oneself, whence one proposes to obtain the principles upon which one intends to build them, and with what right one can expect from these principles a successful issue of the inferences. If they are principles of the understanding (e.g. of causality), it is vain to try by their means to arrive at ideas of pure reason; for those principles hold only of objects of possible experience. If they are to be principles from pure reason, then once more all labour is vain. For reason does indeed have such principles, but as objective principles they are one and all dialectical, and can at most be valid only as regulative principles of the systematically connected employment of experience. But if proofs of this alleged kind are already in existence, then set against the deceptive conviction the *non liquet* of your matured power of judgment; and although you cannot yet penetrate the illusion of such proofs, you are fully entitled to demand the deduction of the principles employed in them. If these principles are supposed to have sprung from mere reason, such a deduction can never be furnished you. Thus you need not even trouble yourselves with the working out and refutation of every groundless illusion, but can dismiss, all at once and in a body, the whole dialectic, inexhaustible as it is in artifices, at the tribunal of a critical reason which demands laws.",[]],"krv-a-1232":["The second peculiarity of transcendental proofs is this: that for each transcendental proposition only a single proof can be found. If I have to infer not from concepts but from the intuition which corresponds to a concept, whether it be a pure intuition, as in mathematics, or an empirical intuition, as in natural science, the intuition which I take as basis gives me a manifold material for synthetic propositions. I can connect this material in more than one way, and since I am permitted to start from more than one point, I can arrive at the same proposition by different paths.",[]],"krv-a-1233":["Now every transcendental proposition, however, proceeds from one concept only, and states the synthetic condition of the possibility of the object in accordance with this concept. The ground of proof can therefore be only a single one, since apart from this concept there is nothing further by which the object could be determined. The proof can therefore contain nothing more than the determination of an object in general in accordance with this concept, which is likewise only a single one. In the Transcendental Analytic, for instance, we derived the principle that everything which happens has a cause from the single condition of the objective possibility of a concept of that which happens in general: namely, that the determination of an event in time, and therefore this [event] as belonging to experience, would be impossible if it did not stand under such a dynamical rule. This is also the only possible ground of proof. For it is only in so far as an object is determined for the concept by means of the law of causality that the represented event has objective validity, that is, truth. Other proofs of this principle have indeed been attempted, for instance from contingency. But when this is closely examined, no mark of contingency can be found save that of happening, that is, of existence preceded by the non-existence of the object; and so we always come back again to the same ground of proof. If the proposition is to be proved that everything which thinks is simple, we do not dwell upon the manifold of thinking, but keep solely to the concept of the I, which is simple and to which all thinking is related. The case is similar with the transcendental proof of the existence of God, which rests solely upon the reciprocability of the concepts of the most real and of the necessary being, and cannot be sought anywhere else.",["- krv-a-1230: The sentence \"gar nicht einzusehen ist, wie mich das blosse Bewustseyn … überführen solle\" is syntactically tangled; I split it off from the \"Bedenklichkeit\" clause and left the closing \"solle\" clause as it stands. The English is therefore incomplete in construction (\"is yet to lead me …\"). Smoothing is possible but would alter the sentence.","- krv-a-1230: \"Bedenklichkeit\" rendered \"objection\"; \"Paralogism\" kept as \"paralogism\"; \"Apperception\" as \"apperception\"; \"Vermögen\" as \"power\"; \"Kraft\" as \"force\".","- krv-a-1230: \"in welchem das Denken allein enthalten seyn kan\" read as a thing in which thinking alone (not extension etc.) can be contained; the \"Hiedurch nun, daß …\" sentence is rendered as a \"it is by the fact that … that I discover\" construction.","- krv-a-1230: \"Verstande\" in \"im ersteren Verstande\" rendered \"sense\", not \"understanding\", as the meaning requires.","- krv-a-1231: \"euch\" and \"ihr\" rendered \"you\"; \"der trüglichen Ueberzeugung das non liquet … entgegen\" kept the Latin in italics. \"Gerichtshofe\" rendered \"tribunal\".","- krv-a-1233: \"dieser\" in \"wenn dieser beim Lichten betrachtet wird\" taken to refer to the proof from contingency (Zufälligkeit); I supplied \"[event]\" for \"(Begebenheit)\" which Kant himself adds in parentheses, so the brackets are retained only as the supplied gloss.","- krv-a-1233: \"Reciprocabilität\" rendered \"reciprocability\"; \"dynamische Regel\" as \"dynamical rule\"; \"Beweisgrund\" as \"ground of proof\"."]],"krv-a-1234":["This cautionary remark greatly reduces the task of criticism in dealing with the assertions of reason. Where reason carries on its business through mere concepts, only one proof is possible, if indeed any proof is possible at all. Hence when we see the dogmatist come forward with ten proofs, we may be sure that he has none. For if he had one which (as must be the case in matters of pure reason) proved apodeictically, what need would he have of the others? His intention is the same as that of the parliamentary advocate: one argument is for this member of the bench, another for that, so that he may exploit the weakness of his judges. These judges, without going deeply into the matter, and in order to be quickly rid of the business, seize upon the first thing that happens to strike them, and decide accordingly.",["- \"Critik der Vernunftbehauptungen\" rendered \"criticism ... in dealing with the assertions of reason\"; the genitive is objective.","- \"vor diesen ... vor ienen\" (for \"für\") is rendered \"for this member of the bench ... for that\"; \"member of the bench\" is supplied from \"Richter\" in the sentence.","- The last sentence is divided and \"die\" is resolved as referring to the judges.","- \"apodictisch\" rendered \"apodeictically\"."]],"krv-a-1235":["The third peculiar rule of pure reason, when it is subjected to discipline in respect of transcendental proofs, is that its proofs must never be apagogic but always ostensive. The direct or ostensive proof is, in every kind of knowledge, that which combines conviction of the truth with insight into its sources. The apagogic proof, on the other hand, can produce certainty, but not comprehensibility of the truth in respect of its connection with the grounds of its possibility. The latter kind of proof is therefore rather an expedient in case of need than a procedure which satisfies all the aims of reason. Apagogic proofs have, however, a superiority in evidence over the direct proofs, in that contradiction always carries with it more clearness in the representation than the best connection does, and thereby approaches more nearly to the intuitive character of a demonstration.",["- \"apogogisch\" rendered \"apagogic\" (the standard form); \"oftensiv\" is read as \"ostensiv\" (old spelling or slip).","- \"Erkentniß\" = knowledge; \"Begreiflichkeit\" = comprehensibility; \"Einsicht\" = insight.","- \"diese\" in \"Doch haben diese\" is resolved as the apagogic proofs.","- \"das anschauliche einer Demonstration\" rendered \"the intuitive character of a demonstration\"."]],"krv-a-1236":["The real reason for the use of apagogic proofs in various sciences is probably this. When the grounds from which a certain modeof knowledge is to be derived are too manifold or lie too deeply hidden, we try whether it may not be reached through its consequences. Now the *modus ponens*, which infers the truth of a piece of knowledge from the truth of its consequences, would be allowable only if all possible consequences of it are true. For in that case only one ground is possible for these consequences, and that ground is therefore the true one. This procedure is, however, impracticable, since it exceeds our powers to see all the possible consequences of any proposition we may assume. Yet we do make use of this kind of inference, though admittedly with a certain indulgence, when the purpose is to prove something merely as hypothesis. We then admit an inference by analogy: if as many consequences as we have ever tried agree well with an assumed ground, all the other possible consequences will agree with it also. For this reason a hypothesis can never by this method be transformed into demonstrated truth. The *modus tollens* of inferences of reason, which infer from the consequences to the grounds, proves not only with complete strictness but also very easily. For if even a single false consequence can be drawn from a proposition, that proposition is false. Instead, then, of running through the whole series of grounds in an ostensive proof, which by way of complete insight into its possibility can lead to the truth of a piece of knowledge, we need only find a single false consequence among those that follow from its opposite. This opposite is then false, and the knowledge which we had to prove is therefore true.",["- Typographical fix: \"modeof\" should read \"mode of\"; in the translation read \"a certain mode of knowledge\" for \"eine gewisse Erkentniß\", and \"a piece of knowledge\" elsewhere, since \"knowledge\" has no plural.","- \"Kräfte\" rendered \"powers\"; \"Nachsicht\" \"indulgence\".","- \"zu diesem\" (the consequences) and \"darauf\" resolved by the argument; the long final sentence is divided, and \"dieses Gegentheil\" is made explicit as \"This opposite\".","- \"Vernunftschlüsse\" rendered \"inferences of reason\"."]],"krv-a-1237":["The apagogic mode of proof can be permitted only in those sciences in which it is impossible to substitute the subjective element in our representations for the objective, that is, for knowledge of what is in the object. Where the latter prevails, it must frequently happen that the opposite of a given proposition either contradicts merely the subjective conditions of thought and not the object, or that the two propositions contradict each other only under a subjective condition which, being falsely held to be objective, is false, so that both may be false, and from the falsity of the one no conclusion can be drawn to the truth of the other.",["- \"Subiective/Obiective\" rendered as \"the subjective element / the objective\" (neuter substantive).","- \"dem Obiectiven ... unterschieben\" rendered \"substitute ... for\"; the closing clause, which is irregular in the German (\"und da die Bedingung falsch ist\"), is smoothed, with \"being falsely held to be objective, is false\" following the sense that the condition is false.","- \"Wo dieses leztere aber herrschend ist\" is taken as \"where the latter [the subjective substitution] prevails\"."]],"krv-a-1238":["In mathematics this subreption is impossible; and it is there, accordingly, that apagogic proofs have their proper place. In natural science, since everything there rests on empirical intuitions, that surreption can for the most part be prevented through many comparisons of observations; but in natural science this mode of proof is for the most part of no importance. The transcendental attempts of pure reason, however, are all made within the proper medium of dialectical illusion, that is, of the subjective, which presents itself to reason in its premisses as objective, or even forces itself upon it. Here, as regards synthetic propositions, it cannot be permitted to justify one's assertions by refuting the opposite. For either this refutation is nothing but the mere representation of the conflict of the opposed opinion with the subjective conditions of comprehensibility by our reason, which does nothing to show that the thing itself is to be rejected. (Thus, for instance, unconditioned necessity in the existence of a being absolutely cannot be comprehended by us, and consequently opposes itself subjectively, and with right, to every speculative proof of a necessary supreme being, but wrongly to the possibility of such an original being in itself.) Or both parties, the affirming as well as the denying, deceived by the transcendental illusion, take as their foundation an impossible concept of the object. In that case the rule holds: *non entis nulla sunt praedicata*; that is, whatever has been asserted of the object, whether affirmatively or negatively, is incorrect, and we cannot by apagogic refutation of the opposite arrive at knowledge of the truth. For example, if it is presupposed that the world of sense is given in itself in its totality, it is false that it must be either infinite in space or finite and limited, because both are false. For appearances (as mere representations) which yet would be given in themselves (as objects) are something impossible; and the infinity of this imagined whole would indeed be unconditioned, but would contradict (because everything in appearances is conditioned) the unconditioned determination of magnitude which is yet presupposed in the concept.",["- \"sie\" in \"daher haben sie daselbst auch ihren eigentlichen Platz\" is resolved as the apagogic proofs (the antecedent is distant); \"Subreption\" and \"Erschleichung\" rendered \"subreption\" and \"surreption\" to keep the two words distinct.","- \"Prämissen\" rendered \"premisses\"; \"Sinnenwelt\" \"world of sense\"; \"Grössenbestimmung\" \"determination of magnitude\".","- The long \"Denn, entweder ...\" sentence is divided; the parenthesis is kept as a parenthesis but set as its own sentence, and \"in that case\" is supplied for the \"und da gilt\".","- \"Wesens ... Urwesens\" rendered \"being ... original being\"."]],"krv-a-1239":["The apagogic [apogogische] mode of proof is also the real illusion by which the admirers of the thoroughness of our dogmatic reasoners have always been held in suspense. It is, as it were, the champion who seeks to prove the honour and indisputable right of the party he has taken up by declaring himself ready to fight with anyone who might care to doubt it. But such boasting settles nothing in the matter itself; it determines only the relative strength of the opponents, and even that only on the side of the one who acts as aggressor. The spectators, seeing that each in his turn is now victor and now vanquished, often take occasion from this to doubt sceptically the very object of the dispute. They have, however, no ground for doing so, and it is enough to call out to them: *non defensoribus istis tempus eget* [such defenders the time does not need]. Each must conduct his case directly, by means of a legitimate proof, carried through by a transcendental deduction of the grounds of proof, so that we may see what his claims of reason have to advance on their own behalf. For if his opponent relies on subjective grounds, he is indeed easily refuted, but with no advantage to the dogmatist, who commonly clings in the same way to the subjective causes of judgment, and can likewise be driven into a corner by his opponent. If, however, both parties proceed merely directly, then either they will themselves notice the difficulty, indeed the impossibility, of discovering the title to their assertions, and will in the end be able to appeal only to prescription; or else criticism [Critik] will readily detect the dogmatic illusion, and will constrain pure reason to give up its pretensions, pushed too high in the speculative employment, and to withdraw within the limits of its own proper territory, namely, practical principles.",[]],"krv-a-1240":["Second Chapter of the Transcendental Doctrine of Method. The Canon of Pure Reason.",[]],"krv-a-1241":["It is humiliating to human reason that in its pure employment it accomplishes nothing, and indeed stands in need of a discipline to curb its extravagances and to guard against the illusions which arise from them. On the other hand, it raises reason again and gives it confidence in itself, that it can and must exercise this discipline itself, without allowing any other censorship over it. It is likewise encouraging that the limits which it is constrained to set to its speculative employment at the same time restrict the sophistical pretensions of every opponent, and so can secure against all attack whatever may still remain to it of its previously exaggerated demands. The greatest, and perhaps the only, utility of all philosophy of pure reason is therefore presumably only negative. It serves, that is to say, not as an organon for the extension [of knowledge], but as a discipline for the determination of limits; and instead of discovering truth, it has only the quiet merit of guarding against errors.",[]],"krv-a-1242":["Yet there must surely be somewhere a source of positive modes of knowledge which belong to the domain of pure reason, and which perhaps give occasion to errors only through misunderstanding, while in fact constituting the goal of reason's zeal. For to what other cause could we ascribe the irrepressible desire to gain a firm footing somewhere beyond the bounds of experience? Reason has a presentiment of objects which carry with them a great interest for it. It enters upon the path of mere speculation in order to draw near to them; but they flee before it. Presumably we may hope for better fortune for reason on the one path that still remains open to it, namely, that of practical employment.",[]],"krv-a-1243":["By a canon I understand the sum total of the *a priori* principles of the correct employment of certain faculties of knowledge in general. Thus general logic, in its analytic part, is a canon for understanding and reason in general, but only as regards form, since it abstracts from all content. So the transcendental analytic was the canon of the pure understanding; for the understanding alone is capable of true synthetic knowledge *a priori*. But where no correct employment of a faculty of knowledge is possible, there is no canon. Now all synthetic knowledge of pure reason in its speculative employment is, according to all the proofs so far given, altogether impossible. There is therefore no canon of its speculative employment (for this is dialectical throughout), and all transcendental logic is in this respect nothing but discipline. Consequently, if there is any correct employment of pure reason at all, in which case there must also be a canon for it, this canon will concern not the speculative but the practical employment of reason, which we shall now proceed to investigate.",[]],"krv-a-1244":["First Section of the Canon of Pure Reason. The Ultimate End of the Pure Employment of Our Reason.",[]],"krv-a-1245":["Reason is driven by a propensity of its nature to go beyond the empirical employment, to venture in a pure employment, and by means of mere ideas, to the utmost limits of all knowledge, and to find rest only in the completion of its circle, in a self-subsistent systematic whole. Is this striving now founded merely on its speculative interest, or rather solely and exclusively on its practical interest?",[]],"krv-a-1246":["I shall now set aside the success which pure reason achieves in the speculative sphere, and shall ask only about those problems the solution of which constitutes its ultimate end, whether reason attain this end or not, and in respect of which all other ends have the value of mere means. These highest ends must, in accordance with the nature of reason, in their turn have unity, in order that they may promote in union that interest of humanity which is subordinate to no higher interest.",["- 1239: Kant's one long sentence on the \"champion\" was divided at \"obgleich\" and at \"zwar auch nur\"; \"Blendwerk\" rendered \"illusion\"; \"hingehalten\" as \"held in suspense\" (a choice).","- 1239: \"Critik\" rendered \"criticism\" as the context (the critical method) suits; \"Veriährung\" = \"prescription\" (legal term). The Latin tag is quoted from Ovid-type usage; gloss in brackets is mine.","- 1239: \"vor\" in \"vor Vorteil/vor sie\" is eighteenth-century \"für/vor\"; resolved by sense (\"for\").","- 1241: \"Zutrauen\" given as \"confidence\"; the sentence beginning \"imgleichen\" was split and \"encouraging\" supplied in the English to carry the dependent construction.","- 1241: \"[of knowledge]\" supplied after \"extension\" (Erweiterung), as the sense requires.","- 1242: \"Erkentnissen\" = \"modes of knowledge\" (plural); \"Beeiferung\" = \"zeal\"; \"vor sie\" = \"for it\".","- 1243: \"Erkentnißvermögen/Erkentnißkraft\" = \"faculties of knowledge\"; \"denn der\" resolved as \"the understanding alone,\" not \"the analytic.\"","- 1246: \"Glück\" rendered \"success\"; \"Aufgaben\" = \"problems\"; \"vereinigt\" taken adverbially, \"in union\"."]],"krv-a-1247":["The final aim to which the speculation of reason in its transcendental employment ultimately leads concerns three objects: the freedom of the will, the immortality of the soul, and the existence of God. In regard to all three, the merely speculative interest of reason is very slight; and for the sake of that interest alone we should hardly undertake a wearisome labour of transcendental enquiry, struggling with unceasing obstacles. For whatever discoveries might be made in these matters, we could make no use of them *in concreto*, that is, in the investigation of nature, such as would prove their utility.\n\nThe will may be free, but this can concern only the intelligible cause of our willing. For as regards the phenomena in which the will expresses itself, that is, our actions, we must, in accordance with an inviolable fundamental maxim, without which we cannot exercise reason in its empirical employment, explain them in no other way than all other appearances of nature, namely, in accordance with the unchangeable laws of nature.\n\nSecondly, the spiritual nature of the soul (and with it the soul's immortality) may be capable of being understood. Even so, no account can be taken of it, either as a ground of explanation of the appearances of this life, or in regard to the special character of the future state. For our concept of an incorporeal nature is merely negative; it does not in the least extend our knowledge, and offers no suitable material for inferences, save only such as can pass as fictions, which philosophy does not permit.\n\nThirdly, even if the existence of a supreme intelligence were proved, we should indeed be able to render comprehensible to ourselves from it the purposiveness in the arrangement and order of the world in general. But we should in no way be entitled to derive from it any particular arrangement and order, or boldly to infer such an order where it is not perceived. For it is a necessary rule of the speculative employment of reason that we must not pass by natural causes, nor abandon what we can learn from experience, in order to derive something we know from what wholly surpasses all our knowledge.\n\nIn a word, these three propositions remain always transcendent for speculative reason, and have no immanent employment, that is, none admissible for objects of experience and therefore in any way useful to us. Considered in themselves, they are quite idle, and yet extremely arduous, exertions of our reason.",["- Long opening sentence and the following ones divided into separate paragraphs by the three objects; \"Denn\" and \"Es mag... auch\" kept as concessive constructions.","- \"Phänomene\" rendered \"phenomena\", \"Erscheinungen\" \"appearances\", to keep Kant's two words apart.","- \"Erkentniß\" = knowledge; \"Kentniß\" in \"alle unsere Kentniß\" likewise.","- \"Anstrengungen\" taken as the predicate noun of \"sind\"; \"transsc. Nachforschung\" expanded to \"transcendental enquiry\" (abbreviation in the text).","- \"wol... schwerlich\" rendered \"hardly\"; the supplied \"alone\" and \"such as would prove\" make the sense of \"in concreto... Nutzen bewiese\" explicit."]],"krv-a-1248":["If, then, these three cardinal propositions are not at all necessary to us for knowledge [*Wissen*], and yet are urgently commended to us by our reason, their importance must properly concern the practical only.",["- \"Wissen\" is rendered \"knowledge\" with the German added, since Kant here uses it rather than *Erkenntnis*.","- \"gleichwol\" rendered \"and yet\"; \"wol eigentlich\" as \"must properly\"."]],"krv-a-1249":["Everything is practical that is possible through freedom. But where the conditions for the exercise of our free will [*Willkühr*] are empirical, reason can have only a regulative employment, and can serve only to bring about unity of empirical laws. Thus, for example, in the doctrine of prudence the whole business of reason consists in uniting all the ends set before us by our inclinations into the single end, happiness, and in bringing into agreement the means of attaining it. Reason can therefore furnish only pragmatic laws of free conduct, for the attainment of the ends commended to us by the senses; it cannot furnish pure laws, determined completely *a priori*.\n\nPure practical laws, on the other hand, whose end is given completely *a priori* by reason, and which command not empirically conditioned but absolutely, would be products of pure reason. Such are the moral laws. These alone, therefore, belong to the practical employment of pure reason, and admit of a canon.",["- Two long sentences restated as shorter ones; \"um deswillen\" resolved as \"therefore\".","- \"Willkühr\" = \"will\" with the German; \"Wille\" is also \"will\", so the German is given to keep them distinct.","- \"einigen\" read as \"einzigen\" (single), as the sense requires.","- \"empirischbedingt\" printed as one word; taken as \"empirically conditioned\"."]],"krv-a-1250":["The whole equipment of reason, in the labour which can be entitled pure philosophy, is thus in fact directed only to the three problems mentioned. These themselves, however, have a more remote purpose, namely, what is to be done if the will is free, if there is a God and a future world. Since this concerns our conduct in relation to the highest end, the ultimate purpose of nature, wisely providing for us in the constitution of our reason, is properly directed to the moral alone.",["- \"Bearbeitung\" rendered \"labour\"; \"Absicht\" varied as \"purpose\" and \"aim\" for stylistic reasons.","- \"eigentlich nur aufs Moralische gestellet\" rendered \"is properly directed to the moral alone\"."]],"krv-a-1251":["Caution is necessary, however, since we are here turning our attention to an object that is foreign*) to transcendental philosophy, lest we digress into episodes and violate the unity of the system. On the other hand, in saying too little about this new material, we must not let clearness or conviction suffer. I hope to achieve both by keeping as close as possible to the transcendental, and setting wholly aside whatever may here be psychological, that is, empirical.",["- The asterisk footnote marker is retained; the footnote follows as the next passage.","- Sentence restated in three; \"Behutsamkeit nöthig\" governs both clauses."]],"krv-a-1252":["*) All practical concepts relate to objects of satisfaction or dissatisfaction, that is, of pleasure and displeasure, and therefore, at least indirectly, to objects of our feeling. But feeling is not a capacity of representation of things; it lies outside the whole faculty of knowledge. Consequently the elements of our judgments, so far as they relate to pleasure or displeasure, and therefore the elements of practical judgments, do not belong to the compass of transcendental philosophy, which is concerned solely with pure modes of knowledge *a priori*.",["- \"Vorstellungskraft\" = \"capacity of representation\"; \"Erkentnißkraft\" = \"faculty of knowledge\"; \"Erkentnissen\" = \"modes of knowledge\".","- \"mithin der practischen\" is elliptical; I supplied \"the elements of practical judgments\" and flagged it by restating the clause."]],"krv-a-1253":["And here it must first be remarked that for the present I shall employ the concept of freedom in the practical sense only. The concept in its transcendental meaning I set aside here as already dealt with above; it cannot be empirically presupposed as a ground of explanation of appearances, but is itself a problem for reason.\n\nA power of choice [*Willkühr*] is merely animal (*arbitrium brutum*) if it cannot be determined otherwise than through sensuous impulses, that is, pathologically. But that which can be determined independently of sensuous impulses, and therefore through motives represented only by reason, is entitled free choice (*arbitrium liberum*); and everything connected with this, whether as ground or as consequence, is entitled practical.\n\nPractical freedom can be proved through experience. For it is not merely what stimulates, that is, immediately affects the senses, that determines human choice. We have a power, through representations of what is itself only remotely useful or harmful, to overcome the impressions made on our sensuous faculty of desire. These deliberations as to what, in regard to our whole state, is desirable, that is, good and useful, rest on reason. Reason therefore also gives laws, which are imperatives, that is, objective laws of freedom, and which say what ought to happen, even though it perhaps never does happen. In this they differ from laws of nature, which deal only with what happens; and for this reason they are also entitled practical laws.",["- Opening sentence split; \"den\" (the concept) supplied from \"Begriffs\"; \"oben abgethan\" refers to the discussion of the Antinomy.","- \"Willkühr\" = \"power of choice\"/\"choice\" (as against *Wille*); \"heißt/genant\" = \"is entitled\".","- \"Dieienige\" resolved as \"that [Willkühr]\"; \"Practisch genant\" given in lower case.","- \"Gegenstände... Begehrungsvermögen\" = \"sensuous faculty of desire\"; \"auf entfernete Art\" rendered \"remotely\".","- \"Imperativen\" read as nominative plural predicate: \"which are imperatives\"."]],"krv-a-1254":["Whether reason itself, in those actions through which it prescribes laws, is not in turn determined by other influences, and whether that which, in reference to sensuous impulses, is entitled freedom may not, in respect of higher and more remote operating causes, be again nature—this does not concern us in the practical sphere, where we are asking reason only for the precept of conduct. It is a merely speculative question, which we can set aside so long as our purpose is directed to doing or refraining. We know practical freedom, then, through experience, as one of the natural causes, namely, as a causality of reason in the determination of the will. Transcendental freedom, on the other hand, demands an independence of this reason itself (in respect of its causality in beginning a series of appearances) from all determining causes of the sensible world. To this extent it seems to be contrary to the law of nature, and therefore to all possible experience, and so remains a problem. But this problem does not belong to reason in its practical employment. In a canon of pure reason we have therefore to deal with only two questions, which concern the practical interest of pure reason, and in regard to which a canon of its employment must be possible; namely, Is there a God? Is there a future life? The question of transcendental freedom concerns speculative knowledge only. When we are concerned with the practical, we can set it aside as quite indifferent; and sufficient discussion of it is already to be found in the Antinomy of Pure Reason.",[]],"krv-a-1255":["Second Section of the Canon of Pure Reason. The Ideal of the Highest Good, as a Determining Ground of the Ultimate End of Pure Reason.",[]],"krv-a-1256":["In its speculative employment reason led us through the field of experiences; and since complete satisfaction is never to be found for it there, it led us from experience to speculative ideas. These, however, in the end led us back again to experience, and so fulfilled their purpose in a manner which, though useful, was not at all in accordance with our expectation. One attempt still remains to us: whether pure reason is also to be met with in practical employment; whether in that employment it leads to the ideas which attain the highest ends of pure reason, as we have just stated them; and whether reason may not therefore, from the standpoint of its practical interest, grant what in respect of the speculative interest it entirely denies us.",[]],"krv-a-1257":["All the interest of my reason, speculative as well as practical, is united in the following three questions:",[]],"krv-a-1258":["1. What can I know?",[]],"krv-a-1259":["2. What ought I to do?",[]],"krv-a-1260":["3. What may I hope?",[]],"krv-a-1261":["The first question is merely speculative. We have (as I flatter myself) exhausted all possible answers to it, and have at last found the one with which reason must indeed be satisfied, and with which, so long as it does not look to the practical, it also has cause to be content. But we have remained as far removed from the two great ends to which this whole endeavour of pure reason was really directed, as if from sheer love of ease we had refused the task at the very outset. If, then, it is knowledge that is in question, this at least is certain and settled, that in regard to those two problems it can never fall to our lot.",[]],"krv-a-1262":["The second question is merely practical. As such it may indeed belong to pure reason, but it is then not transcendental but moral; and consequently it cannot in itself be a matter for our critique.",[]],"krv-a-1263":["The third question, namely, If I now do what I ought, what may I then hope? is at once practical and theoretical, in such fashion that the practical serves only as a clue that leads to the answer to the theoretical question, and, when this is pursued to its height, to the speculative question. For all hoping concerns happiness, and stands in the same relation to the practical and the moral law as knowing and the law of nature stand to the theoretical knowledge of things. The former finally issues in the inference that something is (something which determines the ultimate possible end) because something ought to happen; the latter, that something is (something which acts as supreme cause) because something happens.",[]],"krv-a-1264":["Happiness is the satisfaction of all our inclinations, extensively in respect of their manifoldness, intensively in respect of their degree, and protensively in respect of their duration. The practical law based on the motive of happiness I entitle pragmatic (rule of prudence). The law, if there be such, which has no other motive than the worthiness to be happy, I entitle moral (moral law). The former advises what we have to do if we wish to share in happiness; the latter commands how we ought to conduct ourselves in order merely to become worthy of happiness. The former is based on empirical principles; for only by way of experience can I know either what inclinations there are that seek satisfaction, or what the natural causes are that can bring about their satisfaction. The latter abstracts from inclinations and from the natural means of satisfying them, and considers only the freedom of a rational being in general, and the necessary conditions under which alone freedom is in agreement with the distribution of happiness in accordance with principles. It can therefore at least rest on mere ideas of pure reason, and be known *a priori*.",[]],"krv-a-1265":["I assume that there really are pure moral laws which determine completely *a priori* (without regard to empirical motives, that is, to happiness) the doing and refraining, that is, the employment of the freedom of a rational being in general; and that these laws command absolutely (not merely hypothetically, on the presupposition of other empirical ends), and are therefore in every respect necessary. This proposition I can rightly presuppose, appealing not only to the proofs of the most enlightened moralists, but to the moral judgment of every man, when he is willing to think such a law clearly.",[]],"krv-a-1266":["Pure reason, then, contains—not indeed in its speculative employment, but yet in a certain practical employment, namely the moral—principles of the possibility of experience, that is, of such actions as, in accordance with moral precepts, might be met with in the history of mankind. For since reason commands that such actions ought to happen, they must also be able to happen; and a special kind of systematic unity, the moral, must therefore be possible. The systematic unity of nature, on the other hand, could not be proved in accordance with speculative principles of reason. For reason has causality in respect of freedom in general, but not in respect of nature as a whole; and moral principles of reason can produce free actions, but not laws of nature. Accordingly the principles of pure reason, in their practical, and more especially in their moral employment, have objective reality.",["- 1254: Restated the long opening sentence as two clauses joined by a dash; \"Naturursachen\" rendered \"natural causes\"; \"Wissen\" = \"knowledge\" (speculative knowledge), not Erkenntnis.","- 1254: \"Natur seyn möge\" rendered \"be again nature\"; \"Antinomie\" capitalised as a title of the section.","- 1254: \"dem Naturgesetze … zuwider\" — \"contrary to the law of nature\"; the sentence \"Allein vor die Vernunft … nicht\" split off.","- 1256: \"Gesichtspuncte ihres practischen Interesse\" — \"standpoint of its practical interest\"; the final clause is loosely constructed (\"nicht … gewähren könne\") and I have rendered it as a question with \"may not therefore\".","- 1261: Sentence beginning \"Wir haben\" has irregular construction (\"sind aber … geblieben\"); smoothed into separate sentences, with \"But\" supplied.","- 1263: \"Leitfaden\" = \"clue\"; \"hoch geht\" rendered \"is pursued to its height\" (a judgment as to sense); \"Jenes/dieses\" resolved as \"former/latter\" (hoping with practical, knowing with theoretical).","- 1264: \"Bewegungsgrund\" = \"motive\"; \"protensive\" kept as \"protensively\"; \"heißen\" not used by Kant here, \"nenne ich\" = \"I entitle\".","- 1265–1266: \"Freyheit\" and \"Schlechterdings\" rendered \"freedom\", \"absolutely\"; in 1266 the sentence \"indessen daß …\" was divided and \"moral\" repeated for clarity; \"Principien der Möglichkeit der Erfahrung\" kept literally."]],"krv-a-1267":["I entitle the world a moral world, in so far as it would be in accordance with all moral laws. (Such a world it can be, in view of the freedom of rational beings; and, in view of the necessary laws of morality, it ought to be.) In this respect it is thought merely as an intelligible world, since abstraction is made in it from all conditions (ends) and even from all obstacles to morality within it (weakness or impurity of human nature). To this extent, then, it is a mere idea, though a practical one, which can and ought actually to have its influence upon the world of sense, in order to make that world, as far as possible, conformable to the idea. The idea of a moral world has therefore objective reality. It does not refer to an object of an intelligible intuition (of which we can form no thought whatsoever); it refers to the world of sense, but to that world as an object of pure reason in its practical employment, and as a *corpus mysticum* of the rational beings in it, in so far as the free will [*Willkühr*] of each, under moral laws, is in thoroughgoing systematic unity with itself and with the freedom of every other.",[]],"krv-a-1268":["That was the answer to the first of the two questions of pure reason which concern the practical interest: Do that through which you will become worthy to be happy. The second question asks: If I so conduct myself as not to be unworthy of happiness, may I also hope thereby to be able to participate in it? In answering this, the point at issue is whether the principles of pure reason, which prescribe the law *a priori*, also necessarily connect this hope with it.",[]],"krv-a-1269":["I maintain, then, that just as the moral principles are necessary in accordance with reason in its practical employment, so it is equally necessary, in accordance with reason in its theoretical employment, to assume that everyone has ground to hope for happiness in the same measure as he has made himself worthy of it in his conduct; and that the system of morality is therefore inseparably, though only in the idea of pure reason, bound up with the system of happiness.",[]],"krv-a-1270":["Now in an intelligible, that is, in the moral world, in the concept of which we abstract from all obstacles to morality (the inclinations), such a system of happiness, proportioned to morality and bound up with it, can also be thought as necessary. For freedom, partly moved and partly restrained by moral laws, would itself be the cause of universal happiness; and rational beings, under the guidance of such principles, would themselves be the authors both of their own enduring welfare and of that of others. But this system of self-rewarding morality is only an idea. Its carrying out rests on the condition that everyone does what he ought; that is, that all actions of rational beings take place as if they sprang from a supreme will which comprehends in itself, or under itself, all private will. Now the obligation from the moral law remains valid for each particular use of freedom, even if others do not conduct themselves in conformity with that law. Consequently neither the nature of the things of the world, nor the causality of the actions themselves and their relation to morality, determines how their consequences will stand in relation to happiness. The necessary connection which has been indicated, between the hope of being happy and the unceasing endeavour to make oneself worthy of happiness, cannot be known through reason if we take nature alone as our basis. It may be hoped for only if a highest reason, which commands in accordance with moral laws, is at the same time laid down as the cause of nature, and so as the basis of it.",[]],"krv-a-1271":["I entitle the idea of such an intelligence, in which the morally most perfect will, united with the highest blessedness, is the cause of all happiness in the world, in so far as happiness stands in exact proportion to morality (as worthiness to be happy), the ideal of the highest good. Pure reason can therefore find the ground of the practically necessary connection of the two elements of the highest derived good, namely of an intelligible, that is, moral world, only in the ideal of the highest original good. Now we must necessarily represent ourselves through reason as belonging to such a world, although the senses present to us nothing but a world of appearances. We shall therefore have to assume that world as a consequence of our conduct in the world of sense, since the latter does not offer us any such connection; and so as a world that for us is future. Thus God and a future life are two presuppositions which, according to the principles of pure reason, are inseparable from the obligation that this same reason imposes upon us.",[]],"krv-a-1272":["Morality in itself constitutes a system, but happiness does not, save in so far as it is distributed in exact accordance with morality. This, however, is possible only in the intelligible world, under a wise author and ruler. Reason finds itself constrained either to assume such a ruler, together with life in such a world, which we must regard as future, or to look upon the moral laws as empty figments of the brain. For the necessary consequence of these laws, which this same reason connects with them, would have to fall away without that presupposition. Hence everyone also regards the moral laws as commands. This they could not be if they did not connect with their rule, *a priori*, appropriate consequences, and thus carry with them promises and threats. But this again they cannot do unless they reside in a necessary being, as the highest good, which alone can make possible such a unity of purpose.",[]],"krv-a-1273":["Leibniz entitled the world the kingdom of grace, in so far as attention is given in it solely to the rational beings and their connection according to moral laws under the government of the highest good; and he distinguished it from the kingdom of nature, in which rational beings do indeed stand under moral laws, but expect no other results of their conduct than those which accord with the course of nature in our world of sense. To regard ourselves as in the kingdom of grace, where all happiness awaits us, except in so far as we ourselves limit our share in it through being unworthy to be happy, is a practically necessary idea of reason.",[]],"krv-a-1274":["Practical laws, in so far as they at the same time become subjective grounds of actions, that is, subjective principles, are entitled maxims. The judging of morality, in respect of its purity and consequences, takes place in accordance with ideas; the observance of its laws, in accordance with maxims.",[]],"krv-a-1275":["It is necessary that our whole course of life be subordinated to moral maxims. But it is at the same time impossible that this should happen unless reason connects with the moral law, which is a mere idea, an efficient cause which determines for conduct in accordance with that law an outcome exactly corresponding to our highest ends, whether in this life or in another. Without a God, therefore, and a world not now visible to us but hoped for, the glorious ideas of morality are indeed objects of approval and admiration, but not springs of purpose and action; for they do not fulfil the whole end which is natural to every rational being, and which is determined *a priori* and made necessary by that same pure reason.",["- krv-a-1267: *Willkühr* left in German beside \"free will\"; \"Willkühr\" is a choice (cf. \"private will\" in 1270). The parenthesis on \"can and ought\" was restated as a separate sentence. \"Gegenstand ... intelligibelen Anschauung\" kept literal. \"Die Idee ... ging\" smoothed into two clauses.","- krv-a-1267: In \"aber als einen Gegenstand\" the construction is irregular; I supplied \"that world as\" and \"it refers to\" to carry the sense.","- krv-a-1268: \"die ... betrafen\" read as relative clause on the questions; \"derselben\" taken to refer to the second question.","- krv-a-1269: \"nach der Vernunft\" rendered \"in accordance with reason\"; the long sentence was left intact but \"daß ... sey\" clauses were kept parallel.","- krv-a-1270: Long sentence divided into several. \"restringirte\" rendered \"restrained\"; \"Wolfarth\" as \"welfare\". \"Daß iederman thue\" etc. kept. \"Als Ursache der Natur zum Grunde gelegt\" expanded with \"and so as the basis of it\" to render the repeated *zum Grunde*.","- krv-a-1271: \"vor uns künftige Welt\" rendered \"for us future\"; \"Erscheinungen\" = appearances. \"Seligkeit\" rendered \"blessedness\" to distinguish it from *Glückseligkeit* (happiness).","- krv-a-1272: \"Hirngespinste\" = \"figments of the brain\"; \"Verheissungen und Drohungen\" = promises and threats. \"Daher auch\" sentence divided and \"Dieses ... thun\" resolved as \"this again they cannot do\".","- krv-a-1275: \"Ausgang\" rendered \"outcome\"; \"Triebfedern\" = \"springs\"; \"Vorsatz\" = \"purpose\". \"dem Verhalten ... bestimt\" rendered \"determines for conduct ... an outcome\"."]],"krv-a-1276":["Happiness alone is, for our reason, far from being the complete good. Reason does not approve it (however much inclination may desire it) unless it is united with worthiness to be happy, that is, with moral conduct. But morality alone, and with it mere worthiness to be happy, is likewise still far from being the complete good. To complete the good, he who has conducted himself in a manner not unworthy of happiness must be able to hope that he will participate in it. Even reason, when free from all private aims and without considering any interest of its own, puts itself in the position of a being who has to distribute all happiness to others; it cannot judge otherwise. For in the practical idea the two elements are essentially combined. They are so combined, however, that it is the moral disposition, as condition, which makes possible the share in happiness, and not, conversely, the prospect of happiness which first makes possible the moral disposition. For in the latter case the disposition would not be moral, and therefore also not worthy of complete happiness, which in the eyes of reason recognises no other limitation than that which arises from our own immoral conduct.",[]],"krv-a-1277":["Happiness, therefore, in exact proportion to the morality of rational beings, whereby they are rendered worthy of it, alone constitutes the highest good of a world into which we must, in accordance with the precepts of pure but practical reason, transport ourselves. This world is, indeed, only an intelligible world, since the sensible world does not promise us, from the nature of things, any such systematic unity of ends. Nor can the reality of this unity be grounded on anything save the presupposition of a highest original good. In this, self-subsisting reason, equipped with all the sufficiency of a supreme cause, establishes, maintains and fulfils, in accordance with the most perfect purposiveness, the universal order of things, though in the sensible world that order is very deeply hidden from us.",[]],"krv-a-1278":["This moral theology has, then, the peculiar advantage over speculative theology that it leads inevitably to the concept of a single, most perfect and rational original being. To this concept speculative theology does not so much as point us on objective grounds, still less can it convince us of it. For in neither transcendental nor natural theology, however far reason may lead us in them, do we find any significant ground for assuming only one being, which we should set above all natural causes and on which we should have sufficient reason to make these causes dependent in every respect. If, on the other hand, we consider from the standpoint of moral unity, as a necessary law of the world, the cause which alone can give this law its appropriate effect, and therefore also obligatory force for us, it must be a single supreme will which comprehends all these laws within itself. For how could we find perfect unity of ends among different wills? This will must be omnipotent, in order that the whole of nature and its relation to morality in the world may be subject to it; omniscient, that it may know the inmost part of our dispositions and their moral worth; omnipresent, that it may be immediately at hand for every need which the highest good of the world demands; eternal, that at no time this harmony of nature and freedom may be lacking; and so on.",[]],"krv-a-1279":["But this systematic unity of ends in this world of intelligences is, as mere nature, only a sensible world, though it can be entitled, as a system of freedom, an intelligible, that is, a moral world (*regnum gratiae*). It leads inevitably also to the purposive unity of all the things which constitute this great whole, in accordance with universal laws of nature, just as the former unity leads to it in accordance with universal and necessary moral laws; and it thus unites practical with speculative reason. The world must be represented as having sprung from an idea, if it is to harmonise with that use of reason without which we should hold ourselves unworthy of reason, namely the moral use, which rests entirely on the idea of the highest good. Thereby all investigation of nature acquires a direction towards the form of a system of ends, and in its widest extension becomes physico-theology. But physico-theology, since it started from moral order as a unity grounded in the essence of freedom and not contingently instituted through external commands, brings the purposiveness of nature to grounds which must be inseparably bound up a priori with the inner possibility of things. It thereby leads to a transcendental theology, which takes the ideal of highest ontological perfection as a principle of systematic unity, connecting all things in accordance with universal and necessary laws of nature, since they all have their origin in the absolute necessity of a single original being.",[]],"krv-a-1280":["What use can we make of our understanding, even in respect of experience, if we do not set ourselves ends? The highest ends, however, are those of morality, and these only pure reason can make known to us. Provided with these ends, and with them as guiding thread, we can make no purposive use, in respect of knowledge, of our knowledge of nature itself, save where nature has itself introduced purposive unity. For without this unity we should not even have reason, since we should have no school for it, and no culture through objects which would furnish the material for such concepts. That purposive unity is, however, necessary, and grounded in the very essence of the power of choice (*Willkür*); the unity which contains the condition of its application *in concreto* must therefore be necessary likewise. Thus the transcendental enhancement of our knowledge of reason would be not the cause but merely the effect of the practical purposiveness which pure reason imposes upon us.",[]],"krv-a-1281":["We accordingly find also in the history of human reason that, before the moral concepts were sufficiently purified and determined, and before the systematic unity of ends in accordance with them was understood, and indeed from necessary principles, knowledge of nature and even a considerable degree of cultivation of reason in many other sciences could produce, on the one hand, only crude and vagrant concepts of the Deity, and on the other left behind a remarkable indifference in regard to this question altogether. A more extended treatment of moral ideas, made necessary by the exceedingly pure moral law of our religion, sharpened reason upon the object, through the interest which it compelled reason to take in it. Without the aid either of extended knowledge of nature or of correct and trustworthy transcendental insights (which have at all times been lacking), these ideas brought about a concept of the divine being which we now hold to be correct. We do so not because speculative reason convinces us of its correctness, but because it harmonises completely with the moral principles of reason. And so in the end it is always pure reason alone, though only in its practical use, that has the merit of connecting with our highest interest a mode of knowledge which mere speculation can only fancy, but cannot make good. Thereby pure reason does not, indeed, make it a demonstrated dogma, but does make it an absolutely necessary presupposition for reason's most essential ends.",["- krv-a-1276: Divided the long sentence \"Selbst die… kan nicht anders urtheilen\" into two; \"Stücke\" rendered \"elements\"; \"Wolverhalten\" as \"moral conduct\" (glossing sittlichen).","- krv-a-1276: \"vor\" = \"für\" (for); \"Würdigkeit\" = worthiness. In the last sentence \"die\" refers to Glückseligkeit.","- krv-a-1277: Split the single German sentence into three; \"da\" (twice) rendered \"since\" and \"In this\" (relative sense). \"Zwecke\" = ends.","- krv-a-1278: Split the \"Dieser Wille muß…\" series with semicolons; \"Weltbeste\" = \"good of the world\". \"Urwesen\" = original being.","- krv-a-1279: The opening sentence is irregular (it lacks a clear grammatical predicate for \"diese Einheit… welche… kann\"); I smoothed and recast it. Kant's \"ins Wesen der Freiheit\" kept literal.","- krv-a-1280: *Willkür* kept in German beside \"power of choice\"; \"Steigerung\" = \"enhancement\"; \"Erkentniß\" rendered \"knowledge\", with \"Vernunfterkentniß\" as \"knowledge of reason\". The sentence \"Mit diesen nun versehen…\" is awkward in the original; I rendered it literally in sense. \"Schule… Cultur\" translated as \"school… culture\".","- krv-a-1281: Kant's \"brachten sie\" has plural subject (moral ideas); rendered \"these ideas brought about\". \"Erkentniß\" in last sentence = \"mode of knowledge\" (the singular indefinite). \"specutative\" corrected silently to speculative."]],"krv-a-1282":["But when practical reason has reached this lofty point, namely, the concept of a single primal being as the highest good, it must not presume to proceed as if it had raised itself above all the empirical conditions of the concept's application, and had soared to the immediate knowledge of new objects, so as to start out from this concept and to derive the moral laws themselves from it. For it was precisely these laws, whose inner practical necessity led us to the presupposition of a self-subsistent cause, or of a wise ruler of the world, in order to give effect to them. Consequently we cannot in turn regard them as contingent and as derived from mere will, especially from a will of which we should have no concept at all, had we not formed it in accordance with those laws. So far as practical reason has the right to lead us, we shall not hold actions to be obligatory because they are commands of God, but shall regard them as divine commands because we are inwardly obligated to them. We shall study freedom under the purposive unity according to principles of reason; and we shall believe ourselves to be in conformity with the divine will only in so far as we hold sacred the moral law, which reason teaches us from the nature of actions themselves, and shall believe that we serve that will only by furthering the world's good in ourselves and in others. Moral theology is thus of immanent use only. It enables us to fulfil our vocation [*Bestimmung*] here in the world, by fitting ourselves into the system of all ends, and by guarding against the fanatical, or even impious, abandonment of the guiding thread of a morally legislative reason in the conduct of a good life, for the purpose of connecting that conduct immediately with the idea of the highest being. That would be a transcendent use; but, like that of mere speculation, it must pervert and frustrate the ultimate ends of reason.",["- Restated the long opening sentence's \"Denn... und daher\" chain as two sentences; \"daher\" rendered \"Consequently\".","- \"dazu... seyn\" (original \"verbindlich seyn\", sic) read as \"verbindlich sind\"; \"es\" in \"nur von immanentem Gebrauche\" supplied as \"It enables us\".","- The final sentence's construction is irregular (\"nicht schwärmerisch... zu verlassen\"); I resolved it as \"guarding against the... abandonment\", supplying the sense of \"guarding against\". Doubtful syntax.","- *Bestimmung* given as \"vocation\" (a choice; the fixed term \"determination\" does not suit here).","- \"Weltbeste\" = \"world's good\"; \"Weltregierer\" = \"ruler of the world\"."]],"krv-a-1283":["Third Section of the Canon of Pure Reason. Opining, Knowing and Believing.",["- *Meinen, Wissen, Glauben* rendered with gerunds (\"Opining, Knowing and Believing\"), a choice."]],"krv-a-1284":["Taking to be true [*Vorwahrhalten*] is an occurrence in our understanding which, though it may rest on objective grounds, also requires subjective causes in the mind of the person who judges. If it is valid for everyone, in so far as he has reason, its ground is objectively sufficient, and the taking to be true is then entitled conviction. If it has its ground only in the special character of the subject, it is entitled persuasion.",["- *Vorwahrhalten* given as \"taking to be true\", with German on first use; *Ueberzeugung* = conviction, *Ueberredung* = persuasion.","- \"Gemüth\" = mind; \"heißt\" and \"genant\" = \"is entitled\"."]],"krv-a-1285":["Persuasion is mere illusion, because the ground of the judgment, which lies solely in the subject, is regarded as objective. Such a judgment has therefore only private validity, and the taking to be true cannot be communicated. Truth, however, rests on agreement with the object, and in respect of the object the judgments of every understanding must therefore be in agreement (*consentientia uni tertio, consentiunt inter se*). The touchstone of taking to be true, whether it be conviction or mere persuasion, is therefore, externally, the possibility of communicating it and of finding it valid for the reason of every human being. For in that case there is at least a presumption that the ground of the agreement of all judgments, notwithstanding the diversity of the subjects among themselves, will rest upon the common ground, namely the object, with which they will all accordingly agree, and so prove the truth of the judgment.",["- Split the final long sentence's content only lightly; the last \"denn\" clause kept in one sentence, with \"so prove\" smoothing \"dadurch... beweisen werden\".","- The Latin tag is kept in italics, untranslated by Kant's text: \"things agreeing with a third agree with one another\"."]],"krv-a-1286":["Persuasion, accordingly, cannot be distinguished subjectively from conviction, if the subject has the taking to be true before him merely as an appearance [*Erscheinung*] of his own mind. But the experiment of testing the grounds of it, which are valid for us, on the understanding of others, to see whether they produce on alien reason the same effect as on our own, is, though a merely subjective means, still one by which, while conviction is not indeed brought about, the merely private validity of the judgment can be discovered, that is, something in it which is mere persuasion.",["- Restated the second sentence's \"zwar nicht... aber doch\" so that the \"zwar\" belongs to \"Ueberzeugung zu bewirken\"; the first \"zwar\" (before \"nicht unterschieden\") is left unrendered.","- *Erscheinung* = appearance."]],"krv-a-1287":["If, moreover, we can develop the subjective causes of the judgment, which we take for objective grounds of it, and so explain the deceptive taking to be true as an occurrence in our mind, without needing the character of the object for the purpose, we then expose the illusion and are no longer deceived by it, although we are still always tempted to some degree, if the subjective cause of the illusion is inherent in our nature.",["- \"Schein\" = illusion; \"versucht\" = tempted."]],"krv-a-1288":["I can assert nothing, that is, pronounce it as a judgment necessarily valid for everyone, save what produces conviction. Persuasion I may keep for myself, if I find it agreeable, but I cannot, and ought not to, try to make it hold outside myself.",["- \"als was\" read as \"save what\"; \"wol\" in \"wol befinde\" = \"find it agreeable\"."]],"krv-a-1289":["Taking to be true, or the subjective validity of judgment, in relation to conviction (which at the same time holds objectively), has the following three stages: opining, believing, and knowing. Opining is a taking to be true that is consciously insufficient both subjectively and objectively. If it is only subjectively sufficient, and is at the same time held to be objectively insufficient, it is entitled believing. Finally, the taking to be true which is sufficient both subjectively and objectively is entitled knowing. Subjective sufficiency is entitled conviction (for myself), objective sufficiency certainty (for everyone). I shall not pause to elucidate concepts so easily grasped.",["- \"Das leztere\" taken as \"the taking to be true\" (Vorwahrhalten), not \"Meinen\"; \"das Wissen\" rendered \"knowing\" to match the gerunds.","- Here \"Ueberzeugung\" is used in the narrower sense of subjective sufficiency; \"Gewißheit\" = certainty."]],"krv-a-1290":["I must never presume to opine without at least knowing something by means of which the judgment, problematic in itself, acquires a connection with truth, which, though not complete, is yet more than arbitrary invention. Moreover, the law of such a connection must be certain. For if, in respect of this law too, I have nothing but opinion, all is a mere play of the imagination, without the least relation to truth. In judgments from pure reason it is not permitted to opine at all. For since they are not supported on grounds of experience, but everything is to be known *a priori*, where everything is necessary, the principle of connection requires universality and necessity, and therefore complete certainty; otherwise no guidance to truth is to be met with. Hence it is absurd to opine in pure mathematics; we must know, or abstain from all judging. The same holds of the principles of morality, where we may not venture upon an action on the mere opinion that it is permitted, but must know that it is.",["- \"Meinen\" rendered \"opine\"; \"Spiel der Einbildung\" = \"play of the imagination\".","- Divided the long \"Denn, weil...\" sentence only lightly, preserving its structure."]],"krv-a-1291":["In the transcendental employment of reason, on the other hand, opining is indeed too little, but knowing is too much. In a merely speculative regard we cannot judge here at all; for subjective grounds of taking to be true, such as can produce believing, do not deserve assent in speculative questions, since they cannot be kept free of all empirical assistance, nor be communicated to others in equal measure.",["- \"Beifall\" = assent; \"Glauben\" here \"believing\"."]],"krv-a-1292":["But it is only in a practical relation that the taking to be true which is theoretically insufficient can be entitled believing. This practical purpose is either that of skill or that of morality, the first being for arbitrary and contingent ends, the second for ends that are absolutely necessary.",["- \"überall blos\" rendered \"only\"; \"Geschicklichkeit\" = skill, \"Sittlichkeit\" = morality."]],"krv-a-1293":["Once an end has been set before us, the conditions of its attainment are hypothetically necessary. This necessity is subjective, but is sufficient only comparatively, if I know of no other conditions under which the end could be attained. It is sufficient absolutely, and for everyone, if I know with certainty that no one can be acquainted with any other conditions leading to the proposed end. In the first case my presupposition, and my holding of certain conditions to be true, is a merely contingent belief; in the second case it is a necessary belief. A physician must do something for a patient who is in danger, but he does not know the nature of the illness. He observes the symptoms [Erscheinungen], and since he knows nothing better, judges it to be consumption. His belief is contingent even in his own judgment; another might perhaps hit upon the truth more happily. Such contingent belief, which yet underlies the actual employment of means to certain actions, I entitle pragmatic belief.",["- \"Glaube\" is rendered \"belief\" throughout; \"Vorwahrhalten\" as \"holding to be true\" (the Kemp Smith usage).","- \"Erscheinungen\" here means the symptoms of the patient, not \"appearances\" in the technical sense; the German is added in brackets because of the technical term.","- \"Ich nenne … den pragmatischen Glauben\" rendered \"I entitle\", following the method for *nennen*; the last sentence is slightly reordered for English.","- \"Hypothetischnothwendig\" (OCR/old spelling joined) is rendered \"hypothetically necessary\"."]],"krv-a-1294":["The usual touchstone for deciding whether what someone asserts is mere persuasion, or at least subjective conviction, that is, firm belief, is betting. Often a man pronounces his propositions with so confident and inflexible a defiance that he seems to have set aside all apprehension of error. A wager gives him pause. Sometimes it turns out that he has persuasion enough to be estimated at the value of one ducat, but not of ten. For he will venture the first, but when it is ten he becomes aware, for the first time, of what he had not previously noticed, namely that it is quite possible he has erred. If we imagine in thought that we have to stake the happiness of our whole life on something, our triumphant judgment very greatly dwindles; we become extremely timid, and so discover for the first time that our belief does not reach so far. Thus pragmatic belief has only a degree, which, according to the difference of the interest at stake, may be great or small.",["- \"Ueberredung\" = persuasion; \"Ueberzeugung\" = conviction.","- \"Wir ... so allererst\" smoothed as \"for the first time\"; the long sentence beginning \"Wenn man sich in Gedanken vorstellt\" is kept as one sentence but lightly recast.","- \"worauf\" (\"on something\") is indefinite in the original; I have not specified further."]],"krv-a-1295":["Now we may in many cases conceive in thought an undertaking, and imagine ourselves to have sufficient grounds for it, if only there were a means of establishing the certainty of the matter, even though in relation to an object we can undertake nothing at all, so that the holding to be true is merely theoretical. Hence in merely theoretical judgments there is an analogue of practical judgments, to the holding to be true of which the word belief is appropriate, and which we may entitle doctrinal belief. If it were possible to decide the matter by some experience, I should be ready to stake everything I possess on the proposition that at least some one of the planets we see is inhabited. Hence I say that it is not merely opinion, but a strong belief (on the correctness of which I would already risk many of the advantages of life), that there are inhabitants of other worlds also.",["- The opening \"Weil aber ... so giebt es\" is a long concessive-causal period; I restated it with the main clause (\"Hence ... there is an analogue\") following, and moved the \"although/in relation to an object\" clause to the end of the first sentence.","- \"und den wir ... nennen können\": \"den\" refers grammatically to \"Glauben\"; rendered \"which\".","- \"Analogon\" rendered \"analogue\"; Kant's Latin term could be kept in italics (*analogon*), but here the English suffices.","- \"Daher sage ich\" rendered \"Hence I say\"; the antecedent is the planets example."]],"krv-a-1296":["We must now admit that the doctrine of the existence of God belongs to doctrinal belief. For although, as regards theoretical knowledge of the world, I have nothing at my disposal which necessarily presupposes this thought as the condition of my explanations of the appearances of the world, and am rather bound so to use my reason as if everything were mere nature, yet purposive unity is so important a condition of the application of reason to nature that I cannot ignore it, especially as experience offers me abundant examples of it. But I know of no other condition under which this unity could serve me as a guide in the investigation of nature than the presupposition that a supreme intelligence has ordered everything according to the wisest ends. Consequently, to presuppose a wise author of the world is a condition of an aim which, though contingent, is yet not unimportant, namely, that of having guidance in the investigation of nature. The outcome of my experiments, moreover, so often confirms the serviceability of this presupposition, and nothing decisive can be adduced against it, that I should be saying much too little if I were to call my holding to be true a mere opining. Even in this theoretical relation it can be said that I firmly believe in a God. But even so this belief is not, in the strict sense, practical; it must be entitled a doctrinal belief, which the theology of nature (physico-theology) must necessarily everywhere produce. In regard to this same wisdom, and in view of the excellent endowment of human nature and the brevity of life so ill suited to it, sufficient ground can equally be found for a doctrinal belief in the future life of the human soul.",["- Kant's long sentence \"Der Ausgang meiner Versuche ... daß ich viel zu wenig sage\" has an irregular construction (a semicolon where \"so that\" is needed); I supplied \"that ... so\" to make the consequence clear and divided the sentence after \"mere opining\".","- \"Versuche\" rendered \"experiments\"; it could also be \"attempts\".","- \"Welturheber\" = \"author of the world\"; \"Weltursache\" appears in the next passage.","- \"Theologie der Natur\" rendered \"theology of nature\"."]],"krv-a-1297":["In such cases the expression \"belief\" is an expression of modesty from the objective point of view, but at the same time of firmness of confidence from the subjective. If I were to call the merely theoretical holding to be true here a hypothesis, which I should be entitled to assume, I should thereby already pledge myself to have a concept of the nature of a cause of the world and of another world which is fuller than I can actually exhibit. For whatever I assume, even if only as hypothesis, I must know sufficiently in respect of its properties that I need invent not its concept but only its existence. The word belief, however, concerns only the guidance which an idea gives me, and its subjective influence on the furtherance of the actions of my reason, which holds me fast to it, although I am not in a position to give an account of it from the speculative point of view.",["- \"Hypothese ... berechtigt wäre\" rendered \"which I should be entitled to assume\"; the connection with \"heißen\" is not here involved.","- \"Weltursache\" = \"cause of the world\"; \"seinen Eigenschaften\" refers to the thing assumed.","- \"Vernunfthandlungen\" = \"actions of my reason\" (literally \"reason-actions\").","- \"von ihr ... Rechenschaft zu geben\": \"ihr\" = the idea."]],"krv-a-1298":["But merely doctrinal belief has something wavering about it; we are often shaken in it by difficulties which arise in speculation, although we invariably return to it again.",["- \"aus demselben gesezt\" = \"put out of it\", i.e. thrown off from it; rendered \"shaken in it\"."]],"krv-a-1299":["The case is quite otherwise with moral belief. For here it is absolutely necessary that something must happen, namely, that I comply in all respects with the moral law. The end is here inescapably fixed, and according to all my insight there is only one possible condition under which this end connects with all other ends taken together and thereby has practical validity, namely, that there be a God and a future world. I also know quite certainly that no one is acquainted with other conditions which lead to this same unity of ends under the moral law. But since the moral precept is at the same time my maxim (as reason commands that it ought to be), I shall inevitably believe in the existence of God and in a future life, and I am sure that nothing can make this belief waver, since my moral principles themselves would thereby be overthrown, and I cannot renounce them without becoming detestable in my own eyes.",["- \"sittlich\" and \"moralisch\" both rendered \"moral\"; \"Vorschrift\" as \"precept\".","- \"Der Zweck ist hier unumgänglich festgestellt\" = \"inescapably fixed\".","- \"führe\" (subjunctive) rendered indicatively, \"lead\".","- The sentence \"Da aber ...\" is kept as one, with parenthesis retained."]],"krv-a-1300":["In this way, when all the ambitious designs of a reason that wanders beyond the limits of all experience have been frustrated, enough still remains to us to give us reason for being satisfied, from the practical point of view. No one, indeed, will be able to boast that he *knows* that there is a God and a future life; for if he knows this, he is precisely the man for whom I have long been searching. All knowledge, if it concerns an object of mere reason, can be communicated, and I should therefore be able to hope that under his instruction my own knowledge would be extended in so wonderful a degree. No, the conviction is not logical but moral certainty; and since it rests on subjective grounds (those of the moral disposition), I must not even say, \"It is morally certain that there is a God,\" etc., but, \"I am morally certain,\" etc. That is to say, the belief in a God and in another world is so interwoven with my moral disposition that I am in as little danger of losing the former as I am anxious lest the latter [*der zweite*] should ever be torn from me.",["- Restated the opening clause (\"Auf solche Weise … übrig: daß …\") by smoothing the construction into one flowing sentence with \"give us reason for being satisfied\".","- \"Wissen\" rendered \"knows\"/\"knowledge\"; \"Ueberzeugung\" = \"conviction\"; \"Gewißheit\" = \"certainty\"; \"Gesinnung\" = \"disposition\".","- \"der erstere … der zweite\": strictly \"the former … the latter\" refers to God and the other world; Kant's gender (\"die erstere\", \"der zweite\") is irregular, so I followed the sense. Flagged as a textual irregularity in the German.","- \"Ueberzeugung\" at the start of the \"No,\" sentence: the German reads \"die Ueberzeugung\" without further specification; I kept it as \"the conviction\"."]],"krv-a-1301":["The only point open to objection here is that this rational belief presupposes moral dispositions. If we leave these out of account and suppose a man wholly indifferent to moral laws, the question which reason propounds becomes merely a problem for speculation. It can then indeed still be supported by strong grounds from analogy, but not by grounds to which the most obstinate scepticism would have to surrender.* But in these questions no man is free from all interest. For even if he be cut off from moral interest through lack of good dispositions, enough still remains, even in this case, to make him fear a divine existence and a future life. For this nothing more is required than that he at least cannot pretend to any certainty that there is no such being and no future life. Since this would have to be proved through mere reason, and therefore apodeictically, he would have to establish the impossibility of both, and this no reasonable man can undertake. That would be a negative belief, which, though it could not give rise to morality and good dispositions, could yet give rise to their analogue, namely, a powerful restraint upon the outbreak of evil dispositions.",["- Divided the long sentence beginning \"Denn hiezu wird nicht mehr erfodert\" into two; the relative \"wozu\" is resolved as \"this would have to be proved\".","- \"apodictisch\" rendered \"apodeictically\" (Kemp Smith's spelling); \"Analogon\" kept as \"analogue\".","- \"der Bösen\" (genitive plural) rendered \"of evil dispositions\" by supplying the sense; this is a supplied gloss, not in the German, which says literally \"the outbreak of the evil\".","- \"Aufgabe vor die Speculation\" = \"a problem for speculation\"."]],"krv-a-1302":["* The human mind (as I believe happens necessarily in every rational being) takes a natural interest in morality, although this interest is not undivided and practically preponderant. Strengthen and enlarge this interest, and you will find reason very docile, and even more enlightened, in uniting the speculative interest with the practical. But if you do not take care that you first make men good, at least in some half-way fashion, you will never make sincere believers of them.",["- \"Gemüth\" = \"mind\". \"aufrichtiggläubige Menschen\" = \"sincere believers\".","- Smoothed \"Sorget ihr aber nicht davor: daß …\" into a conditional sentence; the final exclamation mark is dropped as a matter of English idiom."]],"krv-a-1303":["\"But is this all,\" it will be said, \"that pure reason achieves in opening up prospects beyond the limits of experience? Nothing more than two articles of belief? Even the common understanding could surely have achieved as much without consulting the philosophers!\"",["- Supplied quotation marks to mark the objector's voice; \"wird man sagen\" is thus placed inside the quotation. Kant's exclamation retained.","- \"gemeiner Verstand\" = \"common understanding\"."]],"krv-a-1304":["I do not here propose to extol the service which philosophy has rendered to human reason through the laborious efforts of its criticism, even supposing that in the outcome it should be found to be merely negative; of this something will be said in the following section. But do you then demand that a mode of knowledge [*Erkenntniß*] which concerns all men should transcend the common understanding and be revealed to you only by philosophers? The very point you censure is the best confirmation of the correctness of the preceding assertions. For it discloses what could not at first have been foreseen, namely, that in matters which concern all men without distinction nature is not to be charged with any partial distribution of her gifts, and that the highest philosophy, in dealing with the essential ends of human nature, cannot advance further than the guidance which nature has vouchsafed even to the commonest understanding.",["- \"Critik\" rendered \"criticism\" here. \"Erkentniß\" in the singular rendered \"mode of knowledge\" because the sense is a particular kind of knowledge.","- Divided the final long sentence after \"assertions\" and resolved \"da es … entdekt\" as a causal \"For it discloses\".","- \"der Natur … partheyischen Austheilung\" given as \"partial distribution\"."]],"krv-a-1305":["Third Chapter of the Transcendental Doctrine of Method. The Architectonic of Pure Reason.",["- \"Methodenlehre\" = \"Doctrine of Method\" following Kemp Smith's usage."]],"krv-a-1306":["By an architectonic I understand the art of constructing systems. Systematic unity is what first raises ordinary knowledge to the rank of science, that is, makes a system out of a mere aggregate of knowledge. Architectonic is therefore the doctrine of the scientific [element] in our knowledge in general, and it belongs necessarily to the doctrine of method.",["- Divided the German sentence in two. \"des scientifischen\" is rendered \"the scientific [element]\", the bracketed noun being supplied.","- \"gemeine Erkentniß\" = \"ordinary knowledge\"."]],"krv-a-1307":["Under the government of reason our modes of knowledge cannot form a rhapsody, but must form a system, in which alone they can support and further the essential ends of reason. By a system I understand the unity of the manifold modes of knowledge under one idea. This idea is the rational concept of the form of a whole, in so far as the scope of the manifold and the position of the parts relatively to one another are determined *a priori* by this concept. The scientific rational concept therefore contains the end and the form of the whole which is congruent with it. The unity of the end, to which all the parts are related, and in the idea of which they are also related to one another, makes it possible to note the absence of any part from our knowledge of the rest. There can be no contingent addition, and no undetermined magnitude of perfection which has not its limits determined *a priori*. The whole is thus an organised unity (*articulatio*), not an aggregate (*coacervatio*). It may grow from within (*per intus susceptionem*), but not by external addition (*per appositionem*). It is like an animal body, the growth of which adds no member, but without altering their proportion makes each member stronger and more effective for its purposes.",["- Restated the sentence beginning \"Die Einheit des Zwecks\" as two sentences. \"gegliedert\" rendered \"organised unity\" to bring out the contrast with \"gehäuft\" (aggregate); literally \"articulated\".","- \"Erkentnisse\" plural = \"modes of knowledge\". \"Rhapsodie\" retained. \"so fern\" resolved as \"in so far as\".","- \"worauf sich alle Theile … beziehen\" smoothed; \"bey der Kentniß der übrigen vermißt werden kan\" rendered freely as \"to note the absence of any part from our knowledge of the rest\"."]],"krv-a-1308":["The idea, if it is to be carried out, requires a schema, that is, an essential manifoldness and order of the parts determined *a priori* from the principle of the end. A schema which is not outlined in accordance with an idea, that is, from the chief end of reason, but empirically, in accordance with aims that happen to present themselves (the number of which cannot be known beforehand), yields technical unity. The schema, on the other hand, which arises only in consequence of an idea (where reason prescribes the ends *a priori* and does not wait to find them empirically), establishes architectonic unity. What we entitle science cannot arise technically, owing to the resemblance of the manifold or to the contingent use of knowledge *in concreto* for all sorts of arbitrary external ends. It arises architectonically, by reason of the affinity of its parts and their derivation from a single supreme and inner end, which first makes the whole possible. Its schema must contain the outline (*monogramma*) and the division of the whole into members in accordance with the idea, that is, *a priori*, and must distinguish this whole with certainty and according to principles from all others.",["- Divided the final sentence into three. \"dasienige, was wir Wissenschaft nennen\" rendered \"what we entitle science\" per the method's \"is entitled\".","- \"dessen Schema\" resolved as referring to science; \"dieses\" as \"this whole\".","- \"Verwandschaft\" = \"affinity\"; \"Ausführung\" rendered \"carried out\"."]],"krv-a-1309":["No one attempts to bring a science into being without having an idea as its ground. But in the working out of the science the schema, indeed even the definition which at the very outset he gives of his science, very seldom corresponds to his idea. For the idea lies in reason like a germ, in which all the parts lie hidden, still very much enfolded and barely recognisable under microscopic observation. For this reason sciences, since they are all devised from the standpoint of some universal interest, must be explained and determined not in accordance with the description which their originator gives of them, but in accordance with the idea which, from the natural unity of the parts that he has brought together, is found to have its ground in reason itself. For it will then be found that the originator, and often his latest successors also, are groping about an idea which they have not succeeded in making clear to themselves, and that they are therefore unable to determine the proper content, the articulation (systematic unity) and the limits of the science.",["- \"Schema\" is left as \"schema\" (Kant's technical term); \"Idee\" = idea. \"kennbar\" rendered \"recognisable\".","- The last sentence is slightly recast: \"die sie sich selbst nicht haben deutlich machen\" becomes \"which they have not succeeded in making clear to themselves\"; \"und daher\" begins a second clause with \"that\" supplied.","- The long second sentence of the German is split into two at \"denn diese liegt\"; \"For\" preserved to keep the connection."]],"krv-a-1310":["It is unfortunate that only after we have long collected, rhapsodically, in accordance with the direction of an idea lying hidden within us, many modes of knowledge relating to it, as building material, and indeed have for long periods put them together technically, is it at last possible for us to see the idea in a clearer light and to outline a whole architectonically in accordance with the ends of reason. Systems seem to have been formed, like worms, by a *generatio equivoca*, from the mere confluence of aggregated concepts, at first mutilated, in time complete; although they all had their schema, as the original germ, in reason, which was merely developing itself. Consequently not only is each of them articulated in accordance with an idea, but they are all, moreover, purposively united with one another, as members of a whole, in a system of human knowledge, and so allow of an architectonic of all human knowledge. In our present time, since so much material has already been collected, or can be taken from the ruins of fallen older edifices, such an architectonic would be not only possible but not even very difficult. We content ourselves here with the completion of our task, namely, merely to outline the architectonic of all knowledge from pure reason; and we begin only from the point where the common root of our faculty of knowledge divides and throws out two stems, one of which is reason. By reason I understand here the whole higher faculty of knowledge, and I therefore oppose the rational to the empirical.",["- Very long second sentence restated: split at \"ob sie gleich\" and again at \"darum\"; the anacoluthic \"und darum... seyn und... erlauben\" is smoothed into \"Consequently ... and so allow of\".","- \"Erkentnisse\" = \"modes of knowledge\"; \"Erkentnißkraft\" and \"Erkentnißvermögen\" both rendered \"faculty of knowledge\".","- \"die ieziger Zeit ... nicht allein möglich ... würde\" is attached to the architectonic (\"such an architectonic\"), a judgment of the antecedent; the German construction is irregular.","- \"Gewürme\" rendered \"worms\" (vermin/creeping things is possible).","- \"Vollendung unseres Geschäftes\" is read as \"completion of our task\" (the sense is a limitation to what the Critique must finish); the passage is somewhat obscure."]],"krv-a-1311":["If I abstract from all content of knowledge, considered objectively, then all knowledge is, subjectively, either historical or rational. Historical knowledge is *cognitio ex datis*, rational knowledge *cognitio ex principiis*. However a piece of knowledge may originally have been given, it is still historical in the person who possesses it, if he knows only in the degree and to the extent that it has been given to him from elsewhere, whether through immediate experience, through narration, or even through instruction (in universal modes of knowledge). Hence one who has strictly learnt a system of philosophy, for example the Wolffian, even if he had in his head all the principles, definitions and proofs, together with the division of the whole doctrinal edifice, and could count it all off on his fingers, has none the less only a complete historical knowledge of the Wolffian philosophy. He knows and judges only so much as was given to him. If a definition is disputed, he does not know where to find another. He has formed himself upon a reason not his own; but the imitative faculty is not the productive. That is, the knowledge did not spring in him from reason; and although, objectively, it was certainly a rational knowledge, it is subjectively merely historical. He has grasped and retained well, that is, has learnt, and is a plaster cast of a living man. Modes of rational knowledge which are rational objectively (that is, which at the start can arise only from man's own reason) may be entitled so subjectively also only if they have been drawn from the universal sources of reason, from which criticism, and indeed even the rejection of what has been learnt, can spring; that is, only if they have been drawn from principles.",["- \"Streitet ihm eine Definition\" understood as \"if a definition is disputed [against him]\"; the construction is elliptical.","- \"Vernunfterkentnisse\" rendered \"modes of rational knowledge\" (plural); \"Vernunfterkentniß\" as \"rational knowledge\".","- \"das nachbildende Vermögen ist nicht das erzeugende\" rendered \"imitative faculty ... productive\"; \"Gipsabdruck\" as \"plaster cast\".","- The final sentence is restated with \"only if ... only if\" for the awkward \"dürfen nur denn allein ... wenn\"; \"Critik\" = \"criticism\".","- The parenthesis \"(d. i. zu anfangs ...)\" is retained; \"zu anfangs\" is read as \"at the start\"."]],"krv-a-1312":["All rational knowledge is now either that from concepts or that from the construction of concepts; the former is entitled philosophical, the second mathematical. Of the inner difference between them I have already treated in the first chapter. A piece of knowledge, then, can be objectively philosophical and yet subjectively historical, as in the case of most pupils, and of all who never look beyond the school and remain pupils all their lives. It is, however, remarkable that mathematical knowledge, as one has learnt it, can still count subjectively as rational knowledge, and that no such distinction is found in it as in philosophical knowledge. The reason is that the sources of knowledge from which alone the teacher can draw lie nowhere else than in the essential and genuine principles of reason, and consequently cannot be obtained by the pupil from anywhere else, nor be in any way disputed. This is so because the employment of reason here takes place only *in concreto*, though none the less *a priori*, namely in pure and, for that very reason, infallible intuition, and excludes all illusion and error. Among all the sciences of reason (*a priori*), therefore, only mathematics can be learnt, never philosophy (save historically); as regards reason, we can at the most only learn to philosophise.",["- \"Ein Erkentniß\" rendered \"A piece of knowledge\" since the English cannot take the article or plural directly.","- \"im ersten Hauptstücke\" rendered \"first chapter\" (the Doctrine of Method, ch. 1, the Discipline of Pure Reason).","- \"fehlerfreien\" rendered \"infallible\" (literally \"free from error\").","- The sentence beginning \"Die Ursache ist\" is split at \"und dieses zwar darum\"; \"nirgend anders hergenommen\" read as \"obtained from anywhere else\".","- The last sentence is rendered \"can be learnt\" as the sense requires; the German is elliptical (\"man kan ... nur allein Mathematik ... lernen\")."]],"krv-a-1313":["The system of all philosophical knowledge is philosophy. It must be taken objectively if by it we understand the archetype for judging all attempts at philosophising, which is to serve in the judging of every subjective philosophy, whose structure is often so manifold and so changeable. Philosophy is in this sense a mere idea of a possible science, which is nowhere given *in concreto*, but which we seek to approach by many different paths, until the one and only footpath, much overgrown by sensibility, is discovered, and the copy, hitherto unsuccessful, is made, so far as is granted to man, equal to the archetype. Until then no philosophy can be learnt; for where is it, who is in possession of it, and by what can it be recognised? We can only learn to philosophise, that is, to exercise the talent of reason in following its universal principles in certain existing attempts, yet always with reservation of reason's right to investigate those principles in their very sources, and to confirm or reject them.",["- \"das Urbild\" = \"archetype\", \"Nachbild\" = \"copy\" (Kant's contrast Urbild/Nachbild).","- \"Sinnlichkeit\" = \"sensibility\"; \"verwachsene\" rendered \"overgrown\".","- \"iene\" in the last clause is taken to refer to \"Principien\" (hence \"those principles\"); it could conceivably refer to the \"Versuche\" (attempts).","- The first sentence has been slightly restructured; \"welche iede subiective Philosophie zu beurtheilen dienen soll\" attached to \"archetype\"."]],"krv-a-1314":["Until then, however, the concept of philosophy is only a scholastic concept [*Schulbegriff*], namely of a system of knowledge which is sought only as a science, without having as its aim anything more than the systematic unity of this knowledge, and therefore the logical perfection of knowledge. But there is also a cosmical concept [*Weltbegriff*] (*conceptus cosmicus*), which has always lain at the basis of this name, especially when it was, as it were, personified and represented as an archetype in the ideal of the philosopher. In this sense philosophy is the science of the relation of all knowledge to the essential ends of human reason (*teleologia rationis humanae*), and the philosopher is not an artist in the field of reason [*Vernunftkünstler*] but the lawgiver of human reason. In such a meaning it would be very boastful to call oneself a philosopher, and to presume to have equalled the archetype which lies only in the idea.",["- \"Weltbegriff\" is rendered \"cosmical concept\", following Kant's own Latin gloss *conceptus cosmicus*; \"Schulbegriff\" as \"scholastic concept\".","- \"Vernunftkünstler\" rendered \"artist in the field of reason\" (a mere technician of reason); German given in brackets.","- \"dieser Benennung\" is taken to refer to the name \"philosophy\".","- \"gleichgekommen zu seyn\" rendered \"to have equalled\"."]],"krv-a-1315":["The mathematician, the student of nature, and the logician, however brilliant the progress which the first may make in knowledge of reason in general, the second in philosophical knowledge in particular, are yet only artists of reason. There is still a teacher in the ideal, who employs them all, using them as instruments for the furtherance of the essential ends of human reason. Him alone we should have to entitle the philosopher. But since he is himself nowhere to be met with, while the idea of his legislation is to be found everywhere in every human reason, we shall keep solely to the latter, and shall determine more precisely what philosophy, in accordance with this cosmical concept*), prescribes by way of systematic unity from the standpoint of ends.",[]],"krv-a-1316":["*) Cosmical concept [*Weltbegriff*] is here entitled that which concerns what necessarily interests everyone. Accordingly, if a science is regarded merely as one of the skills for certain optional ends, I determine its aim in accordance with scholastic concepts [*Schulbegriffe*].",[]],"krv-a-1317":["Essential ends are not on that account the highest ends; of these, given complete systematic unity of reason, there can be only one. Essential ends are therefore either the final end or subaltern ends, which belong to the former necessarily as means. The final end is none other than the whole vocation [*Bestimmung*] of man, and the philosophy which deals with it is entitled morals. Because of this pre-eminence which moral philosophy has over every other occupation of reason, the ancients also, when they spoke of the philosopher, always meant, at the same time and above all, the moralist. And even the outward appearance of self-mastery through reason leads us, by a certain analogy, to call a man a philosopher still, despite his limited knowledge.",[]],"krv-a-1318":["The legislation of human reason (philosophy) has two objects, nature and freedom, and thus contains the law of nature as well as the moral law, at first in two distinct systems, but in the end in a single philosophical system. The philosophy of nature deals with all that is; the philosophy of morals, only with what ought to be.",[]],"krv-a-1319":["All philosophy, however, is either knowledge from pure reason or knowledge of reason from empirical principles. The former is entitled pure, the latter empirical philosophy.",[]],"krv-a-1320":["The philosophy of pure reason is either propaedeutic (preliminary exercise), which investigates the faculty of reason in respect of all pure knowledge *a priori*, and is entitled critique; or, secondly, it is the system of pure reason (science), that is, the whole of philosophical knowledge, true as well as illusory, derived from pure reason and presented in systematic connection, and is entitled metaphysics. This name may, however, also be given to the whole of pure philosophy, inclusive of critique, so as to comprehend together both the investigation of everything that can ever be known *a priori* and the exposition of that which constitutes a system of pure philosophical modes of knowledge of this kind, distinguished as this is from all empirical, and likewise from the mathematical, employment of reason.",[]],"krv-a-1321":["Metaphysics is divided into that of the speculative and that of the practical employment of pure reason, and is therefore either metaphysics of nature or metaphysics of morals. The former contains all the pure principles of reason, derived from mere concepts (thus excluding mathematics), for the theoretical knowledge of all things; the latter contains the principles which determine action and omission *a priori* and make them necessary. Now morality is the only conformity to law in actions which can be derived completely *a priori* from principles. Hence the metaphysics of morals is properly pure morals, in which no anthropology (no empirical condition) is made the ground. The metaphysics of speculative reason is what is commonly called metaphysics in the narrower sense. Since, however, the pure doctrine of morals belongs nevertheless to the special stem of human, and indeed philosophical, knowledge from pure reason, we shall let it retain that title, although we here set it aside as not belonging to our present purpose.",[]],"krv-a-1322":["It is of the utmost importance to isolate modes of knowledge which differ from others in kind and origin, and to take careful precautions lest they run together in a mixture with others with which they are commonly combined in use. What chemists do in the separation of materials, and what mathematicians do in their pure doctrine of magnitude, is still more incumbent upon the philosopher. He must be able to determine with certainty the share which a special kind of knowledge has in the wandering employment of the understanding, and its own proper value and influence. Hence human reason, ever since it has thought, or rather reflected, has never been able to dispense with a metaphysics, yet has never been able to present it sufficiently purified of all foreign elements. The idea of such a science is as old as speculative human reason; and what reason does not speculate, whether in the scholastic or in the popular manner?\n\nWe must, however, admit that the distinction between the two elements of our knowledge, of which the one is completely within our power *a priori*, while the other can be taken only *a posteriori* from experience, remained very indistinct even among professional thinkers. Consequently it could never bring about the determination of the limits of a special kind of knowledge, and therefore never the genuine idea of a science which has so long and so greatly occupied human reason. When it was said that metaphysics is the science of the first principles of human knowledge, what was thereby indicated was not a quite special kind of knowledge, but only a rank in respect of universality. Metaphysics could not thus be distinguished with certainty from the empirical; for among empirical principles also some are more universal, and therefore higher, than others. In a series of such subordination, in which we do not distinguish what is known completely *a priori* from what is known only *a posteriori*, where is the division to be made which would separate the first part and the highest members from the last and subordinate ones?\n\nWhat would we say if chronology could designate the epochs of the world only by dividing them into the first centuries and those following? We should ask: does the fifth, the tenth century, and so on, also belong to the first? In the same way I ask: does the concept of the extended belong to metaphysics? You answer, Yes. Well, and that of body also? Yes. And that of fluid body? You are taken aback; for if this goes on, everything will belong to metaphysics. From this we see that the mere degree of subordination (the particular under the universal) cannot determine the limits of a science; in our case what determines them is complete heterogeneity and difference of origin.\n\nBut what obscured the fundamental idea of metaphysics from yet another side was this: as knowledge *a priori* it shows a certain homogeneity with mathematics. In respect of origin *a priori* this makes the two akin. But in respect of the mode of knowledge, namely from concepts in the former as compared with judging *a priori* merely through the construction of concepts in the latter, and so in respect of the difference between philosophical and mathematical knowledge, there appears so decided a heterogeneity that men always, as it were, felt it, but could never reduce it to distinct criteria. The consequence was that, since philosophers themselves erred in developing the idea of their science, the working out of it could have no determinate end and no sure guide. Proceeding upon a plan so arbitrarily made, ignorant of the way they should take, and always disputing among themselves over the discoveries which each claimed to have made on his own path, they brought their science into contempt, first with others and finally even with themselves.",["- krv-a-1315: Kant's sentence is irregular (the plural subject with singular \"die erstere … die zweite\"); I smoothed it, keeping \"first\" and \"second\" for mathematician and student of nature. *Vernunftkünstler* = \"artists of reason\"; *Weltbegriff* = \"cosmical concept\" (Kemp Smith's usage).","- krv-a-1316: Footnote kept as a footnote. \"Cosmical concept\" and the German in square brackets added for the term.","- krv-a-1317: *Bestimmung* rendered \"vocation\" here (the sense is the whole destiny of man), with the German bracketed. The final sentence's construction (\"selbst macht der äussere Schein … daß\") was recast.","- krv-a-1319: *Vernunfterkentniß* rendered \"knowledge of reason\"; the sense is rational knowledge from empirical principles.","- krv-a-1320: *Propädevtik* given as \"propaedeutic\". The long final clause was left as one sentence but its sense is tangled (\"von allem empirischen … unterschieden ist\" attaches to the system); I resolved it by judgment.","- krv-a-1321: *Verstand* in \"im engeren Verstande\" rendered \"in the narrower sense\", not \"understanding\". \"Jene Benennung\" taken to refer to \"metaphysics\", as the argument requires.","- krv-a-1322: Divided into paragraphs and shorter sentences. \"Zu Stande bringen\" etc. restated. The clause \"dadurch sie also vom Empirischen …\" is smoothed. \"Von Gewerbe\" rendered \"professional\"."]],"krv-a-1323":["All pure knowledge *a priori* thus constitutes, in virtue of the special faculty of knowledge in which alone it can have its seat, a peculiar unity; and metaphysics is that philosophy which is to present this knowledge in such systematic unity. The speculative part of metaphysics, which has especially appropriated that name, namely the part which we entitle metaphysics of nature, and which considers everything, so far as it *is* (not what ought to be), from concepts *a priori*, is now divided in the following manner.",["- Restated \"nemlich die, welche\" as \"namely the part which\"; the German relative is loosely constructed (die for den), and I took Theil as antecedent.","- \"Erkentniß\" = knowledge; \"Erkentnißvermögen\" = faculty of knowledge. \"Sitz haben\" kept as \"have its seat\".","- \"iene Erkentniß\" taken as the pure knowledge *a priori* just mentioned."]],"krv-a-1324":["Metaphysics in the narrower sense of the term consists of transcendental philosophy and the physiology of pure reason. The former considers only the understanding and reason themselves, in a system of all concepts and principles which relate to objects in general, without assuming objects that would be given (*Ontologia*). The latter considers nature, that is, the sum of given objects (whether they be given to the senses or, if we choose, to some other kind of intuition), and is therefore physiology (though only *rationalis*). Now the employment of reason in this rational study of nature is either physical or hyperphysical, or, better, either immanent or transcendent. The former relates to nature so far as our knowledge of it can be applied in experience (*in concreto*); the latter to that connection of the objects of experience which transcends all experience. This transcendent physiology accordingly has for its object either an inner or an outer connection, both of which, however, pass beyond possible experience. The former is the physiology of nature as a whole, that is, transcendental knowledge of the world; the latter is the physiology of the connection of nature as a whole with a being above nature, that is, transcendental knowledge of God.",["- Divided nothing, but replaced some of Kant's commas and semicolons with full stops and colons for clarity at \"Der erstere … der zweite\".","- \"Obiecte\" and \"Gegenstände\" both rendered \"objects\"; \"im engeren Verstande\" = \"in the narrower sense\" (Verstand here means sense, not understanding).","- \"iene … diese\" resolved as inner (world) and outer (God) connection respectively, as the sequel requires.","- \"Gotteserkentniß\" = knowledge of God; \"Welterkentniß\" = knowledge of the world."]],"krv-a-1325":["Immanent physiology, on the other hand, considers nature as the sum of all objects of the senses, and therefore as it is given to us, but only in accordance with *a priori* conditions under which it can be given to us at all. There are, however, only two kinds of objects of this physiology. 1. Those of the outer senses, and therefore the sum of them, corporeal nature. 2. The object of inner sense, the soul, and, in accordance with its fundamental concepts in general, thinking nature. The metaphysics of corporeal nature is entitled physics, but, since it is to contain only the principles of our knowledge of it *a priori*, rational physics. The metaphysics of thinking nature is entitled psychology, and for the reason just given only the rational knowledge of it is here to be understood.",["- \"derselben\" (physiology) in \"Gegenstände derselben\" taken as referring to immanent physiology.","- \"heißt\" = \"is entitled\"; \"ihrer Erkentniß\" = \"our knowledge of it\" (supplied \"our\" for sense).","- \"Seele\" = soul; \"Grundbegriffen\" = fundamental concepts."]],"krv-a-1326":["The whole system of metaphysics accordingly consists of four principal parts: 1. Ontology. 2. Rational physiology. 3. Rational cosmology. 4. Rational theology. The second part, namely the doctrine of nature of pure reason, contains two divisions, *physica rationalis*\\*) and *psychologia rationalis*.",["- The footnote marker \"*)\" is kept as \\*) to attach to the footnote that follows (krv-a-1327).","- \"Naturlehre\" = doctrine of nature."]],"krv-a-1327":["\\*) Let it not be supposed that I understand by this what is commonly called *physica generalis*, which is more mathematics than philosophy of nature. For the metaphysics of nature is wholly distinct from mathematics. It has also by no means so many extensions of insight to offer as mathematics has, but it is none the less very important for the criticism of the pure knowledge of understanding in its application to nature in general. In default of such criticism, even mathematicians, in adhering to certain common concepts which are in fact metaphysical, have unwittingly burdened the doctrine of nature with hypotheses, which vanish when these principles are subjected to criticism, without thereby in the least detracting from the employment of mathematics in this field, an employment which is quite indispensable.",["- Restated the long final sentence's parenthesis as an appositional clause (\"an employment which is quite indispensable\").","- \"Critik\" rendered \"criticism\"; \"Verstandeserkentnisses\" = \"knowledge of understanding\" (literal; i.e. knowledge through the pure understanding).","- \"Erweiternde Einsichten\" = \"extensions of insight\" (the sense is insights that enlarge knowledge).","- Kant's \"ia nicht\" rendered \"not … supposed\" with emphasis lost."]],"krv-a-1328":["The original idea of a philosophy of pure reason itself prescribes this division. The division is therefore architectonic, in accordance with the essential ends of reason, and not merely technical, drawn up according to affinities accidentally observed and, as it were, at haphazard; and for that very reason it is also unalterable and legislative. There are, however, certain points which arouse misgivings and might weaken the conviction of its conformity to law.",["- Supplied \"The division\" for \"sie\" (resolved as the Abtheilung).","- Split the first sentence at the semicolon into a new sentence with a continuing clause; \"derselben\" in the last sentence taken as referring to the division.","- \"Gesetzmässigkeit\" = conformity to law (legitimacy is also possible)."]],"krv-a-1329":["First, how can I expect knowledge *a priori*, and therefore metaphysics, of objects so far as they are given to our senses, and therefore *a posteriori*? And how is it possible to know the nature of things according to principles *a priori*, and to arrive at a rational physiology? The answer is that we take nothing from experience beyond what is necessary to give us an object, partly of outer sense and partly of inner sense. The former is done through the mere concept of matter (impenetrable, lifeless extension), the latter through the concept of a thinking being (in the empirical inner representation: I think). For the rest, we should have to abstain, in the whole metaphysics of these objects, entirely from all empirical principles which might add to the concept some experience or other, in order to draw from it any judgment about these objects.",["- Divided the opening sentence into two questions; \"so fern … seyn und, wie…\" is irregular and was smoothed.","- \"Jenes … dieses\" = the former (outer sense) and the latter (inner sense).","- Final sentence: \"über den Begriff, noch irgend eine Erfahrung\" is slightly awkward (the \"noch\" may be a misprint for \"nach\"); rendered by sense, flagged as doubtful. \"müsten\" rendered as \"should have to\"."]],"krv-a-1330":["Second: what, then, becomes of empirical psychology, which has from of old maintained its place in metaphysics, and from which in our times such great things have been expected for the enlightenment of metaphysics, after the hope of accomplishing anything serviceable *a priori* had been abandoned? I answer that it goes where the proper (empirical) doctrine of nature has to be placed, namely on the side of applied philosophy, for which pure philosophy contains the principles *a priori*; and that it must accordingly be combined with the latter, but not mixed with it. Empirical psychology must therefore be wholly banished from metaphysics, and is already completely excluded from it by the very idea of metaphysics. Yet in accordance with scholastic usage we shall still have to allow it a little place there (though only as an episode), and that from economic motives, because it is not yet so rich as to constitute a study by itself, and is yet too important to be wholly expelled or attached elsewhere, where it would find even less affinity than in metaphysics. It is thus merely a stranger who has been admitted for so long, and to whom we grant a residence for some time, until he can take up his own dwelling in a comprehensive anthropology (the pendant to the empirical doctrine of nature).",["- \"derselben\" after \"Aufklärung\" resolved as metaphysics; \"iener\" in \"mit iener\" taken as applied philosophy (i.e. empirical doctrine of nature), which the argument requires; rendered \"the latter\" for clarity.","- The relative clause \"die also mit iener zwar verbunden\" is restated as a separate clause with \"it must\".","- \"Schulgebrauch\" = scholastic usage; \"Bewegursachen\" = motives; \"Plätzchen\" = little place."]],"krv-a-1331":["Such, then, is the general idea of metaphysics. At the outset more was demanded of it than can reasonably be required, and for a time men delighted themselves with pleasing expectations; but at last, finding themselves deceived in their hope, it has fallen into general contempt. From the whole course of our critique we shall have become sufficiently convinced that, although metaphysics cannot be the foundation of religion, it must yet always remain its bulwark; and that human reason, which is dialectical already by the trend of its nature, can never dispense with such a science, which curbs it and, through scientific and completely evident self-knowledge, prevents the devastations which a lawless speculative reason would otherwise quite infallibly produce in morals as well as in religion. We can therefore be assured, however coy or disdainful those may be who know how to judge a science not by its nature but solely by its accidental effects, that we shall always return to it as to a beloved with whom we have quarrelled; for since essential ends are here concerned, reason must work unceasingly either for thorough insight or for the destruction of good insights already existing.",["- The first sentence's participial construction (\"da man … fand\") is irregular; I made \"men\" the subject of the first part, but kept \"it has fallen\" (metaphysics), which preserves Kant's shift and makes the sentence readable. Slight smoothing.","- \"Selbsterkentniß\" = self-knowledge; \"Critik\" = \"critique\"; \"scientifisch\" = scientific.","- \"dieienige\" (those) = those who behave coyly; \"thun\" supplied verb carried by \"may be\".","- \"zügelt\" = curbs; \"Verwüstungen\" = devastations."]],"krv-a-1332":["Metaphysics, therefore, [as metaphysics] of nature and of morals alike, and above all the critique of reason, which ventures on its own wings and which precedes as a preparatory exercise (propaedeutic), together constitute what alone can be entitled philosophy in the genuine sense. Philosophy relates everything to wisdom, but by the path of science, the only path which, once it has been opened, is never overgrown and permits no wandering from the way. Mathematics, natural science, and even the empirical knowledge of man, have a high value as means, for the most part to ends that are contingent, but ultimately to ends that are necessary and essential to mankind. This, however, they can do only through the mediation of a knowledge of reason from mere concepts, which, call it what we may, is properly nothing but metaphysics.",[]],"krv-a-1333":["For this very reason metaphysics is also the culmination of all culture of human reason, and this culture is indispensable even if we set aside the influence of metaphysics, as science, upon certain determinate ends. For metaphysics considers reason in terms of its elements and highest maxims, and these must underlie the very possibility of some of the sciences and the use of them all. That, as mere speculation, it serves rather to prevent errors than to extend knowledge does no harm to its worth. On the contrary, this gives it dignity and authority, through the office of censor, which secures general order and harmony, indeed the well-being of the scientific commonwealth, and keeps its courageous and fruitful labours from straying away from the chief end, universal happiness.",[]],"krv-a-1334":["Fourth Chapter of the Transcendental Doctrine of Method. The History of Pure Reason.",[]],"krv-a-1335":["This title stands here only to mark a place which is left over in the system and which will have to be filled in the future. I content myself with casting, from a purely transcendental point of view, namely that of the nature of pure reason, a cursory glance over the whole of the labours hitherto devoted to it. To my eye, this presents, indeed, edifices, but edifices only in ruins.",[]],"krv-a-1336":["It is remarkable enough, though in the natural course of things it could not have been otherwise, that men in the childhood of philosophy began at the point at which we should now prefer to end: with the study, first, of the knowledge of God and of the hope [of a future life], or even of the constitution of another world. However gross the religious concepts which the old customs, surviving from the rude condition of the peoples, may have introduced, this did not prevent the more enlightened part from devoting itself to free enquiry into this subject. It was readily seen that there can be no more thorough and trustworthy way of pleasing the invisible power which governs the world, and so of being happy at least in another world, than a good course of life. Theology and morals were therefore the two motive forces, or better, the two points of reference, of all abstract enquiries of reason to which men have since devoted themselves. The former, however, was properly that which gradually drew merely speculative reason into the occupation which in the sequel became so famous under the name of metaphysics.",[]],"krv-a-1337":["I shall not here attempt to distinguish the periods at which this or that change in metaphysics occurred. I shall merely present, in a cursory sketch, the difference in the Idea which occasioned the principal revolutions. In this connection I find that the most notable changes on this arena of controversy have been brought about in respect of three aims.",[]],"krv-a-1338":["1. In respect of the object of all our modes of knowledge through reason, some philosophers were merely sensualists, others merely intellectualists. Epicurus may be entitled the foremost philosopher of sensibility, Plato of the intellectual. This distinction between the schools, however subtle, had begun in the earliest times, and has long been maintained without interruption. The former maintained that reality is to be found solely in objects of the senses, and that everything else is imagination. The latter said, on the contrary, that in the senses there is nothing but illusion, and that only the understanding knows what is true. Yet the former did not on that account deny reality to concepts of the understanding; but for them this reality was only logical, whereas for the others it was mystical. The former admitted intellectual concepts, but accepted only sensible objects. The latter demanded that the true objects should be purely intelligible, and asserted an intuition through the pure understanding, which, being unaccompanied by any senses, is in their opinion only confused.",[]],"krv-a-1339":["2. In respect of the origin of modes of pure knowledge of reason: whether they are derived from experience, or have their source in reason independently of it. Aristotle may be regarded as the head of the empiricists, Plato of the noologists. Locke, who in more recent times followed the former, and Leibniz, who followed the latter (though at a sufficient distance from his mystical system), have nevertheless been unable to bring this dispute to any decision. Epicurus, at least, proceeded for his part much more consistently in accordance with his sensualist system (for he never went with his inferences beyond the limits of experience) than Aristotle and Locke, above all the latter. Locke, after deriving all concepts and principles from experience, goes so far in the use of them as to assert that we can prove the existence of God and the immortality of the soul (though both objects lie wholly outside the limits of possible experience) just as evidently as any mathematical theorem.",[]],"krv-a-1340":["3. In respect of method. If anything is to be entitled method, it must be a procedure in accordance with principles. Now the method which at present prevails in this field of enquiry may be divided into the naturalistic and the scientific. The naturalist of pure reason adopts as his principle that, with regard to the most exalted questions which constitute the problem of metaphysics, more can be accomplished through common reason without science (which he calls sound reason) than through speculation. He maintains, therefore, that the size and distance of the moon can be determined more surely by the eye than by mathematical roundabout methods. This is mere misology reduced to principles; and, what is most absurd of all, the neglect of all artificial means is extolled as a method peculiar to itself for extending our knowledge. For as regards the naturalists, who act from want of greater insight, no just blame can be laid upon them. They follow common reason, without boasting of their ignorance as a method which is supposed to contain the secret of fetching truth out of the deep well of Democritus. *Quod sapio satis est mihi, non ego curo, esse quod Arcesilas aerumnosique Solones*, Pers. [\"What I know is enough for me; I care not to be what Arcesilas was, or the wretched Solons\"] is their motto, with which they can live contented and deserving of approval, without troubling themselves about science or confusing its business.",[]],"krv-a-1341":["As regards those who adopt a scientific method, they have here the choice of proceeding either dogmatically or sceptically, but in all cases they are under obligation to proceed systematically. In naming here, in respect of the former, the celebrated Wolff, and, in respect of the latter, David Hume, I may, for my present purpose, leave the rest unnamed. The critical path alone is still open. If the reader has had the kindness and patience to travel this path in my company, he may now judge whether, if it pleases him to contribute his share towards making this footpath into a highroad, that which many centuries have not been able to accomplish may not be attained before the present century runs out: namely, to bring human reason to complete satisfaction in regard to that which has always engaged its love of knowledge, though hitherto in vain.",["- 1332: The opening \"Metaphysik also, sowol der Natur, als der Sitten\" is elliptical; I supplied \"[as metaphysics]\" and divided the long final sentence in two. \"Verstande\" in \"im ächten Verstande\" is rendered \"in the genuine sense\".","- 1332: \"Kentniß\" and \"Vernunfterkentniß\" are rendered \"knowledge\" and \"knowledge of reason\".","- 1333: Divided the sentence beginning \"Daß sie, als blosse Speculation...\" in two. \"Cultur\" is rendered \"culture\" (cultivation). \"Maximen\" is retained as \"maxims\".","- 1335: Divided the final relative clause (\"welches freilich...\") into a separate sentence; \"meinem Auge\" is kept as \"to my eye\".","- 1336: \"[of a future life]\" is supplied after \"Hoffnung\" to complete the sense, which is not explicit in the German. The long sentence \"Was auch die alte Gebräuche...\" is restated in two.","- 1338: \"Sensual- andere blos Intellectualphilosophen\" is rendered \"sensualists\" and \"intellectualists\". \"Vernunfterkentnisse\" is rendered \"modes of knowledge through reason\" (plural).","- 1340: \"Grösse und Weite des Mondes\" is rendered \"size and distance of the moon\" (Weite = distance, a judgment of what the example requires). \"seine, Erkentniß\" has an odd comma in the German (a misprint for \"seine Erkentniß\"); I read \"our knowledge\", generalising the pronoun. The bracketed translation of the Persius is supplied by me, not in the text; the Latin is left unitalicised-in-quotation only as a quotation, in italics.","- 1341: The A-edition has \"Wolf\" in the German; I give the usual English spelling \"Wolff\". \"Heeresstrasse\" is rendered \"highroad\"."]]};
